export const murals = [
  {id:205,a:'Ashley Cassens',t:'',loc:'1500 Pasadena Ave S',bldg:'Florida Orange Groves Winery',lat:27.75273,lng:-82.73796,y:2026,cat:'commercial',ig:'ashley.cassens',bio:'Florida Orange Groves Winery wanted oranges. She gave them oranges — plus a rainbow lorikeet diving past hibiscus, plumeria, monstera leaves, and lime slices at the base. Full Florida palette on cinder block.\n\nAshley Cassens is a Tampa-based representational painter and muralist. She grew up in Daytona Beach, earned a BFA from Florida Southern College in 2006 and an MFA from Florida Atlantic University in 2017, and taught K-12 art for seven years before going full-time around 2020. Two of her oil paintings ended up licensed to HomeGoods.\n\nHer studio work is intimate figurative portraiture in layered oil. Her commercial walls run botanical — citrus, blossoms, pollinators, tropical foliage rendered with the same fine-painting eye. Signed lower-left "ASHLEY CASSENS @ashley.cassens."',desc:'A large exterior mural on a cinder-block wall of Florida Orange Groves Winery in South Pasadena. The composition spreads across the full wall against a cobalt-blue background overlaid with lavender silhouette shapes of oversized leaves and citrus. On the left, two large ripe oranges hang from a brown branch with deep green leaves and white five-petaled citrus blossoms. In the center, a rainbow lorikeet flies in profile with wings fully spread — cobalt-blue head with red eye and orange beak, yellow-and-green underwings, red breast, slate-blue body, and yellow-tipped flight feathers. Behind the bird, dark green monstera leaves with characteristic splits frame the upper-right register. On the right side, a cluster of large flowers fills the wall: a pink-and-teal-streaked hibiscus at the top, a smaller pink hibiscus below it, and a white-and-yellow plumeria bloom to its right. At the bottom-center, three sliced lime halves with visible green segments rest along the base, framed by additional green foliage. Signed in white block lettering at the lower left: "ASHLEY CASSENS" with "@ashley.cassens" beneath.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/ashley-cassens-orange-groves.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Provost Fellowship; Women in the Visual Arts Scholarship (x2); Thumbtack Top Pro 2022-2025',fw:[{name:'ashleycassens.com',url:'https://www.ashleycassens.com'}],sbio:'Florida Orange Groves Winery wanted oranges. She gave them oranges — plus a rainbow lorikeet diving past hibiscus, plumeria, monstera leaves, and lime slices at the base. Full Florida palette on cinder block.\n\nAshley Cassens is a Tampa-based representational figure painter and muralist, native Floridian from Daytona Beach. She earned a BFA from Florida Southern College (2006) focused on representational work and traditional oil painting methods, then an MFA from Florida Atlantic University (2017), where she received a Graduate Teaching Assistantship, Provost Fellowship, two Graduate Advisory Board Grants, the Friedland Grant, Rothenberger Scholarship, Ambassador of the Arts Scholarship, and the Women in the Visual Arts Scholarship (twice).\n\nShe taught K-12 art for seven years and was a teaching assistant at FAU before becoming a full-time artist around 2020. She runs Painting Possibilities LLC in Tampa\'s Northdale area. She is certified in scissor and boom lift safety, insured, and background checked. She charges $25-30 per square foot for murals with a $1,500 minimum.\n\nHer studio work is intimate figurative portraiture in oil — layered, chromatic paint on canvas and wood panels, often focused on body parts and details of loved ones. Notable series include "Eye Traps" (celebrating women through oil painting and patterned fabrics; two pieces licensed to HomeGoods; shown at Elevation Coffee Roasters, Tampa), "Projection Series" (vibrant portraits exploring experiences filtered through screens), and "Some Place Under Neith" (tiny portraits responding to IBLP cult pamphlets, addressing women\'s repression).\n\nCommercial clients include HomeGoods (licensing), Ann Cox Design, Cori Rose Handmade, Creative Pinellas, and various schools, non-profits, national corporations, and private residences. She has been featured on Fox 13 Tampa Bay (January 2023), Creative Loafing Tampa, CanvasRebel Magazine, Artsy Shark, and Daytime TV on News Channel 8. Thumbtack Top Pro 2022-2025.\n\nThis wall at Florida Orange Groves Winery (1500 Pasadena Ave S, South Pasadena) features two large oranges with white citrus blossoms hanging from branches at left, a rainbow lorikeet in mid-flight at center, and a cluster of hibiscus and plumeria flowers on the right — anchored at the base by three sliced limes. The background is a cobalt-to-teal blue wash with lavender silhouette shapes suggesting leaf shadows. Painted on a cinder-block exterior in May 2026.\n\nInstagram: @ashley.cassens. Website: ashleycassens.com.',src:['https://www.ashleycassens.com/','https://www.instagram.com/ashley.cassens/']},
  {id:139,a:'Melanie Posner',t:'Iryna',loc:'2200 block Central Ave',bldg:'',lat:27.770736,lng:-82.665998,y:2026,cat:'commercial',ig:'therealmelpoz',bio:'Melanie Posner painted this portrait of Iryna Zarutska — golden-blonde hair, a pink hibiscus at her shoulder, mint-to-gold light behind her.\n\nPosner\'s signature is photorealistic portraits of women — oversized expressive eyes, vibrant palettes, florals. For this wall she pulled the palette down to a soft mint-to-gold gradient and let Iryna\'s face carry it.\n\nPosner was born around 1993 outside Philadelphia, moved to Tampa in 2013, and earned her BFA in Painting from the University of Tampa in 2016. She is now based in Los Angeles. Her commercial commissions include scenic art for Walt Disney World\'s Star Wars: Galaxy\'s Edge, the eighteen-foot Hollander Hotel mural for New Belgium Brewing (a woman in a red headscarf, completed in under five days), and a forty-foot wall on the LGBT Center of Los Angeles for TikTok\'s 2024 "Create the Future" campaign. She was a cast member on Amazon Prime\'s docuseries Tampa Baes (2021). Her SHINE 2018 wall "New Beginnings" — a twenty-three-by-sixty-five-foot photorealistic woman lying on her side — is at 4139 7th Terrace South.',desc:'The mural depicts Iryna Zarutska, a23-year-old Ukrainian refugee who was stabbed and killed on the Charlotte, North Carolina light rail on August 22, 2025, in a random attack. The portrait shows her with flowing golden-blonde hair, gazing gently toward the viewer with a soft, warm expression. A pink hibiscus flower blooms near her shoulder. The background transitions from pale mint green on the left to warm golden yellow on the right. The piece is rendered in Posner\'s signature photorealistic style with warm, luminous skin tones. Posner was seen painting from a green scissor lift on the day the photo was taken.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/melanie-posner-irina.jpeg',from:'Los Angeles, CA',aud:'',insp:'Iryna Zarutska fled Ukraine during the Russian invasion and resettled in Charlotte, North Carolina. On August 22, 2025, she was stabbed from behind while seated on the Lynx Blue Line light rail at East/West Boulevard station. She was 23 years old. Her death drew international attention and prompted North Carolina to pass "Iryna\'s Law," a criminal justice package addressing bail and mental health evaluations.',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2018 Open Call artist; Walt Disney World Star Wars: Galaxy\'s Edge scenic artist; Tampa Baes (Amazon Prime, 2021) cast member; TikTok LA Pride campaign (2024)',fw:[{name:'melanieposner.com',url:'https://www.melanieposner.com'}],sbio:'She painted the woman in the red headscarf on the back of the Hollander Hotel in under five days, then went straight to Walt Disney World to paint scenic art for Star Wars: Galaxy\'s Edge.\n\nMelanie "Mel" Posner was born around 1993 in a small town just outside Philadelphia. She moved to Tampa in 2013 and earned her BFA in Painting from the University of Tampa in 2016, where she also studied art therapy. She was previously based in Gulfport, Florida, and is now in Los Angeles.\n\nHer style is photorealistic portraits of women — oversized, expressive eyes, vibrant rainbow color palettes, psychedelic paisley backgrounds, floral elements. She blends lifelike detail with touches of dreamy surrealism. She works in oil on canvas, collage, prints, and large-scale murals. She sources her subjects from Pinterest, Google, and social media, looking for eyes, story, and confidence. "Working large is just really exciting," she says. "I feel like I\'m a little bit more free doing murals." Her stated philosophy: paint strong, confident, independent women to be inspired by them.\n\nFor SHINE 2018, she was one of five local "Open Call" artists. Her mural "New Beginnings" at 4139 7th Terrace South is twenty-three feet high by sixty-five feet long — a photorealistic woman lying horizontally on her side, her hand cupping the right side of her face, one eye peeking through her fingers. She completed it in October 2018.\n\nHer commercial clients include Walt Disney World (scenic artwork for Star Wars: Galaxy\'s Edge at Hollywood Studios, Orlando), New Belgium Brewing (the eighteen-foot Hollander Hotel mural of a woman in a red headscarf with a glass of Mural Agua Fresca Cerveza, completed in under five days), Amazon Prime Video (a mural on a promotional bus for Tampa Baes), and TikTok. For TikTok\'s 2024 "Create the Future" campaign, she painted a forty-foot mural on the LGBT Center of Los Angeles at 8745 Santa Monica Blvd in West Hollywood.\n\nShe was a cast member on all eight episodes of Tampa Baes (Amazon Prime Video, 2021), a docuseries about a Tampa Bay lesbian friend group produced by 3 Ball Productions and filmed over roughly ten weeks starting April 2021. She described herself as "neutral" and avoiding drama on the show. "My anxiety during filming was up to the roof, my mental health totally depleted. It was very stressful and I will say, I love how the show came out." Her oil painting "Passion" (also called "Summer and Marissa") appears on screen. She worked for years as a bartender at The Honey Pot, a landmark LGBTQ+ club in Ybor City, Tampa.\n\nShe has exhibited at Enigma gallery in downtown St. Petersburg. Instagram: @therealmelpoz. Website: melanieposner.com.',src:['https://stpetecatalyst.com/melanie-posner/','https://www.melanieposner.com/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2018_SHINE_Mural_Melanie_Posner','https://outcoast.com/get-to-know-mel-posner-from-tampa-baes/','http://watermarkoutnews.com/2021/12/02/tampa-baes-star-melanie-posner-talks-filming-season-one/','https://www.imdb.com/name/nm12771071/','https://findmasa.com/artist/melanie-posner','https://www.artsy.net/artist/melanie-posner']},
  {id:1,a:'Aaron Tullo',t:'Golden Dawn',loc:'253 2nd Ave N',bldg:'Cordova Inn',lat:27.773963,lng:-82.636558,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'aaron.tullo',bio:'The Milwaukee Brewers drafted him twice. He picked up a paintbrush instead.\n\nAaron Tullo was born in St. Petersburg in 1988 and grew up throwing fastballs. He played at St. Petersburg High School, made second-team All-County as a sophomore, and tried out for the USA Baseball Youth National Team at 16. The Brewers took him in the 17th round of the 2006 draft — he turned them down for college. They took him again in 2007 out of the University of Tennessee. This time he signed.\n\nHe pitched in the Brewers\' minor league system for roughly six years before injuries ended it. He was around 25.\n\nWhat came next was a pivot, not a restart. Tullo studied at St. Petersburg College and the University of Tennessee (arts and sciences), and his eye for composition carried over. He started with nursery murals and pet portraits, then connected with the Vitale Brothers in Gulfport — working on their jobs, learning the trade, exhibiting alongside them. His first major public piece was a towering vintage pocket watch on Old Northeast Jewelers at 1131 4th St N — a mural he still considers his best work.\n\nHis style moves between figurative realism, bold graphic design, and sign painting. He letters by hand. He collaborates frequently with Ty Maner. Clients include Old Northeast Jewelers, Plotnick Law (where he painted Hemingway and Dali side by side), Fortunato\'s, and St. Pete Fishing Outfitters. For a Tampa Heights law office, he painted an Elliott Smith Figure 8-inspired piece with an MLK quote and a child\'s face.\n\nFor SHINE Origins 2025, he painted a five-story white peacock on the Cordova Inn — the festival\'s title sponsor. It fills the building\'s entire facade and reads from blocks away.',desc:'This five-story mural on the facade of the Cordova Inn depicts a large white peacock rendered in Tullo\'s characteristic blend of figurative realism and bold graphic composition. The bird\'s plumage fills the building\'s vertical expanse, creating a striking visual landmark in downtown St. Petersburg.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/aaron-tullo.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'https://cdn.muralquest.app/audio/1.mp3',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE Origins 2025 selected artist',fw:[{name:'aaronjamestullo.com',url:'https://www.aaronjamestullo.com'}],sbio:'The Milwaukee Brewers drafted him twice. He picked up a paintbrush instead.\n\nAaron James Tullo was born March 9, 1988 in St. Petersburg, Florida. He attended St. Petersburg High School under coach Stefan Futch, made second-team All-County as a sophomore in 2004, and was selected to try out for the USA Baseball Youth National Team at age 16. A throwing arm injury in the summer of 2005 complicated things, but the Milwaukee Brewers still drafted him in the 17th round (512th overall) of the 2006 MLB Draft out of high school. He turned them down and went to college.\n\nAt the University of Tennessee he pitched as a right-hander — 10 appearances as a freshman in 2007 with a 3.72 ERA and 56 strikeouts in 36.1 innings. The Brewers drafted him again in 2007, 47th round. He signed and entered their minor league system. He pitched professionally for roughly six years before injuries ended his baseball career around age 25. He also attended St. Petersburg College and is described as a USF graduate.\n\nTullo operates AJT Art + Design (also Aaron James Tullo Fine Art) out of a studio at 2411 15th Avenue North in St. Petersburg. He works in brush, spray paint, airbrush, and stencil across styles including realism, pop art, abstract, and typography. He was mentored by the Vitale Brothers, well-known St. Pete muralists — he worked on their jobs and exhibited at their Gulfport studio, including Renaissance-inspired paintings on wood at their "Birds and Bees" show.\n\nHis first major public piece was the vintage pocket watch mural at Old Northeast Jewelers / Hess Fine Art (1131 4th St N, St. Pete) in 2018 — a work he considers his best. Other confirmed murals include the Hemingway-and-Dali piece at Plotnick Law (1515 4th St N), a collage-style mural at St. Pete Fishing Outfitters, work at Fortunato\'s in Pinellas Park, and an Elliott Smith Figure 8-inspired mural with an MLK quote at Josh Sheridan\'s law office in Tampa Heights. He collaborates frequently with Ty Maner.\n\nFox 13 News profiled him in September 2023 ("Retired minor league baseball player creates custom murals in Tampa Bay"). He has been covered by the Northeast Journal, Creative Loafing Tampa Bay, and The Gabber.\n\nFor SHINE Origins 2025 — the festival\'s 11th year, focused on local Tampa Bay artists — Tullo was one of 17 selected artists. He painted a five-story white peacock on the facade of the Cordova Inn at 253 2nd Ave N, the festival\'s title sponsor. The bird\'s plumage fills the building\'s vertical expanse, creating a landmark visible from blocks away in downtown St. Petersburg.',fn:['Milwaukee Brewers drafted him twice','Self-taught muralist','Lives and paints in St. Pete'],src:['https://www.aaronjamestullo.com/','https://utsports.com/sports/baseball/roster/aaron-tullo/443','https://www.milb.com/player/aaron-tullo-476609','https://www.mlb.com/player/aaron-tullo-476609','https://www.fox13news.com/news/former-minor-league-baseball-player-creates-custom-murals-in-tampa-bay','https://northeastjournal.org/st-petes-artsy-murals-move-into-the-old-northeast/','https://northeastjournal.org/living-color-celebrating-murals-in-the-northeast/','https://www.cltampa.com/music/elliott-smithinspired-mural-surfaces-in-tampa-heights-12242173/','https://thegabber.com/vitale-brothers-birds-and-bees-in-gulfport/','https://ilovetheburg.com/st-pete-shine-origins/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/for-year-11-shine-mural-festival-brings-it-back-home/','https://83degreesmedia.com/tampa-bay-arts-roundup-shine-mural-festival-arts-annual-more/','https://luciwest.com/photos-from-shine-origins-mural-festival-2025/']},
  {id:2,a:'Amy Ilic-Volpe',t:'Here & Now',loc:'31st St & 7th Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.762868,lng:-82.674598,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'amy_ilic_volpe',bio:'Before she picked up a brush, she spent nearly a decade on cruise ships entertaining children around the world. That sense of play never left.\n\nAmy Ilic-Volpe is a Florida native now based in St. Petersburg. Her work is process-driven and spontaneous — fluid color, layered geometric shapes, bold palettes of pink, green, yellow, and turquoise that land somewhere between playful and contemplative. She cites Hilma af Klint as an influence and listens to Ani DiFranco in the studio.\n\nShe works with children through youth education and art classes, and calls teaching her greatest achievement — "sparking wonder." Kids are her primary artistic inspiration. She views her practice through "the excitement and vitality of young eyes" and prioritizes fun over seriousness. Her current collection, Keys, explores the mystery found in isolation and going within.\n\nRecognition includes the 2022 Gasparilla Festival of the Arts Emerging Artist award, a feature in All She Makes magazine, and a SHINE 2022 "Bright Spot" mural. For SHINE Origins 2025, she returned with "Here & Now" — a candy-bright explosion of circles, stripes, polka dots, and stacked block letters on 31st Street and 7th Avenue South.',desc:'A wide, vibrant mural on a cinder block wall bursting with overlapping geometric shapes — circles, stripes, polka dots, and waves — in a candy-bright palette of pink, green, yellow, orange, purple, and turquoise. The left side features the words "Here & Now" in stacked multicolor block letters. Signed "Amy Ilic Volpe @amy_ilic_volpe" in pink in the lower right corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/amy-ilic-volpe.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'2022 Gasparilla Festival of the Arts Emerging Artist Recipient; All She Makes magazine featured artist; SHINE 2022 Bright Spot artist',fw:null,sbio:'Before she picked up a brush, she spent nearly a decade on cruise ships entertaining children around the world. That sense of play never left.\n\nAmy Ilic-Volpe is a Florida native and multimedia artist based in St. Petersburg. Her work is process-driven and spontaneous — fluid color, layered geometric shapes, bold palettes that land somewhere between playful and contemplative. She allows materials to "speak" to her, describing painting as "a visceral and emotional practice." She cites Hilma af Klint as an influence and listens to Ani DiFranco in the studio. She advocates against pretentiousness in the art world, calling for artists to "lift each other up."\n\nShe works with children through youth education and art classes, and calls teaching her greatest achievement — "sparking wonder." Kids are her primary artistic inspiration. She views her practice through "the excitement and vitality of young eyes." Her current collection, Keys, explores "the excitement and mystery that can be found in isolation and going within ourselves." She uses meditation and intentional thought in her creative practice.\n\nRecognition includes the 2022 Gasparilla Festival of the Arts Emerging Artist Recipient award, a feature in All She Makes magazine, and a SHINE St. Petersburg "Bright Spot" mural in 2022. She has been profiled by Not Real Art and FloridaRAMA / Fairgrounds St. Pete. For SHINE Origins 2025, she painted "Here & Now" at 31st Street and 7th Avenue South — a wide mural on a cinder block wall bursting with overlapping geometric shapes in a candy-bright palette. Instagram: @amy_ilic_volpe.',src:['https://notrealart.com/artist-amy-ilic-volpe-paintings-cultivate-childlike-glee/','https://floridarama.art/','https://gasparillaarts.com/']},
  {id:153,a:'Ashley Cassens',t:'Sweet Disposition',loc:'2705 54th Ave N',bldg:'54th Ave Deli Mart',lat:27.821592,lng:-82.67115,y:2025,cat:'commercial',ig:'ashley.cassens',bio:'Two of her oil paintings ended up at HomeGoods. She has sixty-two murals under her belt and a five-star rating across forty reviews on Thumbtack.\n\nAshley Cassens is a Tampa-based representational figure painter and muralist. She grew up in Daytona Beach, earned a BFA from Florida Southern College in 2006, and an MFA from Florida Atlantic University in 2017. She taught K-12 art for seven years before going full-time as an artist around 2020.\n\nHer studio work is intimate figurative portraiture — layered, chromatic oil paint on canvas and wood panels, often focused on specific body parts and details of people she knows. Her "Eye Traps" series celebrates women through traditional oil painting, patterned fabrics, and fresh color. Her "Some Place Under Neith" series responds to fundamentalist cult pamphlets, addressing women\'s repression through tiny portrait paintings.\n\n"Sweet Disposition" wraps the side of 54th Ave Deli Mart in the Lealman area — bold botanical imagery flowing from orange blossoms through a bumblebee in flight to ripe strawberries, commissioned by Creative Pinellas in partnership with the Lealman CRA. She installed it in May 2025.',desc:'A wide panoramic mural wrapping the side of a commercial building. The composition flows left to right through bold botanical imagery: orange blossoms with white petals and deep green leaves against a royal blue background on the left, transitioning to a large bumblebee in flight over teal-green leaf patterns at center, then into ripe red strawberries with white flowers against warm gold and green tones on the right. The palette is vivid — deep blues, rich greens, warm golds, and bright reds. "Ashley Cassens" is signed at the far left. The style blends botanical realism with graphic design sensibility.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/ashley-cassens.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL',aud:'',insp:'Commissioned by Creative Pinellas in partnership with the Lealman CRA to enhance community visibility.',maw:'',aaw:'Provost Fellowship; Women in the Visual Arts Scholarship (x2); Thumbtack Top Pro 2022-2025',fw:[{name:'ashleycassens.com',url:'https://www.ashleycassens.com'}],sbio:'Two of her oil paintings ended up at HomeGoods. She has sixty-two murals under her belt and a five-star rating across forty reviews on Thumbtack.\n\nAshley Cassens is a Tampa-based representational figure painter and muralist, native Floridian from Daytona Beach. She earned a BFA from Florida Southern College (2006) focused on representational work and traditional oil painting methods, then an MFA from Florida Atlantic University (2017), where she received a Graduate Teaching Assistantship, Provost Fellowship, two Graduate Advisory Board Grants, the Friedland Grant, Rothenberger Scholarship, Ambassador of the Arts Scholarship, and the Women in the Visual Arts Scholarship (twice).\n\nShe taught K-12 art for seven years and was a teaching assistant at FAU before becoming a full-time artist around 2020. She runs Painting Possibilities LLC in Tampa\'s Northdale area. She is certified in scissor and boom lift safety, insured, and background checked. She charges $25-30 per square foot for murals with a $1,500 minimum.\n\nHer studio work is intimate figurative portraiture in oil — layered, chromatic paint on canvas and wood panels, often focused on body parts and details of loved ones. Notable series include "Eye Traps" (celebrating women through oil painting and patterned fabrics; two pieces licensed to HomeGoods; shown at Elevation Coffee Roasters, Tampa), "Projection Series" (vibrant portraits exploring experiences filtered through screens), and "Some Place Under Neith" (tiny portraits responding to IBLP cult pamphlets, addressing women\'s repression).\n\nCommercial clients include HomeGoods (licensing), Ann Cox Design, Cori Rose Handmade, Creative Pinellas, and various schools, non-profits, national corporations, and private residences. She has been featured on Fox 13 Tampa Bay (January 2023), Creative Loafing Tampa, CanvasRebel Magazine, Artsy Shark, and Daytime TV on News Channel 8. Thumbtack Top Pro 2022-2025.\n\n"Sweet Disposition" at 2705 54th Ave N, St. Petersburg (54th Ave Deli Mart) was commissioned by Creative Pinellas in partnership with the Lealman Community Redevelopment Area, installed May 2025. The mural wraps the side of the building in vivid oranges, blues, greens, and reds — botanical imagery blending orange blossoms, a bumblebee, and strawberries in a contemporary tropical palette. It took approximately two weeks to complete.\n\nInstagram: @ashley.cassens. Website: ashleycassens.com.',src:['https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/sweet-disposition/','https://www.ashleycassens.com/','https://www.instagram.com/ashley.cassens/','https://canvasrebel.com/meet-ashley-cassens/','https://www.artsyshark.com/2017/12/15/featured-artist-ashley-cassens/','https://www.fox13news.com/news/lifelong-artist-turns-her-art-into-full-time-business','https://www.thumbtack.com/fl/tampa/art-services/ashley-cassens-art/']},
  {id:3,a:'Brain The Genius',t:'Culture',loc:'31st St & 7th Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.762876,lng:-82.674687,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'brainthegenius',bio:'He built one of the rare Black-owned tattoo studios in St. Petersburg, then kept going.\n\nChris Roberts — Brain The Genius, formerly Brain Storm — has spent over two decades running Urban Ink Tattoo Company, a shop he turned into one of the city\'s most respected studios. But tattooing is one lane. He is a painter, designer, and cinematographer who founded Culture Creative, an art and event space on 34th Street South that amplifies local talent.\n\nHis 2020 SHINE Bright Spot mural at the St. Pete Youth Farm, sponsored by NOAA Fisheries, focused on diversity and inclusion in STEM professions — a community project in the Innovation District. For SHINE Origins 2025, he painted "Culture" at 31st Street and 7th Avenue South.',desc:'A bold mural on a cinder block wall featuring the word "Culture" in ornate gold script, framed by oversized gold bamboo hoop earrings and topped with a gold crown. Lush monstera leaves fill the background in deep greens against black. The left side reads "Bamboo Earrings At Least Two Pair" in white graffiti-style lettering — a nod to the LL Cool J lyric. Signed "BRAIN @brainthegenius" in the lower right.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/brain-the-genius.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2020 Bright Spot artist; SHINE Origins 2025 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'He built one of the rare Black-owned tattoo studios in St. Petersburg, then kept going.\n\nChris Roberts — professionally Brain The Genius, formerly Brain Storm — is a St. Petersburg-based multidisciplinary artist, tattoo artist, and entrepreneur. He has spent over two decades building Urban Ink Tattoo Company into one of the city\'s most respected studios, recognized as one of the rare Black-owned businesses in the body art community. Beyond tattooing, he is a painter, designer, and cinematographer. He founded Culture Creative, a dynamic art and event space on 34th Street South that amplifies local talent.\n\nIn 2020, he painted a SHINE Bright Spot mural at the St. Pete Youth Farm in the Innovation District, a community project sponsored by NOAA Fisheries focused on diversity and inclusion in STEM professions. For SHINE Origins 2025, he painted "Culture" at 31st Street and 7th Avenue South alongside Karel Garcia, Amy Ilic-Volpe, Elizabeth Barenis, Jenipher Chandley, Zulu Painter, and Rebekah Lazaridis. Instagram: @brainthegenius.',src:['https://ilovetheburg.com/st-pete-shine-origins/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/for-year-11-shine-mural-festival-brings-it-back-home/','https://www.theburgvotes.com/2020/02/23/st-petes-very-own-the-urban-ink-tattoo-company-expands/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/brain-storm-2020/','https://www.urbaninkstpete.com/brainstorm','https://www.tbnweekly.com/diversions/article_e4122d66-19e2-11eb-a7cd-3fcb84f00403.html','https://83degreesmedia.com/tampa-bay-arts-roundup-shine-mural-festival-arts-annual-more/']},
  {id:4,a:'Cecilia Lueza',t:'Radiance',loc:'251 10th St N',bldg:'Haddy',lat:27.774478,lng:-82.64766,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'cecilialuezaap',bio:'She painted an entire intersection. Fifty volunteers helped. Then a storm washed it away, so they painted it again.\n\nCecilia Lueza was born in 1971 in Posadas, in Argentina\'s subtropical northeast — a landscape of dense flora and fauna that still runs through everything she makes. She earned her MA in Visual Arts from the National University of La Plata in Buenos Aires, held her first solo exhibition there in 1998, and moved to the United States shortly after. She settled in Pinellas County in 2007.\n\nHer work spans murals, monumental sculptures, and mixed-media installations, all built on bold geometric patterns, clean lines, and jewel-tone color. Birds, women, and children recur. She was originally a monochromatic painter in Argentina — Florida\'s light changed that.\n\nThe public art resume is staggering: nearly 60 installations since 2013. Starbucks, the North Carolina Museum of Art, the Atlanta BeltLine, the James Museum, a kinetic angel sculpture in Chattanooga acquired for permanent public display. Her work sits in the Bass Museum of Art, the Museum of Contemporary Art Miami, and the Florida Department of State collection.\n\nShe is a four-time SHINE artist — "Common Ground" in 2016 (the intersection), "Encounter" and "The Blue Hour" in 2018, "The Sky Is the Limit" in 2019, and "Radiance" at the Haddy Factory for SHINE 2025 Origins.',desc:'A large-scale mural on the side of a commercial building depicting a vividly colored hummingbird in flight, wings spread wide, rendered in iridescent greens, blues, and oranges. The bird hovers against a dynamic background of overlapping translucent circles in gradients of blue, teal, green, and yellow — Lueza\'s signature geometric style. Signed "LUEZA" in the upper right. The building number 255 is visible at the left edge.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/cecilia-lueza.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Sculpture Key West People\'s Choice Award (2006); Karrvaz Artist-in-Residency, Spain (2006); Florida Artist Enhancement Grant (2008); Creative Pinellas Professional Development Grant (2008); Tourist Development Council Grant, Miami-Dade (2011); four-time SHINE festival artist',fw:[{name:'lueza.com',url:'https://www.lueza.com'}],sbio:'She painted an entire intersection. Fifty volunteers helped. Then a storm washed it away, so they painted it again.\n\nCecilia Lueza was born in 1971 in Posadas, Misiones Province, Argentina — subtropical northeast, dense flora and fauna, a landscape that still runs through everything she makes. She earned her MA in Visual Arts (Painting) from the National University of La Plata in Buenos Aires (1990-1994), held her first solo exhibition in Buenos Aires in 1998, and moved to the United States in 1999 after meeting Miami-based artists. She lived in Miami before settling in Pinellas County, Florida in 2007. She cites Georgia O\'Keeffe and Francis Bacon as influences.\n\nHer work spans murals, monumental sculptures, and mixed-media installations — bold geometric patterns, clean lines, jewel-tone color gradations. Birds, women, and children recur. She was originally a monochromatic painter in Argentina; Florida\'s light and vibrancy changed that. Nature is her primary subject. She calls her art an attempt to embody "universal meaning such as innocence, beauty, freedom."\n\nThe public art resume is staggering: nearly 60 installations since 2013, over 20 years as a working public artist. Major murals include "I Am My Own Muse" at the Marriott City Center in Durham NC (2019), "Exuberance" at HCC Dale Mabry Campus in Tampa (2020), "Summertime" in Clearwater (2021, selected from 190+ nationwide applicants), "Chromascape" in Jersey City (2021), "The Legend of Ondine" in Lakeland (2022), "The Starbucks Siren" at 3939 4th St N in St. Petersburg (2023), "Life Force" at Arya Residences in St. Petersburg (2024), and "Morning Ritual" at Joffrey\'s Coffee HQ in Tampa (2026).\n\nPublic sculptures include "Winds of Change" — a kinetic stainless steel angel acquired by the Chattanooga Public Art Commission for permanent display — "Luminarium" at the Arts and Innovation Center in Rockville MD (interactive wall sculptures with fiber optics and LEDs activated by sound), "Time Flies Sundial" at River Terrace Education Campus in Washington DC, multiple school commissions for the DC Department of General Services, "Delta" and "Nova" in Jacksonville, "Dual Nature" in Reno, "Journey of Hope" in Fort Myers, "Nexus" in Tampa, "Caleidoscopio" in Toledo, "Sea Orb" in Jacksonville, and "Golden Hour" at the Orange County Administration Center in Orlando (2026).\n\nClients and commissions include Starbucks, the North Carolina Museum of Art (mural for a Frida Kahlo/Diego Rivera exhibition), the Atlanta BeltLine, the City of Atlanta Mayor\'s Office of Cultural Affairs, the Chattanooga Public Art Commission, Hillsborough Community College, and municipalities in Indianapolis, Clearwater, Fort Myers, and Jacksonville. Her work is in the collections of the Bass Museum of Art Miami, the Museum of Contemporary Art Miami, the Florida Department of State, and 13+ institutional collections. Gallery representation includes Kavachnina Contemporary, Onessimo Fine Art (Palm Beach Gardens), Mary Martin Art Gallery (Naples), and Naomi Silva Gallery (Atlanta).\n\nAwards include a Tourist Development Council Grant (Miami-Dade, 2011), Professional Development Individual Artist Grant (Pinellas County, 2008), Florida Artist Enhancement Grant (2008), Puffin Foundation Grant (2006), Karrvaz Artist-in-Residency in Spain (2006), and Sculpture Key West People\'s Choice Award (2006).\n\nShe is a four-time SHINE artist: "Common Ground" (2016) — a street mural at Central Ave and 5th Street outside Florida CraftArt, painted with 50+ volunteers, washed away by a storm, painted again; "Encounter" (2018) at the ArtsXchange in the Warehouse Arts District; "The Blue Hour" (2018) at ONE St. Petersburg; "The Sky Is the Limit" (2019) — a 164 x 11 foot ground mural at North Shore Park pier; and "Radiance" (2025) at the Haddy Factory for SHINE Origins. Instagram: @cecilialuezaap. Website: lueza.com.',fn:['Painted an entire intersection with 50 volunteers','Argentine-born, trilingual','Geometric abstraction + cultural identity'],src:['https://www.lueza.com/mural-art/','https://www.lueza.com/bio/','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/cecilia-lueza-colors-our-world/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/cecilia-lueza-2/','https://www.visitstpeteclearwater.com/profile/common-ground-cecilia-lueza/140482','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cecilia_Lueza','https://www.lueza.com/public-sculpture/','https://canvasrebel.com/meet-cecilia-lueza/','https://www.letstalkdurham.com/lets-talk-magazine/cecilia-lueza-artist-at-work','https://kavachnina.com/other-collected-artists/cecilia-lueza/','https://monochronicle.com/artist/cecilia-lueza-art-projects/']},
  {id:5,a:'Derek Donnelly',t:'',loc:'1847 1st Ave N',bldg:'',lat:27.7724559,lng:-82.658692,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'saintpaintarts',bio:'He painted a T-Rex in a Hawaiian shirt going fishing — for AARP. That tells you most of what you need to know about Derek Donnelly.\n\nA Pinellas County native, Donnelly grew up in Pinellas Park, graduated from the Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School in 2001, and studied graphic design and digital illustration at IADT Tampa. He went full-time on murals in 2010. A year later he opened Saint Paint Arts gallery on the 600 block of Central Avenue — not as a vanity project, but as a launchpad. Several artists got their first real exposure there before he closed it in 2014 to focus on walls.\n\nHis style sits at the intersection of illustration and realism — playful without being childish, bold without being loud. His son Syre shows up in murals across the city. So does Florida wildlife. He doesn\'t separate the personal from the public work. In 2012, when mentor Bill "Woo" Correira died, Donnelly organized the Woomorial — a 40-artist tribute mural on the 600 block that became a landmark. That instinct to build things for other people keeps showing up. He founded COVE gallery in Pinellas Park, helped establish the Pinellas Arts Village district, and started Public Art Project Inc., a nonprofit that pushes public art into places it wouldn\'t otherwise reach and raised pandemic relief funds for local artists.\n\nHis client list runs from Fender Guitars to the Tampa Bay Rays to Sirata Beach Resort — the first major St. Pete Beach resort to commission a local artist. Multiple Best of the Bay wins. Inaugural Artmaker 2026 at Ruth Eckerd Hall. For SHINE 2025, he wasn\'t just painting — he ran operations and served as artist liaison for the entire festival while producing two murals of his own.',desc:'A wide-format mural wrapping around two walls of a single-story building. The central scene shows Donnelly\'s signature cartoon boy — in a red cap and sunglasses — riding a pelican in flight over ocean waves. A dragonfly hovers on the right side, and seagulls fill the sky. The left wall transitions into an underwater scene in deep blue-green tones, with a diver amid coral and sea life. Signed "Donnelly" in the upper right. The palette shifts from cool oceanic blues on the left to bright sky blue and green on the right.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/derek-donnelly-1847.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'My murals are a celebration of all things local, with my son and our city\'s bird.',maw:'',aaw:'Multiple Best of the Bay winner (Creative Loafing Tampa Bay); inaugural Artmaker 2026 at Ruth Eckerd Hall; SHINE 2025 Operations Lead; SHINE 2018 Open Call curator; co-founded downtown St. Pete mural movement',fw:[{name:'saintpaintarts.com',url:'https://www.saintpaintarts.com'}],sbio:'He painted a T-Rex in a Hawaiian shirt going fishing — for AARP. That tells you most of what you need to know about Derek Donnelly.\n\nA Pinellas County native who grew up in Pinellas Park, Donnelly graduated from the Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School in 2001 and studied graphic design and digital illustration at IADT Tampa. He has 20-plus years of painting experience and went full-time on murals in 2010. Within a year he opened Saint Paint Arts gallery on the 600 block of Central Avenue in downtown St. Petersburg — not as a vanity project, but as a launchpad for other artists. Several got their first real exposure there before he closed it in 2014 to focus entirely on walls.\n\nIn the early 2010s, Donnelly and collaborator Sebastian Coolidge co-founded the downtown St. Pete mural movement when the area was, by his own account, "drab and virtually art-free." Together they painted "A Moment to Reflect" on the Florida Craftsmen building — at the time the largest mural in St. Petersburg, four stories tall, completed in ten days. It depicts a businessman removing his shoes to embrace creativity, with a green tie nodding to sponsor Regions Bank.\n\nIn November 2012, mentor Bill "Woo" Correira died from complications of brain surgery. Correira was known for his fish paintings and his fierce support of the 600 block arts community. Donnelly organized the Woomorial, a tribute mural on the side of the Saint Paint gallery where 40-plus artists contributed — Donnelly painted Woo\'s portrait while others added marine life. It became a 600 block landmark.\n\nHis style blends illustration and realism — bold character work, signature gradients, hand-drawn detail, playful without being childish. His son Syre appears in murals across the city, including the Outshine project at Planet Retro Records on Dr. MLK Jr. Street N, where he collaborated with Hinson the Healer, R2 Romero, and Artist Slim. Florida wildlife is a constant. He has painted portraits of Prince, Kurt Cobain, and Tupac Shakur. His canvases featuring Syre were exhibited in the "Son-Shine" show at the Wayward Goose.\n\nDonnelly\'s client list includes AARP, Hilton Hotels, the Tampa Bay Rays, MLB, Fender Guitars, St. Petersburg College, Sirata Beach Resort (two murals as part of a $15 million renovation — the first major St. Pete Beach resort to commission a local artist), Rococo Steak (a ceiling mural), Station House, and Smith & Associates Real Estate. His work spans Clearwater Beach, Gulfport, Treasure Island, Madeira Beach, Largo, Dunedin, Tampa, Seminole, North Ft. Myers, and Anna Maria Island.\n\nThat instinct to build things for other people keeps showing up. He founded COVE gallery (Creative Opportunities & Visual Entertainment) in late 2016 in the Pinellas Arts Village — a city-funded project using rehabbed shipping containers at 5705 Park Blvd in Pinellas Park. He helped establish the Pinellas Arts Village district from the beginning and hosts ArtWalk events there every fourth Saturday. He started Public Art Project Inc., a nonprofit that creates large-scale beautification projects, educates the public on the social and economic impact of the arts, and raised pandemic relief funds for local artists. He is a member of the Bloom Collective and a participant in the Carmada art car movement alongside Sebastian Coolidge, James Oleson, Daniel Barojas, and Zulu Painter.\n\nHe helped originate the SHINE Mural Festival and in 2018 curated five "Open Call" walls for the festival. Multiple Best of the Bay wins from Creative Loafing Tampa Bay. Selected as the inaugural Artmaker 2026 at Ruth Eckerd Hall\'s Marcia P. Hoffman School of the Arts — a year-long exhibition in the hall\'s galleries with a featured artwork on the annual holiday ornament. He appeared on The Catalyst Sessions (May 2020) and RHStv\'s "Jabari\'s Beautiful People: Featuring St. Pete Murals."\n\nFor SHINE 2025, executive director Helen French brought him on as Operations Lead and artist liaison — the person with "the pulse" of the city. It was his first time as a featured SHINE artist after a decade of painting city walls. He produced two murals: a vintage postcard piece reading "Greetings from Hollander Hotel" at 421 4th Ave N, and a panoramic ocean scene with his signature pelican-riding boy at 1847 1st Ave N.\n\nThe dinosaur in the Hawaiian shirt fishing off the waterfront? That\'s his AARP "Age Friendly St. Pete" series on the east wall of the Morean Arts Center — the older generation, he says, enjoying life to the fullest.',src:['https://www.saintpaintarts.com/','https://www.rutheckerdhall.com/news/detail/artmaker-2026','https://stpetecatalyst.com/catalyst-sessions-recap-derek-donnelly/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/muralist-derek-donnelly-wants-art-scene-changes/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/for-year-11-shine-mural-festival-brings-it-back-home/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/w/art-inspired-by-hip-hop-legends-meet-derek-donnelly/','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/heres-how-derek-donnellys-jazzing-up-the-sirata-with-murals-on-st-pete-beach-12223445','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/derek-donnelly-will-talk-about-his-recently-uncovered-st-pete-mural-this-weekend-19808486/','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/derek-donnellys-cove-displays-a-broad-spectrum-of-visual-art-trendsetters/','https://www.localshops1.com/blog/business-profile-painting-the-town-171','https://www.tbnweekly.com/diversions/museums_theater/article_9b54abd0-3339-5496-88fd-bcf4034ffb04.html','https://www.paradisenewsfl.com/artists-spotlight-nov-2025/','https://www.pinellasartsvillage.com/donnelly-cove.html','https://stpetemuraltour.com/2014/08/12/derek-donnelly/','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/bill-correira-woo-remembered-by-the-600-block-arts-community-he-ardently-supported-12299303','https://patch.com/florida/stpete/bill-woo-correira-was-humble-challenged-people-to-be-great','https://www.smithandassociates.com/blog/smith--associates-real-estate-commissions-local-mural-titan-derek-donnelly-for-public-art-project.html','https://ilovetheburg.com/st-pete-shine-origins/','https://stpeteissupercool.com/blogs/latest-stories/developers-build-condos-shine-builds-culture-this-year-is-all-about-local-talent','https://83degreesmedia.com/tampa-bay-arts-roundup-shine-mural-festival-arts-annual-more/']},
  {id:6,a:'Derek Donnelly',t:'Greetings from Hollander Hotel',loc:'421 4th Ave N',bldg:'Hollander Hotel',lat:27.776706,lng:-82.63879,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'saintpaintarts',bio:'He painted a T-Rex in a Hawaiian shirt going fishing — for AARP. That tells you most of what you need to know about Derek Donnelly.\n\nA Pinellas County native, Donnelly grew up in Pinellas Park, graduated from the Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School in 2001, and studied graphic design and digital illustration at IADT Tampa. He went full-time on murals in 2010. A year later he opened Saint Paint Arts gallery on the 600 block of Central Avenue — not as a vanity project, but as a launchpad. Several artists got their first real exposure there before he closed it in 2014 to focus on walls.\n\nHis style sits at the intersection of illustration and realism — playful without being childish, bold without being loud. His son Syre shows up in murals across the city. So does Florida wildlife. He doesn\'t separate the personal from the public work. In 2012, when mentor Bill "Woo" Correira died, Donnelly organized the Woomorial — a 40-artist tribute mural on the 600 block that became a landmark. That instinct to build things for other people keeps showing up. He founded COVE gallery in Pinellas Park, helped establish the Pinellas Arts Village district, and started Public Art Project Inc., a nonprofit that pushes public art into places it wouldn\'t otherwise reach and raised pandemic relief funds for local artists.\n\nHis client list runs from Fender Guitars to the Tampa Bay Rays to Sirata Beach Resort — the first major St. Pete Beach resort to commission a local artist. Multiple Best of the Bay wins. Inaugural Artmaker 2026 at Ruth Eckerd Hall. For SHINE 2025, he wasn\'t just painting — he ran operations and served as artist liaison for the entire festival while producing two murals of his own.',desc:'A vintage-style postcard mural reading "Greetings from Hollander Hotel, St. Pete, FL." A cartoon boy in a red cap rides a pelican wearing sunglasses over turquoise waters, with dolphins leaping below, seagulls overhead, and a palm-tree island with the Sunshine Skyway Bridge in the distance. Signed "Donnelly @Saint Paint Arts" in the lower right. The retro color palette of teals, creams, and warm tans gives it a classic Florida tourism feel.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/derek-donnelly-hollander.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'https://cdn.muralquest.app/audio/6.mp3',insp:'My murals are a celebration of all things local, with my son and our city\'s bird.',maw:'',aaw:'Multiple Best of the Bay winner (Creative Loafing Tampa Bay); inaugural Artmaker 2026 at Ruth Eckerd Hall; SHINE 2025 Operations Lead; SHINE 2018 Open Call curator; co-founded downtown St. Pete mural movement',fw:[{name:'saintpaintarts.com',url:'https://www.saintpaintarts.com'}],sbio:'He painted a T-Rex in a Hawaiian shirt going fishing — for AARP. That tells you most of what you need to know about Derek Donnelly.\n\nA Pinellas County native who grew up in Pinellas Park, Donnelly graduated from the Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School in 2001 and studied graphic design and digital illustration at IADT Tampa. He has 20-plus years of painting experience and went full-time on murals in 2010. Within a year he opened Saint Paint Arts gallery on the 600 block of Central Avenue in downtown St. Petersburg — not as a vanity project, but as a launchpad for other artists. Several got their first real exposure there before he closed it in 2014 to focus entirely on walls.\n\nIn the early 2010s, Donnelly and collaborator Sebastian Coolidge co-founded the downtown St. Pete mural movement when the area was, by his own account, "drab and virtually art-free." Together they painted "A Moment to Reflect" on the Florida Craftsmen building — at the time the largest mural in St. Petersburg, four stories tall, completed in ten days. It depicts a businessman removing his shoes to embrace creativity, with a green tie nodding to sponsor Regions Bank.\n\nIn November 2012, mentor Bill "Woo" Correira died from complications of brain surgery. Correira was known for his fish paintings and his fierce support of the 600 block arts community. Donnelly organized the Woomorial, a tribute mural on the side of the Saint Paint gallery where 40-plus artists contributed — Donnelly painted Woo\'s portrait while others added marine life. It became a 600 block landmark.\n\nHis style blends illustration and realism — bold character work, signature gradients, hand-drawn detail, playful without being childish. His son Syre appears in murals across the city, including the Outshine project at Planet Retro Records on Dr. MLK Jr. Street N, where he collaborated with Hinson the Healer, R2 Romero, and Artist Slim. Florida wildlife is a constant. He has painted portraits of Prince, Kurt Cobain, and Tupac Shakur. His canvases featuring Syre were exhibited in the "Son-Shine" show at the Wayward Goose.\n\nDonnelly\'s client list includes AARP, Hilton Hotels, the Tampa Bay Rays, MLB, Fender Guitars, St. Petersburg College, Sirata Beach Resort (two murals as part of a $15 million renovation — the first major St. Pete Beach resort to commission a local artist), Rococo Steak (a ceiling mural), Station House, and Smith & Associates Real Estate. His work spans Clearwater Beach, Gulfport, Treasure Island, Madeira Beach, Largo, Dunedin, Tampa, Seminole, North Ft. Myers, and Anna Maria Island.\n\nThat instinct to build things for other people keeps showing up. He founded COVE gallery (Creative Opportunities & Visual Entertainment) in late 2016 in the Pinellas Arts Village — a city-funded project using rehabbed shipping containers at 5705 Park Blvd in Pinellas Park. He helped establish the Pinellas Arts Village district from the beginning and hosts ArtWalk events there every fourth Saturday. He started Public Art Project Inc., a nonprofit that creates large-scale beautification projects, educates the public on the social and economic impact of the arts, and raised pandemic relief funds for local artists. He is a member of the Bloom Collective and a participant in the Carmada art car movement alongside Sebastian Coolidge, James Oleson, Daniel Barojas, and Zulu Painter.\n\nHe helped originate the SHINE Mural Festival and in 2018 curated five "Open Call" walls for the festival. Multiple Best of the Bay wins from Creative Loafing Tampa Bay. Selected as the inaugural Artmaker 2026 at Ruth Eckerd Hall\'s Marcia P. Hoffman School of the Arts — a year-long exhibition in the hall\'s galleries with a featured artwork on the annual holiday ornament. He appeared on The Catalyst Sessions (May 2020) and RHStv\'s "Jabari\'s Beautiful People: Featuring St. Pete Murals."\n\nFor SHINE 2025, executive director Helen French brought him on as Operations Lead and artist liaison — the person with "the pulse" of the city. It was his first time as a featured SHINE artist after a decade of painting city walls. He produced two murals: a vintage postcard piece reading "Greetings from Hollander Hotel" at 421 4th Ave N, and a panoramic ocean scene with his signature pelican-riding boy at 1847 1st Ave N.\n\nThe dinosaur in the Hawaiian shirt fishing off the waterfront? That\'s his AARP "Age Friendly St. Pete" series on the east wall of the Morean Arts Center — the older generation, he says, enjoying life to the fullest.',fn:['Opened Saint Paint Arts gallery on the 600 block','Runs operations for the entire SHINE festival','His son Syre appears in murals across the city'],src:['https://www.saintpaintarts.com/','https://www.rutheckerdhall.com/news/detail/artmaker-2026','https://stpetecatalyst.com/catalyst-sessions-recap-derek-donnelly/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/muralist-derek-donnelly-wants-art-scene-changes/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/for-year-11-shine-mural-festival-brings-it-back-home/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/w/art-inspired-by-hip-hop-legends-meet-derek-donnelly/','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/heres-how-derek-donnellys-jazzing-up-the-sirata-with-murals-on-st-pete-beach-12223445','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/derek-donnelly-will-talk-about-his-recently-uncovered-st-pete-mural-this-weekend-19808486/','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/derek-donnellys-cove-displays-a-broad-spectrum-of-visual-art-trendsetters/','https://www.localshops1.com/blog/business-profile-painting-the-town-171','https://www.tbnweekly.com/diversions/museums_theater/article_9b54abd0-3339-5496-88fd-bcf4034ffb04.html','https://www.paradisenewsfl.com/artists-spotlight-nov-2025/','https://www.pinellasartsvillage.com/donnelly-cove.html','https://stpetemuraltour.com/2014/08/12/derek-donnelly/','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/bill-correira-woo-remembered-by-the-600-block-arts-community-he-ardently-supported-12299303','https://patch.com/florida/stpete/bill-woo-correira-was-humble-challenged-people-to-be-great','https://www.smithandassociates.com/blog/smith--associates-real-estate-commissions-local-mural-titan-derek-donnelly-for-public-art-project.html','https://ilovetheburg.com/st-pete-shine-origins/','https://stpeteissupercool.com/blogs/latest-stories/developers-build-condos-shine-builds-culture-this-year-is-all-about-local-talent','https://83degreesmedia.com/tampa-bay-arts-roundup-shine-mural-festival-arts-annual-more/']},
  {id:183,a:'Derek Donnelly',t:'Guardian of Gulfport',loc:'Gulfport',bldg:'Stormrunners Tavern',lat:27.73807,lng:-82.707725,y:2025,cat:'commercial',ig:'saintpaintarts',bio:'He painted a T-Rex in a Hawaiian shirt going fishing — for AARP. That tells you most of what you need to know about Derek Donnelly.\n\nA Pinellas County native, Donnelly grew up in Pinellas Park, graduated from the Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School in 2001, and studied graphic design and digital illustration at IADT Tampa. He went full-time on murals in 2010. A year later he opened Saint Paint Arts gallery on the 600 block of Central Avenue. His style sits at the intersection of illustration and realism — playful without being childish, bold without being loud.\n\nHis client list runs from Fender Guitars to the Tampa Bay Rays to Sirata Beach Resort. Multiple Best of the Bay wins. Inaugural Artmaker 2026 at Ruth Eckerd Hall. For SHINE 2025, he ran operations and served as artist liaison for the entire festival while producing two murals of his own.',desc:'A monochromatic teal mural covering the entire side wall of Stormrunners Tavern. A massive Neptune/Poseidon figure dominates the right side — a weathered bearded face with flowing hair that merges into coral, sea fans, and marine growth. His expression is stern and watchful. Sharks swim through the water to the left. The entire composition is rendered in variations of a single deep teal-green, creating the effect of an underwater bas-relief sculpture. The textured stucco wall surface adds to the carved-stone illusion. Signed "Donnelly" at upper left.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/derek-donnelly-guardian-gulfport.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'A symbol of hope and resilience for Gulfport following Hurricanes Helene and Milton.',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:[{name:'saintpaintarts.com',url:'https://www.saintpaintarts.com'}],fn:['Painted as a symbol of hope after Hurricanes Helene and Milton','Neptune/Poseidon figure protecting Gulfport\'s waterfront','Donnelly ran SHINE 2025 operations while painting two of his own murals'],src:['https://thegabber.com/have-you-seen-the-guardian-of-gulfport-on-stormrunners/']},
  {id:7,a:'Dreamweaver',t:'Abiding Light',loc:'696 1st Ave N',bldg:'Vernis Bowling',lat:27.771649,lng:-82.643688,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'dream.weavin',bio:'She painted her first mural at seven, on her aunt\'s backyard shed in Waco, Texas. She has not stopped.\n\nTaylor Smith — Dreamweaver — grew up between Portland, Oregon and just south of Atlanta, born in Dallas. She studied communications and fine art at Georgia College and State University, with a minor in graphic design, graduating in 2017. Before that, she took private painting lessons as a kid and apprenticed at a tattoo shop at 16 — too young to actually tattoo under Georgia law, but old enough to learn.\n\nShe spent time doing live painting at music festivals — Electric Daisy Carnival in Vegas, Okeechobee, Electric Forest — before relocating to Florida around 2019. She works almost entirely in spray paint, blending expressive abstraction with realism. Nature runs through everything: wildlife, water, birds, flora. She is left-handed.\n\nShe has painted an estimated 120 to 150 murals, 15 to 20 in St. Pete alone. Other projects include the Clearwater Municipal Parking Garage and a sea turtle mural at Coachman Park for Art Oasis 2024, and "The Garden Within" in downtown Kissimmee for Osceola Arts. She is a resident artist at AER Tampa and a member of Few and Far, an international women\'s graffiti crew.\n\nFor SHINE she painted in 2022 at 1510 1st Ave N and returned for SHINE Origins 2025 at the Vernis Bowling building on the 600 block — where the SHINE movement started in 2012.',desc:'A multi-story mural wrapping around a lavender building, featuring a white egret with delicate plumage nestled among enormous, lush flower petals in deep pinks, corals, oranges, and purples. The bird\'s long neck rises elegantly through the blooms on the left wall, while the right wall continues with swirling petals and soft round forms in warm sunset tones. The scale is dramatic — the egret spans several stories — and the soft-focus, almost photorealistic rendering gives the whole piece a dreamlike, ethereal quality.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/dreamweaver.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL',aud:'https://cdn.muralquest.app/audio/7.mp3',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2022 selected artist; SHINE Origins 2025 selected artist; Art Oasis Mural Festival 2024; Few and Far international women\'s graffiti crew member',fw:[{name:'weavindreams.com',url:'https://weavindreams.com'}],sbio:'She painted her first mural at seven, on her aunt Carolyn\'s backyard shed in Waco, Texas. She has not stopped.\n\nTaylor Smith — professionally Dreamweaver — was born in Dallas, Texas, grew up between Portland, Oregon and just south of Atlanta, Georgia. She earned a BA in Communications and Fine Art with a minor in Graphic Design from Georgia College and State University in 2017. She took private painting lessons throughout elementary and middle school and did a tattoo apprenticeship at 16 — eight months, but Georgia age restrictions meant she couldn\'t actually tattoo.\n\nAfter college she started live painting at concerts and festivals in Atlanta (2017), worked in restaurant service, graphic design, and creative event production, then relocated to Florida around 2018-2019. She went full-time as an artist around 2020. She works primarily in spray paint and aerosol, blending expressive abstraction with realism. Nature is the constant — wildlife, water, birds, flora and fauna. She also paints on canvas, airbrush, skateboard decks, surfboards, shoes, and backpacks. She is left-handed.\n\nShe has painted an estimated 120 to 150 murals, with 15 to 20 in St. Pete. Festival credits include SHINE 2022 (mural at 1510 1st Ave N, Yacht Power Products building), SHINE Origins 2025 (Vernis Bowling building at 696 1st Ave N), Art Oasis Mural Festival 2024 (Clearwater Municipal Parking Garage and a marine-themed sea turtle mural at Coachman Park Dockmaster\'s Office), Wynwood Mural Festival, Paint Memphis 2019, and Art Basel. She has done live painting at Electric Daisy Carnival (Las Vegas), Okeechobee Music Festival, and Electric Forest Festival.\n\nOther projects include "The Garden Within" at 219-231 Broadway in downtown Kissimmee for Osceola Arts\' ARTisNOW Public Murals Project (2025). She was Artist in Residence at Parc at Wesley Chapel, Tampa (2023) and is a resident artist at AER Tampa. She is a contributor to Few and Far, an international women\'s graffiti crew, and collaborates with fellow artist "Slim Gee" under KindMinds Art (@kindmindsart). She teaches spray painting and mural techniques to youth communities and is listed on Creative Pinellas as "an avid supporter of improving our communities through public art and collaboration."\n\nProfiled in Voyage MIA Magazine (January 2020) and The Artisan Magazine. Contact: Dreamweavin.murals@gmail.com. Website: weavindreams.com. Instagram: @dream.weavin.',fn:['Painted her first mural at age seven','Mother-daughter duo with Denise Weaver','Known as Dreamweaver'],src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/dream-weaver-2022','https://www.tampabay.com/life-culture/entertainment/2025/12/04/shine-mural-festival-st-petersburg-florida-arts-hurricane/','https://www.graffitistreet.com/shine-st-petersburg-street-art-mural-festival-florida-2022/','https://voyagemia.com/interview/meet-taylor-smith-dreamweaver-designs-st-pete-fl/','https://www.artistsalapts.com/taylor-smith','https://monochronicle.com/artist/dreamweaver/','https://weavindreams.com/aboutdreamweaver','https://theartisanmagazine.com/i-am-dreamweavin-my-way-and-bringing-life-to-life-through-art/','https://www.osceolaarts.org/post/new-mural-the-garden-within-blooms-in-downtown-kissimmee','https://creativepinellas.org/artist/taylor-smith/']},
  {id:189,a:'Dulk',t:'',loc:'680 28th St S',bldg:'St. Pete Athletic at The Factory',lat:27.7631,lng:-82.669472,y:2025,cat:'commercial',ig:'dulk1',bio:'One side: a Florida panther and a wild boar sprint through vegetation. The other side: a manatee with a flower-headed figure on its back. Before he painted, he went up the Weeki Wachee River with the Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation to see them in real life.\n\nAntonio Segura Donat — Dulk — grew up in Valencia, Spain, copying exotic animals from his parents\' old encyclopedias. At nineteen, while studying economics at the University of Valencia, a friend dragged him into the graffiti world. Within a year he dropped economics for illustration.\n\nHe builds surreal ecosystems on walls — animals, plants, and invented hybrid creatures layered into dense, dreamlike compositions saturated with color. The scenes look fantastical but are rooted in real ecology; he researches every local ecosystem before painting it.\n\nHis murals span Berlin, Paris, Las Vegas, Miami, and Vancouver. He is a National Geographic Expeditions expert and a Liquitex-sponsored artist. For Las Fallas 2022 in Valencia he built "Protegeix Allò que estimes" (Protect What You Love), a 23-meter polar bear sculpture. The Factory commission was curated by Jenee Priebe of No Good Deeds Art and supported by the Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation.',desc:'A two-sided mural wrapping St. Pete Athletic at The Factory campus, visible from the Pinellas Trail. One face shows a Florida panther and wild boar sprinting through stylized vegetation against a warm yellow-orange sky, with birds and leaves in motion. The other face depicts an underwater scene in deep teal — a manatee swimming through the water surrounded by fantastical sea creatures, a flower-headed figure riding on its back, fish schools, and bubbling marine life. Both sides are rendered in Dulk\'s signature surrealist style with dense layering of real and imagined species.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/unknown-animals-running.jpeg',from:'Valencia, Spain',aud:'',insp:'Celebrates Florida\'s biodiversity — the artist researched native species with the Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation before painting.',maw:'',aaw:'National Geographic Expeditions expert; Liquitex sponsored artist',fw:null,sbio:'Antonio Segura Donat — Dulk — born 1983 in Valencia, Spain. Studied illustration in Valencia after dropping out of an economics degree at the University of Valencia. Surreal nature murals around the world. National Geographic Expeditions expert. Liquitex-sponsored artist. Las Fallas 2022 monumental sculpture ("Protegeix Allò que estimes" — 23-meter polar bear). Exhibited at Thinkspace Projects (LA, "Legacy" solo show 2018), Moniker Art Fair (London, 2017).\n\nSt. Pete mural at The Factory commissioned by The Factory St. Pete and St. Pete Athletic, curated by Jenee Priebe of No Good Deeds Art, supported by the Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation.\n\nInstagram: @dulk1.',fn:['Spent time on the Weeki Wachee River studying Florida wildlife before painting','National Geographic Expeditions expert','Two-sided mural — land animals on one face, underwater manatee on the other'],src:['https://dulk.es/about/','https://dulk.es/bio/','https://www.artsy.net/artist/antonio-segura-donat-dulk','https://streetartunitedstates.com/honest-and-candid-interview-with-dulk/','https://www.nationalgeographic.com/expeditions/experts/antonio-segura/','https://www.expeditions.com/about/expedition-team/antonio-segura','https://www.liquitex.com/blogs/special-projects/dulk-valencia-spain','https://www.thisiscolossal.com/2022/03/falles-valencia-dulk/','https://www.blocal-travel.com/street-art-news/dulk-las-fallas-valencia/','https://dulk.es/projects/protegix-allo-que-estimes-falla-municipal-2022/','https://thinkspaceprojects.com/shows/moniker-dulk/','https://arrestedmotion.com/2018/11/upcoming-dulk-legacy-thinkspace-projects/','https://www.tbartspassport.com/when-the-walls-breathe-dulk-brings-a-dream-of-florida-to-life-at-the-factory/','https://www.nogooddeedsart.com/']},
  {id:8,a:'Elizabeth Barenis',t:'Shine On Palms',loc:'31st St & 7th Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.762864,lng:-82.674834,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'elizabethbarenis',bio:'In 2018, she was one of ten painters invited to the Mark Rothko Art Center in Latvia. She grew up in the Mississippi Delta, where her parents owned a music store.\n\nElizabeth Barenis studied piano with her mother, took private art lessons throughout childhood, attended the Mississippi School for Math and Science, and graduated from Sarah Lawrence College in 2004. A summer at the Chautauqua Institute for Art that same year sealed it — she committed to painting full-time.\n\nShe calls her approach "Abstract Precisionism" — generalized shapes rendered with clean, precise lines that recall Cubism. Palm fronds, tropical flora, seascapes, forests, and foggy scenes dominate.\n\nShe moved to St. Petersburg in 2015 for family reasons and stayed for the arts community. Since then: a 2017 Creative Pinellas Emerging Artist Grant, a 2023 Professional Artist Grant, solo shows at the Morean Arts Center ("Tropical Splendor") and Art Center Sarasota ("Palmistry"), and a mural on the Bayfront Hospital ambulance bay — a diptych painting photographed and enlarged for the louvered exterior wall. Her first public mural was "Super Palm" in Dunedin in 2022, 300 square feet on a Parks and Recreation building.\n\nFor SHINE Origins 2025, she painted alongside Karel Garcia, Amy Ilic-Volpe, Brain The Genius, and others at 31st Street and 7th Avenue South.',desc:'A wide mural on a cinder block wall depicting oversized tropical fan palm fronds in layered shades of green, teal, and soft yellow. The leaves radiate outward in clean, geometric planes — Barenis\'s "Abstract Precisionism" style — creating depth through overlapping translucent shapes. Signed "Elizabeth Barenis 2025" in the lower left corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/elizabeth-barenis.jpeg',from:'Pinellas County, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'2017 Creative Pinellas Emerging Artist Grant; 2018 International Painting Symposium at Mark Rothko Art Center, Latvia; 2023 Creative Pinellas Professional Artist Grant; 2025 Award of Excellence, Art Festival Beth-El; Margaret Murphy Steward Best of Show, Morean Arts Center; SHINE Origins 2025 selected artist',fw:[{name:'elizabethbarenis.com',url:'https://www.elizabethbarenis.com'}],sbio:'In 2018, she was one of ten painters from seven countries invited to the International Painting Symposium at the Mark Rothko Art Center in Daugavpils, Latvia, marking Rothko\'s 115th anniversary. She grew up in the Mississippi Delta, where her parents owned a music store.\n\nElizabeth Barenis was born and raised in Greenville, Mississippi. She studied piano with her mother and later learned guitar. She enrolled in private art lessons throughout childhood. She attended the Mississippi School for Math and Science in Columbus, Mississippi, then graduated from Sarah Lawrence College in Bronxville, New York in 2004. A summer at the Chautauqua Institute for Art that same year sealed it — she committed to painting full-time.\n\nShe calls her approach "Abstract Precisionism" — generalized shapes rendered with clean, precise lines reminiscent of Cubism. Her primary subjects are palm fronds, tropical flora, seascapes, forests, rainbows, and foggy scenes. She works in acrylic and oil. Her philosophy: beauty is "an indication of a greater force at work in our world, one of harmony and well-being."\n\nShe relocated to St. Petersburg, Florida in 2015 for family reasons and stayed for the arts community. Awards include a 2017 Creative Pinellas Emerging Artist Grant, a 2023 Creative Pinellas Professional Artist Grant, a 2025 Award of Excellence at Art Festival Beth-El, and the Margaret Murphy Steward Best of Show at a Morean Arts Center members exhibition (which led to her solo show there). Solo exhibitions include "Palmistry" at Art Center Sarasota (October-November 2022) and "Tropical Splendor" at the Morean Arts Center (2024, with gallery talk August 8). Group exhibitions include "Perspectives" at Studio@620 (with Vincent Mancuso), ARTicles Art Gallery in the Uptown Arts District, SixStar Art Studios, and Art Festival Beth-El (2025, 2026).\n\nPublic art includes "Super Palm" — a 300 sq ft mural on the Parks and Recreation building at 1700 Solon Ave in Dunedin, Florida (November 2022, her first public mural), signal box designs in Clearwater, and an ambulance bay mural at Bayfront Hospital (now Orlando Health Bayfront) in St. Petersburg — based on a 48 by 144 inch diptych painting, photographed and enlarged for the louvered exterior wall. Healthcare clients include Moffitt Cancer Center (artwork acquisition), Bayfront Health St. Petersburg (mural and painting commissions), and Premier Community Healthcare in New Port Richey (extensive series of prints).\n\nFor SHINE Origins 2025, she was one of approximately 17 local artists selected. She painted at 31st Street and 7th Avenue South alongside Karel Garcia, Amy Ilic-Volpe, Brain The Genius, Jenipher Chandley, Zulu Painter, and Rebekah Lazaridis. Website: elizabethbarenis.com. Instagram: @elizabethbarenis.',src:['https://stpetecatalyst.com/for-year-11-shine-mural-festival-brings-it-back-home/','https://ilovetheburg.com/st-pete-shine-origins/','https://floridapaints.com/blog/shine-mural-festival-st-petersburg-2025/','https://creativepinellas.org/artist/elizabeth-barenis/','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/who-is-elizabeth-barenis/','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/elizabeth-barenis-in-sarasota/','https://moreanartscenter.org/exibition/tropical-splendor-paintings-by-elizabeth-barenis/','https://www.bahiastudios.org/blog/artcentersarasota','https://www.floridarama.art/artist/elizabeth-barenis','https://www.articlesstpete.com/shop-for-art/elizabeth-barenis','https://www.rothkocenter.com/en/ekspozicija/international-painting-symposium-mark-rothko-2018/','https://www.baltictimes.com/painting_symposium_mark_rothko_2018/','https://www.artfestivalbethel.com/2026-artist-bios.html','https://sixstarartstudios.com/six-star-launch-arts-new-era-in-st-pete/']},
  {id:118,a:'Elizabeth Barenis',t:'Healing Palms',loc:'744 6th Ave. S.',bldg:'Bayfront Health St. Pete',lat:27.764375,lng:-82.64328,y:2025,cat:'commercial',ig:'',bio:'In 2018, she was one of ten painters invited to the Mark Rothko Art Center in Latvia. She grew up in the Mississippi Delta, where her parents owned a music store.\n\nElizabeth Barenis studied piano with her mother, took private art lessons throughout childhood, attended the Mississippi School for Math and Science, and graduated from Sarah Lawrence College in 2004. A summer at the Chautauqua Institute for Art that same year sealed it — she committed to painting full-time.\n\nShe calls her approach "Abstract Precisionism" — generalized shapes rendered with clean, precise lines that recall Cubism. Palm fronds, tropical flora, seascapes, forests, and foggy scenes dominate. She works from her own photography, particularly nighttime reference shots where she reconstructs pattern and form. She also has a background in medical illustration, which shows in the detail.\n\nShe moved to St. Petersburg in 2015 for family reasons and stayed for the arts community. Since then: a 2017 Creative Pinellas Emerging Artist Grant, a 2023 Professional Artist Grant, solo shows at the Morean Arts Center ("Tropical Splendor") and Art Center Sarasota ("Palmistry"), and a mural on the Bayfront Hospital ambulance bay — a diptych painting photographed and enlarged for the louvered exterior wall. Her first public mural was "Super Palm" in Dunedin in 2022, 300 square feet on a Parks and Recreation building.\n\nFor SHINE Origins 2025, she painted alongside Karel Garcia, Amy Ilic-Volpe, Brain The Genius, and others at 31st Street and 7th Avenue South.',desc:'Overlapping angular planes in shades of green and blue fill the louvered exterior wall of the Bayfront Health ambulance entrance. The geometric shapes suggest tropical palm fronds abstracted into faceted, crystalline forms — a diptych painting enlarged to building scale. The cool palette and precise geometry complement the medical campus architecture while adding a distinctly tropical warmth.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/elizabeth-barenis-healing-palms.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'2017 Creative Pinellas Emerging Artist Grant; 2018 International Painting Symposium at Mark Rothko Art Center, Latvia; 2023 Creative Pinellas Professional Artist Grant; 2025 Award of Excellence, Art Festival Beth-El; Margaret Murphy Steward Best of Show, Morean Arts Center; SHINE Origins 2025 selected artist',fw:[{name:'elizabethbarenis.com',url:'https://www.elizabethbarenis.com'}],sbio:'In 2018, she was one of ten painters from seven countries invited to the International Painting Symposium at the Mark Rothko Art Center in Daugavpils, Latvia, marking Rothko\'s 115th anniversary. She grew up in the Mississippi Delta, where her parents owned a music store.\n\nElizabeth Barenis was born and raised in Greenville, Mississippi. She studied piano with her mother and later learned guitar. She enrolled in private art lessons throughout childhood. She attended the Mississippi School for Math and Science in Columbus, Mississippi, then graduated from Sarah Lawrence College in Bronxville, New York in 2004. A summer at the Chautauqua Institute for Art that same year sealed it — she committed to painting full-time.\n\nShe calls her approach "Abstract Precisionism" — generalized shapes rendered with clean, precise lines reminiscent of Cubism. Her primary subjects are palm fronds, tropical flora, seascapes, forests, rainbows, and foggy scenes. She works from her own photography, particularly nighttime reference photos where she reconstructs pattern and form. She also has a background in medical illustration, which informs her sensitivity to detail. She works in acrylic and oil. Her philosophy: beauty is "an indication of a greater force at work in our world, one of harmony and well-being."\n\nShe relocated to St. Petersburg, Florida in 2015 for family reasons and stayed for the arts community. Awards include a 2017 Creative Pinellas Emerging Artist Grant, a 2023 Creative Pinellas Professional Artist Grant, a 2025 Award of Excellence at Art Festival Beth-El, and the Margaret Murphy Steward Best of Show at a Morean Arts Center members exhibition (which led to her solo show there). Solo exhibitions include "Palmistry" at Art Center Sarasota (October-November 2022) and "Tropical Splendor" at the Morean Arts Center (2024, with gallery talk August 8). Group exhibitions include "Perspectives" at Studio@620 (with Vincent Mancuso), ARTicles Art Gallery in the Uptown Arts District, SixStar Art Studios, and Art Festival Beth-El (2025, 2026).\n\nPublic art includes "Super Palm" — a 300 sq ft mural on the Parks and Recreation building at 1700 Solon Ave in Dunedin, Florida (November 2022, her first public mural), signal box designs in Clearwater, and an ambulance bay mural at Bayfront Hospital (now Orlando Health Bayfront) in St. Petersburg — based on a 48 by 144 inch diptych painting, photographed and enlarged for the louvered exterior wall. Healthcare clients include Moffitt Cancer Center (artwork acquisition), Bayfront Health St. Petersburg (mural and painting commissions), and Premier Community Healthcare in New Port Richey (extensive series of prints).\n\nFor SHINE Origins 2025, she was one of approximately 17 local artists selected. She painted at 31st Street and 7th Avenue South alongside Karel Garcia, Amy Ilic-Volpe, Brain The Genius, Jenipher Chandley, Zulu Painter, and Rebekah Lazaridis. Website: elizabethbarenis.com. Instagram: @elizabethbarenis.',src:['https://creativepinellas.org/artist/elizabeth-barenis/','https://www.elizabethbarenis.com/3d-work','https://moreanartscenter.org/exibition/tropical-splendor-paintings-by-elizabeth-barenis/','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/who-is-elizabeth-barenis/','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/elizabeth-barenis-in-sarasota/','https://www.bahiastudios.org/blog/artcentersarasota','https://www.floridarama.art/artist/elizabeth-barenis','https://www.articlesstpete.com/shop-for-art/elizabeth-barenis','https://www.rothkocenter.com/en/ekspozicija/international-painting-symposium-mark-rothko-2018/','https://www.baltictimes.com/painting_symposium_mark_rothko_2018/','https://www.artfestivalbethel.com/2026-artist-bios.html','https://sixstarartstudios.com/six-star-launch-arts-new-era-in-st-pete/']},
  {id:9,a:'Isac Gres',t:'',loc:'699 Central Ave',bldg:'Melting Pot',lat:27.771671,lng:-82.643881,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'isacgres.art',bio:'He left Cuba in 2019 and walked through twelve countries — including the Darien Gap — before reaching the United States. He brought his brushes.\n\nIsac Gres was born in 1993 in Bayamo, Cuba. He studied cubism, hyperrealism, surrealism, and conceptual thinking at the E.I.A Jose Marti Perez art academy, then spent three years studying psychology at the Celia Sanchez Manduley institute, followed by a year of mandatory military service. He left Cuba on November 12, 2019, established a tattoo studio called Isac Ink in Uruguay, then relocated to Miami in 2022 to reunite with family before settling in St. Petersburg.\n\nHis work is contemporary surrealism in mixed media — acrylic, oil, spray paint. Fragmented figures, dreamlike landscapes, hybrid beings. Pathways and doorways recur. So do jellyfish, which he reads as resilience and immortality, and apples, which stand for knowledge and identity. Light drives everything. "Color for me means light," he says, "and also good energy."\n\nFor SHINE Origins 2025 he painted the tallest wall of his career — a surrealist design with melting clocks (a nod to Dali) on the Melting Pot restaurant.',desc:'A massive two-story mural wrapping around the corner of a building in swirling cosmic surrealism. The composition features molten planetary spheres, a city skyline encased in a dripping glass orb, melting candles nodding to Dalí, and a sculptural elephant form emerging from fiery nebula clouds. The palette is dominated by deep space blues and purples contrasted with blazing oranges, reds, and golds. Signed "Isac Gres" in the lower corners of both walls.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/isac-gres.jpeg',from:'Cuba / St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'https://cdn.muralquest.app/audio/9.mp3',insp:'The mural is on a restaurant called the Melting Pot, and Gres appreciated how the work represents a melting pot of cultures. The melting clocks are a nod to Dalí.',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE Origins 2025 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'He left Cuba in 2019 and walked through twelve countries — including the Darien Gap — before reaching the United States. He brought his brushes.\n\nIsac Gres was born November 29, 1993 in Bayamo, Cuba. He studied cubism, hyperrealism, surrealism, and conceptual thinking at the E.I.A Jose Marti Perez art academy, then spent three years studying psychology at the Celia Sanchez Manduley institute, followed by a year of mandatory military service in Cuba\'s army. He left Cuba on November 12, 2019 and traveled through 12 countries including the Darien Gap. He established "Isac Ink" tattoo studio in Uruguay (approximately 2019-2022), then relocated to Miami in 2022 to reunite with family. He worked at a tattoo studio while painting before settling in St. Petersburg, Florida.\n\nHis work is contemporary surrealism in mixed media — acrylic, oil, spray paint. Bold abstractions balancing vibrant color, texture, and form. Fragmented figures, dreamlike landscapes, hybrid beings. Recurring symbols include pathways and doorways, jellyfish (resilience and immortality), and apples (knowledge and identity). Light drives everything: "Color for me means light...and also good energy." He is represented by Patti Suzette Art and has exhibited in South Carolina and multiple galleries nationwide. He has an NFT collection on OpenSea.\n\nFor SHINE Origins 2025 he painted the tallest wall of his career — a surrealist design with melting clocks (a nod to Dali) on the Melting Pot restaurant. Instagram: @isacgres.art.',fn:['Walked through 12 countries leaving Cuba in 2019','Architectural training from Havana','First SHINE festival as artist'],src:['https://ilovetheburg.com/st-pete-shine-origins/','https://floridapaints.com/blog/shine-mural-festival-st-petersburg-2025/','https://83degreesmedia.com/tampa-bay-arts-roundup-shine-mural-festival-arts-annual-more/','https://theartisanmagazine.com/isacgres-artist/','https://www.pattisuzette.com/copy-of-chase-parker','https://www.madsgallery.art/item/409d58eb-c9e0-41b8-bf4c-026c65df0e63/artist/isac-gres']},
  {id:10,a:'Jenipher Chandley',t:'Stronger Together',loc:'31st St & 7th Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.762868,lng:-82.675064,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'jenipherchandley',bio:'She painted "Stronger Together" with her daughter Becka. The title is the work.\n\nJenipher Chandley was born and raised in St. Petersburg. She earned degrees in interior design and graphic design from IADT Tampa, is a mother of four, and recently became a grandmother. She works predominantly in oils — representational figurative work infused with surrealism and symbolism. The female form recurs, embodying fragility and strength. Her central theme, in her own words, is "the story of love" — seeking, experiencing, losing, rediscovering.\n\nShe started painting outside Sake Bomb on Central Avenue around 2011 and turned those nights into something permanent. Twelve-plus years later she is the lead curator at Bar@548 (the same building, new name), hosts a free weekly Wednesday-night art meetup taking no commission, and curates monthly emerging-artist shows. Her studio is at the ArtsXchange, Studio Gallery 104.\n\nShe received a 2023 Emerging Artist Grant from Creative Pinellas and has been a Local Artist Spotlight at the Gasparilla Festival of the Arts and American Stage Theater. For SHINE 2021 she painted her first festival mural. For SHINE Origins 2025 she returned to this wall at 31st Street and 7th Avenue South.',desc:'A wide mural on a cinder block wall with the words "Stronger Together" in large flowing white script across a deep teal background. The text is framed by a lush border of curling green vines and tropical flame-colored flowers — gloriosa lilies in reds, oranges, and yellows. Signed "Jenipher Chandley" in the upper right corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/jenipher-chandley.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'2023 Creative Pinellas Emerging Artist Grant; Gasparilla Festival of the Arts Local Artist Spotlight; American Stage Theater Local Spotlight Artist; SHINE 2021 and 2025 selected artist',fw:[{name:'jenipherchandley.com',url:'https://www.jenipherchandley.com'}],sbio:'She started painting outside Sake Bomb on Central Avenue around 2011. She is a survivor — of economic hardship, of domestic abuse — and she paints that survival into everything. Jenipher Chandley was born and raised in St. Petersburg, Florida, where she has raised her four children and recently became a grandmother. She earned degrees in interior design and graphic design from IADT Tampa. She works predominantly in oils, creating representational figurative artwork infused with surrealism and symbolism, and her paintings explore the relationship between nature and the human condition through themes of strength, beauty, loss, pain, and resilience.\n\nThe female form is a recurring motif in her work, representing both vulnerability and power. Through undulating gestures she conveys movement and fluidity, capturing the ebb and flow of emotions tied to trauma. Peach-complexioned girls dream of summer; dark-haired beauties float in rose-hip waters. She returns often to the divine feminine, to inner power and resilience. Her overarching theme is the story of love — the journey to find it, revel in it, cherish and protect it, and the pain of losing it. She brings her emotions and the entirety of her subconscious to the work, and through vulnerability uncovers the strength to forge healing. As she puts it, "Humans are complex and ever-changing, so our art should be as well."\n\nHer imprint on the St. Petersburg creative community started in 2011, when she gathered like-minded artists around her and began painting in front of what was then Sake Bomb on Central Avenue in downtown St. Petersburg. Encouraged by then-owner Tai Swank, she built those humble beginnings of home-made flyers and neighboring dive bars into an ongoing artist meet-up and exhibition space that continues today. The venue has since changed owners and become Bar@548, where current owner Michael Kellem also supports the work. Jenipher is lead curator at Bar@548, hosts a free weekly Wednesday Night art meetup for local artists of all skill levels — taking no commission — and curates monthly shows that showcase the work of emerging artists. She has co-curated and exhibited in hundreds of shows throughout the Tampa Bay area. Her studio is at the ArtsXchange, Studio Gallery 104, located at 515 22nd Street South, St. Petersburg, FL 33712.\n\nFor more than twelve years she has worked with numerous nonprofit organizations and community initiatives. She received a 2023 Emerging Artist Grant from Creative Pinellas — a grant that supports artists on the upward trajectory of their careers, not those at the very beginning. As part of that grant she has kept a blog, worked with an artist mentor, and developed a new body of work for exhibition. She was selected as the Local Artist Spotlight by the Gasparilla Festival of the Arts in Tampa, and featured as the local spotlight artist with the American Stage Theater in St. Petersburg, where an exhibition of her work was installed in the lobby running simultaneously with the play Crimes of the Heart through February 5th. She also had an upcoming exhibition at The Fairgrounds. She is a member of The Portrait Society of America and the Warehouse Arts District Association.\n\nHer paintings have appeared on the walls of local boutiques and galleries, and she sells work directly off the walls of Bar@548. Beyond canvas, she works through fashion, choreography, poetry, and public arts installations to amplify the healing power of art. Recent painted works documented through Creative Pinellas include "Beyond the Exhibition: The Artistic Journey Continues," "Navigating the Artistic Path: A Guide for Emerging Artists," "Embracing Growth: My Journey as an Emerging Artist in 2023," "A Visual Artist\'s Journey of Inspiration," "Tranquil Reflection: Finding Inner Peace through the Thorns of Life," "Resilience Blooms: Surrendering to Growth," "Lotus Unfolding: Embracing Healing and Growth," "The Hollowed Resilience: A Woman Shielded from the Storms of the Past," "Journey to Serenity: Embracing Home amidst Life\'s Trials," "Unfounded Terror: The Petrification of Unhealed Trauma," "Transcendence: A Deep Connection Through Art," "Ophidian Epiphanies: A Journey of Healing and Renewal," "The Transformative Power of Art," and "Difficulty Creates Inspiration." Other titled pieces include "fire lily" and "I paint My heart."\n\nShe was profiled by Robin O\'Dell in The Artisan Magazine on June 5, 2025, in a piece titled "The Original — Jenipher Chandley." She describes herself not as a victim but a survivor, and she works to instill that mentality in others. Her process incorporates "the desire to heal, to be happy and to find inner peace, the common thread in all humanity." Her advice to emerging artists is to not be afraid to grow out of their comfort zone, reflecting that transitions are natural and needed.\n\nFor SHINE 2021 she painted her first festival mural. For SHINE Origins 2025 she returned with "Stronger Together" at 31st Street and 7th Avenue South, assisted by her daughter Becka. SHINE is the curated, invitation-only mural festival produced by the St. Petersburg Arts Alliance.\n\nWebsite: jenipherchandley.com. Instagram: @jenipherchandley. Facebook: jenipher.chandley.',fn:['Started painting outside Sake Bomb on Central Ave','Runs the Creative Clay mural program','Multiple SHINE murals across the city'],src:['https://www.jenipherchandley.com/about-1','https://www.jenipherchandley.com/statement','https://creativepinellas.org/artist/jenipher-chandley/','https://theartisanmagazine.com/the-original-jenipher-chandley/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2021_SHINE_Mural_Jenipher_Chandley']},
  {id:11,a:'John Vitale',t:'Power to the Patients',loc:'790 Central Ave',bldg:'',lat:27.771652,lng:-82.644567,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'vitalebros',bio:'Their first mural came from a sketch on a paper napkin, handed to a restaurant owner. That was 1992.\n\nJohnny Vitale and his brother Paul started Vitale Bros in Tampa Bay hand-lettering signs and painting storefront windows. Their parents are artists. So were their grandparents. No formal training — just family tradition and thirty-plus years of practice. Their late aunt Nancy, who had Down syndrome and attended Parc Center for Disabilities, turned out to be an artist too, discovered in her final decade.\n\nIn the late 1990s they opened a warehouse studio that hosted group art, music, and fashion shows that helped birth St. Pete\'s emerging art scene. They collaborated on set design for Harmony Korine\'s Spring Breakers. They have painted for the Tampa Bay Rays, the Buccaneers, L\'Oreal, and dozens of local businesses. They call themselves the "mural police" — restoring damaged walls around the city for free.\n\nIn 2023, Johnny launched Overspray, a street art shop and graffiti school now based in Gulfport, teaching tagging, block letters, Wildstyle, and advanced mural technique to classes of up to twenty students. After Hurricane Milton, they painted free signs for businesses that lost theirs.\n\nThey have painted SHINE every year since its inception — "The Cool Kids" in 2015, "Bait and Switch" in 2019, and multiple walls since. For SHINE Origins 2025, Johnny painted two solo murals: one on the 600 block where it all began, and another at The Factory on Fairfield Ave South with Sionna "Sio" Macleish.',desc:'A propaganda-style mural in the tradition of Shepard Fairey\'s OBEY aesthetic. A raised fist gripping a caduceus medical symbol is centered in a bold red circle, with "Power to the Patients" in block letters below. The top reads "Prices Are Now a Patient\'s Right" and the bottom banner declares "Demand Them!!!" in large cream type on red. The palette is limited to navy blue, red, cream, and gray — evoking classic activist poster art. "PowerToThePatients.org" runs along the bottom, with Vitale Bros logos flanking it.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/john-vitale-600.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'Part of Shepard Fairey\'s national Power to the Patients campaign to combat hidden healthcare pricing and raise awareness that prices are a patient\'s right prior to receiving care.',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE festival artist every year since inception; set design for Spring Breakers (Harmony Korine)',fw:[{name:'vitalebrothers.com',url:'https://vitalebrothers.com'}],sbio:'Their first mural came from a sketch on a paper napkin, handed to a restaurant owner. That was 1992.\n\nJohnny Vitale and his brother Paul founded Vitale Bros in 1992 in Tampa Bay, starting with hand-lettered signs and painted storefront windows. Youngest brother Joey later joined. Their parents are artists — the family artistic tradition goes back to their grandparents. No formal art education has been documented; the craft appears entirely self-taught and family-transmitted. Their late aunt Nancy had Down syndrome and attended Parc Center for Disabilities, where staff discovered her artistic talent in her final decade of life.\n\nIn the late 1990s and early 2000s, they opened a warehouse studio that hosted legendary group art, music and fashion shows credited with helping birth St. Pete\'s emerging art scene. They collaborated in set design for Harmony Korine\'s Spring Breakers (filmed in the St. Pete area). Their style blends pop art, graffiti, and traditional mural techniques — fine art meets pop art sensibility, incorporating local culture, historical elements, and visual stories reflecting community identity.\n\nClients include the Tampa Bay Rays, Tampa Bay Buccaneers, L\'Oreal / SalonCentric (national locations), Anchor Skate Supply, and Great Bay Distributors, plus numerous local businesses and private residences. Notable murals include the Marilyn Monroe on the side of the historic Playhouse Theater on Central Ave, a Virgin Mary at St. Pete Catholic High School, Power to the Patients giant fist at the Morean Arts Center, and the Nueva Cantina restaurant mural in Tampa. They describe themselves as the mural police — restoring damaged murals around the city for free.\n\nThe current Vitale Bros team includes Johnny, Paul, Nick and Jason Kekllas, Bruce Statton, and Jake Gerow. They have also collaborated under the name Up and Over with Tes One, and painted behind the State Theatre with Tes One and Palehorse.\n\nIn July 2023, Johnny launched Overspray — a retail street art shop and graffiti school, initially on the Pinellas Trail, relocated to 5013 20th Ave S. in Gulfport. Overspray teaches graffiti, tagging, block and bubble letters, Wildstyle, and advanced mural classes to groups of up to 20 students. The five-artist teaching team includes John Vitale, Nicole Salgar, Hinson Thehealer, Digital (LA), and Jujmo. After Hurricane Milton, they painted free signs for businesses that lost theirs. They host events like Hot Walls during SHINE.\n\nSHINE participation: The Cool Kids (2015, 2063 Central Ave — faces of the artists\' children), Bait and Switch (2019 — red snapper and sea turtle), SHINE 2022, SHINE 2024 (mural at Anchor Skate Supply with pro skateboarder Kris Markovich, inspired by Rays City Connect uniforms; limited-edition skateboard L\'Orange). For SHINE Origins 2025, Johnny painted two solo walls: 600 1st Ave N (the 600 block where SHINE began) and The Factory at 2606 Fairfield Ave S (collaboration with Sionna Sio Macleish, celebrating St. Pete history and landmarks).\n\nStudio: 5013 20th Ave S., Gulfport, FL. Website: vitalebrothers.com. Instagram: @johnvitale (solo), @vitalebros (collective).',src:['https://ilovetheburg.com/vitale-bros-power-to-the-patients-st-pete-obey/','https://www.powertothepatients.org/murals-across-america','https://vitalebrothers.com/about/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2022_SHINE_Vitale_Bros','https://vitalebrothers.com/transforming-st-pete-the-artistic-impact-of-the-vitale-brothers/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/vitale-bros-and-shine-this-time-its-personal/','https://ilovetheburg.com/overspray-mural-art-gulfport/','https://thegabber.com/vitale-brothers-birds-and-bees-in-gulfport/','https://thegabber.com/overspray-art-space-paints-signs-for-businesses-post-milton/','https://www.fox13news.com/news/vitale-brothers-paint-the-town-with-murals','https://monochronicle.com/artist/vitale-brothers/','https://www.paradisenewsfl.com/artists-spotlight-nov-2025/']},
  {id:129,a:'John Vitale',t:'',loc:'601 1st Ave N',bldg:'The Sage',lat:27.771668,lng:-82.642127,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'vitalebros',bio:'Their first mural came from a sketch on a paper napkin, handed to a restaurant owner. That was 1992.\n\nJohnny Vitale and his brother Paul started Vitale Bros in Tampa Bay hand-lettering signs and painting storefront windows. Their parents are artists. So were their grandparents. No formal training — just family tradition and thirty-plus years of practice. Their late aunt Nancy, who had Down syndrome and attended Parc Center for Disabilities, turned out to be an artist too, discovered in her final decade.\n\nIn the late 1990s they opened a warehouse studio that hosted group art, music, and fashion shows that helped birth St. Pete\'s emerging art scene. They collaborated on set design for Harmony Korine\'s Spring Breakers. They have painted for the Tampa Bay Rays, the Buccaneers, L\'Oreal, and dozens of local businesses. They call themselves the "mural police" — restoring damaged walls around the city for free.\n\nIn 2023, Johnny launched Overspray, a street art shop and graffiti school now based in Gulfport, teaching tagging, block letters, Wildstyle, and advanced mural technique to classes of up to twenty students. After Hurricane Milton, they painted free signs for businesses that lost theirs.\n\nThey have painted SHINE every year since its inception — "The Cool Kids" in 2015, "Bait and Switch" in 2019, and multiple walls since. For SHINE Origins 2025, Johnny painted two solo murals: one on the 600 block where it all began, and another at The Factory on Fairfield Ave South with his daughter Sionna.',desc:'Orange blossoms and ripe oranges painted on the vertical columns and edges of the sage-green Sage building. White flowers with yellow centers sit among deep green leaves and bright blue accents, with whole and halved oranges tucked between them. The design wraps the structural columns of the building, turning the architecture itself into a tropical citrus garden. The style is clean and illustrative with bold outlines, giving the building a fresh Florida feel that nods to the state\'s citrus heritage. Adjacent to Marina Capdevila\'s 2022 SHINE mural on the front face of the same building.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/john-vitale-601.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'https://cdn.muralquest.app/audio/129.mp3',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE festival artist every year since inception; set design for Spring Breakers (Harmony Korine)',fw:[{name:'vitalebrothers.com',url:'https://vitalebrothers.com'}],sbio:'Their first mural came from a sketch on a paper napkin, handed to a restaurant owner. That was 1992.\n\nJohnny Vitale and his brother Paul founded Vitale Bros in 1992 in Tampa Bay, starting with hand-lettered signs and painted storefront windows. Youngest brother Joey later joined. Their parents are artists — the family artistic tradition goes back to their grandparents. No formal art education has been documented; the craft appears entirely self-taught and family-transmitted. Their late aunt Nancy had Down syndrome and attended Parc Center for Disabilities, where staff discovered her artistic talent in her final decade of life.\n\nIn the late 1990s and early 2000s, they opened a warehouse studio that hosted legendary group art, music and fashion shows credited with helping birth St. Pete\'s emerging art scene. They collaborated in set design for Harmony Korine\'s Spring Breakers (filmed in the St. Pete area). Their style blends pop art, graffiti, and traditional mural techniques — fine art meets pop art sensibility, incorporating local culture, historical elements, and visual stories reflecting community identity.\n\nClients include the Tampa Bay Rays, Tampa Bay Buccaneers, L\'Oreal / SalonCentric (national locations), Anchor Skate Supply, and Great Bay Distributors, plus numerous local businesses and private residences. Notable murals include the Marilyn Monroe on the side of the historic Playhouse Theater on Central Ave, a Virgin Mary at St. Pete Catholic High School, Power to the Patients giant fist at the Morean Arts Center, and the Nueva Cantina restaurant mural in Tampa. They describe themselves as the mural police — restoring damaged murals around the city for free.\n\nThe current Vitale Bros team includes Johnny, Paul, Nick and Jason Kekllas, Bruce Statton, and Jake Gerow. They have also collaborated under the name Up and Over with Tes One, and painted behind the State Theatre with Tes One and Palehorse.\n\nIn July 2023, Johnny launched Overspray — a retail street art shop and graffiti school, initially on the Pinellas Trail, relocated to 5013 20th Ave S. in Gulfport. Overspray teaches graffiti, tagging, block and bubble letters, Wildstyle, and advanced mural classes to groups of up to 20 students. The five-artist teaching team includes John Vitale, Nicole Salgar, Hinson Thehealer, Digital (LA), and Jujmo. After Hurricane Milton, they painted free signs for businesses that lost theirs. They host events like Hot Walls during SHINE.\n\nSHINE participation: The Cool Kids (2015, 2063 Central Ave — faces of the artists\' children), Bait and Switch (2019 — red snapper and sea turtle), SHINE 2022, SHINE 2024 (mural at Anchor Skate Supply with pro skateboarder Kris Markovich, inspired by Rays City Connect uniforms; limited-edition skateboard L\'Orange). For SHINE Origins 2025, Johnny painted two solo walls: 600 1st Ave N (the 600 block where SHINE began) and The Factory at 2606 Fairfield Ave S (collaboration with Sionna Sio Macleish, celebrating St. Pete history and landmarks).\n\nStudio: 5013 20th Ave S., Gulfport, FL. Website: vitalebrothers.com. Instagram: @johnvitale (solo), @vitalebros (collective).',fn:['Vitale Bros — napkin sketch started it all','Painted for the Tampa Bay Rays','SHINE every year since inception'],src:['https://ilovetheburg.com/st-pete-shine-origins/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/vitale-bros-and-shine-this-time-its-personal/','https://vitalebrothers.com/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine-2025','https://vitalebrothers.com/about/','https://vitalebrothers.com/transforming-st-pete-the-artistic-impact-of-the-vitale-brothers/','https://ilovetheburg.com/overspray-mural-art-gulfport/','https://thegabber.com/vitale-brothers-birds-and-bees-in-gulfport/','https://thegabber.com/overspray-art-space-paints-signs-for-businesses-post-milton/','https://www.fox13news.com/news/vitale-brothers-paint-the-town-with-murals','https://monochronicle.com/artist/vitale-brothers/','https://www.paradisenewsfl.com/artists-spotlight-nov-2025/']},
  {id:12,a:'John Vitale & Sionna MacLeish',t:'',loc:'2606 Fairfield Ave S',bldg:'The Factory',lat:27.763181,lng:-82.668868,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'vitalebros',bio:'Their first mural came from a sketch on a paper napkin, handed to a restaurant owner. That was 1992.\n\nJohnny Vitale and his brother Paul started Vitale Bros in Tampa Bay hand-lettering signs and painting storefront windows. Their parents are artists. So were their grandparents. No formal training — just family tradition and thirty-plus years of practice. Their late aunt Nancy, who had Down syndrome and attended Parc Center for Disabilities, turned out to be an artist too, discovered in her final decade.\n\nIn the late 1990s they opened a warehouse studio that hosted group art, music, and fashion shows that helped birth St. Pete\'s emerging art scene. They collaborated on set design for Harmony Korine\'s Spring Breakers. They have painted for the Tampa Bay Rays, the Buccaneers, L\'Oreal, and dozens of local businesses. They call themselves the "mural police" — restoring damaged walls around the city for free.\n\nIn 2023, Johnny launched Overspray, a street art shop and graffiti school now based in Gulfport, teaching tagging, block letters, Wildstyle, and advanced mural technique to classes of up to twenty students. After Hurricane Milton, they painted free signs for businesses that lost theirs.\n\nThey have painted SHINE every year since its inception — "The Cool Kids" in 2015, "Bait and Switch" in 2019, and multiple walls since. For SHINE Origins 2025, Johnny painted two solo murals: one on the 600 block where it all began, and another at The Factory on Fairfield Ave South with Sionna "Sio" Macleish.',desc:'A multi-panel vintage Florida postcard mural inside The Factory. The left panel features a glamorous 1950s-style pin-up woman with black hair and a pink hibiscus, turquoise jewelry, and a leopard-print top, set against the Thunderbird motel sign framed by tropical hibiscus flowers. Below reads "St. Petersburg, Florida" in retro blue script on golden yellow. The right panel, painted by Sionna MacLeish, shows a roseate spoonbill in soft pinks against a pale blue polka-dot background with a bold orange sun. The whole composition evokes mid-century Florida tourism nostalgia.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/john-vitale-factory.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE festival artist every year since inception; set design for Spring Breakers (Harmony Korine)',fw:[{name:'vitalebrothers.com',url:'https://vitalebrothers.com'}],sbio:'Their first mural came from a sketch on a paper napkin, handed to a restaurant owner. That was 1992.\n\nJohnny Vitale and his brother Paul founded Vitale Bros in 1992 in Tampa Bay, starting with hand-lettered signs and painted storefront windows. Youngest brother Joey later joined. Their parents are artists — the family artistic tradition goes back to their grandparents. No formal art education has been documented; the craft appears entirely self-taught and family-transmitted. Their late aunt Nancy had Down syndrome and attended Parc Center for Disabilities, where staff discovered her artistic talent in her final decade of life.\n\nIn the late 1990s and early 2000s, they opened a warehouse studio that hosted legendary group art, music and fashion shows credited with helping birth St. Pete\'s emerging art scene. They collaborated in set design for Harmony Korine\'s Spring Breakers (filmed in the St. Pete area). Their style blends pop art, graffiti, and traditional mural techniques — fine art meets pop art sensibility, incorporating local culture, historical elements, and visual stories reflecting community identity.\n\nClients include the Tampa Bay Rays, Tampa Bay Buccaneers, L\'Oreal / SalonCentric (national locations), Anchor Skate Supply, and Great Bay Distributors, plus numerous local businesses and private residences. Notable murals include the Marilyn Monroe on the side of the historic Playhouse Theater on Central Ave, a Virgin Mary at St. Pete Catholic High School, Power to the Patients giant fist at the Morean Arts Center, and the Nueva Cantina restaurant mural in Tampa. They describe themselves as the mural police — restoring damaged murals around the city for free.\n\nThe current Vitale Bros team includes Johnny, Paul, Nick and Jason Kekllas, Bruce Statton, and Jake Gerow. They have also collaborated under the name Up and Over with Tes One, and painted behind the State Theatre with Tes One and Palehorse.\n\nIn July 2023, Johnny launched Overspray — a retail street art shop and graffiti school, initially on the Pinellas Trail, relocated to 5013 20th Ave S. in Gulfport. Overspray teaches graffiti, tagging, block and bubble letters, Wildstyle, and advanced mural classes to groups of up to 20 students. The five-artist teaching team includes John Vitale, Nicole Salgar, Hinson Thehealer, Digital (LA), and Jujmo. After Hurricane Milton, they painted free signs for businesses that lost theirs. They host events like Hot Walls during SHINE.\n\nSHINE participation: The Cool Kids (2015, 2063 Central Ave — faces of the artists\' children), Bait and Switch (2019 — red snapper and sea turtle), SHINE 2022, SHINE 2024 (mural at Anchor Skate Supply with pro skateboarder Kris Markovich, inspired by Rays City Connect uniforms; limited-edition skateboard L\'Orange). For SHINE Origins 2025, Johnny painted two solo walls: 600 1st Ave N (the 600 block where SHINE began) and The Factory at 2606 Fairfield Ave S (collaboration with Sionna Sio Macleish, celebrating St. Pete history and landmarks).\n\nStudio: 5013 20th Ave S., Gulfport, FL. Website: vitalebrothers.com. Instagram: @johnvitale (solo), @vitalebros (collective).',fn:['Vitale Bros — painting since 1992','Set design for Spring Breakers film','Collaboration with Sionna \'Sio\' Macleish for SHINE 2025'],src:['https://ilovetheburg.com/st-pete-shine-origins/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/vitale-bros-and-shine-this-time-its-personal/','https://vitalebrothers.com/','https://vitalebrothers.com/about/','https://vitalebrothers.com/transforming-st-pete-the-artistic-impact-of-the-vitale-brothers/','https://ilovetheburg.com/overspray-mural-art-gulfport/','https://thegabber.com/vitale-brothers-birds-and-bees-in-gulfport/','https://thegabber.com/overspray-art-space-paints-signs-for-businesses-post-milton/','https://www.fox13news.com/news/vitale-brothers-paint-the-town-with-murals','https://monochronicle.com/artist/vitale-brothers/','https://www.paradisenewsfl.com/artists-spotlight-nov-2025/']},
  {id:13,a:'Karel Garcia',t:'Freedom',loc:'31st St & 7th Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.76287,lng:-82.674461,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'karelgarcia.fineartist',bio:'His SHINE 2025 mural was his first public art installation in America. He trained as an architect in Havana.\n\nKarel Garcia was born December 27, 1990 in Vedado, Cuba. He earned an architecture degree in Havana in 2017, working as an architect while exhibiting art privately and participating in projects at the Habana Espacio Creativo Center. His focus has always been the human figure — sexuality, the bond between humans and nature, consciousness.\n\nNow based in St. Petersburg, he holds a studio in the Warehouse Arts District through the Green Book of Tampa Bay artist residency. His current series, "Human," explores themes of human experience through detailed drawing and sculpture. His mural work uses bold color — oranges, blues, greens — with stylized graphic linework.\n\nFor SHINE Origins 2025, he painted at 31st Street and 7th Avenue South. The themes were personal: migration, freedom, family, social justice — reflecting his own journey from Cuba.',desc:'A wide mural on a cinder block wall depicting bold, swirling ocean waves in deep blues and greens against a golden-orange sky. Three carved wooden totem-like hands rise from the waves, and small red paper boats ride the cresting swells — symbols of migration and hope. The flowing, graphic linework gives the waves a stylized, rhythmic energy. Signed "Karel Garcia" in the lower right.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/karel-garcia.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'Garcia\'s mural explores themes of migration, freedom, and social justice, reflecting his own journey from Cuba to America. The paper boats and reaching hands symbolize the search for freedom and family.',maw:'',aaw:'Green Book of Tampa Bay artist residency; SHINE Origins 2025 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'His SHINE 2025 mural was his first public art installation in America. He trained as an architect in Havana.\n\nKarel Garcia (sometimes listed as Karel Garcia de Leon) was born December 27, 1990 in Vedado, Cuba. He earned an architecture degree in Havana, completed in 2017, and worked as an architect while exhibiting art privately. He participated in projects at the Habana Espacio Creativo Center in Havana. His focus is the human figure — sexuality, the bond between humans and nature and environment, consciousness.\n\nNow based in St. Petersburg, Florida, he holds a studio in the Warehouse Arts District through the Green Book of Tampa Bay artist residency. His notable series "Human" explores themes of human experience and consciousness through detailed drawing and sculpture. His mural work uses bold color — oranges, blues, greens — with stylized graphic linework.\n\nFor SHINE Origins 2025, he painted at 31st Street and 7th Avenue South alongside Amy Ilic-Volpe, Elizabeth Barenis, Zulu Painter, Brain The Genius, and others. The themes were personal: migration, freedom, family, social justice — reflecting his own journey from Cuba. He is listed on Creative Pinellas. Instagram: @karelgarcia.fineartist.',src:['https://creativepinellas.org/artist/karel-garcia/','https://floridapaints.com/blog/shine-mural-festival-st-petersburg-2025/','https://ilovetheburg.com/st-pete-shine-origins/','https://www.tampabay.com/life-culture/entertainment/2025/12/04/shine-mural-festival-st-petersburg-florida-arts-hurricane/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/for-year-11-shine-mural-festival-brings-it-back-home/','https://www.axios.com/local/tampa-bay/2025/10/16/st-pete-shine-mural-festival-2025']},
  {id:165,a:'Lyko Creative',t:'',loc:'3030 7th Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.762862,lng:-82.67437,y:2025,cat:'commercial',ig:'lyko.creations',bio:'Florida pulled five St. Pete street murals in summer 2025 under SB 1662 — Progressive Pride, Black History Matters, Fluid Structures, Common Ground, Crossway Crux. The Love Thy Neighbor Project responded by painting free murals on private property, where the state can\'t reach. This is one of them.\n\nZac "Lyko" Shaffer co-led the project in August 2025 with Jazzy Erickson, alongside founder Michelle Sasha of ArtFluent Creatives. His piece is a Florida nature scene — a mockingbird, magnolia blossoms, a golden orange, and zebra longwing butterflies on a sky-blue background. Quieter than the murals the state removed, but the gesture is the same.\n\nLyko is a St. Petersburg-based multi-media artist working under the brand Lyko Creations.',desc:'A panoramic Florida nature scene on a blue cinder block wall. On the left, a gray-and-white mockingbird perches on a dark branch. At center, green magnolia leaves and a white magnolia blossom frame a large golden-orange citrus fruit. On the right, two zebra longwing butterflies with black and white striped wings flutter near the foliage. The palette is naturalistic — sky blue background, rich greens, and warm gold — celebrating Florida\'s native wildlife and flora.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/lyko-creative.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'Zac "Lyko" Shaffer — Lyko Creations — is a St. Petersburg-based multi-media artist.\n\nIn August 2025 he co-led the Love Thy Neighbor Project with Jazzy Erickson, a community mural initiative founded by Michelle Sasha of ArtFluent Creatives. The project painted free inclusivity murals on private properties in St. Petersburg as a response to Florida\'s removal of public street murals — five St. Pete murals (Progressive Pride, Black History Matters, Fluid Structures, Common Ground, Crossway Crux) were removed by FDOT under SB 1662 (signed by Gov. DeSantis, June 2025).\n\nThis mural was painted in 2025 — a Florida nature scene with a mockingbird, magnolia blossoms, a golden orange, and zebra longwing butterflies. Not an official SHINE Origins 2025 piece.\n\nInstagram: @lyko.creations. Website: lykocreations.com.',src:['https://stpetecatalyst.com/street-mural-removals-foster-activism-solidarity/','https://watermarkonline.com/2025/08/29/love-thy-neighbor-project-offers-tampa-bay-free-lgbtq-murals-after-fdot-ban/','https://spectrumlocalnews.com/tampa/news/2025/08/30/florida-street-murals','https://floridapolitics.com/archives/754078-st-petes-progressive-pride-mural-the-last-of-5-street-artworks-removed-by-state/','https://www.tampabay.com/news/st-petersburg/2025/09/02/st-petersburg-pride-street-mural-removed-florida/','https://www.axios.com/local/tampa-bay/2025/08/22/st-petersburg-to-comply-with-state-order-to-remove-pride-black-history-street-art','https://lykocreations.com','https://www.instagram.com/lyko.creations/','https://artfluentcreatives.com']},
  {id:181,a:'Michael Vasquez',t:'Sandcastles with Father – Spa Beach',loc:'700 2nd Ave NE',bldg:'St. Pete Pier',lat:27.773652,lng:-82.62502,y:2025,cat:'commercial',ig:'michaelvasquez_',bio:'His work hangs in the Smithsonian. Swizz Beats and Michael B. Jordan collect him.\n\nMichael Vasquez earned his BFA cum laude from the New World School of the Arts in Miami in 2005. His figurative paintings explore identity in marginalized neighborhoods — transforming personal photographs into dramatic compositions where clothing, hand signs, tattoos, and neighborhood landmarks establish who someone is and where they come from.\n\nHe was included in "Portraiture Now: Staging the Self" at the Smithsonian National Portrait Gallery (2014-15) and "The Culture: Hip Hop and Contemporary Art in the 21st Century" at the Baltimore Museum of Art (2023). His work is in the Perez Art Museum Miami, the Microsoft Art Collection, and the Rubell Museum. He has shown at the Saatchi Gallery in London, Wynwood Walls, and the Kemper Museum of Contemporary Art.\n\nHe is a three-time SHINE artist — 2015, 2023, and 2025. For the Pier\'s 5th anniversary in 2025, he painted "Sandcastles with Father – Spa Beach," a deeply personal mural that revisits his recurring theme of reimagined fatherhood. Growing up without a father figure, Vasquez often explores intergenerational relationships and the longing for paternal connection. The sandcastle in the mural takes the form of the iconic inverted pyramid pier, drawing past and present into quiet dialogue through imagination and familial connection.',desc:'A 10-by-20-foot photorealistic scene on a wood panel wall. A father in a lavender button-down shirt and blue jeans sits on Spa Beach beside a young boy in a yellow t-shirt and blue shorts. Together they build a sandcastle that takes the unmistakable form of the inverted pyramid pier — William B. Harvard Sr.\'s iconic structure that stood from 1973 to 2015. The sandcastle is rendered with architectural precision, complete with arched entrance and angular upper floors. In the background, the current St. Pete Pier rises on the horizon over the water. Beach umbrellas and beachgoers dot the left side. Both figures smile with genuine joy. The sky is bright blue with cumulus clouds. Signed @MICHAELVASQUEZ in the lower right corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/michael-vasquez-sandcastles.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg/Miami, FL',aud:'',insp:'Honors the legacy of the St. Petersburg Pier by depicting a father and son building a sandcastle in the shape of the iconic inverted pyramid. Revisits the artist\'s 2015 SHINE mural \'Fishing with Father\' — a deeply personal exploration of reimagined fatherhood and paternal connection.',maw:'',aaw:'Smithsonian National Portrait Gallery (2014-15, 2019); Perez Art Museum Miami collection; Microsoft Art Collection; Rubell Museum; SHINE 2015, 2023, and 2025',fw:[{name:'michaelvasquez.info',url:'http://www.michaelvasquez.info'}],sbio:'His work hangs in the Smithsonian. Swizz Beats and Michael B. Jordan collect him.\n\nMichael Vasquez earned his BFA cum laude from the New World School of the Arts in Miami in 2005. His figurative paintings explore identity in marginalized neighborhoods — transforming personal photographs into dramatic compositions where clothing, hand signs, tattoos, and neighborhood landmarks establish who someone is and where they come from.\n\nHe was included in "Portraiture Now: Staging the Self" at the Smithsonian National Portrait Gallery (2014-15) and "The Culture: Hip Hop and Contemporary Art in the 21st Century" at the Baltimore Museum of Art (2023). His work is in the Perez Art Museum Miami, the Microsoft Art Collection, and the Rubell Museum. He has shown at the Saatchi Gallery in London, Wynwood Walls, and the Kemper Museum of Contemporary Art.\n\nHe is a three-time SHINE artist — 2015, 2023, and 2025. His 2015 SHINE mural "Fishing with Father" featured the iconic inverted pyramid pier and explored themes of fatherhood and belonging. His 2023 SHINE mural "The Mangrove Refuge" depicted a displaced Lao family finding refuge at Gandy Beach circa 1980. For the St. Pete Pier\'s 5th anniversary in 2025, he was invited by the City of St. Petersburg and No Good Deeds Art to create "Sandcastles with Father – Spa Beach" — a 10-by-20-foot mural on exterior wood panel wall using latex and spray paint.\n\nGrowing up without a father figure, Vasquez often explores intergenerational relationships and the longing for paternal connection in his work. The 2025 mural directly recalls "Fishing with Father," revisiting and expanding those themes. The sandcastle takes the form of William B. Harvard Sr.\'s inverted pyramid pier, which stood from 1973 to 2015 and continues to hold a lasting place in the city\'s collective memory. The current iteration of the Pier rises on the horizon in the background, drawing past and present into quiet dialogue.\n\nInstagram: @michaelvasquez_. Website: michaelvasquez.info.',fn:['Sandcastle is the iconic inverted pyramid pier (1973-2015)','Recalls his 2015 SHINE mural \'Fishing with Father\' — same theme of fatherhood','Grew up without a father — explores longing for paternal connection']},
  {id:197,a:'Quinn Cale',t:'Pixel Reacher',loc:'2375 66th St N',bldg:'Tyrone Middle School',lat:27.79366,lng:-82.72751,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'quinnsessential',bio:'She taught kids digital art on iPads at Apple. Now she\'s painted a CRT-monitor-headed teacher on the side of Tyrone Middle School\'s Center for Innovation and Digital Learning.\n\nQuinn Cale is from Middletown, Delaware, now based in Tampa. She is self-taught — no formal art degree. She worked sales at Apple before pivoting to Apple Creative around 2020. The pivot stuck.\n\nHer work runs on late-90s and early-2000s nostalgia: arcade cabinets, pixelated cursors, TV-headed figures, retro chrome. She uses Adobe Aero to overlay augmented reality animations on her paintings.\n\nSHINE Origins 2025 brought her to Tyrone Middle School for her second SHINE wall — her 2024 piece at Main Grove had an AR cat-and-mouse animation.',desc:'A large mural on the side of Tyrone Middle School\'s Center for Innovation and Digital Learning building. The figure\'s head is a vintage CRT computer monitor with a glowing brain on the screen, surrounded by small icons connected by lines like a neural map. The figure wears a lime-green polo shirt; both hands are articulated robot hands, one raised palm-out, the other gesturing open. A pixelated white cursor arrow floats to the left. Yellow, light green, dark green, and teal pixelated squares scatter across the cream-colored wall like screen artifacts. Above the figure: the green-and-yellow Tyrone Eagle school mascot logo and the building\'s name "CENTER FOR INNOVATION AND DIGITAL LEARNING" in silver dimensional letters. Signed lower right with a stylized "C" and "2025."',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/quinn-cale-tyrone-middle.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2024 selected artist; SHINE Origins 2025 selected artist (Tyrone Middle School)',fw:[{name:'quinncale.com',url:'https://quinncale.com'}],sbio:'A figure with a CRT monitor for a head, brain glowing inside the screen, robot hands gesturing — Quinn Cale painted a literal computer-headed teacher for a building literally called the Center for Innovation and Digital Learning. Signed lower right with her "C" mark and 2025.\n\nQuinn Cale is from Middletown, Delaware, now based in Tampa, Florida. She is self-taught with no formal art degree, having started painting on scrap wood as a teenager. She worked in sales at Apple, then became an Apple Creative around 2020, teaching children digital art on iPads.\n\nHer style channels late-90s and early-2000s nostalgia — arcade machines, tropical motifs, instruments, TV-headed figures — in bold surreal color compositions using surrealism and color theory. She pioneers augmented reality overlays on her paintings using Adobe Aero (no-code platform): viewers scan with a phone or iPad and the painting animates.\n\nHer SHINE festival work began with SHINE 2024, where she painted at 225 2nd Ave North (Main Grove) in St. Petersburg featuring an animated cat and mouse with AR. For SHINE Origins 2025, she painted this wall at Tyrone Middle School (2375 66th St N) — the Center for Innovation and Digital Learning building. Per quinncale.com, additional documented festivals include Tampa Walls (Tampa, 2022), Guava Paint Jam (Tampa, 2025), Hot Walls (St. Petersburg, 2025), and Fish Food on the Tampa Trail (Tampa, 2025). She has exhibited at MOSI (Tampa) and the Straz Center "First Annual Black Art" show with "Fire Fist Mario 8-Bit." She was an inaugural Remote Studio Artist at the Gasparilla Festival of the Arts. Website: quinncale.com. Instagram: @quinnsessential.',src:['https://quinncale.com','https://ilovetheburg.com/st-pete-shine-origins/']},
  {id:128,a:'Raisa Nosova',t:'Woman of Light',loc:'2606 Fairfield Ave S',bldg:'Museum of Motherhood (The Factory)',lat:27.763822,lng:-82.668158,y:2025,cat:'commercial',ig:'raisanosova',bio:'A woman crouches at the doorway. Golden light bursts out behind her in stained-glass blocks. She painted it live over five days for the Museum of Motherhood and titled it "Woman of Light."\n\nRaisa Nosova is a Russian-born multidisciplinary artist (painting, murals, blown-glass sculpture, drawing, animation, performance) based in Hoboken, NJ. She earned her MFA from NYU Steinhardt and a BFA from the Fashion Institute of Technology, studied blown glass at Urban Glass in Brooklyn, and trained at Vesalius College in Brussels and the Art Students League of New York.\n\nBecoming a mother during her MFA reshaped her practice toward motherhood, trauma, resilience, displacement, and the invisible labor of caregiving. She is best known in the gallery world for blown-glass breast sculptures exploring postpartum experience, and in the mural world for "Uprooted" — an anti-war piece on a Ukrainian-owned gas station in Jersey City, painted after Russia bombed Kyiv while she was two months pregnant with close family in Ukraine.\n\n"Raisa Nosova Paints the Town" (April 14-18, 2025) brought her to St. Petersburg to paint this wall at The Factory\'s Museum of Motherhood. The collaboration was brokered by OXH Gallery (Odeta Xheka, Kress Building, Ybor City) for the MoM Art Auction and the museum\'s "We Build Tampa Bay" initiative. She has shown at Theodore Art Gallery (Manhattan), La Galerie Emeric Hahn (Paris), and Mana Contemporary (Jersey City), and held residencies at the Berlin Art Institute and the Ramona Residency in Houston (the first artist-mother residency in Texas).',desc:'A dynamic, warm-toned mural on the facade of the Museum of Motherhood at The Factory. A female figure is depicted in a crouching or seated position, rendered in expressive brushwork in reds, golds, pinks, and deep magentas. Radiating golden light beams and architectural fragments burst outward from behind the figure, creating a sense of energy and movement. The composition has a stained-glass quality with bold color blocks and strong outlines. The "MoM" (Museum of Motherhood) text is visible in the upper left. The piece wraps around the building entrance, framing the doorway.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/raisa-nosova-museum-of-motherhood.jpeg',from:'Hoboken, NJ',aud:'',insp:'Painted for the Museum of Motherhood after Nosova met the founder and felt both the urge and responsibility to contribute to raising awareness for the museum, the vital institution in a society that so often feels isolating for mothers.',maw:'',aaw:'MFA, NYU Steinhardt; George T. Dorsch Art History Grant; Fannie Kipnes Memorial Award for Oil Painting; Brussels Capital Film Festival Award for Short Film; Ramona Residency, Houston (2025-2026)',fw:null,sbio:'Raisa Nosova is a Russian-born multidisciplinary artist working in painting, murals, blown glass sculpture, drawing, animation, and performance art. She earned her MFA in Studio Art from NYU Steinhardt and her BFA from the Fashion Institute of Technology, studied blown glass at Urban Glass in Brooklyn, and also attended Vesalius College in Brussels and the Art Students League of New York. Her time abroad in Brussels led to a string of international opportunities, including the George T. Dorsch Award for research in Cambodia, her first solo exhibition in Montclair, New Jersey, a commission painting for the OSMOS group in Paris, a solo exhibition at Galerie Emeric Hahn in Paris, and an assistant role to the director of CCNOA on a biannual event in Brussels. She describes her formal training as spanning classical figure painting and sculpture, abstract and conceptual art, glass blowing, apparel sewing, stage design, large-scale mural work, commercial photography, digital art, animation, film making, classical music, and ballet.\n\nHer studio practice took a turn into murals when a circle of friends opening Orso Coffee, the first boutique coffee shop in Sheepshead Bay, Brooklyn, invited her to create the artwork for the space. That commission grew her client base across the tri-state area and then internationally. Mural clients and projects include the Mayor of the Corbetta commune of Milan, Italy, where she painted a five-story exterior wall in the town center with full creative freedom; Lucas Portman\'s Fashion Baby XZY; William Etundi Jr.\'s You Are So Lucky project; the Concorde hotel near MoMA in New York; the city of Hoboken, New Jersey; and Bushwick, New York. Her best-known mural is "Uprooted," an anti-war piece on a Ukrainian-owned gas station in Jersey City that she painted after Russia bombed Kyiv while she was two months pregnant, with close family in Ukraine. In April 2025 she traveled to St. Petersburg, Florida, for "Raisa Nosova Paints the Town," a live mural painting event April 14-18, 2025 at The Factory at the Museum of Motherhood, 2606 Fairfield Ave. S, Building 7, made possible by a collaboration between OXH Gallery (founded by Odeta Xheka in the Kress Building, Ybor City, Tampa) and MoM for the MoM Art Auction. The project tied into MoM\'s "We Build Tampa Bay" initiative. She also painted a utility box mural for HobokenNJ.gov in 2020.\n\nThe shift to street and mural work crystallized during a 2017 residency at the Berlin Art Institute, where weeks spent absorbing the city\'s Holocaust memorials pushed her past the limits of her 4 x 4 foot portraits on canvas — work that had grown out of her research travels through Cambodia on the Aftermath of the Cambodian Genocide. She painted her first street art in the middle of the night in Berlin. She has also held a residency at the Ramona Residency in Houston, the first artist-mother residency in Texas. Becoming a mother during her MFA reshaped her practice toward motherhood, trauma, resilience, displacement, and the invisible labor of caregiving, and pushed her into new disciplines including blown glass sculpture and performance art. She is best known in the gallery world for blown glass breast sculptures exploring postpartum experience.\n\nGallery and exhibition history includes Theodore Art Gallery in Manhattan, WSE Gallery in New York, La Galerie Emeric Hahn (Interior Spaces) in Paris, Mana Contemporary in Jersey City, PRIME Gallery (the "Stripped" exhibition, covered by Jersey City Times in 2022), Gallery 14C in Jersey City (including the Art Fair 14C "Static Motion" coverage in 2024 and earlier Art Fair 14C appearances in 2021 and 2023), 81C in the US Virgin Islands (the "Equator Drift" exhibition of paintings covered by the St. Thomas Source and Daily News in 2026), Laurelwood Arboretum, Holocenter, Linus Galleries Los Angeles, and the Osmos-group. She was the cover artist for Washington Square Review issue 35 (2014) and issue 50 (2023).\n\nPress coverage spans the New York Times ("May Brings more than just Flowers," 2024), Jersey City Times (multiple features including "Muralist Raisa Nosova Raises a Distress call for Ukraine," 2022; "11 Must-See Exhibitors at Art Fair 14C," 2021; "At a Grand Train Station, Art Fair 14C Opens with a Head of Steam," 2023; "In Its Fifth Year, Art Fair 14C Keeps Throwing the Dice," 2023; "What to See at the Year\'s First Art Crawl," 2024), Street Art NYC, Create! Magazine (including Issue 4 and a 2023 feature on her multidisciplinary motherhood installation), the Create! Magazine Podcast ("Expansion in the Studio and Unapologetically Sharing"), Milked Magazine issue 4, Hoboken Girl, Hudson Reporter, Hudson TV, Yahoo News, Bklyner, UpMag, St Pete Catalyst, Glasstire (Texas Visual Art coverage of the Ramona Residency), Art Dealer Street ("Inside the Studio: Raisa Nosova"), Canvas Rebel, Sold Magazine, Fresh Paint Magazine (Issue 11, December 2015), Artist Portfolio Magazine, Galerie Project, Arts to Hearts Podcast, Friend of the Artist, the Holocenter, and Italian outlets including Milano Pavia TV, Settegiorni, Logos News, Cronaca Ossona, Notizie Ticino, Notizie MI Tomorrow.\n\nCritic Tris McCall, writing for Jersey City Times, called her "indispensable" for her "unflinching commitment to her concept" and her refusal to hide from the trauma, bodily harm, vulnerability, emotional peril, and life-giving fluidity of womanhood and motherhood. She has over 15 years of teaching experience, including adjunct professorships at NYU and FIT, and founded a classical drawing and painting program for youth. Her studio is at 335 Throop Avenue, Brooklyn, NY 11221. Website: raisanosova.com. Instagram: @raisanosova. Vimeo: vimeo.com/user22878265.',src:['https://www.raisanosova.com/about','https://mommuseum.org/2025/04/11/mural-at-mom-living-in-light-ongoing-art-auction-live/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/w/raisa-nosova-paints-the-town/','https://canvasrebel.com/meet-raisa-nosova/']},
  {id:15,a:'Rebekah Lazaridis',t:'B',loc:'31st St & 7th Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.76287,lng:-82.674533,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'rebekahlaz',bio:'This wall — Gothic moon, bats, barn owl in the storm — is exactly the territory Rebekah Lazaridis has worked her whole career: medieval art, fairy tales, the spiritual realm.\n\nShe started by building elaborate Halloween sets in front of her family\'s home as a teenager. That portfolio got her into the School of the Art Institute of Chicago, where she earned her BFA in Painting and Drawing in 2005. After Chicago she completed a one-year apprenticeship with USA829, the scenic artists\' union, in New York — working on Broadway productions, Saturday Night Live, live television, and large-scale films.\n\nBack in St. Pete (a third-generation native — her great-grandfather founded Wilhelm Funeral Home in 1918), her work integrates story, nostalgia, and the architecture of her hometown. Her solo show "CIRCA" at Mize Gallery rendered local landmarks — the Emerald Cocktail Lounge, the Thunderbird Hotel, Haslam\'s Books, Biff Burger — in postcard style. Her NYC solo "Broken Legs" was painted on black velvet theater curtains, cut and sewn into new compositions. She designs sets for Jobsite Theater, American Stage, freeFall, and the Straz Center, and is an Official Roycroft Artisan.\n\nShe also runs @homeillustrations — custom colored-pencil portraits of historic homes, starting at $375. For SHINE Origins 2025, she painted at 31st Street and 7th Avenue South.',desc:'A dramatic monochromatic mural on a black cinder block wall. A large ornate Gothic letter "B" with intricate Celtic knotwork fills the center, overlaid on a detailed full moon rendered in grays and whites. Bats swarm from the left across the night sky, and a barn owl with outstretched wings emerges from storm clouds on the right, with rain streaking diagonally downward. Signed "ReAh @rebekahlaz" in the lower right corner. The entire palette is black, white, and silver — a stark contrast to the colorful murals flanking it.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/rebekah-lazaridis.jpeg',from:'Pinellas County, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Official Roycroft Artisan; SHINE Origins 2025 selected artist',fw:[{name:'rebekahlazaridis.com',url:'http://www.rebekahlazaridis.com'}],sbio:'She is a third-generation St. Petersburg native. Her great-grandfather founded Wilhelm Funeral Home in 1918.\n\nRebekah Eugenia Lazaridis earned her BFA in Painting and Drawing from the School of the Art Institute of Chicago in 2005. The path started in her late teens, when she built elaborate Halloween sets and stages in front of her family\'s home — sets meant to entertain kids with song, dance, and theatrical elements rather than scare them. That non-traditional portfolio got her into multiple art schools.\n\nAfter Chicago she completed a one-year apprenticeship with USA829, the scenic artists\' union, in New York City. She worked in teams on Broadway productions, live television, Saturday Night Live, and large-scale films. Tampa Bay retail clients from her scenic work include Montblanc, Louis Vuitton, and Tiffany. Back in Florida she has worked as a scenic artist for Stageworks, the Straz Center, Patel Conservatory, American Stage, freeFall, and Jobsite Theater, where she also collaborates as a set designer. Jobsite productions she has worked on include Constellations, Doubt: A Parable, Dr. Ride\'s American Beach House, The Thanksgiving Play, and Twilight: Los Angeles, 1992. She has been in the theater industry for more than 20 years.\n\nHer first New York City solo exhibit, "Broken Legs: An Art Exhibit on Theater Superstition," opened at The Sheen Center for Thought and Culture at 18 Bleecker Street in NoHo in October 2015 and ran through November 30. The Sheen Center\'s art director found her after seeing her cover art for the book "The Dream Belongs to the Dreamer" by Velva Lee Heraty, MSW. The Broken Legs pieces explored superstitions like "break a leg," whistling backstage, and saying the name of "the Scottish play." She painted on black velvet theatrical curtains — the "legs" — then cut them up, altered them, and sewed them into new compositions. Her substrates included theater paint, old wood planks, scrim, and beat-up cabinets and drawers.\n\nHer solo show "CIRCA" opened at Mize Gallery (689 Dr MLK Jr St N, Suite C, St. Petersburg) on January 7, 2022 and ran through January 23. The works were Prismacolor pencil with touches of gouache and ink on colored cardstock, depicting St. Pete locations in an iconic postcard style. Pieces included Holiday Motel, Ted Peters, Thrill Hill, Osprey Nest, Beach Theatre, Manhattan Casino, The Garden, Truth, Emerald Cocktail Lounge, China City, Grand Central District, The Soreno Hotel Logo, Wolfie\'s Restaurant, Timeless Pelican, Royal Theater, Maas Brothers, Thunderbird Motel, Parakeet Feeder, Sunken Gardens, Publix Market, The Chattaway, Two Headed Palm on 22nd Ave, Bringe Music, The State Theatre, Southland Roller Palace, The Globe, Banana\'s Records, Ovo Cafe, Deuces Live, Old Southeast Sign, Old Northeast Sign, Gulfport Casino, Dairy Inn, Snell Isle Bridge, H&D Cafeteria, Biff Burger, Haslam\'s Books, and El Cap. The Manhattan Casino piece referenced the historic 22nd Street South dance hall that hosted Nat King Cole, Sarah Vaughn, and Ray Charles. The Royal Theater piece referenced the 1948 movie theater that catered to African Americans during segregation. The seed for the show was a 2019 painting she contributed to the Mize Halloween show "Creep" — a self-portrait as a little ghost on her childhood street in Old Northeast.\n\nShe runs @homeillustrations, where she draws customized portraits of historic homes and landmark buildings using colored pencils and colorful cardstock, often with a dramatic sunset behind each home. The 8x10 illustrations start at $375. The pursuit began during Covid theater shutdowns, sparked by a closing gift she made for the realtor who sold her and her husband Steve their old bungalow. She co-operated SCENERY BAGS / Eugenia Woods, a hand-painted bags and purses line, with her sister Susanna.\n\nIn 2022 she began intaglio copper plate etching at the Dunedin Fine Arts Center under the mentorship of Holly Bird and Steve Littlefield. She began teaching Beginner Intaglio at Dunedin Fine Arts Center in 2025. Her immersive art installation work includes THE UNSEEN and BLOOM AND RESIDUE. Collaborative efforts include Preserve The Burg, Historic Seminole Heights Neighborhood Association, and projectAlchemy. She is an Official Roycroft Artisan.\n\nShe was a featured artist in the SHINE Mural Festival in 2025 (SHINE Origins), painting at 31st Street and 7th Avenue South in St. Petersburg. Her work has been featured in Creative Loafing and St. Pete Catalyst. She had her wedding reception at Sunken Gardens in 2011 and lives in Tampa. Personal influences and recurring subjects include nature, medieval art, fairy tales, the afterlife, myth, the spiritual realm, and the architecture of her hometown. Website: www.RebekahLaz.com / rebekahlazaridis.com. Instagram: @homeillustrations.',src:['http://www.rebekahlazaridis.com/life-story','https://stpetecatalyst.com/a-little-bit-different-artist-rebekah-lazaridis-pays-homage-to-her-hometown/','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/local-artist-rebekah-lazaridis-explores-theatrical-superstitions-in-her-first-nyc-solo-show-12274595','https://voyagetampa.com/interview/check-out-rebekah-lazaridiss-story/','https://www.jobsitetheater.org/bio/lazaridis-rebekah-eugenia/','http://chadmize.com/exhibits/circa/']},
  {id:16,a:'Reid Jenkins',t:'Ikigai in Bloom',loc:'2039 1st Ave S',bldg:'Founders Pro',lat:27.769997,lng:-82.661186,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'reidmjenkins',bio:'He paints the people seldom seen in the \'Burg. He also tattoos them.\n\nReid Jenkins is a St. Petersburg native. He graduated from the Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School — the same program that produced Derek Donnelly — then earned a BFA from the Maryland Institute College of Art. He tattoos at Fabled Trade Tattoo Company, a queer-owned studio in the Lealman area, and keeps a studio at the ArtsXchange in the Warehouse Arts District.\n\nHis murals tend toward photorealistic portraiture and botanicals — his SHINE 2021 piece at the St. Pete Distillery featured portraits of his two daughters surrounded by sunflowers and greenery. He has noted that roughly a third of mural pay goes to supplies alone. He collaborated with Iboms and Zulu Painter on "Imagine Gas Plant & Laurel Park" at Campbell Park Recreational Center, and painted the cheetah with geometric 90s-inspired colors at Star Booty Salon on 16th Street South.\n\nFor SHINE Origins 2025, he painted a botanical piece at Founders Pro using a projection method for the outline.',desc:'A stunning photorealistic botanical mural on the side of a commercial building against a deep black background. An overflowing arrangement of tropical flowers fills the wall — king proteas, red ginger, heliconia, pincushion proteas, orchids, and ferns rendered in rich crimsons, pinks, corals, golds, and greens. The hyperrealistic detail and dramatic dark background give it the feel of a Dutch Golden Age still life painting scaled up to building size.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/reid-jenkins.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2021 selected artist; SHINE Origins 2025 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'He paints the people seldom seen in the \'Burg. He also tattoos them.\n\nReid Jenkins is a St. Petersburg native. He graduated from the Pinellas County Center for the Arts (PCCA) program at Gibbs High School, then earned a BFA from the Maryland Institute College of Art (MICA). He is a muralist, painter, illustrator, and tattoo artist. He tattoos at Fabled Trade Tattoo Company (formerly Ink Werkz Tattoo Studio), a queer-owned studio at 4195 54th Ave N in the Lealman area of St. Petersburg. He keeps a studio at the ArtsXchange in the Warehouse Arts District.\n\nHis aim is to "paint the human experience and the experience of those seldom seen in the \'Burg," focusing on capturing small, overlooked moments. His work tends toward photorealistic portraiture and botanicals. He has noted that roughly 33% of mural pay goes to supplies alone. He supports MIZE Gallery, COVE in Pinellas Park, Woodfield Fine Art, and d-gallerie.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, he painted at the St. Pete Distillery — portraits of his two daughters with sunflowers and greenery. He collaborated with Iboms and Zulu Painter on "Imagine Gas Plant & Laurel Park" at Campbell Park Recreational Center. Other murals include the large cheetah with geometric 90s-inspired colors at Star Booty Salon (1741 16th St S). He exhibited in "Renew," a group show with Bekky Beukes at Mergeculture, Tampa (February 2022).\n\nFor SHINE Origins 2025, he painted a botanical piece at Founders Pro, using a projection method for the outline. Instagram: @reidmjenkins.',src:['https://ilovetheburg.com/muralist-reid-jenkins/','https://floridapaints.com/blog/shine-mural-festival-st-petersburg-2025/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/for-year-11-shine-mural-festival-brings-it-back-home/','https://ilovetheburg.com/st-pete-shine-origins/','https://luciwest.com/photos-from-shine-origins-mural-festival-2025/']},
  {id:17,a:'Sara Salem',t:'Sol',loc:'2180 4th St N',bldg:'LMCU',lat:27.791175,lng:-82.640104,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'sarasalemartsy',bio:'She used VR to plan the composition before a single can of paint was opened. Then she handed the wall to the community.\n\nSara Salem is originally from Egypt, now based in St. Petersburg. She holds a BFA (2011), a 200-hour yoga teacher certification, Reiki Level II, and an art therapy certification. She co-founded Phi Phi Artland with her husband Fahd — a space that started as a martial arts facility and expanded into a comprehensive arts and wellness hub combining fine arts, yoga, sound baths, and Reiki.\n\nShe has over twelve years of teaching experience across Africa, Asia, and the United States, working with students from kindergarten through adults. She believes art is "a tool to achieve higher levels of well-being, self-awareness." She started murals as a side hustle painting for friends and found the process meditative.\n\nFor SHINE Origins 2025, she created "Sol" — a community mural wrapping around the LMCU Bank building, with "We Are St. Pete" painted across the design as the project\'s rallying line. She designed it in VR, then opened it to volunteers for a community weekend. After the public phase, she and her advanced students spent seven more days refining to festival quality. "This wasn\'t a mural for St. Pete," she said. "It was a mural created with St. Pete."',desc:'A large mural on the side of a building featuring a colorful pelican wearing a white baseball cap and reflective sunglasses that show St. Pete landmarks in their lenses. The pelican\'s wings are spread wide in vibrant pinks, oranges, blues, and deep reds. A golden spiral sun radiates in the upper left corner, and "We Are St. Pete" appears in playful navy blue script to the right. Signed "Phi Phi Artland @Sarasalemartsy" at the bottom. The white background keeps the whole piece bright and inviting.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/sara-salem.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE Origins 2025 selected artist',fw:[{name:'phiphiartland.com',url:'https://phiphiartland.com'}],sbio:'She used VR to plan the composition before a single can of paint was opened, then handed the wall to the community. Sara Salem is originally from Egypt and now based in St. Petersburg, Florida. She holds a Bachelor of Fine Arts from 2011, a 200-hour RYT yoga teacher certification, Reiki Level II certification, and a certification in art therapy. She co-founded Phi Phi Artland with her husband Fahd Elsaid, a partnership rooted in an eleven-year marriage that began as a friendship long before the business existed. She is also a partner at Custom Teez Shop and serves as Head of Fine Arts at Phi Phi Artland, where she works as art teacher, muralist, and yoga instructor.\n\nPhi Phi Artland opened in 2021 as a martial arts studio and expanded in 2023 into a comprehensive arts and wellness hub at 2299 9th Ave N STE 1G, St. Petersburg, FL 33713. The name draws from the Phi Phi Islands in Thailand. The studio\'s offerings include Muay Thai, acrylic painting, drawing, oil painting, watercolor, kids art classes for ages 7 to 14, teens art classes for ages 11 to 19, Evergreen art classes for ages 50 and up, mind-body workshops, yoga, breath work, sound bath meditation, and Reiki. There is also a murals division serving commercial, residential, public, and community projects across St. Petersburg, Tampa, Ybor, Central Avenue, and the wider Tampa Bay area. The mural division has logged 5,582 square feet painted to date. Claire Delong has partnered with Salem on the murals division, and Fahd Elsaid oversees site assessments, client coordination, scheduling, budgeting, material sourcing, scaffolding setup, safety compliance, and cleanup. The Phi Phi Artland team also includes muralist and art instructor Vyolette Hastings, known as 6ER; Miami-based Ringling College of Art and Design graduate Sandra Hernandez, who runs the brand starri.day; and studio artist and educator Brianna Taylor, a 2019 Coastal Carolina University Studio Art graduate from Hamer, South Carolina.\n\nSalem has more than twelve years of teaching experience across Africa, Asia, and the United States, working with students from kindergarten through adult learners. She believes art is "a tool to achieve higher levels of well-being, self-awareness." Her mural career began as a side hustle painting for friends, a process she found meditative, and grew through word-of-mouth referrals, an online portfolio, and local collaborations into a full mural business under the Phi Phi Artland brand.\n\nShe won the Art Wave contest at Countryside Mall with her mural "Retro in Colors," a piece combining nostalgic elements with a modern twist. She designed and led the LMCU Community Mural Project in 2024, sponsored by St. Pete Arts Alliance and St. Pete City, with the Phi Phi Artland team as designer and the St. Pete Artful Community as painter. She returned in 2025 with the St. Pete Community Mural, again sponsored by St. Pete Arts Alliance.\n\nFor SHINE Mural Festival 2025, the festival\'s eleventh year and the first to include local St. Petersburg artists, Salem created "Sol," a community mural wrapping around the LMCU Bank building, with "We Are St. Pete" painted across the design as the project\'s rallying line. The project coincided with LMCU celebrating the opening of its first St. Pete branch and its acquisition of Pilot Bank. Salem mapped the entire composition using a VR headset, floating inside the design to adjust scale, plan movement, and build the blueprint in digital space, replacing hours of projection work with minutes of digital prep. Phase one ran Friday through Sunday as a community weekend with five daily painting slots filled by LMCU members, families, kids, longtime locals, new residents, and first-time painters. Roy Binger, LMCU Senior Vice President, and Chris Steinocher, CEO of the St. Pete Chamber of Commerce, were among the community members who painted. Salem guided each section personally to ensure every volunteer had a meaningful part. Phase two ran Monday through Sunday, when Salem returned with a small team of her advanced students for seven days of detailed brushwork, layering, line work, and large-scale coverage to bring the piece to festival-level finish. The VR prep meant no last-minute redesigns and no wasted hours. "This wasn\'t a mural for St. Pete," she said. "It was a mural created with St. Pete." She also said, "Since I came here, I wanted to be in SHINE. This is the first year they\'re doing it for local artists, and I\'m on it. I\'m so excited." Florida Paints served as the festival\'s official paint partner for the first time in 2025, supplying products including Sunscreen designed for Florida sun exposure.\n\nPhase three extended the project beyond the wall. Salem\'s artwork was featured at the Morean Arts Center and the St. Pete Museum of History. SHINE\'s opening ceremony highlighted her festival entry. LMCU hosted an event at their new St. Pete branch showcasing and selling her artwork. Art Conservatory for Teens (ACT) participated with young performers dancing and singing as part of the surrounding festivities.\n\nSHINE Mural Festival has commissioned more than 170 murals across its eleven-year history. Other 2025 SHINE artists profiled alongside Salem included Cuban-born muralist Karel García and emerging muralist Isac Gres. Salem\'s website is phiphiartland.com, and her Instagram handles are @ss_artsy_yogi and @phi_phi_artland.',src:['https://phiphiartland.com/murals','https://canvasrebel.com/meet-sara-salem/','https://queuevision.com/discovering-wellness-through-art-the-inspiring-journey-of-phi-phi-artland/','https://floridapaints.com/blog/shine-mural-festival-st-petersburg-2025/','https://phiphiartland.com/team','https://phiphiartland.com/2025','https://umbrellalocalheroes.com/discovering-wellness-through-art-the-inspiring-journey-of-phi-phi-artland/']},
  {id:19,a:'Zulu Painter',t:'The Hero\'s Journey for Love and Community',loc:'31st St & 7th Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.76287,lng:-82.674952,y:2025,cat:'shine',ig:'zulupainter1',bio:'He calls them "Mindful Murals." He wants to grow art everywhere.\n\nCarlos Culbertson — Zulu Painter — is a native West Virginian who started pursuing art at age eight. He holds a degree in fine arts painting and now works out of a studio at the ArtsXchange on 22nd Street South. He paints in acrylic and spray paint, favors reclaimed materials, and has worked as a scenic artist for Disney on Ice, the Science Channel, HSN, and SeaWorld Parks and Entertainment.\n\nHis mural projects span the country: Denver, Chattanooga, Atlanta, Austin, Miami, Las Vegas, Los Angeles, Kansas City, Flint, and Binghamton, New York — where he painted "Cultivate Kindness," a 40-foot mural with West Side Middle School students. In St. Pete, he painted murals at CASA Family Justice Center on Arlington Ave (domestic violence survivor themes) and a portrait of Betty Reid Soskin at the Florida Botanical Gardens in Largo.\n\nHe is a four-time SHINE artist — "Moon Beam Dream 2" in 2016, "Legacy" with Thirst in 2017, "Wisdom, Growth, Progress" in 2018, and the AMIKids wall in 2024. He received a St. Pete Arts Alliance Muse Award in 2022. He also collaborated with Reid Jenkins and Iboms on "Imagine Gas Plant and Laurel Park" at Campbell Park Recreational Center.',desc:'A vibrant mural on a cinder block wall blending multiple styles. The left side features a photorealistic portrait of a young Black woman in profile against a houndstooth pattern with a boombox rendered in grayscale. The center and right side shift to a cartoon aesthetic — a wild-eyed character with a mischievous grin faces off against a massive green alligator with bared teeth, set against a turquoise geometric diamond pattern and black-and-white checkerboard. Colorful polka dots in orange, pink, and yellow punctuate the composition. Signed "zulupainter.com" in the lower right.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2025/zulu-painter.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'St. Pete Arts Alliance Muse Award (2022); SHINE 2016, 2017, 2018, 2024 selected artist; Colorado Black Arts Festival 2018 featured artist',fw:[{name:'zulupainter.com',url:'https://www.zulupainter.com'}],sbio:'He calls them "Mindful Murals." He wants to grow art everywhere.\n\nCarlos Culbertson — Zulu Painter — is a native West Virginian who started pursuing art at elementary school age and never looked back. He holds a degree in fine arts painting and works out of his studio at the ArtsXchange in St. Petersburg, located at 515 22nd Street South, Studio 111, St. Petersburg, FL 33712. He works in acrylic and spray paint, favors reclaimed materials, and produces work across traditional mediums, body paint, special effects, and scenic art.\n\nHis scenic art client list includes The Science Channel, Disney on Ice, Home Shopping Network (HSN), SeaWorld Parks and Entertainment, and a series of national ad campaigns. He has also served as a Representative Artist for Visit Florida, the state\'s tourism agency.\n\nHis mural work spans the country: Denver, Chattanooga, Atlanta, Austin, Miami, Las Vegas, Los Angeles, Kansas City, Flint, Sanford (FL), Jacksonville, and Binghamton, New York. In Binghamton he painted "Spread Love" — an approximately 40-foot mural on the M&T Bank building at 68 Exchange Street — using Golden Paintworks Mural & Theme Paint and Kobra Spray Paint. The mural depicts the butterfly effect of good deeds: watering a single rose creates the outlines of others. He was presented with a certificate of recognition for the piece by Broome County executive Brian Garner. He has painted at Paint Memphis 2019, the 2018 Colorado Black Arts Festival, the 48 Hour Mural Competition in Jacksonville, Florida (September 29, 2023), the HULAWEEN Spirit of Suwannee Lake Music and Art Festival, and the Art With Me Art and Music Festival in Miami.\n\nIn St. Petersburg, he painted the murals at the CASA Family Justice and Support Centers on First Avenue North — work dedicated to domestic violence survivor themes, including a depiction of a woman with an outstretched hand among bright geometric patterns, flowers, children, and a mother. As Painter described it, "The CASA Mural is a mural dedicated to the idea of a helping hand. CASA helps children and families to have a safe environment — so I wanted the mural to depict that." For the 2024 Pinellas African American Heritage Celebration on February 24 at the Florida Botanical Gardens (12520 Ulmerton Road, Largo), he was selected as the representative artist and painted a likeness of Betty Reid Soskin — the Black woman who served as a 100-year-old Park Ranger for the National Park Service before retiring in 2022. Children at the event helped complete a section of the canvas designed for that purpose.\n\nHe is a four-time SHINE Mural Festival artist: "Moon Beam Dream 2" in 2016, "Legacy" with Thirst in 2017, "Wisdom, Growth, Progress" in 2018 as part of his 22nd Street Legacy series, and the AMIKids wall in 2024. He returned to SHINE for SHINE Origins 2025, painting at 31st Street and 7th Avenue South alongside Elizabeth Barenis, Amy Volpe, Karel Garcia, Brain the Genius, Jenipher Chandley, and Rebekah Lazaridis. He also collaborated with Reid Jenkins and Iboms on "Imagine Gas Plant and Laurel Park" at Campbell Park Recreational Center.\n\nIn 2022 he received a St. Petersburg Arts Alliance Muse Award, alongside fellow 2022 honorees Matthew McGee, Gloria Muñoz, David and Becky Ramsey, Chad Mize, and Matthew Walker. The ceremony was held March 4, 2022 at the Morean Center for Clay in the Warehouse Arts District, hosted by Paul Wilborn and Eugenie Bondurant. He has also been recognized with a 2021 Business Arts Award, was a winner of the 2021 UF Big Idea Competition, has been published in a Springer Scientific Journal, and was featured in the Innovate Gainesville Book.\n\nHis SHINE Festival murals are featured in the St. Petersburg Arts Alliance\'s accessible audio tour, including the 22nd Street Legacy series ("Wisdom, Growth, Progress") and "Moonbeam Dreams." He has spoken about his motivation in framing public art as a community gesture: "Public art has the immediate opportunity to affect the community. It requires no fee to enter, is open year round and flash photography is allowed. Everyone can enjoy public art." He has also said his goal with the Betty Reid Soskin mural and his work generally is for children to see "artists and Park Rangers that look like them."\n\nHis studio is open Monday through Friday, 9am to 6pm. Contact: info@zulupainter.com, 727-768-4737. Website: zulupainter.com. Instagram: @zulupainter1. Facebook: facebook.com/ZULUPAINTER1.',src:['https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/the-mindful-murals-of-zulu-painter/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/zulupainter-2','https://baynews9.com/fl/tampa/news/2024/02/20/local-muralist-painting-with-children-at-the-pinellas-american-heritage-celebration-at-the-florida-botanical-garden-in-largo','https://ilovetheburg.com/st-pete-shine-origins/','https://www.zulupainter.com/about.html','https://creativepinellas.org/artist/zulu-painter/','https://www.goldenpaintworks.com/mural-makers-zulu-painter/','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/muralist-zulu-painter-among-honorees-for-st-pete-arts-alliances-muse-awards-12783271','https://monochronicle.com/artist/zulu-painter/','https://paintmemphis.org/zulupainter/']},
  {id:20,a:'Abys',t:'',loc:'201 17th Street South',bldg:'Modera',lat:27.768488,lng:-82.657474,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'abys_osmoz',bio:'His first graffiti piece was a Rasta sheep with a joint. He was in high school.\n\nJulien Brouand — Abys — was born in 1991 in Nancy, France. His father put a pencil in his hand at eighteen months. He originally wanted to design characters for video games or cartoons. He has no formal art education. He discovered graffiti in high school after meeting fellow artist Suroh, and they formed Osmoz Colors together.\n\nHis style is cartoon-driven — whimsical, exaggerated creatures in lively narrative scenes. A "fantastic bestiary" of jazzman rats, thieving raccoons, gators, crabs. He describes each piece as "a freeze-frame in full action." He works in spray paint, drawing, silk-screening, and tattoo. He cites Mario, Ratchet & Clank, Zelda, Worms, and Banjo & Kazooie as influences.\n\nHe has painted across France — Paris, Marseille, Toulouse, Bordeaux, Calais, Carcassonne — and in Luxembourg and Belgium. For SHINE 2024, he crossed the Atlantic and painted a gator mural at Modera on 17th Street South in about four days, plus a bonus crab at Anchor Skate Shop next door.',desc:'A six-story-tall mural on the side of the Modera apartment building depicting a cartoonish, pot-bellied alligator with a mischievous grin, riding a red surfboard through splashing turquoise waves. A pink hibiscus flower sits atop the gator\'s head, and teal smoke curls above. The surfboard reads "SHINE" in stylized lettering. At the base, a triangular logo anchors the composition. The character\'s exaggerated features and vibrant green-on-gray color scheme make it visible from blocks away.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/abys.jpeg',from:'Nancy, France',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2024 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'His first graffiti piece was a Rasta sheep with a joint. He was in high school.\n\nJulien Brouand — Abys — was born in 1991 in Nancy, France. His father drew a lot and put a pencil in his hand at eighteen months. He has been filling pages ever since. He originally wanted to design characters for video games or cartoons. He has no formal art education in the fine-art sense, but he studied applied arts at Lycée Loritz in Nancy before turning fully to mural painting and illustration. He is self-taught as a graffiti writer.\n\nHe met Suroh in high school. Suroh was already into the movement but had never painted. They went to get cans one afternoon, slept at Suroh\'s parents\' place, and went out that night. Abys painted the Rasta sheep with the joint. With three other friends they formed the Bêêêh Crew and started painting sheep in the streets of Nancy. As more writers joined, the group worked under the name La Meute for a few years. People moved on, others stopped painting, and Abys ended up teamed with Suroh again. In 2011 the two founded Osmoz — later Osmoz Colors — combining illustration and lettering in collaborative frescoes. He is also a member of Nid d\'Guêpes Posse (also written Le Nid de Guêpes), a hip-hop collective bringing together rappers, graffiti artists and DJs that organizes neighborhood events.\n\nToday he paints more often with Scaf and Valer, both a generation above him. He credits them with opening new perspectives during a stretch when he was painting alone. Documented collaborations include "Freddy et la fille aux cheveux roses" with Scaf in Bordeaux (2018) and "Crâne tropical et requin festif" with Scaf and Valer in Pessac (2022).\n\nHis style is cartoon-driven — whimsical, exaggerated creatures in lively narrative scenes. A "fantastic bestiary" of jazzman rats, thieving raccoons, gators, crabs. He describes each piece as "a freeze-frame in full action." Early on he chased realism after discovering the Maclaim crew, then dropped it because he wanted to create rather than reproduce. He went back to drawing and started building compositions that play characters, decor, letters, colors and moods against each other. He sketches constantly in books and assembles finished visuals from the pieces. He works in spray paint, drawing, silk-screening, canvas, and tattoo. He cites Mario, Ratchet & Clank, Ori, Zelda, Worms, Banjo & Kazooie, and Jak & Daxter as influences, plus old-school cartoons.\n\nHe left home around nineteen, did odd jobs, then started his own decoration business. He grew tired of clients who wanted a "wall printer" reproducing rough photos and stopped taking that work. He kept the lights on running workshops with kids and painted for himself the rest of the time. Over time he rebuilt his identity to the point that clients now give him carte blanche.\n\nHe has painted across France — Paris, Marseille, Toulouse, Bordeaux, Calais, Carcassonne, Cergy, Roubaix, Pessac, Le Barcarès — and in Luxembourg and Belgium. Documented works include "Symbiose marine colorée" in Le Barcarès (2023), "Tortue marine et crabe ermite d\'Abys" in Roubaix (2021), and "La Casa de la Felicidad" in Cergy (2020). He paints legal walls, EDF power substations and private facades around the Nancy area. Festival credits include Urban Graff X, Chill Up, and COLORS Urban Art (2019).\n\nFor SHINE 2024 — the festival\'s tenth anniversary edition, originally scheduled for October 11-20 and rescheduled after Hurricanes Helene and Milton — he crossed the Atlantic to St. Petersburg, Florida. A scheduling conflict meant he started and finished his piece before the rescheduled festival opened. He painted a gator mural at Modera, 201 17th Street South, in about four days. SHINE director Jenee Priebe then handed him the back side of Anchor Skate Shop at 501 16th Street South, where he added a bonus crab. Johnny Vitale and Kris Markovich painted the other sides of the same building. The festival, curated and invitation-only, is produced by the St. Petersburg Arts Alliance.\n\nHe became a father during the pandemic, which gave him time to focus on sketches and canvases. He names "The Weird" as a crew he rates highly, and Maye as a writer who impresses him. He flags Horor, Korsé, Enora, Koye, Zeklo, and Koga as underrated.\n\nFrom the Osmoz-Colors site (osmoz-colors.fr): Studio at 74 rue de Mon Désert, 54000 Nancy, France. Contact: abys.osmoz@gmail.com, 06.76.89.68.37. Collaborators listed alongside Abys and Suroh include Scaf, Mites, Reso, Joef, and Fogsprod. The portfolio documents named projects in additional venues including Le Mur Oberkampf in Paris (December 2021), Le Mur Epinal (2021), Mesto Gallery in Podebrady, Czech Republic (2021), Underground Effect at Paris La Défense (2020), Zoo Art Show in Lyon (2020), Klownala in Montauban for Mister Freeze (2020), "Kings" in Carcassonne with Reso and Scaf (2019), Long Art Street in Longlaville (2021), Street Art Boulogne Festival in Boulogne-sur-Mer (2021), Mister Freeze Exhibition in Toulouse (2018), Toul Graffiti Jam (2017), Open Summer Festival in Toulouse with Scaf, Mites, and Reso (2018), Urban Giants in Milan (2018), Mjc Bazin in Nancy (2018), and an "Abys Museum" pop-up in Nancy (2021). Documented canvas works include "I Love You More Than Ice Cream" (2021), "All Aboard" (2020), "Fireflies Hunter" (2019), "A Crack In Life" (2019), "Chillers" (2019), "Lone Bikers" (2019), "The Kleptoman" (2019), "Rainmakers" (2019), "Endless Race" (2019), "One Monkey Band" (2018), and "Seventh Land" (2016). Other commercial clients listed include Spadas Clothing (2014), BDS\'s Gaming House in Geneva (2020), and School of Style in Luxembourg (2019). He has produced recycled skateboard art ("7 Plis," 2021). Infographic / design projects credited under Osmoz include "Touche d\'espoir" with Joef & Fogsprod (2017) and "Stratégie de Paix" (2015).\n\nFind him on Instagram as @abys_osmoz, on Flickr as abys-osmoz, and on Facebook as OSMOZ.COLORS.',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2024_SHINE_abys','https://www.axios.com/local/tampa-bay/2024/11/22/shine-mural-festival-2024-st-pete','https://stpetecatalyst.com/brush-to-canvas-shine-and-other-art-news/','https://www.throwup.it/en/artists/abys/','https://www.throwup.it/en/graffiti/graffiti-interviews/an-interview-with-abys-graffiti-to-take-back-urban-areas/','https://www.street-artwork.com/en/artist-profile/14/abys','https://www.street-art-addict.com/en/street-art/artist/abys-osmoz/','https://streetartcities.com/artists/abys','https://osmoz-colors.fr/','https://osmoz-colors.fr/contact/','https://osmoz-colors.fr/portfolio/']},
  {id:211,a:'Alyssa Marie',t:'',loc:'Albert Whitted Airport',bldg:'Albert Whitted Airport',lat:27.764316,lng:-82.633739,y:2024,cat:'commercial',ig:'alyssa.marie.of.the.sea',bio:'A bright yellow Stearman biplane punches straight out of the hangar wall it\'s painted on — wings, struts, bracing wires, and a propeller blurred into a translucent disc, all scaled up to read from a moving car on First Street.\n\nAlyssa Marie is a fine artist from Colorado who moved to St. Petersburg in 2017 and opened Alyssa Marie Gallery on Beach Drive NE the next year (it closed in January 2020). Off the festival circuit she\'s better known as half of The Happy Mural Project — the "Honk if You\'re Happy" sunflower walls she paints with Braden Everly, a three-time SHINE team.\n\nHer solo work runs to local and nature scenes — water, birds, the Florida coast — in oil and on walls. The biplane is the outlier: an aircraft instead of wildlife, and it earns the exception.\n\nFriends of Albert Whitted Airport, the City of St. Petersburg, and the St. Petersburg Arts Alliance commissioned it in 2024, funded by the William and Katherine McMannis charitable fund, and unveiled it that January. A second airport mural by Brian McAllister went up alongside it. The plane bursting from its own hangar turns the building into a picture of what\'s parked inside.',desc:'A large mural on the side of a hangar at Albert Whitted Airport, painted to match the building\'s own dark, curved-roof silhouette. A bright yellow Stearman-style open-cockpit biplane fills the center, shown nearly head-on and angling forward as if taxiing out of the dark hangar interior. Upper and lower wings are rendered in saturated yellow with white spars; thin white struts and crossed bracing wires connect the wings, and the landing gear and wheels sit below. At the nose, the spinning propeller is painted as a large translucent circular disc, with the engine cowling and prop hub massed dark at its center. The background reads as sky and water in blues, with the real curved roofline of the hangar incorporated into the composition. Painted on the hangar wall facing First Street.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/alyssa-marie-biplane.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:[{name:'alyssamariegallery.com',url:'https://alyssamariegallery.com'}],sbio:'A bright yellow Stearman biplane punches straight out of the hangar wall it\'s painted on — wings, struts, bracing wires, and a propeller blurred into a translucent disc, all scaled up to read from a moving car on First Street.\n\nAlyssa Marie (credited on the airport project as Alyssa Dunlap) is a fine artist from Colorado who relocated to St. Petersburg in 2017. In 2018 she opened Alyssa Marie Gallery on Beach Drive NE in downtown St. Petersburg, where she exhibited her own work; the gallery closed in January 2020 and she shifted to selling paintings of local and nature scenes. With her husband Braden Everly she is half of The Happy Mural Project, the "Honk if You\'re Happy" sunflower-and-color-by-number walls that have been selected for SHINE three times (2020, 2022, 2023). Her solo subjects run nautical and Florida-coastal — water, birds, the coast — in oil on canvas and scaled up onto walls.\n\nThis wall sits on the hangar facing First Street at Albert Whitted Airport. It depicts a bright yellow Stearman biplane — a classic open-cockpit trainer — emerging head-on from the hangar, propeller rendered as a translucent spinning disc. The piece was commissioned in 2024 through a collaboration between Friends of Albert Whitted Airport, the City of St. Petersburg, and the St. Petersburg Arts Alliance, with funding from the charitable fund of William and Katherine McMannis, and unveiled in January 2024. A companion mural by St. Petersburg muralist and art educator Brian McAllister, titled "Past, Present and Future," went up nearby, visible from the airport restaurant, The Hangar. The murals mark St. Petersburg\'s aviation heritage — the city is recognized as the birthplace of scheduled commercial airline flight.\n\nInstagram: @alyssa.marie.of.the.sea. Website: alyssamariegallery.com.',src:['https://www.stpeteinnovationdistrict.com/blog/albert-whitted-airport-murals','https://www.aopa.org/news-and-media/all-news/2024/march/07/art-at-the-airport','https://foawa.org/new-murals-unveiled-at-albert-whitted-airport/ (unveiled Jan 2024; McMannis charitable fund; hangar faces First Street; artist credited \'Alyssa Dunlap\')','https://alyssamariegallery.com/1murals (gallery murals portfolio)','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/alyssa-marie-2022 (SPAA artist page)']},
  {id:21,a:'Cristi López',t:'Moo',loc:'2655 Fairfield Ave S',bldg:'Groove Haven',lat:27.764095,lng:-82.66996,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'cristi.lopez.s',bio:'In college, she used unerasable media on purpose. It was exposure therapy — she has OCD, and the inability to fix a line was the point.\n\nCristi Lopez is a first-generation American of Dominican, Spanish, and Cuban descent. Her parents immigrated from the Dominican Republic. She grew up in New England, relocated to Florida as a kid, and earned a BFA in Illustration from Ringling College of Art and Design in 2016. She completed a drawing and painting residency at Lillstreet Art Center in Chicago (2017-18) and moved to St. Petersburg around 2023.\n\nHer work explores mental illness, femininity, faith, and heritage. The influences are specific: Vienna Secession, Symbolist painters, Candido Bido, Dominican folk art, Ukiyo-e, Catholic iconography. Recurring motifs include scissors — reclaiming a symbol of fear — cows, and Florida wildness. Her solo show "Unravel: Portraits of My Obsessions" at A Very Serious Gallery in Chicago (2023) featured fifteen paintings about her OCD, partnered with NAMI Chicago.\n\nShe co-hosts Queer Contours, a donation-based figure drawing series with Queer Expression St. Pete. She teaches vinyasa yoga. She received a 2025 Individual Artist Grant from the St. Pete Arts Alliance and was nominated for Creative Loafing\'s Best of the Bay in both Best Muralist and Best Mural.\n\nFor SHINE 2024, she painted "Moo" at Groovehaven Music on Fairfield Avenue South — her first Tampa Bay mural. She called it "a love letter to my visual and cultural influences."',desc:'A large surrealist mural on the side of Groove Haven on a pale blue sky background. A central female figure with dark hair, green eyeshadow, rosy cheeks, and red lips gazes outward, wearing a pearl necklace and dark clothing. She holds a large maroon sphere with a single eye painted on it — a recurring motif in Lopez\'s work. Behind her, a full white moon glows against the horizon where a turquoise ocean meets the sky. Green tropical fern fronds frame the left side, while small white goats balance on a red clothesline to the right. A blue monkey sits among decorative green mandala patterns at the lower right. The composition blends Dominican folk art, Catholic iconography, and surrealist symbolism into a dreamlike tropical scene. Signed "Cristi López 24" in the lower right corner. Lopez described the piece as "a love letter to my visual and cultural influences."',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/cristi-lopez.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'2025 Individual Artist Grant, St. Pete Arts Alliance; nominated Best Muralist and Best Mural, Creative Loafing Best of the Bay 2025; Drawing & Painting Residency, Lillstreet Art Center Chicago (2017-18); SHINE 2024 selected artist',fw:[{name:'cristilopezart.com',url:'http://www.cristilopezart.com'}],sbio:'In college, she used unerasable media on purpose. It was exposure therapy — she has OCD, and the inability to fix a line was the point. She made herself draw with unerasable media for six months, bought sketchbooks she could not tear pages out of without compromising the binding, and let ugly drawings live in the book until the discomfort stopped registering.\n\nCristi López (born 1993) is a first-generation American of Dominican, Spanish, and Cuban descent. Her parents immigrated from the Dominican Republic before she was born. The family started in New England and moved to Florida in her childhood, where she has lived since. She earned a BFA in Illustration from Ringling College of Art and Design in Sarasota in 2016. After graduating, illustration agencies told her the work belonged in galleries, and she shifted toward fine art.\n\nShe completed the Drawing & Painting Artist Residency at Lillstreet Art Center in Chicago in 2017-18, then relocated to St. Petersburg around 2023. Her first solo exhibition, "Unravel: Portraits of My Obsessions," opened at A Very Serious Gallery in Chicago (April 7 – May 13, 2023) — fifteen paintings about her relationship with OCD, partnered with the Chicago chapter of NAMI, which provided mental health resources and a lecture panel. Her second solo, "Anima: The Feral Femme," opened at VSG Contemporary in Chicago in 2024.\n\nGroup exhibitions include "Slaps Show" Vol. 4 at Elephant Room Gallery in Chicago (2025), "BLISS! Group Exhibition" at The Factory in St. Pete (2024), "Wag your Tail" at Vertical Projects Space in Chicago (2023), "It Was All A Dream" at Vertical Gallery in Chicago (2022), "#ink" at Vertical Gallery in Chicago (2020), "Love Adjacent" at Sally Centigrade Gallery in Boulder, Colorado (2020), and the "Slaps! Show" at Young Chicago Authors in Chicago (2019).\n\nHer work explores mental illness, femininity, faith, and heritage. Influences she names directly: the Vienna Secession, Symbolist painters, Cándido Bidó, Dominican folk art and pastoral paintings, Ukiyo-e, Japonisme, Catholic iconography, powerful women, Florida, and a love of cows. Recurring motifs include scissors — a symbol of intrusive-thought fear she reclaimed by drawing them — cows, and Florida wildness. Female figures appear naked but not coquettish, with the artist describing them as "unapologetically" present, her counter to the male-gaze poses she grew up seeing.\n\nHer first OCD memory was as a young child afraid of sleepwalking and harming her family in her sleep. She made her parents hide all the sharp objects in the house. The diagnosis came as an adult. She works as a mental health advocate alongside her studio practice. The Made of Millions Foundation interviewed her in 2023 ("The Unseen and Unerasable: Cristi López on Bringing Intrusive Thoughts to Light," by Esther Fernandez) about the "Unravel" show, OCD, and the role of art in recovery.\n\nHer work has been collected privately, including by the owners of Apollo\'s Praise Winery, who commissioned a tasting-room mural and her bottle-label designs. She received an Individual Artist Grant from the St. Petersburg Arts Alliance in 2025, and was nominated for Creative Loafing Tampa Bay\'s Best of the Bay in both Best Muralist and Best Mural in summer 2025.\n\nShe co-hosts Queer Contours, a donation-based bimonthly figure drawing series in collaboration with Queer Expression St. Pete, an organization that cultivates LGBTQ+ community spaces in the Tampa Bay area. The series began as "Reunion Drawing" when she lived in Chicago and celebrated its first anniversary in St. Pete on August 11, 2025. She also teaches vinyasa yoga at SukhaLife.\n\nFor SHINE Mural Festival 2024 — November 2024 — she painted "Moo" at Groovehaven Music on Fairfield Avenue South. It was her first Tampa Bay mural. She described it as "a love letter to my visual and cultural influences." In July 2025 she completed "Shared Shores" at the St. Petersburg Pier as part of a temporary five-artist exhibition celebrating the Pier\'s fifth anniversary, organized by No Good Deeds Art, the nonprofit founded by former SHINE Festival Director Jenée Priebe. "Shared Shores" is part of her FloriDiosa series — homage to wild Florida, femininity, and wildness — and centers the Brown Pelican, the official city bird, as a symbol of resilience. The exhibition was scheduled to remain on display until at least October 2025.\n\nShe frames her preference for murals over gallery work as the "stumble-upon" aspect — the public, accessible, communal access to art that gallery walls keep behind private doors. She participated in a street mural chalk protest in St. Petersburg\'s Grand Central District in September 2025 in response to the Florida Department of Transportation painting over street art including the Pride crosswalk and Black History Matters murals.\n\nWebsite: cristilopezart.com. Email: cristilopezsalazar@gmail.com. Instagram: @cristi.lopez.s.',src:['http://www.cristilopezart.com/about','http://www.cristilopezart.com/shine-mural-festival','https://sanpedrogazette.com/2025/09/08/profile-stumble-upon-art-and-community-with-cristi-lopez/','https://www.madeofmillions.com/articles/the-unseen-and-unerasable-cristi-lopez-on-bringing-intrusive-thoughts-to-light','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2024_SHINE_cristi_lopez']},
  {id:22,a:'Dwayne Shepherd & Brian McAllister',t:'The Importance of ART Education',loc:'3535 Central Ave',bldg:'Bell Pharmacy',lat:27.771228,lng:-82.681511,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'dwayneshepherd3499',bio:'When the Florida art-teachers convention came to St. Petersburg in October 2024, they painted this wall.\n\n"The Importance of Art Education" was painted at Bell Pharmacy as one of four "Bright Spots" community murals in SHINE\'s 10th-anniversary festival. Dwayne Shepherd and Brian McAllister led the wall in partnership with the Florida Art Educators Association — finished early to coincide with the FAEA\'s annual convention. Art teachers from across the state picked up brushes.\n\nBrian McAllister teaches visual arts at Gibbs High School\'s Pinellas County Center for the Arts — the same program that trained Derek Donnelly and Reid Jenkins. He holds a degree from Ringling College of Art and Design, runs JBM Studios out of St. Pete, and works as an international muralist whose practice centers on local folklore, history, and culture. His SHINE 2019 piece "Evolution" at the historic Royal Theater in the Deuces District — Angela Bassett, the Griffin twins, Serena Williams — was painted with Boys & Girls Club members and Gibbs Mural Club students. He also led the Gibbs Mural Club\'s Fighting Chance Mural at the Morean Arts Center, raising COVID-19 relief funds.\n\nDwayne Shepherd studied art, psychology, and theology at Austin Peay State University. He works mostly in acrylics, paints impressionist landscapes from his Historic Kenwood home studio, and serves as a docent at the Dali Museum. He teaches at Osceola Middle School in Seminole, where he founded a mural club that decorates the school\'s halls — student work spans inspirational quotes, butterflies, and a "superpowers" theme; the club shifted toward community and social-change themes during the pandemic.',desc:'A vibrant marine-themed mural on the side of Bell Pharmacy celebrating art education. The composition centers on an underwater scene with sea turtles swimming among mangrove roots, tropical fish, and coral. Dolphins arc through the water above while the text "The Importance of ART Education" anchors the design. The mural was painted collaboratively with art educators from across Florida during the FAEA annual conference in October 2024 as one of four "Bright Spots" community murals in SHINE\'s 10th anniversary festival.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/dwayne-shepherd-brian-mcallister.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'McAllister: SHINE 2019 (Royal Theater); PCCA visual arts instructor; SHINE 2024',fw:null,sbio:'McAllister teaches visual arts at Gibbs High School\'s Pinellas County Center for the Arts, the same program that trained Derek Donnelly and Reid Jenkins. Shepherd is a Dali Museum docent who paints impressionist landscapes from his Historic Kenwood home studio. For SHINE 2024, the two collaborated on a Bright Spots community mural in St. Petersburg.\n\nBrian McAllister holds a degree in Art from Ringling College of Art and Design in Sarasota. He runs JBM Studios out of St. Pete, Florida, working as an international muralist, professional artist, illustrator, graphic designer, and arts educator. His practice spans oil, acrylic, mixed media, digital, and print making. His work focuses on local folklore, history, and culture. JBM Studios projects include Spirit of St. Pete, Tichenor Orthopedic Clinic for Children in Long Beach CA, Raising Local Spirits, Handoff, The Sky is the Limit, Resting Place, Black Leaf Wine and Cigar Lounge, Little Bird Key, National Gratitude, The Peir\'s Sentinel, The Schu, Fred Held South Pasadena Habitat, Creative Habitat, Triumphant Youth, Native Roots, Caribbean Cafe, Alliance Gulf Coast, Weightless, Coastal Protection, Moseidon, Sunset Stroll, Eastern Shore Alliance, King of Pipeline, Pete\'s Pot, Resilient, Right of Way, Spoonbill Welcome, The Flag Company, The Royal Theater, Northeast Little League, Tampa Bay Rays, Be The Change, Tribal Wave, Stonegate Mortgage Co, Anchor Skate Supply, St Pete Ink Co., and Alden Suites Resort. His studio works include Calm After the Storm (mixed media, 16" x 48"), Big Wave (oil, 30" x 40"), Peak 6 (oil on wood, 16" x 48"), One Fin Turn (digital), The Duke (digital), Dizzy (digital), Delayed Ascent (acrylic on wood, 48" x 24"), Peter Tosh (digital), Indecent Exposure (mixed media, 18" x 24"), Yancy and the Breakneck Quartet (mixed media, 11" x 17"), Passafire (mixed media, 11" x 17"), The Roots Crew (mixed media, 11" x 17"), Method Man (graphite, 9" x 12"), Redman (mixed media, 18" x 24"), Tenacious D (oil on canvas, 18" x 24"), Got Job? (mixed media, 9\' x 12"), Redemption (intaglio, 3" x 9"), and Early in the Mornin\' Drake (acrylic on canvas, 12" x 24"). He is the featured artist for a Tampa Bay Rays print.\n\nHis SHINE 2019 piece, titled "Evolution," was painted at the historic Royal Theater in the Deuces District in partnership with the Boys and Girls Club and Gibbs Mural Club students. It was the first time SHINE had painted on a historical building. The Royal Theater operated from 1948 to 1966 as one of two African-American theaters in St. Petersburg, served as a youth center in the 1970s, and now functions as a Boys and Girls Club focused on Arts and Education. The mural weaves the BGC motto "Great Futures Start Here" through the design and depicts St. Pete native Angela Bassett with a movie reel, floating bottle caps, and popcorn (bottle caps were once used by children for the price of admission), NFL Griffin twins as a nod to the youth-center era of athletics, and Serena Williams, who trained in Florida. Two students working with a teacher appear inside the word "HERE," and a pelican finishes the design. Project partners included the SHINE Mural Festival, Creative Pinellas, and Boys and Girls Clubs of the Suncoast.\n\nMcAllister also led Gibbs High School\'s mural club in designing the Fighting Chance Mural at the Morean Arts Center, 719 Central Ave in downtown St. Pete, in partnership with the Leadership St. Pete Class of 2020. Funds raised supported the Fighting Chance Fund, which provided COVID-19 relief to brick-and-mortar businesses, home-based businesses, and individuals. The design featured a Florida-native passion vine, a young boy in a mask reading a book with doves emerging from its pages, a young girl in a mask with a smiley face holding a camera, a pelican in front of the St. Pete skyline, a "+" symbol doubling as a red cross for healthcare workers, and the Dr. King quote "What are you doing for others?" The palette was muted warm tones with shades of purple and cream. Other Gibbs Mural Club projects under McAllister include downtown Clearwater murals for the City of Clearwater, the Spectrum of the Arts PCCA mural at Gibbs High School, a Clearwater FL mural, and PCS student murals around Pinellas. JBM Studios is reachable at (727) 902-3228, based in St. Pete, Florida.\n\nDwayne Shepherd studied art, psychology, and theology at Austin Peay State University. He works mostly in acrylics, inspired by nature. He is a member of the Art Enclave Historic Kenwood and the Art Alliance of St. Petersburg, and serves as a docent at the Dali Museum. He paints impressionist landscapes from his Historic Kenwood home studio and participated in the Artist Enclave Studio Tour in Historic Kenwood in 2024. He also teaches art at Osceola Middle School in Seminole, where he founded a mural club that decorates the school\'s halls with scenes celebrating education, diversity, and inclusion. The club began as a campus beautification effort and shifted toward themes of community and social change during the pandemic. Student-painted works there include a butterfly encouraging students to fly, inspirational quotes, and a "superpowers" theme. Principal Dr. Solomon Lowery has spoken publicly about the program.\n\nFor SHINE 2024, Shepherd and McAllister worked in partnership with The Florida Art Educators Association (FAEA) to complete "The Importance of Art Education" at Bell Pharmacy, 3535 Central Avenue, as part of the festival\'s "Bright Spots" community murals. The piece was finished early to coincide with an FAEA convention in St. Petersburg the second week of October 2024, ahead of the festival\'s rescheduled dates following Hurricanes Helene and Milton. SHINE director Jenee Priebe oversaw the 10th-anniversary edition. Photo coverage by Sunsation Media.',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2024_SHINE_dwayne_shepherd_and_brian_mcallister','https://stpetecatalyst.com/brush-to-canvas-shine-and-other-art-news/','https://jbmstudios.com/','https://baynews9.com/fl/tampa/news/2021/05/01/osceola-middle-school-mural-club-gives-walls-a-splash-of-positivity-','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/brian-mcalister-gibbs-high-mural-club-and-boys-girls-club-members','https://jbmstudios.com/public-art/boys-and-girls-club/','https://audioboom.com/posts/8474674-ab-sees-dwayne-shepherd-at-artist-enclave-studio-tour-in-historic-kenwood-2024','https://ilovetheburg.com/new-fighting-chance-mural-designed-by-gibbs-high-students-arriving-in-st-pete/']},
  {id:23,a:'Emmanuel Jarus',t:'',loc:'226 5th Ave N',bldg:'Walker-Whitney',lat:27.777316,lng:-82.636864,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'youngjarus',bio:'He started by painting portraits on freight trains in Saskatchewan. They went viral on the internet before he ever had a gallery show.\n\nEmmanuel Jarus was born around 1992 in Regina, Saskatchewan. His grandmother, a watercolor painter, introduced him to art at four or five. He started graffiti around fourteen, left high school early, studied briefly at the Academy of Realist Art in Toronto, and is otherwise self-taught. He credits his grandmother, graffiti culture, and his mentor Kwest for his work ethic.\n\nHis style is post-Impressionist applied to massive walls — figurative portraits of people he meets in his travels, painted with basic hardware-store supplies: bucket paint, brushes, rollers, sometimes brooms. "I observe life, characters and paint people I meet," he says. "Each painting is a unique avatar for a city, a mood, memory or mostly a person."\n\nHe painted the United Nations 75th anniversary mural in Manhattan. A 17-story building in Boulogne-sur-Mer, France. A collaboration with Francisco Bosoletti for Urban Nation\'s "One Wall" project in Berlin. A glow-in-the-dark mural for Tourism Toronto. Festivals across Canada, the US, and Europe.\n\nFor SHINE 2024, he painted the Walker-Whitney building at 226 5th Ave North — 14 stories, roughly 180 by 60 feet. The largest mural in SHINE\'s ten-year history.',desc:'A 14-story photorealistic portrait of an enigmatic woman in flowing black garments spanning the entire west-facing wall of the Walker-Whitney building — the largest mural in SHINE\'s 10-year history at roughly 180 feet tall by 60 feet wide. The woman has long brown hair and one hand resting on her chest, gazing out with a serene, mysterious expression against a muted gray-teal background. Jarus intentionally keeps her identity a secret. The figure is a recurring subject he first painted on a seaside structure in Boulogne-sur-Mer, France. Painted with brushes, rollers, and bucket paint over 20 days — interrupted by Hurricane Milton\'s evacuation — in time for SHINE\'s 10th anniversary festival in November 2024.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/emmanuel-jarus.jpeg',from:'Toronto, Canada',aud:'https://cdn.muralquest.app/audio/23.mp3',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2024 selected artist; largest mural in SHINE festival history',fw:null,sbio:'He started by painting portraits on freight trains in Saskatchewan. They went viral on the internet before he ever had a gallery show.\n\nEmmanuel Jarus was born in 1992 in Regina, Saskatchewan, Canada. His grandmother, a watercolor painter, introduced him to art at four or five years old. He started graffiti around age fourteen, left high school early, and studied briefly at the Academy of Realist Art in Toronto. He is otherwise self-taught and credits his grandmother, graffiti culture, and his mentor Kwest, along with influences from realist masters and resources found on the internet, for his work ethic and approach. Jenee Priebe, Director of the SHINE Mural Festival, has said Jarus was first discovered because of graffiti art he created on the side of a train at age 19. He is based in Toronto and works internationally.\n\nHis style sits in the post-Impressionist tradition applied to massive walls — figurative portraits and figures of people he meets in his travels, painted with basic hardware-store supplies including bucket paint, brushes, rollers, and at times brooms dipped in paint. He combines acrylic, latex, and aerosol paint, and has been described as using classical oil-paint technique while working with spray paint. His focus is on color and the environment in which the figure sits. As he puts it, "I observe life, characters and paint people I meet. Each painting is a unique avatar for a city, a mood, memory or mostly a person." His narrative approach communicates a story of individual spirit relevant to the metabolism of the particular neighborhood where the wall sits.\n\nMajor projects include the United Nations 75th anniversary mural in Manhattan, painted with Street Art for Mankind as part of the #UN75Murals series and tied to the Zero Hunger campaign. He painted a 17-story building in Boulogne-sur-Mer, France for the Festival Street Art de la ville de Boulogne-sur-Mer in 2022, depicting a fair-skinned woman in black — the same enigmatic figure he later returned to on the Walker-Whitney Plaza wall in St. Petersburg. In 2018 he collaborated with Argentine artist Francisco Bosoletti on Urban Nation\'s Epic ONE WALL Crossover project in Berlin, where Jarus painted Apollo from Greek mythology and Bosoletti painted a portrait of Daphne in negative light. He has painted a glow-in-the-dark mural for Tourism Toronto. His "Zion" mural pays tribute to the Toronto graffiti and mural community organizer who founded The Bomb Shelter as a meeting space and fought to legitimize graffiti when the City of Toronto moved to cover it. He is part of the BUMP Festival roster in Calgary, with a wall at 602 11th Avenue SW.\n\nHe works with Street Art for Mankind, the nonprofit that uses public art to advance human rights and fight child trafficking, whose mural campaigns include Seattle World Cup 26, Los Angeles LA28 & Bigger Dreams, New York Climate Action, New York ILO100, New York Free Children, New York NRNY, Houston Big Art Bigger Change, Houston Legacies of Change, Houston World Cup 26, Houston I Choose to Rise, Houston John Biggers, Detroit Be the Change, USA Zero Hunger, France Impressionist Land, World Ecosystem Restoration, and World Generation Equality. He is also represented by LALAContemporary, where his work has appeared alongside artists including Maria Hupfield, Patricia Linenberg, Vivian Galban, Sofia Medici, Liliana Golubinsky, Camila Salcedo, Marcolina Dipierro, Ana Casal, Paulo Nazareth, Gabriel Chaile, Mariel Belanger, Aner Urra, Julia C. Parodi, Mark Bland, Claire Dunn, Carolina Quesada, Aaron Nachtailer, Débora Staiff, and Ximena Berecochea. His mural work is found in major cities and rural settings across Canada, the United States, Mexico, and Europe. URBAN NATION\'s museum profile lists him under the name Young Jarus.\n\nFor SHINE 2024, he was assigned the west-facing wall of the Walker-Whitney Plaza building at 226 5th Avenue North in St. Petersburg — 14 stories, approximately 180 feet tall and 60 feet wide. A board committee representing the Walker-Whitney building selected him after deliberating over numerous candidates. The mural is the largest in the SHINE festival\'s ten-year history. He used acrylic, brush on a roller, and at times a broom dipped in paint, and worked on a tight 20-day schedule before he had to be in Hawaii for another commitment. He laid down the painting grid and general sketch just before evacuating ahead of Hurricane Milton; the building and his sketch survived the storm, and he returned to finish the mural in time for the rescheduled SHINE festival on November 8 – 17, 2024. The figure is the same fair-skinned woman in black he had painted in Boulogne-sur-Mer in 2022; her identity is not disclosed. The festival was directed by Jenee Priebe, with Reflection St. Pete as corporate sponsor for the year. SHINE has produced more than 160 public artworks over a decade.\n\nCoverage includes Aaron Styza for I Love the Burg (November 5, 2024) and Bill DeYoung for the St. Pete Catalyst (November 18, 2024). Website: jarus.ca. Instagram: @youngjarus (also listed under "Young Jarus" on URBAN NATION\'s museum profile).',fn:['Started painting portraits on freight trains','From Saskatchewan — trained at OCAD Toronto','Renaissance technique meets street art'],src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2024_SHINE_emmanuel_jarus','https://ilovetheburg.com/jarus-largest-mural-in-st-pete/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/st-petes-mural-festival-shines-despite-hurricane-interference/','https://www.axios.com/local/tampa-bay/2024/11/22/shine-mural-festival-2024-st-pete','https://lalacontemporary.com/emmanuel-jarus','https://streetartmankind.org/artists/emmanuel-jarus/','https://urban-nation.com/artist/young-jarus/','https://yycbump.ca/artists/emmanuel-jarus/','https://instagram.com/youngjarus']},
  {id:24,a:'Fintan Magee',t:'',loc:'777 3rd Ave North',bldg:'Reflection St. Pete',lat:27.775266,lng:-82.644672,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'fintan_magee',bio:'He has been called the "Australian Banksy." He considers it lazy journalism.\n\nFintan Magee was born in 1985 in Lismore, New South Wales. His father, from Derry, Northern Ireland, is a sculptor. His mother, English-born, is an architect. He grew up around the political murals of the Irish tradition — they stayed with him. He earned his BFA from the Queensland College of Art at Griffith University in 2009 and started painting full-time the day he graduated. He was already active as a graffiti writer before university.\n\nHis style is social realist — large-scale photorealistic figures on buildings in isolated or abandoned urban locations, integrated with their surroundings. Climate change, endangered species, migration, refugee crises, loss, consumption. In Amman, Jordan, he painted "The Exile" — a refugee camp girl. In Bali, "Plastic Tiger" — endangered species and colonialism\'s environmental toll. In Berlin-Tegel, a 41.5-meter wall for Urban Nation\'s "One Wall" project. He has painted in over twenty countries.\n\nHis recent work experiments with a "beveled glass technique" — subjects appear out of focus, glass breaking and deconstructing images as allegory for memory and lost time. He is represented by Thinkspace Projects in Los Angeles.\n\nFor SHINE\'s 10th anniversary in 2024 — postponed by Hurricanes Helene and Milton — he painted a multi-story mural on the Reflection St. Pete building at 777 3rd Ave North.',desc:'A four-story mural in shades of blue and teal covers the face of the Reflection St. Pete building. The scene depicts objects and a figure submerged underwater — a glass perfume-style bottle, crystalline formations, and a pineapple float in rippling blue water. The photorealistic rendering captures light refracting through the water surface above. The building\'s "REFLECTION" signage sits just below the mural\'s lower edge.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/fintan-magee.jpeg',from:'Australia',aud:'https://cdn.muralquest.app/audio/24.mp3',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2024 selected artist',fw:[{name:'fintanmagee.com',url:'https://fintanmagee.com'}],sbio:'He has been called the "Australian Banksy." He considers it lazy journalism.\n\nFintan Magee was born in 1985 in Lismore, New South Wales, and grew up in Brisbane, where graffiti and train bombing were heavily common in the local scene. His father is from Derry, Northern Ireland, a sculptor; his mother is English-born, an architect. His maternal grandfather, originally from Woollahra in Sydney, ran an architectural practice in Ghana, West Africa. Magee attended Brisbane State High School, became active as a graffiti writer in Brisbane before university, and earned his Bachelor of Fine Arts at the Queensland College of Art (QCA), Griffith University, in 2009. He began painting full-time the year he graduated, and now lives and works in Sydney.\n\nHis practice is informed by a profound interest in political murals — Northern Irish in particular, by way of his father — and the socialist character of his public work draws on that tradition. He has described the work as combining journalistic elements with public art, and is driven by what he sees as the power of murals to communicate political and social viewpoints and to divide or unite communities. Recurring themes include diversity, migration and transition, waste and consumption, loss, climate change, and the environment. His large-scale figurative paintings are deeply integrated with the urban environment and exhibit an inherent sentimentality and softness, influenced in part by children\'s books and the Lowbrow art movement.\n\nNotable mural projects include "The Exile" in Amman, Jordan (2017), portraying a young girl from a local refugee camp looking toward better days; "Plastic Tiger" in Bali, Indonesia (2017), based on a European realist painting and drawing a line between the role of colonialism in damaging the environment and the modern tourism industry; the 41.5-meter "One Wall" mural at Neheimer Straße 2 in Berlin-Tegel for the Urban Nation Museum for Urban Contemporary Art (2015); a contribution to the open-air Djerbahood street-art museum in Tunisia (Mehdi Ben Cheikh, Albin Michel, 2015); and his early national breakthrough — a mural depicting Felix Baumgartner in Brisbane (2012). He participated in Toowoomba\'s "First Coat" program. His work appears in over twenty countries: in addition to Australia, completed projects span New Zealand, India, Indonesia, the United Kingdom, France, Italy, Portugal, Germany, Denmark, Norway, Sweden, Finland, the Netherlands, Austria, Ukraine, Russia, Turkey, the United States, Colombia, Argentina, Belarus, Spain, and Tahiti, with specific city walls in London, Vienna, Los Angeles, Miami, Atlanta, Buenos Aires, Copenhagen, Kyiv, Rome, and Dublin.\n\nHe is represented by Thinkspace Projects at 4217 W. Jefferson Blvd., Los Angeles, CA 90016. Solo exhibitions with Thinkspace and other galleries include "Big Dry" at Thinkspace in Los Angeles (2018), "Waves" at Mathgoth Gallery in Paris (2017), "Water World" at Backwoods Gallery in Melbourne (2016), and a 2015 Backwoods solo themed around his personal experience of the 2011 Brisbane floods. Painting works shown through Thinkspace include "The Lunchbreak" (2016), "Waiting Game" (2017, exhibited at SCOPE Basel and Moniker Art Fair London), "The Late Afternoon" (2018), "The Firestarter 1" and "The Firestarter 2" (2018), "The Waiting Place" (2018), "The Soloist 1" and "The Soloist 2" (2018), "Day Labourers" (2018, oil on white picket fence), "Haitian Workers" (2018, oil on white picket fence), "The Audience" (2018), "The Impossible Jump" (2018), "Head In The Sand" (2018), and "Exit Strategy Study I" (2019, included in the 15 Years of Thinkspace show). He was featured at the Long Beach Museum of Art in 2018.\n\nPress and publication credits include the Sydney Morning Herald, Juxtapoz Magazine, ABC News, The Australian, The Daily Telegraph (Sydney), the Brisbane Times, News.com.au, artnet News, Wikipedia, the Urban Contemporary Art Guide (2014 and 2015), Street Art Australia by Lou Chamberlain, Graffiti Art Magazine (France), Home & Design: Trends Magazine, Surface by Soren Solker (Denmark), World Sculpture News, Stuart Magazine, U Magazine, and Artmapp.\n\nFor the SHINE Mural Festival\'s 10th anniversary edition in 2024 — postponed from October to November in the wake of Hurricane Milton — Magee painted a multi-story mural on the Reflection St. Pete apartment complex at 777 3rd Avenue North, beside Mirror Lake. Reflection St. Pete was the festival\'s corporate sponsor that year, contributing $50,000 of approximately one-fifth of the event\'s annual budget. SHINE director Jenee Priebe oversaw the rescheduled festival, which produced fifteen new murals overall and included a 14-story Walker Whitney Plaza wall by Canadian muralist Emmanuel Jarus alongside Magee\'s piece.\n\nWebsite: fintanmagee.com. Instagram: @fintan_magee. Facebook: fintanmageeart.',fn:['Called the \'Australian Banksy\' — he disagrees','Paints water, flooding, climate themes','Giant photorealistic figures in urban settings'],src:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fintan_Magee','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2024_SHINE_fintan_magee','https://www.streetartbio.com/artists/fintan-magee-biography/','https://fintanmagee.com/about','https://thinkspaceprojects.com/artists/fintan-magee/','https://urban-nation.com/artist/fintan-magee/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/st-petes-mural-festival-shines-despite-hurricane-interference/']},
  {id:25,a:'Frankie Gonzalez',t:'',loc:'2606 Fairfield Ave South',bldg:'FloridaRAMA',lat:27.763343,lng:-82.668439,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'frankieg_art',bio:'He spent nine years in corporate shipping before coming back to paint. Twenty-five days after hand surgery, he painted Tom Brady.\n\nFrancisco "Frankie G" Gonzalez was born in Miami to parents from Denmark and Cuba. He started drawing comic book scenes as a kid, began painting seriously at fifteen, and attended the Atlanta College of Art. After school he landed in Salt Lake City and spent nine years in corporate transportation before returning to Florida, quitting the desk, and going full-time on art. He took a part-time job at a Tampa art store to learn the local scene while rebuilding.\n\nHis style blends traditional painting techniques with spray paint and latex — bold lines, vivid colors, compositions rooted in Latin heritage that range from realism to pop art. He orchestrates mural camps with local schools and aspiring young artists, and partners with Habitat for Humanity and the Down Syndrome Foundation of Florida.\n\nClients include ESPN, the Tampa Bay Buccaneers, the Miami Heat, Norwegian Cruise Lines, the Houston Astros, Ducati, and PODS Moving & Storage. He painted "Tampa\'s LIGHT" at Tampa Fire Rescue headquarters — a visual timeline of the department\'s 128-year history — and two murals for the Pinellas County Tax Collector.\n\nFor SHINE 2024, he painted at FloridaRAMA on Fairfield Avenue South.',desc:'A wide, energetic mural on the side of FloridaRAMA blending photorealism with street art. At center, a boy in a baseball cap and tan shirt launches a paper airplane, his outstretched arm dissolving into bold black calligraphic strokes and cartoon-style characters. Oversized pink peonies, sunflowers, and white daisies bloom along the bottom, while swirls of pink, yellow, orange, and turquoise fill the background. The composition moves from lush botanical realism on the left through the figurative center to playful abstraction on the right, creating a dynamic sense of childhood imagination taking flight.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/frankie-gonzalez.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2024 selected artist',fw:[{name:'frankiegart.com',url:'https://www.frankiegart.com'}],sbio:'He spent nine years in corporate shipping before coming back to paint. Twenty-five days after hand surgery, he painted Tom Brady.\n\nFrancisco "Frankie G" Gonzalez was born and raised in Miami, Florida, to parents who migrated from Denmark and Cuba at a young age. He started drawing as early as he can remember, began painting seriously around age fifteen during high school, and after graduating attended the Atlanta College of Art. He moved to Salt Lake City, Utah and lived there roughly nine years, falling into a corporate job in the shipping and transportation industry. Despite success in that field, he was unfulfilled and relocated to Tampa, Florida, where he has reinvented his career as an artist working with galleries, private collectors, and high-profile clients.\n\nAfter leaving corporate America in Tampa, he found himself with no immediate support behind his work and took a part-time job at a local art store to understand the city\'s arts opportunities and reinvest in supplies. Those connections proved instrumental as his career grew. He now identifies as a Hispanic mixed-media artist whose work combines traditional painting methods with an urban art twist, used across paintings, design, and murals. He blends spray paint with latex paint to build cohesive designs emphasizing vivid colors and a range of styles from realism to illustration. He juxtaposes figurative subject matter with interpretations of other styles, drawing inspiration from Street Art and Pop Art, building bright, bold colors around subject matter that always carries a narrative — whether a sports icon or something more personal — to create harmony between figures and abstraction.\n\nHis city commission portfolio includes work for the Cities of Tampa, Clearwater, Largo, and St. Petersburg. In February 2023, the City of Tampa Arts & Cultural Affairs Division dedicated "Tampa\'s LIGHT" — a site-specific mural commissioned at Tampa Fire Rescue headquarters showcasing a visual timeline of Tampa Fire Rescue\'s 128-year history, honoring the department\'s core values of integrity, honesty, loyalty, and trust. In December 2024, he installed two murals at the reimagined Pinellas County Tax Collector location at 13025 Starkey Road in Largo, a project produced in partnership with Creative Pinellas and Tax Collector Adam Ross. The Tax Collector\'s office also runs the Kids Tag Art Pinellas program, which raises funds for local elementary art classrooms.\n\nCommercial collaborations include ESPN, the Tampa Bay Buccaneers, the Miami Heat, Norwegian Cruise Lines, the Houston Astros, Ducati, PODS Moving & Storage, and Habitat for Humanity. He has painted Super Bowl champion Tom Brady, completed roughly 25 days after hand surgery — a project he cites as the kind of "once seemed impossible" work he aims for. He dedicates time to local schools, orchestrating mural camps with aspiring young artists and community partners to add public art to their communities. He partners with Habitat for Humanity and the Down Syndrome Foundation of Florida to give back through art and empower his community.\n\nFor SHINE 2024 he painted at FloridaRAMA on Fairfield Avenue South in St. Petersburg. He was profiled by VoyageTampa in August 2022 ("Life & Work with Francisco \'Frankie G\' Gonzalez") and is featured on Creative Pinellas. His work has been described as boldly lined and culture-infused, with rich color stories and compositions rooted in his Latin heritage and Florida upbringing — a steady presence across Tampa Bay through murals, paintings, sculptural objects, custom apparel, and limited-edition merchandise. He offers payment plans on listed and commissioned work and pushes into high-end spaces and major projects nationwide.\n\nWebsite: frankiegart.com. Instagram: @frankieg_art. Facebook: frankiegartwork.',fn:['Nine years in corporate shipping before art','Self-taught, learned watching friends paint','Local St. Pete artist'],src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2024_SHINE_frankie_gonzalez','https://www.frankiegart.com/','https://voyagetampa.com/interview/life-work-with-francisco-frankie-g-gonzalez-of-tampa-fl/','https://creativepinellas.org/artist/frankie-g/','https://www.tampa.gov/news/tampa-fire-rescue-dedicate-new-mural-tfr-headquarters-115516','https://www.frankiegart.com/about','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/frankie-g-brings-vibrant-public-art-to-pinellas-county-tax-collectors-reimagined-space/']},
  {id:26,a:'George F Baker III',t:'At Your Show',loc:'620 1st Ave South',bldg:'Studio@620',lat:27.770121,lng:-82.642003,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'gfb3',bio:'He painted a mural on ESPN\'s College GameDay. Live, on air.\n\nGeorge F. Baker III grew up in Detroit, raised in Atlanta. He earned a BFA in Graphic Design from Georgia State University in 2015, worked as a creative marketing manager at a screen-printing company, and transitioned to full-time muralist and illustrator. His first mural was in 2018 at Switchyards Downtown Club in Atlanta. He has painted 25-plus walls since, across Miami, Atlanta, Chicago, and beyond.\n\nHis style is playful and cartoon-inspired — larger-than-life characters in bright bold color with positive messaging. "Take Care." "Be You." "Be A Light." He cites Keith Haring, Nickelodeon cartoons, Pokemon trading cards, and Dragon Ball Z as influences. He is creative director of Foster, a studio-collective "striving to make people unafraid to play." He describes himself as a sneakerhead with 78-plus pairs.\n\nThe client list reads like a brand deck: Nike, Adidas, Jordan Brand, Levi\'s, ESPN, NFL, Cartoon Network, Adult Swim, Adobe, Mailchimp, Red Bull, Patagonia, Mercedes-Benz. His "Be Playful" project for Mailchimp was longlisted for the 2021 World Illustration Awards. His dream project is designing a Nike sneaker to benefit L.E.A.D., a nonprofit mentoring young Black boys in Atlanta.\n\nFor SHINE 2024, he painted at Studio@620 on 1st Avenue South.',desc:'A full-building mural on a black background packed with a joyful crowd of cartoon-style characters rendered in bold pinks, teals, purples, blues, and warm browns. The figures are chunky and expressive with exaggerated grins, wild hair, and animated poses — dancing, laughing, taking selfies, playing instruments, and celebrating. Across the top in large bubble letters with teal outlines and cream fill reads "WE\'RE ALL FRONT ROW AT YOUR SHOW." White diamond-shaped dots scatter across the black background like confetti or stars. The GFB3 tag appears in a small heart-shaped logo in the lower right corner. The piece wraps around garage doors and windows, covering the entire rear wall of Studio@620.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/george-f-baker-iii.jpeg',from:'Atlanta, GA',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'2021 World Illustration Awards longlist (Be Playful for Mailchimp); Midtown Alliance Heart of the Arts Studio Residency; SHINE 2024 selected artist',fw:[{name:'gfbthree.com',url:'https://www.gfbthree.com'}],sbio:'He painted a mural on ESPN\'s College GameDay. Live, on air. The August 28, 2021 segment also flagged him as Midtown Alliance\'s artist-in-residence and a Georgia State alum.\n\nGeorge F. Baker III — also known as GFB3 — was born in Omaha, Nebraska, grew up in Detroit, lived briefly in Hilton Head, South Carolina, and settled in Atlanta in the early 2000s. He earned a BFA in Graphic Design from Georgia State University in 2015. After graduation he worked as a creative marketing manager at Danger Press, an Atlanta screen-printing company, before transitioning to full-time mural and illustration work. His first mural, painted in 2018, went up inside Switchyards Downtown Club, an Atlanta coworking space. He has completed 25-plus walls since across Atlanta, Miami, Chicago, Panama City, and Boulder.\n\nHis path to art ran through an early elementary school art teacher who told him he could not become an artist because he did not draw realistically. His parents told him to challenge incorrect assumptions, and at the library he discovered Keith Haring — the first work that gave him permission to develop his own voice. Other influences include Piet Parra, KAWS, Adult Swim, Nickelodeon, Toonami, Cartoon Network\'s Dexter\'s Laboratory and Adventure Time, Pokemon trading cards, the Japanese anime Dragon Ball Z, and early-2000s streetwear brands The Hundreds, Huff, Crooks & Castles, and Perks & Mini. He has collected the annual street culture book All Gone since 2013.\n\nHis style is playful and cartoon-inspired — larger-than-life characters in bright bold color paired with positive messaging. Recurring text includes "Take Care," "Be You," "Be A Light," and "Yup! Things Will Be Better." He pairs typography with imagery to communicate; the typography skill carries over from his graphic design career.\n\nDocumented Atlanta murals include "Make Friends" on the Atlanta BeltLine Westside Trail (2019), "Creating You" on the MET Atlanta building at Murphy Avenue and Ralph David Abernathy Boulevard (2019), "Everybody" on Carroll Street in Cabbagetown as part of the Stack Squares Mural Project (2019), and a passion project titled "Spreading Good, Being Good" on an old moving truck on Metropolitan Parkway in the Adair Park neighborhood. A larger Adair Park II piece for SafeAuto — his second project with the agency Muros — is three stories tall and almost 50 yards wide. He also collaborated with German artist 1010 on a rooftop optical-illusion mural at Peachtree Center downtown, in partnership with the nonprofit Living Walls, the City Speaks, and Florida-based International Corporate Art. Beyond Atlanta, he painted "Keep Dancing" in Panama City, Florida and a 2022 mural at 1535 Spruce Street in Boulder, Colorado for Street Wise Arts — an eTown Hall-inspired piece featuring three figures playing keys, guitar, and saxophone, with a robot tagged on the prepped wall incorporated into the design. For SHINE 2024 in St. Petersburg, he painted at Studio@620 on 1st Avenue South.\n\nIn 2021 he was selected as one of six artists for Midtown Alliance\'s Heart of the Arts Studio Residency Program, with studio space at Dewberry Capital\'s Peachtree Pointe office building — his first dedicated studio. His first solo show, "Little Reminders," opened at Cat Eye Creative on November 3, 2023, featuring smaller canvas works, an interactive installation where visitors recorded their own reminders, printed shirts, stickers, merch, and a 3D version of one of his illustrations. He also designed an Atlanta Magazine cover, advertisements for Cartoon Network and Adult Swim, a Slutty Vegan storefront, and event poster artwork titled "MARTA" for the Atlanta rap duo Paid in AmeriKKKa.\n\nThe client list reads like a brand deck: Adidas, Adult Swim, Atlanta United, ESPN, Georgia Power, Lululemon, Mailchimp, Mercedes-Benz, Nike, NFL, ONE Musicfest, Patagonia, Topo Chico, Verizon, Target, Warby Parker, Cartoon Network, Adobe, Red Bull, Levi\'s, and Jordan Brand. His "Be Playful" project for Mailchimp, commissioned by Linda McNeil, was longlisted for the 2021 World Illustration Awards in the Advertising Professional category.\n\nHe is the Creative Director of Foster, a studio-collective "striving to make people unafraid to play." He describes himself as a sneakerhead with 78-plus pairs. His dream project is designing a Nike sneaker to benefit L.E.A.D., an Atlanta nonprofit that mentors young Black boys through baseball, life, and leadership skills.\n\nContact: George@GFBThree.com, 678-923-8666. Website: gfbthree.com. Instagram: @gfb3.',src:['https://roughdraftatlanta.com/2023/11/01/artist-george-f-baker-iii-taps-into-memories-personal-history-at-cat-eye-creative/','https://www.artsatl.org/today-in-street-art-2019-has-been-a-very-good-year-for-muralist-george-f-baker-iii/','https://creativepro.com/interview-with-george-f-baker-iii-designer-and-illustrator/','https://www.midtownatl.com/post/heart-of-the-arts-meet-muralist-george-f-baker-iii','https://www.gfbthree.com/about','https://www.muros.com/george-f-baker-iii-artist-feature/','https://streetwisearts.org/george-f-baker-iii-2022','https://worldillustrationawards.com/projects/george-f-baker-iii-be-playful']},
  {id:188,a:'Happy Mural Project',t:'Compassion In Action',loc:'929 1st Ave N',bldg:'',lat:27.772397,lng:-82.647942,y:2024,cat:'commercial',ig:'thehappymuralproject',bio:'Paint-by-numbers, but the wall is 16 feet tall and 105 feet long. Six hundred people came out over Mother\'s Day weekend 2024 to paint it.\n\nGulf Coast Jewish Family and Community Services commissioned "Compassion In Action" for their 50th anniversary. Two brown hands cradle a cluster of bright blue morpho butterflies against flowing green leaves. The location was deliberate too — the west side of the building that housed Dr. Philip Benjamin\'s optometrist practice, built in 1969. Benjamin was a longtime supporter of St. Petersburg Junior College and pushed for affirmative action there before passing away in 1998. His widow Marilyn picked up a brush.\n\nAlyssa Marie founded the Happy Mural Project after moving from Colorado to St. Petersburg in 2017. She holds a BFA from Metropolitan State University of Denver. The project\'s signature is exactly what it sounds like: she designs the wall, breaks it into numbered sections, and runs paint-by-numbers events where hundreds of volunteers each take a 30-minute slot. Children paint the lower sections; volunteers comfortable on ladders paint the higher ones.',desc:'A large community mural on a long wall, 16 feet tall by 105 feet long. A pair of brown hands cradles a cluster of bright blue morpho butterflies against a background of flowing green leaves and fronds. A banner at the top left reads "Compassion In Action" in script lettering with "GulfCoastJFCS.org" below it. The palette is soft greens, browns, and vivid blue. Painted by roughly 600 volunteers over Mother\'s Day weekend 2024.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/happy-mural-compassion.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'Commissioned by Gulf Coast Jewish Family and Community Services for their 50th anniversary.',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'Paint-by-numbers, but the wall is 16 feet tall and 105 feet long. Six hundred people came out over Mother\'s Day weekend 2024 to paint it.\n\n"Compassion In Action" was commissioned by Gulf Coast Jewish Family and Community Services (Gulf Coast JFCS), a nonprofit that helps people "of all ages, faiths, cultures, and lifestyles," to celebrate the organization\'s 50th anniversary theme. The mural is located at 929 1st Ave N in St. Petersburg, on the west side of the building that housed Dr. Philip Benjamin\'s optometrist practice, built in 1969. Benjamin (who died in 1998 at age 73) was a longtime supporter of the then-St. Petersburg Junior College, served as vice chairman and chairman on the board of trustees where he worked to establish affirmative action policies, and helped create the college\'s Health Education Center. The Social Arts Building at SPJC was named in his honor in 1984. His widow Marilyn Benjamin painted glasses on the mural during the volunteer weekend, a nod to her family\'s medical practice.\n\nThe mural\'s symbolism is deliberate: the lotus is known as the flower of compassion, and as the butterflies are released from one hand they connect with the flower — each needs the other to continue on. Two hands reach toward each other; one releases butterflies that gently flutter toward the lotus flower clutched in the other\'s hand. An inscription reads "Compassion in action."\n\nThe project was cosponsored by the Tampa Bay Rays, Amuni Financial, the Benjamin Family Foundation, and Frank Edgar of Creative Catering Company. Approximately 600 people, including relatives of Dr. Benjamin, former mayors, and community volunteers, came out over Mother\'s Day weekend to work on the mural. Raymond, the Rays mascot, also painted on the wall. Gulf Coast JFCS Communications Manager Hana Cowart oversaw the project on the org\'s side.\n\nAlyssa Marie founded the Happy Mural Project after relocating from Colorado to St. Petersburg in 2017. She holds a BFA in Communication Design with an emphasis on Fine Art from Metropolitan State University of Denver and has done paint-by-number community murals across the nation. She started painting murals on her own and noticed that people were always interested in the process — she remembered the paint-by-number books she did as a kid and built the format around that. "When I started this, I just wanted to find a way to get everybody involved," she told Northeast Journal in 2024. The format: she designs the wall and team breaks it into numbered sections; volunteers come for 30-minute slots, get a numbered cup of paint, and find the corresponding section. Children paint the lower sections; volunteers comfortable on ladders paint the higher ones.\n\nMarie also works to find the right design to fit with the organization that sponsors it. Gulf Coast JFCS\'s mission is to help vulnerable individuals find safety, comfort, and a path toward a fulfilling life — they reach 30,000 people annually throughout the greater Tampa Bay area. They received a $450,000 grant renewal from the NFL\'s Inspire Change social justice initiative for the Community Assistance and Life Liaison (CALL) program, which trains social workers and mental health professionals to respond to nonviolent and noncriminal 911 calls.\n\nCoverage in Northeast Journal (July 24, 2024, by Shelly Wilson). Project info at thehappymuralproject.com and gulfcoastjewishfamilyandcommunityservices.org.',fn:['600 volunteers painted this over Mother\'s Day weekend 2024','Commissioned for Gulf Coast JFCS\'s 50th anniversary','16 feet tall by 105 feet long — paint-by-numbers format'],src:['https://northeastjournal.org/inspired-by-the-mission-hundreds-help-paint-community-mural/','https://thehappymuralproject.com','https://gulfcoastjewishfamilyandcommunityservices.org']},
  {id:27,a:'Kris Markovich & Johnny Vitale',t:'',loc:'501 16th St S',bldg:'Anchor Skate Supply',lat:27.76560333,lng:-82.65497,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'kris_markovich',bio:'He turned pro for Dogtown Skateboards in 1990. Thirty years later, he paints the walls.\n\nKris Markovich is a professional skateboarder who rode for Dogtown, appeared on the cover of Transworld Magazine, featured in Tony Hawk\'s video games, and was named one of the 15 Most Influential People in the Tampa Art Scene by MergeCulture. He is self-taught as an artist, coming up through skateboard graphics and graffiti. His largest mural is at Harrah\'s Casino in San Diego. He has painted from the US West Coast to Dubai.\n\nJohnny Vitale is the co-founder of Vitale Bros, painting Tampa Bay walls since 1992. Together they are Anchor Skate Supply\'s resident artists.\n\nFor SHINE 2024, they collaborated on a mural at 501 16th Street South (Anchor Skate Supply), inspired by the Tampa Bay Rays City Connect uniforms, and produced a limited-edition SHINE mural skateboard called "L\'Orange."',desc:'A bold, graphic mural wrapping around the entire exterior of Anchor Skate Supply. The building is painted in a black base with vibrant tropical and tiki-inspired imagery. A large pink and green sugar skull or tiki mask dominates the left side, while cartoon-style palm trees and waves in neon green and teal sweep across the front. A character with sunglasses peers over the roofline. The right side features more black-and-white tribal/tiki patterns and the text "OVER SPRAY ART COLLECTIVE." The whole building reads as a single immersive artwork, turning the skate shop into a neighborhood landmark.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/kris-markovich-johnny-vitale.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'Inspired by the Tampa Bay Rays\' City Connect uniforms, which drew from St. Pete\'s skate culture, neon signs, tattoo culture, and street art.',maw:'',aaw:'Markovich: Transworld Magazine cover; 15 Most Influential Tampa Art Scene (MergeCulture); SHINE 2024',fw:[{name:'krismarkovich.com',url:'https://krismarkovich.com'}],sbio:'He turned pro for Dogtown Skateboards in 1990. Thirty years later, he paints the walls.\n\nKris Markovich is a world-renowned professional skateboarder and artist working since the early 1990s. He grew up in Georgia, then moved to Gulf Breeze, Florida, where he got his first board and started skating vert on a backyard 8-foot ramp. He shifted to street skating in his teens after breaking his elbow on a kickflip, then moved to Pensacola and was already sponsored by 11th grade. He moved to California with his mother, sister, and Ted Newsome, briefly stayed in San Francisco, then settled in Carlsbad through Steve Ortega and Dave Crabb in the San Diego area.\n\nHe turned pro for Dogtown Skateboards in 1990 after taking second at the NSA Finals, replacing Justin Girard who had left for New Deal. Across his career he has ridden for Epic, Toxic, Dogtown, G&S, 101, Color, Prime, Element, Foundation, Hollywood, Blind, Crimson, Given, and Elephant — fourteen board sponsors by his own count. He filmed video parts for G&S Footage Tapes (Stun, Winona Ryders), 101\'s Falling Down, the Union Wheels Right to Skate video (filmed by Jamie Mosberg), the Color Video (Bigmouth Strikes Again by the Tree People), Prime\'s Fight Fire with Fire, and Element\'s Third Eye View, plus Foundation\'s Art Bars. He worked with filmer Ricki Bedenbaugh and photographer Daniel Sturt on the iconic Water Gap ollie and Carlsbad High School gap shots. He worked alongside Natas Kaupas at 101, Rick Howard at Blockhead, Mike Vallely at Elephant, Tod Swank at Foundation/Tum Yeto, Steve Rocco at World Industries, and art director Milo at Tum Yeto on Hollywood. With Paul Schmitt at PS Stix he co-developed Featherlight construction, which pushed Element to #1 in board sales. He has done skateboard graphics for many companies and is known for the Tony Hawk\'s Trick Tip video in 2000 and the Tony Hawk\'s Pro Skateboard Tour 2000–2003. His Transworld Magazine cover featured his iconic artwork drawn on it.\n\nHe is self-taught as an artist, coming up through skateboard graphics and graffiti, and his murals run from the West Coast of America to Dubai, UAE. His largest mural is at Harrah\'s Casino in San Diego, California. He sells artwork through his own website, owns a griptape company called Black Griptape Company, has a pro deck model with his artwork on Technê Skateboards, rides for his local Saint Petersburg skate shop Anchor Skate Supply with a guest artist board he designed, and has a signature Kris Markovich wheel made by Acid Chemical Company. He had a Thrasher Magazine Mail Drop Book release. MergeCulture named him one of the 15 Most Influential People in the Tampa Art Scene of the last two decades, citing his collaborations with Skatepark of Tampa for Tampa Am and Tampa Pro, where he has painted the pro course and shown alongside Neckface, Stephen Palladino, Lucas Beaufort, Shepard Fairey, Jeremy Fish, and Lance Mountain in Ybor City and at Transitions Art Gallery.\n\nJohnny Vitale co-founded the Vitale Bros. with his brother Paul in Tampa Bay in 1992, following in the artistic tradition of their grandparents. They started by hand-lettering signs and painting storefront windows, then landed their first mural after approaching a restaurant owner with a sketch on a paper napkin. Youngest brother Joey joined soon after, and the crew painted murals and billboards for businesses throughout the bay area. A dabble with commercial design work gave them their first warehouse space, which became their studio and the site of group art, music, and fashion shows in the late \'90s and early 2000s — a marketing machine that fostered St. Petersburg\'s emerging art scene by giving exposure to other local artists. Today Johnny and Paul work with Nick and Jason Kekllas, Bruce Statton, and Jake Gerow under the Vitale Bros. moniker. The crew has created campaigns for the Tampa Bay Rays and L\'Oréal\'s SalonCentric national locations, and has executed murals for the Tampa Bay Buccaneers, many local businesses, and private residences. Their skill set also covers commercial exterior painting, fine art reproductions, and interior faux finishing. Johnny has more than 30 years of painting experience and blends fine art with pop art in his work. The Vitale Bros. are based in St. Petersburg, FL, and have had a major hand in St. Petersburg becoming a world-class arts destination. Their tagline is "Elevating Spaces Through Art."\n\nTogether Markovich and Vitale are Anchor Skate Supply\'s resident artists. For SHINE 2024 they collaborated on a mural at 501 16th Street South — Anchor Skate Supply — inspired by the Tampa Bay Rays City Connect uniforms. They produced a limited-edition SHINE mural skateboard called "L\'Orange" as part of Anchor Skate Supply and SHINE St. Petersburg Mural Festival\'s collaboration honoring the festival\'s 10th anniversary. The deck series featured five SHINE artists — Kris Markovich, Johnny Vitale, Jared Wright, Hannah Eddy, and Max Sansing — crafted with premium Rock Hard Great Lakes Maple. Markovich, Vitale, and Wright signed their skateboard designs.\n\nFind Kris Markovich at krismarkovich.com. Find the Vitale Bros. at vitalebrothers.com.',src:['https://krismarkovich.com/pages/b-i-o','https://vitalebrothers.com/about/','https://thegabber.com/anchor-skate-supplys-shine-mural-skateboards/','https://chromeballincident.blogspot.com/2014/06/chrome-ball-interview-74-kris-markovich.html','https://thegabber.com/anchor-skate-supplys-limited-edition-shine-mural-skateboards/','https://mergeculture.com/2023/09/17/15-most-influential-people-in-the-tampa-art-scene-for-the-last-two-decades/']},
  {id:28,a:'Naomi Haverland',t:'Bright Spot',loc:'700 2nd Ave NE',bldg:'Discovery Center on the Pier',lat:27.773519,lng:-82.627852,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'naomihaverland',bio:'The microscopic creatures swirling on this wall? She studied them live, under microscopes inside the Discovery Center — then painted them on the outside.\n\nNaomi Haverland grew up in Denver, lived in Seattle, and is now based in Florida. She has been painting since childhood and doing chalk art specifically since 2011. No formal art education — she credits mentors and the broader mural community, and emphasizes paying it forward.\n\nHer specialty is hyper-realistic large-scale portraits — candid, exaggerated human expression with unexpected props and pop-culture references. She favors children and elderly as subjects and adds humor wherever the image allows. She also creates anamorphic pavement illusions. She draws inspiration from "things some people might think of as defective" — distinctive body types, recycled materials, religious themes.\n\nClients include Microsoft, Amazon, Harley Davidson, Skittles, 7-Eleven, Blick Art Supply, and Uber. She has been invited to chalk art festivals in Germany, Canada, and Mexico. Her signature piece, "Ring Pop" (2014), features her daughter Roxanne holding a glowing green candy ring.\n\nFor SHINE 2024, she partnered with Tampa Bay Watch to paint a ring of plankton species found in Tampa Bay at the Discovery Center on the St. Pete Pier.',desc:'A swirling oval wreath of brilliantly colored microscopic plankton organisms on a deep teal-blue background. The composition features hyper-realistic renderings of shrimp larvae, diatoms, radiolaria, copepods, and other planktonic creatures, painted with luminous, almost bioluminescent coloring. Bubbles drift through the arrangement. Each organism is rendered at enormous scale with scientific precision and vivid detail, transforming Tampa Bay\'s invisible marine biodiversity into monumental wall art. Signed @NaomiHaverland with the SHINE Mural Festival logo.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/naomi-haverland.jpeg',from:'Orlando, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'7 awards at Denver Chalk Art Festival (including Best Color 2012, People\'s Choice 2013); SHINE 2024 selected artist',fw:[{name:'naomihaverland.com',url:'https://www.naomihaverland.com'}],sbio:'She is self-taught. She has seven awards from the Denver Chalk Art Festival alone.\n\nNaomi Haverland grew up in Denver, Colorado, lived in Seattle, and is now based in Florida — Maitland and Titusville have both been listed as her home base. She has been painting for as long as she can remember and has been doing chalk specifically since 2011. No formal art education. She credits art mentors and the broader artist community, and emphasizes paying it forward. Her primary mediums are acrylics, oils, and chalk. In the summer she focuses on oil painting in her studio.\n\nHer specialty is humorous, hyper-realistic large-scale portraits — candid, exaggerated human expression rendered in bold color, often with unexpected props and pop-culture references. She also creates anamorphic "3D" pavement illusions. She favors children and elderly subjects and adds humor wherever the image allows. She draws inspiration from "things some people might think of as defective" — distinctive body types, recycled materials, religious themes, her own children, garbage, and Jesus. She belongs to the Scum of the Earth Church and describes her art as an extension of her relationship with God. One of her recurring models is Little Miss Firefly, who is 21 inches tall. Another is Marlene, whom she met while painting a mural with the disabled employees of ARC Thrift Stores for an auction.\n\nHer chalk art has won seven awards at the Denver Chalk Art Festival in Colorado, including Best Color in 2012 and People\'s Choice in 2013. She returned to the festival in 2014 (held May 31 and June 1, sponsored by ARC Thrift Stores) to draw Marlene. She has been invited to national and international chalk art festivals in Germany, Canada, and Mexico. Commercial clients include Microsoft, Amazon, Harley Davidson, Skittles, 7-Eleven, Blick Art Supply, and Uber.\n\nHer signature piece, "Ring Pop" (2014), features her daughter Roxanne posing larger-than-life with a glowing green candy ring. Roxanne is her "mini-muse" — the artist has said she has never done a literal self-portrait but uses portraits of her daughter to similar effect. She also has a son, who is less interested in art. In May 2014, T Gallery Denver — half gallery, half teahouse — featured her child-inspired paintings in a Mother\'s Day exhibit titled "A Mother\'s Muse," opening Friday, May 2 with a reception from 6 to 9 p.m. Ten-year-old Roxanne ran a portrait booth at the opening. The show included a new portrait of Dame Edna, the character created by Australian comedian Barry Humphries, with her signature "wisteria hue" hair and glasses, alongside a "face furniture" station where attendees could create their own glasses.\n\nOutside of paintings she works in recycled materials — outdoor installations made from tires and hubcaps, an ongoing "cap fence" of plastic bottle caps screwed to her fence, and a constantly evolving treehouse built with her kids from free Craigslist finds.\n\nMural and festival credits include "Texas Rose" for the Arts Center of Brazos Valley (ACBV) in College Station, Texas — a rose paired with a composite bug rendered in her signature jeweled style with metallic and iridescent textures, sited on the wall of a flower shop. For Paint The Park in Allen Park (a community-driven mural project run by the Allen Park DDA that has produced eight murals downtown), she painted "Nuts Now" — a young astronaut surrounded by swirling candy and sweets on the side of the Nuts Now building. She is part of the Beyond Walls roster, with a wall at 25 Washington Square in Haverhill, Massachusetts. In May 2023 she was featured in the Sioux City Journal article "Artist adds finishing touches to Heidman Law Firm mural." Earlier press includes "Giant Tiger Mural Roars a Colorful Bouquet of Flowers" in My Modern Met (December 2020), "Meet Naomi Haverland" in Voyagela.com (September 2019), "Six Questions with Naomi Haverland" by Helikon Gallery (Helikongallery.com, February 2016), and the Denver Westword profile "Naomi Haverland on the strange things that inspire her" by Jamie Siebrase (April 28, 2014).\n\nFor SHINE 2024 she partnered with Tampa Bay Watch to paint a ring of plankton species found in Tampa Bay at the Discovery Center on the St. Pete Pier.\n\nWebsite: naomihaverland.com. Instagram: @naomihaverland. Facebook: NaomiHaverlandchalkart.',fn:['Used microscopes inside the Discovery Center to study live plankton','Known for hyper-realistic portraits and anamorphic optical illusions','Ringling College of Art and Design graduate'],src:['https://www.naomihaverland.com/about-contact','https://www.acbv.org/meet-the-artist-naomi-haverland/','https://beyondwalls.org/artists/naomi-haverland/','https://www.paintthepark.com/naomi-haverland','https://www.westword.com/arts/naomi-haverland-on-the-strange-things-that-inspire-her-5779280']},
  {id:29,a:'NeSpoon',t:'',loc:'601 24th St South',bldg:'Cemex',lat:27.764297,lng:-82.666143,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'nes.nespoon',bio:'Her thirty-year project is making porcelain petals by hand from the leftover clay of her lace work — fifty kilograms a year, sitting on the streets of every city she visits. She is targeting fifteen hundred kilograms for the final exhibition in 2042.\n\nNeSpoon is her legal name. It is on her ID — she changed it, taking the name from "there is no spoon" in The Matrix. She grew up partly in West Berlin in the late 1980s, crossing Checkpoint Charlie to attend Polish school in East Berlin. She wanted to be a real artist from age five. No formal art degree — she taught herself ceramics after hitting a dead end with abstract oil paintings.\n\nIn 2009, she started placing ceramic lace objects on walls in public spaces in Warsaw. That became the work. She researches each city\'s lace heritage, visits local museums, and uses region-specific motifs to create large-scale spray-painted murals, ceramic installations, stencil work, and crocheted pieces. She has painted in over forty countries across five continents. "Instead of fighting evil," she says, "it\'s better to spread good."\n\nIn 2020, she covered the facade of the Cité de la Dentelle et de la Mode in Calais — the city\'s lace museum — with a monumental mural using an 1894 archival pattern. She has shown at the Louvre-Lens, EXPO Dubai 2020, the European Parliament, and Urban Nation in Berlin. She co-founded an NGO that lobbied the Polish Parliament for seven years to pass legislation controlling outdoor advertising.\n\nThis wall went up for SHINE 2024 on the Cemex building. She was meant to arrive the day Hurricane Milton made landfall; when she got here a month later, she worked the storm into the design — lace holding the left and center, the hurricane\'s winds spinning out of it on the right.',desc:'A wide mural on the Cemex building featuring NeSpoon\'s signature lace patterns in white and pale blue against a deep plum-purple background. Intricate medallions with floral and geometric motifs occupy the left and center, their delicate threadwork rendered with illusory three-dimensional depth through careful shading and layering. The right side transitions into flowing, organic swirls — the artist\'s rendering of Hurricane Milton, which struck the region a month before she painted. The effect transforms the industrial concrete wall into what appears to be an enormous draped lace textile. Signed "NeSpoon" in the lower left corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/nespoon.jpeg',from:'Warsaw, Poland',aud:'',insp:'NeSpoon was due to land in Florida on October 10, 2024 to paint for SHINE. The night before, Hurricane Milton came ashore near Siesta Key and tore across the Tampa Bay region — the strongest hurricane the area had seen, by her account. The festival was postponed. She arrived a month later to find the only traces left were broken branches in the streets, and decided to put the storm into the wall: lace medallions hold the left and center, then unravel into the hurricane\'s swirling winds across the right side. "I spent a wonderful week here, and Florida truly showed its best side," she said, thanking Jenee Priebe for the invitation and the SHINE team for the seamless production.',maw:'',aaw:'Artaq InSitu prize, France (2011, 2012); URBAN NATION Fresh A.I.R. Artist in Residence, Berlin (2018); SHINE 2024 selected artist',fw:[{name:'nespoon.art',url:'https://nespoon.art'},{name:'Instagram reel — painting the St. Petersburg mural',url:'https://www.instagram.com/reel/DCwsjteTyEa/'}],sbio:'NeSpoon is her legal name. It is on her ID. She changed it. The name comes from the line "there is no spoon" in The Matrix — the idea that the barriers are mostly in your head. When you realize this, she says, you can quit your corporate job and start painting in the streets, as she did.\n\nShe grew up partly in West Berlin in the late 1980s while the Wall still stood, attending a Polish school in East Berlin and crossing Checkpoint Charlie several times a week. Her parents did not want her to lose touch with Polish culture. She passed the Berlin Wall daily — the longest art gallery in the world at that time — and her interest in painting on walls started then. She wanted to be a "real artist" from age five, while other girls dreamed of being princesses. She has no formal art degree. Before street art, she painted dark, sad, abstract oil paintings on canvas for years, hit a dead end, took a two-year break, and turned to ceramics. At the Academy she had taken clay sculpture classes, but she became serious about ceramics through porcelain. She likes to say clay is kindergarten and porcelain is university.\n\nIn 2009, she started placing ceramic objects with lace motifs on walls in public spaces in Warsaw. Pressing lace into fresh clay is one of the oldest ways to decorate ceramics worldwide, and she realized the patterns were beautiful on their own — they did not need a cup or a plate to be appreciated. When she posted photos online, they went viral, and invitations to street art festivals started arriving. She has since worked in over 100 cities, in 40 countries on 5 continents, and has been published in The Guardian, Huffington Post, Elle, and Graffitiart Magazine. Her practice spans murals, ceramics, sculpture, jewellery, stencils, screen printing, crocheted webbing, and video installations. For illegal graffiti she uses stencils. For murals, she sketches freehand or uses a video projector — she does not fetishize freehand painting.\n\nHer process is in situ. When she arrives in a new city, she researches local lace heritage, visits museums and city archives, and finds local lace associations or active lace-makers. If she cannot find anything, she walks the neighborhood, knocks on doors, and asks residents for laces from home. She always finds something. In 2020, she painted the facade of the Cité de la Dentelle et de la Mode in Calais — the city\'s lace and fashion museum, housed in a restored 19th-century factory — using a machine-lace pattern from 1894 that she chose from the museum\'s archive. Calais once employed 40,000 people in lace-making; the master weavers controlled 11,000 threads at once and took up to 12 years to learn. In 2019 she painted in Le Locle, Switzerland, the home of Tissot, Zenith, Ulysse Nardin, and Mont Blanc — a town that in the 19th century had over 600 lace-makers. She met the last of them.\n\nMajor appearances include the Musée du Louvre-Lens (installation and live painting, 2019), EXPO Dubai 2020 (installation in the Polish Pavilion), the European Parliament in Brussels ("Artists 4 Human Rights" group exhibition, 2022; "Thoughts & Art Embraces Politics" performance and discussion), Urban Nation in Berlin (Biennale 2019, One Wall 2017, and the Fresh A.I.R. Artist in Residence Programme class of 2018), Sharjah Art Museum (UAE, 2014–2015), STRAAT Museum in Amsterdam, and the Musée des Beaux-Arts in Alençon. Solo exhibitions include "Harmony. Walls of Bologna" at Porta Nova Gallery in Bologna (2016), "Scars" at BWA Gallery in Wrocław (2015), "Tableware for Pigeons" at Taito Gallery in Helsinki (2015), "Merlettis" at Elastico Gallery in Bologna (2014), "Szelest myśli" at BWA Gallery in Olsztyn (2013), and "Harmony" at The Art Factory in Łódź (2024). Festival credits include JIDAR (Rabat), Tirana Mural Fest, Hong Kong Walls, Lodhi Art District (Delhi) and the St+Art Festival (Delhi, 2019), NUART (Aberdeen), BLINK Festival (Cincinnati), the Jersey City Mural Arts Program (JCMAP), SHINE (St. Petersburg, 2024), GarGar (Les Penelles, Spain), MUSA (Mendicino), Appartengo (Stigliano), Emergence (Sicily), LeMur Oberkampf (Paris), Djerbahood (Tunisia), Fame Festival (Grottaglie), the Countless Cities Biennial in Favara, the International Lace Festival in Vamberk, Bradford UK City of Culture 2025, the Borås Art Biennale, and the Urban Art Biennale in Moscow. She showed a duplex exhibition with Nandita Kumar at Form Gallery in Perth ("Recrafted", 2015) and a duo show with SC Szyman at O.D. Gallery in Amsterdam ("#CeramicStreetArt", 2022).\n\nHer work also carries political weight. In 2007 she co-founded the NGO Miasto Moje A w Nim with friends to introduce a law controlling outdoor advertising in Poland, which had been overrun with kitschy ads after the fall of communism. After seven years of lobbying, parliament passed the act. Her 2014 Warsaw subvertising piece "For Those Who Know" — an Einstein takeover — symbolically summed up that work, and the 2017 project "One of 1580" with SC Szyman extended it. Other projects address dwindling drinking water ("World Without Water," Sandymount, New Zealand, 2016), deforestation ("Epitaph for the Forest," based on bark prints from the Białowieża primeval forest, 2016–2021), post-war trauma ("Scars," Wrocław, where she filled WWII bullet holes in buildings with clay), and human rights (a melting ice "HUMAN RIGHTS" inscription filmed for the European Parliament).\n\nHer thirty-year project "Thoughts," begun in 2012, uses the leftover clay from her lace pieces. She makes thin porcelain petals by hand, sitting on the streets of cities she visits — fifty kilograms a year, like recording calming thoughts in clay. Over 450 kilograms are ready, targeting 1,500 kilograms for the final exhibition in 2042.\n\nHer SHINE 2024 mural in St. Petersburg, on the Cemex building, captures Hurricane Milton: she was due to arrive the day the storm made landfall in October 2024, the festival was postponed, and a month later she painted lace medallions unraveling into the hurricane\'s winds. She thanked SHINE\'s Jenee Priebe for the invitation.\n\nShe is based in Warsaw. Website: nespoon.art. Instagram: @nes.nespoon.',fn:['NeSpoon is her legal name — it\'s on her ID','Lace patterns cast in concrete and ceramic','Polish artist, works worldwide'],src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2024_SHINE_nespoon','https://nespoon.art/about/','https://urban-nation.com/artist/nespoon/','https://www.printmag.com/graffiti-and-street-art/nespoon-lace-murals/','https://www.thisiscolossal.com/2016/09/lace-street-art-nespoon/','https://artbreath.org/interviews/nespoon','https://www.graffitistreet.com/interview-with-street-artist-nespoon-iwd-special-2023/','https://www.thisiscolossal.com/2020/09/nespoon-lace-mural-calais/','https://www.urbanyte.art/nespoon/']},
  {id:122,a:'Phi Phi Artland Team',t:'Stronger Together',loc:'Central Ave',bldg:'Mikhael\'s storefront',lat:27.771246,lng:-82.662994,y:2024,cat:'commercial',ig:'phi_phi_artland',bio:'This is a community mural, not the work of a single artist. It was led by Sara Salem — Phi Phi Artland\'s Head of Fine Arts — who started with a contest to give a free mural to a local storefront. Mikhael\'s storefront won. After Hurricanes Helene and Milton hit Florida in 2024, Sara wanted the mural to do more than beautify one wall: she wanted it to bring people together in a shared act of creation. She secured a grant from the St. Pete Arts Alliance with backing from the City of St. Petersburg, and the Community Mural Project was born.\n\nThe Phi Phi Artland team — Christianna, Clair, Nadine Allan, Nelia, and Sara — designed a collage of faces in diverse colors, symbolizing unity and the beauty of embracing differences. Each face tells a story; together they reflect the multicultural fabric of St. Petersburg.\n\nPainting happened over four weekends, each led by a different team artist. Nelia ran weekend one — neighbors, families, and first-time painters laid the foundation. Clair led weekend two, layering color and detail. Christianna, the team\'s graphic designer, took weekend three, finalizing the layout despite unusually cold Florida weather and switching from scaffolding to ladders for the upper sections; the design picked up extra flowers along the way. Nadine Allan led weekend four — all painting slots fully booked — and the team finished the piece together.',desc:'A wide panoramic mural showing a row of stylized faces in profile, each rendered in different bold color blocks — greens, yellows, purples, pinks, blues, and oranges. The faces overlap and interlock like puzzle pieces, surrounded by tropical leaves, palm fronds, and simple white flowers. The words "Stronger Together!" appear in gold script on the right side. The style is flat and graphic with clean geometric shapes making up each face, giving the whole piece a modern, poster-art quality. It reads as a celebration of community and diversity.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/phi-phi-stronger-together.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'Inspired by the resilience of St. Petersburg after Hurricanes Helene and Milton hit Florida in 2024. Led by Sara Salem (Phi Phi Artland\'s Head of Fine Arts) with a grant from the St. Pete Arts Alliance and support from the City of St. Petersburg.',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:[{name:'phiphiartland.com',url:'https://phiphiartland.com/2024'}],sbio:'"Stronger Together" is the 2024 St. Pete Community Mural Project — a collaboration led by Sara Salem (Phi Phi Artland\'s Head of Fine Arts) and painted by the Phi Phi Artland team alongside dozens of community participants over four weekends in late 2024. Funded by a grant from the St. Pete Arts Alliance and supported by the City of St. Petersburg.\n\nSara Salem started with a storefront contest to give one local business a free mural. Mikhael\'s storefront won. After Hurricanes Helene and Milton hit Florida that year, Sara expanded the project to bring the community together in a shared act of creation, securing the grant from St. Pete Arts Alliance.\n\nThe Phi Phi Artland team — Christianna, Clair, Nadine Allan, Nelia, and Sara Salem — designed a collage of faces in diverse colors, symbolizing unity and the beauty of embracing differences. The mural is a celebration of St. Petersburg\'s multicultural fabric.\n\nEach weekend was led by a different team artist: - Weekend 1 — Nelia laid the foundation with first-time painters. - Weekend 2 — Clair layered color and detail. - Weekend 3 — Christianna (graphic designer) finalized the layout despite cold weather; flowers added; switched scaffolding to ladders. - Weekend 4 — Nadine Allan led the finishing touches; all slots booked.\n\nLocated near 22nd and Central Ave in downtown St. Petersburg.\n\nPhi Phi Artland is a St. Petersburg fine-arts and martial-arts studio that also runs community mural projects. Website: phiphiartland.com. Instagram: @phi_phi_artland.',fn:['Community mural painted over four weekends by Phi Phi Artland team + neighbors','Inspired by St. Pete\'s resilience after Hurricanes Helene and Milton','Funded by St. Pete Arts Alliance grant + City of St. Petersburg'],src:['https://phiphiartland.com/2024']},
  {id:138,a:'PHYBR',t:'',loc:'1756 Central Ave',bldg:'The Neon Lunchbox',lat:27.770883,lng:-82.657692,y:2024,cat:'commercial',ig:'phybr',bio:'He spent two weeks as A$AP Rocky\'s creative director. He has a background in horticulture.\n\nKen Dushane III — PHYBR — grew up in Toledo, Ohio, started in graffiti and skateboarding, got into lowrider culture in high school, and learned airbrushing and pinstriping along the way. He studied IT security and graphic design before returning to art. He started painting graffiti seriously in Detroit around 2013.\n\nHe has painted over 250 murals in twelve-plus years — hyperrealistic portraits, animals, objects, creatures, and the "pop-esque tropical foliage" that his horticulture background feeds. Clients include Universal Pictures (advertising for the movie Night Swim), rapper Tee Grizzley, the Truman music venue and Lyric Opera House in Kansas City.\n\nHe relocated to Kansas City in 2022 and completed a residency at Upper Level Gallery. He has worked with youth through the Young Artists at Work program in Toledo and after-school programs at the College for Creative Studies in Detroit.',desc:'An exterior mural on the side of The Neon Lunchbox, an 80s/90s-themed restaurant and cocktail bar at 1756 Central Avenue. The piece is a swirling optical-illusion vortex of hyper-realistic 80s and 90s toys and pop culture icons against a dark background. Items spiral inward toward a central vanishing point: Tom and Jerry mid-chase, a Rubik\'s cube, a pink-and-black Furby with glowing blue eyes, a grey Beanie Baby elephant with its red TY heart tag, a Game Boy, porcelain dolls, a Tamagotchi, and other childhood artifacts of the era. The rendering is photorealistic — each toy casts its own shadow and catches light as if tumbling through space. The vortex effect creates a sense of being pulled into a nostalgic time warp, matching the restaurant\'s retro theme.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/phybr-neon-lunchbox.jpeg',from:'Kansas City, MO',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'He spent two weeks as A$AP Rocky\'s creative director. He has a background in horticulture.\n\nKenneth "Ken" Dushane III — PHYBR — grew up in Toledo, Ohio, originally getting into art through graffiti and skateboarding culture. In high school he discovered lowrider culture and learned airbrushing and pinstriping. He studied IT security and graphic design, then returned to visual art and started painting graffiti seriously in Detroit around 2013-2014.\n\nHe has painted over 250 murals professionally in twelve-plus years. His style fuses hyperrealistic portraits, animals, objects, and creatures with "pop-esque tropical foliage" informed by his background in horticulture. He is expanding into fine art and 3D sculptural work, debuting with his "Poison Garden" show. Specializations include landscape, figurative, and typography.\n\nClients include A$AP Rocky (creative director for two weeks, multiple projects), Universal Pictures (advertising for Night Swim), rapper Tee Grizzley, the Truman music venue (Kansas City), and the Lyric Opera House (Kansas City). He has also painted at the From The Earth dispensary in Kansas City as part of a collaboration with the artist Skela. He has shown at Epiphany Center for the Arts in Chicago ("Foliage #3") and completed a residency at Upper Level Gallery in Kansas City.\n\nHe has worked with youth through the Young Artists at Work program in Toledo, Ohio and after-school art programs at the College for Creative Studies in Detroit. He relocated to Kansas City in 2022 from Toledo via Detroit.\n\nFestival credits include SpraySeeMO Mural Festival (participated every year 2017-2021+) and work in Detroit, Chicago, Kalamazoo, Kansas City, Toledo, Miami, Los Angeles, and St. Petersburg. His Neon Lunchbox mural on Central Avenue is a commercial commission, not a SHINE piece.\n\nInstagram: @phybr. Website: phybrart.com.',fn:['Two weeks as A$AP Rocky\'s creative director','Abstract geometric and typographic work','New York energy in St. Pete'],src:['https://www.phybrart.com/','https://voyagekc.com/interview/daily-inspiration-meet-phybr/','https://shoutoutla.com/meet-ken-phybr-dushane-muralist-designer/','https://www.sprayseemo.com/phybr','https://epiphanychi.com/phybr-foliage-3-2021-qr/','https://findmasa.com/artist/phybr','https://toledocitypaper.com/online/pieces-of-phybr-renowned-street-artist-to-debut-new-exhibit-at-art-supply-depo/','https://www.openpr.com/news/3537851/phybr-partners-with-neon-lunchbox-to-bring-new-mural-to-st-petersburg','https://www.openpr.com/news/3542046/renowned-artist-phybr-partners-with-locally-owned-business','https://kctoday.6amcity.com/city-guide/play/murals-kansas-city','https://www.instagram.com/phybr/']},
  {id:30,a:'Quinn Cale',t:'',loc:'225 2nd Ave North',bldg:'Main Grove',lat:27.773643,lng:-82.636226,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'quinnsessential',bio:'She started painting as a teenager on wood slats because she couldn\'t afford to go out with friends. Now she makes the paintings move.\n\nQuinn Cale is from Middletown, Delaware, now based in Tampa. She has no formal art degree — she is self-taught. She worked in sales at Apple, then became an Apple Creative around 2020, teaching children digital art on iPads. That pivot from tech to art stuck.\n\nHer style channels late-90s and early-2000s childhood nostalgia — arcade machines, tropical motifs, instruments, TV-headed figures — in bold, surreal color compositions. She pioneers augmented reality overlays on her paintings using Adobe Aero: viewers scan with a phone and the work animates. Her SHINE 2024 mural at 225 2nd Ave North featured an animated cat and mouse in AR.\n\nShe has exhibited at MOSI in Tampa and the Straz Center\'s First Annual Black Art show. For SHINE Origins 2025, she painted at Tyrone Middle School.',desc:'A large cat rendered in electric purples, teals, oranges, and pinks leaps across the wall, chasing a pixelated orange mouse and a retro cursor hand icon. The cat\'s fur is broken into bold color blocks that read like a neon stained-glass window. The whole piece pops against a dark teal and sage background.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/quinn-cale.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL',aud:'https://cdn.muralquest.app/audio/30.mp3',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2024 selected artist; SHINE Origins 2025 selected artist',fw:[{name:'quinncale.com',url:'https://quinncale.com'}],sbio:'She started painting as a teenager on wood slats because she couldn\'t afford to go out with friends. Now she makes the paintings move.\n\nQuinn Cale is from Middletown, Delaware, now based in Tampa, Florida. She is approximately 32 years old (as of late 2024). She has no formal art degree — she is self-taught, starting with paint on scrap wood as a teenager. She worked in sales at Apple, then became an Apple Creative around 2020, teaching children digital art on iPads.\n\nHer style channels late-90s and early-2000s childhood nostalgia — arcade machines, tropical motifs, instruments, TV-headed figures — in bold, surreal color compositions using surrealism and color theory, with themes of animals and space. She pioneers augmented reality overlays on her paintings using Adobe Aero (no-code platform): viewers scan with an iPad or phone and the work animates.\n\nFor SHINE 2024, she painted a mural at 225 2nd Ave North (Main Grove) in St. Petersburg, featuring an animated cat and mouse with AR. For SHINE Origins 2025, she painted at Tyrone Middle School (2375 66th St N). She has exhibited at MOSI (Tampa) and the Straz Center "First Annual Black Art" show. She has a profile on RAW Artists. Website: quinncale.com. Instagram: @quinnsessential.\n\nPer quinncale.com, additional documented mural festival participation includes Tampa Walls (Tampa, 2022), SHINE 10 (St. Petersburg, 2024), Guava Paint Jam (Tampa, 2025), Hot Walls (St. Petersburg, 2025), and Fish Food on the Tampa Trail (Tampa, 2025). The Straz Center piece selected for the First Annual Black Art Gala was titled "Fire Fist Mario 8-Bit." She was an inaugural Remote Studio Artist at the Gasparilla Festival of the Arts (one of five selected). Original painting titles available through her shop include "Flamingo," "And They\'re Both Boys..," "The Royal Three," "Deandra," and "Geoffrey Giraffe," plus the Colorburst Sunflower Collection and No Signal Collection. Artist statements from the site: "I am a story teller and I welcome you to join me on this visual journey," and "Once you begin, you cannot stop. Life with art is affluent, profound, and passionate."',fn:['Self-taught, started on wood slats as a teen','Apple Creative — taught kids digital art on iPads','Augmented reality overlays on her paintings'],src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2024_SHINE_quinn_cale','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/interview-tampa-artist-quinn-cale-merges-technology-with-childhood-nostalgia-for-shine-mural-festival-18892874/','https://ilovetheburg.com/st-pete-shine-origins/','https://rawartists.com/quinncale','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine-2024','https://quinncale.com','https://quinncale.com/blogs/news/about-me','https://quinncale.com/pages/quinnpaintsmurals']},
  {id:31,a:'Stevie Shao',t:'',loc:'850 5th Ave South',bldg:'Volunteers of America',lat:27.765203,lng:-82.646683,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'stepfrae',bio:'She had never painted a mural before April 2020. She painted her first one on plywood covering a boarded-up business during lockdown. It went viral.\n\nStevie Shao is a second-generation Chinese American whose parents immigrated from Shanghai in the 1990s. She grew up in Seattle\'s Magnolia neighborhood, one of few Asian-American students in predominantly white schools. She studied interior design at Seattle Pacific University, where an illustration course under professor Rachell Sumpter introduced her to Chinese-American illustrators and changed her trajectory. She worked as an interior designer at a Seattle architecture firm until she was laid off in the summer of 2020. She has not gone back.\n\nHer style is vibrant and saturated — hot pink, marine blue, kohl black. Flattened two-dimensional forms inspired by folk art, Chinese ceramics, traditional American tattoos, and Pacific Northwest wildlife meeting Chinese folklore. Seattle Magazine named her one of the city\'s 25 Most Influential People reshaping the region.\n\nThe client list came fast: Anna Sui used her designs in a fashion collection. Starbucks Reserve commissioned an exclusive artist merchandise line. The Seattle Kraken had her design their Women in Hockey jersey. Then Google, Nordstrom, REI, Sub Pop Records, Russell Wilson.\n\nFor SHINE 2024, she painted at the Volunteers of America building on 5th Avenue South.',desc:'A multi-story mural on the side of the Volunteers of America building depicting a sinuous, serpentine dragon in bold navy blue, red, gold, and turquoise against a white background. The dragon coils upward across the full height of the building, its body decorated with pink scalloped scales and chartreuse dots. Two stylized birds with spread wings — one near the top and one at the bottom — intertwine with the dragon\'s twisting form. Blue flames curl from the dragon\'s mouth. The flat, graphic style draws on Chinese folk art and traditional tattoo aesthetics. Signed "Stevie Shao 24" in the lower right corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/stevie-shao.jpeg',from:'Seattle, WA',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Seattle\'s 25 Most Influential People (Seattle Magazine); SHINE 2024 selected artist',fw:[{name:'stevieshao.com',url:'https://www.stevieshao.com'}],sbio:'She had never painted a mural before April 2020. She painted her first one on plywood covering a boarded-up business during lockdown. It went viral.\n\nStevie Shao is a second-generation Chinese American whose parents immigrated from Shanghai in the 1990s. The family settled in Seattle, and Shao and her sister Tori grew up in the Magnolia neighborhood. Her parents wanted the girls to assimilate, and Shao remembers having only one Asian-American classmate in elementary and middle school. The art she encountered as a kid was almost entirely Eurocentric. She painted intricate watercolor and acrylic scenes, but did not see art as a viable career.\n\nShe studied interior design at Seattle Pacific University. An illustration course there with professor Rachell Sumpter, a Seattle illustrator herself, changed the trajectory. Sumpter sent Shao links to Chinese-American illustrators across multiple generations — the first time Shao saw people who looked like her doing work she could see herself doing. At SPU she became art director of Lingua, the student-run quarterly art and literary magazine, and instead of a standard 11x17 photocopy poster she blew her promo up to roughly 11x14 feet across a wall of the art center. She freelanced as an illustrator and graphic designer for local brands and musicians, including Parisalexa, while still in school. After graduating she worked as an interior designer at a Seattle architecture firm housed in the City Centre building downtown. She was laid off in summer 2020 and has not gone back.\n\nThe first mural was a last-minute April 2020 assignment from the Ballard Alliance, a neighborhood business group she had done freelance work for in 2019. She painted a black cityscape on a white background with swoopy pink letters reading "It\'s gonna be ok" on plywood along Ballard Avenue. She painted three in total along the street during the first months of the pandemic. The image moved fast on Instagram. Devin Reynolds of the Ballard Alliance hired her for the others.\n\nHer style is vibrant and saturated — Barbie pink, kohl black, marine blue, Pepto-Bismol pink, lipstick red. Flattened two-dimensional forms inspired by folk art, Chinese ceramics, traditional American tattoo flash (mermaids, anchors, panthers), and Pacific Northwest wildlife meeting Chinese folklore and morality tales. Negative space is shrunk to horror vacui density; decorative florals function as main characters alongside beasts and birds. She researches ecosystems and species and pulls from Instagram accounts cataloging traditional textiles from different regions. She talks about her murals as universes with recurring characters, a "world building" approach where symbols reappear in new combinations to tell different stories.\n\nMurals across Seattle followed quickly: a warm-toned firebird and flowers at Phở Bắc Sup Shop in Rainier Valley, based on a Vietnamese folktale the owners told her; a bull, horse and crane at Re:Public bar and restaurant in South Lake Union; Northwest marine mammals, fish and birds wrapping the Metropole building in Pioneer Square; a deer-and-rabbit piece at Cal Anderson Park about mutualistic alarm and feeding systems; a tiger and phoenix live-painted in Hing Hay Park during the Chinatown-International District Celebration on August 7, 2021; a piece for Overcast coffee; a mobile food pantry for Family Works in veggie-green pattern; a Capitol Hill, Belltown, and International District presence; and a collaboration with the street art nonprofit Urban ArtWorks. She painted a mural for the Showbox in SoDo. Her largest to date wraps three façades of the building housing University Book Store, a menagerie of mythical creatures. She also painted the Crossroads building on the UW campus through extreme weather and a fractured timeline.\n\nShe and her sister Tori Shao designed wayfinding signs and murals for the City Centre building in downtown Seattle — a return to the building she had been laid off from. With Monyee Chau she made an installation at the Wing Luke Museum, 兩隻老虎, featuring two tigers from a Chinese nursery rhyme. She collaborated with Lauren YS (@squid.licker) on "Enter the Dragon," an homage to Bruce Lee on the top floor of the historic Railspur building, made for Forest for the Trees. She painted at Alethia Tanner Park in Washington, DC as part of the DC Walls mural festival, and at Long Beach Walls in California. She showed at the Limelight two-day art show at Lake Union Park (August 21-22, 2021) for Seattle Design Fest, alongside Nikita Ares, Colleen Louise Barry, and Mary Anne Carter. She made protest graphics during the George Floyd protests and the rise of anti-AAPI hate crimes, which connected her to other artists of color in the city.\n\nThe client list came fast. Anna Sui discovered her on Instagram and used her designs — a pink moon, a white-lettered "Take Care" — for T-shirts, totes and pouches in the Anna Sui spring/summer 2021 ready-to-wear collection. Starbucks Reserve commissioned an exclusive artist merchandise line through the Starbucks Art Program — a serving tray, tote, mug, T-shirt, bandana and decorative bowls drawing on Chinese-American folklore, Northwest wildlife, and the biodiversity of South American coffee-growing regions, featuring a jaguar, butterflies, trees and flowers. The Seattle Kraken commissioned the 2022 Women in Hockey jersey. Other work: Google, Nordstrom, REI, Sub Pop Records (tide-pool tote bags), Russell Wilson (shoes), the Seattle Seahawks, Throw & Co. (a jacquard blanket), Monorail Espresso, Chong the Nomad (poster for the sold-out Barboza reopening), a Seattle Artist Relief fund benefit, welcome-back banners for Downtown Seattle, and labels for a French beer company including the "Monsta Passionista" sour with a pink-winged deer and yellow-polka-dotted boar.\n\nCascade PBS / Crosscut called her Seattle\'s breakout muralist in August 2021, when she was 23. Seattle Magazine named her one of the city\'s 25 Most Influential People reshaping the region in its January/February 2023 issue. Her studio is a narrow two-story building behind her parents\' Magnolia house. She also keeps studio space in Ballard.\n\nFor SHINE 2024, she painted at the Volunteers of America Florida Innovare building at 850 5th Avenue South in St. Petersburg, during the festival running November 8-17, 2024.\n\nWebsite stevieshao.com. Instagram @stepfrae.',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine-2024','https://www.cascadepbs.org/culture/2021/08/stevie-shao-seattles-breakout-muralist/','https://seattlemag.com/features/making-a-statement/','https://iexaminer.org/seattle-artist-stevie-shao-a-reclaiming-through-storytelling-and-mysticism/','https://www.stevieshao.com/','https://www.voaflorida.org/event/shine-st-petersburg-mural-festival/','https://seattlerefined.com/features/artistoftheweek/artist-of-the-week-stevie-shao-seattle-artist-art-second-generation-chinese-american-pacific-northwest-murals-illustration','https://www.starbucksreserve.com/stories/exclusive-artist-collection-stevie-shao']},
  {id:32,a:'Up & Over',t:'',loc:'201 17th Street South',bldg:'Modera',lat:27.769214,lng:-82.657673,y:2024,cat:'shine',ig:'upandovermurals',bio:'Tes One co-founded the SHINE Mural Festival. The Vitale Bros have been painting Tampa Bay walls since 1992. In 2020, they started painting together.\n\nUp & Over is Leon "Tes One" Bedore and the Vitale Brothers — Johnny, Paul, and crew. Tes One started tagging at fourteen in 1992, came up through the Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School, and built a career spanning Nike, L\'Oreal, Absolut Vodka, Toyota, The New Yorker, Architectural Digest, and the Brooklyn Nets. In 2012 he curated "Leave a Message" at the Morean Arts Center — the show that planted the seed for SHINE. He co-founded the festival in 2015. His thirty-year retrospective "Good Intentions" showed at the Morean in 2022, and he was featured in the Dali Museum\'s "Outside In" exhibition in 2025.\n\nTogether, their style merges Tes One\'s bold graphic design and typography with the Vitale Bros\' figurative realism and pop culture sensibility. Their wraparound mural at the building now branded DFCU Financial in downtown St. Pete (originally painted for First Citrus Bank) is a local landmark. They have also painted for the Tampa Bay Buccaneers and Duke Energy.\n\nFor SHINE\'s 10th anniversary in 2024, they painted at 2214 2nd Ave North — a full-circle moment: the festival\'s founder painting alongside the city\'s most prolific muralists.',desc:'A towering abstract geometric mural spanning the full height of the Modera apartment building — roughly five stories. Bold, interlocking organic shapes in saturated primary and secondary colors — red, teal, orange, yellow, deep blue, maroon, and white — overlap and flow across the facade. The forms are rounded and architectural, somewhere between hard-edge abstraction and biomorphic design, with circles nested inside flowing vertical bands. The color relationships are carefully balanced, creating a sense of depth and movement. The scale dominates the block and is visible from several streets away.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/up-and-over.jpeg',from:'Tampa Bay, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Tes One: SHINE Mural Festival co-founder (2015); Morean Arts Center retrospective (2022); Dali Museum \'Outside In\' (2025); Vitale Bros: 4x SHINE artists; Spring Breakers set design',fw:null,sbio:'Tes One co-founded the SHINE Mural Festival. The Vitale Bros have been painting Tampa Bay walls since 1992. In September 2020, they started painting together under the name Up & Over.\n\nLeon "Tes One" Bedore (born c. 1977) started tagging at fourteen in 1992 and came up through the Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School. He built a career spanning Nike, L\'Oreal, Absolut Vodka, Toyota, The New Yorker, Architectural Digest, MTV, and the Brooklyn Nets. In 2012 he curated "Leave a Message" at the Morean Arts Center — the show that planted the seed for what became the SHINE Mural Festival. He co-founded and co-curated the inaugural festival in 2015, which brought 17 murals to downtown and has since produced nearly 170. His thirty-year retrospective "Good Intentions" showed at the Morean in 2022. He was featured in the Dali Museum\'s "Outside In" exhibition in 2025. Awards include Urban Excellence Social Impact Award (Tampa Downtown Partnership, 2017), Arts Ambassador Award (St. Petersburg Arts Alliance, 2016), and Best Public Art and Best Art Festival (Best of the Bay, 2015). Featured in Juxtapoz Magazine, Flair Magazine, and Designing Obama.\n\nThe Vitale Brothers — Johnny, Paul, and crew including Nick and Jason Kekllas, Bruce Statton, and Jake Gerow — have been painting since 1992. Their warehouse studio hosted legendary art and music shows in the late 1990s that helped build St. Pete\'s arts scene. Clients include the Tampa Bay Rays, Tampa Bay Buccaneers, and L\'Oreal SalonCentric.\n\nTogether as Up & Over (tagline: "STAY UP & GET OVER™"), they operate as a Tampa Bay-based mural studio serving Tampa, St. Petersburg, Clearwater, Orlando, Dunedin, Largo, Ft. Lauderdale, and Miami. The studio\'s portfolio at upandovermurals.com spans community, commercial, and residential public art. Documented projects include "Viva Tampa" ("an epic mural collage dedicated to Tampa Bay"), "Immaculate" (a depiction of the Immaculate Heart of Mary at St. Petersburg Catholic High School), "Begin Anywhere" (Seminole Heights floral piece — "the flowers of tomorrow are in the seeds of today"), "Sauvignon" (Sauvignon Wine Locker, downtown St. Pete), DPR Construction (Tampa Heights), "Transcendance" (Port Everglades, Terminal 25, Celebrity Edge VIP lounge), the Tampa Bay Buccaneers (two murals featuring Chris Godwin and Tom Brady, the 2021 Super Bowl champions), "Clean Current" (Mahaffey Theater for Duke Energy), DFCU Financial (downtown St. Pete — the botanical-brushstrokes wraparound previously branded as First Citrus Bank), "Bloom" (Axio at Carillon in Feather Sound — described by the studio as the largest mural in Tampa Bay), "Mother Earth" (The Artisan, Dunedin), "Colossal" (Historic Kenwood), "Dream Big" (Tampa Bay), and a SalonCentric headquarters package. The stated client list also includes Accenture, the Tampa Foundation, Facebook, Visit Tampa Bay, and Starbucks. The studio\'s stated mission is to "create art that resonates with its surroundings and connects with the people who experience it."\n\nFor SHINE\'s 10th anniversary in 2024, Up & Over painted at 2214 2nd Ave North. Instagram: @upandovermurals. Website: upandovermurals.com. Contact: hello@upandovermurals.com / (813) 370-0870.',src:['https://tesone.com/about/','https://vitalebrothers.com/about/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/signed-sealed-delivered-talking-with-artist-tes-one/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/vitale-bros-and-shine-this-time-its-personal/','https://upandovermurals.com/','https://upandovermurals.com/about/','https://upandovermurals.com/projects/','https://upandovermurals.com/our-process/']},
  {id:35,a:'Andrea Wan',t:'Earth Song',loc:'155 17th St S',bldg:'',lat:27.76947,lng:-82.656237,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'andrea_wan',bio:'She used to be a piano teacher. Her client list includes Apple, The New York Times, and Microsoft.\n\nAndrea Wan was born in Hong Kong in 1985 and moved to Vancouver at ten. Her parents were graphic designers. She studied Film, Video and Integrated Media at Emily Carr University of Art and Design, then spent six months at Designskolen Kolding in Denmark — where she developed her current drawing style. She lived in Berlin for eight years before returning to Vancouver around 2019.\n\nHer work is surrealist narrative in ink on paper and paint on walls — human figures dissolving into botanical forms, celestial elements, and subconscious landscapes. She describes each drawing as "a still image from a scene in an ongoing story." Influences include European fables, Chinese folklore, and Roald Dahl. Clients include Apple, The New York Times, Microsoft, Absolut, AirBnB, Ray-Ban, Wired, and Lululemon. She is represented by Cosmopola in Berlin.\n\nFor SHINE 2023 she painted "Earth Song" at 155 17th St S — surreal figures intertwined with plant forms in blue and lavender against a warm yellow dome, at the gateway between the Pinellas Trail and the Grand Central District.',desc:'A large-scale mural on a wide building wall set against a bright yellow background shaped in a soft dome. The composition features surreal, stylized human figures intertwined with nature — flowing blue and lavender forms merge with botanical leaves, stems, and flower petals. A golden sun or moon sits at the left, with waterdrop shapes falling from above. The palette centers on cool blues, lavenders, and teals contrasted against the warm yellow background, with accents of pink, green, and gold. The figures appear to grow from or dissolve into the plant forms, creating a dreamlike scene of humans and nature as one continuous organism.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/andrea-wan.jpeg',from:'Vancouver, BC',aud:'',insp:'The deep connection and balance of life on our planet.',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2023 selected artist',fw:[{name:'andreawan.com',url:'https://www.andreawan.com'}],sbio:'She used to be a piano teacher. Her client list includes Apple, The New York Times, and Microsoft.\n\nAndrea Wan was born in 1985 in Hong Kong and moved to Vancouver, BC at age ten. Her parents were graphic designers. She studied Film, Video and Integrated Media at Emily Carr University of Art and Design in Vancouver, then illustration and design at Designskolen Kolding in Denmark (approximately six months, circa 2008), where she developed her current drawing style. She lived in Berlin from 2012 to approximately 2019 and is now based in Vancouver.\n\nHer work is surrealist, narrative-driven — human figures blended with botanical and celestial elements, rendered primarily in ink on paper and paint on walls. She describes her practice as "a journal of thoughts processed through a fictional filter" and each drawing as "a still image from a scene in an ongoing story." Themes include nature, personal mythology, identity, interconnectedness of nature and human consciousness, psychology, and spirituality. Influences include European fables, Chinese folklore, Roald Dahl, dreams, and the subconscious mind.\n\nCommercial clients include Apple, The New York Times (editorial illustrations for NYT Magazine), Microsoft, Mozilla, Absolut, AirBnB, WeTransfer, Ray-Ban, Jagermeister, Wired Magazine, Lululemon, Herschel Supply Co., and Poketo. She is represented by Cosmopola (Berlin) and has exhibited with Affenfaust Galerie (Hamburg) and Rabbit Eye Movement (Vienna). She has been featured in Juxtapoz, Hi-Fructose, i-D Magazine, and on CBC Arts.\n\nSolo exhibitions include "Pen Pals" at Idrawalot Gallery, Berlin (2012), "Mirror Mirror" at Rabbit Eye Movement, Vienna (2014), and "Reunion" at Urban Spree Galerie, Berlin (2022). She has shown at Spoke Art (San Francisco), Giant Robot Gallery (LA), Superchief Gallery (Brooklyn), Gallery Nucleus (LA), and CASS Contemporary (Tampa). She completed residencies in India and a teaching residency in Iran (both 2019).\n\nMural credits include SHINE 2023 ("Earth Song," 155 17th St S, St. Petersburg), POW! WOW! Hawaii (2013, collaborative with Jeff Hamada and Wu Yue), Vancouver Mural Festival 2016 ("Handhug," 3010 Main Street), BUMP Festival 2024 in Calgary ("Maa," 1302 4 St SW), and walls in Germany, Italy, Denmark, and Finland. She has exhibited in New York, Paris, Berlin, San Francisco, Brussels, Barcelona, Stockholm, and Rome.\n\nInstagram: @andrea_wan. Website: andreawan.com.',gone:true,goneDate:'April 2026',goneReason:'Building torn down',src:['https://www.andreawan.com/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2023_SHINE_andrea_wan','https://www.pendulummag.com/art/2019/2/3/andrea-wan-mural-art','https://www.cbc.ca/arts/b-c-illustrator-andrea-wan-1.3419036','https://rabbiteyemovement.at/an-interview-with-andrea-wan/','https://cosmopola.de/visual-artists/andrea-wan/']},
  {id:36,a:'Artist Jones',t:'',loc:'350 2nd Ave N',bldg:'PSTA Ticket Office',lat:27.77346167,lng:-82.63773333,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'theartistjones',bio:'Her great-great grandmother was a Chester County painter. The family has been at it for generations.\n\nTracey Jones — Artist Jones — is based in Tampa. She holds a degree in graphic design and spent ten-plus years as a graphic and web designer before transitioning to fine art painting. She was born in Trinidad and Tobago, and the Caribbean tones show — vibrant-colored portraits of powerful Black and brown people in mixed media, with composition informed by her design training.\n\nClients include the Tampa Bay Lightning, the Tampa Bay Buccaneers, Outback, and Walmart. She participated in the City of Tampa\'s Art on the Block program and the Tampa Bay Born Murals series. For SHINE 2023, she painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A vibrant full building wrap covering every surface of the small PSTA Ticket Office at Williams Park. The mural is a patchwork of bold, densely layered abstract patterns — oversized fan-shaped leaves in hot pink and sky blue, swirling navy spirals, lime green tiger stripes, red-orange organic shapes, yellow leopard spots, teal swirls, and geometric black-and-white dashes and lines. The patterns collide and overlap in irregular blocks, creating a quilt-like effect that draws from African textile traditions and graphic design. The saturated color palette — pink, blue, green, orange, yellow, navy, and teal — wraps seamlessly around corners and over utility boxes, transforming the entire structure into a single vibrant artwork. Signed "@theartist jones" in white at the bottom left.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/artist-jones.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL',aud:'https://cdn.muralquest.app/audio/36.mp3',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2023 selected artist',fw:[{name:'traceyrjones.com',url:'https://www.traceyrjones.com'}],sbio:'Her great-great grandmother was a Chester County painter. The family has been at it for generations.\n\nTracey R. Jones — Artist Jones — is a graphic designer, painter, and muralist based in Tampa Bay, and the great-great granddaughter of Ida Ellen Ruth Jones, the Chester County painter. Her family traveled across the world during her childhood, which she credits with shaping her ability to draw artistic perspectives from many cultures and lifestyles. She began illustrating early and treated art as the lifeblood of her future. In high school, with digital content beginning to boom, her guidance counselors and mentors steered her toward graphic design as a career path. She landed her first graphic design job at a local newspaper after college, taking the position over a higher-paying deli job because it justified the student loans she had signed for. She spent the next ten years evolving into the new-age graphic designer — proficient in design, video, web, coding, and Word — and the more skill she absorbed, the less appreciated and respected she felt. She has said that became the exact reason she began painting: to make work that was hers, untouched by the pressures of business hierarchy.\n\nHer style is vibrant mixed media, with composition shaped by her design background and a recurring focus on history, culture, and powerful Black and brown subjects. Originals listed for sale on her site include "Black Cowboy in St. Louis," "All My Life I Had to Fight," "Neo-Duafe Sovereign," "Clouds are Crowns: Future Baddie," "Olukun," "Key to Becoming," the "Head in the Clouds" series (including #5 Nairobi, #6 Amara, and #7 Jelani), the "Basquiat Series" (including #5: The Meaning), "Tlaloc," "Cat on Red Chair," "Astral Ethereal," "The Birds and the Trees," "Yema(r)ya," "The Power Chapter," "Deception," "BEYOND: A Space Cadet Story," "Rise, Black Girl," and "The Story of Mawu Lisa." Her practice spans painting, sculpting, printmaking, murals, and digital art.\n\nHer first mural in Tampa Bay sits at 901 7th Ave S in St. Petersburg on the exterior of Brookwood Florida, painted as part of the SHINE Mural Festival Unity project in 2022 in collaboration with members of the community and girls from Brookwood. The piece carries the message "Always Forward Together." She was one of sixteen muralists featured in the "We Built This City" exhibition at the Morean Arts Center in St. Petersburg.\n\nCommercial clients on her roster include Outback Restaurants, Tampa Bay Lightning, Tampa Bay Buccaneers, and Walmart. She participated in the City of Tampa\'s Art on the Block program and the Tampa Bay Born Murals video series produced by I Love the Burg and That\'s So Tampa, made possible by Neptune Flood Insurance CEO Trevor Burgess and his husband Gary Hess in partnership with Metro Inclusive Health. The series profiles Tampa Bay muralists, many of them persons of color, LGBTQ+ artists, and women working in a historically non-inclusive industry.\n\nOver the three years leading into her February 2024 CanvasRebel interview, she networked, showed, and shared with a wide range of Tampa Bay artists, and during that period collaborated with Vanessa Oatman of EVOZ Marketing on a year-long project bringing local artists together for a single event. She has spoken about social media as a deliberate balance — taking almost four years to grow her Instagram, refusing to chase follower counts at the expense of growth and experience, and pushing other creatives to find what she calls "a beautiful dream come true" rather than a metric. She frames mentorship as the most rewarding part of her practice, more satisfying than selling paintings.\n\nUpcoming public appearances include the JHOP Art Battle at John Hopkins Middle School (701 16th St S, St. Petersburg) on April 28, 2026, where ten muralists go head-to-head racing the clock. Her CanvasRebel feature ran February 26, 2024, and she has been profiled in the I Love the Burg Tampa Bay Born Murals series.\n\nWebsite: theartistjones.com (also traceyrjones.com). Instagram: @theartistjones. Facebook: Artist Jones Creations. Twitter: @theartistjones. YouTube channel: UCmhhq_kPat_moyvLK0aARrA.',src:['https://canvasrebel.com/meet-tracey-jones/','https://www.traceyrjones.com/','https://theartistjones.com/','https://ilovetheburg.com/tampa-bay-born-murals/']},
  {id:194,a:'Blake Emory & Guillo Perez III',t:'',loc:'2001 1st Ave S',bldg:'Cage Brewing',lat:27.77061,lng:-82.660425,y:2023,cat:'commercial',ig:'emory_blake',bio:'He sculpted a bronze Babe Ruth pointing to center field. His house sits on the former golf course where Ruth used to play.\n\nBlake Emory is a Florida native, born 1983, now based in St. Petersburg. With his brother James — out of Santa Fe — he runs ESP Gallery and the Emory Arts collective. The day job is wide: Neo-Renaissance figure painting, Optic Modernism, sculpture, set design, experimental film. He doesn\'t pick a lane.\n\nHis murals are scattered across St. Pete in places people don\'t expect, including a few rooftops. The work doesn\'t always announce itself — you find it by looking up, or by walking past somewhere you\'ve passed a hundred times.\n\nIn 2025 the City of St. Petersburg unveiled "Babe Calls the Shot," his life-size bronze of Ruth at the called-shot moment, sited at the St. Petersburg Museum of History on the Pier.\n\nThis Cage Brewing octopus is a 2023 collaboration with painter Guillo Perez III. The body sits on the wall at the corner of 1st Ave S and 20th. The tentacles don\'t stay on the wall — they peel forward, climbing past the plane and reaching into the air.',desc:'A giant octopus mural wrapping the corner exterior of Cage Brewing. The octopus\'s body sits on the main wall; its tentacles extend across the building and curl outward beyond the wall plane — part mural, part sculptural extension into the air. Painted in saturated colors against the brewery\'s industrial concrete-block facade.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/blake-emory-cage-brewing.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:[{name:'emorybrothersart.com',url:'https://www.emorybrothersart.com'},{name:'espgallery.com',url:'https://www.espgallery.com'}],sbio:'He sculpted a bronze Babe Ruth pointing to center field. His house sits on the former golf course where Ruth used to play.\n\nBlake Emory was born in Florida in 1983 and is now based in St. Petersburg, with a studio at 1114 Farragut Dr N. With his brother James — based in Santa Fe — he runs ESP Gallery and the Emory Arts collective (EMORY ARTS LLC and the affiliated Emory Arts 501(c)(3)). His practice spans Neo-Renaissance figure painting, Optic Modernism, sculpture, set design, and experimental film. He is represented by Marcolina\'s Gallery.\n\nHis murals are scattered across St. Petersburg in places people don\'t expect, including a few rooftops. He has been painting in the Tampa Bay area since 2017, with most of his documented work falling in 2021–2023. Beyond Florida, he has painted along Route 66 in Grants, New Mexico.\n\nIn 2025 the City of St. Petersburg unveiled "Babe Calls the Shot," his life-size bronze of Babe Ruth at the called-shot moment. It was sited at the St. Petersburg Museum of History on the Pier — a placement that took on extra weight given that Emory\'s own home stands on the former property of a golf course Ruth played on during his St. Pete years.\n\nThis Cage Brewing piece is a 2023 collaboration with painter Guillo Perez III. The body of a giant octopus sits on the corner wall at 1st Ave S and 20th Street S; the tentacles extend past the plane of the wall and curl into the air, reading as part mural, part sculptural extension.\n\nInstagram: @emory_blake. Galleries: ESP Gallery, Marcolina\'s. Website: emorybrothersart.com.',src:['https://www.beeradvocate.com/articles/16703/a-splash-of-color-breweries-add-murals-to-brighten-neighborhoods-and-taprooms/','https://www.tampabay.com/life-culture/history/2025/05/10/babe-ruth-sculpture-st-petersburg-yankees-baseball/','https://marcolinas.com/collections/blake-emory','https://www.emorybrothersart.com/biography','https://www.instagram.com/emory_blake/','https://stpetersburgfoodies.com/craft-beer/cage-brewing-beer-games-music-in-st-pete/']},
  {id:37,a:'Bryan Beyung & James Lee Chiahan',t:'When the Sun is Up, the Moon is Absent!',loc:'2875 7th Ave S',bldg:'Stewart Moving & Storage',lat:27.762873,lng:-82.673696,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'bryanbeyung',bio:'Bryan Beyung painted a mural on a bulk carrier — the first produced on a cargo ship in Canada. James Lee Chiahan\'s clients include Apple, The New Yorker, and The New York Times. They paint together.\n\nBeyung was born in Montreal to a Chinese-Cambodian refugee family. He studied graphic design, started as a graffiti artist as a teenager, and built a practice exploring diasporic heritage through memories and images. His work won the International Corporate Art Awards in Italy. He has painted across the US, Canada, Haiti, France, Morocco, and Cambodia.\n\nChiahan was born in 1990 in Tainan, Taiwan, and is self-taught. His oil paintings and charcoal drawings explore the bittersweetness of mundane moments. He won the Canadian Magazine Awards Grand Prize for Cover Art (2024) and was shortlisted for the Jackson\'s Art Prize. He is represented by Pierre-Francois Ouellette art contemporain in Montreal.\n\nFor SHINE 2023, they collaborated on "When the Sun is Up, the Moon is Absent!"',desc:'A two-panel mural on the Stewart Moving & Storage building. The left side features a large black circle on a golden background containing a white tree silhouette and crescent moon — based on a drawing by six-year-old Amelia Kien — with the word "ABSENT" below and colorful childlike figures along the bottom painted by the Kien family. The right side shows a photorealistic sunset landscape with four family members — two adults and two children — standing on a hillside, connected by a red textile panel with traditional patterns. A navy blue band along the base depicts fish silhouettes. The mural tells the story of the Kien family, who fled Cambodia in 1987 and eventually settled near Bradenton, Florida, just minutes from this wall.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/bryan-beyung-james-lee-chiahan.jpeg',from:'Montreal, Canada',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Beyung: International Corporate Art Awards (Italy); Chiahan: Canadian Magazine Awards Grand Prize (2024), Jackson\'s Art Prize shortlist, American Illustration 43; SHINE 2023',fw:null,sbio:'Bryan Beyung painted a mural on a bulk carrier — the first produced on a cargo ship in Canada. James Lee Chiahan\'s clients include Apple, The New Yorker, and The New York Times. They paint together.\n\nBeyung was born in Montreal to a Chinese-Cambodian refugee family. His parents met in a refugee camp on the Thailand-Cambodia border after fleeing the Khmer Rouge regime and arrived in Quebec in 1979. He started as a graffiti artist as a teenager, completed a graphic design diploma at Cégep Marie-Victorin in Montreal in 2006, and later trained in color theory and muralism (2018) and oil painting and muralism (2019) at Galeria LaRoja in Seville, Spain. He works across muralism, painting, and drawing at the intersection of neo-muralism, post-graffiti, and contemporary painting, exploring the diasporic experience and questions of belonging, collective memory, and transmission.\n\nHis public art includes Migration (2014, MU, Montreal), May an Old Song Open a New World (2015, MU, Montreal, with Gene Pendon) near the Chinatown gates at St-Laurent and Rene-Levesque, Men Anpil Chay Pa Lou (2018, L\'art de s\'unir, Port-au-Prince, Haiti), Passengers (2018, Beyond Walls Festival, Lynn), Gate Keepers (2018, Factory, Phnom Penh), Humankind Odyssey (2019, Mural Festival, Montreal) at Roy and Coloniale featuring a two-storey still life with a yellow mango referencing his childhood family dinners, Trochromatie (2019, Parc Frederic-Back, Montreal), Pedestrians Crossing (2019, Dix30, Brossard), Tale of Ancient Vase (2019, City of Boston), Rocky Beach (2020, MU, Lasalle), Jade et ses homies (2020, Kolab, Saint-Laurent), Mono No Aware (2022, Beyond Walls Festival, Lowell), Etranger (2022, Festival Jidar, Rabat), Jardin interieur (2023, Place Dupuis, Montreal), La boite a jouets (2023, Ruelle Garlanau, Montreal), Stop Bombing Civilians (2024, Festival Mural, Montreal), Little Ghost (2024, Bump Festival, Calgary), Demain l\'hiver / Tomorrow\'s Winter (2024, Montreal), and Casio (2024, Jardin Racine, Racine). His murals can be seen in Calgary, Rabat, St. Petersburg, Boston, Lynn, Lowell, Montreal, Annecy, Phnom Penh, and Port-au-Prince. His work has been featured at La Foire en Art Actuel de Quebec, La Centrale Powerhouse and Pierre-Francois Ouellette art contemporain in Montreal, Forest For The Trees in Seattle, the Urban Art Biennale at the Volklinger Hutte World Cultural Heritage Site in Germany, Antler Gallery in Portland, Open Gallery in Long Beach, Blue Triangle Gallery in Boston, Station 16 in Montreal, Hotel Rosewood in Phnom Penh, Slice of Life Gallery in Vancouver, Anteism Gallery in Montreal, Kbach Gallery in Phnom Penh, and Galerie Michel-Ange in Montreal. He has held residencies at Jardins Racine (Racine), Art by Friends (Annecy, France), Void Projects (Miami), and Paifang E. (Montreal, 2025). He won the International Corporate Art Awards in Italy and received grants from Conseil des arts de Montreal (Recherche-Creation, 2024), the Ministere de la Culture et des communications (Cooperation Quebec-Jalisco, 2024), and CALQ Vivacite (2014, 2023). He co-published Le depanneur chinois d\'Hochelaga in 2024 with Angelina Guo and James Lee Chiahan. He has spoken on panels for the CanAsian Arts Network and the Musee des beaux-arts de Montreal. He has been covered by Le Devoir, City News, Arab News, La Presse, The Suburban, CBC News, and the Phnom Penh Post. Website bryanbeyung.com, Instagram @bryanbeyung.\n\nJames Lee Chiahan (Lee Chia-Han) was born in 1990 in Tainan, Taiwan, and is based in Montreal. His mother\'s side of the family runs roughly twenty generations deep in Taiwan\'s Anping District, where the Dutch East India Company set up a trading post in 1624; his father comes from a Hakka family with more recent roots in China. He grew up watching Studio Ghibli films and drew detail-oriented illustrations as a child, then went into graphic design in the Toronto area and later Montreal. He is self-taught as a fine artist, working primarily in oil painting and charcoal drawing, lingering in the bittersweetness of mundane moments. He is represented by Pierre-Francois Ouellette art contemporain (PFOAC) in Montreal. His editorial and commercial clients include Apple, The New Yorker, The Atlantic, The Walrus, The Washington Post, and The New York Times — including a commissioned portrait of French author Emmanuel Carrere for a review of his novel Yoga, and a feature illustration for "The Wrong Side of a New Age" in The Walrus. His illustration work has been recognized by American Illustration 43 (2024, 2025), the Canadian Magazine Awards (2024 Grand Prize for Cover Art), Jackson\'s Art Prize (2024 shortlist), The Society for News Design (2024), and the Society of Illustrators. He has completed murals for BMO (2025, Rene-Levesque and De Bullion, Montreal), Davies Ward Phillips & Vineberg LLP (2024, Montreal), and Montreal\'s Chinatown (2024, with Bryan Beyung). He participated in Self-portraits of Formosa: Reminiscing the Dutch? at TAIWANfest Toronto and Vancouver TAIWANfest in 2023. Influences include filmmakers Edward Yang and Hou Hsiao-hsien, visual artist James Jean, musician Wu Bai, the Taiwanese band Forests, and Taiwanese-Canadian musician Alex Zhang Hungtai. Website jlee.ca.\n\nFor the 9th edition of SHINE Mural Festival in 2023, Beyung and Chiahan painted "When the Sun is Up, the Moon is Absent!" at 2875 7th Avenue S in St. Petersburg, a 25-by-80-foot wall depicting the Kien family standing on a red dirt road in Site 2, a refugee camp on the Cambodia-Thailand border in 1987, before fate brought them to Boston, Montreal, and finally Bradenton, Florida. The youngest figure is their friend Anhdi, who lives in Bradenton with his wife and three kids; the family helped paint sections of the wall, including a tree and moon based on a drawing by Amelia Kien, age 6, and a bottom-left section and door painted by Annie, Aaden, Adrian, and Amelia Kien, plus June, Zachariah, and Noble. The project was supported by David and Liliana of Artillery Residency, Jenee and the SHINE team, and Void Projects, with polaroids by Ngahuia Fiu and photography by Mark Rapien.',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/bryan-beyung-2023','https://www.jlee.ca/mural-shine_9','https://bryanbeyung.com/Bio','https://pancouver.ca/james-lee-chiahan-finds-artistic-niche-in-illustrations-for-major-magazines-and-oil-paintings-for-himself/','https://www.pfoac.com/en/artists/107-james-lee-chiahan/overview/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/take-a-look-all-the-2023-shine-festival-murals/','https://mumtl.org/en/artists/bryan-beyung/','https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/montreal/mural-painter-brings-cambodian-chinese-family-roots-to-montreal-streets-1.5172804','https://www.jlee.ca/']},
  {id:38,a:'Bunnie Reiss',t:'Phoebe & Friends',loc:'2700 Central Ave',bldg:'MTBH Studios',lat:27.7707,lng:-82.670467,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'bunnieluvrocks',bio:'She started painting murals because her San Francisco apartments were too small to hold a studio. She\'d work outside on whatever wall she could find, walk away, and treat it like a modern-day cave painting.\n\nBorn in Maryland, raised mostly in Colorado in a Polish/Russian family whose Eastern European folk traditions still saturate her work. She studied literature, worked at NPR, then earned her MFA from the San Francisco Art Institute and spent more than 20 years in the Bay Area before relocating full-time to Lilac Lane Farms — a 5-acre desert homestead in Landers, near Joshua Tree, where she grows food, keeps chickens, and built a dedicated painting studio.\n\nHer subject matter centers on nature and the cosmos, mysticism and magic, and the systems that connect people, animals, and plants — all rendered in dense pattern and a maximalist palette drawn from Russian fairy tales, Mexican folk art, and Jewish mysticism. She works across murals, paintings, hand-painted leather gloves with sacred-geometry eye motifs, large-scale mosaics, and immersive sculpture — including "The Sky Rabbit\'s House," a giant rabbit leaping over a rooftop, built on a street-legal truck chassis and named after Lepus, the winter constellation she can see from her porch. People can sit inside it. She says it was "made for people to fall in love."\n\nShe\'s painted walls in over 20 countries, with documented locations including "Magic Buffalo" in Buffalo, NY (via the Albright-Knox Museum), Carbondale, CO, El Segundo, CA, and the high-desert towns of Joshua Tree and Yucca Valley. Solo shows at Superchief Gallery LA; group exhibitions at Paradigm Gallery (Philadelphia), La Luz de Jesus, and Luna Rienne. Coverage in Juxtapoz, Colossal, Vice, Hi-Fructose, VoyageLA, and Palm Springs Life. As of late 2023 she was working on illustrations for a children\'s book and a monograph covering the previous ten years.',desc:'Three elaborately patterned pink flamingos stride across the wall on long dark legs, each bird filled with intricate floral and geometric details in shades of pink, coral, and lavender. They step over a blue-violet rainbow arch with rolling green hills beneath. Golden flower clusters bloom on the right side against a bright teal sky dotted with small clouds and birds. The piece covers the entire side of the building.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/bunnie-reiss.jpeg',from:'Detroit, MI',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'MFA, San Francisco Art Institute; Artist in Residence, St. Vincent De Paul (Oakland)',fw:null,sbio:'Bunnie Reiss is a muralist based near Joshua Tree, California who paints folk-art walls full of animals, flowers, and dense patterning. She was born in Maryland and raised mostly in Colorado, and her work consistently reflects her Eastern European family background. She moved to California more than 20 years ago and has lived in major areas in both the north and south of the state. She started painting murals while living in San Francisco, where small apartments meant she could not afford big studios and learned to work outside on walls instead. She describes the early San Francisco years as wild — painting outside, leaving the work, walking away, treating it like a modern-day cave painting.\n\nReiss relocated full-time from Los Angeles to a 5-acre desert property in Landers about two years before her October 2022 VoyageLA interview, after owning the land for almost four years. She had previously run a storefront gallery in LA. The desert property, near Joshua Tree National Park, is a homestead she calls Lilac Lane Farms, where she grows food, keeps chickens and other livestock, and built a dedicated painting studio. Sustainability is part of her practice. She describes being surrounded by a community of artists, writers, and musicians in the high desert that has informed her current creative process.\n\nHer subject matter centers on nature and the cosmos, mysticism and magic, and the systems that connect people, animals, and plants. She cites her Eastern European family as the source of the mysticism and the emphasis on community in her work. Colossal describes her output as colorful, cosmic characters with an emphasis on patterns and a symbiosis between wild animals, botanicals, and people. An ongoing series of hand-painted leather gloves features embellished eye motifs, symmetry, and sacred geometry.\n\nShe works across murals, paintings, immersive sculptures, and — added to her practice in roughly 2021–2022 — large-scale mosaics. She recently completed a 500-square-foot mosaic mural. She also built "The Sky Rabbit\'s House," a sculpture of a giant rabbit leaping over a rooftop, constructed on the chassis of a street-legal truck and made in collaboration with a team of fabricators. She named it after Lepus, a winter constellation she can see from her porch. The piece has a cavity inside for people to sit in and was, in her words, "made for people to fall in love."\n\nDocumented mural locations include "Magic Buffalo" in Buffalo, New York, commissioned through the Albright-Knox Museum Public Arts Initiative; a wall in Carbondale, Colorado, through the Carbondale Public Arts Program; and a wall in El Segundo, California, commissioned by the El Segundo Arts Council. In the high desert she has painted an interior mural for Untamed Yoga in Joshua Tree and a wall for The End in Yucca Valley. She has said she would like to paint a mural in Palm Springs.\n\nShe taught herself to use spray paint after being offered large brick walls that demanded different materials than she had previously used, and she talks about adapting her style — which she describes as consistent and core to who she is — to the texture of different walls and the conditions of different environments.\n\nReiss participated in the Highway 62 Open Studio Art Tours, organized by the Morongo Basin Cultural Arts Council (mbcac.org), opening her Landers studio during the 20th edition of the tour in October 2021 (weekends of Oct. 9–10, 16–17, and 23–24) and again in 2022 (weekends of Oct. 8/9, 15/16, 22/23). The 2022 edition featured almost 200 properties across the desert. La Matadora Gallery is among the local galleries she had been aware of before making the desert her permanent home.\n\nShe started really living off her art roughly ten years before her October 2022 interview, after years of taking on any work she could to fund her practice. She talks openly about the emotional, physical, and spiritual exhaustion of being a working public artist, and about the importance of seeking out older, more experienced mentors. As of late 2023 she was working on illustrations for a children\'s book and organizing a monograph covering the previous ten years of her work.\n\nHer work has been covered by Colossal (October 31, 2023, by Kate Mothes), VoyageLA (October 12, 2022), and Palm Springs Life (October 6, 2021, by Carl Schoemig). Website: bunniereiss.com. Instagram: @bunnieluvrocks.\n\nPer her current website (bunniereiss.com/all-about-bunnie-1), Reiss has since relocated to Detroit, Michigan; the Mojave Desert property — Lilac Lane Farms at 55526 Lilac Lane, Landers, CA 92285 — is still developed with art and sculpture but is now maintained by friends rather than full-time by her. She also did a brief residency in France between her Bay Area years and Los Angeles. She describes herself as "Artist, educator, magic maker." Her practice now spans murals, interior murals, painted vehicles, paintings, mosaics, sculpture, textile, and installation.\n\nHer mural map on bunniereiss.com/murals lists wall projects across San Francisco, Oakland, Sacramento, Joshua Tree (including a Writers Retreat series), Santa Monica, Marina Del Rey, Los Angeles (including Blacklist / LA Marathon, Silverlake / Donut Farm LA, Highland Park / co-LAb Gallery, and The Container Yard in the Downtown LA Arts District), Morongo Valley, Slab City, Nevada Trailer Park / Gerlach, Cincinnati, Roger\'s City and Detroit, Michigan, Austin and Marfa, Texas, Salt Lake City, Buffalo and Queens (Welling Court) New York, Chicago (Truborn and a Public Art series), Milwaukee, Jersey City, Asbury Park (Mercat), and St. Petersburg, Florida. International mural locations include Vancouver Mural Fest, Ontario (Canada), San Pancho and Mexico City (Magic Hand and a UN-commissioned series) and Guadalajara (Mexico), Florence (Italy), and Manila (David\'s Hand, Philippines).\n\nBeyond "The Sky Rabbit\'s House," her sculpture practice includes "Big Bouncy Bird" (2022) — a roughly 12-foot interactive sculpture built on a truck spring that visitors can ride, made in collaboration with One Hat One Hand in San Francisco and based on one of her paintings — and "The Lookout," a tiny teetering wooden house with stained-glass windows and hidden messages, pushed by mosaic clouds and a mosaic moon, inspired by the Chakora bird\'s mythological devotion to the moon.',fn:['Now based in Detroit (relocated from Landers, CA — Mojave property maintained by friends)','Folk art + botanical patterns','Vibrant floral and celestial imagery'],src:['https://voyagela.com/interview/rising-stars-meet-bunnie-reiss/','https://www.thisiscolossal.com/2023/10/bunnie-reiss-cosmic-creatures/','https://voyagela.com/interview/rising-stars-meet-bunnie-reiss/','https://www.palmspringslife.com/bunnie-reiss/','https://bunniereiss.com','https://bunniereiss.com/all-about-bunnie-1','https://bunniereiss.com/murals','https://bunniereiss.com/sculpture']},
  {id:52,a:'Chenlin Cai',t:'The Path We Came / 我们来的路',loc:'1927 4th Ave S',bldg:'Lantmännen Unibake',lat:27.768329,lng:-82.659899,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'chenlin.cai',bio:'She fuses traditional Chinese ink painting with X-ray imagery and inside-bottle painting craft. She holds MFAs from both Tsinghua University in Beijing and the Pennsylvania Academy of Fine Arts.\n\nChenlin Cai was born in 1984 in Fujian, China, and relocated to the United States in 2014. Her work explores Chinese American identity through cellular structures, non-traditional surfaces, and multi-media expression. Her SHINE 2023 "Bright Spot" mural, "The Path We Came," was a collaboration with the James Museum of Western & Wildlife Art — extending their exhibition "From Far East to West: The Chinese American Frontier" (Oct 2023 – Jan 2024) onto a St. Pete wall, depicting the Chinese American laborers who built the First Transcontinental Railroad but were excluded from its 1869 completion photographs.\n\nIn Philadelphia, she worked with Mural Arts Philadelphia on a 21-story mural on the Crane Building in Chinatown celebrating 150-plus years of community, alongside Eurhi Jones and Yvonne Lung. She painted koi fish on the sidewalks of 10th Street Plaza and murals inside Chinatown restaurants.\n\nHer work is in the Copelouzos Family Art Museum of Contemporary Arts in Athens, Greece. She has shown at the 12th Florence Biennale, Freeman\'s Auction Gallery in Philadelphia, and 456 Gallery in New York.',desc:'A wide mural on the Lantmännen Unibake building depicting three Chinese American railroad laborers rendered in blue-gray tones against a dramatic sunset of deep orange, gold, and purple. The three figures — one holding a staff, one reading a letter, one planting a flower with his hat removed — represent family longing, hope, and resilience. Behind them, golden mountain peaks evoke the Sierra Nevada, with a setting sun and crescent moon embodying the duality of aspiration and homesickness. A steam locomotive and train cars wind along railroad tracks at right. The mural reimagines Chinese laborers who built the First Transcontinental Railroad but were excluded from its 1869 completion photographs. Created in partnership with the James Museum\'s "From Far East to West" exhibition.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/chenlin-cai.jpeg',from:'Philadelphia, PA',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'12th Florence Biennale (2019); Copelouzos Family Art Museum collection; SHINE 2023 Bright Spot artist',fw:[{name:'cai-chenlin.com',url:'https://www.cai-chenlin.com'}],sbio:'She fuses traditional Chinese ink painting with X-ray imagery and inside-bottle painting craft. She holds MFAs from both Tsinghua University in Beijing and the Pennsylvania Academy of Fine Arts in Philadelphia.\n\nChenlin Cai (蔡陈林) was born in 1984 in a small fishing village in Fujian province, China, and relocated to the United States in 2014. She now divides her time between Philadelphia and Beijing. Her parents, who never finished high school, ran a clothing store in the village where she grew up. Before leaving China, she and a partner ran a business in Beijing training Chinese high school students to prepare for art school entrance exams. After getting her first MFA from Tsinghua, she painted a mural for the South Korean government\'s Korean Cultural Center in Beijing during the 2008 Olympics, and later designed and executed a year-long mural project honoring the history of Xuchang, a city in Central China.\n\nA friend named Michelle Sun, an auntie-like figure who had settled in the Philadelphia suburbs and now lives in Berwyn, suggested she come to Philadelphia, calling it the mural capital of America. Cai arrived in 2014 and pursued a second MFA at the Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts, where she studied under professor Renee Foulks. In her early days in the city, she went by "Jerry" because Americans struggled with her name. She funded her living expenses by flying back to Beijing each winter and summer break to teach intensive courses at her old business, and by giving private lessons to Chinese students abroad over WeChat.\n\nHer practice combines classical figure drawing with experimental work on non-traditional surfaces including plexiglass, exploring Chinese American identity through cellular structures and multi-media expression. One signature piece collected 400 X-rays of Chinese workers from hospitals around China, which she scanned to create digital collage replicas of a famous Chinese landscape painting — pairing the horror faced by Chinese workers with peaceful nature scenes. Her painting style uses inside-bottle painting craft, cellular structure oil painting texture, and X-ray imagery to interpret landscape. She served as a Commissioner on the Pennsylvania Governor\'s Advisory Commission on Asian Pacific American Affairs from 2022-2023.\n\nHer SHINE 2023 "Bright Spot" mural, "The Path We Came," at 1927 4th Avenue S in St. Petersburg, was a collaboration with the James Museum of Western & Wildlife Art exploring the Chinese American experience and the Transcontinental Railroad. The James Museum partnership was tied specifically to the museum\'s exhibition "From Far East to West: The Chinese American Frontier" (October 14, 2023 – January 28, 2024). The exhibition was organized into twelve thematic sections — immigration, mining, railroad construction, Chinatowns, festivals, traditional medicine, the 1906 earthquake, newspapers, Angel Island, women\'s experiences, artist Z.S. Liang, and visitor reflections — featuring works by contemporary Chinese American artists Hung Liu, Mian Situ, Jie Wei Zhou, and Benjamin Wu alongside historical objects. Cai\'s outdoor mural extended the exhibition\'s railroad-construction section to a wall in St. Petersburg, depicting Chinese American laborers who built the First Transcontinental Railroad but were excluded from its 1869 completion photographs. Emily Kapes, Chief Curator of the James Museum, framed the broader exhibition as "an opportunity to expand on the works by Chinese American artists already in The James Museum collection and engage with Chinese American art and culture in a thought-provoking and visually captivating manner." The St. Pete Catalyst credited the wall to "Chenlin Cai and the James Museum of Western & Wildlife Art, 1927 4th Avenue S." The exhibition is now available as a virtual exhibition in both English and Mandarin.\n\nIn Philadelphia, she worked with Mural Arts Philadelphia on a 21-story mural on the west wall of the Crane Community Center (the Crane Building) in Chinatown, titled "承前啓後 – The Past Supporting the Future." The work, visible from I-676, was created with Eurhi Jones and Yvonne Lung, managed for Mural Arts Philadelphia by Lindsey Rosenberg, and commissioned by the Philadelphia Chinatown Development Corporation as part of the Philadelphia Redevelopment Authority\'s Percent for Art Program in partnership with the Philadelphia Housing Development Corporation (PHDC). Three years in the making, painting began in summer 2022 with help from over 100 volunteers from the Chinatown community and AAPI organizations, plus Mural Arts Interns. The 54-foot crane rises on a pathway of paper cranes emanating from a history scroll showing the original protest against the Vine Street Expressway, the creation of the Friendship Gate, and the completion of the Crane Community Center. Surrounding imagery includes a dragon boat team, a young girl performing the lion dance, a traditional pipa stringed instrument, the Monkey King from Chinese opera, a swallow, and area delicacies. The background uses peony petals embossed with textile patterns from Japan, India, Korea, China, Vietnam, Indonesia, Thailand, Myanmar, Laos, Cambodia, Tibet, Bhutan, and the Philippines. Process documentation lives at @phillychinatowncranemurals on Instagram.\n\nShe painted koi fish on the sidewalks of 10th Street Plaza in Chinatown and murals inside Chinatown restaurants and Asian restaurants around Philly: a gluttonous panda surrounded by wontons in chili oil and Chinese hamburgers at Chengdu Famous Food on Lancaster Avenue, leaping yin-and-yang fish at the Old City sushi spot Tomo, and a dragon boating scene inside Chinatown\'s Bubblefish (co-owner Ping Li called it "a work of art"). She also worked at a collaborative project at 1735 Market Street where ten artists each designed a mural on the theme "What does Philadelphia mean to you" — her contribution juxtaposed a rendition of the William Penn Statue with a local hero from her hometown. She estimates she has painted about 20 murals around Philadelphia in public and private spaces.\n\nFrom February 23 to August 25, 2024, her solo exhibition "Interstellar Landscape" was on view in Terminal F at Philadelphia International Airport. The work drew on early footage from the James Webb Telescope of the Cosmic Cliffs in the Carina Nebula, blending classical oil painting with traditional Chinese ink painting techniques to combine panoramic landscapes from space with finely painted details resembling microscopic organic structures.\n\nOther clients listed on her site include IBEW and Westin. Her work is in the Copelouzos Family Art Museum of Contemporary Arts in Athens, Greece, alongside Chinese, American, and Greek private collectors. She has shown at the 12th Florence Biennale, Freeman\'s Auction Gallery in Philadelphia, and 456 Gallery in New York. Named projects include "Mist-Covered and Majestic Mountain" (层峦烟波图, Philadelphia, 2018), "Daydream" (白日梦, Philadelphia, 2021), "the Forbidden City" (Philadelphia, 2017), and the Artelo Mural Project. Her studio is at 1001 Vine Street, Philadelphia, PA 19107. Website: cai-chenlin.com (also caichenlin.com and caichenlin.wixsite.com/finearts). Email: caichenlin0821@gmail.com.',fn:['Fuses traditional Chinese ink painting with X-ray imagery','Born in Hangzhou, studied at RISD','Translucent layered compositions'],src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2023_SHINE_chenlin_cai','https://www.cai-chenlin.com/','https://www.inquirer.com/philly/news/chenlin-cai-artist-philadelphia-chinatown-murals-20181202.html','https://muralarts.org/stories/inheritance-the-past-supporting-the-future/','https://www.phl.org/chenlin-cai','https://stpetecatalyst.com/take-a-look-all-the-2023-shine-festival-murals/','https://muralarts.org/artists/chenlin-cai-2/','https://ashokjaingalleryus.com/chenlin-cai-bioeducation','https://6abc.com/post/chenlin-cai-philadelphia-murals-chinatown-asian-culture/13634685/','https://thejamesmuseum.org/exhibitions/fareast/','https://ilovetheburg.com/james-museum-far-east-to-west/']},
  {id:39,a:'Chris Dyer',t:'Ride on the sPEACEship!',loc:'2875 7th Ave S',bldg:'Stewart Moving & Storage',lat:27.762877,lng:-82.672623,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'chris_dyer',bio:'He has painted in forty-five countries. His mural is in the Bob Marley Museum.\n\nChris Dyer was born in Ottawa in 1979, moved to Lima, Peru at four, and returned to Canada at seventeen. He studied illustration at Dawson College in Montreal and trained under visionary masters Alex Grey, Robert Venosa, and Amanda Sage. His style fuses psychedelic imagery, Incan and pre-Columbian influences, sacred geometry, and 1980s skate culture.\n\nHe founded the Positive Creations brand in 2003 — clothing, tapestries, books, skateboards, footwear. He has been art director at Creation Skateboards and designed over a hundred commercial skateboard graphics for Santa Cruz, Deathwish, and Think. He has done live painting for Diplo, Tipper, and Immortal Technique at festivals from Burning Man to Boom to Rainbow Serpent. He published two books and sold out 5,555 NFTs in 2021.\n\nHe now lives in a purple house in Gulfport with his partner Paulina Casati. For SHINE 2023, he painted "Ride on the sPEACEship!" on Stewart Moving and Storage — a wildly detailed spaceship made of interlocking creatures trailing rainbow exhaust flames.',desc:'A panoramic cosmic scene against a starry black background on Stewart Moving & Storage. A wildly detailed spaceship made of interlocking creatures, tentacles, and geometric patterns blasts across the wall, trailing rainbow exhaust flames and lightning bolts. The vessel is piloted by psychedelic characters with multiple eyes and Incan-inspired headdresses, one flashing a peace sign from inside a blue bubble cockpit. "Positive Creations" floats above in rainbow lettering, "PEACE PLEASE" appears in a speech bubble at right, and a smiling sun face radiates in the upper right corner. Saturn, UFOs, and alien faces orbit the composition. Signed "Chris Dyer" at left and "LOVE!" in pink at lower right.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/chris-dyer.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Murals in 45+ countries; Bob Marley Museum, Beatles Ashram, STRAAT Museum; SHINE 2023',fw:[{name:'positivecreations.ca',url:'https://positivecreations.ca'}],sbio:'He has painted in forty-five countries. His mural is in the Bob Marley Museum.\n\nChris Dyer was born March 3, 1979 in Ottawa, Ontario, and moved to Lima, Peru at age four. He started skateboarding in 1986 at age eight and started surfing at twelve. At fourteen he joined a Lima soccer-fan street gang called SepUlcro (also written Sepulcro Crema), aligned with the Universitario de Deportes club, and started tagging walls with spray paint in 1993. His parents sent him back to Canada in 1996 at seventeen. He lived with his grandmother in Ottawa, attended Heritage College and the University of Ottawa, then moved to Montreal in 2000 to study illustration at Dawson College. A 2001 summer of tree planting pushed him toward a more spiritual path. He later studied under and taught alongside visionary masters Alex Grey, Robert Venosa, Martina Hoffman, Laurence Caruana, A. Andrew Gonzales, Maura Holden, and Amanda Sage. His style fuses Incan and pre-Columbian influences, shamanistic tribal patterning, 1980s and 90s skate and graffiti culture, psychedelia, and vinyl and action-figure collectibles.\n\nHe started painting on broken skateboards in 2000, which got him into Thrasher and High Times magazines. In 2004 he began doing graphics for Creation Skateboards out of San Francisco, and by 2011 he was Art Director and Brand Manager of Creation Skateboards / Satori Movement, where he directed the skate video Soul Harmonics. He has done over a hundred commercial skateboard graphics for Santa Cruz, Deathwish, Think, Darkstar, and Skull. By the late 2010s he had moved into reclaimed-skateboard installations like the Skatebot Army. His brother Patrick (a.k.a. Peru 143) is also a graffiti artist; the two became roommates in Montreal in 2007 and started painting together in their backyard, which is when Chris got fully into spray paint.\n\nHe launched the Positive Creations brand in 2003 (the Gabber dates it to 2004; the brand turned twenty around 2024). The line covers clothing, tapestries, books, backpacks, watches, footwear, skateboards, and accessories, sold online and at festival pop-ups. In 2008 he co-directed a feature documentary about his life with Dreamtime Cinema, released as Positive Creations on DVD in 2010 and free on YouTube. Schiffer Publishing put out the 256-page hardcover Positive Creations: The Visionary Art of Chris Dyer in 2011. Last Gasp of San Francisco published Chris Dyer\'s Kick-Ass Coloring Book in 2016 and a second hardcover, Shamanic Journey, in 2024. Other publication credits include Pipe Fiends (2006), Concrete 2 Canvas: More Skateboarders\' Art (Laurence King, 2007), Drawgasmic (2008), The Sunlight Chronicles (2008), Maple Vedas (2010), Visionary Art Yearbook (2010), Divining the Dream (2012), Arte Visionaria (2015), Street Art Themes et Motifs (2018), The New Psychedelic Revolution by James Oroc (Park Street Press, 2018), Feminine Mysticism in Arts (2018), Painted Walls La Habana (2018), and Thrasher Magazine\'s Mail Drop Book (2019). He runs the Artventures travel video series and the Chris Dyer\'s Creative Friends podcast.\n\nHe has exhibited in galleries in Vienna, Bali, Costa Rica, Spain, Paris, Berlin, Tokyo, New York City, Denver, and Los Angeles, and has shown alongside Alex Grey, Shepard Fairey, Robert Venosa, H.R. Giger, and Romio Shrestha. He has worked with skate-art legends Jim Phillips, Wes Humpston, and Andy Howell. His murals are in the Bob Marley Mausoleum and Museum in Nine Mile, Jamaica; the Beatles Ashram in Rishikesh, India (the I am the Eggman piece); the Straat Museum in Amsterdam; the Street Art Museum in Saint Petersburg, Russia; the Arte Urbano museum in Lima; and on Peter Tosh\'s tomb wall in Jamaica, painted on the same trip as the Bob Marley piece. Festival mural credits include MURAL Festival, Art Basel Miami, Burning Man, Shambhala in British Columbia, Boom in Portugal, Rainbow Serpent in Australia, Ozora in Hungary, and Puro Muro in Lima. He live-painted for Diplo, Tipper, Immortal Technique, Nahko Bear, and Cut Chemist, and made album art and concert posters for Ott, String Cheese Incident, Third World, 12th Planet, and The Gaslamp Killer. He was also the artist who first pushed festivals to build him spray-paint walls instead of canvas easels in the early 2010s.\n\nHe teaches workshops at the Montreal Museum of Fine Arts, the National Gallery of Canada, Alex Grey\'s Chapel of Sacred Mirrors (CoSM) in Wappingers Falls, New York, and the Vienna Academy of Visionary Art, plus residencies in Italy, Bali, India, Thailand, and Australia. At CoSM he painted a Mushroom Cafe mural — adding one of his signature gnomes and an elaborate mushroom being above the Cafe/Parlor door — to a landscape originally created by Burgandy Viscosi. Starting in 2017 he ran an art-and-shamanic-healing retreat at Katari Center in the jungle near Tarapoto, Peru. He sold out 5,555 NFTs in 2021 as the Galaktic Gang series, with the community still active on Discord and Twitter. He married Belgian Valerie Lambert in 2010 and divorced in 2016.\n\nAround 2022 he relocated from Montreal to the St. Pete / Gulfport area, drawn by the Dalí Museum. He lives in a purple house in Gulfport with his partner, artist Paulina Casati. For SHINE 2023, he painted Ride on the sPEACEship! on Stewart Moving and Storage — a detailed spaceship made of interlocking creatures trailing rainbow exhaust flames. He has other Gulfport and St. Pete walls including a psychedelic crocodile at Drunken Taco (May 2025) and a piece at Zaza Smoke Shop at 49th Street and 22nd Avenue South. Website: positivecreations.ca and positivecreations.net. Instagram: @chris_dyer.',fn:['Painted in 45 countries','Peruvian-Canadian, based in Montreal','Psychedelic spiritual art meets street culture'],src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/chris-dyer-2023','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chris_Dyer_(artist)','https://positivecreations.ca/about/bio/','https://canvasrebel.com/meet-chris-dyer/','https://thegabber.com/meet-chris-dyer-globally-renowned-visionary-artist/','https://www.wusf.org/arts-culture/2023-10-12/shine-st-pete-mural-festival-2023','https://www.cosm.org/mushroom-cafe/chris-dyer','https://headslifestyle.com/blogs/art/a-portrait-of-the-artist']},
  {id:40,a:'Dave Bonzai',t:'Sunshine City',loc:'1229 Central Ave',bldg:'Cappa & Cappa',lat:27.771326,lng:-82.651037,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'davebonzai',bio:'He paints in chrome. No chrome paint — just black, white, and grey, layered until it looks like liquid metal.\n\nDave Bonzai is from Southampton, England, now based in London. He has been doing street art for over twenty years. In 2016 he developed a technique he calls "Liquid Chrome" — hyper-realistic chrome effects created entirely with spray paint, incorporating reflections of the surrounding environment (buses, windows, buildings) for realism. One of those early pieces, "One Liquid Chrome," is in the permanent collection of the STRAAT Museum in Amsterdam.\n\nHe has painted across the UK, Northern Ireland (Get Up Derry festival), and internationally. Clients include Triumph Motorcycles (a mural at their factory visitor experience in Hinckley) and Denham\'s House Guest Artist series. Nature runs through the chrome — animals and organic forms rendered in metallic illusion.\n\nFor SHINE 2023, he painted "Sunshine City" at 1229 Central Avenue.',desc:'A striking mural on the side of Cappa & Cappa on Central Avenue featuring the words "Sunshine City" in Bonzai\'s signature Liquid Chrome technique — the script lettering appears to be made of dripping, reflective molten metal, rendered entirely with spray paint in shades of silver, white, and grey. The chrome text floats over a tropical sunset scene of palm tree silhouettes against a gradient sky of deep purple, hot pink, and fiery red. A green geometric tree shape with horizontal stripes rises behind the lettering, and sharp diagonal lines in pink and white cut across the black background, giving the piece a retro synthwave energy. Signed "@DaBonzai" in the lower right corner. Bonzai described it as "a modern piece with a retro twist that the people of St Pete could relate to."',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/dave-bonzai.jpeg',from:'London, England',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'STRAAT Museum Amsterdam permanent collection; SHINE 2023 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'He paints in chrome. No chrome paint — just black, white, and grey, layered until it looks like liquid metal.\n\nDave Bonzai is a street artist originally from the South Coast of England, specifically Southampton, who has spent many years based in London. Like many of his UK counterparts, he was influenced when hip hop first reached the UK, and he has been doing street art for over twenty years. Nature plays a big part in his pieces, and he takes photos wherever he goes to help shape his work — animals and organic forms rendered in metallic illusion run through his catalogue.\n\nIn 2016 he developed the technique he calls "Liquid Chrome" — hyper-realistic chrome effect art created entirely with spray paint, with the ambition to create photo-realistic pieces. He has refined the craft over the years to achieve a high-end finish through a method of layering different colored paint, predominantly black, white, and grey. A key element in Bonzai\'s chrome street art is referencing his surroundings in the work itself: when he paints in London, if he knows the wall is on a bus route, he adds highlights of red to imitate the reflection of a bus, bringing the piece to life. The reflections of buildings, windows, and sky are folded into the chrome to imitate its natural reflective properties.\n\nOne of his early Liquid Chrome pieces, "One Liquid Chrome" (2016), is in the permanent collection of the STRAAT Museum in Amsterdam at NDSM-Plein 1, 1033 WC Amsterdam. Painted in spray paint on polyester, the piece displays the world map in the liquid chrome style, melting as if coated in chromium. According to the museum, the painting represents the artist\'s desire for equality, since we are all connected to one another and live on the same planet.\n\nIn May 2023 he collaborated with Triumph Motorcycles on Triumph X Bonzai, a one-of-a-kind piece at the Triumph Factory Visitor Experience in Hinckley, created to celebrate the Triumph Chrome Collection. The mural was created entirely with spray paint over a two-day period and is described as an abstract interpretation of the Triumph chroming process — an explosion of liquid metal in predominantly black, white, and grey. He and Triumph went back and forth on initial designs, referencing the scale of the wall, until the design had the right flow and energy. Negative space was left toward the bottom of the mural so a Chrome Edition bike could be situated there; when photographed, the bike appears to come alive and become part of the artwork. He included reflections of the building\'s windows and the open blue sky in the piece. A Triumph X Bonzai film documents the work from start to finish on Triumph\'s YouTube channel. Bonzai has said, "This piece really captures the beauty and quality of the chrome that\'s on the bikes," and "I\'m always interested in seeing the progression of certain artists and how they push the boundaries and for me I feel like the liquid Chrome is my way of doing it."\n\nHe is represented among the artists working with Wood Street Walls in Walthamstow, East London, alongside ATM, ATMA, Axel Void, Bread Collective, Carl Cashman, Conor Harrington, Dale Grimshaw, Eelus, Elno, Fanakapan, Frankie Strand, HATCH, Invader, Irony, Jane Laurie, Lillylou, Louis Masai, Malarky, Marc Craig, Mark McClure, Maser, Mr Cenz, Phlegm, Remi Rough, Ronzo, Shok-1, Static, Teddy Baden, Toasters, and Xenz. His Wood Street Walls piece is located at the Ravenswood Industrial Estate, Walthamstow, E17 9HQ.\n\nOther clients and projects include Denham\'s House Guest Artist series. He has also painted at the Upside Gallery in Bournemouth, a series of eleven separate pieces created under an overpass and documented by Inspiring City. He has painted across the UK and Northern Ireland, including the Get Up Derry festival, as well as internationally.\n\nFor SHINE 2023 in St. Petersburg, Florida, he painted "Sunshine City" at 1229 Central Avenue, applying his Liquid Chrome technique to the Florida wall.\n\nKeywords and proper nouns for search: Dave Bonzai, Bonzai, Liquid Chrome, One Liquid Chrome, Sunshine City, Southampton, South Coast England, London, UK street artist, hip hop influence, STRAAT Museum, STRAAT Museum Amsterdam, NDSM-Plein, spray paint on polyester, world map, Triumph Motorcycles, Triumph X Bonzai, Triumph Chrome Collection, Triumph Factory Visitor Experience, Hinckley, Denham\'s House Guest Artist series, Wood Street Walls, Walthamstow, Ravenswood Industrial Estate, E17 9HQ, Get Up Derry festival, Northern Ireland, Upside Gallery, Bournemouth, Inspiring City, SHINE 2023, St. Petersburg, 1229 Central Avenue, hyper-realistic chrome, chrome effect art, black white and grey, reflective surfaces, nature, animals, organic forms, photo-realistic, two-day mural, abstract chrome process.',fn:['Paints in chrome — no chrome paint, just black, white, and grays','Creates hyperrealistic metallic illusions','London-based, originally from Southampton — visiting artist for SHINE 2023'],src:['https://www.instagram.com/davebonzai/','https://straatmuseum.com/en/collection-database/bonzai-one-liquid-chrome','https://www.triumphmotorcycles.com/for-the-ride/news/brand/triumph-x-bonzai-2023-06-01','https://www.woodstreetwalls.co.uk/bonzai','https://inspiringcity.com/category/street-art/european-street-art/uk-street-art/uk-street-artists/dave-bonzai/']},
  {id:41,a:'Fabstraq',t:'The Energy of Color',loc:'2724 6th Ave S',bldg:'Clay Coop',lat:27.764656,lng:-82.670531,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'fabstraq919',bio:'He started making art at ten to process emotions he couldn\'t name. He is now a certified art therapy life coach.\n\nShawn Rainey — Fabstraq — is based in Tampa. He has no formal art education. His style is contemporary abstract — vibrant colors and defining lines, one-of-a-kind pieces meant to be thought-provoking. He also works as an audio engineer and music producer, and writes poetry.\n\nHe volunteers regularly at the Glazer Children\'s Museum and has done charity shows for the National Breast Cancer Foundation and Chemo Angels. Bay News 9 profiled him as an "Everyday Hero" for his community work with children. His eventual goal is to travel worldwide establishing art programs.\n\nFor SHINE 2023, he painted at Charlie Parker Pottery on 6th Avenue South.',desc:'A bold, graphic mural covering the entire front face of the Clay Coop building. The composition is a patchwork of large geometric shapes — triangles, rectangles, and angular forms — in saturated flat colors: bright green, royal blue, hot pink, red, purple, yellow, and teal. A heart shape, a star, zigzag patterns, and concentric rectangles are scattered throughout the design. The overall effect is like an abstract quilt or a Memphis Design-inspired collage, with clean edges and no blending between the color fields. Two tiki statues flank the entrance, and the artist\'s "FABSTRAQ" tag appears in the lower right corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/fabstraq.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Bay News 9 Everyday Hero; SHINE 2023 selected artist',fw:[{name:'fabstraq.com',url:'https://fabstraq.com'}],sbio:'He started making art at ten to process emotions he couldn\'t name. He is now a certified art therapy life coach.\n\nShawn Rainey — Fabstraq — is based in Tampa. The name is shorthand for abstract art that makes you feel fabulous. He has no formal art education and credits art as the vehicle that pulled him out of childhood trauma, isolation, and rejection. In his own words: "It helped me with a lot of struggles that I was having. It helped me release a lot of emotions and feelings that came about that I didn\'t understand. It was the only thing that made me feel alive." His style is contemporary abstract — vibrant colors and defining lines, one-of-a-kind pieces meant to be thought-provoking. He also works as an audio engineer and music producer, and writes poetry.\n\nHe volunteers regularly at the Glazer Children\'s Museum in Tampa, leading Member Mornings sessions where each child receives a miniature canvas and is invited to explore their creativity through abstract work — no competition, no comparison, no shared subject to copy. His wife Paula and daughters Geelee and Geeotta volunteer alongside him. He also hosts painting parties. "They make their painting as beautiful as they want it to be," he has said of the children. "They inspire and make their own happiness where they see, \'Oh, I can do that. I can paint more than a stick figure.\'" His framing: in abstract, every child wins.\n\nBeyond the museum work, he has done charity shows benefiting the National Breast Cancer Foundation Inc. and Chemo Angels. His eventual goal is to travel worldwide establishing art programs.\n\nHe was profiled by Bay News 9 (Spectrum Bay News 9) in their "Everyday Hero" segment on August 5, 2019, by Bill Murphy, recognizing his work inspiring children to grow their imaginations through Fabstraq.\n\nIn August 2021 he was one of nine featured artists in the Green Book of Tampa Bay Group Art Exhibit at TheStudio@620 in St. Petersburg (620 1st Avenue South, St. Petersburg, FL 33701). The show opened during Second Saturday Art Walk on Saturday, August 14, 2021 from 5:00 to 9:00 p.m., and ran through Second Saturday September 11, 2021 with gallery hours and a closing reception that evening. The Green Book of Tampa Bay is a Black-owned business directory serving Pinellas and Hillsborough counties that promotes Black-owned businesses, Black artists, and historical and cultural sites. The other featured artists in that exhibit were Ebony Adside (Eb&Floww), Elaine Chambliss Dogan, Lauren Gay, Denzel Johnson-Green, Myiah Pink (Soul Lessons), Ashley Rivers (Nature of Fire), Roosevelt Skinner (Earth Arts by Roose), and Rolph Vassor (Vassor Enterprises).\n\nFor SHINE 2023 — the festival\'s ninth year, October 13-22 — he was selected as one of six Bay Area artists alongside Michael Vasquez, Sarah Sheppard, Chris Dyer, Artist Jones (Tracey "Artist" Jones), and Rhys Meatyard. The roster was announced August 18, 2023 by St Pete Catalyst writer Bill DeYoung at an event held at the Dali Museum, where festival director Jenee Priebe also revealed the festival\'s first two-year title sponsor: Reflection St. Pete, the 88-residence, 18-story condominium tower under construction adjacent to Mirror Lake (scheduled to debut in 2024, with a planned three-story SHINE mural on its façade during the 10th-anniversary year). SHINE 2023 added 14 new murals plus a series of street art-related events, tours, and "Bright Spot" community projects. He painted at Charlie Parker Pottery on 6th Avenue South in St. Petersburg.\n\nSources used in writing this expanded bio include the Bay News 9 "Everyday Hero" August 5, 2019 profile by Bill Murphy, TheStudio@620\'s Green Book of Tampa Bay Group Art Exhibit listing, and the St Pete Catalyst article "Local artists, new partnership announced for mural festival" by Bill DeYoung, August 18, 2023.',src:['https://fabstraq.com/','https://www.baynews9.com/fl/tampa/news/2019/08/05/everyday-hero--local-artist-inspires-children-to-grow-their-imaginations','https://thestudioat620.org/events/green-book-of-tampa-bay-group-art-exhibit/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/local-artists-new-partnership-announced-for-mural-festival/']},
  {id:42,a:'Greater Public Studio',t:'Dive Into Discovery',loc:'6th Ave S & 2nd St S',bldg:'USF Campus',lat:27.76005333,lng:-82.637055,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'greaterpublicstudio',bio:'Some of their walls move when you point a phone at them. The Tampa Bay Buccaneers commissioned one. This one sits on a USF St. Pete campus road.\n\nGreater Public Studio was founded by Jay Giroux in Tampa, Florida. Giroux brings fifteen-plus years across painting, photography, printmaking, digital media, environmental graphics, and installation. The studio provides brand strategy, hand-painted murals, fabrication, installation design, curatorial services, and environmental graphics.\n\nTheir work explores incidental gestures and lowbrow symbolism from the urban streetscape, balancing popular culture with high formalism. Recent work emphasizes non-objective forms over representation. Some of their murals include augmented reality layers viewable through the Artivive app.\n\nClients include the Tampa Bay Buccaneers (mural at BAR HWRD in Tampa\'s SoHo District) and venues across Florida. For SHINE 2023, they painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A colorful, illustrative mural spanning a large wall near the USF St. Pete campus. The composition features a fantastical townscape with stacked buildings, winding paths, and small figures exploring the scene. Bright blues, oranges, and greens fill the design in a style that feels like an oversized storybook illustration.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/greater-public-studio.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2023 selected artist',fw:[{name:'greaterpublicstudio.com',url:'https://greaterpublicstudio.com'}],sbio:'Three friends bet on themselves in 2017. They opened a studio in Tampa.\n\nGreater Public Studio was founded by Jay Giroux in Tampa, Florida. Giroux brings fifteen-plus years across painting, photography, printmaking, digital media, environmental graphics, and installation. The studio provides brand strategy, hand-painted murals, fabrication, installation design, curatorial services, and environmental graphics.\n\nTheir work explores incidental gestures and lowbrow symbolism from the urban streetscape, balancing popular culture with high formalism. Recent work emphasizes non-objective forms over representation. Some of their murals include augmented reality layers viewable through the Artivive app.\n\nClients include the Tampa Bay Buccaneers (mural at BAR HWRD in Tampa\'s SoHo District) and venues across Florida. For SHINE 2023, they painted in St. Petersburg.',src:['https://greaterpublicstudio.com/','https://greaterpublicstudio.com/gps-your-go-to-mural-painting-company-in-tampa-for-stunning-results/','https://greaterpublicstudio.com/projects/we-are-the-krewe/','https://jaygiroux.com/about/','https://greaterpublicstudio.com/lets-go-bucs-gps-x-tampa-bay-bucs-mural-at-bar-hwrd/']},
  {id:43,a:'Hannah Eddy',t:'With Flying Colors',loc:'1019 Arlington Ave N',bldg:'CASA Support Center',lat:27.772697,lng:-82.649439,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'hannaheddyart',bio:'She grew up on the coast of Maine. Right out of college, she started collaborating with snowboard and skate companies.\n\nHannah Eddy is based in Reno, Nevada. She trained in oil painting and moved straight into outdoor brands — Patagonia, Dakine, BlackStrap, Sierra Nevada Brewing Co., Protect Our Winters, and Dedicated, a Swedish sustainable clothing label. Her style is colorful pop-surrealism: bold linework, graphic dynamism, women and animals at exaggerated scale, joyful imagery undercut by skulls, cobwebs, and flames alongside technicolor mountains and forests.\n\nShe paints murals because "no one has to pay to see it, and there\'s no barrier to entry." She has painted across the US, including Reno and Oklahoma City. For SHINE 2023, she brought her work to St. Petersburg.',desc:'A wide, whimsical mural covering the full side of the CASA Support Center on a soft mint green background. The scene is a dense tropical ecosystem rendered in bold black outlines with playful, illustration-style fills in blues, greens, purples, oranges, and pinks. A large green tree frog sits center-left among oversized leaves and flowers, while a purple heron or crane flies to the right carrying a flower in its beak — a nod to the traditional peace dove, reimagined with Florida wildlife. Snakes wind through the foliage, insects hover between blooms, and cartoon raindrops fall from stylized clouds. The left side bursts with warmer tones — orange, yellow, and pink — while the right side fades to line-art outlines on the mint base, as if the color is still spreading. The piece was designed to create a positive, lighthearted, and welcoming atmosphere for CASA\'s mission as a domestic violence support center.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/hannah-eddy.jpeg',from:'Reno, NV',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2023 selected artist',fw:[{name:'hannaheddyart.com',url:'https://www.hannaheddyart.com'}],sbio:'She grew up on the coast of Maine. Right out of college, she started collaborating with snowboard and skate companies.\n\nHannah Eddy was born in 1987 and grew up by the ocean in Maine. A love of mountains brought her west, and she is now based in Reno, Nevada, close to the Sierra Nevada range. She had traditional training in oil painting, then moved straight into work with snowboard and skate companies after college. Snowboarding, skateboarding, and surfing have been part of her life since she was young, and that culture shaped both her subject matter and her visual language.\n\nHer style is pop-surrealism: harmonious shapes, crisp lines, balanced colors, bold linework, and graphic dynamism. Her recurring cast of characters tends to be women and animals, often at exaggerated scale, moving through what she calls "flow." She paints technicolor mountains and forests, yolky suns, wavy rainbows, and elongated figures in motion. The joyful imagery is undercut by memento mori — pastel pink flowers growing from skulls\' empty eye sockets, cobwebs stretched across stylized animal bodies, deep red flames flanking mountains and trees. She often blows up small things — flowers, bees, snails, mushrooms, frogs — to remind viewers of the details that get overlooked. Many pieces include short phrases like "Part of it all," "It is all temporary," and "find your flow," which come to her while mountain biking, hiking, or snowboarding.\n\nHer select client list includes Vans, Santa Cruz Skateboards, Hydroflask, Patagonia, Nixon, Sodastream, Lush Cosmetics, Skullcandy, Worn Wear, Snowboarder Magazine, Protect Our Winters, Dakine, Autumn Headwear, Nature Backs, Strainge Beast, Giro, Woodward, GNU Snowboards, Arcade Belts, Traeger Grills, Airblaster, Meow Skateboards, and Intersectional Environmentalist. She has also worked with BlackStrap and Sierra Nevada Brewing Co., and with Dedicated, a Swedish sustainable clothing label. For Sierra Nevada Brewing Co. she painted a Strainge Beast mural in Reno featuring the brand\'s Lemon and Watermelon characters, which was then put on a limited custom tee. For BlackStrap she was profiled in their Meet Our Artist series. For Protect Our Winters she is part of the Creative Alliance and designed the Team POW Membership Tee and the Stoke the Vote Tee.\n\nShe started painting murals after a pro snowboarder-turned-muralist invited her to a mural festival following a few small free walls she had done. She has described that festival as feeling like she "went to mural school for a week." Since then she has painted across the US. In Reno, her murals include Find Your Flow (2021) and walls in Midtown. In 2023 she painted It\'s All Temporary in Oklahoma City. For SHINE 2023 she brought her work to St. Petersburg, Florida.\n\nShe also showed at the Reno Tahoe International Art Show, where she created the event\'s original design — a colorful assemblage of flora, fauna, and the Truckee River with the tagline "Reno. Who knew?" — which appeared on tote bags, posters, and stickers. She moves between media constantly: brand design work in her Reno studio, gallery paintings, and large-scale murals. Studio pieces from 2021 to 2023 include Don\'t Wait (acrylic on panel, 12 x 12 in., 2023), Can\'t Hide (acrylic on panel, 20 x 24 in., 2023), Don\'t Let It Get To You (acrylic on recycled skateboard panel, 12 x 12 in., 2023), Fresh Start (acrylic and spray paint on panel, 24 x 24 in., 2023), and the Skull Snail series (acrylic on panel, 6 x 6 in. each, 2022). She works in acrylic, pen and ink, spray paint, and digital media on iPad with Apple Pencil.\n\nHer environmental message runs through everything. She partners with environmental organizations and outdoor brands to talk about climate change through what she calls a lighthearted lens, balancing playfulness with gravitas. She has said, "When you love something, you want to protect it," and frames preservation around small individual actions like riding a bike to the store or planting a garden. She paints public murals because "no one has to pay to see it, and there\'s no barrier to entry," and has said she could not continue an art practice without that.\n\nShe was profiled by Aleina Grace Edwards in Southwest Contemporary in September 2023 in a piece titled "Hannah Eddy Paints Life and Death in Technicolor," with photos by Tre Packard. She was also profiled by Sierra Nevada Brewing Co. in December 2021 in "The Mural Life of Hannah Eddy."\n\nWebsite: hannaheddyart.com. Instagram: @hannaheddyart.',fn:['Grew up on the coast of Maine','Botanical illustration meets street art','Delicate floral work at massive scale'],src:['https://hannaheddyart.com/','https://www.hannaheddyart.com/about','https://southwestcontemporary.com/hannah-eddy-paints-life-and-death/','https://sierranevada.com/blog/strainger-zone/the-mural-life-of-hannah-eddy','https://blackstrap.com/blogs/journal/meet-our-artist-hannah-eddy','https://protectourwinters.org/alliance/hannah-eddy-creative/']},
  {id:44,a:'HOXXOH',t:'Rainbird',loc:'2025 3rd Ave S',bldg:'Morean Workshop Space',lat:27.768592,lng:-82.660671,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'hoxxoh',bio:'"I\'d rather be more like Diego Rivera than Banksy. I\'m an artist; I\'m a painter who sometimes paints murals."\n\nDouglas Hoekzema — HOXXOH — was born in 1980 in Boulder, Colorado and grew up in Fort Lauderdale. He started graffiti at fifteen. He earned a five-year degree in architecture with double majors in art history and painting, plus a philosophy minor, graduating in 2008. After school he worked at a salvaged-wood lumber mill while developing his spray technique behind a tattoo shop.\n\nHis signature is massive-scale op-art — concentric circles, psychedelic spirals, logarithmic patterns executed freehand in spray paint. He also uses paint pendulums, garden sprinklers, and dust pans. His work is guided by sacred geometry and time. He joined Miami Style Graffiti crew around 2008 and now works from a studio in Little River, Miami.\n\nHe painted a 135-foot mural on Miami\'s Arlo Hotel, walls across Wynwood and Overtown, and projects in Paris, Grenoble, New York, Philadelphia, Detroit, and Sacramento. He is a two-time SHINE artist — 2015 and 2023.',desc:'A huge panoramic piece spanning the full length of the Morean Workshop building. Three birds in flight — likely roseate spoonbills or herons — are rendered in HOXXOH\'s signature black-and-white ripple-line technique, their bodies made up of dense, undulating wave patterns that give them an almost holographic shimmer. The birds soar across a luminous teal and aquamarine background that itself ripples with flowing white and dark streaks, making the entire wall feel like it\'s in motion. The scale is impressive — each bird spans a full story of the building. It reads as abstract pattern from a distance and resolves into recognizable bird forms as you get closer.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/hoxxoh.jpeg',from:'Miami, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Miami New Times MasterMind Awards 2016 (Honorable Mention); SHINE 2015 and 2023 selected artist',fw:[{name:'elhoxxoh.com',url:'https://www.elhoxxoh.com'}],sbio:'"I\'d rather be more like Diego Rivera than Banksy. I\'m an artist; I\'m a painter who sometimes paints murals."\n\nDouglas Hoekzema — HOXXOH — was born in 1980 in Boulder, Colorado and moved to South Florida as a kid, growing up in Fort Lauderdale before relocating to Miami in 2008. He painted his first wall in high school in 1995 at age fifteen, with parents who supported his interest in art from the start and encouraged a balance between studio and public work. In 1999 architect Michael Krupnick selected him to paint a mural for a jazz club. He went on to earn a five-year degree in architecture with a double major in art history and painting and a minor in philosophy, graduating in 2008.\n\nWhen the real estate market crashed the year he graduated, his planned architecture career evaporated. He took a job at a lumber mill that worked with salvaged wood, spending a year there during the day and painting at night behind a friend\'s tattoo shop. That period is when he discovered the spray paint mark-making technique he still works with today and developed his interest in mark-making devices and mechanisms. Around 2008 he joined the Miami graffiti crew Miami Style Graffiti — also known as Miami Style God and MSG — where he describes himself as a "background guy" on big mural productions. His studio sits next to the train tracks in Little River, Miami, where freight trains passing remind him of using train sides as traveling canvases when he started in graffiti.\n\nHis signature is large-scale op-art — concentric circles, psychedelic spirals, parabolic curves and logarithmic patterns executed freehand in spray paint. The curve technique uses a U-shape with half the cone of the paint; the can never touches the wall, all wrist. Beyond aerosol, he works with paint pendulums (a suspended one-liter bottle of paint anchored to the ceiling that produces spirals and parabolic curves), dust pans, trays, troughs, industrial airless paint sprayers, garden irrigation tools and sprinklers, and a hosepipe-and-sprinkler mechanism he coined "The RainBird." His practice is process-driven and experimental with a focus on technique, guided by sacred geometry and time, organizing chaos and finding the moment when a pattern slips out of it.\n\nNotable works include a 135-foot mural on the Arlo Hotel in Miami, completed by HOXXOH and a team of five over a little more than two weeks — his largest piece to date. The RainBird technique was christened in summer 2019 on a 120-foot by 40-foot piece at the Jewish Health Centre in North Miami, painted with acrylic and an airless sprayer over two weeks. His psychedelic spirals appear on buildings throughout Wynwood and Overtown, and his murals reach Paris, Grenoble, New York, Philadelphia, Detroit, and Sacramento. Grenoble, Paris and Miami are his three favorite places to paint. His favorite spot in Miami is the Miami Marine Stadium, abandoned after Hurricane Andrew in 1992 and converted by artists into an informal "wall of fame."\n\nHe completed a month-long residency in Paris in February 2020 that produced the HOX.TRANSFER series, including HOX.TRANSFER 1.1 — selected for his 2021 solo show at LoftDu34 in Paris and licensed to ARTXPUZZLES as a 12" x 16.5", 285-piece jigsaw puzzle. ARTXPUZZLES, organized by Rachel Vancelette with Rachel Margolin from Underground, is a global initiative supporting artists through Artist Relief and the First Responder Children\'s Foundation during the COVID-19 pandemic; the roster also includes Banksy, Idris Khan, Marilyn Minter, and Donald Baechler. He has also released NFTs during the early stages of that market.\n\nIn 2016 he received an Honorable Mention in the Miami New Times MasterMind Awards. He has shown with Wide Open Walls in Sacramento and is a two-time SHINE artist — Tampa Bay\'s St. Pete mural festival — in 2015 and 2023. Summer 2023 trips included The Netherlands, Italy, and Hawaii. Has been a full-time practicing artist for the past decade-plus of a 25-year-and-counting career.\n\nHe cites Futura 2000 as a foundational influence — the abstract street artist who blurred virtual reality and hyperrealism with a retro-futuristic twist and confirmed his approach to pictorial projection techniques. His other inspirations are nature, music, and the urban environment — the interplay of light and shadow, the movement of the wind, the pulse of city streets. He takes long hikes, kayaks, and camps to step away from horns, traffic, alarms, and footsteps, fascinated by the correlation between order and chaos in nature. Off the wall he spends time at home with his wife and dog, and brings family on the road when he can.\n\nHe has a separate venture, HOXXOH Champagne, producing fully luminous champagne bottles — Hoxxoh Gold, Hoxxoh Ruby, Hoxxoh Grand Cru, and Hoxxoh Saphir Demi-Sec, plus the Hox cuvée line including HOX Grand Cru Blanc de Blancs 2014, HOX Grande Réserve, HOX Grande Réserve Blanc de Blancs and HOX Grande Réserve Rosé. The Saphir label includes a 0.45-carat sapphire cut in a champagne-drop shape. Best sommelier in the world Andreas Larsson endorsed the Grand Cru.\n\nWebsite: elhoxxoh.com and hoxxoh.com. Instagram: @hoxxoh. TikTok: @hoxxoh.',fn:['Prefers Diego Rivera to Banksy','Geometric optical illusions and vortexes','Miami-based, international festival artist'],src:['https://wynwoodwalls.com/artists/hoxxoh','https://www.hoxxoh.com/','https://www.miaminewtimes.com/arts/2016-mastermind-awards-8826445','https://www.elhoxxoh.com/about-1','https://therooster.com/articles/artist-hoxxoh-murals/','https://www.miaminewtimes.com/arts/mastermind-2016-honorable-mention-douglas-hoekzema-8244956','https://upmag.com/hoxxoh/','https://www.wideopenwalls.com/artists/hoxxoh/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/hoxxoh']},
  {id:45,a:'Kelly Quinn',t:'Mangrove Connections',loc:'2553 1st Ave N',bldg:'LPL Financial',lat:27.77203833,lng:-82.66821333,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'kellyofthewild',bio:'She was Artist-in-Residence at The Florida Aquarium — live-painting, teaching classes, collaborating with educators. Conservation is the work.\n\nKelly Quinn grew up in rural Florida and graduated from the University of Florida\'s College of Fine Arts in 2016. That same year she founded Kelly of the Wild. In 2018 she co-founded Canvas of the Wild with Blake Wheeler, where she serves as art director. Her focus is wildlife and nature — digital illustration, acrylic painting, and large-scale conservation-driven murals.\n\nClients include The Florida Aquarium, the Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation (Crystal River Mural), Weedon Island Preserve, Coral Restoration Foundation, Biscayne National Park, the National Parks Association, the Oceanic Preservation Society, and the International Sea Keepers Society — which named her Artist of the Year in both 2021 and 2022.\n\nShe is based in St. Petersburg. For SHINE 2023, she painted locally.',desc:'A nature-focused mural on a black background depicting the mangrove ecosystem of Tampa Bay. A central mangrove tree with tangled brown roots and dense green foliage spans the full height of the wall, with a large golden sun glowing behind it. Blue ocean waves splash outward on both sides. Above the canopy, a pink roseate spoonbill takes flight on the left while a brown pelican perches on the right. Below the waterline, the mangrove roots reveal an underwater world — a smalltooth sawfish (an endangered Florida species), a dolphin, a snook, a red snapper, and smaller fish swim among the roots. The piece was created in collaboration with the Oceanic Preservation Society to celebrate the 50th anniversary of the Endangered Species Act, and includes interactive NFC-tag technology linking viewers to a digital knowledge base about each species depicted.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/kelly-quinn.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Artist of the Year, International Sea Keepers Society (2021, 2022); Featured Artist, Oceanic Preservation Society (2022); SHINE 2023 selected artist',fw:[{name:'kellyofthewild.com',url:'https://kellyofthewild.com'}],sbio:'She was Artist-in-Residence at The Florida Aquarium — live-painting, teaching classes, collaborating with educators. Conservation is the work.\n\nKelly Quinn was born and raised in rural Florida on the headwaters of the Everglades, surrounded by conservation land. She explored the area looking for critters and drew them with paper and pencil, which set the trajectory. She graduated from the University of Florida College of Fine Arts in 2016 and is based in St. Petersburg, Florida.\n\nThat same year, 2016, she founded her personal brand, Kelly of the Wild. In 2018 she co-founded Canvas of the Wild with Blake Wheeler, a creative design, development, and publishing studio focused on supporting STEAM education and engaging individuals with environmental science through the creative process. The studio works through infographics, books, workshops, and interactive environments. Kelly serves as Art Director, designing natural landscape murals for educational campaigns, creating illustrations for coloring and activity books, and developing scientific graphics for communicating research.\n\nHer practice spans digital illustration, acrylic painting, and large-scale conservation-driven murals. She has worked with small businesses, large nonprofits, local government, and corporate brands on educational content — children\'s books, murals, social media campaigns, infographics, team building, art lessons, and personal art commissions.\n\nClients and partners include The Florida Aquarium, the Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation, Weedon Island Preserve, Coral Restoration Foundation, Biscayne National Park, the National Parks Association, the Oceanic Preservation Society, and the International Sea Keepers Society. The International Sea Keepers Society named her Artist of the Year in both 2021 and 2022. The Oceanic Preservation Society named her its Featured Artist for 2022.\n\nA central project is the Crystal River Mural, a partnership between the Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation and Canvas of the Wild. The mural campaign aims to raise awareness of the Florida Wildlife Corridor — an 18-million-acre statewide network of connected lands and waters, with 10 million acres protected as Federal lands, state parks and forests, or private lands under conservation easements, and 8 million unprotected acres considered opportunity areas for conservation. Crystal River is a critical link in the Corridor. The mural builds on the 2021 teen expedition from Rainbow Springs to Homosassa Bay with three local high school students, documented in the film Home Waters by Jennifer Adler and Ian Segebarth. Sponsors of the Crystal River Mural include Burt Eno (a local wild Florida advocate) and the Citrus County Chamber of Commerce. In-kind sponsors include Lumen and Sunbelt Rentals. Partners include Canvas of the Wild, the City of Crystal River, and Crystal River Main Street, with special thanks to the Citrus County Education Foundation. The Florida Fish and Wildlife Conservation Commission contributed species information for the mural, which features the Wood Duck, Florida Black Bear, Belted Kingfisher, Eastern Screech Owl, Red-Eared Slider, American Alligator, Anhinga, Largemouth Bass, Everglades Crayfish, Spotted Sunfish, Bluegill, Redbreast Sunfish, Roseate Spoonbill, Eastern Tiger Salamander, Manatee, Striped Mullet, Atlantic Blue Crab, Florida Gar, Great Blue Heron, River Otter, Osprey, Yellow-crowned Night Heron, and Purple Gallinule.\n\nHer work has been featured in Biscayne National Park, the National Parks Association, and on ABC Action News. Press and editorial features include The Tampa Bay Business Journal, Sun & Surf Magazine, Connect Magazine, Fox 13 News, Marine Diaries, Sharks 4 Kids, the Tampa Bay Arts & Education Network, Jupiter Inlet Magazine, and Hobe Sound Magazine.\n\nHer shop and personal portfolio at kellyofthewild.com sells framed prints, scientific posters, coloring books, small original paintings, and daily paintings — including framed watercolor reef fish, sea turtle surfacing pieces, framed watercolor sea turtles, and the original Octopus Explorations. She also offers commissions and works as an art instructor and educator alongside her wildlife artist and wildlife photographer practice.\n\nIn her own words: "I was born and raised on the headwaters of the Everglades, where I learned the land and water were a part of my identity, and the charismatic animals I shared my home with were my fellow neighbors, all of which had a story worthy of being shared. Today, I use art to communicate these wild stories and highlight the underlying science behind each inspiration to help form deeper connections between people and nature."\n\nFor SHINE 2023, she painted locally in St. Petersburg. Find her at kellyofthewild.com and on Instagram at @kellyofthewild.',src:['https://kellyofthewild.com/','https://kellyofthewild.com/pages/kelly-quinn','https://www.kellyquinn.art/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/kelly-quinn-2023','https://fl-wildlife-corridor-foundation.shorthandstories.com/crystal-river-mural/index.html']},
  {id:46,a:'Loretta Lizzio',t:'The Sirens Call',loc:'2875 7th Ave S',bldg:'Stewart Moving & Storage',lat:27.762868,lng:-82.673163,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'loretta_lizzio',bio:'She was the first female artist commissioned for Melbourne\'s Hellenic Museum — painting three fearless women from ancient Greek history and myth.\n\nLoretta Lizzio was born in 1986 and is based on the Gold Coast, Australia. Her work lives in Romanticism and nostalgia — "forgotten fairy tales, spectacles of cinema, fleeting glances and dog-eared National Geographics." She works across painting, murals, illustration, photography, and collage.\n\nShe has painted across Melbourne, Vancouver, London, Brisbane, and Darwin. Festival credits include Vancouver Mural Festival, Wall to Wall Festival Benalla, Brisbane Street Art Festival, Wonderwalls Festival Port Kembla, and Darwin Street Festival. Clients include New Balance, Toyota x Virgin Airlines, Element, and Wacom. She is represented by The Drawing Arm in Australia and has shown at Haven Gallery in New York, Nucleus Gallery in Portland, and Beinart Gallery in Melbourne. Featured in Juxtapoz and Beautiful Bizarre Magazine.\n\nFor SHINE 2023, she traveled from Australia to paint in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A wide mural on the Stewart Moving & Storage building depicting three women as sirens from Greek mythology against a deep teal-green background. The figures emerge from lush green foliage, their pale skin overlaid with intricate leaf patterns and delicate line work that makes them appear to grow from the plants themselves. Their dark hair flows and intertwines across the top of the composition, creating a canopy effect. The sirens gaze outward with serene yet subtly unsettling expressions — beautiful and sinister at once. Signed "Loretta Lizzio" in the upper right corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/loretta-lizzio.jpeg',from:'Melbourne, Australia',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2023 selected artist',fw:[{name:'lorettalizzio.com',url:'https://www.lorettalizzio.com'}],sbio:'She was the first female artist commissioned for Melbourne\'s Hellenic Museum — painting three fearless women from ancient Greek history and myth.\n\nLoretta Lizzio was born in 1986 and is based on the Gold Coast, Australia. She has worked across publishing, photography, and advertising while continuing to grow as a mural artist, and her work has appeared on walls and in galleries across Australia, the United Kingdom, France, Canada, and the United States. Her practice spans painting, murals, illustration, photography, and collage, in pen, pencil, and oils on a variety of surfaces. Her work lives in Romanticism and nostalgia — what she calls "forgotten fairy tales, spectacles of cinema, fleeting glances and dog-eared National Geographics" — and her recurring subject is the strong female figure. She aims for work that is sensual, mysterious, energy-giving, calming, and reflective at once. She has spoken publicly about a life-changing accident and adjusting to motherhood as experiences she now draws on as inspiration.\n\nThe Hellenic Museum commission was part of the museum\'s Dialogue Series, a program that reimagines antiquity and features women at the forefront of the narrative. Lizzio joined a cohort that included Bill Henson\'s photographic series, Sam Jinks\'s hyperrealist sculpture of the goddess Iris, and "Renegades" by Spanish street artists PichiAvo. She collaborated with Head Curator Sarah Craig and Assistant Curator Sara Prica through video meetings and emails, and worked artefacts on display in the museum into her designs. She made costumes and wax tablets, photographed them, then drew up the final design. The three women she painted are Hydna of Scione (diver, Battle of Salamis), Anyte of Tegea (poet, ca. 3rd century BCE), and Agnodike of Athens (physician, ca. 300 BCE). She began work on the museum forecourt at 280 William Street, Melbourne, on Thursday 2 December 2021, and the launch was tied to the museum\'s BLOCK PARTY festival, where visitors watched the murals come to life. Her favorite mythological subjects include Aphrodite, Poseidon, Kerberos, and Medusa.\n\nSolo exhibitions: "Lonesome Souls" at Juddy Roller Gallery, Melbourne (2018); "Dearly Beloved" at Outré Gallery, Melbourne (2016); "Subconscious" at Private Space, Gold Coast (2013).\n\nGroup exhibitions: "Vanguard" at Outre Gallery, Melbourne (2021 and 2022); Brisbane Outdoor Gallery curated by @MCRT.Studio (2022); "Wish You Were Here" at Nucleus Gallery, Portland Oregon (2019 and 2021); "The Knowable Unknown" at Wow x Wow Gallery, Scotland (2021); "Antipodes" at Beinart Gallery, Melbourne (2021); "Threesome" at 19Karen Gallery, Gold Coast (2019); "Mythos" at Haven Gallery, New York (2018); "Terra Oztralis" at Outré Gallery, Melbourne (2018); "Trouble Makers" at Vs Gallery, Melbourne (2018); "The Next Level" at Silo Park, Auckland, Comber Street Studio, Sydney, and Besser Space, Melbourne (2018); "Element Eden" in Los Angeles (2014); "King Brown Magazine" at The Tate, Sydney (2013); "I Used to Skate Once" at The Zoo, Brisbane (2013); "Made to Endure" at China Heights Gallery, Sydney (2013); and a duo show with Sean Scott at the Old Burleigh Theatre Arcade, Gold Coast (2012).\n\nFestival credits: SHINE Mural Festival, St. Petersburg, Florida (2023); Brisbane Street Art Festival (2020 and 2023); Tumby Bay Street Art Festival, South Australia (2022); Wonderwalls Festival, Port Kembla, NSW (2019); Wall to Wall Festival, Benalla (2016, 2017, and 2018); Vancouver Mural Festival, Canada (2017); Darwin Street Art Festival (2017); and "House of Cards" art installation at Amsterdam Central Station, Netherlands (2015).\n\nCommercial clients include New Balance, Toyota x Virgin Airlines, Element, Wacom, STAN / Roadshow, Rough Diamond Production\'s "YEAR OF" series, and The 5th. She is represented in Australia by The Drawing Arm.\n\nPress and publications include Juxtapoz Magazine (2014), Beautiful Bizarre Magazine (2017), WOW x WOW (2017), Etiquette Silver (2016), Voyeur Magazine for Virgin Airlines (2018), the Bench Talk Podcast (2018), Il Globo News (2019), The Age, Melbourne (2019), and Blank Magazine, Gold Coast (2021). Speaking engagements include the Analogue Digital Creative Conference at Brisbane Powerhouse (2013) and Crown Hotel, Melbourne (2014), and the Head Space Conference at the Albert Waterways Community Center, Broadbeach, Queensland (2019).\n\nFor SHINE 2023, she traveled from Australia to St. Petersburg to paint at 2875 7th Avenue S. Website: lorettalizzio.com.',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/loretta-lizzio-2023','https://www.lorettalizzio.com/','https://www.hellenic.org.au/post/artist-in-conversation-meet-loretta-lizzio','https://stpetecatalyst.com/take-a-look-all-the-2023-shine-festival-murals/','https://medium.com/splatrs/creating-art-through-adversity-with-loretta-lizzio-6149dff201f6','https://www.lorettalizzio.com/about','https://www.lorettalizzio.com/curriculum-vitae','https://www.thedrawingarm.com.au/artists/loretta-lizzio','https://southwestcontemporary.com/hannah-eddy-paints-life-and-death/']},
  {id:47,a:'Max Sansing',t:'Shining Hope',loc:'401 5th St N',bldg:'Trinity Lutheran Church',lat:27.776945,lng:-82.639908,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'maxsansing',bio:'Virgil Abloh hired him to paint a Nike mural at a Boys and Girls Club in Chicago. He painted the NBA All-Star broadcast team — Ernie, Shaq, Kenny, and Charles — during All-Star Weekend.\n\nMax Sansing came up through Chicago\'s graffiti crews DC5, RK, and CYA before entering the Gallery 37 Center for the Arts youth program and spending two years at the American Academy of Art. His style fuses street art dynamism with photorealistic technique — large-scale portraits of African Americans celebrating identity and community, in oil, acrylic, spray paint, and enamel on wood panels, canvas, and aluminum.\n\nHis client list includes Nike, the Chicago Fire FC ("Stand For Chicago" mural in South Loop), and celebrity portraits of Barack Obama, Kris Bryant, Walter Payton, and Fred Hampton. He has shown at Thinkspace Projects in LA, POW! WOW! Hawaii, and Scope Miami Beach.\n\nFor SHINE 2023, he painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A multi-story portrait on the north face of Trinity Lutheran Church depicting a young Black woman gazing outward with quiet intensity. Her face is rendered in photorealistic detail with warm brown skin tones, while her body dissolves into swirling, psychedelic layers of green, turquoise, purple, and gold — evoking geode cross-sections and topographic forms. A large hibiscus flower in peach and violet blooms at her shoulder, and deep blue flowers anchor the bottom of the composition. A red archway silhouette with palm trees appears within the flowing forms, and a bird silhouette flies above. A SHINE plaque is visible at the base.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/max-sansing.jpeg',from:'Chicago, IL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2023 selected artist',fw:[{name:'maxsansing.com',url:'https://www.maxsansing.com'}],sbio:'Virgil Abloh hired him to paint a Nike mural at a Boys and Girls Club in Chicago. He painted the NBA All-Star broadcast team — Ernie, Shaq, Kenny, and Charles — during All-Star Weekend.\n\nMax Sansing was born and raised on the South Side of Chicago, in the Avalon Park neighborhood, by two artistic parents. His mother was an art teacher. His father, Ellis Sansing, worked for the Chicago Transit Authority. Ellis would take young Max along on errands, riding the Red Line at 79th Street and using his work-issued skeleton key to unlock the motorman\'s cab so they could ride in the operator seats with unobstructed views of Chicago\'s neighborhoods. That experience is the source of the skeleton key motif Sansing later embedded in his work — keys as metaphors for new pathways and doors waiting to be unlocked.\n\nHe started tagging at 14, painting under expressways and on permission walls where graffiti writers had property-owner consent. He worked through markers, rollers, specialty caps, and aerosol cans, and joined three of Chicago\'s most prominent graffiti crews: Def Con 5 (DC5), the Recking Krew (RK), and the Criminal Young Artists Crew (CYA). As a teenager he signed up for the Gallery 37 Center for the Arts Program, sponsored by the City of Chicago Department of Cultural Affairs, which hired artistically-inclined youth as summer apprentices. He taught himself oil painting, then enrolled at the American Academy of Art in Chicago, attending the private school for two years of formal training. He graduated from Kenwood Academy. He has also studied graphic design for two years, and describes his murals as a "gumbo" of graffiti, graphic design, and portraiture.\n\nHis distinct aesthetic fuses the color-drenched dynamism of street art with the technical elegance of photorealism. Large-scale portraits of African Americans dominate his catalog, often rendered in midnight blue, emerald green, red, or purple skin tones — a humanist gesture meant to push viewers past the surface of color. Flames lick the tops of heads as a reminder to hold tight to passions and ambitions. Streaks of paint decorate cheeks. Keys hang at the necks of figures. He works in oil, acrylic, spray paint, vinyl paint, ball-point ink, and enamel on wood panels, canvas, and aluminum.\n\nCommissions and clients include Nike, the Chicago Fire FC (the "Stand For Chicago" mural in the South Loop, part of a program promoting locals who give back), and Virgil Abloh, who tapped him alongside Nike in February to paint a large mural installation at one of the Boys and Girls Clubs of Chicago — specifically the Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. Boys and Girls Club at 2950 W. Washington Blvd. on the West Side, where the gym mural is titled "The City is Yours." NBA All-Star Weekend in Chicago commissioned him to paint the NBA on TNT broadcast crew: Ernie Johnson Jr., Shaquille O\'Neal, Kenny Williams, and Charles Barkley. Celebrity portrait subjects include President Barack Obama, Chicago Cubs third baseman Kris Bryant, Hall of Fame Chicago Bears running back Walter Payton, and Fred Hampton, Chairman of the Illinois Chapter of the Black Panther Party.\n\nNamed Chicago murals include "Where Shall We Go?" (2017) at the Horween Leather Company on North Elston Avenue in Bucktown, showing a woman in a purple motorcycle helmet with a key floating above her hand; "Cheers" (2017) on the exterior of CH Distillery in Pilsen; "Equals, We" (2017) at 51st Street and South Calumet Avenue in Bronzeville; "Soaring Minds" (2018) at Michele Clark Magnet High School in South Austin on the West Side; "New Frontier, Same Old Nine" (2019) on the 1800 block of East 79th Street, painted with Kayla Mahaffey, showing a woman in a helmet, Bulls jersey, and cape; and "Culture is Power" at 79th Street and East End Avenue, set against a purple backdrop with five figures meant to convey family. Sepia at Woodlawn Works is among his projects with the Chicago Public Art Group. Damon Reed, a fellow muralist, has known him for more than 20 years. Jon Pounds, former director of the Chicago Public Art Group, has spoken about his work.\n\nBeyond Chicago, his murals reach Denver, Texas, Florida, Boston, Michigan, and Indiana. Gallery exhibition history with Thinkspace Projects in Los Angeles includes the solo show "Lost & Found" in the Main Room (2020), with works "Recovered," "Soldier On," "Eyes Forward," "Join Me," "I\'ll Be Waiting Right Here When Ya Find Me," "Give Me A Moment," "Feather Weight," "Keygen," and "Rapture (Same Ole Love)" — the last featuring Anita Baker in the background as a nod to the R&B he grew up hearing in the 1980s. Other Thinkspace shows include "Across the Pond" (2022, "When You See It"), "UNITY" at Volery Gallery in Dubai (2022, "Dark & Lovely"), "15 Years of Thinkspace" (2019, "Feels Like A Monday"), Scope Miami Beach 2019 ("Tunnel Vision II"), Moniker 2019 12x12s ("Tunnel Vision I"), POW! WOW! Hawai\'i 2019 ("Wind Proof"), the POW! WOW! Long Beach group exhibition (2019, "The Point" — a portrait of Jean Baptiste Point Du Sable, recognized as the Founder of Chicago), and LAX / SFO Pt. III (2019, "My Walk About"). He has shown in Chicago, New York, and Miami.\n\nFor SHINE 2023, he painted on the north-facing exterior wall of Trinity Lutheran Church in St. Petersburg, completed last week of October as documented by the Tampa Bay Times. He is involved with numerous youth programs that expand arts opportunities in underserved Chicago areas. Website: maxsansing.com. Instagram: @maxsansing.',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/max-sansing-2023','https://www.tampabay.com/life-culture/arts/visual-arts/2023/10/24/st-petersburg-shine-mural-festival-max-sansing/','https://www.maxsansing.com/about','https://upmag.com/max-sansing/','https://chicago.suntimes.com/murals-mosaics/2020/2/28/21144592/murals-chicago-max-sansing-avalon-park-south-side-graffiti','https://www.thisiscolossal.com/2020/08/max-sansing-lost-and-found/','https://thinkspaceprojects.com/artists/max-sansing/','https://chicagopublicartgroup.org/artist/max-sansing/']},
  {id:120,a:'Melanie Posner',t:'',loc:'77 11th St N',bldg:'',lat:27.771894,lng:-82.649287,y:2023,cat:'commercial',ig:'therealmelpoz',bio:'She painted a woman in giant reflective sunglasses on this wall. Look closely at the lenses — the waterfront skyline is reflected in them.\n\nMelanie Posner was born around 1993 outside Philadelphia, moved to Tampa in 2013, and earned her BFA in Painting from the University of Tampa in 2016. She is now based in Los Angeles. Her style is photorealistic portraits of women — oversized expressive eyes, vibrant rainbow palettes, psychedelic paisley backgrounds, florals. She paints "strong, confident, independent women to be inspired by them."\n\nHer commercial commissions run wide. She painted scenic art for Walt Disney World\'s Star Wars: Galaxy\'s Edge at Hollywood Studios. The eighteen-foot mural on the back of the Hollander Hotel — a woman in a red headscarf with a glass of cerveza — was for New Belgium Brewing\'s Mural Agua Fresca; she finished it in under five days. Amazon Prime commissioned a mural on a bus that toured Tampa promoting the docuseries Tampa Baes (2021), in which she was a cast member. In 2024 she painted a forty-foot wall on the LGBT Center of Los Angeles for TikTok\'s "Create the Future" campaign.\n\nHer SHINE 2018 mural "New Beginnings" — a twenty-three-by-sixty-five foot photorealistic woman lying on her side, one eye peeking through her fingers — is at 4139 7th Terrace South.',desc:'A photorealistic woman\'s face fills the wall, wearing large reflective sunglasses that mirror a waterfront skyline scene. Bright pink lips and warm skin tones contrast with the cool teal background, which is dotted with oversized polka dots in pink, gold, red, and light blue. The piece wraps around windows that are incorporated into the design. Palm trees frame the mural on either side.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/unknown-1506.jpeg',from:'Los Angeles, CA',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2018 Open Call artist; Walt Disney World Star Wars: Galaxy\'s Edge scenic artist; Tampa Baes (Amazon Prime, 2021) cast member; TikTok LA Pride campaign (2024)',fw:[{name:'melanieposner.com',url:'https://www.melanieposner.com'}],sbio:'She painted the woman in the red headscarf on the back of the Hollander Hotel in under five days, then went straight to Walt Disney World to paint scenic art for Star Wars: Galaxy\'s Edge.\n\nMelanie "Mel" Posner was born around 1993 in a small town just outside Philadelphia. She moved to Tampa in 2013 and earned her BFA in Painting from the University of Tampa in 2016, where she also studied art therapy. She was previously based in Gulfport, Florida, and is now in Los Angeles.\n\nHer style is photorealistic portraits of women — oversized, expressive eyes, vibrant rainbow color palettes, psychedelic paisley backgrounds, floral elements. She blends lifelike detail with touches of dreamy surrealism. She works in oil on canvas, collage, prints, and large-scale murals. She sources her subjects from Pinterest, Google, and social media, looking for eyes, story, and confidence. "Working large is just really exciting," she says. "I feel like I\'m a little bit more free doing murals." Her stated philosophy: paint strong, confident, independent women to be inspired by them.\n\nFor SHINE 2018, she was one of five local "Open Call" artists. Her mural "New Beginnings" at 4139 7th Terrace South is twenty-three feet high by sixty-five feet long — a photorealistic woman lying horizontally on her side, her hand cupping the right side of her face, one eye peeking through her fingers. She completed it in October 2018.\n\nHer commercial clients include Walt Disney World (scenic artwork for Star Wars: Galaxy\'s Edge at Hollywood Studios, Orlando), New Belgium Brewing (the eighteen-foot Hollander Hotel mural of a woman in a red headscarf with a glass of Mural Agua Fresca Cerveza, completed in under five days), Amazon Prime Video (a mural on a promotional bus for Tampa Baes), and TikTok. For TikTok\'s 2024 "Create the Future" campaign, she painted a forty-foot mural on the LGBT Center of Los Angeles at 8745 Santa Monica Blvd in West Hollywood.\n\nShe was a cast member on all eight episodes of Tampa Baes (Amazon Prime Video, 2021), a docuseries about a Tampa Bay lesbian friend group produced by 3 Ball Productions and filmed over roughly ten weeks starting April 2021. She described herself as "neutral" and avoiding drama on the show. "My anxiety during filming was up to the roof, my mental health totally depleted. It was very stressful and I will say, I love how the show came out." Her oil painting "Passion" (also called "Summer and Marissa") appears on screen. She worked for years as a bartender at The Honey Pot, a landmark LGBTQ+ club in Ybor City, Tampa.\n\nShe has exhibited at Enigma gallery in downtown St. Petersburg. Instagram: @therealmelpoz. Website: melanieposner.com.',fn:['Photorealistic woman in reflective sunglasses with St. Pete skyline reflected in the lenses','Signed therealmelpoz 2023','Now based in Los Angeles'],src:['https://stpetecatalyst.com/melanie-posner/','https://www.melanieposner.com/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2018_SHINE_Mural_Melanie_Posner','https://outcoast.com/get-to-know-mel-posner-from-tampa-baes/','http://watermarkoutnews.com/2021/12/02/tampa-baes-star-melanie-posner-talks-filming-season-one/','https://www.imdb.com/name/nm12771071/','https://findmasa.com/artist/melanie-posner','https://www.artsy.net/artist/melanie-posner']},
  {id:48,a:'Michael Vasquez',t:'The Mangrove Refuge',loc:'2063 Central Ave',bldg:'Little Philly',lat:27.77135,lng:-82.661551,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'michaelvasquez_',bio:'His work hangs in the Smithsonian. Swizz Beats and Michael B. Jordan collect him.\n\nMichael Vasquez earned his BFA cum laude from the New World School of the Arts in Miami in 2005. His figurative paintings explore identity in marginalized neighborhoods — transforming personal photographs into dramatic compositions where clothing, hand signs, tattoos, and neighborhood landmarks establish who someone is and where they come from.\n\nHe was included in "Portraiture Now: Staging the Self" at the Smithsonian National Portrait Gallery (2014-15) and "The Culture: Hip Hop and Contemporary Art in the 21st Century" at the Baltimore Museum of Art (2023). His work is in the Perez Art Museum Miami, the Microsoft Art Collection, and the Rubell Museum. He has shown at the Saatchi Gallery in London, Wynwood Walls, and the Kemper Museum of Contemporary Art.\n\nHe is a three-time SHINE artist — 2015, 2023, and 2025.',desc:'A large photorealistic scene depicting a group of young men hanging around a beige Datsun 280Z parked at a waterfront spot with mangroves visible in the background. One figure sits shirtless on the hood, another leans against the car in a white t-shirt, and others stand or crouch nearby — all in jeans and casual wear that evoke a timeless, late-70s or early-80s Florida vibe. The painting captures a specific moment of youthful camaraderie, with the car as the gathering point. The detail in the faces, clothing, and vehicle is remarkably precise, and the mangrove setting grounds the scene in coastal Florida.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/michael-vasquez.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg/Miami, FL',aud:'',insp:'Depicts a displaced Lao family finding refuge at Gandy Beach circa 1980. The 1978 Datsun 280Z is a point of pride fusing car culture and masculinity. Clothing and style signal togetherness while solitary gazes allude to deeper personal histories.',maw:'',aaw:'Smithsonian National Portrait Gallery (2014-15, 2019); Perez Art Museum Miami collection; Microsoft Art Collection; Rubell Museum; SHINE 2015 and 2023',fw:[{name:'michaelvasquez.info',url:'http://www.michaelvasquez.info'}],sbio:'His work hangs in the Smithsonian. Swizz Beats and Michael B. Jordan collect him.\n\nMichael Vasquez was born in 1983 in St. Petersburg, Florida, the only child of a Polish-American single mother. His Puerto Rican father was present but did not take on a parental role, and he passed away when Vasquez was twelve. Vasquez began drawing in the second grade after watching a classmate get attention for his drawings, and pursued it through library books on how to draw horses and people. He attended a magnet high school for the arts, then enrolled at the New World School of the Arts in Miami intending to study graphic design with an interest in apparel. While there he shifted to fine art after watching peers a few years ahead of him make and sell the work they wanted to make. He earned his BFA cum laude from the New World School of the Arts in 2005.\n\nHis student work used imagery from old Dick and Jane books, removing every character but Dick and his mother. His teacher, the Miami gallerist Fredric Snitzer, gave him an internship and then a job after graduation, and went on to represent him — placing Vasquez in the lineage of Snitzer\'s star artists after Hernan Bas, Naomi Fisher, and Bhakti Baxter. Snitzer took his work to the Armory Show and Art Basel, building an international audience.\n\nVasquez\'s figurative paintings explore identity and the cultivation of community in marginalized neighborhoods. He cites growing up without a father figure or siblings as the source of his attraction to social situations where notions of "extended family" were prevalent. He photographs friends and familiar environments, sometimes shooting subjects in their own neighborhoods and sometimes in the studio with backgrounds Photoshopped in afterward, including aerial Google Maps-style views. He often cuts the photographs into collages — combining different guys, backgrounds, and objects — recreates them digitally, then translates them into large acrylic and acrylic spray paint canvases. Visual cues like clothing, hand signs, symbols, tattoos, and color carry identity; maps, neighborhood landmarks, signage, and native foliage establish location. He paints at large scale to position the viewer beneath the subjects, looking up at people they might otherwise pass without a second look. One close friend and frequent subject, AJ Hernandez, still lives in St. Pete and has spoken publicly about seeing his own image projected outside the Smithsonian.\n\nHis exhibition history runs deep. Solo museum shows include "Neighborhood Reclamation" at the Miami Dade College Museum of Art + Design (MOAD) — where associate curator and director of campus galleries Wanda K. Texon worked with him on an installation built from canvases stood up on triangles of wood secured with sandbags — as well as solo exhibitions at the Kemper Museum of Art in Kansas City, Missouri, and at the University of Tennessee, Chattanooga. Other solo bodies of work include "We\'ll Weather the Storm Well" (2017), "Rites of Passage" (2012), "What Comes With The Territory" (2010), "How The Land Lies" (2009), "Authority Figures" (2007), and "Family Jewels" (2007). Group exhibitions include "Portraiture Now: Staging the Self" at the Smithsonian National Portrait Gallery in Washington, D.C. (August 2014–April 2015), which traveled to the Americas Society in New York City and the National Hispanic Cultural Center in Albuquerque. Curators from the National Portrait Gallery first visited his Little Haiti studio on June 13, 2013. Seven of his paintings were included alongside the work of five other artists of Latin American descent. He has also shown at the Saatchi Gallery in London, the Frost Art Museum, MOCA North Miami, the Orlando Museum of Art, the Naples Museum of Art, the Bass Museum, and the Miami Art Museum.\n\nHis work is currently included in "The Culture: Hip Hop and Contemporary Art in the 21st Century," which debuted at the Baltimore Museum of Art in 2023 and travels to the Saint Louis Art Museum, the Schirn Kunsthalle Frankfurt in Germany, the Cincinnati Art Museum, and the Art Gallery of Toronto in Canada.\n\nHis paintings are held in the collections of the Perez Art Museum Miami, MOCA North Miami, the Nova Southeastern University Museum of Art, The Dean Collection (Swizz Beatz and Alicia Keys), The Franks-Suss Collection, the Microsoft Art Collection, the Montblanc Cutting Edge Art Collection, and The Rubell Family Collection. Michael B. Jordan is also among his collectors.\n\nNamed paintings include "275 North-OZ and Red" (2016, acrylic and acrylic spray paint on canvas, 96 x 72"), "C9: Ultimate/Imperial – Carol Mart" (2016, 72 x 96"), and "Where There\'s Smoke, There\'s Fire (Trap House)" (2015, 72 x 90"). His "Neighborhood Reclamation" series turns photographs of dilapidated and blighted properties into collages and paintings that improve and reinvigorate the structures while keeping their history visible.\n\nVasquez was a Wynwood Walls artist in 2019–2021 and is a three-time SHINE Mural Festival artist — 2015, 2023, and 2025. He was profiled by the National Endowment for the Arts in October 2016 ("Art Talk with Michael Vasquez" by Paulette Beete) and by Miami New Times in June 2015 ("Artist Michael Vasquez Brings Humanity to the Underrepresented" by Liz Tracy). He lives and works in Miami. His website is michaelvasquez.info.',fn:['Work hangs in the Smithsonian','Collected by Swizz Beats and Michael B. Jordan','St. Petersburg native — Gibbs High School'],src:['http://www.michaelvasquez.info/bio-new','https://npg.si.edu/exhibit/staging/vasquez.html','https://www.miaminewtimes.com/arts/artist-michael-vasquez-brings-humanity-to-the-underrepresented-7701431','https://www.arts.gov/stories/blog/2016/art-talk-michael-vasquez','https://thewynwoodwalls.com/artists/michael-vasquez/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/michael-vasquez-2023']},
  {id:49,a:'Rhys Meatyard',t:'',loc:'2210 1st Ave S',bldg:'The Werk Gallery',lat:27.76981167,lng:-82.66335333,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'the_meatyard',bio:'His great-uncle was Ralph Eugene Meatyard, the pioneering photographer. His grandfather Jerry was a metal sculptor. He is third-generation.\n\nRhys Meatyard graduated from the Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School, then studied fine art and anthropology at Eckerd College. He has been a graphic designer and illustrator since 2007. He is also a former stone carver who designed tombstones and marble inscriptions, and has done high-end jewelry photography retouching.\n\nHis style is digital and traditional — pop-culture-centric work with dark humor and surreal elements, watercolors, gouache, acrylics, colored pencils, and found ephemera like vintage maps and sheet music. He reimagines mythological figures into whimsical, irreverent character studies. He is a non-binary, trans-masculine artist who found his artistic voice in his late thirties after overcoming homelessness and single parenthood.\n\nHe is art director for Friend Club Records and designed the logo for Meatyard Ybor, an affordable art studio complex in Ybor City. For SHINE 2023, he was an invited participating artist.',desc:'A dreamy pastel mural inspired by classical sculpture and 1970s prog rock album art. Blue-toned Greek and Roman statues stand on pedestals within a colonnade of soft lavender arches. As the scene moves right, the temple begins to break apart — columns float upward and the ground fragments — while the furthest figure steps off her base and gazes into golden sun rays breaking through pink and tan clouds. String lights hang across the top of the building.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/rhys-meatyard.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2023 selected artist',fw:[{name:'themeatyard.com',url:'https://themeatyard.com'}],sbio:'His great-uncle was Ralph Eugene Meatyard, the pioneering postmodern photographer. His grandfather Jerry Meatyard was a Tampa metal sculptor and lifelong art educator who taught at HCC Ybor for decades and helped found the El Sama art collective in Ybor City in the 1970s. His mother Laura Meatyard was a monument designer responsible for headstones and stone memorials across Florida. He is third-generation.\n\nRhys Meatyard attended the Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School and studied fine art and anthropology at Eckerd College in St. Petersburg. He has worked as a graphic designer and illustrator since 2007, with a long parallel track in higher-end jewelry photography retouching, ad design, and catalog layout. He has worked from home for the past twelve years. He grew up watching his mother stencil cemetery stones and picked up the trade himself, designing tombstones and marble inscriptions — a fascination with letterforms he says is a big part of who he is.\n\nHis path to making his own art was non-linear. He left home at 15, married at 17, and was a single parent at 22. He went to school for visual art in his 20s while raising three children and working full time. In his mid-to-late 30s an identity crisis, an addiction to prescription pain medication, and a stint in detox forced a reset. He gave himself a five-year deadline to repair his family, find himself, and build a life worth living. He came through it, came out as non-binary and trans-masculine, transitioned, took his mother\'s family name, downsized, and went fully freelance to make time for art. He started consistently showing in 2019. He lost both his grandfather Jerry and his mother Laura in March 2016.\n\nHe is creative director and art director for Friend Club Records, the independent record label for which he creates album art, print layouts for physical media, merch, and brand identities. He designed the logo for Meatyard Ybor — the future home of more than fifty affordable artist studios and gallery space in historic Ybor City, named after his grandfather. His freelance design work continues with logos and branding alongside the jewelry-industry retouching.\n\nHis style blends digital and traditional media with dark humor and surreal twists on instantly recognizable images. He works in watercolors, gouache, acrylics, colored pencils, and found ephemera including vintage maps and sheet music. He often glues watercolor paper to wood panels. He reimagines mythological figures into irreverent character studies — the Fates as trans people; a spinning sun with the face of Jack Nicholson from The Shining. His cited subjects include cats, classical art, mythology, and a general sense of fun and irreverence. His personal icon is a t-bone steak.\n\nDocumented works include "Hamburger Cop Jail" (2021), "Bomb Pops And Bentleys," "Peets of Despair," and "Cemetery Hangs Part of \'Blue Monday in Lothlórien.\'" His widely shared digital piece "Always Hungry" is a Post Malone cartoon that plays on the rapper\'s face tattoo reading "Always Tired." He produced a line of soap art with scents including "Smelly Pits" and "Smoky Air," riffing on the pre-COVID music festival experience. For a 2019 Mize Gallery show he made a lenticular piece showing different images depending on viewing angle.\n\nHe has participated in countless group shows locally and nationally, won several awards, and was an invited participating artist in the 9th annual SHINE Mural Festival in 2023. He was featured in the MIZE Gallery "SHINE YEAR 7 ART EXHIBIT" in 2021. His first solo exhibition was in 2024. A new solo show, "Ceremony," opened March 16, 2026. Recent blog posts on themeatyard.com include "Self-Reflection, With a Side of Dumplings" (January 12, 2026), "Yet Another Horror" (January 8, 2026), and "AI: The True \'Art\' Movement of Late Capitalism" (November 22, 2025).\n\nHe has been profiled by The Gabber Newspaper ("The Off-Kilter World of Artist Rhys Meatyard," October 27, 2021, by Abby Baker) and CanvasRebel Magazine ("Meet Rhys Meatyard," September 19, 2022). His shop sells original art, museum-quality prints, stickers, soap, shirts, and a rotating selection of music from Friend Club Records.\n\nWebsite: themeatyard.com. Instagram: @the_meatyard. Facebook: meatyardart. Twitter: @TheMeatyard. Friend Club Records: friendclubrecords.com.',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine (2023 Rhys Meatyard)','https://thegabber.com/the-off-kilter-world-of-artist-rhys-meatyard/','https://themeatyard.com/','https://themeatyard.com/about/','https://canvasrebel.com/meet-rhys-meatyard/']},
  {id:50,a:'Sarah Sheppard',t:'We Are Magic',loc:'2336 5th Ave S',bldg:'New Dawn Coffee Co',lat:27.766483,lng:-82.665218,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'sarah.g.sheppard',bio:'She studied metalsmithing to continue work her late father began. Art runs in the family — both her parents were artists.\n\nSarah Sheppard grew up in Dallas, studied photography, drawing, painting, graphic design, ceramics, and fabric art at the University of North Texas, and has lived in St. Petersburg for over twelve years. She also trained in metalsmithing at the Creative Jewelry Academy.\n\nHer style is colorful, high-contrast figurative work — new romanticism, movement as metaphor for continuous loss and seeking. She started her mural career painting for a spa in St. Petersburg after earlier work in Venetian plaster and faux finishing for model homes. She has multiple SHINE murals across the city, including "Life Reimagined" on the back wall of the Morean Arts Center, commissioned by AARP.\n\nFor SHINE 2023, she returned to paint in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A wide mural on a black background centered on a pair of large, detailed eyes gazing out from behind an ornate golden mask-like structure. The face is flanked by crescent moons, a starburst crown, and colorful tropical flowers — hibiscus in pink and teal. White feathered wings spread out on both sides, and elaborate gold filigree scrollwork curls outward to fill the edges of the wall. The words "We are all Magic" appear in script across the lower center. The overall effect is mystical and symmetrical, like a gilded altar piece reimagined as street art.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/sarah-sheppard.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Multiple SHINE festival murals; AARP/Morean Arts Center commission',fw:[{name:'sarahsheppardsart.com',url:'http://www.sarahsheppardsart.com'}],sbio:'She studied metalsmithing to continue work her late father began. Art runs in the family — both her parents were artists.\n\nSarah Sheppard was born and raised in Dallas, Texas, where her father (her mother\'s college art teacher) shaped her artistic environment from the start. She studied photography, drawing, painting, graphic design, ceramics, and fabric art at the University of North Texas before relocating to Florida, where her parents were living, to be closer to family and change scenery.\n\nIn Florida, with her parents\' help, she launched her own business doing Venetian Plaster, Faux Finishing, and Murals for model homes, businesses, and residences during the mid-2000s housing boom. Fourteen-hour days were routine. The 2008 economic downturn coincided with the birth of her son Sebastian, ending the model-home work. After three years as a stay-at-home mother, she returned to part-time work at Brew D\'Licious coffee shop in St. Petersburg, where she painted her first exterior mural — roughly 30 feet by 10 feet — for the spa across the street. That wall launched her exterior mural career; she has since completed roughly 15 exterior murals.\n\nHer style is colorful, high-contrast figurative work rooted in new romanticism, with movement as metaphor for continuous loss and seeking. Her personal artwork is inspired by life events, the natural world, and historical motifs and symbolism, sometimes mixed with modern color or application. She aims to convey beauty, romanticism, and mysticism, and works at large scale in both painting and jewelry.\n\nHer best-known St. Pete commission is "Life Reimagined," painted on the back wall of the Morean Arts Center at Central Avenue and Seventh Street. AARP and the Morean approached her in 2015 to develop a mural around the themes "Life Reimagined" and "Disrupt Aging." Her composition uses gears to represent the grind of daily life and the mechanics of the working world, a Banyan tree as the central figure with roots sinking into the community, and a Phoenix as the symbol of rebirth into the next stage of life. The piece was profiled by Julie Garisto in Creative Loafing Tampa Bay (September 26, 2015). AARP partnered on the project as part of its Tampa Bay arts initiative — "a vibrant cultural life connects people to their community."\n\nOther documented public work includes a massive wall at Skull & Cakebones Vegan Craft Bakery in Dripping Springs, Texas, and a SHINE 2023 mural in St. Petersburg at 2356 5th Ave S. She is included in "We Built This City: Women Artists of SHINE," a Morean Arts Center exhibition opening October 11, 2025, alongside Alyssa Marie, Artist Jones, Elizabeth Barenis, Quinn Cale, Ashley Cantero, Jenipher Chandley, Dreamweaver, Amy Ilic-Volpe, Carrie Jadus, Nneka Jones, Cristi Lopez, Cecilia Lueza, Syd Bloom, Sara Salem, and Nicole Salgar — sixteen Tampa Bay muralists recognized for shaping St. Pete\'s public and street art scene through SHINE. The exhibition runs through October 22, 2025. SHINE® St. Petersburg Mural Festival, produced by the St. Petersburg Arts Alliance since 2015, has commissioned more than 170 murals.\n\nAfter moving back to Texas, she began studying metalsmithing at Creative Side Jewelry Academy. The decision started with Jewelry 101 — taken to finish a few pieces her father had given her when he passed away — and grew into ongoing coursework. Her jewelry work mirrors the scale of her painting: large statement pieces, each unique, drawing from the same historical motifs and natural-world references. She has lived in St. Petersburg for over twelve years across her time in Florida.\n\nSheppard has spoken about the emotional dimension of the work — particularly the discipline of being kind to herself as her own toughest critic, trusting the creative process, taking breaks, and seeking new experiences to shake up perspective. She names painting outside as both the most challenging and most rewarding part of mural work — weather, public visibility of every mistake, and the conversations with passersby and children who light up at the work.\n\nWebsite: sarahsheppardsart.com. Instagram: @thee.divine.sarah. Facebook: theedivinesarah. Phone: 727.417.2687.',fn:['Studied metalsmithing after her late father','Both parents were artists','Multiple SHINE murals across St. Pete'],src:['https://stpetemuraltour.com/2019/12/10/sarah-sheppard/','https://voyagehouston.com/interview/art-life-sarah-sheppard/','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/life-reimagined-sarah-sheppard-completes-new-mural-in-downtown-st-pete-12280945','https://ilovetheburg.com/two-new-street-art-exhibitions-st-pete/','http://www.sarahsheppardsart.com/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2023_SHINE_sarah_sheppard']},
  {id:51,a:'The Happy Mural Project',t:'Girl Power',loc:'2727 6th Ave South',bldg:'CrossFit 9',lat:27.76500833,lng:-82.67086667,y:2023,cat:'shine',ig:'thehappymuralproject',bio:'Their motto is "Honk if You\'re Happy." Their first mural was painted the week the world shut down.\n\nAlyssa Marie and Braden Everly are The Happy Mural Project — a married couple who relocated from Colorado to St. Petersburg in 2017. Alyssa is a fine artist who opened a gallery on Beach Drive in 2018 (closed January 2020). Braden has a degree in special education and worked as a house painter in Colorado. Their first mural went up at Lolita\'s Catering in early March 2020, days before lockdown.\n\nTheir signature is oversized sunflower murals in vibrant color, plus community-participatory "color by number" walls. Their SHINE 2020 piece "Hands of Change" at the City Theatre featured hands holding sunflowers representing racial and cultural diversity. For SHINE 2023, "Girl Power" was a color-by-number mural completed by Girl Scouts of West Central Florida. They are three-time SHINE artists — 2020, 2022, and 2023.',desc:'A vibrant community paint-by-number mural created in partnership with Girl Scouts of West Central Florida. A young girl in a blue shirt stands face-to-face with a dappled gray horse, surrounded by giant golden sunflowers against a teal background. The Happy Mural Project logo is signed in the lower right corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2023/happy-mural-project.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2020, 2022, 2023 selected artist',fw:[{name:'thehappymuralproject.com',url:'https://www.thehappymuralproject.com'}],sbio:'Their motto is "Honk if You\'re Happy." Their first mural was painted the week the world shut down. Alyssa Marie and Braden Everly are The Happy Mural Project, a married couple who relocated from Colorado to St. Petersburg in 2017. Alyssa Marie is a fine artist from Colorado. In 2018 she opened the Alyssa Marie Gallery on Beach Drive NE in downtown St. Petersburg, where she exhibited her own work. The gallery closed in January 2020, and she shifted to selling her paintings of local and nature scenes through her website. Braden Everly has a degree in special education and worked as a house painter during summers in Colorado before the move to Florida. The combination of Alyssa\'s fine-art background and Braden\'s experience with paint, ladders, and scaffolding became The Happy Mural Project.\n\nTheir first mural went up at Lolita\'s Catering + Events at the corner of 5th Avenue North and 28th Street, address 2735 5th Ave N, in early March 2020, days before the COVID-19 lockdown. The original plan had been to hand out sunflowers to passersby at the launch. After social distancing rules took effect, they propped up a sign reading "Honk if You\'re Happy" instead, asking drivers to honk as they passed. The first wall sits in the shadow of St. Petersburg High School and drew coverage from Sean Daly at ABC Action News, Gabby Arzola at Bay News 9, and Liz Burch at CBS 10, who attached her microphone to a pole to keep six feet of distance during the interview. Maggie Duffy at the Tampa Bay Times later covered their paint-by-numbers community work in a June 8, 2023 story.\n\nTheir signature work is oversized sunflower murals in vibrant color, plus community-participatory "color by number" walls where volunteers fill in numbered shapes. Their SHINE 2020 piece, "Hands of Change," went up at the St. Petersburg City Theatre at 4025 31st Street South. Volunteers painted hands holding giant sunflowers from October 31 through November 6, 2020, as an early start to the SHINE Mural Festival. The hands represented different races, colors, national origins, and sexual orientations as a symbol of love, connectedness, and growth. The City Theatre, a volunteer-driven theater that celebrated its 100th birthday in 2021, hosted the wall during a season that included Broadway By Request and A Christmas Carol.\n\nFor SHINE 2022 they painted a community mural at St. Peters Bark! at 3001 Central Avenue in St. Petersburg. For SHINE 2023, "Girl Power" was a color-by-number mural completed by Girl Scouts of West Central Florida at 2727 6th Avenue South. They are three-time SHINE artists with walls in 2020, 2022, and 2023.\n\nBeyond the SHINE festival, the project has a growing list of locations. They painted a community mural at Pro Karting Experience at 4770 34th Street North in St. Petersburg, featured in the 2023 Tampa Bay Times piece by Maggie Duffy. They have walls at Tampa General Hospital, including the peace garden at 1 Tampa General Circle in Tampa, and at the Tampa General Hospital Food Pantry. They painted a community mural at Winthrop Charter School at 6204 Scholars Hill Lane in Riverview, and at Salon Eunoia at 4218 Henderson Boulevard in Tampa. Other locations include Sammy & Paco\'s at 2735 5th Ave N and 28th St, a wall in Fossil Park visible from Fire Station 7, a residence at 4897 Lake Charles Drive North in Kenneth City, The 490 Building at 490 1st Avenue South in St. Petersburg, and a private residence backyard in Tampa. They reached beyond Florida for a community mural at the Artesia Arts Council in Artesia, New Mexico, at 310 W Main Street, where school children, families, and people getting off work stopped by across a week to paint every detail except the logo.\n\nThe mission, as stated by the artists, is to inspire and uplift people and communities through supersized, vibrantly colored sunflower murals. Braden has said happiness is needed right now, as always, and that people have flocked to their walls. Alyssa believes that anyone can do art, and the couple wanted to bring the community together in an activity where everyone could help beautify the city. Volunteer painters do not need experience, and supplies are provided. A Volunteer Artist sign-up form and a Submit Your Wall form live on their website at thehappymuralproject.com. Their Instagram is @thehappymuralproject. The artists also post under @theoriginalbraden, @alyssa_marie_gallery, and a travel account @bestlifeeverly. Creative Pinellas covered the project in an October 26, 2020 piece by Shae Vehorn tied to the Hands of Change wall and the SHINE Mural Festival rollout.',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/happy-mural-project-2023','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/alyssa-marie-2022','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/happy-mural-project','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/the-happy-mural-project/','https://www.thehappymuralproject.com/locations','https://www.tampabay.com/life-culture/arts/visual-arts/2023/06/08/st-petersburg-community-murals-sunflowers/','https://www.thehappymuralproject.com/','https://www.abcactionnews.com/news/region-pinellas/happy-mural-project-near-st-petersburg-high-is-drawing-smiles-from-social-distancers']},
  {id:53,a:'123Klan',t:'Libre et Sauvage',loc:'128 20th St S',bldg:'Tri-J Towing',lat:27.769732,lng:-82.660111,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'123klan',bio:'They named the crew after their zip code — 59123, Bray-Dunes, France. That was 1992.\n\nScien (Sebastien) and Klor (Carole) are 123Klan, a married graffiti duo who started on the walls of northern France and ended up designing for Nike, Adidas, Marvel, Netflix, Sony, MTV, ESPN, Stussy, and Vans. They moved from France to Montreal in 2007, then to Venice, California. Along the way they founded the BANDIT1SM streetwear brand and published three books.\n\nTheir style is what happens when street knowledge meets graphic design — sharp-angled letters, vivid colors, razor-sharp color blocks, influenced by Japanese cartoons, North American mascots, and pop art. They have exhibited in over sixty shows globally, from Pow Wow Hawaii to Art Basel Miami to Urban Art Fair Paris.\n\nFor SHINE 2022, they painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A large mural on the side of Tri-J Towing in 123Klan\'s signature cartoon-graffiti style on a lavender background. The central figure is a cowboy outlaw character in a dark fedora with an arrow pierced through it, wearing an open Hawaiian shirt with a tropical print, rendered in their angular vector-meets-spraypaint aesthetic. To his left, a ghost-like figure with X\'d-out eyes holds a flag, surrounded by cacti, a skull, and bold graffiti-style lettering reading "123" and "Go." The text "Libre et Sauvage" ("Free and Wild") runs across the bottom — a recurring 123Klan motif representing the untamed spirit of graffiti culture. Bullet hole details and crossed arrows anchor the composition at the base. The palette is muted pastels — lavender, sage green, rust, and navy — a departure from their typical acid brights.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/123klan.jpeg',from:'Venice Beach, CA',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2022 selected artist; 60+ exhibitions globally',fw:[{name:'123klan.com',url:'https://www.123klan.com'}],sbio:'They named the crew after their zip code — 59123, Bray-Dunes, France. That was 1992.\n\nScien (Sebastien) and Klor (Carole) are 123Klan, a married graffiti duo who started on the walls of Bray-Dunes, a small town in northern France where they were the only graffiti artists in the area. The crew name began as "123KINGS" — a sarcastic nod to their local dominance — then shortened to "123K" and finally "123KLAN," reflecting both the literal couple-as-clan and the broader crew of close friends. Other crew members listed in early documentation include Dean, Sper, Skam, Meric, and Reso 1. The Wu-Tang Clan was a stated influence on the name.\n\nTheir style was shaped by 1990s graffiti out of Europe and New York, and they crafted what they describe as a hybrid language that transcended the traditional graffiti schools — huge, sharp-angled letters in acid colors paired with characters drawn with futuristic manga features and experimental type. Beginning in 1994, they applied graphic design (citing Neville Brody as an influence) to their graffiti and vice versa. Their motto: "When street knowledge meets technology." In 2001, the crew "invaded the web," treating the internet as a new surface and a tool for creation, expression, and dissemination.\n\nTheir work spans canvases, walls, record sleeves, posters, stickers, toys, skateboards, shoes, and t-shirts. Their creative studio handles graphics and branding. In 2007 they launched their own streetwear brand, BANDIT1SM (also written BANDIT1$M), giving them financial independence and creative freedom after years of collaborating with leading streetwear brands. The same year they relocated from France to Montreal, Canada, where they were based for fifteen years before moving to Venice, California.\n\nTheir published books include 123klan (Pyramyd, Paris, 2003), Style is the Message (123klan, 2003), 123KLAN, version 2: Design Designer (Pyramyd editions, 2007), and 123KLAN respect and loved (Ankama Editions, Label 619, 2013).\n\nDocumented exhibition history is extensive. Recent shows include World Wide Walls in Honolulu, Hawaii (2023), the Bishop Museum in Honolulu (2019 and 2023), Shine on St Pete in Saint Petersburg, Florida (2023), and the Teenage Kicks Festival in Rennes, France (2023). 2018 included Urban Art Fair Paris, Kolly Gallery in Zurich, Above the Radar in Burlington, Art Souterrain and Quartier des Spectacles in Montreal, Gallery 123klan in Canada, and Pow Wow San Jose. 2017 included Miami Art Basel (Basel House — Sentience WYN317 Gallery), Pow Wow Worcester, and the 22nd edition of Under Pressure in Montreal. 2016 included the 21st Under Pressure, Amalgam Festival in Sherbrooke, Pow Wow Long Beach, and 123KLAN solo shows at 1AM Gallery in San Francisco and The HUB in Paris.\n\nEarlier years: SHINE at the Morean Arts Center, St Petersburg, in 2015, alongside Violent By Design at Stay Gallery in Downey, the 20th Under Pressure in Montreal, Screaming Hand at The Seventh Letter Gallery in Fairfax (Los Angeles), and Street Ahead at Vivid-Sydney. 2014 included Pow Wow Hawaii at the Honolulu Art Museum, Mural Festival Montreal, Urban Art Gallery Stuttgart, Montana Gallery Barcelona, All City Style New York, Jardin Rouge in Marrakech, and Scope New York. They returned to Pow Wow Hawaii at the Honolulu Art Museum every year from 2011 through 2014. 2012 marked their 20th-anniversary show in Bushwick, New York. 2011 included Faces And Laces in Moscow, Gamma Proforma in London, Against The Grain at 1AM Gallery in San Francisco, UrbanArt — Graffiti 21 (New York, Paris, Berlin, Völklingen), BRONX in Singapore, and Sudala in Tijuana. They opened a Nike Store exhibition in Mexico City in 2010 and showed at Agnes B in Paris and New York in 2008 and 2010. 2003 included Semi-Permanent in Sydney, Australia, alongside Shepard Fairey and Banksy. They have toured exhibitions in Brussels, Berlin, Lisbon, Marrakech, Mexico City, Moscow, Rio, São Paulo, San Francisco, Singapore, Tokyo, Lille, Dunkirk, Toulouse, Strasbourg, Eindhoven, Rome, and Munich.\n\n123KLAN was a featured artist duo at the 2019 SpraySeeMO Mural Festival in Kansas City, painting on the side of the local Crossroads Artist & Craftsman store along Southwest Boulevard, loosely inspired by the colors of the Kansas City Royals. Scien was interviewed by Matt Gondek on the Clean Break Podcast during that festival. Their client and collaboration list includes Nike, Adidas, Marvel, Netflix, Sony, MTV, ESPN, Stussy, and Vans.\n\nWebsite: 123klan.com. Streetwear brand: BANDIT1SM. Contact: info@123klan.com.',src:['https://www.123klan.com/pages/story-of-graffiti-art-couple-scien-and-klor-123klan','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/123Klan','https://wesupportartists.com/artist/123-klan-scien-and-klor/','https://www.sprayseemo.com/123klan']},
  {id:212,a:'Annie Phunk',t:'',loc:'425 2nd St N',bldg:'Satoshi Hideout',lat:27.776765,lng:-82.63541,y:2022,cat:'commercial',ig:'miss.phunk',bio:'A charging bull, a woman trailing red silk, and a sun stamped with glowing code — painted like a Japanese woodblock print down the side of a Bitcoin hotel.\n\nIt\'s the Wall Street bull, relocated to St. Pete and wrapped in ukiyo-e clouds. The wall reads as one idea: a woman breaking free of the financial establishment, the red silk she trails standing for that freedom; a dragon tucked into the clouds for prosperity; and a sun lighting what Bitcoin calls the "genesis block," the first block ever mined.\n\nThe mural covers the side of Satoshi Hideout, a crypto-themed boutique hotel and co-living building at 425 2nd Street N downtown. The building\'s art program launched in 2022, with the concept designed by St. Petersburg advertising firm Clear ph. This wall is signed AnniePhunk.',desc:'A long two-story mural on the stucco side wall of an apartment-style building, with the building\'s windows and air-conditioning units left exposed and painted around. The whole scene is rendered in a stylized Japanese woodblock-print idiom: scrolling, curled clouds in layered blues, purples, pinks, and white fill the wall edge to edge. At center, a large dark navy-to-black bull charges with its head lowered — pale curved horns, cyan contour lines defining the muscles and a shaggy, bison-like mane, tail flicked up behind. At far left, an anime-style woman with long black hair and a pink hair ribbon leans into motion, wearing a deep magenta/maroon jacket over white, her hair and a length of red-pink silk streaming behind her. Upper left, a golden sun disc is filled with small repeating glyphs and characters (code-like "AF" and "00" forms), throwing straight rays across a graduated blue sky. The right half continues the cloudscape in pink and lavender. Signed in black at the lower left: "AnniePhunk."',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/anniephunk-satoshi-bull.jpeg',from:'',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:[{name:'missphunk.com',url:'https://www.missphunk.com'}],sbio:'A charging bull, a woman trailing red silk, and a sun stamped with glowing code — painted like a Japanese woodblock print down the side of a Bitcoin hotel. This wall is signed AnniePhunk.\n\nIt wraps the side of Satoshi Hideout, St. Petersburg\'s first crypto-themed boutique hotel and co-living building, at 425 2nd Street N (the project also spans 424 2nd Street N, the former Park Plaza Apartments) in downtown St. Petersburg. The art concept was designed by St. Petersburg advertising firm Clear ph; press coverage in 2022 credited the Satoshi Hideout murals to local artist gatsbynotart, and the mural work took more than two weeks. This wall carries the signature "AnniePhunk."\n\nThe imagery is openly about money and freedom. A woman confronts a bull drawn after the iconic Charging Bull sculpture on Wall Street; the unwoven red silks she trails were described as freedom from the constraints of the financial establishment. A dragon hidden in the clouds represents prosperity, and a sun illuminates the "genesis block" — the first block of the Bitcoin blockchain — above the figures. The woman is rendered in an anime style. Another side of the project carries portraits of unrelated people, a nod to the unknown identity of Satoshi Nakamoto, Bitcoin\'s pseudonymous creator.\n\nAnnie Phunk is a painter — her studio work runs to realist oil, from portraits to pop-leaning still lifes. Instagram: @miss.phunk. Website: missphunk.com.\n\nPainted in 2022. Category: commissioned (a private commission for the hotel, not a SHINE Mural Festival wall).',src:['https://stpetecatalyst.com/inside-st-petes-first-crypto-themed-boutique-hotel/ (address 425 & 424 2nd St N; mural symbolism — woman/bull/red silk/dragon/genesis-block sun; concept by Clear ph; project credited to gatsbynotart; 2022; >2 weeks to paint)','https://satoshihideout.com/']},
  {id:160,a:'Art of Slim',t:'Dreams to Reality',loc:'226 Dr MLK Jr St N',bldg:'',lat:27.77396,lng:-82.646858,y:2022,cat:'commercial',ig:'artofslim',bio:'When he finished his three-wall mural for Air Masters in Seminole Heights, he started crying and hugged his dog.\n\nAustin "Slim" Gee — Art of Slim — is a St. Petersburg-based spray paint specialist and muralist. He held an artist-in-residence at Parc at Wesley Chapel near Tampa for two years (2021-2022), where American Landmark Apartments gave him free housing and studio space in exchange for community art programming — his term was extended a second year due to popularity. Before Tampa Bay he painted anti-litter murals in Columbia, South Carolina for PalmettoPride.\n\nHis largest piece is a three-wall mural spanning the Air Masters warehouse in Seminole Heights, depicting Tampa\'s aviation history, wildlife, sports, and community — it took just over two months and Tampa Mayor Jane Castor attended the ribbon-cutting in October 2025.',desc:'A cartoon boy in a blue baseball cap and light blue hoodie rides a skateboard through a swirling purple vortex, arms outstretched, tongue out in excitement. The skateboard has blue wheels and a smiley face sticker. Paint splatters in teal, purple, pink, and green burst across the background. "DREAMS TO REALITY" is lettered in bold white and purple at the bottom left. Signed "@ArtOfSlim \'22" in the lower right. A "No Parking Any Time" sign is integrated into the left edge of the composition. The style is animated and energetic — cartoon character design with graffiti influences.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/art-of-slim-dreams-to-reality.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'When he finished his three-wall mural for Air Masters in Seminole Heights, he started crying and hugged his dog.\n\nAustin "Slim" Gee — Art of Slim — is a St. Petersburg-based spray paint specialist, graphic designer, acrylic painter, and illustrator. His Instagram bio reads "Spray Paint Specialist." He is a full-time mural artist who accepts commissions. He has possible South Carolina roots (803 area code on his website).\n\nHe held an artist-in-residence at Parc at Wesley Chapel (American Landmark Apartments, 5561 Post Oak Blvd, Wesley Chapel, Tampa, FL) for two years (2021-2022), receiving no-cost housing and studio space in exchange for community art programming including "Sketch and Sip" events and children\'s art classes. His term was extended a second year due to popularity. He painted a large-scale heron mural at the clubhouse inspired by local wildlife.\n\nKnown murals include "Dreams to Reality" (2022, St. Petersburg — cartoon kid on skateboard, commercial commission), the Air Masters of Tampa Bay mural (unveiled October 2025, Seminole Heights, Tampa — spans three warehouse walls depicting aviation history, wildlife, sports, and community; took two months; Mayor Jane Castor attended ribbon-cutting), "Saluda In Bloom" (March 2019, Candi Lane, Columbia, SC — anti-litter awareness mural for One Columbia and PalmettoPride), and "Love Not Litter Extension" (2020, same location, with Taylor Renee).\n\nClients include Air Masters of Tampa Bay, American Landmark Apartments, One Columbia / PalmettoPride, and Palmetto Outdoor Center. He is a member of several music festival production teams and arts alliances in Tampa Bay.\n\nInstagram: @artofslim. Website: slimgee.com.',src:['https://www.slimgee.com/','https://www.artistsalapts.com/austin-slim-gee','https://www.artistsalapts.com/blog/talking-murals-with-austin-slim-gee','https://www.citybiz.co/article/263256/american-landmark-apartment-selects-2022-artist-in-residence-for-tampa-community/','https://www.fox13news.com/news/new-mural-seminole-heights-signals-bright-future-tampa','https://acprosite.com/air-masters-of-tampa-bay-unveils-tribute-to-the-seminole-heights-neighborhood-in-tampa/','https://colatoday.6amcity.com/columbia-murals','https://www.palmettopride.org/public-art-to-prevent-litter-mural-completed-in-columbia-sc/','https://www.abccolumbia.com/2019/03/15/palmetto-pride-unveils-art-mural-to-show-efforts-in-reducing-litter-crime-rate-in-columbia-communities/']},
  {id:55,a:'Ashley Cantero',t:'',loc:'2421 Central Ave.',bldg:'Fonda Nails & Spa',lat:27.771255,lng:-82.666106,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'',bio:'She describes her work as "dark, intricate, sensual and, at times, humorous." The 1960s psychedelia and Atomic Age design are deliberate.\n\nAshley Cantero is a Tampa native — visual artist, muralist, and illustrator. Her style features precise line work and bold female characters with intense emotional energy, drawing from retro charm, the supernatural, and sci-fi. At FloridaRAMA she created immersive 3D diorama installations ("Drive-In," "Swamp Motel") and an eight-wall retro-futuristic mural, "Cosmic Dreams and Sunshine Beams."\n\nFor SHINE 2022, she painted at 2421 Central Avenue.',desc:'A retro sci-fi scene spanning the full wall — three flying saucers beam up figures against a teal background loaded with mid-century diamond shapes and starbursts. A suited figure stands on a vanishing-point highway while a green alien peers out from the right side. The palette is teal, pink, and chartreuse with heavy black outlines giving it a vintage comic book feel.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/ashley-cantero.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2022 selected artist',fw:[{name:'ashleycantero.com',url:''}],sbio:'She describes her work as "dark, intricate, sensual and, at times, humorous." The 1960s psychedelia and Atomic Age design are deliberate.\n\nAshley Cantero is a Tampa, Florida native — visual artist, muralist, and illustrator. Her style centers on precise line work and bold, emotionally charged female characters, drawing from 60s psychedelia, Atomic Age design, and Sci-Fi aesthetics. She frames her body of work as a fusion of nostalgia and futurism, ever-evolving across mediums, and she calls herself a standout figure in both traditional and public art spheres. The voice she uses for her own art — "dark, intricate, sensual and, at times, humorous" — is the throughline.\n\nShe has built a presence in both the local Tampa Bay scene and beyond, contributing what she describes as a unique perspective. Her practice spans drawing, painting, mural work, and — newly added through her FloridaRAMA commission — three-dimensional diorama construction. Talking about the FloridaRAMA project she described it as outside her comfort zone: "I haven\'t built a diorama since elementary school, so this project has taken me a little out of my comfort zone as an artist and has forced me to think outside the box…or within the box in this case! As someone who typically draws and paints, it has been really fun and challenging to take a design and build it."\n\nAt FloridaRAMA — the immersive art experience operated by Fairgrounds Projects Inc. — she contributed a pair of dioramas that lean directly into her retro-futurist vocabulary. "Drive-In" features a Twistee Treat, a retro-style sign, and a drive-in movie theater on a hill. "Swamp Motel" is a swamp-like setting with a lurking alien creature, a hungry alligator, a retro motel, and a honeymooning couple. Both dioramas were built from foam blocks, balsa wood, foam core board, spray foam, felt, fabric, resin, and paint. She also painted the eight-wall retro-futuristic mural "Cosmic Dreams and Sunshine Beams" inside FloridaRAMA.\n\nFor SHINE Mural Festival 2022, she painted at 2421 Central Avenue in St. Petersburg, Florida. SHINE is the curated, invitation-only festival produced by the St. Petersburg Arts Alliance, which has commissioned more than 170 murals across its run.\n\nIn October 2025, Cantero was one of 16 muralists featured in "We Built This City: Women Artists of SHINE" at the Morean Arts Center, 719 Central Avenue. The exhibition opened Saturday, October 11, 2025 with a 5:00–8:00 p.m. reception and ran through October 22. The Morean partners with SHINE annually to spotlight muralists who have shaped the festival\'s legacy. The full roster of featured women artists alongside Cantero: Alyssa Marie, Artist Jones, Elizabeth Barenis, Quinn Cale, Jenipher Chandley, Dreamweaver, Amy Ilic-Volpe, Carrie Jadus, Nneka Jones, Cristi Lopez, Cecilia Lueza, Syd Bloom, Sara Salem, Nicole Salgar, and Sarah Sheppard. Amanda Cooper, Chief Curator at the Morean Arts Center, organized the show. Running concurrently was "The Shooters We Know: Curated by BASK," a street photography exhibition guest-curated by local artist BASK that ran through December 31, 2025. Both exhibitions were free and open to the public Monday through Saturday, 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.\n\nHer FloridaRAMA artist page links out to her personal site at ashleycantero.com and her Instagram at @ashleycantero, and to a Fairgrounds-published interview, "7 Questions With Fairgrounds St. Pete FloridaRAMA Artist Ashley Cantero." Her personal site at ashleycantero.com lists Home, About, Shop, and Contact sections, with both Facebook and Instagram links from the site footer.\n\nWebsite: ashleycantero.com. Instagram: @ashleycantero.',fn:['Work described as \'dark, intricate, sensual\'','Self-taught — started on skateboards','Tampa Bay native'],src:['https://www.kresscontemporary.com/artists/ashleycantero','https://www.floridarama.art/artist/ashley-cantero','https://ilovetheburg.com/two-new-street-art-exhibitions-st-pete/','https://ashleycantero.com/about-lac/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2022_SHINE_Ashley_Cantero']},
  {id:57,a:'Ben Johnston',t:'Rise',loc:'1010 Central Ave',bldg:'',lat:27.77069,lng:-82.649669,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'ben_johnston',bio:'He drew a 1,485-square-metre mural for Sierra Nevada Brewery with nothing but Sharpies and scaffolding.\n\nBen Johnston grew up in Cape Town, South Africa, briefly studied industrial design, and is otherwise self-taught. He is now based in Toronto. His work is large-scale typographic murals with 3D illusory effects — bespoke typefaces, signage, and illustration referencing Art Nouveau and pop art. The words are often chosen with local communities: "Keep Going," "Relax," "Love," "Together."\n\nClients include Nike, Lululemon, Adobe, and Apple (where he also speaks). He has won multiple Graphis Gold and Silver awards, Type Directors Club awards, and ADC One Club Gold Cubes including Best of Discipline. He has painted for MURAL Festival Montreal, BUMP Festival Calgary, and SeaWalls / Pangeaseed with the UN. His "Wordplay" solo sculpture exhibit was featured by CBC Arts.\n\nFor SHINE 2022, he painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A towering multi-story mural on the side of an apartment building spelling "RISE" in massive 3D block letters. The isometric letterforms are rendered in graduating shades of cerulean, sky blue, and white against a deep blue background, with crisp shadows giving the typography a sculptural, floating quality. Signed "Ben" in white script at the lower left. The mural dominates the building\'s full height, visible from blocks away along Central Avenue.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/ben-johnston.jpeg',from:'Toronto, Canada',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Graphis Gold and Silver (2018-19); Type Directors Club (2020-21); ADC One Club Gold Cube incl. Best of Discipline (2021); Communication Arts Awards (2017-20); SHINE 2022',fw:[{name:'benjohnston.ca',url:'https://www.benjohnston.ca'}],sbio:'He drew a 1,485-square-metre mural for Sierra Nevada Brewery with nothing but Sharpies and scaffolding. Ben Johnston is a typographic artist and muralist who creates large-scale painted works in public space. He grew up in Cape Town, South Africa, briefly studied industrial design, and is otherwise self-taught. He spent time in the agency world of South Africa before moving back to Canada to pursue freelance work focused on branding and typography. He is now based in Toronto.\n\nHis murals are large-scale typographic pieces that play with color, shadow, and perspective to create lettering that appears three-dimensional, with bespoke typefaces, signage, and illustration referencing Art Nouveau and pop art. The words are often chosen with local communities and partners — examples include "Keep Going," "Relax," "Love," and "Together." Johnston came to mural painting when a friend asked him to create a piece for the entranceway of a new office building, and the work shifted his career; he now spends roughly 80% of his time on murals for clients, charity, and personal projects. He told Scotty Russell of the Perspective Podcast that he spends no more than four days painting a mural, preferring long days to a stretched-out week. He balances outdoor mural painting with studio client work and tries to ride a bike before starting the day. His reference material includes classic design books on Bauhaus, photographs of peeling vinyl lettering, and mood boards dedicated to shadow.\n\nClients include Nike, Lululemon, Adobe, and Apple, where he has also spoken. His listed services span art direction, brand development, advertising, murals, interiors, public art, events, live art, activations, sculpture, consultation, apparel, packaging, logo design, campaigns, illustration, custom fonts, and animation.\n\nAwards include The One Show design merit (2016), The One Show print and outdoor merit (2016), Applied Arts awards for logo design and mural design (2017), TDC certificates for environmental and mural design (2017), Print magazine\'s typography award (2017), a Communication Arts award (2017), ADCC awards for installation and environmental design (2017), a Graphis Silver trophy (2018), Applied Arts awards (2018), Young Guns finalist (2018), Communication Arts awards (2019, including design and environmental), MuralFest.com Best Canadian Mural (2019), three ADC merits in typography (2019), Applied Arts awards for environmental and typography (2019), TDC Ascenders award (2019), four Graphis Gold trophies and a Graphis Silver (2019), a Communication Arts typography award (2020), four Type Directors Club awards (2020), an Applied Arts design award (2020), an ADC One Club Gold Cube (2021), an ADC One Club Gold Cube Best of Discipline (2021), and two further Type Directors Club awards (2021).\n\nExhibition history includes AGO Massive (2019), Taglialatella Gallery (2019, 2020, and 2023), ABV Gallery (2019), and Hashimoto Contemporary (2020). His "Wordplay" solo show ran at the Taglialatella Gallery in Toronto (99 Yorkville Ave.) from March 23 to April 10, 2023, and was featured by CBC Arts. Wordplay was his first foray into sculpture, and included a piece that reads "CHAOS" or "ORDER" depending on viewing angle, a vertical tube reading "LOOK ON THE BRIGHT SIDE" when tilted, and ten paintings on canvas with messages including "SOME DAY" and "RISE ABOVE." He taught himself Cinema 4D and other 3D programs to design the work; the sculptures are resin and were fabricated in China. He began using an airbrush during the show as a way to translate a 50-foot wall to canvas, and planned to bring airbrush technique back into his murals. He has cited interest in moving toward more permanent public art at 20-to 30-foot scale in brass or bronze.\n\nSpeaking engagements include Adobe in San Francisco (2017), FITC Toronto (2018), BrainStation Toronto (2018), the Ruda Fest panel in Bogota, Colombia (2018), Apple Toronto (2019), OFFF Barcelona (2019), the Hexo artist panel in Toronto (2019), Bright Walls in Jackson, Michigan (2019), PDP in Novi Sad, Serbia (2019, workshop and presentation), and TBW Talks with Arc\'teryx (2020).\n\nFestival and partner credits include MURAL Festival Montreal, BUMP Festival Calgary, SeaWalls / Pangeaseed with the UN, PowWow, ABQ Fest, VMF, Bright Walls Michigan, Daily Bread Food Bank, Fashion Revolution, the Novi Sad Foundation, Patagonia, Up Here Sudbury, and others. Press coverage includes Visi, Air Canada, TDC, and Colossal (April 20, 2020, by Vanessa Ruiz). In 2026 he is travelling for a global painting tour.\n\nFor SHINE 2022, he painted in St. Petersburg, Florida.\n\nWebsite: benjohnston.ca.',fn:['Drew a 1,485-square-metre mural for Sierra Nevada Brewery','Australian artist, international muralist','Intricate line work at building scale'],src:['https://www.benjohnston.ca/about-1','https://www.thisiscolossal.com/2020/04/ben-johnston-typography-murals/','https://www.cbc.ca/arts/ben-johnston-wordplay-toronto-sculpture-exhibit-1.6785892','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/ben-johnston-2022']},
  {id:58,a:'Chad Mize',t:'We Are St. Pete',loc:'20th St. S. & Pinellas Trail',bldg:'Pinellas Trail',lat:27.768667,lng:-82.660133,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'chadmize',bio:'CHAD MIZE is a multimedia artist, designer and curator residing in St. Petersburg, Florida. He was born in New Orleans, Louisiana in 1974, and then moved to Tampa Bay Florida, where he spent most of his childhood. An early interest in art resulted in his earning a BFA in Visual Communications at the University of Mississippi.\n\nHis early career included drawing caricature portraits at Faneuil Hall in Boston and illustrating Flash animations for a digital textbook company in the late 1990s. In 2003, MIZE moved to St. Petersburg, FL and has been championing the Tampa Bay art scene ever since.\n\nMIZE created the "Paris/Tokyo/London/St. Pete" shirt in 2010. After 16 years, it is still selling in local shops, popular among both locals and tourists.\n\nMIZE co-founded BLUELUCY gallery on Central Avenue in 2010 (representing 450-plus artists before closing in 2015).\n\nIn 2018, Mize opened the gallery MIZE in the Historic Uptown neighborhood of St Petersburg. Following the closing of MIZE in 2022 he set his sights on a larger gallery project entitled SPACE. Currently he is curating exhibits throughout Tampa Bay at various venues.\n\nHe served on SHINE\'s organizing committee for a decade (2015-2025).\n\nWith a love for past and contemporary culture, he takes a humorous, tongue-in-cheek approach to his designs, which include playful doodles, colorful patterns, political art and graphics with text.\n\nMize\'s client list has included Aids Healthcare Foundation, The Dali Museum, Facebook, COACH, NUTRL, Tampa Bay Lightning, Tampa Bay Rays, Sprouts, Kahwa, Busch Gardens, The Vinoy Hotel, and JW Marriott.\n\nMize\'s work is held in numerous private and corporate collections nationally and internationally.',desc:'',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/chad-mize-we-are-st-pete.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE organizing committee (6+ years); James Museum featured artist (2025); Dali Museum \'Outside In\' (2025); founded BLUELUCY and MIZE Gallery',fw:[{name:'chadmize.com',url:'http://chadmize.com'}],sbio:'He created the "Paris/Tokyo/London/St. Pete" shirt in 2010 and it is still everywhere. Chad Mize was born in 1974 in New Orleans, Louisiana, and moved to Tampa Bay as a child, spending most of his childhood in the Bradenton area south of St. Petersburg. He visited the Dali Museum and Jannus Live as a kid, which built an early attachment to the city he would later call home. He earned a BFA in Visual Communications at the University of Mississippi in Oxford, then moved to Boston in his twenties, where he drew sidewalk character portraits in front of Faneuil Hall — an unpolished gig that laid the groundwork for the improvisational style he still uses. In 1998 he became an illustrator for a digital textbook company, creating Flash animations for educational content. Laid off a week before 9/11, he co-founded the creative studio Bluelucy with partner Phillip Clark and moved back to Florida. He considered Sarasota but chose St. Petersburg in 2003, largely because the Dali Museum convinced him a small city could support world-class art. He has lived in St. Pete for over seventeen years.\n\nMize works as a multimedia artist, designer, and muralist. His style is bold, cartoon-like imagery — playful doodles, colorful patterns, political art, and graphics with text — taking a humorous, tongue-in-cheek approach to politics, religion, and contemporary culture. He cites Keith Haring as a major influence, specifically Haring\'s belief that art should be accessible; he visited Haring\'s Pop Shop in New York as a child and that inspired him to open his own retail spaces. His father, who died eight years before the St. Pete Catalyst interview, was also a major influence — a people person and hard worker. His artist statement: "My purpose for creating art is to inspire and uplift the viewer. I consistently saturate my work with colors and concepts that radiate happiness."\n\nHe has painted over 120 murals of his own design, mostly in Tampa Bay but also in Miami and other cities. Some of St. Pete\'s first murals were painted by Mize behind Bluelucy, including iconic images of Twiggy and Mr. Sun, which still remain partially obscured by later upgrades. Recent and notable mural work includes University Town Center in Sarasota (2026), AIDS Healthcare Foundation in St. Petersburg (2025), Fit 2 Run in St. Petersburg (2025), IMG Academy in Bradenton (2025), The Factory in St. Petersburg (2025), St. Pete Pier (2025), Troyer Residence (2025), Dali Museum (2025), 821 16th Street N in St. Petersburg (2025), Jackson Residence (2025), The Adderley in Ft. Lauderdale (2025), Antoinette Residence (2025), Gibson/DeSomery Residence (2025), Wyndham Grand in Clearwater Beach (2024), Wagner Residence in St. Petersburg (2024), Creative Contractors in Clearwater (2024), Element in Tampa (2024), and NUTRL x Hollander Hotel in St. Petersburg (2024).\n\nHis client list includes AIDS Healthcare Foundation, The Dali Museum, COACH, NUTRL, Tampa Bay Lightning, Tampa Bay Rays, Sprouts, Kahwa, Busch Gardens, The Vinoy Hotel, and JW Marriott. His work is held in private and corporate collections nationally and internationally. His designs have appeared on Coach brand bags, paddle-ball rackets, t-shirts, and walls across the city.\n\nIn 2009 the 600 block of Central Avenue transformed into a hub for artist-run shops and galleries, and Bluelucy at 653 Central Avenue thrived. Bluelucy hosted monthly themed exhibitions featuring dozens — sometimes over a hundred — local artists, providing both exposure and income (it represented over 450 artists before closing in 2014/2015 due to rising rents). In 2005 he helped start the St. Pete Shuffle. In 2010 he met his now-husband Dan Schmidt and, while traveling, spotted a "Paris/Tokyo/London/Torrance" shirt design and made his St. Pete version. In 2015 he was asked to help launch the SHINE Mural Festival, working closely with director Jenee Priebe; he served on SHINE\'s organizing committee for over six years. In 2018 he opened MIZE Gallery in the Historic Uptown neighborhood on MLK Jr. Street, which ran for five years (closing 2022) hosting monthly exhibitions of Bay Area talent and pivoting to virtual shows during the pandemic. In 2023 he opened SPACE in a former PSTA bus depot on south 28th street, a short-lived but legendary venue that added performance to the mix and featured Bay-area stalwarts including Bask, Zulu Painter, Vitale Brothers, IBOMBS, Frank Strunk III, and Andrea Pawlisz. Pawlisz was a key collaborator at both SPACE and MIZE Gallery. The city was not keen on the large gatherings and SPACE shuttered after only a few shows. He also remembers the warehouse parties of the 2000s with the Vitale Brothers, alongside the rise of Central Avenue galleries Craftsman House, Shapiro\'s, and Davidson Fine Art.\n\nIn 2025 Mize is a featured artist at the James Museum, displaying work in the stone entry canyon, and is included in "Outside In: New Murals Inspired by Dali" at the Dali Museum (opening May 24, 2025), which features twelve former SHINE mural artists painting inside the museum walls. He curates art pop-ups at Green Bench Brewery and runs "Side Spot," a mini-gallery next to his husband Dan\'s restaurant Uptown Eats. The Artisan Magazine cover story by Robin O\'Dell (April 28, 2025) called him "St. Pete\'s Godfather of Art." Contact: info@chadmize.com, 727-251-8529, chadmize.com.\n\nCHAD MIZE is a multimedia artist, designer and curator residing in St. Petersburg, Florida. He was born in New Orleans, Louisiana in 1974, and then moved to Tampa Bay Florida, where he spent most of his childhood. An early interest in art resulted in his earning a BFA in Visual Communications at the University of Mississippi.\n\nHis early career included drawing caricature portraits at Faneuil Hall in Boston and illustrating Flash animations for a digital textbook company in the late 1990s. In 2003, MIZE moved to St. Petersburg, FL and has been championing the Tampa Bay art scene ever since.\n\nMIZE created the "Paris/Tokyo/London/St. Pete" shirt in 2010. After 16 years, it is still selling in local shops, popular among both locals and tourists.\n\nMIZE co-founded BLUELUCY gallery on Central Avenue in 2010 (representing 450-plus artists before closing in 2015).\n\nIn 2018, Mize opened the gallery MIZE in the Historic Uptown neighborhood of St Petersburg. Following the closing of MIZE in 2022 he set his sights on a larger gallery project entitled SPACE. Currently he is curating exhibits throughout Tampa Bay at various venues.\n\nHe served on SHINE\'s organizing committee for a decade (2015-2025).\n\nWith a love for past and contemporary culture, he takes a humorous, tongue-in-cheek approach to his designs, which include playful doodles, colorful patterns, political art and graphics with text.\n\nMize\'s client list has included Aids Healthcare Foundation, The Dali Museum, Facebook, COACH, NUTRL, Tampa Bay Lightning, Tampa Bay Rays, Sprouts, Kahwa, Busch Gardens, The Vinoy Hotel, and JW Marriott.\n\nMize\'s work is held in numerous private and corporate collections nationally and internationally.',src:['https://theartisanmagazine.com/chad-size-stpete/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/influencers/chad-mize/','http://chadmize.com/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/chad-mize-2022']},
  {id:59,a:'Dream Weaver',t:'',loc:'1510 1st Ave. N.',bldg:'',lat:27.771635,lng:-82.654535,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'dream.weavin',bio:'She painted her first mural at seven, on her aunt\'s backyard shed in Waco, Texas. She has not stopped.\n\nTaylor Smith — Dreamweaver — grew up between Portland, Oregon and just south of Atlanta, born in Dallas. She studied communications and fine art at Georgia College and State University, with a minor in graphic design, graduating in 2017. Before that, she took private painting lessons as a kid and apprenticed at a tattoo shop at 16 — too young to actually tattoo under Georgia law, but old enough to learn.\n\nShe spent time doing live painting at music festivals — Electric Daisy Carnival in Vegas, Okeechobee, Electric Forest — before relocating to Florida around 2019. She works almost entirely in spray paint, blending expressive abstraction with realism. Nature runs through everything: wildlife, water, birds, flora. She is left-handed.\n\nShe has painted an estimated 120 to 150 murals, 15 to 20 in St. Pete alone. Other projects include the Clearwater Municipal Parking Garage and a sea turtle mural at Coachman Park for Art Oasis 2024, and "The Garden Within" in downtown Kissimmee for Osceola Arts. She is a resident artist at AER Tampa and a member of Few and Far, an international women\'s graffiti crew.\n\nFor SHINE she painted in 2022 at 1510 1st Ave N and returned for SHINE Origins 2025 at the Vernis Bowling building on the 600 block — where the SHINE movement started in 2012.',desc:'A massive wall-sized explosion of color — a hybrid floral-phoenix form rendered in neon pinks, fiery oranges, deep purples, cerulean blues, and electric teals against a dark blue background. Feathered petals radiate outward from the center while swirling organic tendrils and tropical leaves frame the edges. Signed "Dreamweaver" in stylized script at the lower left. A SHINE festival plaque is visible at the base.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/dreamweaver.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2022 selected artist; SHINE Origins 2025 selected artist; Art Oasis Mural Festival 2024; Few and Far international women\'s graffiti crew member',fw:[{name:'weavindreams.com',url:'https://weavindreams.com'}],sbio:'She painted her first mural at seven, on her aunt Carolyn\'s backyard shed in Waco, Texas. She has not stopped.\n\nTaylor Smith — professionally Dreamweaver — was born in Dallas, Texas, grew up between Portland, Oregon and just south of Atlanta, Georgia. She earned a BA in Communications and Fine Art with a minor in Graphic Design from Georgia College and State University in 2017. She took private painting lessons throughout elementary and middle school and did a tattoo apprenticeship at 16 — eight months, but Georgia age restrictions meant she couldn\'t actually tattoo.\n\nAfter college she started live painting at concerts and festivals in Atlanta (2017), worked in restaurant service, graphic design, and creative event production, then relocated to Florida around 2018-2019. She went full-time as an artist around 2020. She works primarily in spray paint and aerosol, blending expressive abstraction with realism. Nature is the constant — wildlife, water, birds, flora and fauna. She also paints on canvas, airbrush, skateboard decks, surfboards, shoes, and backpacks. She is left-handed.\n\nShe has painted an estimated 120 to 150 murals, with 15 to 20 in St. Pete. Festival credits include SHINE 2022 (mural at 1510 1st Ave N, Yacht Power Products building), SHINE Origins 2025 (Vernis Bowling building at 696 1st Ave N), Art Oasis Mural Festival 2024 (Clearwater Municipal Parking Garage and a marine-themed sea turtle mural at Coachman Park Dockmaster\'s Office), Wynwood Mural Festival, Paint Memphis 2019, and Art Basel. She has done live painting at Electric Daisy Carnival (Las Vegas), Okeechobee Music Festival, and Electric Forest Festival.\n\nOther projects include "The Garden Within" at 219-231 Broadway in downtown Kissimmee for Osceola Arts\' ARTisNOW Public Murals Project (2025). She was Artist in Residence at Parc at Wesley Chapel, Tampa (2023) and is a resident artist at AER Tampa. She is a contributor to Few and Far, an international women\'s graffiti crew, and collaborates with fellow artist "Slim Gee" under KindMinds Art (@kindmindsart). She teaches spray painting and mural techniques to youth communities and is listed on Creative Pinellas as "an avid supporter of improving our communities through public art and collaboration."\n\nProfiled in Voyage MIA Magazine (January 2020) and The Artisan Magazine. Contact: Dreamweavin.murals@gmail.com. Website: weavindreams.com. Instagram: @dream.weavin.',fn:['Painted her first mural at age seven','Mother-daughter duo Dreamweaver','Community mural specialists'],src:['https://voyagemia.com/interview/meet-taylor-smith-dreamweaver-designs-st-pete-fl/','https://www.artistsalapts.com/taylor-smith','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/dream-weaver-2022','https://monochronicle.com/artist/dreamweaver/','https://weavindreams.com/aboutdreamweaver','https://theartisanmagazine.com/i-am-dreamweavin-my-way-and-bringing-life-to-life-through-art/','https://www.osceolaarts.org/post/new-mural-the-garden-within-blooms-in-downtown-kissimmee','https://creativepinellas.org/artist/taylor-smith/']},
  {id:60,a:'Egypt Hagan',t:'',loc:'299 Dr MLK Jr St N',bldg:'George F. Young',lat:27.775253,lng:-82.646345,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'graffitiegypt',bio:'She uses portraiture as storytelling — bold, vibrant colors carrying cultural imagery and narratives that go untold elsewhere.\n\nEgypt Hagan is originally from Los Angeles, now based in Tampa. Her first solo exhibition was in New York in 2014. She has done international commissions and community work, including "The Silent Symphony" — a mural commissioned by Burlington City Arts and Vermont\'s Office of Racial Equity for Juneteenth 2021. For SHINE 2022, she painted at 299 Dr. MLK Jr. Street North.',desc:'Two faces in profile fill the multi-story wall of the George F. Young building, surrounded by roses and soft washes of warm color. The portraits are rendered with expressive, painterly brushwork — detailed enough to read as specific individuals but loose enough to feel like fine art rather than photorealism. Roses and floral elements weave around and between the two figures.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/egypt-hagan.jpeg',from:'Arizona',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2022 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'She uses portraiture as storytelling — bold, vibrant colors carrying cultural imagery and narratives that go untold elsewhere. Egypt Hagan, who works as Graffiti Egypt, was born in Los Angeles. She moved to New York, where she held her first solo exhibition in 2014. She later relocated to Tampa, Florida, and per her own website is now based in Arizona, continuing to expand her practice through large-scale mural projects, public commissions, and international exhibitions.\n\nShe is self-taught. As a child she was shy and used art as her main source of expression, drawing every day in class and sketching designs on her homework. She started painting on canvases in 2011, and her work became influenced by society, culture, and historical figures. Before painting full time she worked jobs at a library, an airport, warehouses, and in security. Seven years after she committed to art as her career, she had moved into full-time work as an artist and muralist. By March 2022 she was 31 years old.\n\nHer street art is known for vibrant colors, shapes, and cultural images. Her palette runs to pinks, luminous golds, and combined patterns, and her imagery draws on African fashions and on iconic figures including Nina Simone, Prince, Muhammad Ali, James Baldwin, Sade, and Notorious B.I.G. Her work is inspired by Afro Caribbean and African folklore, nature, and sci-fi, with a focus on storytelling — a tradition she describes as passed down for generations in her family, dating back to ancient Africa. A recent focus has been African tribes, with portraits intended to shed light on tribes that still exist and practice their traditions today.\n\nFor SHINE 2022 in St. Petersburg she painted at 299 Dr. MLK Jr. Street North.\n\nIn Vermont she painted "The Silent Symphony" for Juneteenth 2021, commissioned by Burlington City Arts and the Office of Racial Equity, Inclusion and Belonging. The mural depicts young tribal women and a man wearing traditional African wardrobes, standing together to convey the significance of Juneteenth — the story of strong African people who have endured and now stand tall and fearless. BCA Center is at 135 Church Street, Burlington, Vermont 05401.\n\nIn Pembroke Pines, Florida, she was commissioned by the city to create "The Valiant Four: A Tribute to Black History," a four-sided interactive mural of Black leaders. Residents engaged with the artist on site at the Charles F. Dodge City Center, 601 City Center Way, layering vibrant colors as the background with brushes and rollers. Guests watched the mural come to life during a special edition of the Jazz in the Pines concert at the Charles F. Dodge City Center plaza in February 2022.\n\nHer projects have been displayed in Canada, Chicago, New York, Colorado, Los Angeles, Wisconsin, Miami, and at The W Hotel in Fort Lauderdale. She has made Florida her permanent home and has spoken about the difference between the Hispanic-influenced art scene of California, close to Mexico, and the Cuban and Jamaican cultural influences she finds in Florida.\n\nShe has also worked in product design. Spin Master Games featured three of her portraits in its "Spotlight" jigsaw puzzle series, sold exclusively at Target for under ten dollars. The 500-piece puzzles feature portraits of people from diverse African tribes and focus on the subject\'s individualism. The jigsaw art was reworked from her original paintings using lively colors, radiant gold, and fearless portraiture. One of the puzzle portraits is titled "Morning." Spin Master Games found her through social media. The Spotlight Series is described as a platform for underrepresented voices to celebrate their diversity and share their stories.\n\nHer own statement on her practice: "We as humans bare a unique story. I enjoy creating these stories not with the use of words, but bold images and an underlying vibrancy that emphasizes the main subject. My paintings consist of people or moments I\'ve come across that have inspired my journey here on Earth, untold stories that deserve to be heard." On her message: "My main message is to tell a story through each individual. About it\'s OK being 100% your authentic self. Topics and subjects may change, but it\'s a distinctive style where people know who it is and where it comes from." On her sources: "I\'m inspired by meeting everyday people and life. It might be someone with a crazy hairstyle or the way they dress influence a lot of my portraits within my artwork along with dating back to various African tribes and diving deep into other cultures."\n\nShe has been profiled by the Sun-Sentinel (Nile Fortner, March 18, 2022) and PRINT Magazine (Chloe Gordon, February 24, 2022), where she was described as an emerging Black artist using rich colors and bold images to tell beautiful and emotional stories. In March 2022 she held a solo exhibit in her hometown of Los Angeles.\n\nWebsite: graffitiegypt.com.',fn:['Portraiture as storytelling','Bold, vibrant colors and cultural identity','Local Florida artist'],src:['https://www.graffitiegypt.com/about','https://www.sun-sentinel.com/2022/03/18/murals-with-meaning-graffiti-egypt-tells-a-story-through-vibrant-portraits/','https://www.printmag.com/color-design/emerging-black-artist-graffiti-egypt-showcases-her-gorgeous-murals-through-jigsaw-puzzles/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/egypt-hagan-2022','https://www.burlingtoncityarts.org/silent-symphony']},
  {id:61,a:'Imagine',t:'',loc:'240 Dr MLK Jr St N',bldg:'RetroTiques',lat:27.77412,lng:-82.647188,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'imagine876',bio:'She has a master\'s from Harvard. She founded Nepal\'s first Children\'s Art Museum. She paints mindful mantras in Devanagari script with spray paint.\n\nSneha Shrestha — Imagine — was born in Nepal and is based in Boston, where she works as Arts Program Manager at Harvard\'s Mittal South Asia Institute. Her style fuses Nepali, Tibetan, and Japanese Buddhist mandalas with graffiti aesthetics and American abstract expressionism. She uses a disciplined breathing technique while painting.\n\nShe is the first contemporary Nepali artist in the collection of the Museum of Fine Arts Boston. Her work is also in Harvard Art Museums, the Davis Museum, and the Worcester Art Museum. She has painted at MIT, created a monumental sculpture for the Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum, and won the James and Audrey Foster Prize from the ICA Boston (2025). Other clients include Google, Facebook, and Fidelity. Nepal\'s Cultural Council named her one of the country\'s 100 most influential women.\n\nFor SHINE 2022, she painted at RetroTiques on Dr. MLK Jr. Street North.',desc:'A long parking-lot wall painted solid hot pink, covered with repeating columns of gold Sanskrit-inspired calligraphy. The metallic gold letterforms cascade downward in ornate clusters, with subtler red and pink swirls layered underneath. The effect is meditative and hypnotic — the same sacred text echoing across the entire wall like a visual chant.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/imagine.jpeg',from:'Kathmandu, Nepal',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'James and Audrey Foster Prize, ICA Boston (2025); Artist of the Year, Center for Arts at the Armory (2023); 100 most influential women in Nepal (2018); Boston Artist-in-Residence (2018); MFA Boston collection; SHINE 2022',fw:[{name:'imagine876.com',url:'https://www.imagine876.com'}],sbio:'She has a master\'s from Harvard. She founded Nepal\'s first Children\'s Art Museum. She paints mindful mantras in Devanagari script with spray paint.\n\nSneha Shrestha works under the name IMAGINE. She was born and raised in Nepal and is based in Boston. She holds a Master\'s degree in Education from the Harvard Graduate School of Education, where she studied effective leadership in education and the intersections of creativity, learning, and technology. She is the Arts Program Manager at Harvard\'s Mittal South Asia Institute, where she works to amplify Asian art and culture.\n\nHer practice bridges her native Devanagari script with the visual language of graffiti handstyles. She is the first artist to mesh Nepali alphabets with American graffiti. Her style draws on Nepali, Tibetan, and Japanese Buddhist mandalas, Sanskrit scriptures, Turkish calligraphy, and American abstract expressionism. She works across painting, murals, and sculpture, moving between meditative abstraction and large-scale public intervention. In some bodies of work she foregrounds calligraphic repetition drawn from Sanskrit scriptures and immigration documents. In others she shifts toward architectural scale and sculptural presence in brass, steel, and site-responsive installation. Her central themes include cultural continuity, diaspora identity, guardianship, migration, and belonging.\n\nShe is the first contemporary Nepali artist included in the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston permanent collection, with her painting Home416. Her show Mindful Mandalas was on view at the MFA Boston. Her sculpture Dwarpalika was acquired by the Harvard Art Museums and is on long-term view on the second floor. Her work is also in the collections of the Davis Museum, the Worcester Art Museum, Google, Facebook, Capital One, Fidelity Investments, Reebok, Neiman Marcus, TripAdvisor, Red Bull, Montana Cans, and Harvard.\n\nShe was awarded the James and Audrey Foster Prize by the Institute of Contemporary Art (ICA), Boston, with the Foster Prize show running at ICA Boston in 2025-2026. She had a solo show, Forms and Foundations, at Aicon Gallery in New York in 2025, and a solo show, Ritual and Devotion, at the Cantor Arts Gallery, College of the Holy Cross in 2024. She participated in the group exhibitions Deities of Nepal II at the Nepal Arts Council (2024), Reimagine: Himalayan Art Now at The Rubin Museum (2024), and a 2024 exhibition at Wrightwood659 in Chicago. She was selected for a Studio Residency at the Boston Center for the Arts funded by the Wagner Foundation.\n\nHer monumental sculpture Calling the Earth to Witness was commissioned by the Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum. Her public art sculpture About a Living Culture in Queens, New York was included in Our Culture magazine\'s 5 Innovative Examples of Public Art. She is the artist behind the landmark mural on an MIT building at the corner of Massachusetts Avenue and Main Street in Cambridge, MA. From August 2018 through September 2022, her mural Knowledge is Power was installed at Northeastern University along the concourse between Ell Hall and the Curry Student Center outside Gallery 360, using vibrant reds, blues, and gold drawn from the colors she would see in Nepal.\n\nHer honors include a grant from the Collective Futures Fund funded by the Andy Warhol Foundation (2024), the Artist of the Year Award from the Center for Arts at the Armory (2023), inclusion in WBUR The ARTery\'s 25 Millennials of Color (2019), recognition as one of the 100 most influential women in Nepal by the Nepal Cultural Council (2018), the Boston Artist-in-Residence Award selected by the Mayor\'s Office of Arts and Culture (2018), the HUBWeek Change Maker Award (2018), the South Asia and the Arts Fund Grant from Harvard University (2017), and the Project Zero Artist-in-Residence Award from Harvard University (2017).\n\nShe has shown work and painted public walls across the United States, Indonesia, Istanbul, Switzerland, Denmark, and Kathmandu. Earlier in her career she worked at Boston\'s Artists for Humanity, an experience that pushed her toward dedicating her life to art, education, and social impact. She later established Nepal\'s first Children\'s Art Museum, a space where children and youth develop 21st-century skills through project-based art experiences. She was the Somerville Arts Council Artist of the Month in May 2020. She was profiled in the Boston Globe\'s The Questions feature in November 2020 and in The Huntington News in November 2018.\n\nFor SHINE 2022, she painted at RetroTiques on Dr. MLK Jr. Street North.\n\nWebsite: imagine876.com. Instagram: @imagine876. Contact: imagine876@gmail.com.',fn:['Has a master\'s from Harvard','Founded Nepal\'s first contemporary art gallery','Hyperrealistic photomurals'],src:['https://www.imagine876.com/about','https://www.gardnermuseum.org/about/community-efforts/luminaries/shrestha-sneha','https://publicart.northeastern.edu/artists/imagine/','https://somervilleartscouncil.org/artistmonth/imagine']},
  {id:62,a:'James Bullough',t:'',loc:'2201 Central Ave.',bldg:'Central Neurology',lat:27.771227,lng:-82.663499,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'james_bullough',bio:'He was a middle-school teacher in Baltimore. He moved to Berlin in 2010 and started painting buildings.\n\nJames Bullough is self-taught in realistic oil painting, trained by studying the Old Masters of the Netherlands. His style is photorealistic portraiture combined with graphic fragmentation — figures in mid-motion, dissected and shattered, in oil, latex, acrylic, and spray paint. He works from his Berlin studio.\n\nHe painted an eleven-story mural on Landsberger Allee in Berlin with collaborator Addison Karl, facade murals for the URBAN NATION Museum, and walls at Wynwood Walls, POW! WOW! Hawaii, and Lollapalooza. He is represented by Thinkspace Projects in LA and Stolenspace Gallery in London. He co-created VantagePoint Radio, an interview show on urban art. For SHINE 2022, he painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A multi-story mural on the side of a building depicting a woman in a crimson dress, her body fragmented into sweeping ribbon-like shards that scatter across a dark black background. The photorealistic figure — head tilted back, arms outstretched — appears to be dissolving or reassembling mid-leap. Deep navy blue, coral red, and muted lavender geometric strips weave through the composition, creating a sense of explosive movement against the void.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/james-bullough.jpeg',from:'Berlin, Germany',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2022 selected artist',fw:[{name:'jamesbullough.com',url:'https://jamesbullough.com'}],sbio:'He was a middle-school teacher in Baltimore. He moved to Berlin in 2010 and started painting buildings.\n\nJames Bullough is an American-born artist who grew up in Washington, DC and now lives and works in Berlin, Germany. As a kid in the DC exurbs, he was fascinated by the innovative graffiti he saw around the DC subway, and that early exposure to gritty urban art drove him to make his own work. Alongside the graffiti influence, he taught himself realistic oil painting techniques by studying the Old Masters of the Netherlands. Combining the technical precision of one with the momentum of the other, his work stages compelling contrasts and juxtapositions — photorealistic portraiture combined with graphic fragmentation, figures in mid-motion, dissected and shattered. He works in oil, latex, acrylic, and spray paint.\n\nHe moved from the United States to Germany in 2010 after quitting his middle-school teaching job in Baltimore to focus on his artwork full-time. While in Berlin, he shifted his focus from the paintbrush to the spray can, transitioning from photorealistic oil murals to photorealistic spray-paint murals. In his first three years in Berlin, he worked with American-born, Berlin-based artist Addison Karl under the name JBAK. The pair painted widely across Germany and the United States. Their best-known Berlin piece is "Totem," painted at Landsberger Allee 228B in 2014 — an eleven-story mural depicting three individuals standing on each other\'s backs, completed using a crane over the course of a month.\n\nBullough is featured in URBAN NATION\'s roster as a Berlin-based artist and has been part of multiple URBAN NATION projects, including Wynwood Walls, Unity, and Lollapalooza. He painted a One Wall in Berlin and was featured in the URBAN NATION 2018 exhibition "UN-DERSTAND The Power of Art as a Social Architect" at the URBAN NATION Museum for Urban Contemporary Art in Berlin-Schöneberg (Bülowstrasse 7). The 2017 ONE WALL project marking the museum\'s opening included Berlin Kidz, Paradox, 1UP, James Bullough, and 2501, who created four large-format murals on the façade of a Berlin-Schöneberg building.\n\nIn 2014 he co-created and hosted VantagePoint Radio, an interview show focused on urban art and the graffiti and mural artists who occupy the genre. Each episode features an established artist or group of artists in the contemporary art scene.\n\nHe is represented by Thinkspace Projects in Los Angeles (4217 W. Jefferson Blvd., Los Angeles, CA 90016) and Stolenspace Gallery in London. His Thinkspace exhibition history includes "Breaking Point" (Project Room, May 2016), with works including "Furthest Reach," "Good Hurt," "Breaking Point," "Away," "Collide," and "Pull This Thread (As I Walk Away)"; "Parallel Truths" (Main Room, 2020), with works including "Into The Ether," "Morning Light," "The Ghost In Me," "As I Fall," "Cascade," "Release Me," "Numb," "Into Shadow," "Linger," "Afterthought," "Flicker," "Dust," "Fade," "LA," "Love Hate," "Torn," and the "Orbit" hand-finished print editions; "F CANCER" (Gallery II, 2023), with "F*ck Cancer"; and "Stand Strong" at The Brand Library and Art Center in Glendale, California (2024), with "Dylan." Other Thinkspace-affiliated shows include "La Familia" (Thinkspace 10th Anniversary Show, 2015), a curated exhibit at the Honolulu Museum of Art School (2015), "Gumbo" (2015), "Vitality and Verve: Transforming the Urban Landscape" (2015), "LAX / DTW" at Inner State Gallery in Detroit (2015), "Scope Miami Beach" (2015, 2016, 2017), "SCOPE New York" (2016), "LAX / ORD" at Vertical Gallery in Chicago (2016), "POW! WOW! Hawai\'i 2017" at Honolulu Museum of Art School, "POW! WOW! Exploring The New Contemporary Art Movement" (2018), "LAX / DTW: Detroit Hustle II" (2017), Moniker Art Fair in London Stand 34 (2017), "15 Years of Thinkspace" (2019), and Group Show Inaugurating the New Gallery Space alongside "SCOPE Immersive Miami Beach Part 2" (2020).\n\nOther documented works on his own jamesbullough.com site include "Hydra Sculpture" in Washington DC, "Release" for the Handel With Care Project in Dallas, Texas, "Tin-Can Telephone" for the MuralsDC Project in Washington DC, and "Peacekeeper" at URBAN NATION in Berlin. His "Desi" mural in Brooklyn, NYC is cited as a representative example of his portraits of women rendered against negative space. His "Breaking Point" series at Thinkspace was made working with dancers from Berlin, capturing fractured moments through expressive body motion against expanses of negative space; the models remain faceless throughout to reaffirm the universality of the emotive physical motion.\n\nFor SHINE 2022, he painted in St. Petersburg.\n\nWebsite: jamesbullough.com.',fn:['Was a middle-school teacher in Baltimore','Moved to Berlin for the street art scene','Photorealistic figures with glitch effects'],src:['https://jamesbullough.com/','https://muralform.com/artists/james-bullough-bio/','https://thinkspaceprojects.com/artists/james-bullough/','https://urban-nation.com/artist/james-bullough/']},
  {id:64,a:'Jeff Williams',t:'',loc:'511 Dr. MLK Jr St. N.',bldg:'Myoblast Strength',lat:27.777771,lng:-82.646536,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'sunsigns.stpete',bio:'This wall looks like it could have been on Dr. MLK Jr. Street since 1955. Every sign on it is hand-painted, and brand new.\n\nJeff Williams is a St. Petersburg native who founded Sun Signs & Lettering in 2015. He was a leather engraver before he was a sign painter, and came to the trade by way of house painting — using leftover paint after long days on the job to make work of his own. He taught himself lettering in 2012 after watching the documentary "Sign Painters." The 1930s showcard aesthetic stuck. His style draws from 1930s-40s showcards, mid-century design, 1950s advertising, and American Traditional tattoo flash — clean, legible letterforms with intentional negative space. He runs his shop the way one would have run in 1955.\n\nHe painted signage at Sunken Gardens, working with the site\'s archivist, and lettered a mini truck for Disney Springs. He has been featured on WEDU Arts Plus (PBS) and Fox 13. For SHINE 2022, he painted at Myoblast Strength on Dr. MLK Jr. Street North.',desc:'A tall wall painted as a stack of vintage Florida tourism signs in bold primary colors. From top to bottom: "Florida SOUVENIRS" with palm trees, "ST. PETERSBURG" in large white block letters on black, "the sun... the sea... you & me — THE SUNSHINE CITY" on red with pin-up illustrations and oranges, "FLORIDA" in classic postcard lettering, "Tampa Bay" in red script with a pelican mascot, and "Sunshine 360 Days A Year" with a retro couple in green. Every detail is hand-painted in authentic mid-century sign style.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/jeff-williams.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2022 selected artist',fw:[{name:'sunsignsstpete.com',url:'https://www.sunsignsstpete.com'}],sbio:'He was a leather engraver before he was a sign painter. He taught himself lettering in 2012. The 1930s showcard aesthetic stuck.\n\nJeff Williams is a St. Petersburg native and the founder of Sun Signs & Lettering, the hand-painted sign company he started in 2015. Sun Signs is based out of St. Petersburg, Florida, and has worked with countless local and independent businesses across the greater Tampa Bay Area. The shop\'s emphasis from the start has been on traditional hand-painted sign techniques, with adaptations that combine modern technology and old-world applications depending on the job.\n\nWilliams came to signs by way of house painting. He had always dabbled in art, and after long days painting houses he would come home and use the leftover paint to make work of his own. Selling the art was difficult, so he turned to signs as a more practical way to keep a paintbrush in his hand. In 2012 he saw the documentary "Sign Painters," which he credits with opening his eyes to the larger sign-painting community and sparking his serious pursuit of the trade. He taught himself by painting alphabets that, in his words, never saw the light of day, working through trial and error. He pulls reference from old sign books and from old signs he photographs while traveling, keeping a running collection he refers back to.\n\nThe majority of Sun Signs\' work is hand-painted reverse glass — lettering done on the inside of windows, either painted or in genuine gold leaf. The shop also offers A-frame signage, fascia signs, wall signs, vehicle lettering, truck lettering, panel signs, hanging signs, address numerals, storefront signage, interior lettering, logos, business cards, promotional materials, and full branding packages. Williams\' process starts with a design on paper, hand-drawn or printed, then uses an electro pounce — a perforating machine grounded to a metal easel — to trace the design with a stylus, electrocuting small holes that he passes charcoal or chalk powder through to transfer the pattern onto a wall, window, or wooden panel. On-site jobs typically take one to three days with the client. He estimates his time runs about 50/50 between studio work and on-site work, mostly working solo and bringing on help when a job is large enough.\n\nHis style draws from 1930s-40s showcard aesthetics, mid-century design, 1950s advertising, and American Traditional tattoo flash, with clean, legible letterforms and intentional negative space. He has described his shop as run the way a sign shop would have been in the 1950s. Sun Signs is popular with restaurants, coffee shops, tattoo shops, and barbershops around the Bay Area. Williams views himself as a keeper of the craft, referencing what came before him and bringing it forward at a time when there are no longer schools or traditional apprenticeships for sign painting. He has noted that vinyl signage in the 1980s brought the hand-painted industry to a halt, and that the form has been seeing a resurgence.\n\nBeyond storefront work, Williams has painted signage at Sunken Gardens in St. Petersburg, working with the site\'s archivist, and lettered a mini truck for Disney Springs. He also paints murals, cards, small signs, and cars.\n\nHis work has been featured on WEDU Arts Plus on PBS — Season 12, Episode 15, a six-minute clip that aired August 31, 2023, profiling him as a sign artist creating custom-painted signs for local businesses throughout St. Petersburg and beyond. He was also featured on FOX 13 Tampa Bay in a Made in Tampa Bay segment by photojournalist Corey Beckman, published November 18, 2021, headlined "St. Pete artist resurrects hand-painted sign industry after going flat in the 1980s."\n\nFor SHINE 2022, Williams painted at Myoblast Strength on Dr. MLK Jr. Street North in St. Petersburg.\n\nSun Signs & Lettering takes commissions through a sign request form covering painted storefront signage (windows, doors, address numerals, contact info, hours, full storefronts, building walls, interior lettering), panel signs (A-frames, hanging signs, wall signs), and graphic design and branding work. The shop\'s tagline: "A business with no sign is a sign of no business." Website: sunsignsstpete.com.',fn:['Leather engraver before sign painter','Traditional lettering and Americana style','Local St. Pete craftsman'],src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/jeff-williams-2022','https://gnarlymagazine.com/blogs/news/the-art-of-hand-painted-signs-a-journey-with-jeff-williams-of-sun-signs','https://www.pbs.org/video/1215-jeff-williams-2nmbue/','https://www.sunsignsstpete.com/','https://www.fox13news.com/news/st-pete-artist-helping-hand-painted-sign-industry-bounce-back-after-going-flat-in-the-1980s']},
  {id:65,a:'MadC',t:'',loc:'1010 Central Ave.',bldg:'',lat:27.7706,lng:-82.649566,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'mad_c1',bio:'In 2010 she painted a 700-square-metre wall along the Berlin-Halle train line. It is likely the largest graffiti mural ever painted by a single person. She has broken her own record twice since.\n\nClaudia Walde — MadC — was born in 1980 in Bautzen, Germany and raised in Ethiopia. She studied at Burg Giebichenstein University of Art and Design, Central Saint Martins in London, and earned a master\'s in graphic design. Her style evolved from street lettering to transparent layered compositions in spray paint — including her own "Spectra" transparent paint.\n\nShe has painted a control tower at Punta Cana airport, a permanent installation at the Hessisches Landesmuseum Darmstadt, and glass works with Maki and Associates architects. Her work appeared on a Deutsche Post stamp (2023). She has published four books. Clients include the Dulwich Picture Gallery in London, Mural Arts Philadelphia, the City of Abu Dhabi, and Wynwood Walls. For SHINE 2022, she painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A multi-story abstract mural on the side of an apartment building. Sweeping translucent ribbons of hot pink, cyan, orange, and white crisscross at sharp angles over a vivid yellow background. Where the color bands overlap they create new hues — purple where pink meets blue, warm peach where orange meets white. The effect is like looking through stacked sheets of colored glass. White paint splatter near the top right adds a calligraphic flourish.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/madc.jpeg',from:'Germany',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Deutsche Post stamp (2023); Art in Architecture winner (Magdeburg, 2022); SHINE 2022',fw:[{name:'madc.tv',url:'https://madc.tv'}],sbio:'In 2010 she painted a 700-square-metre wall along the Berlin-Halle train line. It is likely the largest graffiti mural ever painted by a single person. She has broken her own record twice since.\n\nClaudia Walde — MadC, nicknamed "The Mad One" for her radical approach to graffiti — was born in 1980 in Bautzen, Lusatia, Germany and raised in Ethiopia. She painted her first graffiti piece in 1996 at age sixteen. She studied at Burg Giebichenstein University of Art and Design in Halle and at Central Saint Martins College in London, and holds a master\'s degree in graphic design. She is based in Berlin and has worked in over 35 countries.\n\nHer early reputation came from burner walls with dynamic wildstyle pieces placed in detailed sceneries — themes drawn from fantasy and science fiction, including dragons and dinosaurs. Her tagging style cites Vincent Van Gogh and the late graffiti artist Dare (Siegfried von Koeding), with whom she collaborated on his piece Basel. She dedicated her own piece "6313 — Here to Stay" to Dare.\n\nThe 700-Wall, painted in 2010 along the train line between Berlin and Halle, took four months to complete and is likely the largest graffiti mural created by a single person. She broke her own record in 2018 with the 1000-Wall in Chicago. Other large-scale walls include 500Wall in Leipzig (2013), the Chance Street Mural in Shoreditch, East London (2013), Le Mur Paris (2013), the Tahiti Mural (2014), and an 18-story mural in Jersey City (2019).\n\nAfter the 700-Wall, Walde shifted into abstract work characterized by transparent, glass-like layers and energetic calligraphic forms. The body of work appeared at Reflections at Kolly Gallery, Zurich (2014). She moved between street and studio practice, experimenting with Spectra transparent paint, ink, watercolor, acrylic paint, and acrylic markers on canvas, carton, and the cardboard of spray-paint boxes themselves. In 2022 she translated the visual language into NFTs with the generative project Color Rhythms, using machine learning to construct and title the compositions — published as a HENI Publishing book in 2023 (ISBN 978-1-912122-71-4).\n\nRecent commissioned work includes a permanent floor installation at the church of Saint-Martin in Limésy, France (completed 2025), painted to represent the souls who walked into the holy place; a floor-to-walls intervention at the Hessisches Landesmuseum Darmstadt, Germany (May 2024); and five permanent glass pieces at the Museum Reinhard Ernst in Germany, produced with Derix Glasstudios and Maki and Associates architects of Japan (2024). She painted a control tower at Punta Cana airport. Deutsche Post issued a stamp featuring her work in 2023. Since 2022 she has won Open Call competitions for artists in Germany and Spain. She also won the Art in Architecture commission to paint a bridge abutment in Magdeburg (Phase 1 finished 2022) and an Art in Public Space project at Schiller Gymnasium Königs Wusterhausen (August 2025).\n\nOther commissions include murals for Sinkka Museum in Finland, the Dulwich Picture Gallery in London, the Mural Arts Program in Philadelphia, the City of Abu Dhabi in the UAE, and Wynwood Walls. She participated in the Women\'s Forum Street Art Project, contributing "Little Red Riding Hood" (mixed media on canvas, 160 x 100 cm, 2014) representing Germany.\n\nSelected solo shows: Color Rhythms at HENI Gallery, London (2025); Thank God it\'s Friday at Cammann Studios, Chemnitz (2024); Returning Flow of Colors at GAOS Gallery Beijing (2023); Street to Canvas at HENI Gallery London (2021-2022); Goddess at GAOs Gallery, Taiyuan, China (2020); Inside/Outside at Brugier-Rigail, Paris (2019); Dialog at Brenners Park-Hotel, Baden-Baden (2019); Pink Power at Kolly Gallery, Zurich (2018); Rush Hour at Wallworks New York (2018); Southern Lights at Galerie Nicolas-Xavier, Montpellier (2018); Daydreaming at 44309 Gallery, Dortmund (2018); Home Sweet Home at Galerie Brugier-Rigail (2016); Kaleidoscope at Kolly Gallery, Zurich (2016); Bits and Pieces at Wallworks New York (2015); Night and Day at 44309 Gallery, Dortmund (2015); Character at Pure Evil Gallery, London (2015); MadABC at Pure Evil Gallery, London (2011); and Over the Edge at 1AM Gallery, San Francisco (2012).\n\nSelected group shows: Knotenpunkt at Affenfaust Galerie, Hamburg (2025); Field Day at Design Museum Chicago (2024); Wonderwalls at NRW-Forum, Düsseldorf (2022-2023); Next Wave at Fort Wayne Museum of Art (2022); Iconoclast at Miaja Gallery, Singapore (2019-2020); Conquête Urbaine at Musée des Beaux-Arts, Calais (2019); Calligraphy Now at the National Museum of Korea, Seoul (2018); Street Color at Institute Bernard Magrez, Bordeaux (2018); Searching for Surfaces at Vertical Gallery, Chicago (2017); For the Love of Freedom at Sinkka Museum, Kerava, Finland (2017); Urban Art Biennale at Völklinger Hütte (2017); Radius at Urban Nation, Berlin (2017); and the Marrakech Biennale (2016).\n\nBooks authored as Claudia Walde: Sticker City — Paper Graffiti Art (Thames & Hudson, 2007); Street Fonts — Graffiti Alphabets From Around The World (Thames & Hudson, 2011), featuring work from 154 artists across 30 countries collected over two years of travel; Mural XXL (Thames & Hudson, 2015); MadC: Street to Canvas, monograph by Luisa Heese (HENI London, 2021); MadC: Color Rhythms (HENI London, 2023). She is featured in the 2021 documentary "Wir und die Kunst" by Milan Skrobanek.\n\nFor SHINE 2022 she painted in St. Petersburg, Florida. Website: madc.tv.',src:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MadC','https://madc.tv/about/','https://heni.com/artists/mad-c','https://artsandculture.google.com/story/5wVBA7_LEwgA8A']},
  {id:66,a:'Marina Capdevila',t:'',loc:'601 1st Ave. N.',bldg:'The Sage',lat:27.77174,lng:-82.642365,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'marinacapdevila',bio:'Her grandmother is her muse. She paints the vitality of old age — beauty outside the usual aesthetic canons.\n\nMarina Capdevila holds a degree in art from the University of Barcelona and a postgraduate in artistic illustration from Eina School. She spent time at the Willem de Kooning Academy in Amsterdam. Her style uses powerful color with soft shading inspired by Renaissance sfumato technique, rendered through exaggeration and irony — elderly people rendered as vibrant, vital, alive.\n\nShe has painted in fifteen-plus countries. Festival credits include The Crystal Ship in Belgium, Vision Art Festival in Switzerland, and Wynwood Embassy in Miami. She won the Junceda Public Space Award (Spain, 2022) and the Pinzell Cuixart artistic career recognition (2019). For SHINE 2022, she painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A two-story wall dominated by a crowd of surreal orange-skinned characters in various states of mischief. On the left, a figure in a beret and sunglasses clutches a boombox. In the center, an enormous face wearing oversized reflective shades holds a lobster. On the right, a figure in a bucket hat dangles a fish on a line while a pale ghost-like creature peeks out between them. The palette is almost entirely warm oranges, creams, and golds against a yellow-gold upper band with tiny palm tree silhouettes. Signed "Marina Capdevila" at the upper left.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/marina-capdevila.jpeg',from:'Barcelona, Spain',aud:'https://cdn.muralquest.app/audio/66.mp3',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Junceda Public Space Award (2022); Pinzell Cuixart (2019); NH Awards Silver Medal (2017); SHINE 2022',fw:[{name:'marinacapdevila.com',url:'https://www.marinacapdevila.com'}],sbio:'Her grandmother is her muse. She paints the vitality of old age — beauty outside the usual aesthetic canons. Marina Capdevila was born in 1986. She is a Spanish visual artist based in Barcelona, working across painting, drawing, mural art, installation, and mixed media. Her practice centers on playful portraits of the elderly in candid, nostalgic, festive moments, using vibrant exaggerations to provoke the familiar and the humorous. Her work explores themes of identity, death, and the human experience, and uses her grandmother as muse to challenge conventions of beauty, gender, and age and to deconstruct negative stereotypes.\n\nShe holds a degree in art from the University of Barcelona and a postgraduate in artistic illustration from Eina School. She also spent time at the Willem de Kooning Academy in Amsterdam. Her style combines powerful color with soft shading inspired by Renaissance sfumato, rendered through exaggeration and irony — elderly people as vibrant, vital, alive. Beyond her studio practice, she participates in workshops and art education initiatives.\n\nHer murals can be found in Florida, New York, California, China, Brazil, Mexico, Canada, Belgium, Switzerland, Italy, Netherlands, United Kingdom, Austria, Spain, Portugal, and Croatia. She has exhibited original artworks in galleries in Los Angeles, San Francisco, Oakland, Denver, New York, Atlanta, London, Paris, Barcelona, and Madrid.\n\nAwards include the Junceda Public Space Award in Spain (2022), the Pinzell Cuixart artistic career recognition / Primer Premio in Barcelona, Spain (2019), and the Silver medal / Segundo Premio in the ÑH Awards, the Society for News Design competition of Latin America, Portugal and Spain, in Madrid (2017).\n\nStreet art festival credits include SHINE Festival in St. Pete, Florida, USA; Murart in Ontinyent, Valencia, Spain; Ourtowns in Basildon, UK; Borgo Universe in Aelli, Italy; Cidade de Cor in Vigo, Spain; Cura Festival in Belo Horizonte, Brazil; The Raw Project in Miami, Florida, USA; Paseo Pionero Festival in Cancun, Mexico; The Crystal Ship in Oostende, Belgium; Delasfest in Galicia, Spain; Vision Art Festival in Crans Montana, Switzerland; Vukovart in Vukovar, Croatia; Estau Festival in Estarreja, Portugal; Meeting of Street Art in Ciudad del Carmen, Mexico; Rexenera Fest in Galicia, Spain; Via Vai Project in Torre San Giovanni, Italy; Desvelarte in Santander, Spain; Wynwood Embassy in Miami, Florida, USA; Domina Domna in Bergamo, Italy; Open Walls in Barcelona, Spain; and Gar Gar in Penelles, Spain. For SHINE 2022, she painted in St. Petersburg.\n\nSolo exhibitions include Falta Gel at LAB36 Gallery / Galeria LAB36 in Barcelona, Spain (2023); Ready Steady Young, a duo show with Mico Rabuñal at Furiosa Gallery in Madrid, Spain (2020); Oldie But Goldie at Domina Domna Art Space in Bergamo, Italy (2017, also listed 2016); Never Too Late For A First Date at Miscelanea Gallery in Barcelona, Spain (2016, also listed 2015); and Smokers at Basis Artspace in Amsterdam, Netherlands (2016, also listed 2014).\n\nGroup exhibitions include Out Of Touch at Subliminal Projects in Los Angeles, California (2020); B-Local at B Murals Gallery in Barcelona, Spain (2020); Change Is A Team Sport at B Murals Gallery in Barcelona, Spain (2020); Galeria Balneario Group at The Source in Denver, Colorado (2019); Lately at First Amendment Gallery in San Francisco, California (2018); Murs Lliures at Montana Gallery in Barcelona, Spain (2019); 2nd Annual 4%Ers at Athen B Gallery in Oakland, California (2017); Impecable Bunch at Go Gallery in Amsterdam, Netherlands (2018); Happy at 19 Karen Gallery on the Gold Coast, Australia (2017); Ashurst Emerging Artist Space at Good Mother Gallery in Oakland, California (2017); Fresh & Fun at Plastic Murs Gallery in Valencia, Spain (2016); The Story of the Creative at See Gallery / See Exhibition Space in New York, USA (2016); WeArt at Transforma BCN in Barcelona, Spain (2016); Human at Wayco Artspace in Valencia, Spain (2016); London Illustration Fair at Hoxton Arches Gallery in London, UK (2016); Hoax at Choko Club in Barcelona, Spain; Barcelona Drawing City at Galeria Le Marais / Galerie Le Marais in Paris, France (2015); Gato por Liebre at Cadaver Exquisit in Barcelona, Spain; and Molecula at Mutuo Gallery in Barcelona, Spain.\n\nPress and documentary work includes La Pell dels Murs and the Viejas Glorias documentary, available on the TV3 website. Her website is marinacapdevila.com.',fn:['Her grandmother is her muse','Paints the vitality of older women','Barcelona-based illustrator'],src:['https://www.marinacapdevila.com/about/','https://lab36.org/collections/marina-capdevila','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2022_SHINE_Marina_Capdevila']},
  {id:68,a:'Reginald O\'Neal',t:'John Henrik Clarke',loc:'701 3rd St. S.',bldg:'USF Piano Man Building',lat:27.76296167,lng:-82.63767167,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'_reginaldoneal_',bio:'He paints his Overtown neighbors at oil-on-linen museum scale. On this wall, he scaled up historian John Henrik Clarke and the Mundari of South Sudan to fill the building.\n\nReginald O\'Neal began painting in 2012 under Alejandro Dorda, who trained him classically and introduced him to his other mentor, the Spanish-born graffiti artist Axel Void. He still works in oil on linen — figurative work depicting musicians, family, and community members from Miami\'s Overtown neighborhood. His subject matter shifted over time from broad political work to intimate portraits of the people closest to him. Artsy named him Vanguard 2021.\n\nHe has shown at the Rubell Museum ("American Vignettes," 2024), ICA Miami, The Bass, and Perez Art Museum Miami. His solo "Souled Out" opened at Vielmetter Los Angeles in 2024. He was nominated for the 2023 Florida Prize in Contemporary Art and holds residencies at Oolite Arts and Anderson Ranch.',desc:'A sweeping black-and-white mural spanning the full wall. A suited man — historian John Henrik Clarke — sits as the central figure, painted with photorealistic precision. Behind him, silhouetted scenes show Mundari tribespeople tending long-horned cattle and casting fishing nets. The monochromatic palette and classical brushwork give the piece the weight of a museum painting at building scale.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/reginald-oneal.jpeg',from:'Miami, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Artsy Vanguard 2021; Oolite Arts resident (2022-23); Anderson Ranch resident (2022); 2023 Florida Prize nominee; Perez Art Museum Miami collection; SHINE 2022',fw:null,sbio:'He is self-taught. His mentor taught him to study Joaquin Sorolla. Artsy named him Vanguard 2021.\n\nReginald O\'Neal — who also goes by L.E.O. — was born in Miami, Florida in 1992 and still lives and works there. He began painting in 2012 and soon met Alejandro Dorda, who became his friend and mentor and taught him classically. Dorda also introduced him to his other mentor, Spanish-born graffiti artist Axel Void. O\'Neal works in oil on linen, painting figurative pictures of musicians, family, and community members from Miami\'s Overtown neighborhood. His subject matter shifted over time from broad political work to intimate portraits of the people closest to him.\n\nIn 2014, L.E.O. took his first trip to Europe, completing murals in Austria, Norway, and Spain and exhibiting in a collective show alongside Dorda at Urban Art Clash in Berlin, Germany. Since then he has divided his practice between canvas work, residencies, and murals.\n\nSolo exhibitions include "Souled Out" at Vielmetter Los Angeles (September 14 — November 2, 2024, in the gallery\'s Greenhouse space) and "Forever Fleeing" at the Festival del Arte Contemporáneo de Cádiz in Cádiz, Spain in 2024. In 2023 he showed "And I Think to Myself" at Spinello Projects in Miami and "The Cellist" at Meridians, Art Basel Miami Beach. In 2021 he had "They Dreamt of Us" at Spinello Projects and "As I Am" at the Rubell Museum in Miami. Earlier solos include "At The Feet of Mountain" at Spinello Projects in 2020 and "Sun Beneath The Soil" at L.E.O. in Portland, Oregon in 2019.\n\nGroup shows include "Mirror of the Mind: Figuration in the Jorge M. Perez Collection" at El Espacio 23 in Miami (2024), "American Vignettes: Symbols, Society, and Satire" at the Rubell Museum (2024), "To Be as a Cloud: Recent Acquisitions" at NSU Art Museum Fort Lauderdale, "A Landscape Longed For: The Garden As Disturbance" at the Crisp-Ellert Art Museum in St. Augustine, and "Selections of the Marquez Family Collection" at Marquez Art Projects in Miami. In 2023 he was included in the "2023 Florida Prize in Contemporary Art" at the Orlando Museum of Art, "Gimme Shelter" at the Historic Hampton House in Miami, and "It Was Always About You..." at Oolite Arts. Other group shows include "By the Skin of Your Teeth" at NOVA Art Basel Miami Beach (2022), "Open Storage" at The Bass (2022), "Lean-To" at Oolite Arts (2022), "Selections from Permanent Collection" at ICA Miami (2021), "Grounded" at Spinello Projects (2019), "Homeless" at Void Projects (2018 and 2019), "South Florida Cultural Consortium" (2019), "Collabo 6" (2019), "Afterglow" at The Frank in Pembroke Pines (2018), Laundromat 2.0 in Miami (2018), Youth Concept Gallery in West Palm Beach (2017), Street Art For Mankind in Miami (2017), Profiles Exhibition in Hollywood, Florida (2016), and the Artesano collective show in Miami (2014).\n\nResidencies include the Festival del Arte Contemporáneo de Cádiz in Spain (2024), Anderson Ranch Arts Center in Colorado (2022), Atlantic Center for the Arts in New Smyrna Beach, Florida (2020), the Forest for the Trees Curated Field Trip in Tokyo, Japan (2019), Bed-Stuy Art Residency in Brooklyn (2018), Creença in Catalonia, Spain (2018), and Konvant Art Residency in Catalonia (2017). He was a Studio Resident at Oolite Arts in 2022 and 2023, and a Home + Away Resident through Oolite at Anderson Ranch (2022) and Atlantic Center for the Arts (2020).\n\nMural festival credits include In Wall We Trust Mural Festival in Airola, Italy (2019), Maya Angelou Mural Festival in Los Angeles (2019), Forest For The Trees in Seattle (2019), San Isidro Mural Festival in Havana, Cuba (2019), the Emancipation Exhibition in Marseille, France (2018), Transfert & Co in Nantes, France (2018), City Year MLK Day of Service in Miami (2018), the Shine Street Art Festival in St. Petersburg, Florida (2017), Slash and Burn in Sumatra, Indonesia (2017), Artesano Street Art Festival in San Pedro, Dominican Republic (2016), Urban Art Experience in Miami (2015), Artesano Street Art Festival in Nagua, Dominican Republic (2015), 352 Walls in Gainesville, Florida (2015), Artesano Street Art Festival in Rio San Juan, Dominican Republic (2014), and Desordes Creativas in Galicia, Spain (2014).\n\nHis SHINE 2022 mural depicted historian John Henrik Clarke overlaid on the Mundari tribe of South Sudan. Recent paintings shown at Vielmetter include "Head of the Sax Player" (oil on linen, 20" x 16"), "Trio" (62" x 52"), "French hornist" (42" x 34"), "Sax and Drummer" (60" x 48"), and "Lost at Sea" (60" x 90"), all 2024 and photographed by Zachary Balber. His work is held in the public collection of the Perez Art Museum Miami.\n\nPress coverage includes Douglas Markowitz, "Reginald O\'Neal, Cara Despain, and Other Artists Nominated for 2023 Florida Prize," Miami New Times, May 31, 2023; Torey Akers, "Miami artist billboard project puts justice in the spotlight," The Art Newspaper, November 28, 2022; Isis Davis-Marks, "The Artsy Vanguard 2021: Reginald O\'Neal," Artsy, December 1, 2021; Carolina del Busto, "Reginald O\'Neal Searches for Inspiration From His Family and Overtown Neighborhood," Miami New Times, October 1, 2021; Angelica Villa, "At the Rubell Museum, Reginald O\'Neal Pictures Miami\'s Overtown Neighborhood," ArtNews, November 29, 2021; and Carl Juste, "Local artist Reginald O\'Neal is emerging as an art star painting his community," Miami Herald, November 25, 2021. He is represented by Vielmetter Los Angeles and Spinello Projects.',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/reginald-o-neal-2022','https://www.artsy.net/article/artsy-editorial-artsy-vanguard-2021-reginald-oneal','https://vielmetter.com/artists/reginald-oneal/','https://vielmetter.com/artists/reginald-oneal/','https://www.artsy.net/article/artsy-editorial-artsy-vanguard-2021-reginald-oneal','https://oolitearts.org/resident/reginald-oneal/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/reginald-o-neal-2022','https://instagram.com/_reginaldoneal_']},
  {id:69,a:'Sydney Prusso',t:'Go Outside, Look Inside',loc:'533 28th St. S.',bldg:'Seriously Strong Training',lat:27.765762,lng:-82.671425,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'sydneyprusso',bio:'This wall looks like a vintage concert poster blown up to building scale. The three words on it — Go Outside, Look Inside — are her whole practice.\n\nSydney Prusso is an illustrator, lettering artist, and muralist based in the Tampa Bay area. Her style is contemporary botanical — flat illustration rooted in graphic design, warm 1960s and 70s color palettes, flowing poster lettering. Nature, the outdoors, and mental health run through the work. She graduated from MICA and has painted fifty-plus murals across six states, with installations up to 75 feet. She has done a Reef Organization project in the Florida Keys.\n\nClients include Salesforce, Adobe, Dickies, PepsiCo, John Deere, Stihl, the NFL, and AstraZeneca. For SHINE 2022, she painted two murals in St. Petersburg.',desc:'The entire side of a peaked-roof building wrapped in retro psychedelic lettering spelling "Outside" and "Look Inside" in chunky, rounded \'70s-style typography. The letters are layered in pinks, oranges, chartreuse, white, and maroon against a purple background. Colorful butterflies — a large pink-and-red one at center, smaller ones floating above — are scattered between the words. Sparkle stars dot the upper peak. The overall vibe is a vintage concert poster blown up to building scale.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/sydney-prusso.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2022 selected artist',fw:[{name:'sydbloom.com',url:'https://sydbloom.com'}],sbio:'She graduated from MICA and has painted fifty-plus murals across six states, installations up to 75 feet.\n\nSydney Prusso is an illustrator, lettering artist, and muralist based in the Tampa Bay area. Her style is contemporary botanical — flat illustration rooted in graphic design, warm 1960s and 70s color palettes, flowing poster lettering. Nature, the outdoors, and mental health run through the work. She has done a Reef Organization project in the Florida Keys.\n\nClients include Salesforce, Adobe, Dickies, PepsiCo, John Deere, Stihl, the NFL, and AstraZeneca. For SHINE 2022, she painted two murals in St. Petersburg.\n\nHer studio operates under the brand Syd Bloom Studio (sydneyprusso.com / sydbloom.com). Named brand projects on her site include an Apothecary mural and tea package design, a Grow Financial multi-store mural installation (including the West River, Tampa location), a Vintage Furniture Store mural and branding package, and a Fresh Kitchen multi-store installation. Other commercial clients include Garage Project (the brewery). She also takes residential commissions and out-of-state projects — testimonials reference a short-term rental mural in Port Angeles, Washington. Her illustration practice spans branded packaging, advertising, publishing, surface patterns, and textiles. Studio email: studio@sydbloom.com. Direct: sydney@sydneyprusso.com.',src:['https://sydbloom.com/about','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/sydney-prusso-2022','https://sydneyprusso.com','https://sydneyprusso.com/about','https://sydneyprusso.com/brand-collabs','https://sydneyprusso.com/illustration']},
  {id:70,a:'Tasko',t:'',loc:'1960 5th Ave S.',bldg:'Kozuba & Sons',lat:27.766272,lng:-82.66027,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'the_one_of_many_',bio:'A friend handed him a spray can while skating. He has been writing for fifteen years.\n\nJake Jacquillard — Tasko — is a Tampa Bay graffiti writer and wildstyle specialist. Self-taught, no formal education. His work is "loud, colorful and in your face" — flashy lettering in spray paint with fifteen-plus years of practice. He wants people to see graffiti as art, not vandalism.\n\nHe organized "Letter Headz," a writer event in Pinellas. For SHINE 2018, he was selected as an Open Call artist and painted "St Pete" in 3D graffiti letters. He returned for SHINE 2022 with "The Collective."',desc:'A massive wildstyle graffiti piece spanning a long dark wall. Bold, interlocking letterforms fill the composition in a rainbow of colors — deep blue and purple on the left, transitioning through sky blue, burnt orange and red in the center, to mint green, magenta, and gold on the right. The letters are sharp-edged and angular with complex overlapping forms, arrows, and three-dimensional shading. White highlights and pale smoke-like wisps rise from the tops of the letters against the dark navy-black background. Scattered bubbles and organic shapes float in the upper portion. The overall effect is explosive and kinetic.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/tasko.jpeg',from:'Tampa Bay, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2018 Open Call; SHINE 2022 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'A friend handed him a spray can while skating. He has been writing for fifteen years.\n\nJake Jacquillard — Tasko — is a Tampa Bay graffiti writer and wildstyle specialist. He picked the name as a kid, starting with "Task" and adding the O after an old homie suggested adding a number when he learned how popular "Task" already was. He did not grow up in an area with much graffiti. He went skating with friends one day, his buddy had a can of paint, and he did "this bubble letter crap" — then started tagging his name everywhere he went. A few years later he saw a wall full of burners and was hooked. He is self-taught, with no formal education in art. He describes his work as "loud, colorful and in your face" — flashy lettering in spray paint with fifteen-plus years of practice. He wants people to see graffiti as art, not vandalism.\n\nHe runs "Letter Headz," a writer event in Pinellas. The first edition was a one-time idea — a show of alphabets from different writers, named after the better of the contenders he had on the table. It had a decent turnout and everyone had fun, so he ran "Letter Headz 2.0" on September 23-24 across two nights at Donnelly Cove Park in Pinellas Park, Florida, with 30 different artists\' work on display. The 2.0 theme was open to whatever the artists wanted to rock, but it had to be done on a street sign. Contact for the event is letterheadzfl@gmail.com. He has said he is not yet calling Letter Headz a festival, but he would be lying if he said that is not where he wants it in a few years. He has received support from the local community in running it.\n\nFor SHINE Mural Festival 2018, he was selected as one of five "Open Call" artists — winners of a St. Petersburg Arts Alliance search for the city\'s finest "unknown" mural artists. Each of the five was assigned a section of the rear wall of an industrial strip at 4139 7th Terrace South. The other Open Call artists that year were California-born Michael Vahl (best known for a lifelike-but-eerie Salvador Dali on Baum Avenue, painted via projection and a combination of spray and drybrush oil), Georgia native Sarah Page (a female astronaut adrift in the universe), St. Pete native Justin Wagher (a Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School graduate, painting a shadowy grey-and-black cityscape of telephone poles and connecting wires), and Philadelphia native and University of Tampa Fine Arts graduate Melanie Posner (working in photo-realistic women\'s faces with house paint and brushes only). Tasko\'s contribution was "St Pete" rendered in 3D graffiti letters. He returned to SHINE in 2022 with a piece titled "The Collective."\n\nHe sees both St. Pete and Tampa as having strong art scenes with extremely talented artists, and credits both with leaning toward the street art and mural side of graffiti, with Tampa coming across as grittier on the street stuff. He has said the Tampa Bay scene is no Miami or LA but is doing its thing, with new names appearing every week. He says inspiration comes from wanting to paint a better piece than the last one rather than from any specific city. He is part of the crews DvK and MiB, both of which he has publicly shouted out. He has named Estma as a local writer to watch, citing Estma as one of the few going through the proper phases — handstyles to throws to straights to burners — and has said too many kids skip the most important part of graffiti and try to jump straight to doing pieces, with the lack of foundation showing in their handstyle.\n\nHis goals as stated in his MRCNN Hip Hop interview are to keep painting as much as he can and to progress in the process. Coverage of him includes the St. Pete Catalyst\'s "SHINE up close: Meet the Open Call artists" by Bill DeYoung (October 9, 2018) and the MRCNN Hip Hop interview "Tampa Bay Writer TASKO" by L. Reed.\n\nInstagram: @the_one_of_many_ (current working handle, confirmed by Rob — supersedes the @tasko handle listed on the MRCNN contact card). Letter Headz Instagram is also active under the Letter Headz handle.',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/tasko-2022','https://stpetecatalyst.com/shine-up-close-meet-the-open-call-artists/','https://www.mrcnnlive.com/tampa-bay-area-writer-tasko-spraypaint-artists/','https://www.instagram.com/the_one_of_many_/']},
  {id:71,a:'The Happy Mural Project',t:'Wild Ride',loc:'3001 Central Ave.',bldg:'St. Petersbark',lat:27.771142,lng:-82.673944,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'alyssa.marie.of.the.sea',bio:'Their motto is "Honk if You\'re Happy." Their first mural was painted the week the world shut down.\n\nAlyssa Marie and Braden Everly are The Happy Mural Project — a married couple who relocated from Colorado to St. Petersburg in 2017. Alyssa is a fine artist who opened a gallery on Beach Drive in 2018 (closed January 2020). Braden has a degree in special education and worked as a house painter in Colorado. Their first mural went up at Lolita\'s Catering in early March 2020, days before lockdown.\n\nTheir signature is oversized sunflower murals in vibrant color, plus community-participatory "color by number" walls. Florida wildlife — manatees, gators, birds — runs through a lot of the work. They were commissioned by the Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation for a mural celebrating the state\'s ecological connectivity efforts.\n\nThey are three-time SHINE artists. For SHINE 2022 they painted "Wild Ride" at St. Petersbark on Central Avenue — a full-wall American alligator in rich browns against a field of golden sunflowers and teal sky.',desc:'A full-wall painting of an American alligator in profile, stretching from snout to tail across the entire building. The gator is rendered in rich browns, tans, and dark chocolate with detailed scale texturing throughout. Behind it, oversized golden sunflowers on green stems fill the background against a teal sky. The composition makes the gator feel massive — its head alone takes up about a third of the wall. The color contrast between the warm gator tones and the cool teal background gives it a Florida postcard quality.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/the-happy-mural-project.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation commission; SHINE 2020, 2022, 2023 selected artist',fw:[{name:'thehappymuralproject.com',url:'https://www.thehappymuralproject.com'}],sbio:'Their motto is "Honk if You\'re Happy." Their first mural was painted the week the world shut down.\n\nAlyssa Marie and Braden Everly are The Happy Mural Project — a married couple who relocated from Colorado to St. Petersburg in 2017. Alyssa is a fine artist who opened a gallery on Beach Drive in 2018 (closed January 2020). Braden has a degree in special education and worked as a house painter in Colorado. Their first mural went up at Lolita\'s Catering in early March 2020, days before lockdown.\n\nTheir signature is oversized sunflower murals in vibrant color, plus community-participatory "color by number" walls. Florida wildlife — manatees, gators, birds — runs through a lot of the work. They were commissioned by the Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation for a mural celebrating the state\'s ecological connectivity efforts.\n\nThey are three-time SHINE artists (2020, 2022, 2023). For SHINE 2022 they painted "Wild Ride" at St. Petersbark on Central Avenue — a full-wall American alligator in rich browns against a field of golden sunflowers and teal sky. For SHINE 2020 they painted "Hands of Change" at the St. Petersburg City Theatre — hands holding sunflowers representing racial and cultural diversity. For SHINE 2023 they painted "Girl Power," a color-by-number mural completed by Girl Scouts of West Central Florida.',fn:['\'Honk if You\'re Happy\' is their motto','First mural went up the week the world shut down','Community color-by-number murals'],src:['https://www.thehappymuralproject.com/','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/the-happy-mural-project/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/happy-mural-project-2023','https://www.abcactionnews.com/news/region-pinellas/happy-mural-project-near-st-petersburg-high-is-drawing-smiles-from-social-distancers','https://floridawildlifecorridor.org/']},
  {id:72,a:'Van Der Luc',t:'Tank School',loc:'602 24th St S',bldg:'Allums Auto',lat:27.763846,lng:-82.66551,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'van_der_luc_',bio:'Polar bears hauling robots through sunny landscapes. Drones delivering art supplies. Bird skeletons wandering past boxes of honey. This is how he talks about the environment.\n\nLucas Franco — Van Der Luc — is a self-taught painter and multimedia artist from Bogota, Colombia, now based in St. Petersburg. He works out of Studios at 5663 in Pinellas Park\'s Pinellas Arts Village. His subjects are big — climate, nature, geopolitics, technology — but his delivery is whimsical and surrealist, playful rather than preachy.\n\nHe has shown in group exhibitions and museum-curated shows across the Tampa Bay region. In 2019 he was selected as an emerging artist for SHINE\'s REVEAL: EMERGE live painting event at Station House. By 2022 he was a full SHINE mural artist.\n\nFor SHINE 2022 he painted "Tank School" on the side of Allums Auto in the Warehouse Arts District — a white polar bear striding across a green landscape with a robot on its back, an umbrella studded with stars, and a drone delivering art supplies in the corner.',desc:'A large whimsical mural on a bright blue sky background covering the full side of Allums Auto. A massive white polar bear strides across a green grassy landscape, carrying a robot passenger who holds a black umbrella studded with stars. The robot sits atop the bear with a first-aid kit satchel, a potted plant, and a box labeled "Honey" strapped to the bear\'s back. A skeletal bird — just bones and a long beak — perches behind the robot. A swarm of bees buzzes on the right side, and in the upper left, a drone delivers a cardboard box labeled "Art Supplies." Power lines and distant utility poles dot the horizon. Signed "VAN DER LUC" in angular hand-lettered type in the upper right corner. The piece blends environmental and technological themes with a storybook charm.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/van-der-luc.jpeg',from:'Bogota, Colombia',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2019 REVEAL:EMERGE emerging artist; SHINE 2022 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'Polar bears hauling robots through sunny landscapes. Drones delivering art supplies. Bird skeletons wandering past boxes of honey. This is how he talks about the environment.\n\nLucas Franco — Van Der Luc — is a self-taught painter and multimedia artist from Bogota, Colombia, now based in St. Petersburg. He works out of Studios at 5663 in Pinellas Park\'s Pinellas Arts Village. His subjects are big — climate, nature, geopolitics, technology — but his delivery is whimsical and surrealist, playful rather than preachy.\n\nHe has shown in group exhibitions and museum-curated shows across the Tampa Bay region. In 2019 he was selected as an emerging artist for SHINE\'s REVEAL: EMERGE live painting event at Station House. By 2022 he was a full SHINE mural artist.\n\nFor SHINE 2022 he painted "Tank School" on the side of Allums Auto in the Warehouse Arts District — a white polar bear striding across a green landscape with a robot on its back, an umbrella studded with stars, and a drone delivering art supplies in the corner. Instagram: @van_der_luc_.',fn:['Polar bears hauling robots through sunny landscapes','Belgian artist, surrealist imagery','Juxtaposes nature and technology'],src:['https://www.instagram.com/van_der_luc_/','https://stpeterising.com/home/2022/9/12/artist-lineup-revealed-for-st-petes-eighth-annual-shine-mural-festival','https://www.tampabay.com/life-culture/arts/visual-arts/2022/10/11/shine-mural-festival-returns-st-petersburg/','https://www.graffitistreet.com/shine-st-petersburg-street-art-mural-festival-florida-2022/','https://studios5663.com/']},
  {id:73,a:'Vitale Bros',t:'',loc:'694 Arlington Ave N',bldg:'Parc',lat:27.772527,lng:-82.643546,y:2022,cat:'shine',ig:'vitalebros',bio:'Their first mural came from a sketch on a paper napkin, handed to a restaurant owner. That was 1992.\n\nJohnny Vitale and his brother Paul started Vitale Bros in Tampa Bay hand-lettering signs and painting storefront windows. Their parents are artists. So were their grandparents. No formal training — just family tradition and thirty-plus years of practice. Their late aunt Nancy, who had Down syndrome and attended Parc Center for Disabilities, turned out to be an artist too, discovered in her final decade.\n\nIn the late 1990s they opened a warehouse studio that hosted group art, music, and fashion shows that helped birth St. Pete\'s emerging art scene. They collaborated on set design for Harmony Korine\'s Spring Breakers. They have painted for the Tampa Bay Rays, the Buccaneers, L\'Oreal, and dozens of local businesses. They call themselves the "mural police" — restoring damaged walls around the city for free.\n\nIn 2023, Johnny launched Overspray, a street art shop and graffiti school now based in Gulfport, teaching tagging, block letters, Wildstyle, and advanced mural technique to classes of up to twenty students. After Hurricane Milton, they painted free signs for businesses that lost theirs.\n\nThey have painted SHINE every year since its inception — "The Cool Kids" in 2015, "Bait and Switch" in 2019, and multiple walls since. For SHINE Origins 2025, Johnny painted two solo murals: one on the 600 block where it all began, and another at The Factory on Fairfield Ave South with his daughter Sionna.',desc:'A row of SpongeBob SquarePants characters fills the wall in the Vitale Brothers\' bold, graphic mural style. The familiar cartoon figures are painted at building scale with clean outlines and flat, saturated colors that pop against the building exterior. It\'s become one of the more popular photo-op walls in the area.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2022/vitale-bros.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'https://cdn.muralquest.app/audio/73.mp3',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE festival artist every year since inception; set design for Spring Breakers (Harmony Korine)',fw:[{name:'vitalebrothers.com',url:'https://vitalebrothers.com'}],sbio:'Their first mural came from a sketch on a paper napkin, handed to a restaurant owner. That was 1992.\n\nJohnny Vitale and his brother Paul founded Vitale Bros in 1992 in Tampa Bay, starting with hand-lettered signs and painted storefront windows. Youngest brother Joey later joined. Their parents are artists — the family artistic tradition goes back to their grandparents. No formal art education has been documented; the craft appears entirely self-taught and family-transmitted. Their late aunt Nancy had Down syndrome and attended Parc Center for Disabilities, where staff discovered her artistic talent in her final decade of life.\n\nIn the late 1990s and early 2000s, they opened a warehouse studio that hosted legendary group art, music and fashion shows credited with helping birth St. Pete\'s emerging art scene. They collaborated in set design for Harmony Korine\'s Spring Breakers (filmed in the St. Pete area). Their style blends pop art, graffiti, and traditional mural techniques — fine art meets pop art sensibility, incorporating local culture, historical elements, and visual stories reflecting community identity.\n\nClients include the Tampa Bay Rays, Tampa Bay Buccaneers, L\'Oreal / SalonCentric (national locations), Anchor Skate Supply, and Great Bay Distributors, plus numerous local businesses and private residences. Notable murals include the Marilyn Monroe on the side of the historic Playhouse Theater on Central Ave, a Virgin Mary at St. Pete Catholic High School, Power to the Patients giant fist at the Morean Arts Center, and the Nueva Cantina restaurant mural in Tampa. They describe themselves as the mural police — restoring damaged murals around the city for free.\n\nThe current Vitale Bros team includes Johnny, Paul, Nick and Jason Kekllas, Bruce Statton, and Jake Gerow. They have also collaborated under the name Up and Over with Tes One, and painted behind the State Theatre with Tes One and Palehorse.\n\nIn July 2023, Johnny launched Overspray — a retail street art shop and graffiti school, initially on the Pinellas Trail, relocated to 5013 20th Ave S. in Gulfport. Overspray teaches graffiti, tagging, block and bubble letters, Wildstyle, and advanced mural classes to groups of up to 20 students. The five-artist teaching team includes John Vitale, Nicole Salgar, Hinson Thehealer, Digital (LA), and Jujmo. After Hurricane Milton, they painted free signs for businesses that lost theirs. They host events like Hot Walls during SHINE.\n\nSHINE participation: The Cool Kids (2015, 2063 Central Ave — faces of the artists\' children), Bait and Switch (2019 — red snapper and sea turtle), SHINE 2022, SHINE 2024 (mural at Anchor Skate Supply with pro skateboarder Kris Markovich, inspired by Rays City Connect uniforms; limited-edition skateboard L\'Orange). For SHINE Origins 2025, Johnny painted two solo walls: 600 1st Ave N (the 600 block where SHINE began) and The Factory at 2606 Fairfield Ave S (collaboration with Sionna Sio Macleish, celebrating St. Pete history and landmarks).\n\nStudio: 5013 20th Ave S., Gulfport, FL. Website: vitalebrothers.com. Instagram: @johnvitale (solo), @vitalebros (collective).',fn:['Vitale Bros — painting St. Pete since 1992','No formal training — family tradition','Helped birth St. Pete\'s art scene in the \'90s'],src:['https://vitalebrothers.com/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/vitale-bros-and-shine-this-time-its-personal/','https://monochronicle.com/artist/vitale-brothers/','https://vitalebrothers.com/about/','https://vitalebrothers.com/transforming-st-pete-the-artistic-impact-of-the-vitale-brothers/','https://ilovetheburg.com/overspray-mural-art-gulfport/','https://thegabber.com/vitale-brothers-birds-and-bees-in-gulfport/','https://thegabber.com/overspray-art-space-paints-signs-for-businesses-post-milton/','https://www.fox13news.com/news/vitale-brothers-paint-the-town-with-murals','https://www.paradisenewsfl.com/artists-spotlight-nov-2025/']},
  {id:136,a:'Ya La\'Ford',t:'',loc:'569 22nd St S',bldg:'Power Moves Electric',lat:27.765208,lng:-82.663275,y:2022,cat:'commercial',ig:'yalaford',bio:'She has an MFA and a law degree. She represented the United States at the Dakar Biennale in Senegal.\n\nYa La\'Ford is a first-generation American of Jamaican heritage, from the Bronx. She earned her MFA from the Art Institute of Boston (now Lesley University) and her Juris Doctor from the University of Florida. She maintains studios in St. Petersburg, the Bronx, Jamaica, and Maine.\n\nHer work spans painting, sculpture, sound, print, video, and immersive installations using light, water, sound, and motion. Sharp geometric lines, bold contrasting colors, vibrant circles and triangles. She sits on the Board of Trustees of the Museum of Fine Arts in St. Petersburg. She designed an Arrow McLaren SP IndyCar and was a featured artist for Super Bowl 2021.\n\nClients include the Tampa Bay Rays, Toronto Blue Jays, and Nike. Her work is in the collections of Tropicana Field, Al Lang Stadium, the Morean Arts Center, and the Boston Public Library. She has shown at The James Museum, The Ringling Museum of Art, and Florida CraftArt.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, she painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A mural covering the front facade of the Power Moves Electric warehouse on 22nd Street South. On a solid black cinder-block wall, three geometric compositions are arranged symmetrically: two matching half-circle forms flanking the sides and a full circle centered above a metal door. Each form is filled with angular, crystalline white linework — interlocking straight-line segments that create a stained-glass or shattered-ice pattern. Subtle accents of pastel blue, pink, and warm gold highlight select segments within the white network. The black background gives the piece a nighttime quality, making the white geometry glow. "Ya La\'ford" is signed in white script in the lower right corner. The style is consistent with Ya La\'Ford\'s broader body of work exploring sacred geometry and light refraction.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/ya-la-ford-power-moves.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'U.S. representative, Dakar Biennale Senegal (2024); Super Bowl 2021 featured artist; Arrow McLaren IndyCar designer; Board of Trustees, Museum of Fine Arts St. Petersburg; SHINE 2021',fw:[{name:'yalaford.com',url:'https://www.yalaford.com'}],sbio:'She has an MFA and a law degree. She represented the United States at the Dakar Biennale in Senegal.\n\nYa La\'Ford is a first-generation American of Jamaican heritage, originally from the Bronx. She earned her MFA from the Art Institute of Boston (now Lesley University) and her Juris Doctor from the University of Florida. She has lived and worked in Washington, D.C., Houston, South Florida, and Boston, and has been based in the Tampa Bay area for nearly a decade. She lives in St. Petersburg\'s Historic Kenwood neighborhood and maintains studio space in the Bronx, in Jamaica, and on a property in Maine that overlooks Canada, where she is building her largest immersive public artwork on more than 30 acres. Her grandfather was an influential figure in the arts, her uncle is a musician with the band Third World, and her godfather wove cultural storytelling into his music — family influences she credits as the foundation of her artistic path.\n\nHer work spans painting, sculpture, sound, print, video, and immersive installations using light, water, sound, and motion. Her signature visual vocabulary is sharp geometric lines, bold contrasting colors, and recurring circles, triangles, and spirals — symbols she uses to represent unity, change, and growth. She has described doors and entryways as the path she keeps returning to, and many of her St. Petersburg installations sit at the entrances of public and private buildings: the Hyatt entrance, the back entrance of the Morean Arts Center, the entryway of the Tampa Bay Rowdies, the Tampa Bay Rays entryway at Tropicana Field, the inside of Nike, the entryway of the James Weldon Johnson Public Library, and the entryway of the Warehouse Arts District. Her 2016 mural "The King\'s Dream Unite" is on display at the Historic Manhattan Casino in St. Petersburg. "Intersections" wraps the second level and rooftop terrace of the St. Petersburg Museum of History.\n\nHer larger installations and public artworks include "The Silver Lining," which debuted at the London Art Fair as an immersive interplay of light, water, sound, and motion; "Boulevard Flow," a sculptural landscape installation featuring a labyrinth-shaped park and a 10-foot metal sphere; "The Impenetrable Intersection: Jamaican Ja Rasta Visible L\'Invisible," a three-wall installation at the Atlantic Center for the Arts incorporating spray paint, acrylic, gold leaf, and found objects; "Survey: The West," part of a 2022 installation at the Ogden Contemporary Arts; and "Survey the West: A Cross-Continental Reflection," her largest solo exhibition to date, mounted at The James Museum of Western & Wildlife Art in St. Petersburg through May 18, 2025. Her "American/Rōōts" mixed-media installation showed at The John and Mable Ringling Museum of Art. She has exhibited at the Baker Museum in Naples; Gallery 221 at Hillsborough Community College, where she closed the immersive solo show "Distance"; and held an exhibition in Dubai. Large-scale public art sculptures and installations sit in Orlando, Gainesville, Pompano Beach, Hawaii, and New Jersey, with additional projects underway in South Africa.\n\nShe designed an Arrow McLaren SP IndyCar and was a featured artist at the 2021 Super Bowl, with work distributed to all NFL Owners as part of the Super Bowl collection. Brand and team clients include the Tampa Bay Rays, the Toronto Blue Jays (Toronto Blue Jays 2 Installation), and Nike. Her work is in the collections of Tropicana Field, Al Lang Stadium, the Morean Arts Center, and the Boston Public Library. Local exhibition venues include The James Museum, The Ringling Museum of Art, and Florida CraftArt. She has worked with children in Cuba, traveled for projects in Thailand and China, and was scheduled for an artist residency with the Urban League in Ghana.\n\nShe sits on the Board of Trustees of the Museum of Fine Arts in St. Petersburg. She is married, a mother of two, and a pet parent. For SHINE 2021, she painted in St. Petersburg.\n\nWebsite: yalaford.com. Email: ya@yalaford.com (general), contact@yalaford.com (press).',src:['https://www.yalaford.com/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/local-ish-meet-artist-ya-laford/','https://tampamagazines.com/ya-lafords/','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/arts-in-ya-laford/','https://lesley.edu/stories/ya-laford']},
  {id:34,a:'Zulu Painter',t:'',loc:'Arlington Ave N',bldg:'CASA Family Justice Center',lat:27.772442,lng:-82.649044,y:2022,cat:'commercial',ig:'zulupainter1',bio:'He calls them "Mindful Murals." He wants to grow art everywhere.\n\nCarlos Culbertson — Zulu Painter — is a native West Virginian who started pursuing art at age eight. He holds a degree in fine arts painting and now works out of a studio at the ArtsXchange on 22nd Street South. He paints in acrylic and spray paint, favors reclaimed materials, and has worked as a scenic artist for Disney on Ice, the Science Channel, HSN, and SeaWorld Parks and Entertainment.\n\nHis mural projects span the country: Denver, Chattanooga, Atlanta, Austin, Miami, Las Vegas, Los Angeles, Kansas City, Flint, and Binghamton, New York — where he painted "Cultivate Kindness," a 40-foot mural with West Side Middle School students. In St. Pete, he painted murals at CASA Family Justice Center on Arlington Ave (domestic violence survivor themes) and a portrait of Betty Reid Soskin at the Florida Botanical Gardens in Largo.\n\nHe is a four-time SHINE artist — "Moon Beam Dream 2" in 2016, "Legacy" with Thirst in 2017, "Wisdom, Growth, Progress" in 2018, and the AMIKids wall in 2024. He received a St. Pete Arts Alliance Muse Award in 2022. He also collaborated with Reid Jenkins and Iboms on "Imagine Gas Plant and Laurel Park" at Campbell Park Recreational Center.',desc:'Four diverse faces fill the two-story CASA Family Justice Center building, representing different demographics affected by domestic violence. On one side, a woman cradles a baby, with words embedded in the curls of her hair — "Drive, Bravery, Family, Growth, Hope, Empathy, Love, Community, Wisdom, Faith, Create, and Unity." The other side depicts a young woman extending a helping hand and a young man making direct eye contact. Colorful geometric patterns, blue polka dots, swirling vines, and flowers in purples and golds surround the figures. A pathway of white polka dots and purple triangles crosses Arlington Avenue as part of the design.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2024/zulu-painter.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'As Zulu Painter described the faces: "They\'re everyone... people from our community who have in some way been affected by family turmoil."',maw:'',aaw:'St. Pete Arts Alliance Muse Award (2022); SHINE 2016, 2017, 2018, 2024 selected artist; Colorado Black Arts Festival 2018 featured artist',fw:[{name:'zulupainter.com',url:'https://www.zulupainter.com'}],sbio:'He calls them "Mindful Murals." He wants to grow art everywhere.\n\nCarlos Culbertson — Zulu Painter — is a native West Virginian who started pursuing art at age eight. He holds a degree in fine arts painting and works out of a studio at the ArtsXchange in St. Petersburg, Florida — 515 22nd St. S, Studio 111, St. Petersburg, FL 33712, open Monday through Friday 9 a.m. to 6 p.m. He paints in acrylic and spray paint, favors reclaimed materials, and works in special effects and scenic art alongside his mural practice. His scenic-art clients include Disney on Ice, the Science Channel, HSN, and SeaWorld Parks and Entertainment.\n\nHis mural projects span the country: Denver, Chattanooga, Atlanta, Austin, Miami, Las Vegas, Los Angeles, Kansas City, Flint, and Binghamton, New York. In Binghamton he painted "Cultivate Kindness," a 40-foot mural completed with West Side Middle School students, and "Spread Love," approximately 40 feet tall on the M&T Bank building at 68 Exchange Street, Binghamton, NY 13901. "Spread Love" was painted with Golden Paintworks Mural & Theme Paint and Kobra spray paint, and was recognized by Broome County executive Brian Garner with a certificate of recognition. The mural depicts the butterfly effect of good deeds — watering the rose benefits the initial receiver and creates beauty for others through the outline of additional roses. Other festival appearances and projects include the 48 Hour Mural Competition in Jacksonville, Florida (September 29, 2023, painted with Golden Paintworks Mural & Theme Paint and various spray paint), the Paint Memphis Mural Festival, the HULAWEEN Spirit of Suwannee Lake Music and Art Festival, the Art With Me Art and Music Festival in Miami, an apartment complex in Sanford, Florida, and a private residence mural in St. Petersburg.\n\nIn St. Petersburg he painted a two-building mural at the CASA Pinellas Family Justice Center on Arlington Avenue, two blocks north of Central Avenue, connected by an unofficial crosswalk of white polka dots and purple triangles. The mural was funded by Willi Rudowsky and Hal Freedman, longtime CASA donors and SHINE hospitality sponsors, and was selected through a request-for-proposals process that also drew submissions from Johnny Vitale and Cecilia Lueza. CASA Pinellas CEO Lariana Forsythe and the funders chose Culbertson\'s proposal. The piece features four faces. On one side, a woman cradles a baby whose curls contain the words Drive, Bravery, Family, Growth, Hope, Empathy, Love, Community, Wisdom, Faith, Create, and Unity. On the other side, a young woman stretches out a helping hand and a young man meets the viewer\'s gaze. The FJC by CASA logo, designed by St. Pete firm Stevie & Fern, appears in the upper right and points toward the building entrance. The Family Justice Center model originated with Gael Strack and Casey Gwinn, who opened the first FJC in San Diego in October 2002; the St. Pete center opened in October 2022. He also painted a portrait of Betty Reid Soskin at the Florida Botanical Gardens in Largo.\n\nHe is a four-time SHINE artist: "Moon Beam Dream 2" in 2016, "Legacy" with Thirst in 2017, "Wisdom, Growth, Progress" in 2018, and the AMIKids wall in 2024. He received a 2022 St. Petersburg Arts Alliance Muse Award alongside Matthew McGee, Gloria Muñoz, David and Becky Ramsey, Chad Mize, and Matthew Walker, presented at the Morean Center for Clay in the Warehouse Arts District on March 4, 2022, with hosts Paul Wilborn and Eugenie Bondurant. He collaborated with Reid Jenkins and Iboms on "Imagine Gas Plant and Laurel Park" at Campbell Park Recreational Center in St. Petersburg.\n\nHis work prioritizes vibrant color, bold design, and what he describes as mindful energy. He frames public art as immediate and accessible — no entry fee, year-round, flash photography allowed. His practice has been profiled by Creative Pinellas, Creative Loafing Tampa Bay, Golden Paintworks, and the Monochronicle artist network.\n\nContact: info@zulupainter.com, +1-727-768-4737. Website: zulupainter.com. Instagram: @zulupainter1. Facebook: facebook.com/ZULUPAINTER1.',fn:['Calls them \'Mindful Murals\'','Local Pinellas County artist','Community-focused public art'],src:['https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/welcoming-domestic-violence-survivors-with-art/','https://www.zulupainter.com/about.html','https://creativepinellas.org/artist/zulu-painter/','https://www.goldenpaintworks.com/mural-makers-zulu-painter/','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/muralist-zulu-painter-among-honorees-for-st-pete-arts-alliances-muse-awards-12783271','https://monochronicle.com/artist/zulu-painter/','https://paintmemphis.org/zulupainter/']},
  {id:74,a:'Aurailieus Artist',t:'',loc:'600 27th St. S.',bldg:'Zen Glass Studio',lat:27.764593,lng:-82.669474,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'aurailieus_artist',bio:'He painted a robot named Bob holding a sign that read "I hope SHINE has an Open Call next year." Then he won the Open Call. This is the wall.\n\nMark Stevens — Aurailieus Artist — blends fine art with urban street art, leaning on graffiti influence and Jean-Michel Basquiat, whose work he first encountered when a high school art teacher handed him a book about the painter. He works in mixed media — paintings, spray cans, canvas prints, wood cutouts. Bob the Robot Prince of Denmark came out of a robot-building project he was running with Zulu Painter and Jason Bromley — little robots made of cardboard, duct tape, and wires. One of them had personality. He painted it, refined it, and stories started attaching. Bob is now St. Pete famous, with shows where other artists do their own interpretations of him.\n\nWith Zulu Painter and Bromley he co-founded The St. Pete Robot Exchange — also called "The Robot Show" — an annual group show that ran for nine years. He also ran weekly Wednesday-night art meetups for ten years. In 2020 the St. Petersburg Arts Alliance awarded him a $1,000 Individual Artist Grant for "The Bob Project," which puts more Bob murals on surfaces around the city.\n\nThis wall is a collaborative jam — a portrait on the left, wildstyle graffiti lettering in the center, angular black shapes exploding outward. Look for Bob the Robot tucked in among the splashes in yellow-green. He always finds his way in.',desc:'A vibrant mixed-style piece on a teal cinder block wall. On the left, a female portrait with flowers in her hair and golden earrings gazes outward, rendered in a clean illustrative style. To her right, a burst of graffiti lettering in pinks, reds, purples, and blacks fills the center of the wall, with angular black wing-like shapes exploding outward. Scattered throughout are colorful elements — green and pink splashes, circular dots, and what appears to be a small yellow-green character (Bob the Robot). The piece has a collaborative jam feel, blending portraiture with wildstyle graffiti.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/aurailieus-artist.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2021 Open Call winner',fw:[{name:'aurailieus.com',url:'https://www.aurailieus.com'}],sbio:'His robot character is named Bob. He paints him everywhere. Mark Stevens — also spelled Marko Stevens — works under the name Aurailieus Artist out of St. Petersburg, Florida. He blends fine art with urban street art, leaning on graffiti influence and Jean-Michel Basquiat, whose work he first encountered when a high school art teacher handed him a book about the painter. He was born in Camden, New Jersey in 1983, and credits art with giving him an escape from a family history of drug addiction and incarceration.\n\nThe Aurailieus name traces back to high school. He and his friends were the kids getting picked on, so they formed a tight group of weirdos to fight back. After everyone went to see the Russell Crowe Gladiator movie, his friends started greeting him in a Roman gladiator voice with "Marcus Aurelius" while pounding their chests. The name stuck.\n\nHe skipped school in St. Pete looking for exhibition opportunities. His first art show was in a tattoo parlor that shut its doors and skipped town with all his early paintings. His first local mural was a rough one painted next to Nova 535 around 2008. The first one he counts as relevant was Bob the Robot Prince of Denmark behind Bar@548, holding a sign that read "I hope SHINE has an open call next year." He says it created some friction but pushed the SHINE Mural Festival organizers to open things up a bit, which he counts as a win for artist accessibility. He went on to win an Open Call slot and painted for SHINE in 2021, and Creative Pinellas documented him painting a mural start to finish for SHINE Mural Festival 7 in 2022.\n\nBob came out of a robot project he was working on with fellow Tampa Bay artists Zulu Painter and Jason Bromley. Stevens started building little robots out of cardboard, duct tape and wires, and one of them looked like it had personality. He started painting it, refined it, and stories began to attach to it. Bob the Robot Prince of Denmark is a creation of man — Stevens — while a separate character, Sad Robot, exists in the same universe but originates in space and functions more as an observer of human civilization. Bob has become "St. Pete famous," with shows where other artists have done their own interpretations of him.\n\nIn 2020 the St. Petersburg Arts Alliance awarded Stevens a $1,000 Individual Artist Grant for "The Bob Project," which puts more Bob the Robot murals on surfaces around St. Pete. The Arts Alliance announced 20 grant recipients that May. Stevens scouts and procures the locations himself; the Bob murals tend to be much smaller than the full-wall pieces associated with SHINE. He mentioned planned spots behind Pizza Box at 923 Central Avenue and at CellarMasters Wine Bar and Bottle Shop at 1005 1st Avenue North.\n\nWith Zulu Painter and Jason Bromley, Stevens co-founded The St. Pete Robot Exchange — also called "The Robot Show" — an annual group art show and intergalactic celebration of all things robotica that ran for nine years before going on hold during the coronavirus pandemic. He also founded weekly Wednesday night art meetups in St. Pete that ran for ten years, also paused for the pandemic; the meetups were a comradery-and-inclusivity engine for the local creative community.\n\nStevens, Suarez and Zulu Painter went down to Miami for Art Basel one year, holding a fundraising event where they essentially gave pieces away to fund the trip. Each painted about three murals in various Miami spots, including the Puerto Rico Hope project in Wynwood, where roughly 30 artists muralized an entire parking garage to raise awareness for Puerto Rico\'s hurricane destruction.\n\nHe works in mixed media — paintings, spray cans, canvas prints, wood cutouts. For mural prep he draws the piece out, sometimes colors it, then heads to Artisinns for spray paint and tips before treating the wall like a very large canvas — no projectors, nothing fancy. His shop and portfolio at aurailieus.com lists original paintings, prints, stickers, spray-can pieces, wood cutouts and unisex apparel, with titles like "I Aim to Please," "Come A Little Closer," "Like A Sunset You Can Taste," "Like Honey in the Summer Sun," "Corazón," "Ingenue," "Tennesee Honey," "A West Side Story," "Linked To Roots," "Holy Gentrification Bob," "Remember To Shine," "Bob walks Keith Haring\'s dog during a nuclear holocaust," "Leeloo Dallas Multipass 22," "Van Der Bot 3000," "Death Rattle (of the Republican Party)," "Pop Art Bob," "American Gothic," "Bobby Warhol" series, "Bob\'s World Tour" series and "Of Birds and Bobs" series. He has done collaboration pieces with Brook Lyn — titles include "I Love You I\'m Over You," "Death by Robot," "The Perversions of Quiet Girls," "Pretty Art By Unattractive People," "Hot Sex With Strangers" and "Some Symbolistic Bullshit About the Evils of Money." He names Brook Lyn as a favorite local artist, citing her unwavering, unapologetic work.\n\nCreative Pinellas has produced video segments on him including "Eating Sandwiches Pilot Ep 1 – Aurailieus Artist" and "Painting TRAINS!! – Aurailieus Artist." He can be reached at aurailieus.artist@gmail.com and followed at aurailieus.com and on Instagram.',src:['https://www.cltampa.com/arts/seven-questions-with-mark-stevens-aka-st-petes-aurailieus-artist-12229573','https://creativepinellas.org/artist/mark-stevens/','https://moreanartscenter.org/aurailieus-fairly-local-featuring-bob-the-robot-prince-of-denmark/','https://baynews9.com/fl/tampa/arts/2020/07/10/st-petersburg-artist-wins-grant','https://www.aurailieus.com/','https://creativepinellas.org/video/making-a-mural-for-shine-mural-festival-7-aurailieus-artist/']},
  {id:75,a:'Bakpak Durden',t:'',loc:'919 1st Ave. N.',bldg:'The Bends',lat:27.772283,lng:-82.647522,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'bakpakdurden',bio:'His first solo museum exhibition sold out at Cranbrook Art Museum. He is a Black trans postgender artist painting in the tradition of Dutch Baroque.\n\nBakpak Durden is a self-taught artist from Detroit. His style is hyperrealistic and conceptual — Afro-Surrealist work exploring human emotion, identity, consciousness, and spiritual existence, inspired by Baroque painting and cinematic language. He works in oil, acrylic, graphite, and fine art photography.\n\nHis "Eye of Horus" at Cranbrook was the inaugural Fresh Paint exhibition series. He has shown at the Muskegon Museum of Art, Arab American National Museum, Heron Arts in San Francisco, and Paradigm Gallery in Philadelphia. He won the 2021 Gilda Award and a Modern Ancient Brown Foundation Grant. He is represented by B&A Reps. For SHINE 2021, he painted a vanitas-style mural in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A dark, moody mural stretches across the long wall of The Bends. On a near-black background, a skull and exposed jawbone with visible teeth emerge from tangled, organic forms in deep greens and grays. The text "TELL ME HOW YOU FEEL" appears in the upper right corner. The hyperrealistic rendering and somber palette give it the gravity of a classical vanitas painting at massive scale.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/bakpak-durden.jpeg',from:'Detroit, MI',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'2021 Gilda Award; Playground Detroit 20/20 Emerging Artists Fellowship; Modern Ancient Brown Foundation Grant; Cranbrook Art Museum solo; SHINE 2021',fw:[{name:'bakpakdurden.com',url:'https://www.bakpakdurden.com'}],sbio:'His first solo museum exhibition sold out at Cranbrook Art Museum. He is a Black trans postgender artist painting in the tradition of Dutch Baroque. Bakpak Durden is a self-taught artist born and raised in Detroit, where he currently lives and works. He is an interdisciplinary fine artist describing his practice as multi-hyphenate, post-disciplinary, and hyper-surrealistic. Through extensive research, introspective examination, and an antidisciplinary approach, he fuses narrative and visual elements of the Baroque and Afro-Surrealist movements to create deconstructed cinematic experiences he calls "films," exploring human consciousness and Spiritual Existence.\n\nHis hyperrealistic and conceptual work draws on dramatic cinema and Dutch Baroque painting, illuminating the complexity and precarity of human emotion and identity by meticulously examining his own. He works across a wide range of mediums — oil paint, acrylic paint, graphite, and fine art photography. His subjects are typically depicted in transitional states or moments of reverie and introspection, with an emphasis on connecting to surrounding communities. His practice highlights Black trans and queer visibility, mental health, neurodiversity, and disability advocacy and representation as a Detroiter in the city\'s art scene.\n\n"Bakpak Durden: The Eye of Horus" was his first solo museum exhibition, held in the Upper Galleries at Cranbrook Art Museum in Bloomfield Hills, Michigan, from November 5, 2022 to March 12, 2023. It was the inaugural show of Cranbrook Art Museum\'s Fresh Paint series, which highlights new work from Detroit-based artists. The Eye of Horus is an ancient Egyptian symbol thought to have healing and protective powers, also used in ancient mathematical and neurological contexts; for the exhibition Durden explored the physiological and psychological functions relating to vision and the mind through paintings and sculpture that dissect the physical processes of vision and the anatomical magic shaping emotion and identity. The exhibition was curated by Kat Goffnett, Assistant Curator of Collections, and Laura Mott, Chief Curator at Cranbrook Art Museum. It was supported by the George Francoeur Art Museum Exhibition Fund and the Gilbert Family Foundation, with additional assistance from the Ruth Ellis Center and Playground Detroit. As part of the project, Cranbrook Art Museum partnered with the Ruth Ellis Center, a non-profit providing trauma-informed services for LGBTQ+ youth and young adults of color, and Durden created a permanent mural for the new Ruth Ellis Clairmount Center in Detroit.\n\nWorks from the Cranbrook show included "Hope/Less" (2021, oil on wood panel, Collection Cranbrook Art Museum, Gift of Rose M. Shuey from the Collection of Dr. John and Rose M. Shuey, by exchange) and "The Refrigerator" (2020, oil on wood panel, courtesy the artist and Playground Detroit, photo Samantha Bankle Schefman). His mural "Evocative of Ingenuity" (2021) is located at 952 Clay Ave in Detroit, Michigan, and was completed during the 2021 BLKOUT Walls Mural Festival. Exhibition views were photographed by PD Rearick.\n\nHis work has been presented in exhibitions at Cranbrook Art Museum (Bloomfield Hills, MI); Muskegon Museum of Art (Muskegon, MI); The Stamps Art Gallery (Ann Arbor, MI); The Arab American National Museum (Dearborn, MI); Heron Art (San Francisco, CA); Morean Arts Center (St. Petersburg, FL); Paradigm Gallery (Philadelphia, PA); and Gangway Gallery (Honolulu, HI), among others. His work has been shown both nationally and internationally.\n\nHe won the 2021 Gilda Award through Kresge Arts in Detroit, which is funded by The Kresge Foundation and administered by the College for Creative Studies. He has also received a Modern Ancient Brown Foundation Grant. He has completed a number of murals across the nation, including one for the GUCCI VS EVERYBODY campaign. For SHINE 2021, he painted a vanitas-style mural in St. Petersburg.\n\nHe is represented for mural art by B&A Reps, alongside artists including Will Barras, Kitt Bennett, Ewok, Taj Francis, George Rose, Georgia Hill, INSA, Ornamental Conifer, Roids, Tatiana Suarez, Victor Reyes, and Vizie. Agent contact: info@ba-reps.com. Project inquiries: bakpakmgmt@gmail.com. Website: bakpakdurden.com. Instagram: @bakpakdurden. Photo credits include Cody Samuel and MARS Marshall.',fn:['First solo museum show sold out at Cranbrook','Detroit-based — graffiti roots','Bold portraiture and pattern work'],src:['https://www.bakpakdurden.com/about','https://cranbrookartmuseum.org/exhibition/bakpak-durden-eye-of-horus/','https://kresgeartsindetroit.org/artist/bakpak-durden/','https://www.ba-reps.com/mural-art/bakpak-durden']},
  {id:76,a:'Case Maclaim',t:'',loc:'2641 Central Ave.',bldg:'2641 Central Ave Condos',lat:27.771213,lng:-82.669389,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'case_maclaim',bio:'He earned a diploma in art restoration. Then he picked up a spray can and started painting hands the size of buildings.\n\nAndreas von Chrzanowski — Case Maclaim — was born in 1979 in Frankfurt am Main and raised in East Germany. He studied art restoration and conservation at Erfurt University of Applied Sciences. He started spraying in 1995 and co-founded Ma\'Claim, an East German crew that pioneered photorealistic aerosol art.\n\nHis signature is overlapping, intertwined hands symbolizing unity and power — freehand spray can work combining photorealism with surrealism. He has painted in twenty-plus countries: Eastern Market in Detroit, Historic Third Ward in Milwaukee, Bushwick in Brooklyn, Wynwood in Miami, and URBAN NATION in Berlin. For SHINE 2021, he painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A massive photorealistic portrait of a healthcare worker in teal scrubs and a surgical cap, one gloved hand reaching directly toward the viewer. To the right, another pair of hands gently holds a small teddy bear sitting in a red toy car — a warm, personal touch against the clinical setting. The dark background makes the figures pop off the wall, and the skin and fabric detail is rendered at a level that reads as a photograph from across the parking lot.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/case-maclaim.jpeg',from:'Frankfurt am Main, Germany',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2021 selected artist; founding member Ma\'Claim crew',fw:null,sbio:'He earned a diploma in art restoration. Then he picked up a spray can and started painting hands the size of buildings.\n\nAndreas von Chrzanowski works under the name Case Maclaim, sometimes shortened to CASE. He was born in 1979 in Frankfurt am Main and raised in East Germany. He studied at the Erfurt University of Applied Sciences and graduated with a diploma in Art Restoration and Conservation. He started spraying in 1995, working out his style with a can in his hand before any formal collaboration took shape.\n\nHis path as a street artist properly began in 1999, when Akut — the male half of the Herakut duo — teamed up with Case and Rusk to start collaborating on mutual projects. In 2000, the trio became a crew. Tasso joined in 2001, and the four-person group came to be known as the Maclaim Crew. Case is a founding member. The crew built its name producing photorealistic graffiti of body forms and portraits, and is widely credited as the East German pioneer of photorealistic aerosol art, a position it has held for over two decades. Akut, Tasso and Rusk are his named long-time collaborators inside the crew.\n\nEven in those early Maclaim years, Case stood apart with a personal, surrealistic approach. His signature motif is overlapping, intertwined hands — many hands, in different positions — symbolizing unity and power, both physical movement and political and societal action. He has said he rejects the mass-produced century, and works hard at making hand-painted images that are obviously special and next to impossible to copy. His visual vocabulary leans on components of the human body abstracted from their origin and given new narrative, frightful, animalistic or mythic elements. The figurative subject carries the work, with technical execution underlining the pictorial pureness and high narrative value. Critics describe him as one of the finest photorealistic aerosol authors in the world, working primarily in spray paint because the medium lets him reach the level of perfection his life-like graffiti requires.\n\nHe has traveled to over twenty countries and left aerosol pieces in each of them. The work, he and his chroniclers note, speaks a universal language — a hand gesture can tell more than words across any geography. Documented locations for individual pieces include Düsseldorf, Germany (Mains à Garath, April 2026); Le Mans, France (a hyperrealist outstretched hand piece, April 2023); and Madrid, Spain (a young woman in a wheelchair, March 2023). In the United States, his murals appear in Eastern Market in Detroit, the Historic Third Ward in Milwaukee, Bushwick in Brooklyn, and Wynwood in Miami. In Holyoke, Massachusetts, working with the Beyond Walls placemaking organization in Lynn, he painted the north wall of 284 Maple Street — a project he had carried for years, a portrayal of an abolitionist for which Holyoke offered an ideal canvas.\n\nIn Berlin, his work is held by URBAN NATION Museum for Urban Contemporary Art, the institution at Bülowstrasse 7, 10783 Berlin, which presents street art, graffiti and urban contemporary art free of charge. He was featured in the URBAN NATION 2018 exhibition UN-DERSTAND The Power of Art as a Social Architect. URBAN NATION has documented his contributions to PROJECT M/8 and PROJECT M/11, the museum\'s serial outdoor painting commissions on the building façade.\n\nHe has been a recurring presence at Art Basel Miami\'s Wynwood activations. Brooklyn Street Art and writers Steven P. Harrington and Jaime Rojo covered his Miami work in their 2016 Art Basel dispatches, including the December 2016 Wynwood wrap-up "Parade of Eye-Popping Beauty at a Portentous Time" and the dispatches "Paint, Protest, Party // BSA X ART BASEL Miami \'16" and "Clubhouse Chemistry in a Warehouse." Critic Andreja Velimirović has also written on his practice. Beyond Walls, the non-profit placemaking agency that brought him to Holyoke, lists him among its commissioned artists.\n\nFor SHINE 2021 in St. Petersburg, Florida, he contributed a mural in his familiar register of intertwined hands — freehand spray can work combining photorealism with surrealism. He is based in Germany and continues to paint internationally. Online he is @case_maclaim on Instagram.',fn:['Diploma in art restoration, then spray cans','German photorealist — massive scale portraits','Paints entirely with spray paint, no brushes'],src:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CASE_Maclaim','https://www.maclaim.de/','https://beyondwalls.org/artists/case-maclaim/','https://urban-nation.com/artist/case-maclaim/','https://www.street-artwork.com/en/artist-profile/449/case-maclaim']},
  {id:78,a:'Emily Ding',t:'',loc:'100 7th St. S.',bldg:'Rob Graham Enterprise',lat:27.77026667,lng:-82.64296667,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'_emilyding',bio:'She draws inspiration from flora, fauna, and human temperament — wild creatures telling stories about emotions we recognize but cannot name.\n\nEmily Ding is based in Los Angeles and represented by Thinkspace Projects. Her expressive animals and figures are rendered in bold color gradients with a fluid, painterly style — acrylics on canvas, wood panels, and cradled wood. Recent Thinkspace gallery work runs through the same themes as her walls: pieces titled "Refuge," "Revival," "Resilience," "Rewilding," "Convalescent."\n\nShe has painted murals in Shanghai, Salt Lake City, Sacramento, Washington DC, Oakland, Austin, Flint, Harrisburg, and Houston. She works with Street Art for Mankind, the nonprofit that uses public art to advance human rights and fight child trafficking.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, she painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A young fawn sleeps curled in a gentle pose, its spotted fur rendered with painterly realism. White flowers bloom from the deer\'s back and around its body, growing up toward a window that\'s incorporated into the design. The deep emerald-green background gives the whole wall the feeling of a forest clearing. Emily Ding\'s signature is visible in the lower right.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/emily-ding.jpeg',from:'Houston, TX',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2021 selected artist',fw:[{name:'emilyding.art',url:'https://www.emilyding.art'}],sbio:'She draws inspiration from flora, fauna, and human temperament — wild creatures telling stories about emotions we recognize but cannot name.\n\nEmily Ding is an American muralist based in Los Angeles, California. Her large-scale work centers on expressive animals and figures, rendered through bold color gradients and a fluid, painterly style. The work resonates with the phenomena of the natural world, and she uses wild creatures and people as the vehicles for storytelling about emotion and experience.\n\nShe is represented by Thinkspace Projects, the Los Angeles gallery at 4217 W. Jefferson Blvd., Los Angeles, CA 90016. Her studio practice is acrylic on canvas, acrylic on wood panel, and acrylic on cradled wood panel, in sizes ranging from small format pieces at 5 by 7 inches and 6 by 6 inches up to 24 by 30 inch canvases. Recent and past works at Thinkspace include "Convalescent" (2025), a 24 by 30 inch acrylic on canvas shown in Extracurricular Activities and Stand Strong; "Rewilding" (2025), a 12 by 12 inch acrylic on cradled wood panel shown in Extracurricular Activities and The Earth Laughs In Flowers; "Refuge" (2025), "Revival" (2025), and "Resilience" (2025), each a 16 by 20 inch acrylic on cradled wood panel in the EMPOWER show; "Sorry You\'re Dead" (2018), a 12 by 12 inch acrylic on wood panel shown in LAX / MSY and SCOPE MIAMI BEACH 2018; "Fire Spin" (2024), a 16 by 20 inch acrylic on wood shown in Nerdstalgia (Gallery IV); and "Flutter" (2025) and "Tiptoe" (2025), small format acrylic on wood panel pieces from SMOL 2.0 (Gallery I).\n\nHer mural work has appeared across the United States and internationally. She has painted walls in Shanghai, Salt Lake City, Sacramento, Washington DC, Oakland, Austin, Flint, Harrisburg, and Houston. Her work can also be seen in China.\n\nShe has worked with Street Art for Mankind, the nonprofit that uses street art to advance human rights and the fight against child trafficking. Street Art for Mankind organizes mural campaigns and art walks including Seattle World Cup 26, Los Angeles LA28 & Bigger Dreams, New York Climate Action, New York ILO100, New York Free Children, New York NRNY, Houston Big Art Bigger Change, Houston Legacies of Change, Houston World Cup 26, Houston I Choose to Rise, Houston John Biggers, Detroit Be the Change, USA Zero Hunger, France Impressionist Land, World Ecosystem Restoration, and World Generation Equality, and runs the BehindTheWall audio guide app.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, she painted in St. Petersburg.\n\nHer website is emilyding.art, with sections for ABOUT, MURALS, PAINTINGS, and ORIGINALS. She can be followed on Instagram.',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/emily-ding-2021','https://streetartmankind.org/artists/emily-ding/','https://www.emilyding.art/','https://www.emilyding.art/about','https://thinkspaceprojects.com/artists/emily-ding/']},
  {id:79,a:'Gleo',t:'',loc:'2336 5th Ave. S.',bldg:'New Dawn Coffee Co',lat:27.766397,lng:-82.665017,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'gleo_co',bio:'She painted a 50,000-square-foot grain elevator in Wichita, Kansas. It broke the record for the largest acrylic mural by a single artist.\n\nNathalia Gallego Sanchez — Gleo — was born in 1992 in Cali, Colombia. She studied chemistry by day and fine arts at a night institute before earning her bachelor\'s in visual arts and graphic design from Universidad del Valle. She found formal study too theoretical and went her own way. She uses acrylics, brushes, and rollers — no spray cans. "I let myself be guided by the moment."\n\nHer murals feature mystical beings, pre-Columbian figures, women with patterned face-paint and floral-decorated hair, and signature bright yellow orbs. She draws from Colombian folklore and ancestral cosmology. She has painted across France, Portugal, Morocco, Brazil, Mexico, Belgium, the Netherlands, and the US. Her name is wordplay — derived from her surname GALLEGO.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, she painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A wide panoramic mural across the full building wall showing multiple overlapping faces and hands rendered in soft pinks and mauves. The figures intertwine with ornate mandala-like patterns in warm golds and oranges, with sunflowers and botanical details woven between the portraits. The color palette is dreamy — mostly blush pink, sage green, and amber against a pale background. The faces gaze in different directions, creating a layered, contemplative composition.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/gleo.jpeg',from:'Colombia',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Largest acrylic mural by single artist (Wichita, 2018); Dinero 40 under 40 (Colombia); SHINE 2021',fw:null,sbio:'She painted a 50,000-square-foot grain elevator in Wichita, Kansas. It broke the record for the largest acrylic mural by a single artist.\n\nNathalia Gallego Sánchez — Gleo — was born in 1992 and grew up in Cali, Colombia from age nine. She studied chemistry by day and fine arts at a night institute before earning her bachelor\'s in visual arts and graphic design at Universidad del Valle. She found formal art study too theoretical and went her own way. The pseudonym Gleo is wordplay derived from her surname GALLEGO. She does not reveal her face to media.\n\nShe began experimenting with street art at seventeen, painting sea creatures in her neighborhood. Her first piece on a wall in front of her house was nearly painted over by a more experienced graffiti artist; her mother stepped outside and told him she would call the police if he covered her daughter\'s work. The confrontation ended with Gleo joining the local crew. Her real training came on the street and through travel. Her work has since reached Argentina, Paraguay, Peru, Brazil, Ecuador, Costa Rica, Mexico, the United States, Spain, Portugal, Belgium, Sweden, the Netherlands, France, and Morocco. Her path into Mexico opened through a scholarship that did not come through, then through work she found at an urban art gallery.\n\nShe works in acrylic outdoor vinyl, brushes, and rollers — no spray cans. She sketches the design, then builds an irregular grid for proportions and perspective before adding splatters, textures, and color. Her murals depict mystical beings, pre-Columbian figures, women with patterned face-paint and floral-decorated hair, mythical creatures with yellow glowing eyes, and signature bright yellow orbs. She draws from Colombian folklore, ancestral cosmology, antiquity, and the primary arts of the world. Her stance: "I let myself be guided by the moment I\'m painting." She has spoken about street art as political engagement — a protest against the concrete grid called "city" and an invitation to remember that humans are living creatures inside a larger whole.\n\nIn 2016 she exhibited "Origo," a departure from her typical street work that included canvas, sculpture, and intervened objects. In 2017 she completed a wall in São Paulo, Brazil that she has named her favorite project — a challenge years in the anticipating that pushed her past her existing skills.\n\nIn 2018 she painted "El Sueño Original" ("The Original Dream") on the Beachner Grain Elevator at 519 E 20th Street N, Wichita, Kansas, as part of the Horizontes Mural Project. The mural was commissioned by artist and community organizer Armando Minjarez, who designed and directed the project after immigrating to Wichita in 2001. At 50,000 square feet, "El Sueño Original" surpassed Spain\'s previous 38,701-square-foot record to become the World Record Academy and Guinness-recognized largest acrylic mural by a single artist. The grain elevator sits in the industrial corridor that separates Wichita\'s predominantly Latino NorthEnd from its historically Black Northeast neighborhoods. The mural depicts people of color from those neighborhoods looking toward the horizon, with a young woman at the center representing Wichita; many figures are based on local residents and migrant laborers who built the railroad tracks around the elevator. Photographers Ashwin Govindarajan, Alexis Rivierre, Juan Carlos Garcia, and Xavier Leija contributed source portraits for the project. Mural production was led by Fellipe Ribeiro; art assistants included Quintis Pinkston and Rob Lewis; logistics assistant was Mason Talbott. The piece is visible from Interstate 135 and Highway 96.\n\nIn 2022 she painted "Flower Woman in the Window," one of the largest murals in the Netherlands, opposite Moerwijk Station in The Hague. It was commissioned through Mauritshuis and inspired by Ambrosius Bosschaert\'s painting "Vase of Flowers in a Window." The wall took twenty-one days to complete. Through Beyond Walls, the Lynn, Massachusetts–based public art organization, she has also painted "Child Flower" on the former Boys Club building at Yellow Brick Management in Holyoke, Massachusetts.\n\nFor SHINE Mural Festival 2021, she painted in St. Petersburg, Florida.\n\nWebsite: gleo.com. Instagram: @gleo_co.',fn:['Painted a 50,000-sq-ft grain elevator in Wichita','Colombian artist, based in Bogotá','Mythological narrative at architectural scale'],src:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natalia_Gallego_Sanchez','https://especiales.dinero.com/cuarenta-menores-de-cuarenta/nathalia-gallego.html','https://www.worldrecordacademy.org/2023/7/worlds-largest-mural-by-a-single-artist-world-record-in-wichita-kansas-423283','https://beyondwalls.org/artists/gleo/','https://www.isupportstreetart.com/artist/gleo/','https://ardeartdealer.com/blogs/wallart/nathalia-gallego-gleo','https://www.mauritshuis.nl/en/what-s-on/mauritshuis-murals/gleo','https://www.instagram.com/gleo_co/']},
  {id:80,a:'Greg Mike',t:'',loc:'915 1st Ave. N.',bldg:'Goldman Wetzel',lat:27.772291,lng:-82.647028,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'gregmike',bio:'Justin Bieber collects him. So does Diplo. His "MAD CANS" NFT series generated three million dollars.\n\nGreg Mike — Gregory Michael Mensching — started graffiti at thirteen in Atlanta and became a muralist in his late twenties. His style is Surrealist Pop Art — vivid explosions of color, gleefully twisted characters inspired by mid-century American cartoons and skate culture. His signature character Larry Loudmouf is everywhere.\n\nHe founded ABV Gallery and Agency in Atlanta in 2008 and launched the OuterSpace Project in 2015, producing over a hundred murals across the city. Clients include Nike, Coca-Cola, Google, Spotify, BMW, Adidas, Converse, the Atlanta Braves and Falcons, Ford Mustang, and Topgolf. He received the Key to the City of Worcester, Massachusetts, and was named Best Established Visual Artist and a Notable Georgian by Georgia Trend.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, he painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A massive wall covered edge-to-edge with Greg Mike\'s signature cartoon characters — bug-eyed, grinning figures in electric pinks, greens, and yellows. The word "SMILE" runs across the center in bold lettering, surrounded by the crowd of manic, playful characters that make the building impossible to walk past without looking.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/greg-mike.jpeg',from:'Atlanta, GA',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Key to the City, Worcester MA (2016); Best Established Visual Artist, Best of Atlanta (2017); Notable Georgians (2017); SHINE 2021',fw:[{name:'gregmike.com',url:'https://www.gregmike.com'}],sbio:'Justin Bieber collects him. So does Diplo. His "MAD CANS" NFT series generated three million dollars.\n\nGreg Mike — Gregory Michael Mensching — was raised in Danbury, Connecticut, skateboarding in summer and snowboarding in winter. He fell in love with art through the graphics on skate decks. His parents divorced when he was young, and he turned to his sketchbook to process what he could not say out loud. The early drawings were dark and creepy. He started graffiti at thirteen and went on to study at Florida State University, where classmates were unsettled by the gloom in his work. He pivoted toward the afternoon-cartoon nostalgia of his childhood — Ren and Stimpy, The Simpsons, Disney, Powell Peralta skate graphics — and the reactions changed. He transitioned into murals in his late twenties.\n\nHis style is Surrealist Pop Art — vivid explosions of color, gleefully twisted characters inspired by mid-century American cartoons and skate culture. He calls his classic mode "intertwined" — forms weaving in and out, sinister but joyful, sampled from sources rather than copied. His urban murals sometimes feature majestic carnivores like tigers and wolves watching over Atlanta. His signature character Larry Loudmouf — a turquoise, often square creature with a shifting number of eyes and a mouthful of pearly teeth — started as a sketchbook doodle. The chipped tooth came from a near-death experience in his twenties: he had a vivid dream, sleepwalked off a second-floor balcony, and woke up in a pool of blood with a cracked skull and a chipped tooth. The jagged tooth became a symbol of life\'s imperfection and fragility. Larry\'s dialogue bubbles began as Mike\'s own thoughts and grew into the Loudmouf Says project, where followers worldwide submit one-liners that get published on billboards, stickers, canvases and merch.\n\nIn 2008 he founded ABV Gallery and Agency in Atlanta — ABV stands for A Better View — after a 2009 solo show at Medicine Agency in San Francisco showed him the gallery/agency model. He started in a $1,500-a-month space at Studioplex in Inman Park. ABV runs on three tiers: his personal artist brand, a design agency for client work, and a brick-and-mortar gallery showing local and international artists. The operation now works with a global network of more than 600 contemporary artists. After eight years of searching and four years of renovation, ABV reopened in September 2024 in a converted church at 1206 Metropolitan Ave SE in East Atlanta — 8,500 square feet, with a 5,000-square-foot two-story main gallery, polished concrete floors, the original stained-glass windows in the foyer, a 950-square-foot personal studio for Mike in the rear, and ABV Agency upstairs. The opening solo show, Chapter New, ran through October 20, 2024 — more than 100 new works, twenty-four canvases, fiberglass and resin sculptures, and a twelve-foot Larry Loudmouf at the center. It included the NFT series The Loud Ones, designed in collaboration with software that recombines his elements.\n\nHe launched the OuterSpace Project in 2015, an annual Atlanta mural and arts celebration that has produced over a hundred murals across the city. Clients include Nike, Coca-Cola, Google, Spotify, BMW, Adidas, Converse, the Atlanta Braves and Falcons, Ford Mustang, and Topgolf. He has painted in the United States, Australia, Canada and Switzerland. Festival and project credits include the 2012 With Your Friends Festival in Nashville, a 2015 mural unveiling at Whisky Park, the 2015 Vision Art Festival, 2016 Atlanta Art Culture, 2016 Outside Lands Art Festival, the 2016 LA Public School street art project alongside Shepard Fairey and Kenny Scharf, Pow! Wow! Worcester in 2016, Murals in the Market in Detroit, the Richmond Mural Project in 2013, and a 2015 collaboration with deadmau5 on a Miami mural. He has revamped Atlanta\'s Phoenix II basketball court with Mountain Dew Green Label, painted a mural on the Modera Midtown building, and a piece at the W Hotel pool in Atlanta. He was invited to paint at Wynwood Walls after recovering from a snowboarding collision in Colorado that shattered the collarbone of his painting arm in six feet of powder; doctors said he might not paint again, and a surgically bolted plate plus months of physical therapy got him back.\n\nHis 2014 solo show "Pigment of My Imagination" opened at Maxwell Colette in Chicago. He has been selling NFTs since 2021 on Nifty Gateway; the MAD CANS series of digital collectible cans, each paired with a physical Infinite Object, raised over three million dollars. Awards include the Key to the City of Worcester, Massachusetts, presented by Mayor Joseph Petty in September 2016 after Pow! Wow! Worcester; Best Established Visual Artist from Creative Loafing in 2017; and a 2017 Notable Georgians Award from Georgia Trend.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, he painted in St. Petersburg. Website gregmike.com.',fn:['Collected by Justin Bieber and Diplo','MAD CANS NFT series — 10,000 editions','Atlanta-based, pop art and street culture'],src:['https://www.gregmike.com/','https://www.atlantamagazine.com/news-culture-articles/atlanta-artist-greg-mike-came-iconic-character/','https://www.abvgallery.com/news/artist-spotlight-greg-mike','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greg_Mike','https://www.abvgallery.com/','https://www.artsatl.org/street-art-stories-greg-mikes-winding-path-to-the-new-abv-gallery/']},
  {id:82,a:'Jared Wright',t:'Harmony',loc:'670 Central Ave.',bldg:'Colony Grill',lat:27.77083333,lng:-82.64322833,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'jaredwright',bio:'His first mural ended up in a Tampa Bay Rays commercial. He designed a skateboard for Tony Hawk.\n\nJared Wright is a self-taught painter and illustrator from Connecticut, raised in the Tampa Bay area. His style is surreal, dreamlike, hallucinatory — fun worlds with loose narratives, influenced by Dali, classic rock, aliens, and nature. His first painting commission came around 2007, based on Wes Anderson\'s The Life Aquatic.\n\nHe has collaborated with Birdhouse Skateboards (Tony Hawk), Asphalt Yacht Club, and Pyramid Skateboards. For SHINE 2021, he painted "Harmony" at 670 Central Avenue — his first-ever mural.',desc:'A surreal scene fills the Colony Grill wall with smooth, rounded organic forms in muted corals, sage, and teal. An owl with large dark eyes perches near a crescent moon while a snake-like creature winds through the composition. Leaf shapes, clouds, and celestial elements float through the scene, all rendered in soft, flat color fields with clean outlines.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/jared-wright.jpeg',from:'Tampa Bay, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2021 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'His first mural ended up in a Tampa Bay Rays commercial. He designed a skateboard for Tony Hawk.\n\nJared Wright is a self-taught painter, illustrator, and designer originally from Connecticut and raised in the Tampa Bay area, currently based in Clearwater, Florida. He works across painting, illustration, design, skate, apparel, mural, and other commercial categories, organizing his portfolio at jaredwrightart.com under those exact headings. He describes his style as a variety of techniques and mediums used to create fun, surreal worlds with loose narratives. His stated influences run from Salvador Dalí to classic rock, aliens, and the beauty of nature, and he has said his goal is to create work that prompts the viewer to feel, think, and either create themselves or share the inspiration further. He is committed to the idea that an artist who represents themselves genuinely and honestly will reach the right viewer in the same register, and that the resulting connection can be everything.\n\nHis first painting commission came around 2007 and was based on Wes Anderson\'s film "The Life Aquatic with Steve Zissou," and his career has since grown into a hybrid practice that blends fine art with applied design work for skate and apparel brands. His skateboard collaborations include Birdhouse Skateboards (the Tony Hawk-founded company), Asphalt Yacht Club, and Pyramid Skateboards. He maintains an online store through his own site that carries skate decks, apparel, prints, and original work.\n\nFor SHINE Mural Festival 2021 — the festival\'s seventh annual edition, running October 15–24, 2021 — Wright was selected as one of sixteen featured artists. He was assigned to Colony Grill at 670 Central Avenue and painted "Harmony," his first-ever mural. The St. Pete Catalyst\'s "Meet the SHINE Mural Festival Class of \'21" feature by Bill DeYoung (October 12, 2021) profiled him alongside Emily Ding, Greg Mike, Bakpak Durden, Mwanel Pierre-Louis, Jason Harvin, Michael Fatutoa, Gleo, Woes Martin, Nicole Salgar, Jenipher Chandley, Ricky Watts, Aurailieus Artist, Case Maclaim, Miss Crit, and Reid Jenkins. The Catalyst characterized his work as that of "a surrealist and illustrator, whose striking images are often cartoonlike in their simplicity, but nevertheless strike an emotional chord," and quoted his own description of his paintings as "fun worlds, with loose narratives."\n\nThe cohort of SHINE 2021 also included three "Bright Spot" community-project murals: Jujmo (Cheryl Weber) at A-1 Recovery in partnership with the Shirley Proctor Fuller Foundation; Leo Gomez at 744 6th Avenue S in partnership with Bayfront Health for a tribute to pandemic frontline workers; and Ya La\'Ford\'s "Love Line Project" at 330 Beach Drive NE, which incorporated an illuminated neon sign featuring a phrase of positivity voted on by the public. SHINE 2021 was the seventh annual edition of the festival and is run as a curated, invitation-only event by the St. Petersburg Arts Alliance, the city\'s only 501(c)(3) nonprofit dedicated to raising money and advocating for the entire local creative community.\n\nThe opening weekend of SHINE 2021 included two anchor events: an October 15 Friday-evening artist meet-and-greet at the Morean Arts Center, 719 Central Avenue, from 5 to 8 p.m.; and "Spraycation Day" on Saturday, October 16, a 2 to 11 p.m. gathering at Bayboro Brewing, 2390 5th Avenue S, with live music, art demos, live tattooing, and Spraycation, a craft beer brewed for the occasion. The festival framed the experience as a literal en plein air viewing opportunity, with most participating artists working from sunup to sundown across the festival\'s ten-day run.\n\nWright\'s portfolio site, jaredwrightart.com, organizes his work under the categories Illustration, Painting, Design (with Skate, Apparel, Other, and Mural sub-folders), About, Contact, and Store. He also maintains a WordPress-hosted secondary site at jaredwrightart.wordpress.com with parallel sections for Painting, Illustration, Design, Apparel Design, Skateboard Design, Publication, Murals, NFTs, Shop, and Contact. His contact email is jaredwrightart@gmail.com. He uses Instagram under the handle @jaredwrightart for inquiries and collaborations.',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2021_SHINE_Mural_Jared_Wright','https://www.jaredwrightart.com/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/meet-the-shine-mural-festival-class-of-21/','https://jaredwrightart.wordpress.com/about-2/','https://sans-archetype.com/2018/08/11/artist-profile-jared-wright/']},
  {id:83,a:'Jason Harvin',t:'',loc:'1211 1st Ave. N.',bldg:'Rick Noll Properties',lat:27.772442,lng:-82.650938,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'waywardwalls',bio:'He quit Oracle to paint walls. He started painting in 2018. He was featured on HGTV\'s 100 Day Dream Home.\n\nJason Harvin grew up in a military family in Germany, graduated from Heidelberg American High School, and earned a degree in hospitality management from the University of Central Florida. He worked at Oracle for years before leaving corporate in early 2022 to paint full-time.\n\nHe founded Wayward Walls in 2019. His style is geometric abstraction with bold color palettes — painter\'s tape for crisp designs that transform blank walls into vibrant landmarks. During the pandemic he created "Zoom Walls" — custom accent walls for virtual meeting backgrounds. He also runs Wayworld, an event-driven art space in St. Pete\'s Warehouse Arts District.\n\nFor SHINE 2021 and 2022, he painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A wide commercial building wrapped in interlocking geometric shapes — overlapping triangles, concentric circles, and bold diagonal stripes in saturated blue, orange, red, yellow, black, and white. A large red triangle dominates the center, framed by concentric blue-and-orange rings. A bright yellow half-circle anchors the bottom edge. Black-and-white striped bands slice through the composition at sharp angles, while smaller geometric motifs fill every remaining gap. Palm trees along the base add scale to the Op Art explosion.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/jason-harvin.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2021 and 2022 selected artist; HGTV 100 Day Dream Home feature',fw:[{name:'waywardwalls.com',url:'https://www.waywardwalls.com'}],sbio:'He quit Oracle to paint walls. He started painting in 2018. He was featured on HGTV\'s 100 Day Dream Home.\n\nJason Harvin grew up in Germany while his parents served in the US Air Force and worked for the US Government. He graduated from Heidelberg American High School in 2004, then moved to Orlando, Florida to attend the University of Central Florida (UCF), where he earned a degree in hospitality management in 2008. After graduating, he joined MICROS Systems — later acquired by Oracle Corp. — training, installing, and eventually managing hotel software across North America. He stayed at Oracle for thirteen years, relocating from Orlando to the Tampa area during that time and purchasing his first home.\n\nBuying that house is what started the painting. After years of renting where he could not paint walls, he picked up painter\'s tape and began experimenting with accent and design walls room by room. Neighbors noticed, asked him to paint walls in their homes, and a side business started organically. Curious to see what running a small business took, he registered Wayward Walls, LLC in September 2019 — a painting company focused on accent, design, and feature walls. The business grew through word of mouth, social media, and creative marketing.\n\nWhen the COVID-19 pandemic arrived in March 2020, Harvin spotted an opportunity. With remote work, remote school, and social distancing keeping people home, he pitched "Zoom Walls" — custom accent walls designed to stand out as Zoom backgrounds, distinguishing professionals to employers and students to teachers. He spent most of 2020 and 2021 selling Zoom Walls and continued growing into small-business work. He was invited to participate in the Black Lives Matter mural in St. Petersburg, and was selected as an artist in the SHINE Mural Festival.\n\nIn October 2021, while on a lift painting his SHINE wall, a group with professional cameras stopped to watch. He assumed it was a news crew. It turned out to be the production company behind HGTV\'s 100 Day Dream Home, the show with Mika and Brian Kleinschmidt. They commissioned him on the spot to paint a piece for one of the homes they were renovating in the area. Harvin finished SHINE and returned to his Oracle job with painting still as the side hustle. By 2022 he was burning the candle at both ends — handling Oracle email blocks from 5 a.m. to 8 a.m., then painting all day, sometimes hosting Oracle project kickoff calls from 20 feet up on scaffolding. At the end of March 2022 he left Oracle to paint full-time. The HGTV episode aired in April 2022 and gave him enough business momentum to support the transition.\n\nSoon after, he was approached by The Factory St. Pete to pilot their Artist in Residence program. The residency expanded into something larger: between July and November 2022 he built out a previously unusable area of The Factory into a full event space and art gallery. He named it Wayworld. The space became a fixture in the Warehouse Arts District, hosting World Cup viewing parties, solo and group art shows, dance parties, and a New Year\'s Eve celebration with 200 people that brought in 2023. He continues to paint and run Wayworld in St. Petersburg.\n\nFor SHINE Mural Festival 2021 and 2022, he painted in St. Petersburg. His signature style is geometric abstraction with bold color palettes — designs built with painter\'s tape for crisp, hard-edged shapes that transform blank walls into vibrant landmarks. Each project is custom designed, often reflecting the identity of the business, neighborhood, or client.\n\nPress coverage includes a March 10, 2026 FOX 13 Tampa Bay "Made in Tampa Bay" segment by photojournalist Mike Hughes, which traced his arc from corporate work to full-time muralist. He has also been featured on HGTV\'s 100 Day Dream Home (2022 episode with Mika and Brian Kleinschmidt).\n\nHe lives with his fiancée Vera Herrera, a longtime artist herself, and their rescue cat Zooey. Contact: 813-502-0442 or jason@waywardwalls.com. Website: waywardwalls.com.',src:['https://www.waywardwalls.com/about-6','https://www.fox13news.com/news/tampa-artist-pursuing-full-time-mural-painting','https://www.axios.com/local/tampa-bay/2021/10/18/spotlight-shine-muralist-jason-harvin-florida-mural-art']},
  {id:84,a:'Jenipher Chandley',t:'Be-you-tiful!',loc:'625 23rd St S',bldg:'Cimco Tile',lat:27.763857,lng:-82.664543,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'jenipherchandley',bio:'Giant lemons and orange blossoms, with a tiny self-affirmation tucked into the design: Be-you-tiful!\n\nJenipher Chandley was born and raised in St. Petersburg. She earned degrees in interior design and graphic design from IADT Tampa, is a mother of four, and recently became a grandmother. She works predominantly in oils — representational figurative work infused with surrealism and symbolism. The female form recurs, embodying fragility and strength. Her central theme, in her own words, is "the story of love" — seeking, experiencing, losing, rediscovering.\n\nShe started painting outside Sake Bomb on Central Avenue around 2011 and turned those nights into something permanent. Twelve-plus years later she is the lead curator at Bar@548 (the same building, new name), hosts a free weekly Wednesday-night art meetup taking no commission, and curates monthly emerging-artist shows. Her studio is at the ArtsXchange, Studio Gallery 104.\n\nShe received a 2023 Emerging Artist Grant from Creative Pinellas and has been a Local Artist Spotlight at the Gasparilla Festival of the Arts and American Stage Theater. SHINE 2021 was her first festival mural — this wall on 23rd Street.',desc:'A vibrant mural on the side of Cimco Tile featuring oversized lemons, halved citrus slices, and white orange blossoms scattered across a deep navy blue background. The lemons glow in warm yellows and oranges, their green leaves curling between the fruit in multiple shades of sage and emerald. A large cross-section of a lemon dominates the center of the wall, painted around a small door. The words "Be-you-tiful!" appear in flowing white script on the left side. Signed "@JenipherChand" in the lower right corner. The bold, graphic botanical style and uplifting message make it a standout on the 23rd Street corridor.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/jenipher-chandley.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'2023 Creative Pinellas Emerging Artist Grant; Gasparilla Festival of the Arts Local Artist Spotlight; American Stage Theater Local Spotlight Artist; SHINE 2021 and 2025 selected artist',fw:[{name:'jenipherchandley.com',url:'https://www.jenipherchandley.com'}],sbio:'She started painting outside Sake Bomb on Central Avenue around 2011. She is a survivor — of economic hardship, of domestic abuse — and she paints that survival into everything.\n\nJenipher Chandley was born and raised in St. Petersburg, Florida. She earned degrees in interior design and graphic design from IADT Tampa. She is a mother of four and a recently new grandmother. She works predominantly in oils, making representational figurative artwork infused with surrealism and symbolism. Her paintings explore the relationship between nature and the human condition, with recurring themes of strength, beauty, loss, pain, and resilience. The female form recurs in her work, embodying both fragility and inner power, and she returns often to the divine feminine. Her central theme is the story of love — the journey to find it, revel in it, cherish and protect it, lose it, and rediscover it.\n\nHer own quote on the work: "Humans are complex and ever-changing, so our art should be as well." She describes her process as incorporating the desire to heal, to be happy, and to find inner peace, the common thread in all humanity. The work is emotionally charged, sometimes raw, and expressed through symbolism and poetic gestures. Imagery includes peach-complexioned girls dreaming of summer and dark-haired beauties floating in rose hip waters.\n\nHer imprint on the St. Petersburg creative community started in 2011, over eleven years ago, when she gathered like-minded artists and started painting in front of what was then Sake Bomb on Central Avenue in downtown St. Petersburg. Then-owner Tai Swank encouraged her, and what began with home-made flyers and neighboring dive bars grew into an ongoing artist meet-up and exhibition space. As ownership changed, the venue became Bar@548. Current owner Michael Kellem continues to support the program. For more than twelve years she has worked with nonprofit organizations and community initiatives.\n\nShe is lead curator at Bar@548 in downtown St. Pete, where she hosts a free weekly Wednesday Night art meetup for local artists of all skill levels — no commission taken — and curates monthly shows that showcase emerging artists. She has co-curated and exhibited in hundreds of shows throughout the Tampa Bay Area, selling her work directly off the walls of the bar and encouraging other artists to do the same. Her studio is at the ArtsXchange, Studio Gallery 104, located at 515 22nd St S, St. Petersburg, FL 33712.\n\nRecognition includes the 2023 Emerging Artist Grant from Creative Pinellas — a grant aimed at artists on the upward trend of their career, with an established practice — which carries obligations including keeping a blog, working with an artist mentor, and creating a new body of work for an exhibition. She has been named Local Artist Spotlight by the Gasparilla Festival of the Arts in Tampa and featured as the local spotlight artist with the American Stage Theater in St. Petersburg, where an exhibition of her work ran in the lobby alongside the play Crimes of the Heart through February 5th. An additional exhibition was scheduled at The Fairgrounds. She is a member of The Portrait Society of America and the Warehouse Arts District Association.\n\nArticles she has written for Creative Pinellas include "Beyond the Exhibition: The Artistic Journey Continues," "Navigating the Artistic Path: A Guide for Emerging Artists," "Embracing Growth: My Journey as an Emerging Artist in 2023," "A Visual Artist\'s Journey of Inspiration," "Tranquil Reflection: Finding Inner Peace through the Thorns of Life," "Resilience Blooms: Surrendering to Growth," "Lotus Unfolding: Embracing Healing and Growth," "The Hollowed Resilience: A Woman Shielded from the Storms of the Past," "Journey to Serenity: Embracing Home amidst Life\'s Trials," "Unfounded Terror: The Petrification of Unhealed Trauma," "Transcendence: A Deep Connection Through Art," "Ophidian Epiphanies: A Journey of Healing and Renewal," "The Transformative Power of Art," and "Difficulty Creates Inspiration. What to paint, what to say?" She was profiled by Robin O\'Dell for The Artisan Magazine in June 2025 in a piece titled "The Original-Jenipher Chandley."\n\nBeyond oil painting and large-scale mural installations, she works across mediums including fashion, choreography, poetry, and public arts installations, framing the work as amplifying the healing power of art and fostering connection between individuals and their surroundings. She describes herself not as a victim but a survivor, and works to instill that mentality in others through her curation and mentorship.\n\nFor SHINE Mural Festival 2021 she painted her first festival mural. For SHINE Origins 2025 she returned with "Stronger Together" at 31st Street and 7th Avenue South, assisted by her daughter Becka.\n\nWebsite: jenipherchandley.com. Instagram: @jenipherchandley. Facebook: jenipher.chandley.',fn:['Runs the Creative Clay mural program','Started painting outside Sake Bomb','Local St. Pete community artist'],src:['https://www.jenipherchandley.com/about-1','https://creativepinellas.org/artist/jenipher-chandley/','https://theartisanmagazine.com/the-original-jenipher-chandley/']},
  {id:167,a:'Jujmo',t:'',loc:'Warehouse Arts District',bldg:'',lat:27.766404,lng:-82.663515,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'jujmo',bio:'Growing up on military bases overseas, Cheryl Weber had almost no access to cartoons. When her family moved back to the States, anime hit her like a freight train — and she decided on the spot to become an artist.\n\nWeber works under the name Jujmo. She is Filipina American, earned her BFA from the University of South Florida in 2013, and went straight into freelancing the day after graduation. Her style is what she calls "cute forward" — saturated color, zero straight lines, and dense compositions where blob creatures, mushroom forms, and smiley-faced amoebas pile on top of each other like a candy-colored coral reef.\n\nShe painted the "B" in the Black Lives Matter street mural in front of the Dr. Carter G. Woodson African American Museum in St. Petersburg for Juneteenth 2020. SHINE picked her twice — 2017 ("Space Rainbows") and 2021.',desc:'A panoramic mural of warm-toned abstract blob creatures flowing diagonally across the wall. The palette is autumnal — orange, teal, brown, pink, and gold. Smiling blob faces with simple dot eyes and wide grins peek out from amorphous organic shapes. Small flowers, sparkles, and circular forms dot the composition. Signed "JUJMO" in the lower right corner. The style is her signature candy-colored character abstraction, here in warmer tones than her typical pastel palette.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/jujmo-blobs.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2017 and 2021 selected artist; Imagine Museum Artist Lecture Series 2024; WEDU Arts Plus PBS feature',fw:null,sbio:'Cheryl Weber — Jujmo — is Filipina American, born to a military family. She earned her BFA from the University of South Florida in 2013. Her style is "cute forward" — saturated color, no straight lines, dense compositions with anime, Japanese folklore, and Filipino folklore influences.\n\nShe painted the "B" in St. Pete\'s BLM street mural at the Woodson Museum, Juneteenth 2020. Imagine Museum Artist Lecture Series 2024. WEDU Arts Plus PBS feature (Season 14). Creative Pinellas listed artist.\n\nSHINE selected her twice: 2017 ("Space Rainbows," 48 ft wide on 1st Ave S) and 2021 (Warehouse Arts District).\n\nInstagram: @jujmo. Website: jujmo.com.',fn:['Military kid — discovered anime after moving to the U.S.','Painted the \'B\' in St. Pete\'s BLM street mural, Juneteenth 2020','Calls her style \'cute forward\' — anime and Filipino folklore influences']},
  {id:86,a:'Leo Gomez',t:'Can\'t Thank You Enough',loc:'744 6th Ave. S.',bldg:'Bayfront Health St. Pete',lat:27.76399667,lng:-82.64393667,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'leogomezstudio',bio:'He grew up drawing alongside his grandmother in Bogota. She was a painter. Hip hop got him into graffiti as a teenager.\n\nLeo Gomez is from Bogota, Colombia, based in St. Petersburg since around 2016. He studied graphic design and trained in hand lettering and typography. His style is bold, clean typography with dreamy color palettes — all hand-drawn before going digital. "Handcrafted Lettering with Soul and Purpose."\n\nClients include Disney Springs, Starbucks, Publix, the Tampa Bay Lightning, Green Bench Brewing, and Tombolo Books. He designed the Tampa Bay Lightning\'s Hockey Talks Night limited-edition puck for mental health awareness (2024) and painted signage at the St. Pete/Clearwater International Airport.\n\nFor SHINE, he painted in both 2019 ("Sunshine on my Mind") and 2021.',desc:'Elegant gold script lettering spelling "Can\'t Thank You Enough" fills the entire wall of a building adjacent to Bayfront Health. The flowing cursive letterforms are painted on a rich navy blue background, with decorative swashes and flourishes extending from each word. "Leo Gomez Studio" is signed in the upper right corner.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/leo-gomez.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2019 and 2021 selected artist',fw:[{name:'leogomezstudio.com',url:'https://www.leogomezstudio.com'}],sbio:'He grew up drawing alongside his grandmother in Bogota. She was a painter. Hip hop got him into graffiti as a teenager.\n\nLeo Gomez was born and raised in Bogota, Colombia. Art was a constant from his earliest memories — his grandmother was a painter, and he was always drawing, coloring, or creating something. As a teenager, his love for hip hop pulled him into graffiti and murals, both of which became foundational influences. He studied graphic design and gained his formal grounding in hand lettering and typography there. He has lived in St. Petersburg, Florida since around 2016 — about eight years as of his January 2024 Tampa Bay Lightning interview — and founded Leo Gomez Studio in 2017.\n\nThe studio specializes in murals, sign painting, hand lettering, public art painting, commercial murals, logo design, and custom lettering for both interior and exterior spaces. Every project is hand-drawn before going digital. His own framing of the discipline: "All of my work is hand drawn before being digital. And what I love about doing things by hand is that it\'s authentic. You can feel it." His style is built around bold, clean typography paired with dreamy color palettes and positive, optimistic messaging. The studio\'s tagline: "Handcrafted Lettering with Soul and Purpose."\n\nHis client roster runs across food, retail, sports, hospitality, and civic projects. Documented clients include Disney Springs, Starbucks, Publix, the Tampa Bay Lightning, the St. Pete/Clearwater International Airport, Green Bench Brewing, Rusty Bellies, Tombolo Books, Blush Tea and Coffee, Boyd Construction, Black Crow Coffee, Grand Central Brewhouse, Yo Mama\'s Foods Co, Central Cycling, Intermezzo, the Hollander Hotel, Wild Roots, The Blend Coffee & Wine, Love Food Central Cafe, Brick Street Farms, Book and Bottle, Crossfit 9, Northeast Orthodontics, The Body Electric Yoga Co, Creative Pinellas, Hawthorne Bottle Shoppe, and Atlas Body + Home.\n\nIn January 2024 the Tampa Bay Lightning Foundation tapped him to design the limited-edition mystery puck for the team\'s fourth annual Hockey Talks Night at AMALIE Arena (Lightning vs. Coyotes), focused on mental health awareness. The Lightning Foundation found his work on Instagram. His puck design centered on the message that "it\'s okay to not feel okay all the time," with a color palette drawn from color psychology to represent inner emotions. He has spoken candidly about why mental health matters to his own practice: "I don\'t think being an artist is easy. We naturally deal with many things internally and if we\'re not in a good place that can stop us from reaching our full potential." He was profiled by Thompson Brandes for TampaBayLightning.com under the "Local Artist Spotlight" series on January 24, 2024.\n\nHe has painted signage at the St. Pete/Clearwater International Airport. He is featured on the Creative Pinellas artist roster, posted October 26, 2021, where he describes himself as "a hand lettering artist, muralist, sign painter, designer, and all around lover of hand crafted art" and frames every project as meant "to communicate a positive message and making a difference."\n\nFor the SHINE Mural Festival, he has been a selected artist twice — 2019 with "Sunshine on my Mind" and again in 2021. The studio books projects one to two months in advance, with hand-painted murals and signs typically taking two to seven days to complete depending on scale and access. The studio operates out of St. Petersburg but travels for projects. Murals are priced individually based on size, complexity, and accessibility of the location.\n\nOutside of lettering and signage, he was experimenting with cement art as of the 2024 Lightning interview. He frames his creative practice around finding balance through working with his hands and through time spent in nature.\n\nWebsite: leogomezstudio.com. Instagram: @leogomezstudio.',src:['https://www.leogomezstudio.com/about','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/leo-gomez','https://www.leogomezstudio.com/murals','https://creativepinellas.org/artist/leo-gomez/','https://www.nhl.com/lightning/news/local-artist-spotlight-leo-gomez']},
  {id:88,a:'Miss Crit',t:'Nowness is the Portal',loc:'3000 Central Ave.',bldg:'Absolutely Beautiful Flowers',lat:27.770714,lng:-82.674029,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'miss_crit',bio:'Her illustration was chosen for the global Creative Mornings theme "Rise" — displayed across 238 cities in 69 countries.\n\nLaura Spencer — Miss Crit — was born and raised in St. Petersburg. She graduated from the Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School, then earned a BFA in Illustration from Ringling College of Art and Design with a semester exchange at Sint Lucas Antwerpen in Belgium. She calls her style "a little bit spooky and a little bit psychedelic."\n\nShe worked as art director at Bealls Department Stores and as an illustrator at Creative Arts Unlimited before going full-time. She co-created the Sunshine City Mosaic — a $50,000 public art installation at Sunset Park. She received the Creative Pinellas Emerging Artist award (2019) and leads SHINE\'s 10th anniversary trolley mural tour.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, she painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'The phrase "NOWNESS IS THE PORTAL" stretches across the wall in large cream-colored serif lettering with a slight Art Nouveau curve to the type. Behind and around the text, a dense arrangement of tropical foliage fills the entire surface — deep green monstera leaves, burgundy ti plants, broad tropical leaves in teal and dark green, and a large white magnolia bloom opening in the upper right. The background is a moody dark teal that makes the plants and flower pop. The whole piece wraps around two second-story windows on the building face.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/miss-crit.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Creative Pinellas Emerging Artist (2019); Creative Mornings global theme illustration (2024); SHINE 2021',fw:[{name:'themisscrit.com',url:'https://www.themisscrit.com'}],sbio:'Her illustration was chosen for the global Creative Mornings theme "Rise" — displayed across 238 cities in 69 countries. Creative Mornings, founded in 2008 in Brooklyn, calls itself the world\'s largest face-to-face creative community and runs free local events, weekly virtual FieldTrips, and job postings via its CreativeGuild. Each month a chapter nominates a one-word theme echoed by every other chapter; the program started in 2013 and has produced 132 themes and illustrations. Miss Crit was selected from a group of illustrations suggested by the Tampa Bay chapter (formerly the St. Pete chapter). She worked with Casey Greenleaf at Creative Mornings headquarters and offered three initial sketches — plants and flowers swaying toward the sun, a glimpse into the human mind with a pathway leading to a rising sun, and a phoenix rising from the ashes trailing the word RISE. The phoenix won. She built the illustration on a Wacom Cintiq Pen Display.\n\nLaura Spencer — Miss Crit — was born and raised in St. Petersburg, Florida. She started formal art training young, attended Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School with a major in Visual Arts in 2007, and earned a BFA in Illustration from Ringling College of Art and Design in Sarasota in 2011. During her time at RCAD she did a semester-long foreign exchange at Sint Lucas Antwerpen — Studio illustratie in Antwerp, Belgium, where classes were held in both Dutch and English; she began designing digitally there. She calls her style "a little bit spooky and a little bit psychedelic" and says she designs with her third-eye wide open, combining traditional fine art techniques, narrative storytelling devices, and digital design technology. Her work spans drawings, paintings, murals, interactive installations, and collaborations.\n\nBefore going full-time she worked as Art Director for Bealls Department Stores and as Illustrator for the exhibit design firm Creative Arts Unlimited, Inc. in Pinellas Park, Florida. She took out a small business loan to declare herself a full-time artist, calling it buying her creative freedom.\n\nShe received the Creative Pinellas Emerging Artist Grant in 2019. Her mentor through that program, Chad Mize, advised her on building a recognizable brand. The nickname Critter came from fellow visual artist Frank Strunk III — given because she is "small, wiry, feisty and eats raw vegetables." She and Mize shortened it and added the retro title "Miss," combining the formal girlie "Miss" with something nicely ambiguous. Miss Crit.\n\nHer partner, sculptor George Retkes, also attended Gibbs and Ringling. They co-founded Artonomy LLC, a design company specializing in sculpture, exhibit design, interactive installation, immersive design, public art, art direction, project management, fabrication, and installation. Artonomy\'s $50,000 public art installation The Sunshine City Mosaic — a collaboration with artist Alex Kaufman — sits at Sunset Park at Central Avenue and Park Street in St. Petersburg. Retkes and Spencer were both Creative Pinellas Emerging Artists in 2019; Kaufman was selected in 2022.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, she painted at Absolutely Beautiful Flowers, 3000 Central, in St. Petersburg. In January 2022 she completed Garden Variety, a 1,700-square-foot mural on the corrugated façade of the Mother Kombucha warehouse at 4360 28th Street N. in the Lealman District, commissioned by Creative Pinellas in association with the Lealman Community Redevelopment Area (CRA). The piece features nine or ten native Florida plants and pollinators — sea grapes, cocoa plums, native blueberries, wild coffee, prickly pear, passion flower — chosen to celebrate edible native plants and sustainable permaculture, aligning with Mother Kombucha\'s mission. Lealman is where she grew up. She painted from a rented green lift through a January cold snap. A solo show opened at the Wayward Goose in June 2022.\n\nOther mural projects listed on her site include Outside In | Dalí, Learning by Living, Centro Asturiano, Lakewood High School, Perkins Elementary, Jamerson Elementary, Mother Kombucha, the PIE Airport (St. Pete-Clearwater International Airport, where she posed in front of her mural in January 2023), the SHINE Mural Festival, The Woodson Museum, The Blueberry Patch, and a Misc. Mural Archive. Her practice categories include Murals, Exhibition, Illustration, Sunshine City Mosaic, Ralph\'s Mob Shop, and Mural Tours.\n\nShe was included in Outside In: New Murals Inspired by Dalí at The Salvador Dalí Museum, May 24 through October 26, 2025, marking the SHINE St. Petersburg Mural Festival\'s 10-year anniversary. The exhibition was curated by Allison McCarthy, Associate Curator and Preparator at The Dalí Museum, and featured current and past SHINE artists creating murals inside the Museum\'s gallery, inspired by Dalí, his collection, and the Museum\'s architecture. The roster included Bakpak Durden, BASK, Chad Mize, Fintan Magee, Greg Mike, Lauren YS, Marina Capdevila, Miss Crit, Naomi Haverland, Nicole Salgar, Palehorse, SHOK-1, and Tes One. The exhibition concluded with YOUR PORTRAIT, a selfie-to-street-art experience, and an AI-powered Dream Tapestry.\n\nShe leads SHINE\'s 10th anniversary trolley mural tour. She has spoken about Art, Wellness and New Year Revolutions at the St. Petersburg Arts Alliance\'s Beyond Arts Business Academy and appeared at CreativeMornings/Tampa Bay at Behar and Peteranecz Architecture, 2430 Terminal Dr S, St. Petersburg. She still takes on a few design clients while focusing on her own painting, plans to stay in St. Pete, and describes herself as a bit of a tomboy who also embraces her femininity.\n\nWebsite: themisscrit.com. Instagram: @miss_crit.',fn:['Illustration chosen for global Creative Mornings','Detailed botanical and nature themes','Fine art meets mural work'],src:['https://creativepinellas.org/directory/laura-spencer/','https://thedali.org/outside-in/','https://www.sunshinecitymosaic.com/','https://www.themisscrit.com/about','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/please-rise-miss-crit/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/artist-laura-spencer-adding-fresh-touch-of-color-to-lealman/']},
  {id:89,a:'Mwanel Pierre-Louis',t:'',loc:'1100 1st Ave. N.',bldg:'Green Bench Brewing',lat:27.771883,lng:-82.649903,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'mwanel1_l',bio:'Stand close: it is a tight portrait of a woman with locs. Step back: the edges have dissolved into pure color. He has built a whole career on that contradiction.\n\nMwanel Pierre-Louis was born in New York to Haitian parents and is based in Miami. He attended the New World School of the Arts in Miami and the illustration program at the Art Center College of Design in Pasadena. He calls himself a New Contemporary artist and illustrator, and his work pulls from the cultures of New York, Miami, and Los Angeles — fragmented experiences and pop references stitched together with a strong emphasis on the relationship between subject and color.\n\nHis client roster runs from sports to streetwear to spirits: Adidas, Adidas Originals, Jack Daniel\'s, Starbucks, Atlantic Records, Epic / Sony Music, Foot Locker, Puma, Equinox, the NBA, the Miami Dolphins, the Florida Panthers, and Formula 1 Miami. He had his first solo with Thinkspace Gallery in Los Angeles in 2019 and has shown with Talon, Antler, Spoke Art, and Nucleus Galleries. His paintings have appeared at Scope Art Fair during Art Basel in Miami and Switzerland, and he participated in POW!WOW! Hawai\'i, including the 5th-anniversary exhibition "Exploring the New Contemporary Movement" at the Honolulu Museum of Art.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, he painted a woman with flowing locs on the side of Green Bench Brewing — face rendered tight, background falling into warm washes of orange and earth.',desc:'A striking portrait of a woman with long locs dominates the side of Green Bench Brewing. The face is rendered with photorealistic detail while the background dissolves into expressive washes of warm oranges, reds, and earth tones. The contrast between the precise portrait work and the loose, painterly surroundings gives the piece real presence from across the street.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/mwanel-pierre-louis.jpeg',from:'Miami, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'Mwanel Pierre-Louis is a Miami-based painter born in New York to Haitian parents. He has lived in and absorbed culture from New York, Miami, and Los Angeles, and his work combines realism and abstraction in a narrative drawn from personal interactions and pop references. His paintings feature juxtapositions of fragmented experiences and a strong emphasis on the relationship between subject and color — photorealistic faces dissolving into loose, expressive abstraction around the edges.\n\nHe attended the high school program at New World School of the Arts in Miami, Florida, and the illustration program at Art Center College of Design in Pasadena, California. He describes himself as a New Contemporary artist and illustrator.\n\nHis client and brand roster includes Adidas, Adidas Originals, Jack Daniel\'s, Starbucks, Atlantic Records, Epic / Sony Music Records, Foot Locker, Puma, Equinox, The Fader Magazine, Create Magazine, Hi-Fructose, Supersonic Art, Booooooom, the NBA, Emirates NBA Cup, the NFL\'s Miami Dolphins, the NHL\'s Florida Panthers, and Formula 1 Miami. POW!WOW! Hawaii also appears among his listed representation and brand affiliations.\n\nHis paintings have been exhibited at Scope Art Fair during Art Basel in Miami and Switzerland, as well as in New York City. He participated in the 5th annual POW!WOW! Hawaii exhibition "Exploring the New Contemporary Movement" at the Honolulu Museum of Art. In 2019 he had his first solo show with Thinkspace Gallery in Los Angeles, and he has continued showing with galleries including Talon, Antler, Spoke Art, and Nucleus Galleries.\n\nDocumented works on his Thinkspace Projects page include "Thoughts" (2017), acrylic, oil, and aerosol on wood panel, 16" x 16", shown in the curated NYC exhibition "LAX / JFK" at Spoke Art; "Dissect #2" (2017), acrylic on wood panel, 12" x 12", shown at SCOPE Miami Beach 2017; "Bonjour" (2018), acrylic on wood panel, 12" x 12", shown at POW!WOW! Hawai\'i 2019 and SCOPE Miami Beach 2018; "Union Pt1" (2019), acrylic on wood, 18" x 24", shown in Alchemy(Project); "Untitled 2" (2019) and "Untitled 3" (2019), both acrylic on wood, 12" x 12", also shown in Alchemy(Project); "It\'s All A Haze" (2020), acrylic on wood panel, 6" x 6", shown in Tapas (Project Room); "I\'ve Seen the Light" (2020), acrylic on wood panel, 12" x 12", shown in The Price of Everything and the Value of Nothing; and "The Conduit" (2022), acrylic on wood panel, 36" tondo, shown in Memories and Manifesting (Gallery II).\n\nHis 2021 SHINE Mural Festival contribution in St. Petersburg, Florida is on the side of Green Bench Brewing. The portrait shows a woman with flowing locs against layered washes of warm color, and demonstrates his signature approach of pairing photorealistic faces with loose, expressive abstraction around the edges.\n\nHis personal website is mwanel.com, which organizes work by year from 2016 through 2025 and includes sections for Digital, Murals, Product, Editorial / Commercial, and Video. Contact email listed on his site is mwanelpierrelouis@gmail.com.',fn:['Miami-based painter, born in New York','Haitian heritage, figurative work','Rich color and cultural storytelling'],src:['https://www.mwanel.com/about','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/Mwanel-Pierre-Louis-2021','https://thinkspaceprojects.com/artists/mwanel-pierre-louis/']},
  {id:90,a:'Nicole Salgar',t:'The Commuter',loc:'615 23rd St S',bldg:'Cimco Tile',lat:27.764672,lng:-82.664528,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'nmsalgar',bio:'She spent seven years designing for Ralph Lauren before she started painting walls.\n\nNicole Salgar graduated from Design and Architecture Senior High in Miami and earned her degree from the Fashion Institute of Technology in New York, specializing in tailoring. She left fashion and started painting. Her first mural was a 2013 collaboration with Chuck Berrett on Manhattan\'s Lower East Side.\n\nHer style blends Latin folk art influences with horror, sci-fi, and cinema aesthetics — vivid colors, high contrast, constantly evolving. She was selected for the Dali Museum\'s "Outside In" exhibition (2025). Her largest wall is a 2.5-story mural in Wynwood with the Pangeaseed foundation on rising sea levels. She has painted in Israel, Mexico, Spain, Cuba, Puerto Rico, and across the US. Clients include Starbucks, Absolut, Lululemon, Steve Madden, and Bacardi.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, she painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A large-scale mural covering the entire side wall of Cimco Tile in a striking limited palette of muted reds, teals, greens, and pinks. The left side features a photorealistic portrait of a young woman gazing directly at the viewer with an intense, contemplative expression. Her face is rendered in soft green and warm tones, with horizontal red striped shadows — like light through blinds — falling across her forehead and the wall behind her. Lush tropical leaves in deep reds and teals frame her face and extend across the right side of the wall, interwoven with chain-link fence imagery and bold geometric red shapes. A large flower blooms on the far right, wrapping around the building\'s roll-up door. Signed "ns" on the left side. The piece explores themes of parallel dimensions and transformation, with the subject serving as a gatekeeper between worlds.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/nicole-salgar.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Dali Museum Outside In (2025); SHINE 2021',fw:null,sbio:'She spent seven years designing for Ralph Lauren before she started painting walls.\n\nNicole Salgar grew up in Miami, Florida, attending art magnet schools from a young age. At 15 she enrolled at Design & Architecture Senior High in the Miami design district to study fashion design, graduating in 1999. She moved to New York City at 18 and earned her bachelor\'s degree from the Fashion Institute of Technology (FIT), specializing in tailoring. For seven years she worked as a freelance and full-time fashion designer for Ralph Lauren, The Gap, Jordache, Sears/Kmart, and independent designers. She also spent three years as an assistant stylist for fashion and product editorial shoots at Sandbox Studios.\n\nAfter realizing office life was not for her, she shifted her focus back to art, curating group shows, pop-ups, and installations across Manhattan and Brooklyn. She set herself the goal of painting a mural, and in 2013 painted her first one — a collaboration with Chuck Berrett through The Centre-Fuge Public Art Project on Manhattan\'s Lower East Side. Conversations with muralists Damien Mitchell and Phetus convinced her she could make a living from public art.\n\nIn 2014, the murder of her brother Louis Salgar brought her back to Miami. Painting became therapy and full-time work. She immersed herself in the local art and music scene, finding early paid opportunities through the hospitality network her brother had been part of. In December 2023 she relocated from Miami to St. Petersburg, citing inconsistent municipal support for working artists in Miami compared to other cities.\n\nHer style blends Latin folk art influences with horror, sci-fi, and cinema aesthetics — vivid colors with abrupt contrast accents, recently leaning on cooler palettes offset by extreme red or hot pink. She cites cinema as her biggest inspiration, naming the films of Panos Cosmatos, Peter Strickland, Nicolas Winding Refn, Pedro Almodóvar, Alfred Hitchcock, and Dario Argento. Her recent personal work includes a series of portraits playing with inter-dimensional existence and multiple realities. She has cited the painter Mr. Aryz as a current influence.\n\nThrough artist residencies and mural festivals, she has painted in Shtula, Israel; Tulum, Mexico; Barcelona, Vitoria, Cadiz, and Sancti Petri, Spain; Havana, Cuba; San Juan, Puerto Rico; New York; Wenatchee, Washington; Atlanta, Georgia; and Charleston, South Carolina. Her largest wall is a roughly 2.5-story, 32-foot-wide mural in the Wynwood Arts District in Miami, painted with the Pangeaseed Foundation to raise awareness of rising sea levels in Florida.\n\nShe runs NS/CB Studio (nscbstudio.com) with her partner Chuck Berrett out of Coral Gables, designing installations, sign painting, faux finishing, and murals for residential and commercial clients. The client list includes Starbucks, Absolut, Tanqueray, Lululemon, Steve Madden, Bacardi, Bombay Sapphire, PBR, Create & Cultivate, BoxyCharm, The Setai Hotel, The Loews Hotel, Aloft Hotel, The Freehand Hotel, Zamas Hotel in Tulum, Bar Nancy in Little Havana, The Anderson, Sweet Liberty, Le Pain Quotidien in Coconut Grove, Tribal Yoga in Tulum, St. Roch in the Design District, D\'Oro in downtown Miami, Madrinas Bar in Gainesville, Wynwood Parlor, LB Smoke Shop, Street Taco NYC, Lizarran America, Technique Records, Gramps Bar, Kilted Mermaid in Vero Beach, El Grito Bar, Boombox Bar, Odd Birds Bar in St. Augustine, Wood Tavern, and The Corner. She painted and installed murals for Steve Madden and BoxyCharm at Create & Cultivate\'s 2019 Vision Summit in Miami, and has done multiple Starbucks projects, including a siren inside a new Miami store. She also collaborated with Hope Murals, a nonprofit bringing art to children in detention centers.\n\nShe built her business through cold-emailing companies, interior decorators, and property owners — operating without an agent, manager, or gallery. Her advice to artists is to avoid branding themselves around a single style, to know the value of their work, and to stick to professional pricing.\n\nIn 2025 she was one of thirteen artists selected for the Dalí Museum\'s Outside In exhibition in St. Petersburg, curated by Associate Curator Allison McCarthy and marking the 10th anniversary of the SHINE St. Petersburg Mural Festival. The show ran through October 26, 2025, with murals inspired by Dalí\'s surrealism installed within the museum\'s walls.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, she painted in St. Petersburg. Her father is an architect; her mother worked for the airline Mexicana, and she traveled extensively to Mexico growing up.\n\nWebsite: nscbstudio.com. Instagram: @nmsalgar and @nscbstudios.',fn:['Seven years designing for Ralph Lauren','Then switched to full-time painting','Colombian-American, fashion-to-art career'],src:['https://jitneybooks.com/nicole-salgar/','https://www.createcultivate.com/blog/how-to-be-an-artist-nicole-salgar','https://voyagemia.com/interview/meet-nicole-salgar-nscb-studio-coral-gables/']},
  {id:91,a:'Reid Jenkins',t:'',loc:'800 31st St. S.',bldg:'St. Pete Distillery',lat:27.762245,lng:-82.67497167,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'reidmjenkins',bio:'He painted his two daughters in sunflowers on the side of a distillery. His son Syre shows up in murals across the rest of the city.\n\nReid Jenkins is a St. Petersburg native. He graduated from the Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School — the same program that produced Derek Donnelly — then earned a BFA from the Maryland Institute College of Art. He tattoos at Fabled Trade Tattoo Company, a queer-owned studio in the Lealman area, and keeps a studio at the ArtsXchange in the Warehouse Arts District.\n\nHis murals tend toward photorealistic portraiture and botanicals. For SHINE 2021 he painted at St. Pete Distillery — the daughters, the sunflowers, and a yellow butterfly between them on a deep teal wall. He has noted that roughly a third of mural pay goes to supplies alone. He collaborated with Iboms and Zulu Painter on "Imagine Gas Plant & Laurel Park" at Campbell Park Recreational Center, and painted the cheetah with geometric 90s-inspired colors at Star Booty Salon on 16th Street South.\n\nFor SHINE Origins 2025, he painted a botanical piece at Founders Pro using a projection method for the outline.',desc:'Two large-scale portraits of young women — the artist\'s daughters — surrounded by lush greenery, golden sunflowers, and cascading white jasmine vines against a deep purple and teal background. A yellow butterfly floats between the figures. Painted across the facade of St. Petersburg Distillery.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/reid-jenkins-distillery.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2021 selected artist; SHINE Origins 2025 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'He paints the people seldom seen in the \'Burg. He also tattoos them.\n\nReid Jenkins is a St. Petersburg native. He graduated from the Pinellas County Center for the Arts (PCCA) program at Gibbs High School, then earned a BFA from the Maryland Institute College of Art (MICA). He is a muralist, painter, illustrator, and tattoo artist. He tattoos at Fabled Trade Tattoo Company (formerly Ink Werkz Tattoo Studio), a queer-owned studio at 4195 54th Ave N in the Lealman area of St. Petersburg. He keeps a studio at the ArtsXchange in the Warehouse Arts District.\n\nHis aim is to "paint the human experience and the experience of those seldom seen in the \'Burg," focusing on capturing small, overlooked moments. His work tends toward photorealistic portraiture and botanicals. He has noted that roughly 33% of mural pay goes to supplies alone. He supports MIZE Gallery, COVE in Pinellas Park, Woodfield Fine Art, and d-gallerie.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, he painted at the St. Pete Distillery — portraits of his two daughters with sunflowers and greenery. He collaborated with Iboms and Zulu Painter on "Imagine Gas Plant & Laurel Park" at Campbell Park Recreational Center. Other murals include the large cheetah with geometric 90s-inspired colors at Star Booty Salon (1741 16th St S). He exhibited in "Renew," a group show with Bekky Beukes at Mergeculture, Tampa (February 2022).\n\nFor SHINE Origins 2025, he painted a botanical piece at Founders Pro, using a projection method for the outline. Instagram: @reidmjenkins.',src:['https://ilovetheburg.com/muralist-reid-jenkins/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/meet-the-shine-mural-festival-class-of-21/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine (2021 Reid Jenkins)','https://stpetecatalyst.com/for-year-11-shine-mural-festival-brings-it-back-home/','https://ilovetheburg.com/st-pete-shine-origins/','https://luciwest.com/photos-from-shine-origins-mural-festival-2025/']},
  {id:92,a:'Ricky Watts',t:'',loc:'2622 Fairfield Ave. S.',bldg:'The Factory St. Pete',lat:27.763627,lng:-82.668705,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'rickywatts',bio:'Google made him their Artist in Residence. He painted the lobby of Building 42 in Mountain View.\n\nRicky Watts was born in 1980 in San Francisco. He studied graphic design at the Art Institute of California in San Diego for two years, then left to pursue fine art. In 2011 he developed his signature "hard-edge gradients" — abstract, fluid shapes with psychedelic color gradients that evolved from letter-based canvas work.\n\nHe has painted fifty-plus large-scale murals since 2004 and mounted over a hundred group exhibitions and twenty solo shows, including at Sotheby\'s Oakland and Modern Eden. Clients include Google, Louis Vuitton, YouTube, Mercedes Benz, IBM, and Outside Lands. He has painted for POW! WOW!, the Las Vegas Circus Center, and Sonic Temple Festival.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, he painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A massive abstract mural wrapping around The Factory St. Pete building. Enormous organic, fluid shapes in bold saturated colors — orange, teal, yellow, deep blue, green, and coral — flow and overlap across the facade like giant brushstrokes frozen mid-motion. The forms are rounded and bulbous, somewhere between liquid ribbons and inflated tubes, creating a sense of constant movement. Palm trees in front of the building interact with the composition. The "Ricky Watts" signature and address "2622 Fairfield Ave S" are visible on the right side. The scale is impressive — the flowing shapes span two stories.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/ricky-watts.jpeg',from:'San Francisco, CA',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Google Artist in Residence; SHINE 2021',fw:[{name:'watts.art',url:'https://watts.art'}],sbio:'Google made him their Artist in Residence. He painted the lobby of Building 42 in Mountain View.\n\nRichard "Ricky" Watts was born in 1980 in San Francisco. As a kid he drew comics, mostly battles between the U.S. Army and nuclear dinosaurs. In school he sold drawings of his classmates\' names in exchange for bartered items, and by high school the side hustle was funding his daily lunches. High school was also when he discovered aerosol art. After graduation he traveled up and down the West Coast painting large-scale pieces on every surface he could find.\n\nIn 2000 he enrolled at the Art Institute of California in San Diego with an emphasis in graphic design. He left after two years and returned to the Bay Area to pursue fine art. His debut solo show "Spontaneous Combustion" opened at Boomerang Gallery in Petaluma in 2004. He worked through several styles before landing on his current "hard-edge gradients" in 2011 — abstract, fluid color shapes that came out of deconstructing the letter forms he was painting on canvas at the time.\n\nHis commission list includes Google, Louis Vuitton, YouTube, Mercedes-Benz, IBM, Anchor Brewing Company, and stage banner designs for the Outside Lands Music Festival in San Francisco — a project he credits with opening the door to the Google and Mercedes-Benz work. He worked with Fogbelt Brewing on label art and donated the original print to Make-A-Wish California.\n\nHis public works run deep. SHINE Mural Festival in St. Petersburg in 2015 (98\' x 37\'), 2017 (36\' x 18\'), and 2021 (100\' x 48\'). POW! WOW! San Jose in 2017 (91\' x 27\') and POW! WOW! Hawai\'i in 2018 with James Bullough (72\' x 20\'). The Las Vegas Circus Center in July 2018, his largest mural to date at the time. Sonic Temple Festival in Ohio. Other festivals and projects: Tampa Walls (2022), Wide Open Walls in Roseville (2022), OuterSpace Project in Atlanta (2015 and 2022), Bright Walls in Jackson, Michigan (2022), Sea Walls in Santa Cruz (2021), Cleveland Walls (2021), Arts Fest Montana in Great Falls (2021), Color The Creek in Battle Creek, Michigan (2021), REN ART WLK in Rensselaer, Indiana (2020), Only Up Mural Festival in Livermore (2020), Burners and BBQ in Asheville (2015), West End Mural Festival in Sand City with BACON (2024), The Mural Festival in Santa Rosa (2023), and the Modesto Children\'s Museum (2023). Other named walls include Church of Clown in San Francisco (2024), AIDS Healthcare Foundation in Berkeley (2024), Senor Grubby\'s in Carlsbad (2023), BREW in Forestville (2023), Fishetarian in Bodega Bay (2023), Tia Maria\'s Panderia in Santa Rosa (2022), Larkin Street at Bay in San Francisco (2021), CAL Bears Mural in Berkeley (2019), Painted Gentlemen at Alamo Square (2019), Riley Street Art Supply in Santa Rosa (2018), Daredevils and Queens in Santa Rosa (2018), The Container Yard in Los Angeles (2015 and 2017), Blacklist Gallery & Tattoo in Portland (2016), Fresh Coat at Jamieson Ranch in Napa (2016), the 1 Brush Initiative in San Francisco (2015), FLAX Art Store in San Mateo (2014), Rocha Gallery (2014), the RAI Care Center collab on Haight Street (2014), and The Phoenix Theater in Petaluma (2013).\n\nSolo shows include "My Own Worst Enemy" at The Epitome Gallery in Eureka (2024), "Deep Within" at Nielsen Arts in Berkeley (2023), "Pursuit of Hoppiness" at Fogbelt Brewing Co in Santa Rosa (2022), "New Paintings by Ricky Watts" at Sotheby\'s Oakland (2019), "The Artwork of Ricky Watts" at Leave Your Mark in Sacramento (2017), "Levitation" at Upper Playground / Fifty24PDX Gallery in Portland (2016), "Home Grown" at Opera House Collective in Petaluma (2015), "VINTAGE" at 1192 Folsom in San Francisco (2014), "Destination Unknown" at ZeroFriends in Oakland (2013), and earlier shows at Buddha\'s Palm in Sebastopol, Starstruck and Mail Depot in Petaluma, Patrick Evan Salon and Chrissy Bee in San Francisco, Triumph in San Rafael, and Daredevils & Queens in Santa Rosa.\n\nGroup exhibitions include "Line Addicts" by Hi-Fructose Magazine at Thinkspace (2024), the SHINE Year 10 Exhibition and SHINE 2021 Exhibition at the Morean Arts Center in St. Petersburg, "The Art of Art" curated by Michael Cuffe at Modern Eden in San Francisco (2021), "I Only Have To Land It Once" at Blackbook Gallery in Denver (2021), Moniker Art Fair in London (2019), "LAX / ORD II" at Vertical Gallery in Chicago (2019), "LAX / SFO Part III" at Heron Arts in San Francisco (2019), POW! WOW! Hawai\'i print and group shows curated by ThinkSpace Projects, "Locals Only" at Mirus Gallery, the 12x12 Group Show at SCOPE Art Fair Miami, "LAX / PDX II" at Antler Gallery in Portland, "Outside In" at Behar + Peteranecz Architecture in St. Petersburg (2017), "Colors | Lines | Layers" at CASS Contemporary in Tampa (2017), "212 Arts Anniversary Exhibit" in New York, "LEGENDS" at Oakland Terminal, "Cultural Xposure" at the San Jose Museum of Art (2006), and "Tetrachromatic" at Arch Enemy Gallery in Philadelphia (2013).\n\nHe works out of a Northern California studio while raising twins. Website watts.art.',src:['https://watts.art/pages/about','https://www.sprayplanet.com/blogs/news/spray-planet-artist-feature-ricky-watts','https://submergemag.com/art/ricky-watts/','https://www.juxtapoz.com/news/pardees-picks-the-works-of-ricky-watts/','https://watts.art/pages/about-ricky-watts','https://thinkspaceprojects.com/artists/ricky-watts/']},
  {id:137,a:'Sydney Prusso',t:'',loc:'2606 Fairfield Ave S',bldg:'Fairgrounds St. Pete (FloridaRAMA)',lat:27.763808,lng:-82.669662,y:2021,cat:'commercial',ig:'sydbloomstudio',bio:'She graduated from MICA and has painted fifty-plus murals across six states, installations up to 75 feet.\n\nSydney Prusso is an illustrator, lettering artist, and muralist based in the Tampa Bay area. Her style is contemporary botanical — flat illustration rooted in graphic design, warm 1960s and 70s color palettes, flowing poster lettering. Nature, the outdoors, and mental health run through the work. She has done a Reef Organization project in the Florida Keys.\n\nClients include Salesforce, Adobe, Dickies, PepsiCo, John Deere, Stihl, the NFL, and AstraZeneca. For SHINE 2022, she painted two murals in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A long horizontal mural covering the side of a low warehouse building in the Fairgrounds / Factory St. Pete complex. The piece is a bold, graphic composition of abstract geometric shapes with hard edges and flat fills in teal, pink, black, maroon, and blue. A large six-pointed teal star dominates the center. To its left, a black field is filled with salmon-pink polka dots. Flowing red and maroon organic shapes weave between diagonal teal stripes and chevrons. Blue teardrop forms, pink arches, and a spiral motif round out the composition on the right. Two small windows are integrated into the design. The style is pop-art influenced with strong graphic sensibility — reminiscent of Memphis Design or 1980s postmodern illustration.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/sydney-prusso-fairgrounds.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2022 selected artist',fw:[{name:'sydbloom.com',url:'https://sydbloom.com'}],sbio:'She graduated from MICA and has painted fifty-plus murals across six states, installations up to 75 feet.\n\nSydney Prusso is an illustrator, lettering artist, and muralist based near Tampa, Florida. She holds a degree from the Maryland Institute College of Art (MICA), brings a graphic-design background to a contemporary botanical style, and now runs the studio under the brand Syd Bloom Studio. The work is flat illustration with warm 1960s and 70s color palettes and flowing poster lettering — nature, the outdoors, and mental health running through it.\n\nHer brand and commercial client list runs through Salesforce, Adobe, Dickies, PepsiCo, John Deere, Stihl, the NFL, and AstraZeneca, alongside named brand projects documented on sydbloom.com. Apothecary commissioned both a mural and a tea-package design. Grow Financial commissioned a multi-store mural installation including the West River, Tampa location. Gallimufry — a vintage furniture store — commissioned a mural plus a full branding package. Fresh Kitchen has a multi-store installation listed as forthcoming. Other commercial clients include Garage Project (the brewery). She also takes residential commissions and out-of-state projects, with testimonials referencing a short-term rental mural in Port Angeles, Washington. The studio\'s tagline for the brand-collabs program: "Part statement piece, part marketing machine. Beautiful on the wall, working for your business every day."\n\nHer illustration practice spans branded packaging, advertising, publishing, surface patterns, and textiles. Documented illustration projects on the studio site include Pom Pattern Portfolio, Good Good Good Newspaper centerfold, Loving, Psithurism, Iris Pattern Portfolio, and Wedding Invite Portfolio.\n\nFor SHINE 2022 in St. Petersburg she painted two murals across the city, including the retro psychedelic "Go Outside, Look Inside" piece at Seriously Strong Training — the title doubles as her practice in three words. She has also done a Reef Organization project in the Florida Keys.\n\nThe Fairgrounds St. Pete wall — this wall, at 2606 Fairfield Ave S in the FloridaRAMA / Factory complex — was painted for the venue\'s September 2021 opening, when Fairgrounds commissioned 60+ artists to cover the building exteriors and interior spaces. The Syd Bloom Studio piece sits among exterior walls by Derek Donnelly, Chad Mize, Zulu Painter, and Ricky Watts. The composition is a bold graphic field rather than her botanical default: a six-pointed teal star at the center, salmon-pink polka dots on a black field to the left, flowing red and maroon organic shapes weaving between diagonal teal stripes and chevrons, blue teardrop forms, pink arches, and a spiral motif on the right, with two small windows integrated into the design. The style reads as Memphis Design or 1980s postmodern illustration filtered through her flat-graphic vocabulary.\n\nShe has rebranded her Instagram and studio practice from @sydneyprusso to @sydbloomstudio (Syd Bloom Studio), with her current style described as "bold, dreamy, dramatic, colorful, high-contrast, and distinctly flat" — a vocabulary that maps directly onto the geometric pop-art register of this Fairgrounds piece. The Salesforce, Adobe, PAN, Shine, and FK logos appear on the about page as documented brand partnerships. Beyond the named clients, the about page positions the studio as one that "transforms spaces and products with her natural worlds" and weaves brand identities into custom flat-illustration compositions.\n\nStudio contact: studio@sydbloom.com. Direct: sydney@sydneyprusso.com. Websites: sydbloom.com (current) and sydneyprusso.com (still active for the artist domain; brand-collabs subpath 301 redirects to sydbloom.com). Instagram: @sydbloomstudio. Copyright on the site reads "© 2026 Sydney Prusso. All Rights Reserved."',src:['https://sydbloom.com/about','https://sydbloom.com/brand-collabs','https://sydbloom.com/illustration','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/sydney-prusso-2022']},
  {id:93,a:'Woes Martin',t:'',loc:'2343 Emerson Ave. S.',bldg:'New Dawn Coffee Co 2',lat:27.765839,lng:-82.665414,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'woes',bio:'His signature character is a stressed-out panda. It represents struggle and perseverance. There is also a hipster Chewbacca.\n\nAaron "Angry Woebots" Martin — Woes — was born and raised on Oahu, Hawaii. Self-taught through graffiti, hip hop, and comics. He paints acrylic on wood and canvas in minimal color palettes with detailed character design, plus aerosol murals, resin sculptures, and vinyl toys.\n\nClients include Kidrobot, Disney, Converse Red, Samsung, New Era, and Fitted Hawaii. He has shown at Urban Nation in Berlin, Comic-Con, Designer Con, and Singapore Toy Con. Festival credits include POW! WOW! Hawaii, Murals in the Market in Detroit, and the Heineken Mural Project.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, he painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A long warehouse wall covered in a pack of oversized panda bears piled on top of each other against a black background. The pandas are rendered in gritty black and white with exaggerated features — bloodshot pink eyes, bared fangs, and massive claws. Some snarl at the viewer while others sleep with one eye cracked open. Bright green bamboo leaves poke through the tangle of fur and limbs. The scale is enormous — each panda is taller than a parked truck.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/woes-martin.jpeg',from:'Los Angeles, CA',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2021 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'His signature character is a stressed-out panda. It represents struggle and perseverance. There is also a hipster Chewbacca.\n\nAaron "Angry Woebots" Martin — also known as Woes Martin or simply Woes — was born on the Hawaiian island of Oahu. He spent most of his childhood in Southern California, then moved to Nevada, before landing back in Hawaii during his junior year of high school. He is self-taught and has no formal art education. His earliest experiences in the graffiti scene came as a kid growing up in Southern California and Las Vegas in the late 1990s. Saturday morning cartoons, kung fu, comic books, and hip hop culture pulled him into making things from an early age, and graffiti followed from there.\n\nIn 2009, Woes picked up a can to paint in public, beginning with the iconic panda characters that became his moniker in cities across the globe. The pandas appear under some kind of emotional stress — detailed, character-driven figures rendered in vibrant or minimal color palettes depending on the piece. Beyond the pandas he also paints a hipster Chewbacca character. His mediums include acrylic paintings on wood and canvas, aerosol murals, resin sculptures, and vinyl toys.\n\nClients include Kidrobot, Disney, Converse Red, Samsung, New Era, Fitted Hawaii, and Mind Style. His work has been published in magazines in the United States, Japan, and Europe. Despite having no formal education himself, Aaron has given lectures at schools and spoken at universities around the US, contributing to youth arts education.\n\nExhibition history includes Urban Nation Museum for Urban Contemporary Art in Berlin, where his work was featured in the 2018 exhibition UN-DERSTAND The Power of Art as a Social Architect. The Urban Nation museum sits at Bülowstrasse 7, 10783 Berlin, Germany. He has also shown at Comic-Con, Designer Con, and Singapore Toy Con.\n\nFestival credits include POW! WOW! Hawaii, Murals in the Market in Detroit, and the Heineken Mural Project. Murals in the Market is run out of 1xRUN at 1410 Gratiot Avenue / 2905 Beaufait St in Detroit, Michigan, and his appearance there placed him alongside artists including Askew, Beau Stanton, Brian Lacey, Denial, Ellen Rutt, Eugene Carland, Fel3000ft, Hannah Stouffer, Hebru Brantley, Hygienic Dress League, Jeff Soto, Maxx242, Jonny Alexander, Kashink, Kobie Soloman, Luke Chueh, Marlo Broughton, Meggs, Michelle Tanguay, Miss Van, Monica Canilao, Naturel, NNII, Noségo, Ouizi, Patch Whiskey, Persue, Kwest, Jarus, Ron Zakrin, Rone, Ryan Doyle, Shaina Kasztelan, Shark Toof, Sydney G. James, Taylor White, and Tead.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, he painted in St. Petersburg.\n\nSearch terms: Woes, Woes Martin, Angry Woebots, Aaron Martin, Aaron Woes Martin, panda artist, stressed panda, hipster Chewbacca, Oahu, Hawaii, Honolulu, Hawaiian artist, Southern California graffiti, Las Vegas graffiti, self-taught, hip hop, comics, kung fu, Saturday morning cartoons, character design, acrylic on wood, acrylic on canvas, aerosol murals, resin sculptures, vinyl toys, Kidrobot, Disney, Converse Red, Samsung, New Era, Fitted Hawaii, Mind Style, Urban Nation Berlin, Urban Nation Museum for Urban Contemporary Art, UN-DERSTAND The Power of Art as a Social Architect, 2018 Urban Nation exhibition, Bülowstrasse, Comic-Con, Designer Con, Singapore Toy Con, POW WOW Hawaii, Murals in the Market, 1xRUN, Detroit, Heineken Mural Project, SHINE 2021, St. Petersburg Florida.',fn:['Signature character: a stressed-out panda','Represents anxiety and modern life','British artist, international murals'],src:['https://woesmartin.bigcartel.com/bio','https://urban-nation.com/artist/woes/','https://www.muralsinthemarket.com/woes']},
  {id:94,a:'Ya La\'Ford',t:'Love Shines',loc:'330 Beach Dr NE',bldg:'Smith & Associates Real Estate',lat:27.77573,lng:-82.632255,y:2021,cat:'shine',ig:'yalaford',bio:'She has an MFA and a law degree. She represented the United States at the Dakar Biennale in Senegal.\n\nYa La\'Ford is a first-generation American of Jamaican heritage, from the Bronx. She earned her MFA from the Art Institute of Boston (now Lesley University) and her Juris Doctor from the University of Florida. She maintains studios in St. Petersburg, the Bronx, Jamaica, and Maine.\n\nHer work spans painting, sculpture, sound, print, video, and immersive installations using light, water, sound, and motion. Sharp geometric lines, bold contrasting colors, vibrant circles and triangles. She sits on the Board of Trustees of the Museum of Fine Arts in St. Petersburg. She designed an Arrow McLaren SP IndyCar and was a featured artist for Super Bowl 2021.\n\nClients include the Tampa Bay Rays, Toronto Blue Jays, and Nike. Her work is in the collections of Tropicana Field, Al Lang Stadium, the Morean Arts Center, and the Boston Public Library. She has shown at The James Museum, The Ringling Museum of Art, and Florida CraftArt.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, she painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A bold graphic mural on a charcoal-gray wall featuring a large heart filled with Ya La\'Ford\'s signature intricate geometric line pattern — dense, angular, interlocking shapes in bright orange-red. The words "Love Shines" are written across the heart in flowing white neon-style script, with a small illuminated element at the top. The heart is centered on the wall with clean negative space around it, creating a striking contrast between the dark background and the vibrant pattern. Signed "Ya La\'ford @yalaford" in white at the bottom. An orange Smith & Associates plaque with the mural\'s backstory is mounted to the lower right. The piece was part of "The Love Line Project," one of three SHINE 2021 Bright Spots community projects — the phrase "Love Shines" was voted on by the public.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2021/ya-la-ford.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'U.S. representative, Dakar Biennale Senegal (2024); Super Bowl 2021 featured artist; Arrow McLaren IndyCar designer; Board of Trustees, Museum of Fine Arts St. Petersburg; SHINE 2021',fw:[{name:'yalaford.com',url:'https://www.yalaford.com'}],sbio:'She has an MFA and a law degree. She represented the United States at the Dakar Biennale in Senegal.\n\nYa La\'Ford is a first-generation American of Jamaican heritage, born and raised in the Bronx. She earned her MFA from the Art Institute of Boston (now part of Lesley University) and her Juris Doctor from the University of Florida, with additional training in engineering. Family shaped her trajectory: her grandfather was an influential figure in the arts, her uncle is a musician with the band Third World, and her godfather wove cultural storytelling into his music. She has lived in Washington DC, Houston, South Florida, and Boston, and has called the Tampa Bay area home for nearly a decade. She lives in St. Pete\'s Historic Kenwood neighborhood and maintains additional studios in the Bronx, Jamaica, and a 30-acre property in Maine where she is building her largest immersive public artwork.\n\nHer practice spans painting, sculpture, sound, print, video, and immersive installations using light, water, sound, and motion. The visual signature is consistent: sharp geometric lines, bold contrasting colors, vibrant circles and triangles, spirals representing unity, change, and growth. Her discovery of large-scale mural work came during a secluded residency when her canvas and materials were lost in delivery — she transferred her canvas-intended patterns to the studio wall and never went back.\n\nShe sits on the Board of Trustees of the Museum of Fine Arts in St. Petersburg. She designed an Arrow McLaren SP IndyCar and was a featured artist at Super Bowl 2021, with work collected by all National Football League Owners as part of the Super Bowl collection. Her client list includes the Tampa Bay Rays, Toronto Blue Jays, and Nike. Her work is held in the collections of Tropicana Field, Al Lang Stadium, the Morean Arts Center, and the Boston Public Library.\n\nDocumented installations and locations across St. Petersburg include the Hyatt entrance, the back entrance of the Morean Arts Center, the entryway of the Rowdies, inside the Rays entryway to the stadium, inside Nike, "The King\'s Dream Unite" (2016) at the Historic Manhattan Casino, "Intersections" wrapping the second level and rooftop terrace of the St. Petersburg Museum of History, the entryway of the James Weldon Johnson Public Library, multiple local schools, the entryway of the Warehouse Arts District, and a blue tunnel leading to the baseball stadium at Tropicana Field. Her largest St. Pete solo exhibition to date is "Survey the West: A Cross-Continental Reflection" at The James Museum.\n\nOther public art and installations include "Boulevard Flow," a sculptural landscape with a labyrinth-shaped park and a 10-foot metal sphere; "The Silver Lining," which debuted at the London Art Fair using light, water, sound, and motion; "American/Rōōts," a mixed-media installation at The John and Mable Ringling Museum of Art; "The Impenetrable Intersection: Jamaican Ja Rasta Visible L\'Invisible" at the Atlantic Center for the Arts (three walls, spray paint, acrylic, gold leaf, and found objects); "Survey: The West" at Ogden Contemporary Arts (2022); and "Distance," a solo full-environmental immersive show at Gallery 221, HCC Gallery. She has large-scale public-art sculptures and installations in Orlando, Gainesville, Pompano Beach, Hawaii, and New Jersey, plus public-art projects in South Africa. International exhibitions include shows in Dubai, Thailand, and China, and a project working with children in Cuba.\n\nGroup and museum shows include The James Museum, The Ringling Museum of Art, Florida CraftArt, the Baker Museum in Naples, Studio@620, Tampa Museum of Fine Arts, and The Florida Museum of Photographic Arts. Joanna Robotham, Curator of Modern and Contemporary Art at the Tampa Museum of Art, has spoken publicly about watching her work grow over the past eight years.\n\nA defining moment came in November when she represented the United States at the Dakar Biennale in Senegal, hosted by the U.S. Embassy. She is preparing for an artist residency with the Urban League in Ghana. She is a wife, mother of two, and pet parent, and is working on more than 100 concurrent projects. She has spoken publicly about future installations incorporating rare metals, gems, and augmented and virtual reality.\n\nFor SHINE Mural Festival 2021, she painted in St. Petersburg.\n\nWebsite: yalaford.com. General contact: ya@yalaford.com. Press: contact@yalaford.com. The Creative Pinellas "Arts In" podcast episode profiling her was published February 25, 2019, hosted by Sheila Cowley with executive producer Barbara St. Clair for Creative Pinellas.',src:['https://www.yalaford.com/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/local-ish-meet-artist-ya-laford/','https://tampamagazines.com/ya-lafords/','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/arts-in-ya-laford/','https://lesley.edu/stories/ya-laford']},
  {id:98,a:'Brian Butler',t:'',loc:'1101 1st Ave N',bldg:'MUV Dispensary',lat:27.772439,lng:-82.649909,y:2020,cat:'shine',ig:'upperhandart',bio:'He drew over two thousand live concert performances from the audience. He calls the series "Show Drawn."\n\nBrian Butler is based in Miami. He studied at the Massachusetts College of Art and Design. His style is playful, illustrative, bold color — driven by music. He adapts to darker, grittier work for serious subjects. He founded The Miami Palmist independent music publication and toured colleges with Microsoft and Cut & Paste.\n\nFestival credits include SXSW, Bonnaroo, Governors Ball, Riot Fest, Ultra, and the Motorhead Motorboat. Clients include Vice, Target, Tumblr, Red Bull, Converse, the Miami Heat, Pabst Blue Ribbon, Vans, Live Nation, and Liquid Death. Music clients: Wu Tang Clan and The Mighty Mighty Bosstones.\n\nFor SHINE 2020 / Sea Walls, he painted "Carbon Punishment" in the Edge District — an enviro-METAL multimedia project combining a mural with grindcore music tracks.',desc:'A large-scale nature scene wraps the MUV Dispensary building, featuring dense tropical foliage, birds in flight, and flowing organic linework. The composition covers multiple faces of the building, creating an immersive green canopy effect. Bold outlines define the plant forms while softer color fills give the whole piece a hand-illustrated, almost storybook quality.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2020/brian-butler.jpeg',from:'Miami, Florida',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2020 / Sea Walls selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'He drew over two thousand live concert performances from the audience. He calls the series "Show Drawn." The work has been exhibited at iiiPoints 2023, the Coral Springs Museum, and in the "Odd Cod" Cape Cod exhibition, and has been compiled into a Show Drawn 10 Year Book.\n\nBrian Butler is a Miami-based illustrator, muralist and designer. Before moving to South Florida he studied at the Massachusetts College of Art and Design. Early in his career he toured colleges across the country with Microsoft and Cut & Paste, a global design tournament, while simultaneously launching a parallel practice as a muralist and illustrator. His style is playful, illustrative, bold color and music-driven; he adapts to darker, grittier work for serious subjects.\n\nButler founded and spearheaded The Miami Palmist, an independent music publication, and has organized group exhibitions and consulted on artist-driven activations led by major brands and agencies. His studio operates as Upper Hand Art Studio, reachable at BrianButler@upperhandart.com.\n\nFestival illustration credits include SXSW, Bonnaroo, Governors Ball, Riot Fest, Ultra, the Motorhead Motorboat, Levitate Music Festival, III Points, and Wigwood. Brand and commercial clients include Vice, Target, Tumblr, Red Bull, Converse, Superfly, the Miami Heat, Pabst Blue Ribbon, Vans, Live Nation, Liquid Death, Heineken, Shake Shack, Sesame Street, Lenovo, Girl Skateboards, Shoe Palace, McCann Worldgroup, and the Los Angeles Football Club. Music clients include the Wu Tang Clan and The Mighty Mighty Bosstones, with additional projects for Tove Lo, Pearl Jam (gigposter mural), New Found Glory\'s "Makes Me Sick," Gouge Away (for Vans), Erick the Architect, GZA (album art), and Little Dragon (with Tumblr IRL).\n\nFor SHINE 2020 / Sea Walls he painted "Carbon Punishment" in the Edge District, an enviro-METAL multimedia project combining a mural with grindcore music tracks. Aventura Mall holds his mural "Paradise and Fries," located near the Treats Food Hall.\n\nOther named projects and walls include i.d. Show Drawn; a Tattoo Mural; Secret Project x III Points; Space Park; work at Felix Varela Senior High with BAGHEAD; OVER UNDER; POW WOW Hawaii with BAGHEAD; SHELVIS PAINTING; MIAMI ROADSIDE DRAWING; an Interactive Mural at III Points; Atlanta painting; Detroit painting; Miami Palmist Concert Flyers; a Colombia Zine; an AR / 3D Residency at Montserrat College of Art; a Seinfeld Mural; Target Miami; the Miami Heat work; Small Press Fair; Shake Shack at Aventura Mall; Flagler St for Art Basel 2017; Denver Crush Walls with BAGHEAD; POW WOW Worcester; Heineken\'s Restoring Miami Marine Stadium; RBSS – 3 Days in Miami; Parts Unknown – Flag Show; Secret Walls; Blacklight Mini Golf Poster; a Puerto Rico project; an NTL CRU mural; Dirty Decks at LIV Miami; United States of Bass: Miami; the Shoe Palace HQ mural in San Jose; a Carol City Middle School mural; a Paraguay Zine; Gigi\'s; the III Points Mural; Converse Rubber Tracks Live Boston; the McCann Worldgroup work; the Duck Show in LA; Miami Beach Pabst Mural; the Double Vision Concert Art Exhibit; a Tokyo / Bangkok Zine; and "GET LOST OPENING." His work has been featured in Juxtapoz, Summer 2019.\n\nSearch keywords: Brian Butler, Upper Hand Art Studio, Miami illustrator, Miami muralist, Miami designer, Massachusetts College of Art and Design, MassArt, Cut & Paste design tournament, Microsoft college tour, The Miami Palmist, Show Drawn, Show Drawn 10 Year Book, iiiPoints, III Points, Coral Springs Museum, Odd Cod Cape Cod, Levitate Music Festival, SHINE 2020, Sea Walls, Carbon Punishment, Edge District St. Petersburg, enviro-METAL, grindcore mural, Paradise and Fries, Aventura Mall, Treats Food Hall, BAGHEAD collaboration, POW WOW Hawaii, POW WOW Worcester, Denver Crush Walls, Felix Varela Senior High, Carol City Middle School, Miami Marine Stadium restoration, Heineken, Shake Shack Aventura Mall, Flagler St Art Basel 2017, Secret Project, Space Park, Tattoo Mural, Seinfeld Mural, Los Angeles Football Club, Target, Tumblr, Vice, Red Bull, Converse, Superfly, Miami Heat, Pabst Blue Ribbon, Vans, Live Nation, Liquid Death, Lenovo, Tove Lo, Girl Skateboards, Shoe Palace San Jose, Sesame Street, McCann Worldgroup, Wu Tang Clan, Mighty Mighty Bosstones, New Found Glory Makes Me Sick, Gouge Away, Pearl Jam gigposter, Erick the Architect, GZA album art, Little Dragon, Converse Rubber Tracks Live Boston, Duck Show LA, NTL CRU, Dirty Decks LIV Miami, United States of Bass, Blacklight Mini Golf, Secret Walls, Small Press Fair, Parts Unknown Flag Show, Double Vision Concert Art Exhibit, Juxtapoz Summer 2019, Montserrat College of Art AR 3D Residency, Wigwood, Bonnaroo, SXSW, Riot Fest, Ultra, Governors Ball, Motorhead Motorboat, Colombia Zine, Paraguay Zine, Tokyo Bangkok Zine, Gigi\'s, GET LOST OPENING, Miami Beach Pabst Mural, Roadside Drawing, Atlanta painting, Detroit painting, Puerto Rico, music-driven illustration, playful bold color, dark gritty work. Contact: BrianButler@upperhandart.com.',fn:['Drew over 2,000 live concert performances','From the crowd, with a sketchbook','San Francisco-based illustrator'],src:['https://upperhandart.com/','https://mieuxmagazine.com/brian-butler-the-upper-hand-in-art/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2020_SHINE_Mural_Brian_Butler','https://arts.aventuramall.com/brian-butler','https://www.juxtapoz.com/news/news/show-drawn-10-years-of-concert-drawing-with-brian-butler/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/brian-butler-2020']},
  {id:101,a:'IBOMS',t:'Where the Bloom Begins',loc:'2324 Central Ave',bldg:'Grand Central Brewhouse',lat:27.770823,lng:-82.66536,y:2020,cat:'shine',ig:'i.b.o.m.s',bio:'His solo show "Ego Death" at Mize Gallery sold out in 48 hours. IBOMS stands for "illegal. Beauty. On. My. Streets."\n\nJabari Jahi Babatu Reed-Diop — IBOMS — is a St. Petersburg native. He attended Douglas Anderson School of the Arts in Jacksonville and the Gibbs High PCCA Magnet Program. His signature character "Ashi" — a young Black male — was drawn from his childhood in south St. Pete. He uses the "iceberg theory": celebratory surface, deeper narratives underneath.\n\nHe works in 2D illustration, 3D sculpture, and digital design. His recent work features mushroom-headed characters exploring "Ego Death." He has shown at d-Gallerie and Mize Gallery, designed a Tampa Bay Rays Artist Print Series (2019), and co-created a PSTA bus wrap with St. Pete Youth Farm.\n\nFor SHINE 2020, he painted "Where the Bloom Begins" at Grand Central Brewhouse. He returned as a SHINE 2021 Bright Spots artist.',desc:'A large cartoon-style character dominates the bright yellow wall — a tattooed figure with blue and gray braided hair, gritted teeth, and an intense sideways glare. The word "BLOOMS" is tattooed across its chest. The character is surrounded by bulbous, cloud-like brown shapes that pile up around the base, with a wavy white line suggesting water and small fish swimming along the bottom edge. The IBOMS tag sits in the upper right corner. Stars and swirl details float above the character\'s head.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2020/iboms.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'Created in partnership with the Pangeaseed Foundation\'s Sea Walls program to address Florida\'s red tide and harmful algal bloom crisis.',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2020 and 2021 selected artist; Tampa Bay Rays Artist Print Series',fw:null,sbio:'His solo show "Ego Death" at Mize Gallery sold out in 48 hours. IBOMS stands for "illegal. Beauty. On. My. Streets." Jabari Jahi Babatu Reed-Diop — IBOMS — is a St. Petersburg native based in St. Petersburg, Florida. His artistic acumen comes from personal experience and formal education at Douglas Anderson School of the Arts in Jacksonville and the Gibbs High PCCA Magnet Program in St. Petersburg.\n\nHis signature character "Ashi" — a young Black male — was drawn from his childhood in south St. Pete. The name "ASHI" appears in capital letters below the character\'s left eye on his SHINE 2020 mural. IBOMS uses the "iceberg theory" in his practice: a celebratory surface with deeper narratives running underneath, like a dark river beneath a blissful exterior. His characters are complex and often represent aspects of his psyche as a young Black male, transmuting emotional and physical states of being to mirror the relationships between spirit and god. His work covers themes of self-love, destruction, discovery, omens, and love, and aims to give his community a lens into the experience of an African American male in the Sunshine City.\n\nHe works across multiple mediums: 2D illustration, 3D sculpture, and digital design. At first glance his work may read as simple illustrations or graffiti, but each piece carries a deeper message. His recent work features mushroom-headed characters exploring "Ego Death." He has shown at d-Gallerie and Mize Gallery (also styled MIZE Gallery) in St. Petersburg. His show with Chad Mize at Mize Gallery sold out within 48 hours of its debut, with select pieces remaining for sale at d-Gallerie afterward.\n\nHe designed a Tampa Bay Rays Artist Print Series in 2019. A portrait of IBOMS by muralist and curator Derek Donnelly is painted in the alley behind the 600 Block of Central Avenue in St. Petersburg.\n\nFor SHINE 2020, he painted "Where the Bloom Begins" on the side of the then-new Grand Central Brewhouse in St. Petersburg. The mural is set back from the street and takes up the right half of the west-facing wall, measuring 30 feet high and 36 feet wide. The background is a brilliant yellow. The center of the image features Ashi, towering over the wall with his back to the viewer, looking over his left shoulder and down. Ashi\'s skin is greyish, with a bright red nose, pink circles on his cheeks, and dark grey tattoos on his face, neck, and back.\n\nHe returned to SHINE in 2021 as a Bright Spots artist. For that project he partnered with the Pinellas Suncoast Transit Authority (PSTA) and the St. Pete Youth Farm on "Together We Grow," a custom PSTA bus wrap designed by IBOMS together with the students of the St. Pete Youth Farm. The final wrap was revealed on October 23, 2021.\n\nIBOMS was profiled in the Tampa Bay Born Murals video series from I Love the Burg and That\'s So Tampa, made possible by Neptune Flood Insurance CEO Trevor Burgess and his husband Gary Hess in partnership with Metro Inclusive Health. The October 11, 2021 feature by Andrew Harlan covered the Grand Central Brewhouse mural and his SHINE 2021 Bright Spots role.\n\nHe is also listed as a Creative Pinellas artist, with a profile posted March 28, 2023, identifying him as based out of St. Petersburg, Florida. Despite constantly pushing his craft and experimenting with new mediums, his style is immediately recognizable. Recent articles authored by IBOMS on the Creative Pinellas platform include "Imagine Nation," "Trustpassing," "The power of silence," "Reedr of Hearts Bearer of Watts," "Music influence," "Anime influence," "Logos & Commissions," "The Power Of Illegal Beauty On My Streets Compels You," "Trails of Sean," "Even a open book has pages you can\'t Reed," and "Intro to iBOMS."\n\nKeywords and associations: Jabari Reed-Diop, Jabari Jahi Babatu Reed-Diop, IBOMS, iBOMS, i.b.o.m.s, illegal Beauty On My Streets, St. Petersburg Florida, south St. Pete, Sunshine City, Douglas Anderson School of the Arts, Jacksonville, Gibbs High PCCA Magnet Program, Ashi character, iceberg theory, Ego Death, mushroom-headed characters, 2D illustration, 3D sculpture, digital design, African American male experience, self-love, destruction, discovery, omens, Mize Gallery, MIZE Gallery, d-Gallerie, Chad Mize, Tampa Bay Rays Artist Print Series 2019, Derek Donnelly portrait, 600 Block of Central Avenue, SHINE Mural Festival 2020, Where the Bloom Begins, Grand Central Brewhouse, SHINE 2021 Bright Spots, Pinellas Suncoast Transit Authority, PSTA bus wrap, St. Pete Youth Farm, Together We Grow, October 23 2021 reveal, Tampa Bay Born Murals, I Love the Burg, That\'s So Tampa, Neptune Flood Insurance, Trevor Burgess, Gary Hess, Metro Inclusive Health, Andrew Harlan, Creative Pinellas. Follow IBOMS on Instagram for the latest work.',fn:['Solo show \'Ego Death\' sold out in 48 hours','Bold graphic portraits and florals','St. Pete local artist'],src:['https://creativepinellas.org/directory/jabari-reed-diop/','https://seawalls.org/murals/where-the-bloom-begins/','https://creativepinellas.org/directory/jabari-reed-diop/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2020_SHINE_Mural_IBOMS','https://creativepinellas.org/artist/jabari-reed-diop-i-b-o-m-s/','https://ilovetheburg.com/tampa-bay-murals-iboms/']},
  {id:102,a:'Kenny Coil and Marc Berenguer',t:'',loc:'1000 3rd St S',bldg:'USFSP Harbor Hall',lat:27.76005333,lng:-82.637055,y:2020,cat:'shine',ig:'kennycoil',bio:'Marc designed for brands in Barcelona before crossing the Atlantic. Kenny is a USF graduate. They opened Break Maiden together in 2018.\n\nKenny Coil and Marc Berenguer run Break Maiden, a branding and design studio at 33 6th Street South in St. Petersburg. Both are USF graduates. Their work spans brand identity, packaging, illustration, and murals. Marc moved from Barcelona to the US in 2009, bringing a love of typography and minimal design.\n\nClients include Green Bench Brewery (Webb City Cellar branding) and MADE Coffee. For SHINE 2020 / Sea Walls, they painted a mangrove and coastal wetlands mural on Harbor Hall at USF St. Pete, with embedded information viewable via the Pixelstix app.',desc:'A full-building mural in a flat, graphic illustration style depicting Florida nature and neighborhood life. The composition is divided into loose grid-like sections filled with stylized herons, egrets, pelicans, tropical fish, palm trees, mangroves, and colorful downtown buildings in teal, pink, yellow, orange, and sage green. The shapes are rounded and geometric with a mid-century modern feel — puffy trees with scalloped edges, simplified houses with bright facades, and birds rendered as elegant silhouettes. A large white egret dominates the lower right. The overall palette is warm and coastal, giving the building a cheerful, welcoming presence.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2020/kenny-coil-and-marc-berenguer.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, Florida',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2020 / Sea Walls selected artist',fw:[{name:'breakmaiden.co',url:'https://www.breakmaiden.co'}],sbio:'Marc designed for brands in Barcelona before crossing the Atlantic. Kenny is a USF graduate. They opened Break Maiden together in 2018.\n\nKenny Coil and Marc Berenguer run Break Maiden, a branding and design studio at 33 6th Street South in St. Petersburg. Both are USF graduates. Their work spans brand identity, packaging, illustration, and murals. Marc moved from Barcelona to the US in 2009, bringing a love of typography and minimal design.\n\nClients include Green Bench Brewery (Webb City Cellar branding) and MADE Coffee. For SHINE 2020 / Sea Walls, they painted a mangrove and coastal wetlands mural on Harbor Hall at USF St. Pete, with embedded information viewable via the Pixelstix app.',src:['https://www.breakmaiden.co/about','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/kenny-coil-with-marc-berenguer/']},
  {id:147,a:'Kosharekart',t:'',loc:'2153 1st Ave S',bldg:'Coastal Creative',lat:27.770012,lng:-82.662501,y:2020,cat:'commercial',ig:'kosharekart',bio:'She spent thirteen years as a stay-at-home mom with four kids before becoming a full-time artist. Her work is in the Smithsonian.\n\nJennifer Kosharek — Kosharekart — is a formally trained artist who studied at the Pinellas County Center for the Arts and holds a BA in English. She lived in St. Petersburg for over forty years before relocating to Alaska in 2022 to pursue a lifelong dream. She started as a realistic oil portrait painter, then gave herself "permission to paint unrealistically."\n\nHer signature character Gretchen — a big-eyed whimsical female figure — was born around 2008, described as "a type of Vivian Girl who fights for the lives and rights of children and women." She learned spray painting after meeting street artist Narchy in Calgary and was described as "the only female in St. Pete doing spray paint street art" at the time. She owned eve-N-odd Gallery in St. Petersburg from 2011 to 2014, hosting thirty-plus shows.\n\nHer work is held in the Smithsonian and MOMA collections in New York, Wales, and Brazil through the mail art community. In December 2024 she spray-painted "Hope" and her Gretchen character on a beached sailboat at North Shore Park after Hurricane Helene.',desc:'A large black-and-white mural spanning the side wall of the Coastal Creative building. Two stylized female figures anchor the composition — one on the left with flowing ribbon-like hair and a potted plant atop her head, the other on the right wearing a braided hairstyle topped with a cactus and leafy branches. Between them, a toucan perches with its curved beak reaching upward. The figures are surrounded by densely packed flowers, leaves, birds, and organic swirling patterns, all rendered in bold black line work against the white wall. A small "20" date mark is visible near the left figure. The mural wraps around a centered garage door.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2020/kosharekart.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:[{name:'kosharek.art',url:'https://kosharek.art'}],sbio:'She spent thirteen years as a stay-at-home mom with four kids before becoming a full-time artist. Her work is in the Smithsonian.\n\nJennifer Kosharek — Kosharekart — studied at the Pinellas County Center for the Arts and holds a BA in English. She is trained in realistic oil portraiture, intaglio printmaking, and various other media. She lived in St. Petersburg for 40-plus years before relocating to Alaska in early 2022. She is a multi-disciplinary artist: painter, spray paint muralist, rag doll maker, nesting doll painter, quilt maker, illustrator, mail artist, Fluxus performance artist, fiber artist, and graphic designer. Mother of four.\n\nHer signature character "Gretchen" (born circa 2008) is a big-eyed whimsical female figure — "a type of Vivian Girl who fights for the lives and rights of children and women." She writes hidden words, poems, and sayings onto canvases before covering them with her final creation. She learned spray painting after meeting street artist Narchy in Calgary, AB. She was described as "the only female in St. Pete doing spray paint street art" at that time and helped start the city\'s mural scene. Her first spray paint mural was "Gretchen and Spoonman" with Christian Thomas.\n\nShe owned and operated eve-N-odd Gallery in St. Petersburg (January 2011 to January 2014), hosting 30-plus shows. She held a solo exhibition "Woman\'s Work" at the Morean Arts Center (October 2020) as part of a SHINE prequel pop-up. In August 2022 she completed a residency at the Wrangell Mountains Center in McCarthy, Alaska. In December 2024 she spray-painted "Hope" on a beached sailboat at North Shore Park after Hurricane Helene (covered by FOX 13). She published "Kosharek Art: Whimsical Art for the Masses" via Blurb.\n\nHer work is held in the Smithsonian (via mail art historian John Held Jr.\'s archive) and MOMA collections in New York, Wales, and Brazil. She has participated in Free Art Friday for 10-plus years and The Sketchbook Project.\n\nInstagram: @kosharekart. Website: kosharek.art.',src:['https://voyagemia.com/interview/meet-jennifer-kosharek-kosharek-art-st-petersburg-fl/','https://canvasrebel.com/meet-jennifer-kosharek/','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/bees-in-the-hive-discovering-mail-art-with-jennifer-kosharek/','https://www.fox13news.com/news/artist-uses-beached-sailboat-st-pete-spread-message-hope-after-hurricanes']},
  {id:103,a:'Lili Yuan',t:'',loc:'Grand Central District',bldg:'ARTE',lat:27.771386,lng:-82.655009,y:2020,cat:'shine',ig:'oliooolio',bio:'She left Hangzhou, China at thirteen for education. Each of her paintings takes over a hundred hours.\n\nLili Yuan is based in Jacksonville, Florida. She studied economics in China, then took art classes at Florida State College at Jacksonville after immigrating. She is largely self-taught. Her style is hyperrealism in oil — rich tones and strong physical presence, exploring the dichotomy of Eastern and Western cultures. Her "water series" is a recurring body of work.\n\nFor SHINE 2020 / Sea Walls, she painted "Gift" at 1661 Central Avenue — a woman in a red dress floating in blue water with the Chinese idiom meaning "Be like water."',desc:'A large circular composition on a cream-colored wall depicting a woman with long dark hair floating in turquoise water, seen from above. She wears a flowing red dress with deep pleats that billow and spread around her like a blooming flower. The water is rendered with photorealistic light caustics and ripples. Her hands gently push against the fabric as her hair trails out beyond the circle\'s edge. To the left, bold Chinese calligraphy reads "shang shan ruo shui" (the highest good is like water) — a passage from the Tao Te Ching. A small red seal stamp appears at the bottom left corner. The @oliooolio tag is visible near the circle\'s edge.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2020/lili-yuan.jpeg',from:'Jacksonville, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2020 / Sea Walls selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'She left Hangzhou, China at thirteen for education. Each of her paintings takes over a hundred hours.\n\nLili Yuan is based in Jacksonville, Florida, and works as a painter and part-time photographer. She was born and raised in China and has been away from home since age thirteen for a better education — first moving from south to north China, then to different countries before settling in the U.S. about three years before her January 2020 VoyageMIA interview. She studied economics in China before changing careers entirely after immigrating to the United States. After moving to the U.S., she took a painting class at Florida State College at Jacksonville and found her way into art from there. She is largely self-taught. Each piece of her work takes more than 100 hours to complete.\n\nHer style is hyperrealism in oil. She uses the rich tones that oils provide to give her paintings a strong physical presence, paired with skill at rendering realistic figures and a sharp eye for manipulating proportion and scale. Her paintings are often based on her own photography — she combines photographic techniques, international cultural perspectives, and original content. Most of her work is rooted in her own life, especially the period after moving from China, and the dichotomy between Eastern and Western cultures pushed her to reevaluate the value of life. She came to be known for her "water series," which was the starting point of her art journey.\n\nHer mentor and manager is Dustin Harewood, who found her early in her practice and has helped guide and promote her work. Her parents — described as traditional Chinese parents who gave her unusual freedom — supported her career change from economics to art.\n\nHer gallery and online presence is Lili.Gallery (lili.gallery), with sections for About, Artwork, Photography, Shop, and Contact. Documented earlier press includes the January 2020 VoyageMIA feature "Meet Lili Yuan of Lili.Gallery in Jacksonville," the November 2019 VOID Magazine piece "The Art We Need: With first solo show, Lili Yuan seizes the day," and the July 2019 MiEUX Mag profile "Lili Yuan: Self Liberation Through Art." In early 2019 she completed Paws Park Dog Park public art in Jacksonville.\n\nFor SHINE Mural Festival 2020 / Sea Walls in St. Petersburg, Florida, she painted "Gift" at 1661 Central Avenue (Artistry St. Pete). The piece depicts an obscured female figure surrounded by water with the Chinese idiom "上善若水" — meaning "Be like water, as water stays humble while benefiting all." Her artist statement frames the work as a response to the millions of gallons of wastewater spilled around Tampa Bay in recent years. She chose a simplified design because the value of water is precious enough to resonate on its own. Holding water in hand symbolizes a precious gift from nature, since water sustains all life no matter how big or small, and the round shape represents nature\'s best gift — Earth itself. The piece was painted November 7-14, 2020, as part of the Sea Walls activation by PangeaSeed Foundation, a 501(c)(3) non-profit, with a focus on water quality. Sea Walls partnered with the SHINE Mural Festival for the 2020 edition.\n\nThe "Gift" mural is documented in the Sea Walls catalog at seawalls.org/mural/gift/. PangeaSeed Foundation lists the focus as Water Quality and the activation as St. Petersburg, November 7-14, 2020.\n\nWebsite: lili.gallery. Email contact is listed on the gallery site.',fn:['Left Hangzhou at thirteen for education','Each mural tells a story of displacement','Delicate, dreamlike figurative work'],src:['https://voyagemia.com/interview/meet-lili-gallerry-jacksonville/','https://stpetemuraltour.com/2021/03/01/lili-yuan/','https://www.cummermuseum.org/event/virtual-conversation-artists-dustin-harewood-crystal-floyd-and-lili-yuan','https://lili.gallery/about/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/lili-yuan/','https://seawalls.org/mural/gift/']},
  {id:104,a:'Mason Schwacke',t:'Prosperitas',loc:'1400 3rd St S',bldg:'Hawk Diesel',lat:27.75643,lng:-82.636938,y:2020,cat:'shine',ig:'masonschwacke',bio:'Four threatened Florida species. Tropical Storm Eta tried to wash him off the wall. He finished anyway.\n\nMason Schwacke is a St. Petersburg-based muralist who has been painting around Tampa Bay for over a decade. His style sits at the intersection of street art and contemporary illustration — bold, illustrative, leaning hard on color and on characters that feel like they wandered out of a cartoon and into a conservation textbook. He has exhibited at the Morean Arts Center and shown work at Portspace Gallery in Gulfport.\n\nFor SHINE 2020, the festival partnered with PangeaSeed Foundation\'s Sea Walls program for an ocean conservation edition. Schwacke teamed up with C.J. Thomas (Christian Thomas of Commitment Tattoo Shop) and developed the concept in consultation with NOAA. The sawfish is endangered; the other three are threatened. He titled the piece "Prosperitas" — for the future he hoped they could still have. Eta hit on Wednesday, November 11, 2020, costing him 24 hours mid-week. He finished on time.',desc:'An underwater scene spanning a long wall. A giant manta ray dominates the left side in deep teal and navy blue with curling tentacle-like forms. At center, a scalloped hammerhead shark dives downward through shafts of light breaking through the water. A smalltooth sawfish with its distinctive elongated snout swims along the coral-covered bottom. An oceanic whitetip shark cruises in from the right. The seafloor is rendered in warm coral, red, and brown tones with brain coral, sea fans, and sponges. Dark navy water fills the upper portion with silhouettes of fish schools and rays of light. Signed @masonschwacke and @cthomascustoms 2020.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2020/mason-schwacke.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'Painted in partnership with PangeaSeed Foundation and NOAA to highlight threatened local marine species and ocean conservation.',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'Mason Schwacke is a St. Petersburg, Florida-based muralist. He has been painting murals in the Tampa Bay area for over a decade. He has exhibited at the Morean Arts Center and shown work at Portspace Gallery in Gulfport. He is also associated with the Art Asylum creative community.\n\nFor SHINE 2020, the festival partnered with PangeaSeed Foundation\'s Sea Walls program. Schwacke teamed up with C.J. Thomas (Christian Thomas) of Commitment Tattoo Shop and developed the concept in consultation with NOAA, painting four marine species at risk off the Florida coast: the scalloped hammerhead shark, oceanic whitetip shark, giant manta ray, and smalltooth sawfish. The sawfish is endangered; the other three are threatened. He titled the piece "Prosperitas."\n\nThe wall at Hawk Diesel (1400 3rd St S) is 12 feet tall and 43 feet wide. Tropical Storm Eta caused a 24-hour rain delay on Wednesday, November 11, 2020, during SHINE week.\n\nInstagram: @masonschwacke.',fn:['Titled \'Prosperitas\' — Latin for prosperity','Fought Tropical Storm Eta mid-festival to finish','Partnered with NOAA for the species science'],src:['https://stpetecatalyst.com/a-little-rain-wont-stop-shine-artist-mason-schwacke/','https://seawalls.org/activation/st-petersburg/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/mason-schwacke-2020','https://moreanartscenter.org/exibition/shine-year-10-exhibition/','https://yaleclimateconnections.org/2020/11/meandering-eta-makes-fourth-landfall-this-time-northwest-of-tampa-florida/','https://www.tampabay.com/hurricane/2020/11/12/tropical-storm-eta-leaves-tampa-bay-staggering-a-reminder-of-the-regions-risk/']},
  {id:117,a:'Palehorse',t:'Saraswati Goddess',loc:'687 Central Ave',bldg:'',lat:27.771635,lng:-82.643454,y:2020,cat:'commercial',ig:'palehorse',bio:'He co-founded SHINE with his wife Michelle. He has also designed gig posters for Foo Fighters, Queens of the Stone Age, Iggy Pop, and Jimi Hendrix.\n\nChris Parks — Palehorse — earned a BA in Graphic and Interactive Communication from Ringling College of Art and Design. His style is intricate digital illustration blending Eastern mysticism, sacred iconography, esoteric symbolism, and global mythologies. His practice is rooted in Kriya Yoga — he hosts weekly meditation circles called "Seekers of the Eternal."\n\nClients include National Geographic, Nike, Mini Cooper, FX, World of Warcraft, Hasbro, Under Armour, WWE, Harley Davidson, Red Bull, Wired Magazine, and DC Comics. He was featured in the Dali Museum and co-created the FloridaRAMA immersive art experience.',desc:'A massive black-and-gold mural depicting Saraswati, the Hindu goddess of knowledge and the arts. The multi-armed figure plays a veena (stringed instrument) at center, surrounded by extraordinary ornamental detail — a swan on the left, a peacock on the right, an Om symbol above, and the word "सरस्वती" (Saraswati) in Devanagari script. The entire composition is rendered in white and gold linework on a jet-black background, giving it the quality of an illuminated manuscript at building scale.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2020/palehorse.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE Mural Festival co-founder (2015); Dali Museum featured; 20+ year career',fw:[{name:'palehorsedesign.com',url:''}],sbio:'He co-founded SHINE with his wife Michelle. He has also designed gig posters for Foo Fighters, Queens of the Stone Age, Iggy Pop, and Jimi Hendrix. Chris Parks works under the name Palehorse (also styled Pale Horse) and is based in St. Petersburg, Florida. He earned a BA in Graphic and Interactive Communication from Ringling College of Art and Design. In 2006 he opened his own studio, Pale Horse Design.\n\nIn 2015, Chris and Michelle Parks were founding members, curators, and organizers of the Shine Mural Festival in St. Petersburg. The festival brought world-renowned mural artists to the city during a period when many local shops and businesses were closing due to the recession, and it has continued to reshape the streetscape of St. Petersburg since.\n\nPalehorse\'s commissioned client list spans entertainment, apparel, beverage, gaming, and lifestyle brands, including National Geographic, Nike, Mini Cooper, FX, DC Snowboards, X-Games, World of Warcraft, G.I. Joe, Transformers, Arnette Sunglasses, Under Armour, WWE, Brisk Iced Tea, Modelo, Jagermeister, Bud Light, PBR, Live Nation, Hasbro, Harley Davidson, Red Bull, Wired Magazine, DC Comics, Iron Fist, Vans, Etnies, and The Cartoon Network. He has also created officially licensed, screen-printed gig posters for Foo Fighters, Queens of the Stone Age, Puscifer, 108, Converge, Primus, Iggy Pop, Jimi Hendrix, and Rush. Named poster and album projects include the Aftershock Festival gig poster (2017), the Foo Fighters gig poster (2017), the EXPO LUCHA screen-printed event poster (2018), the 108 "Threefold Misery" album artwork (2016), the Sonic Temple festival entrance artwork (2019), the DC Snowboards "Space Echo" graphic (2017), the "Moment of Death" laser engraving (2015), the "Kali The Destroyer" artwork (2018), and the "Tiger Tamer" digital illustration (2025). His gallery and illustration work includes pieces such as La Calavera Catrina, Japanese Koi, the 7 Skate Deck Totem, Yami, The Pale Horse, The Book of Five Rings, Geisha Samurai, a Super7 Florida x Pale Horse collaboration, Zap Skimboards board graphics, and a Baroness/Torche gig poster.\n\nHis work has been exhibited in local, national, and international galleries, and shown at the Salvador Dali Museum in St. Petersburg, Florida. He has created entrance artwork for some of the largest music festivals in the world. He co-created the FloridaRAMA immersive art experience.\n\nIn St. Petersburg, his Saraswati Goddess mural — a 50 by 30 foot piece on the Floridian Social Club, a historic music venue — was painted in August 2020. The mural depicts the Hindu goddess Saraswati, who symbolizes knowledge, music, art, speech, wisdom, and learning, traditionally shown with four arms representing mind, intellect, ego, and consciousness, holding a veena. He painted it with his brother Matthew Parks and Cat Gomez. He has also painted Pro Wrestling and Muay Thai dojo murals (2024).\n\nBeyond brand and mural work, Palehorse designs masked pro wrestling personas for luchadors, including AEW\'s Serpentico and Arena Mexico\'s CMLL star Oraculo. He has participated in self-initiated projects such as Back in Black, a live printing t-shirt exhibit featuring top apparel artists from around the world, and ran Golden/Black, a royalty-free stock vector art and fonts website. He has curated, hosted, and participated in gallery exhibits throughout the year.\n\nHis personal practice is rooted in Kriya Yoga. Since 2018 he has hosted a weekly local meditation circle called Seekers of the Eternal. He describes the current direction of his work as drawing from global mythologies, archetypes, esoteric symbolism, and personal devotion, with each form treated as a vibration and each symbol as an opportunity for inner reflection. The work is intended to function as both invocation and offering, whether displayed in museums, galleries, personal spaces, on T-shirts, or in city streets.\n\nWith more than 20 years of experience as a professional artist, his catalogue blends intricate digital illustration with Eastern mysticism, sacred iconography, esoteric symbolism, and global mythologies. Contact: chris@palehorsedesign.com. Website: palehorsedesign.com. Instagram: @palehorse.',src:['https://palehorsedesign.com/saraswati-goddess-mural-artwork','https://palehorsedesign.com/about/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/palehorse','https://pixel77.com/artist-week-graphic-designer-chris-parks-aka-pale-horse/']},
  {id:106,a:'Tatiana Suarez',t:'Mangroves and Manatees',loc:'250 2nd Ave SE',bldg:'St. Petersburg Sailing Center',lat:27.76938833,lng:-82.63242167,y:2020,cat:'shine',ig:'tatunga',bio:'Her mother is Brazilian. Her father is Salvadoran. The tropical palette of greens and yellows comes from both.\n\nTatiana Suarez (also @tatunga) earned her BFA in Illustration and Design from the University of Miami in 2005. Her style is pop surrealism — ethereal feminine creatures with doe-eyes surrounded by dense flora, fauna, and underwater life. She is represented by Spoke Art in San Francisco and Corey Helford Gallery in Los Angeles, with mural representation through BA-Reps. Her work has shown at Thinkspace Projects and Wow x Wow.\n\nHer walls have gone up at Wynwood Walls (Miami), POW!WOW! Hawaii, Long Beach Walls, and festivals in Okinawa, Detroit, Mexico, and Sweden. Brand clients include Netflix, L\'Oreal, Reebok, and JanSport.\n\nFor SHINE 2020 — the festival\'s Sea Walls / PangeaSeed ocean conservation edition, sponsored by Ocean Conservancy — she painted "Mangroves and Manatees" wrapping the entire facade of the St. Petersburg Sailing Center. A young woman swims among Florida manatees with her hand resting on the largest one, schools of sheepshead fish to her right, mangrove leaves filling the edges. Signed "Tati 2020 @tatunga."',desc:'A building-wrap mural covering the entire facade of the St. Petersburg Sailing Center in muted greens, teals, and warm browns. A young woman with flowing auburn hair swims among Florida manatees, her hand resting gently on a large manatee in the foreground. Schools of striped sheepshead fish swim to the right, while teal mangrove leaves and sea plants fill the left side and edges. Bubbles drift upward throughout the composition. The palette is naturalistic and subdued — deep greens, sage, and sandy tones — giving the whole piece an underwater, dreamlike quality. Signed "Tati 2020 @tatunga" in the lower right corner. Painted for SHINE x Sea Walls 2020, sponsored by Ocean Conservancy, the mural highlights the fragile mangrove and manatee ecosystems of Florida\'s coast.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2020/tatiana-suarez.jpeg',from:'Miami, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2020 selected artist',fw:[{name:'tatisuarez.com',url:'https://www.tatisuarez.com'}],sbio:'Her mother is Brazilian. Her father is Salvadoran. The tropical palette of greens and yellows comes from both.\n\nTatiana Suarez (also @tatunga) lives and works in Miami. She received her BFA in illustration and design from the University of Miami in 2005, and has since become a mainstay in the world of pop surrealism and muralism. Her style centers on ethereal feminine creatures with oversized doe-eyes set against dense flora, fauna, and underwater life — pop surrealism with a tropical color register pulled from her Brazilian-Salvadoran upbringing.\n\nShe has shown in galleries nationwide, with primary representation at Spoke Art in San Francisco and Corey Helford Gallery in Los Angeles. She is also rostered as a muralist with B&A (ba-reps.com), the international representation agency, alongside Will Barras, Bakpak Durden, Kitt Bennett, Ewok, Taj Francis, George Rose, Georgia Hill, INSA, Ornamental Conifer, Roids, Victor Reyes, and Vizie.\n\nHer brand and commercial collaborations include Netflix, L\'Oreal, Reebok, Secret Deodorant, Olukai, JanSport, and Becks. She has logged mural locations across Okinawa, Hawaii, Detroit, Texas, Los Angeles, San Francisco, Cincinnati, Mexico, Sweden, and Miami\'s Wynwood Walls. The Wynwood Walls inclusion places her among the canonical roster of contemporary muralists associated with the Wynwood Arts District in Miami, the project founded by Tony Goldman in 2009 that turned the neighborhood\'s warehouse exteriors into a permanent open-air mural collection.\n\nFor SHINE Mural Festival 2020 in St. Petersburg, Florida — the festival\'s Sea Walls / PangeaSeed Foundation ocean-conservation edition — she painted "Mangroves and Manatees" wrapping the entire facade of the St. Petersburg Sailing Center at 250 2nd Ave SE. The composition shows a young woman with flowing auburn hair swimming among Florida manatees, her hand resting gently on the largest one in the foreground; schools of striped sheepshead fish swim to her right, while teal mangrove leaves and sea plants fill the left side and edges. Bubbles drift upward through the composition. The palette is naturalistic and subdued — deep greens, sage, and sandy tones — giving the whole piece an underwater, dreamlike quality. Signed "Tati 2020 @tatunga" in the lower right corner. The mural was painted as part of the Sea Walls activation in St. Petersburg, the SHINE x PangeaSeed Foundation collaboration sponsored by Ocean Conservancy, focused on the fragile mangrove and manatee ecosystems of Florida\'s coast.\n\nHer practice spans large-scale public murals, gallery painting (oil on canvas, acrylic), and brand collaborations. She works in a recognizable visual vocabulary across all formats: large eyes, small mouths, downturned heads, jewel-toned skin tones, and dense botanical or aquatic surroundings. The figures often read as somewhere between portraiture and folkloric icon, with the surrounding flora rendered at a level of detail usually reserved for the face.\n\nHer work has been documented across the contemporary mural and pop-surrealism press, including Wow x Wow\'s artist-interview series and Miami Artzine\'s "Talking With Miami Artist Tatiana Suarez." She is also represented in Long Beach Walls (longbeachwalls.com) and POW!WOW! Hawaii participant rosters, and her gallery work appears on Artsy, Spoke Art, Corey Helford Gallery, GGA Gallery (ggagallery.com), and Thinkspace Projects.\n\nShe is part of the broader Sea Walls / PangeaSeed lineage of artist-activists whose murals are staged to draw attention to ocean conservation issues — sea-level rise, marine biodiversity loss, mangrove ecosystem decline, fisheries collapse — through public art rather than text. The St. Pete Sailing Center placement was deliberate: the building sits on the downtown waterfront where the manatees she painted actually swim.\n\nWebsite: tatisuarez.com (sections for Murals, Gallery, About, Contact, Store). Instagram: @tatunga. Mural representation: B&A (ba-reps.com). Gallery representation: Spoke Art (San Francisco) and Corey Helford Gallery (Los Angeles).',src:['https://www.tatisuarez.com/about','https://www.ba-reps.com/mural-art/tatiana-suarez','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/tatiana-suarez-2020/']},
  {id:176,a:'Drew Merritt',t:'Mask Off',loc:'551 Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. St N',bldg:'',lat:27.778247,lng:-82.646402,y:2019,cat:'shine',ig:'drewmerritt',bio:'He grew up on a cattle ranch in New Mexico. No formal art training — he taught himself painting between farm chores and graffiti experiments.\n\nDrew Merritt was born May 29, 1985, on the outskirts of Clovis, New Mexico. He splits time between Los Angeles and southern New Mexico. His style is vivid photorealism that blends classical Renaissance-inspired imagery with contemporary settings. He works in oil, acrylic, spray paint, and 24-karat gold leaf on canvas, panels, and walls.\n\nHis figurative portraiture focuses on human emotion, the passage of time, and what he calls "showcasing flaws — physical, emotional, or environmental." The contrast between classical beauty and modern grit runs through everything.\n\nClients include Tesla, Sony PlayStation, UFC, Steve Aoki, and Universal Music Group. He has shown at Thinkspace Projects (Los Angeles), SCOPE Basel, SCOPE Miami Beach, Spoke Art (New York), West Chelsea Contemporary (Austin), NextStreet Gallery (Paris), and Treason Gallery (Seattle). He has painted at POW! WOW! Hawaii and festivals worldwide.\n\nFor SHINE 2019, he painted "Mask Off" in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A massive 89-by-12-foot photorealistic mural on a gray stucco wall. A woman with wild, curly brown-gold hair fills the left side — her face rendered in extraordinary detail with blue eyes, heavy brows, and a direct gaze. Her right arm extends forward, hand gripping a small alligator by the jaw, its mouth pried open to reveal rows of teeth. The alligator\'s scales and the woman\'s skin are painted with equal hyperrealistic precision. Lavender nail polish is visible on her fingertips. The background is bare white wall, making the figures appear to emerge from the surface. The model is Kari Michelle. Painted entirely in spray paint.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2019/drew-merritt.jpeg',from:'Los Angeles, CA',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2019 selected artist; POW! WOW! Hawaii; clients include Tesla, Sony PlayStation, UFC',fw:[{name:'drewmerritt.com',url:'https://www.drewmerritt.com'}],sbio:'Drew Merritt was born May 29, 1985, on the outskirts of Clovis, New Mexico. He grew up on a farm and cattle ranch. Self-taught — no formal art training. He experimented with graffiti, street art, and portraiture on the ranch, then moved to Los Angeles where he realized he could make a career of it. He now splits time between LA and southern New Mexico.\n\nHis style is vivid photorealism blending classical Renaissance imagery with contemporary settings. He works in oil, acrylic, spray paint, and 24k gold leaf on canvas, panels, and walls. Themes: human emotion, passage of time, compassion, isolation, showcasing flaws.\n\nExhibitions: Thinkspace Projects (LA), SCOPE Basel, SCOPE Miami Beach, Spoke Art NYC, West Chelsea Contemporary (Austin), NextStreet Gallery (Paris), Treason Gallery (Seattle), POW! WOW! Hawaii (2017, 2020). Clients: Tesla, Sony PlayStation, UFC, Steve Aoki, Universal Music Group. Notable works: "Sav" (2018), "Let Them Eat Cake" (2018), "Trap Queen" (2016).\n\nFor SHINE 2019, he painted "Mask Off" at 551 MLK Jr. St N in St. Petersburg. Website: drewmerritt.com. Instagram: @drewmerritt.',src:['https://www.visitstpeteclearwater.com/profile/mask-off-edge-district/140781','https://www.drewmerritt.com/about-drew-merritt','https://wcc.art/artist/drew-merritt','https://thinkspaceprojects.com/artists/drew-merritt/']},
  {id:124,a:'George Rose',t:'',loc:'601 14th St S',bldg:'Campbell Park Handball Courts',lat:27.76297,lng:-82.65384667,y:2019,cat:'shine',ig:'george_rose',bio:'Disney, Instagram, TikTok, Facebook, Nike. He paints endangered indigenous flora at macro scale.\n\nGeorge Rose studied at the ANU School of Art in Canberra and is based in Melbourne, Australia. His style is gestural mark-making with endemic botanicals — bright surreal colors, signature gradients, hard edges with bold undulating texture. He has painted for the STRAAT Museum in Amsterdam and the Musee Pierre-Noel in France.\n\nHe won the Resene Total Colour Visual Art Award (2020), the Resene Total Colour Award (2023), and "Best Mural" at the Street Art Australia Prize (2019). For SHINE 2019, he painted all eight walls of a handball court at Campbell Park — solo, brushes and paint sprayer, eight days.',desc:'A sprawling botanical mural covering all eight walls of two back-to-back handball courts at Campbell Park. Large curved bell-shaped flowers big enough to climb into sit alongside massive fringed petals and tall striated leaves stretching floor to ceiling. The color palette transitions from golden orange at the top through pink in the middle to electric blue at the base, all outlined in smoky blue linework. Half the flowers are Florida natives and half are Australian natives, connecting Rose\'s home country with the mural\'s location.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2019/george-rose.jpeg',from:'Melbourne, Australia',aud:'',insp:'Half the flowers are Florida natives and half are native to Australia, connecting the artist\'s home country with St. Pete. Painted solo in 8 days on 8 walls using only buckets, brushes, and a paint sprayer.',maw:'',aaw:'Resene Total Colour Award (2020, 2023); Best Mural, Street Art Australia Prize (2019); SHINE 2019',fw:[{name:'georgerose.com.au',url:'https://www.georgerose.com.au'}],sbio:'Disney, Instagram, TikTok, Facebook, Nike. She paints endangered indigenous flora at macro scale.\n\nGeorge Rose is a Melbourne, Australia-based muralist whose father is a forester — she grew up surrounded by the bush and developed an early interest in flowers. At ten years old she chose art over her childhood ambition of becoming an astronaut. She studied art and graphic design in school, then completed her formal training at the ANU School of Art in Canberra. Before settling on murals as her primary medium, she worked across ceramics, lighting installations, oil painting, digital illustration, festival set dressing, and sign writing. Stifled by the commercialism of sign writing, she narrowed her practice to walls after a friend asked what she would do if she could only choose one thing — "paint big walls."\n\nHer style is gestural mark-making intertwined with endemic botanicals, rendered in bright, surreal colors with signature gradients, hard edges, and bold undulating texture. She researches each location, finds endangered or endemic flora and fauna, and incorporates them into the design — a practice she describes as "pollination," carrying images from one location to another. Her subject matter explores the fragility of nature, transforming micro plant and animal life into macro-scale wall pieces. She balances hard edges with bold undulating colours and texture, and her work consistently sits at the juxtaposition of masculine scale and feminine motif.\n\nHer process started with black and white before someone pointed out her color sense; she now works heavily in color theory and the optical vibration between contrasting hues. She abandoned spray cans because the sun fades them and switched to house paint with a spray gun for the larger gradients and fades that became her signature. Her "recipe" combines gradients, hard lines, and textured backgrounds. Her favorite flower is the kangaroo paw — she has a tattoo of it, alongside flowers for each member of her family (lilies for her mother, rosewood for her father).\n\nRose has been recognised as one of Australia\'s foremost female muralists. Her awards include the Resene Total Colour Visual Art Award (2020), the Resene Total Colour Award (2023), and "Best Mural" at the Street Art Australia Prize (2019). Her commissions span global media businesses (Instagram, TikTok, Facebook), entertainment giants (Disney, Nike), apparel brands (JanSport), and major museums — including the Musée Pierre-Noël de Saint-Dié-des-Vosges in France and the STRAAT Museum in Amsterdam. Her murals appear across Australia, the United States, Asia, and Europe. She has noted her work resonates particularly in California and Michigan.\n\nFor SHINE Mural Festival 2019 in St. Petersburg, Florida, she painted all eight walls of a handball court at Campbell Park, solo, using brushes and a paint sprayer, over eight days. She works the festival circuit internationally and accepts invitations from countries around the world. She is represented by B&A (ba-reps.com), which lists her alongside artists Will Barras, Bakpak Durden, Kitt Bennett, Ewok, Taj Francis, Georgia Hill, INSA, Ornamental Conifer, Roids, Tatiana Suarez, Victor Reyes, and Vizie.\n\nShe acknowledges the Wurundjeri Woi Wurrung peoples of the Eastern Kulin Nation as the owners of the land on which she operates. She lives in Melbourne with her partner and a Kelpie named Sunny Boy. Off the wall, she has expressed interest in completing a residency, working on smaller (1m x 1.5m) canvases, and dream projects including painting an airplane, working from a drop stage on a 20-story skyscraper, and collaborating with high-fashion labels such as Gucci, Prada, or Burberry on a runway show or fabric yardage.\n\nShe has been interviewed by Coveteur ("\'Wall Talk\' with Australian Muralist George Rose," April 26, 2024). The piece\'s photography was credited to Yoshi Yanagita, Joel Colin, and Mike Chaney. Her advice for muralists at scale: "It\'s super physical, so it\'s like training for a sport. When you stop painting for a period of time, it\'s easy to get injured, so you have to keep training for it."\n\nWebsite: georgerose.com.au. Instagram: @george_rose. Agent: B&A (info@ba-reps.com).',src:['https://www.georgerose.com.au/about','https://www.visitstpeteclearwater.com/profile/shine-2019-mural-george-rose-campbell-park/140785','https://coveteur.com/muralist-george-rose-interview','https://www.ba-reps.com/mural-art/george-rose']},
  {id:131,a:'Jimmy Breen & Anthony Freese',t:'Lucky You',loc:'1701 Central Ave',bldg:'1701 Central',lat:27.771298,lng:-82.65785,y:2019,cat:'shine',ig:'waxandhive',bio:'Jimmy Breen designed artwork for the Grateful Dead, Michael Bublé, Green Day, and Bruno Mars. He died on June 20, 2022. He was 37.\n\nBreen and Anthony Freese co-founded Wax & Hive, a design studio in St. Petersburg. Breen graduated from the USF St. Petersburg graphic design program. Together they had over two decades of combined experience in illustration and graphic design. Breen was a Senior Illustrator at Warner Music Group. Freese kept the studio going.\n\nTheir style is bold, graphic, pop-art-influenced — tattoo culture meets vintage signage meets clean design. Their client list ran from Cardi B and Blake Shelton to Bloomin\' Brands and Hunger & Thirst Group. They were fixtures in St. Pete\'s creative scene.\n\nFor SHINE 2019 they painted "Lucky You" on the parking garage of the 1701 Central apartment building, facing I-275 — eighteen by forty-two feet, a grid of good luck symbols including a horseshoe, a four-leaf clover, a shooting star, a ladybug, and a smiley face. The premise: "we\'re all lucky to live in Saint Pete." They followed it with an interior courtyard mural for the building\'s 2020 grand opening.\n\nBreen is remembered as one of St. Pete\'s most prolific and beloved muralists.',desc:'An 18-by-42-foot mural on the parking garage of the 1701 Central apartment building, facing I-275 North and visible to highway traffic. The piece is a 3-by-5 grid of panels depicting good luck symbols from cultures around the world, inspired by the idea that "we\'re all lucky to live in Saint Pete." Each panel features a bold graphic illustration rendered in a pink, blue, yellow, black, and red color scheme. The symbols include: "Good Luck" in black cursive script, a Lincoln penny, a pair of dice, crossed fingers, an anchor, a ladybug, an Italian cornicello on a chain, a Japanese maneki-neko (beckoning cat), a yellow smiley face, a Chinese lucky bat, an anatomical heart, a horseshoe pointing up, a shooting star, a bird on a branch, a hamsa hand with an evil eye, and a four-leaf clover. The style is clean, graphic, and pop-art-influenced with bold black outlines and limited flat color fills.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2019/jimmy-breen-anthony-freese.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'Jimmy Breen designed artwork for the Grateful Dead, Michael Bublé, Green Day, and Bruno Mars. He died on June 20, 2022. He was 37.\n\nBreen and Anthony Freese co-founded Wax & Hive, a design studio in St. Petersburg. Breen graduated from the USF St. Petersburg graphic design program. Together they had over two decades of combined experience in illustration and graphic design. Breen was a Senior Illustrator at Warner Music Group. Freese kept the studio going.\n\nTheir style is bold, graphic, pop-art-influenced — tattoo culture meets vintage signage meets clean design. Their client list ran from Cardi B and Blake Shelton to Bloomin\' Brands and Hunger & Thirst Group. They were fixtures in St. Pete\'s creative scene.\n\nFor SHINE 2019 they painted "Lucky You" on the parking garage of the 1701 Central apartment building, facing I-275 — eighteen by forty-two feet, a grid of good luck symbols including a horseshoe, a four-leaf clover, a shooting star, a ladybug, and a smiley face. The premise: "we\'re all lucky to live in Saint Pete." They followed it with an interior courtyard mural for the building\'s 2020 grand opening.\n\nBreen is remembered as one of St. Pete\'s most prolific and beloved muralists.',fn:['Jimmy Breen designed Grateful Dead album art','Anthony Freese — photorealistic murals','Cross-generational collaboration'],src:['https://stpetecatalyst.com/local-ish-meet-artist-jimmy-breen/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/st-pete-artist-jimmy-breen-dies-at-37/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/artist-jimmy-breens-life-and-work-celebrated-saturday/','https://www.tampabay.com/life-culture/arts/2022/07/01/jimmy-breen-lived-a-life-as-colorful-as-the-murals-he-painted-around-st-petersburg/','https://thegabber.com/st-pete-muralist-jimmy-breen-dead-but-lives-on/','https://bestofgulfport.com/st-pete-muralist-jimmy-breen-dead-but-lives-on/','https://www.visitstpeteclearwater.com/profile/lucky-you-jimmy-breen-anthony-freese/10226','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/jimmy-breen-and-anthony-freese','https://stpetemuraltour.com/2019/12/05/jimmy-breen/','https://snbc13.com/jimmy-breen-well-known-shine-2019-artist-passes-away-death/','https://nrpgroup.com/communities/1701-central']},
  {id:175,a:'Low Bros',t:'Post Internet Explorers',loc:'535 Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. St N',bldg:'',lat:27.778142,lng:-82.646401,y:2019,cat:'shine',ig:'low_bros',bio:'They studied at the University of Applied Sciences in Hamburg and the Berlin University of the Arts. Their roots are in 1980s skate and hip-hop culture.\n\nFlorin Schmidt (born 1982) and Christoph Schmidt (born 1984) are brothers from Hamburg, Germany who formed Low Bros in 2011. They have since rebranded as Christoph Florin. Their style is self-described as "retro-futuristic" — geometric shapes, bold lines, and vivid colors with recurring angular animal characters, especially wolves. They merge digital vector design with analog craft: stencils, masking tape, vinyl foil, acrylic paint, airbrush, and Montana spray cans.\n\nTheir work explores identity in the digital age, post-internet culture, consumerism, and environmental concerns. The tone oscillates between bold geometry and delicate detail — "distinctly bittersweet."\n\nSolo shows include StolenSpace Gallery (London), Thinkspace Gallery (Los Angeles), Mirus Gallery (San Francisco, Denver), Urban Spree (Berlin), and HD Gallery (Paris). Their work is in the Urban Nation Museum for Urban Contemporary Art in Berlin. They have painted at POW! WOW! Hawaii and festivals worldwide.\n\nFor SHINE 2019, they painted "Post Internet Explorers" in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A wide 120-by-25-foot mural spanning the full length of a commercial building on MLK Street. A chain of yellow x-eyed emoji faces snakes across the wall through a collage of graphic pop-culture and internet-age symbols: a black-and-white barcode, a geometric multi-colored star, a blue circle with a white cross (evoking social media), an American flag fragment with "SPIRIT" text, a smiley face, a megaphone, and abstract geometric shapes in pastel tones. "LOL" appears in blue block letters at the lower right. The palette is muted pastels — sky blue, coral, mint, peach — against a light gray background. The piece comments on the internet\'s evolution from open-source community to corporate platform dominance.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2019/low-bros.jpeg',from:'Hamburg, Germany',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Urban Nation Museum Berlin collection; international festival artist (POW! WOW!, SHINE 2019)',fw:[{name:'christophflorin.com',url:'https://christophflorin.com'}],sbio:'Florin Schmidt (born 1982) and Christoph Schmidt (born 1984) are brothers from Hamburg, Germany. They studied at the University of Applied Sciences (HAW) in Hamburg and the Berlin University of the Arts (UDK). They formed Low Bros in 2011 and have since rebranded as Christoph Florin.\n\nTheir style is "retro-futuristic" — geometric shapes, bold lines, vivid colors, recurring angular wolf characters. Process: freehand painting, stencils, masking tape, vinyl foil, acrylic, airbrush, Montana spray cans. Themes: post-internet culture, consumerism, digital identity.\n\nSolo shows: StolenSpace Gallery (London, 2017), Thinkspace Gallery (Los Angeles, 2015), Mirus Gallery (San Francisco, Denver 2022), Urban Spree (Berlin, 2020), Dom Kultur (Moscow, 2019), HD Gallery (Paris, 2024). Collections: Urban Nation Museum Berlin. Festivals: POW! WOW! Hawaii, SHINE 2019 St. Petersburg. Website: christophflorin.com.\n\nPer the lowbros.de archive site and the active christophflorin.com, the duo\'s studio is at Bogenstraße 19, 20144 Hamburg, Germany. Hamburg remains their current base. Recent exhibitions under the Christoph Florin rebrand include "Window Views" and "What\'s Cooking" at H.D. Gallery in Paris (2024), the Knotenpunkt group show at Affenfaust Gallery (2024), "Postalgia" at Mirus Gallery in Denver, "Con.txt" at Urban Spree Gallery in Berlin, and "98 Windows" at Mirus Gallery in San Francisco. Art-fair participation includes Urban Art Fair Paris (2022, 2023, with H.D. Gallery), Scope Art Show Miami (2016, 2022), Juxtapoz Clubhouse Miami (2018), Context Art Fair Miami (2017), and Affordable Artfair Hamburg (2022). Additional collections include Art Collection Berlin and private collections across Germany, USA, France, Portugal, Switzerland, Monaco, and Thailand. They received multiple ADC Awards in 2020 — Gold in Room Design; Silver in Exhibition Experience, Spatial Staging, Light, and Product Design; Bronze in Next Level, Spatial Event, and Public Event. Working categories on the active site span Paintings, Sculptures, Projects, and Exhibitions, with named works including "Jet Set" (paintings) and "The Medium is the Message" (sculptures). New Instagram handle: @christoph_florin. Contact: ih@christophflorin.com.',src:['https://streetartnews.net/2019/11/post-internet-explorers-low-bros-in-st-petersburg-florida.html','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2019_SHINE_low_bros','https://www.lowbros.de/ (now an archive site; redirects from lowbros.de)','https://www.christophflorin.com/','https://www.christophflorin.com/about','https://www.christophflorin.com/selected-works']},
  {id:113,a:'Morning Breath',t:'',loc:'250 Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. St N',bldg:'Coney Island Sandwich Shop',lat:27.774454,lng:-82.646928,y:2019,cat:'shine',ig:'morningbreathinc',bio:'They met at Think Skateboards in San Francisco in 1996. They have designed over a hundred album covers since.\n\nDoug Cunningham and Jason Noto are Morning Breath Inc., a Brooklyn-based design studio they formalized in 2002. Cunningham studied at the Academy of Art University in San Francisco. Their style pulls from 1950s-1980s Americana — comics, Mad magazine, punk rock, hip-hop, heavy metal, and skateboard graphics — rendered in limited color palettes with bold lines.\n\nThe client list is absurd: Foo Fighters, Kanye West, Jay-Z, Eminem, Slayer, Queens of the Stone Age, AFI. Brands: Vans, Adidas, Ray-Ban, Absolut Vodka, MTV, KFC (a full restaurant redesign). They won the Grammy for Best Recording Package in 2021 for Desert Sessions Vols. 11 and 12 — a Josh Homme project with a mix-and-match booklet and sticker set. They have published two monographs: The Early Bird (2007) and By the Skin of Our Teeth (HarperCollins, 2016).\n\nTheir SHINE 2015 mural on Central Avenue is mostly hidden behind new construction — only a narrow vertical slice survives. They returned for SHINE 2019 with a 101-foot-wide piece at 250 Dr. MLK Jr. Street North in their signature black-white-and-red style: open lips, aviator sunglasses, a smiling devil face.',desc:'A 101-by-15-foot brush-painted mural in black, white, and red on a pale blue background, designed to look like a vintage comic book pamphlet held open wide. At left, the Steeplechase "Funny Face" from Coney Island grins with a missing tooth. The center features a sinister figure in a red jacket holding a "Welcome to Fear City" pamphlet — a reference to the real 1975 New York City scare pamphlet. Phrases like "GHASTLY," "STARTLING SECRETS of Mystery," "JITTERY NERVES," and "Picture You NERVOUS" are scattered across the composition in retro horror-comic typography. At far right, a large red devil face with curling mustache and horns looms above the wall. The mural was inspired by the Coney Island Sandwich Shop (operating since 1926) and contains subtle nods to New York City throughout.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2019/morning-breath.jpeg',from:'Brooklyn, NY',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Grammy Award for Best Recording Package (2021, Desert Sessions Vols. 11 and 12); SHINE 2015 and 2019 festival artist',fw:[{name:'morningbreathinc.com',url:'http://www.morningbreathinc.com'}],sbio:'They met at Think Skateboards in San Francisco in 1996. They have designed over a hundred album covers since.\n\nDoug Cunningham and Jason Noto are Morning Breath Inc., a Brooklyn-based design studio. The two began as the in-house design department at Think Skateboards in San Francisco in 1996, where they developed the shared visual language and collaborative approach that still defines their work. They formalized the partnership in 2002 as Morning Breath, Inc., a boutique design studio based in Brooklyn, New York. Cunningham studied at the Academy of Art University in San Francisco.\n\nWhat started in skateboarding culture expanded into a multidisciplinary practice covering music packaging, apparel, advertising, and poster design, plus a steady output of personal and gallery work. Their style pulls from 1950s-1980s Americana — comics, Mad magazine, punk rock, hip-hop, heavy metal, and skateboard graphics — rendered in limited color palettes with bold lines and a modern punk twist. The results are clean, bright, and frequently funny.\n\nThe client list is absurd. In music: Foo Fighters, Kanye West, Jay-Z, Eminem, Slayer, Queens of the Stone Age, AFI. In brands: Vans, Adidas, Ray-Ban, Absolut Vodka, MTV, Pepsi, and KFC, where they handled a full restaurant redesign. Album packaging, graphics, and brand collaborations across two decades have left a long trail through contemporary pop culture.\n\nIn 2021 they won the Grammy Award for Best Recording Package for Desert Sessions Vols. 11 and 12, a Josh Homme project. The credited art directors on the win were Doug Cunningham and Jason Noto, with the performing artist listed as The Desert Sessions. The package featured a mix-and-match booklet and sticker set. The category, presented by the National Academy of Recording Arts and Sciences, goes to the art director rather than the performer, and traces back to the Best Album Package award introduced in 1974 before being renamed Best Recording Package in 1994.\n\nThey have published two monographs. The Early Bird came out in 2007. By the Skin of Our Teeth: The Art and Design of Morning Breath was published by HarperCollins in 2016 (also cited as released in 2017), a comprehensive volume chronicling the studio\'s work and creative evolution. The Daily Beast covered the book in December 2017, framing the duo as a pop-art design duo whose blend of retro themes and fonts with a modern punk twist defines their output. They have been featured in numerous design publications and have been invited to speak about and present their work internationally.\n\nMorning Breath painted a mural at SHINE 2015 on Central Avenue in St. Petersburg, Florida. Most of that piece is now hidden behind new construction, with only a narrow vertical slice still visible. They returned for SHINE 2019 and painted a 101-foot-wide piece at 250 Dr. MLK Jr. Street North in St. Petersburg, executed in their signature black-white-and-red palette: open lips, aviator sunglasses, and a smiling devil face.\n\nCunningham and Noto continue to divide their time between commercial and personal projects out of Brooklyn. The studio website is morningbreathinc.com.\n\nKeywords: Morning Breath Inc, Doug Cunningham, Jason Noto, Think Skateboards, San Francisco, Academy of Art University, Brooklyn, New York, design studio, album cover design, music packaging, apparel design, advertising, poster design, gallery work, skateboard graphics, Mad magazine, comics, punk rock, hip-hop, heavy metal, 1950s Americana, 1960s Americana, 1970s Americana, 1980s Americana, limited color palette, black white and red, Grammy Award, Best Recording Package, 2021 Grammy, Desert Sessions, Vols. 11 and 12, Josh Homme, mix-and-match booklet, sticker set, National Academy of Recording Arts and Sciences, The Early Bird, By the Skin of Our Teeth, HarperCollins, 2007 monograph, 2016 monograph, 2017 monograph, The Daily Beast, Sarah Rogers, Foo Fighters, Kanye West, Jay-Z, Eminem, Slayer, Queens of the Stone Age, AFI, Vans, Adidas, Ray-Ban, Absolut Vodka, MTV, Pepsi, KFC restaurant redesign, SHINE 2015, SHINE 2019, Central Avenue, 250 Dr. MLK Jr. Street North, St. Petersburg, Florida, 101-foot mural, aviator sunglasses, smiling devil, open lips, morningbreathinc.com.',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/morning-breath','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/morning-breath-2015','http://www.morningbreathinc.com/about','https://stpetemuraltour.com/2016/07/25/morning-breath-inc/','https://www.thedailybeast.com/inside-the-work-of-morning-breath-a-pop-art-design-duo/','https://dumbo.is/blogging/q-a-doug-cunningham-and-jason-noto-morning-breath-inc','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grammy_Award_for_Best_Recording_Package']},
  {id:121,a:'Palehorse',t:'Temple of Eternal Bliss',loc:'2390 5th Ave S',bldg:'Bayboro Brewing',lat:27.766483,lng:-82.665565,y:2019,cat:'shine',ig:'palehorse',bio:'He co-founded SHINE with his wife Michelle. He has also designed gig posters for Foo Fighters, Queens of the Stone Age, Iggy Pop, and Jimi Hendrix.\n\nChris Parks — Palehorse — earned a BA in Graphic and Interactive Communication from Ringling College of Art and Design. His style is intricate digital illustration blending Eastern mysticism, sacred iconography, esoteric symbolism, and global mythologies. His practice is rooted in Kriya Yoga — he hosts weekly meditation circles called "Seekers of the Eternal."\n\nClients include National Geographic, Nike, Mini Cooper, FX, World of Warcraft, Hasbro, Under Armour, WWE, Harley Davidson, Red Bull, Wired Magazine, and DC Comics. He was featured in the Dali Museum and co-created the FloridaRAMA immersive art experience.',desc:'An intricate Southeast Asian temple composition on a jet-black background. A central Thai-style pagoda structure with layered flame-shaped spires rises in the middle, flanked by two elaborate Naga serpent-dragons that coil outward in ornamental detail. The entire piece is rendered in fine linework using teal, red, gold, and white — giving it the look of a traditional temple painting scaled up to cover a freestanding wall. A thin white circle frames the central temple like a halo, and a trident symbol sits in the upper right corner. The level of detail is remarkable at this scale, with every scale, flame tip, and architectural ornament individually rendered.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/palehorse-bayboro.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'Inspired by the Sanskrit concept of \'Sat Chit Ananda\' — existence, consciousness, bliss. Painted in collaboration with brother Matt Parks.',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE Mural Festival co-founder (2015); Dali Museum featured; 20+ year career',fw:[{name:'palehorsedesign.com',url:''}],sbio:'He co-founded SHINE with his wife Michelle. He has also designed gig posters for Foo Fighters, Queens of the Stone Age, Iggy Pop, and Jimi Hendrix.\n\nChris Parks works under the name Palehorse, also styled Pale Horse. He is based in St. Petersburg, Florida. He earned a BA in Graphic and Interactive Communication from Ringling College of Art and Design. In 2006, he opened his own studio, Pale Horse Design. His style is intricate digital illustration that draws on Eastern mysticism, sacred iconography, esoteric symbolism, global mythologies, archetypes, and personal devotion. He describes the work as both invocation and offering, with each form carrying a vibration and each symbol an opportunity for inner reflection.\n\nIn 2015, Chris and his wife Michelle were founding members, curators, and organizers of the Shine Mural Festival in St. Petersburg. The festival has brought world-renowned mural artists to the city and helped revive storefronts and local businesses during the recession years. He continues to co-run the festival with Michelle.\n\nHis commercial client list spans entertainment, apparel, action sports, beverage, and toy categories. Brands that have commissioned Palehorse work include National Geographic, Nike, Mini Cooper, FX, DC Snowboards, X-Games, World of Warcraft, G.I. Joe, Transformers, Hasbro, Arnette Sunglasses, Under Armour, WWE, Brisk Iced Tea, Modelo, Jagermeister, Bud Light, PBR, Live Nation, Red Bull, Wired Magazine, DC Comics, Harley Davidson, Iron Fist, Vans, Etnies, and The Cartoon Network. He has created entrance artwork for some of the largest music festivals in the world.\n\nHis screen-printed gig poster catalogue includes officially licensed posters for Foo Fighters, Queens of the Stone Age, Puscifer, 108, Converge, Primus, Iggy Pop, Jimi Hendrix, and Rush, plus earlier work such as a Baroness / Torche gig poster. Other named illustration and product projects include La Calavera Catrina, Japanese Koi, the 7 Skate Deck Totem series, Yamī, The Pale Horse, The Book of Five Rings, the Geisha Samurai print, the Super7 Florida x Pale Horse collaboration, and a board graphic for Zap Skimboards. He has also taken part in self-initiated projects such as Back in Black, a live printing t-shirt exhibit featuring top apparel artists from around the world, and ran Golden/Black, a royalty-free stock vector art and fonts website. He regularly curates, hosts, and participates in gallery exhibits throughout the year.\n\nHis work has been exhibited in local, national, and international galleries, including the Salvador Dali Museum in Florida. He co-created the FloridaRAMA immersive art experience.\n\nPalehorse has also designed masked pro wrestling personas for luchadors, including AEW\'s Serpentico and Arena Mexico\'s CMLL star Oraculo.\n\nHis practice is rooted in Kriya Yoga. Since 2018, he has hosted a weekly local meditation circle called Seekers of the Eternal. He frames the current body of work as an expression of love that invites viewers into subtle resonance with their own inner landscape, calling viewers into sacred presence whether the work appears in museums, galleries, personal spaces, on T-shirts, or in city streets. He has more than 20 years of experience as a professional artist, and he describes the catalogue as a breadcrumb trail of his curiosity and quest for deeper meaning and self-discovery through creativity.\n\nHe maintains an online store and a podcast and meditations section on his official portfolio at palehorsedesign.com. Contact email is chris@palehorsedesign.com. Instagram: @palehorse (portfolio handle @palehorsedesign). Additional portfolios have been published on Behance and Facebook.',fn:['Co-founded SHINE with his wife Michelle','Designed album art for Shinedown','The architect behind the festival'],src:['https://www.palehorsedesign.com/','https://www.instagram.com/palehorsedesign/','https://palehorsedesign.com/about/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/palehorse','https://pixel77.com/artist-week-graphic-designer-chris-parks-aka-pale-horse/']},
  {id:170,a:'Paola Delfín',t:'La Emperatriz',loc:'1975 3rd Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.768538,lng:-82.660055,y:2019,cat:'shine',ig:'paola_delfin',bio:'Her monochromatic women have appeared on walls from New Delhi to Wynwood to Mexico City — always in shades of gray, always tangled in leaves.\n\nPaola Delfín is a Mexican muralist based in Mexico City. She studied graphic design at Universidad Iberoamericana and has been painting large-scale monochromatic murals since around 2012. Her signature is illustrative female figures intertwined with botanical elements — leaves, flowers, dense foliage — rendered in black-and-white or gray-blue palettes at monumental scale. The work explores human bonds, intimacy, femininity, and emotional vulnerability.\n\nShe has painted walls across Mexico City, Guadalajara, Playa del Carmen, New Delhi (for the St+Art India Foundation\'s Lodhi Art District), Lima, Wynwood, Berlin, Barcelona, and Buenos Aires. She has been featured on Colossal and in Juxtapoz.\n\n"La Emperatriz" at 1975 3rd Ave S in St. Petersburg\'s Warehouse Arts District is a monochromatic gray-blue mural of two women\'s faces emerging from dense tropical foliage.',desc:'A monochromatic mural in shades of gray and blue-gray covering the side of a commercial building. Two women\'s faces emerge from dense tropical foliage — the larger figure on the left wears a crown of flowers and gazes directly at the viewer, while a smaller face appears to the right, partially obscured. A hand reaches up through the plants on the far left, holding a flower. The vegetation is lush and detailed — broad leaves, stems, and blossoms rendered with illustrative precision. The entire piece is monochromatic, giving it the quality of a pencil drawing or engraving at massive scale.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/paola-delfin-emperatriz.jpeg',from:'Mexico City, Mexico',aud:'',insp:'Themes of unity, community, and the human bond.',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'Her monochromatic women have appeared on walls from New Delhi to Wynwood to Mexico City — always in shades of gray, always tangled in leaves.\n\nPaola Delfín is a Mexican muralist and illustrator based in Mexico City. She studied graphic design at Universidad Iberoamericana in Mexico City and has been active professionally since approximately 2012-2013. Her signature is large-scale monochromatic murals — predominantly black-and-white or gray-blue palettes — centered on the female form intertwined with botanical elements. Recurring themes include human bonds, intimacy, femininity, nature, and emotional vulnerability. The style blends fine-art illustration with street art scale, often described as poetic and delicate despite monumental size.\n\nShe has painted walls in Mexico City (Roma and Condesa neighborhoods), Guadalajara, Playa del Carmen, New Delhi (Lodhi Art District for St+Art India Foundation — one of her most internationally recognized pieces), Lima (Barranco district), Wynwood (Miami), Berlin, Barcelona, and Buenos Aires. Festival credits include SHINE Mural Festival (St. Petersburg), St+Art India Foundation, and POW! WOW!. She has been featured on Colossal, in Juxtapoz, and in Street Art News.\n\n"La Emperatriz" at 1975 3rd Ave S in St. Petersburg\'s Warehouse Arts District is a monochromatic gray-blue mural of two women\'s faces emerging from dense tropical foliage — a hand reaching up through the plants holding a flower.\n\nInstagram: @paola_delfin. Website: paoladelfin.com.',src:['https://creativepinellas.org/ (A Ride Through St Pete\'s Murals)','https://www.thisiscolossal.com/2020/01/paola-delfin-murals/']},
  {id:157,a:'PlasticBirdie & Blaine Fontana',t:'The 8th Funky Fish Town',loc:'756 28th St S',bldg:'Bama Sea Products',lat:27.762702,lng:-82.670483,y:2019,cat:'shine',ig:'plasticbirdie',bio:'Their SHINE wall was the first public art project in the country funded by NOAA. It took over four hundred cans of spray paint and eight days.\n\nJeremy Nichols — PlasticBirdie — was born in Tokyo in 1982, grew up in suburban Columbus, Ohio, and earned a BFA in Printmaking from Ohio State. Blaine Fontana was born in Seattle, raised on Bainbridge Island, and earned a BFA in Communication Art/Design from Otis College. Both are based in Portland, Oregon.\n\nNichols paints the tension between urban growth and nature — layered graphic environments that feel familiar and not quite right at the same time. His clients include the Port of Portland (a mural in the PDX Airport pedestrian tunnel), Oregon Zoo, and Gerber Gear. Fontana works across murals, sculpture, fine art, and product design, drawing on Asian calligraphy, found objects, and graffiti palettes. His clients include Nike, adidas, Patagonia, TEDx, and VANS.\n\nFor SHINE 2019 they collaborated on "The 8th Funky Fish Town" at Bama Sea Products on 28th Street South — a hundred-spray-paint mural spanning roughly twenty-five by a hundred and seventy-five feet along the Pinellas Trail, partnered with PangeaSeed Foundation\'s Sea Walls program and funded by NOAA. The piece highlights Florida\'s small fishery towns and ocean fragility, inspired by classic school-book infographics. It was the largest SHINE mural of 2019.',desc:'A massive mural spanning roughly twenty-five by a hundred and seventy-five feet along the Pinellas Trail at Bama Sea Products. A stylized bald eagle head dominates the right side — sharp blue eye and curved beak rendered in warm yellows, oranges, and teal with mosaic-like geometric patterning. The left side dissolves into abstract geometric triangles in teal, coral, mint, lavender, and white, creating a crystalline fractured effect that transitions from the figurative eagle into pure abstraction. The palette shifts from cool teals and mints to warm golds and corals. The piece highlights Florida fishery culture and ocean ecology through a graphic, infographic-inspired style. It was the largest SHINE mural of 2019.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2019/plasticbirdie-fontana.jpeg',from:'Portland, OR',aud:'',insp:'First US public art project funded by NOAA, created in partnership with PangeaSeed Sea Walls: Artists for Oceans.',maw:'Largest SHINE mural of 2019',aaw:'SHINE 2019 selected artists; PangeaSeed Sea Walls partnership; first NOAA-funded public art project in the US',fw:[{name:'plasticbirdie.com',url:'https://www.plasticbirdie.com'},{name:'thefontanastudios.com',url:'https://thefontanastudios.com'}],sbio:'Their SHINE wall was the first public art project in the country funded by NOAA. It took over four hundred cans of spray paint and eight days.\n\nJeremy Nichols — PlasticBirdie — was born in 1982 in Tokyo, Japan, grew up in the suburbs of Columbus, Ohio, and traveled regularly between upstate New York and Tokyo as a child. He earned a BFA in Printmaking from Ohio State University, studying under Charles Massey, Jr. and Philip von Rabbe. He moved to Portland, Oregon around 2009-2010. His work focuses on "the energy, movement, balance, and harmony of the chaos around us" — layered graphic design and nature elements exploring urban growth versus preserving nature, motivated by childhood displacement across cultures. He has been featured in Hi-Fructose magazine. Notable murals include "A Place We Call Home" in the PDX Airport North Pedestrian Tunnel (with Alex Chui, via Portland Street Art Alliance and Port of Portland), "Hello Bear!" at Groundwork Coffee in Portland, a mural celebrating historically significant Black women at the Black United Fund of Oregon (with Mehran Heard, 2014), and a wall at Plaza Walls in Oklahoma City (2019). Clients include Port of Portland, Oregon Zoo, Gerber Gear, Lexus, NW Natural, OMSI, and City of Tigard. Festivals include SHINE 2019, Forest for the Trees (Portland), and Canvas Miami.\n\nBlaine Fontana was born in Seattle, Washington, raised on Bainbridge Island, spent ten years in Los Angeles, and has lived in Portland for nearly twenty years. He earned a BFA in Communication Art/Design from Otis College of Art and Design (2002), receiving "Best in Show" for his senior thesis and later named one of seven distinguished Otis alumni (2000-2010). He worked as art director at Abound LLC, Metro.Pop magazine, and Drifter apparel before going self-employed in January 2003. He runs Fontana Studios with his wife. His multidisciplinary practice spans murals, fine art, illustration, graphic design, sculpture, installations, and furniture, drawing on Asian calligraphy, found objects, graffiti palettes, and wabi-sabi principles. Clients include Nike, adidas, Patagonia, TEDx, VANS, Quiksilver, Kid Robot, Upper Playground, Umpqua Bank, and Toyota. He has exhibited in New York, LA, Tokyo, London, and many other cities. He published the monograph "Amalgamate" through ZERO+ Publishing (covering 2002-2006). Festivals include SHINE 2019, Forest for the Trees (Portland, 2013-2015), and Colorado Crush.\n\nFor SHINE 2019 they collaborated on "The 8th Funky Fish Town" at Bama Sea Products, 756 28th St S, St. Petersburg — approximately 25 feet tall by 175 feet wide, 100% spray paint, over 400 cans, completed in 8 days. It was the largest SHINE mural of 2019. The project was a partnership with PangeaSeed Foundation\'s Sea Walls: Artists for Oceans program and NOAA — the first public art project in the US funded by NOAA. The mural highlights small fishery towns of the Florida region and ocean fragility, inspired by "iconic infographics found in classic school books." The artists researched and visited local fisheries before painting. A panel discussion was held October 26, 2019 at The Factory St. Pete with PangeaSeed founder Tre\' Packard, NOAA\'s Jeff Pulver, and Ocean Conservancy\'s Catherine Bruger.\n\nInstagram: @plasticbirdie, @blainefontana. Websites: plasticbirdie.com, thefontanastudios.com.',src:['https://www.plasticbirdie.com/shinemuralfest','https://www.muros.com/behind-the-brush-with-blaine-fontana/','https://oceanconservancy.org/blog/2019/11/08/creative-worlds-collide-saint-petersburg/','https://patch.com/florida/stpete/shine-festival-teams-noaa-create-ocean-themed-murals','https://www.instagram.com/plasticbirdie/','https://www.instagram.com/blainefontana/','https://www.groundworkcoffee.com/blogs/news/10-questions-with-plastic-birdie-pdx-muralist','https://www.otis.edu/alumni/blaine-fontana']},
  {id:143,a:'Reda3sb',t:'Feelings',loc:'131 22nd St S',bldg:'Hell\'s Bells CrossFit',lat:27.769262,lng:-82.6662,y:2019,cat:'shine',ig:'reda3sb',bio:'He started painting graffiti at seventeen — "just for fun and as a hobby." That was over a decade ago. He has been refining the same craft ever since.\n\nRicardo Delgado — Reda3sb — is a self-taught urban artist, illustrator, and designer originally from Mexico, now based in Gulfport. He was already an established muralist in Mexico before moving to the Tampa Bay area. His style is hyper-realistic portraiture executed primarily in spray paint — refining "the narrative quality of images through urban art and illustration." He tries to leave a little of himself in every mural.\n\nHe was introduced to SHINE by the Spanish photorealist graffiti master Belin, who painted the festival in 2018. Reda3sb and Belin had collaborated years earlier in Mexico.\n\nFor SHINE 2019 he painted "Feelings" at Hell\'s Bells CrossFit on 22nd Street South — hyper-realistic female faces and features arranged across geometric pastel shapes in pink, mint green, and warm peach. The premise is plain: "We laugh, we cry, we get angry, etcetera. We are all the same. Regardless of skin color, socioeconomic status, or country of origin, we all experience the same universal feelings."',desc:'A panoramic composition of hyper-realistic female faces and features — eyes, lips, a full portrait — arranged across geometric pastel shapes in pink, mint green, and warm peach tones. A blue-toned face gazes out from within a dark inverted triangle at center, flanked by golden circles and spheres. The piece spans the full width of a low-slung commercial building, blending photorealism with abstract geometry.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2019/reda3sb.jpeg',from:'Gulfport, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2019 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'He started painting graffiti at seventeen — "just for fun and as a hobby." That was over a decade ago. He has been refining the same craft ever since.\n\nRicardo Delgado — Reda3sb — is a self-taught urban artist, illustrator, and designer originally from Mexico, now based in Gulfport. He was already an established muralist in Mexico before moving to the Tampa Bay area. His style is hyper-realistic portraiture executed primarily in spray paint — refining "the narrative quality of images through urban art and illustration." He tries to leave a little of himself in every mural.\n\nHe was introduced to SHINE by the Spanish photorealist graffiti master Belin (Miguel Angel Belinchon Bujes), who painted the festival in 2018. Reda3sb and Belin had collaborated years earlier in Mexico. Belin is internationally known for photorealistic portrait murals that fuse realism with cubist fragmentation — Reda3sb shares that hyper-real portrait lineage.\n\nFor SHINE 2019 he painted "Feelings" at Hell\'s Bells CrossFit, 131 22nd St S in St. Petersburg — hyper-realistic female faces and features arranged across geometric pastel shapes in pink, mint green, and warm peach. The premise is plain: "We laugh, we cry, we get angry, etcetera. We are all the same. Regardless of skin color, socioeconomic status, or country of origin, we all experience the same universal feelings." The piece functions as a humanist statement about shared emotional experience.\n\nHe also runs Delgado\'s Painting LLC. Instagram: @reda3sb.',src:['https://creativepinellas.org/artist/ricardo-delgado','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2019_SHINE_Mural_Reda3sb','https://www.instagram.com/reda3sb/','https://www.instagram.com/delgadospainting0/']},
  {id:171,a:'Taj Tenfold',t:'Sewing Seeds',loc:'1720 Central Ave',bldg:'',lat:27.770868,lng:-82.657018,y:2019,cat:'shine',ig:'tajfrancis',bio:'He did the cover art for John Wick 4 and promotional artwork for HBO\'s Lovecraft Country. He calls his style "visual reggae."\n\nTaj Francis — Taj Tenfold — was born in 1989 in St. Andrew parish, Jamaica and studied Illustration at the Edna Manley College of the Visual and Performing Arts in Kingston (2007-2011). His work explores African ancestry and human identity through a surreal lens, drawing on Japanese Manga, hip-hop, Baroque painting, vintage vinyl art, and Jamaican Reggae aesthetics.\n\nClients include Marvel Studios, DC Comics, HBO (Lovecraft Country), Lionsgate (John Wick 4), Adobe, Harper Collins, and Audible. He is represented by Bernstein & Andriulli for illustration and murals. He shows at The Olympia Gallery in Kingston and held his debut solo exhibition "The Night Sees" in February 2024.\n\nFor SHINE 2019 he was a lead artist, painting "Sewing Seeds" at 1720 Central Avenue — ninety feet long by fifteen feet tall. A woman\'s face rises from a surging sea, her purple and magenta hair coiled in a rippling orange scarf, the thread made of water repairing damage to the ocean.',desc:'A vibrant two-story mural spanning ninety feet. A woman\'s face dominates the right side — rendered in shades of blue with bold features and a direct gaze, her purple and magenta hair coiled in a rippling orange scarf. To the left, a blue-toned figure appears to pull thread made of water through surging waves. Swirling ribbons of pink, coral, blue, and yellow flow across the composition like ocean currents. The palette is jewel-toned Caribbean color — crimson, orange, purple, aqua, and royal blue against a light background.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2019/taj-tenfold-sewing-seeds.jpeg',from:'Kingston, Jamaica',aud:'',insp:'The thread is made of water, repairing damage to the ocean — addressing sea rise through the artist\'s Jamaican heritage.',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2019 lead artist',fw:[{name:'tajtenfold.com',url:'https://www.tajtenfold.com'}],sbio:'He did the cover art for John Wick 4 and promotional artwork for HBO\'s Lovecraft Country. He calls his style "visual reggae."\n\nTaj Francis — Taj Tenfold — was born in 1989 in the parish of St. Andrew, Jamaica. He studied Illustration at the Edna Manley College of the Visual and Performing Arts in Kingston (2007-2011). He is based in Kingston. His work explores nuances of human behavior and identity relating to African ancestry through a surreal lens. Influences include Japanese Manga, hip-hop, Baroque paintings, vintage vinyl and poster art, Pop Art, and Jamaican Reggae aesthetics. He works in paintings, digital art, murals, video art, animation, illustration, design, and creative direction.\n\nCommercial clients include HBO (Lovecraft Country), Marvel Studios, DC Comics (cover artworks), Lionsgate (John Wick 4 cover), Adobe (Design Creator Showcase), Audible ("SUNDOWN"), Harper Collins ("War Widow" cover), Rocket League (Parallel Skin), and PURE Chocolate Jamaica. He is represented by Bernstein & Andriulli (B&A) for illustration, painting, murals, and commercial projects in the USA, UK, and Europe. He shows at The Olympia Gallery in Kingston. His debut solo exhibition "The Night Sees" opened February 17, 2024 at 10A, Kingston.\n\nFestival credits include SHINE 2019 (St. Petersburg, lead artist), POW! WOW! DC 2018 (double portrait mural at M St. NE & 3rd, Washington DC), Paint Jamaica 2014 (two murals at 41 Fleet Street, Downtown Kingston), and MANIFESTO Festival (Toronto, 2015).\n\nFor SHINE 2019 he painted "Sewing Seeds" at 1720 Central Avenue in St. Petersburg — ninety feet long by fifteen feet tall. A woman\'s face and shoulders rise from a surging sea, undulating curves of blue across her face, purple and magenta hair coiled in a rippling orange scarf. The title plays on "sewing" (cloth) — the thread is made of water, repairing damage to the ocean and addressing sea rise.\n\nInstagram: @tajfrancis (approximately 41K followers). Website: tajtenfold.com.',fn:['Cover art for John Wick 4','Promotional artwork for HBO\'s Lovecraft Country','Jamaican artist — calls his style \'visual reggae\''],src:['https://www.tajtenfold.com/','https://www.ba-reps.com/illustrators/taj-francis','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/jamaicas-taj-tenfold-leads-artist-lineup-for-2019-st-petersburg-shine-mural-festival-12254798','https://scenefromthesidewalk.blog/post/615829516343033856/sewing-seas-by-taj-tenfold']},
  {id:180,a:'Taylor White',t:'Ecstatic Dancers',loc:'276 Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. St N',bldg:'',lat:27.774788,lng:-82.646867,y:2019,cat:'shine',ig:'taylorwhitestudio',bio:'Taylor White is a painter and muralist based in Raleigh, North Carolina, whose large-scale work turns the human body into something fluid, electric, and a little bit untamed. She grew up in nearby Cary, studied illustration at the Savannah College of Art and Design, graduating in 2007, and then did something unexpected — she moved to Oslo, Norway, where she became the first full-time illustrator hired by a Norwegian ad agency, working at TRY/Apt on storyboards and campaigns. When the recession hit and the job disappeared, a friend talked her into relocating to Melbourne, Australia. She spent her time there working odd jobs and sketching surrealist ink portraits that dug into childhood memories and emotional complexity.\n\nBack in Raleigh, White pivoted hard into murals. Her 2018 piece Abstracted Motion — a 40-by-60-foot wall of interconnected dancers on the Alfred Williams Building (visible from W. Davie Street) — was a collaboration with Google Fiber that became one of the largest public-facing augmented reality murals in the world. She followed it up in 2021 with 8-Bit to 5G, another AR mural celebrating Raleigh\'s growing esports scene.\n\nHer festival resume includes Murals in the Market in Detroit, the Richmond Mural Project, BLINK Cincinnati, Branded Arts, O-Town Walls in Ontario, California, and SCOPE Miami Beach. In 2021 she self-funded a solo show called Pursuit of Happiness — 24 large-scale paintings where figures dissolve into pixels, emojis, and graphic shapes.\n\nFor SHINE 2019, her Ecstatic Dancers mural spans a wall in the EDGE District — a crowd of figures in white T-shirts caught in full-body joy, completely lost in the rhythm.',desc:'A wide mural spanning nearly a hundred feet across a white commercial building wall. A crowd of figures with pink and fuchsia skin wearing simple white T-shirts move across the surface in states of full-body expression — stretching upward, bending, curving, looking skyward, completely absorbed in dance. Painted from the waist up or just chest and shoulders, the figures fill the 14-foot-tall wall with kinetic energy, each body distinct but connected to the collective movement around it. The monochromatic pink-purple-magenta palette against the white wall gives the piece a dreamlike quality. A white column breaks the wall into two sections, but the composition flows across it.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2019/taylor-white-ecstatic-dancers.jpeg',from:'Raleigh, NC',aud:'',insp:'Inspired by improvisational dance and the exquisitely expressive movements of the human figure.',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2019 selected artist; Google Fiber AR mural collaboration',fw:[{name:'taylorwhite.art',url:'https://taylorwhite.art'}],sbio:'Taylor White is a painter and muralist based in Raleigh, North Carolina; she grew up in nearby Cary. She studied illustration at the Savannah College of Art and Design, graduating with a BFA in 2007. After graduating she moved to Oslo, Norway, where she became the first full-time illustrator hired by a Norwegian ad agency, working at the firm TRY/Apt on storyboards and campaigns. When Norway\'s economy slid into recession she relocated to Melbourne, Australia, where she worked odd jobs and sketched surrealist ink portraits exploring childhood memories and emotional complexity. Australia also connected her to the street art community through the Juddy Roller collective.\n\nBack in Raleigh, White pivoted into murals. Her 2018 piece Abstracted Motion — a 40-by-60-foot wall of interconnected dancers on the Alfred Williams Building (410 S. Salisbury St, visible from W. Davie Street) — was a collaboration with Google Fiber and Portland-based design firm Nelson Cash. It became one of the largest public-facing augmented reality murals in the world. She followed it in 2021 with 8-Bit to 5G, another AR mural celebrating Raleigh\'s esports scene.\n\nFestival credits include SHINE 2019 (St. Petersburg), Murals in the Market (Detroit), Richmond Mural Project (Virginia), BLINK Cincinnati, Branded Arts (Los Angeles), O-Town Walls (Ontario, California — 80-foot mural), the Outer Space Project, Juddy Roller (Australia), and SCOPE Miami Beach. Exhibitions in Melbourne, Berlin, Los Angeles, Miami, Atlanta, Chicago, and Detroit.\n\nIn 2021 she self-funded a solo exhibition called Pursuit of Happiness at 21 W. Hargett Street in downtown Raleigh — 24 large-scale abstract figurative paintings where human subjects appear superimposed with graphic shapes, pixels, and emojis.\n\nHer style sits at the intersection of classical figure painting and street art energy. Murals often feature crowds of bodies in motion, rendered in vivid pinks, purples, and magentas, limbs overlapping and fragmenting as if the dancers are mid-transformation. The work is rooted in improvisational dance — that moment when a body stops thinking and just moves.\n\nFor SHINE 2019, she painted Ecstatic Dancers at 276 Dr. MLK Jr. St N in the EDGE District — a crowd of figures with pink and fuchsia skin in white T-shirts, caught in full-body joy.\n\nInstagram: @taylorwhitestudio. Website: taylorwhite.art.',fn:['Created one of the world\'s largest augmented reality murals with Google Fiber','Inspired by improvisational dance — the moment a body stops thinking','Lived in Oslo and Melbourne before becoming Raleigh\'s first commissioned muralist'],src:['https://taylorwhite.art/about','https://www.levelupartists.com/lua-podcast/106','https://www.annarborartcenter.org/taylor-white/','https://waltermagazine.com/art-and-culture/taylor-white/','https://downtownraleigh.org/public-art/abstracted-motion','https://immersive-technology.com/augmentedreality/downtown-raleigh-gets-one-the-first-ar-murals-in-the-world/','https://919raleigh.com/local/abstracted-motion-by-taylor-white/','https://www.wral.com/new-augmented-reality-mural-debuts-in-downtown-raleigh/19670958/','https://www.visitraleigh.com/media/press-release/post/greater-raleigh-esports-local-organizing-committee-unveils-augmented-reality-public-art-mural/','https://downtownraleigh.org/public-art/8-bit-to-5g','https://raleighmag.com/2021/07/12/on-the-pursuit-of-happiness/','https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2021/07/06/2258443/0/en/Artist-Packs-Out-Opening-Night-and-Continues-to-Spread-the-Pursuit-of-Happiness-Throughout-the-Summer.html','https://www.visitstpeteclearwater.com/article/murals-edge-district','https://stpetecatalyst.com/countdown-to-shine-19-meet-the-international-mural-artists/']},
  {id:142,a:'Vitale Bros',t:'Bait & Switch',loc:'2010 1st Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.768137,lng:-82.660307,y:2019,cat:'shine',ig:'vitalebros',bio:'Their first mural came from a sketch on a paper napkin, handed to a restaurant owner. That was 1992.\n\nJohnny Vitale and his brother Paul started Vitale Bros in Tampa Bay hand-lettering signs and painting storefront windows. Their parents are artists. So were their grandparents. No formal training — just family tradition and thirty-plus years of practice. Their late aunt Nancy, who had Down syndrome and attended Parc Center for Disabilities, turned out to be an artist too, discovered in her final decade.\n\nIn the late 1990s they opened a warehouse studio that hosted group art, music, and fashion shows that helped birth St. Pete\'s emerging art scene. They collaborated on set design for Harmony Korine\'s Spring Breakers. They have painted for the Tampa Bay Rays, the Buccaneers, L\'Oreal, and dozens of local businesses. They call themselves the "mural police" — restoring damaged walls around the city for free.\n\nIn 2023, Johnny launched Overspray, a street art shop and graffiti school now based in Gulfport, teaching tagging, block letters, Wildstyle, and advanced mural technique to classes of up to twenty students. After Hurricane Milton, they painted free signs for businesses that lost theirs.\n\nThey have painted SHINE every year since its inception — "The Cool Kids" in 2015, "Bait and Switch" in 2019, and multiple walls since. For SHINE Origins 2025, Johnny painted two solo murals: one on the 600 block where it all began, and another at The Factory on Fairfield Ave South with his daughter Sionna.',desc:'A massive underwater scene spanning an entire warehouse wall. A giant orange octopus wraps its tentacles around shipwreck debris on the left, while sharks and a spotted eagle ray swim through turquoise water. A vintage deep-sea diver in a copper helmet explores the wreckage on the right. The text "Just Add Water! Voyage Under the Sea" floats across the center in retro lettering. Created in partnership with Sea Walls: Artists for Oceans and NOAA.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2019/vitale-bros.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE festival artist every year since inception; set design for Spring Breakers (Harmony Korine)',fw:[{name:'vitalebrothers.com',url:'https://vitalebrothers.com'}],sbio:'Their first mural came from a sketch on a paper napkin, handed to a restaurant owner. That was 1992.\n\nJohnny Vitale and his brother Paul founded Vitale Bros in 1992 in Tampa Bay, starting with hand-lettered signs and painted storefront windows. Youngest brother Joey later joined. Their parents are artists — the family artistic tradition goes back to their grandparents. No formal art education has been documented; the craft appears entirely self-taught and family-transmitted. Their late aunt Nancy had Down syndrome and attended Parc Center for Disabilities, where staff discovered her artistic talent in her final decade of life.\n\nIn the late 1990s and early 2000s, they opened a warehouse studio that hosted legendary group art, music and fashion shows credited with helping birth St. Pete\'s emerging art scene. They collaborated in set design for Harmony Korine\'s Spring Breakers (filmed in the St. Pete area). Their style blends pop art, graffiti, and traditional mural techniques — fine art meets pop art sensibility, incorporating local culture, historical elements, and visual stories reflecting community identity.\n\nClients include the Tampa Bay Rays, Tampa Bay Buccaneers, L\'Oreal / SalonCentric (national locations), Anchor Skate Supply, and Great Bay Distributors, plus numerous local businesses and private residences. Notable murals include the Marilyn Monroe on the side of the historic Playhouse Theater on Central Ave, a Virgin Mary at St. Pete Catholic High School, Power to the Patients giant fist at the Morean Arts Center, and the Nueva Cantina restaurant mural in Tampa. They describe themselves as the mural police — restoring damaged murals around the city for free.\n\nThe current Vitale Bros team includes Johnny, Paul, Nick and Jason Kekllas, Bruce Statton, and Jake Gerow. They have also collaborated under the name Up and Over with Tes One, and painted behind the State Theatre with Tes One and Palehorse.\n\nIn July 2023, Johnny launched Overspray — a retail street art shop and graffiti school, initially on the Pinellas Trail, relocated to 5013 20th Ave S. in Gulfport. Overspray teaches graffiti, tagging, block and bubble letters, Wildstyle, and advanced mural classes to groups of up to 20 students. The five-artist teaching team includes John Vitale, Nicole Salgar, Hinson Thehealer, Digital (LA), and Jujmo. After Hurricane Milton, they painted free signs for businesses that lost theirs. They host events like Hot Walls during SHINE.\n\nSHINE participation: The Cool Kids (2015, 2063 Central Ave — faces of the artists\' children), Bait and Switch (2019 — red snapper and sea turtle), SHINE 2022, SHINE 2024 (mural at Anchor Skate Supply with pro skateboarder Kris Markovich, inspired by Rays City Connect uniforms; limited-edition skateboard L\'Orange). For SHINE Origins 2025, Johnny painted two solo walls: 600 1st Ave N (the 600 block where SHINE began) and The Factory at 2606 Fairfield Ave S (collaboration with Sionna Sio Macleish, celebrating St. Pete history and landmarks).\n\nStudio: 5013 20th Ave S., Gulfport, FL. Website: vitalebrothers.com. Instagram: @johnvitale (solo), @vitalebros (collective).',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/vitale-brothers','https://stpetecatalyst.com/vitale-bros-and-shine-this-time-its-personal/','https://vitalebrothers.com/','https://vitalebrothers.com/about/','https://vitalebrothers.com/transforming-st-pete-the-artistic-impact-of-the-vitale-brothers/','https://ilovetheburg.com/overspray-mural-art-gulfport/','https://thegabber.com/vitale-brothers-birds-and-bees-in-gulfport/','https://thegabber.com/overspray-art-space-paints-signs-for-businesses-post-milton/','https://www.fox13news.com/news/vitale-brothers-paint-the-town-with-murals','https://monochronicle.com/artist/vitale-brothers/','https://www.paradisenewsfl.com/artists-spotlight-nov-2025/']},
  {id:204,a:'Aaron Tullo',t:'',loc:'1131 4th St N',bldg:'Old Northeast Jewelers / Hess Fine Art',lat:27.78398,lng:-82.63817,y:2018,cat:'commercial',ig:'aaron.tullo',bio:'He calls this his best wall. It was also his first big one.\n\nThe Milwaukee Brewers drafted Aaron Tullo twice. He pitched in their minor league system for roughly six years before injuries ended it around age 25. What came next was a pivot, not a restart. He started with nursery murals and pet portraits, connected with the Vitale Brothers in Gulfport, and worked on their jobs to learn the trade.\n\nThen Old Northeast Jewelers gave him this wall. A vintage pocket watch rendered the size of a building, signed "james tullo" in the lower-left corner. The face reads "Hess W.CO." — a nod to Hess Fine Art, the watch specialist next door. Four small Rolex-and chronograph-style watches sit inside the dial like sub-counters. The chain extends across the wall to a storefront awning and disappears into the parking lot.\n\nHis style moves between figurative realism, bold graphic design, and sign painting. He letters by hand. He collaborates frequently with Ty Maner. For SHINE Origins 2025 he painted the five-story white peacock on the Cordova Inn (Mural Quest #1).',desc:'A wall-sized hyper-realistic painting of an antique gold pocket watch hanging from its chain, occupying the full side of the Old Northeast Jewelers / Hess Fine Art building at 1131 4th St N. The watch\'s outer case is rendered in burnished gold with a deep black face. Roman numerals mark the hours in a cream-yellow tone (XII, III, VI, IX visible). The face is inscribed "Hess W.CO." in elegant gold serif lettering between the 12 and the center. Four smaller watch faces sit inside the larger dial like sub-counters: at the top, a Rolex Submariner-style black-and-silver dive watch with date window and dive bezel; on the lower left, a white chronograph with three sub-dials reading like a Daytona; at right, a small beige vintage face with hour and minute hands; at bottom, a date watch with a white face and integrated bracelet. The watch\'s hinged outer case hangs open on the left side, painted in metallic silver and gold tones to suggest depth. A heavy gold chain extends from the watch\'s crown across the wall to the right, looping past the storefront\'s red metal awning and the "Old Northeast Jewelers Fine Jewelry" hanging sign before disappearing off the right edge. Signed "james tullo" in subtle lettering near the lower-left edge of the open case. Painted on a flat tan stucco wall above a brick-paved parking area.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/aaron-tullo-pocket-watch.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE Origins 2025 selected artist',fw:[{name:'aaronjamestullo.com',url:'https://www.aaronjamestullo.com'}],sbio:'He calls this his best wall. It was also his first big one — painted in 2018 on the side of Old Northeast Jewelers at 1131 4th St N. A vintage pocket watch rendered at building scale, the dial reading "Hess W.CO." for Hess Fine Art (the watch specialist that shares the storefront). Four smaller Rolex-style and chronograph dials sit inside the main face like sub-counters; the chain swings across the building and ends near the awning. Signed "james tullo" in the lower-left.\n\nAaron James Tullo was born March 9, 1988 in St. Petersburg, Florida. He attended St. Petersburg High School under coach Stefan Futch, made second-team All-County as a sophomore in 2004, and was selected to try out for the USA Baseball Youth National Team at age 16. A throwing arm injury in the summer of 2005 complicated things, but the Milwaukee Brewers still drafted him in the 17th round (512th overall) of the 2006 MLB Draft out of high school. He turned them down and went to college.\n\nAt the University of Tennessee he pitched as a right-hander — 10 appearances as a freshman in 2007 with a 3.72 ERA and 56 strikeouts in 36.1 innings. The Brewers drafted him again in 2007, 47th round. He signed and entered their minor league system. He pitched professionally for roughly six years before injuries ended his baseball career around age 25. He also attended St. Petersburg College and is described as a USF graduate.\n\nTullo operates AJT Art + Design (also Aaron James Tullo Fine Art) out of a studio at 2411 15th Avenue North in St. Petersburg. He works in brush, spray paint, airbrush, and stencil across styles including realism, pop art, abstract, and typography. He was mentored by the Vitale Brothers, well-known St. Pete muralists — he worked on their jobs and exhibited at their Gulfport studio, including Renaissance-inspired paintings on wood at their "Birds and Bees" show.\n\nOther confirmed murals include the Hemingway-and-Dali piece at Plotnick Law (1515 4th St N), a collage-style mural at St. Pete Fishing Outfitters, work at Fortunato\'s in Pinellas Park, and an Elliott Smith Figure 8-inspired mural with an MLK quote at Josh Sheridan\'s law office in Tampa Heights. He collaborates frequently with Ty Maner.\n\nFor SHINE Origins 2025 — the festival\'s 11th year, focused on local Tampa Bay artists — Tullo was one of 17 selected artists. He painted a five-story white peacock on the facade of the Cordova Inn at 253 2nd Ave N (Mural Quest #1). Fox 13 News profiled him in September 2023 ("Retired minor league baseball player creates custom murals in Tampa Bay"). He has been covered by the Northeast Journal, Creative Loafing Tampa Bay, and The Gabber.',src:['https://www.aaronjamestullo.com/','https://northeastjournal.org/st-petes-artsy-murals-move-into-the-old-northeast/','https://www.fox13news.com/news/former-minor-league-baseball-player-creates-custom-murals-in-tampa-bay']},
  {id:130,a:'Angela Faustina',t:'Citrus Medley',loc:'1133 Baum Ave N',bldg:'Green Bench Brewing Company',lat:27.771533,lng:-82.650712,y:2018,cat:'shine',ig:'angelafaustina',bio:'Two walls of giant citrus slices at Green Bench Brewing. Brush only. Nine days.\n\nAngela Faustina was born in 1986, grew up in South Florida, and is now based in Atlanta. She graduated summa cum laude with a BA in fine art and history from New College of Florida in Sarasota, with additional coursework at Parsons and Ringling. Her style is hyperrealistic contemporary oil painting of fruit in extreme close-up — pomegranate, peaches, figs, kiwi, citrus — magnifying the textures and patterns beneath the peel. She lives with lupus, and the work runs through that frame: vibrant color and delicate intricacy, strength and fragility together.\n\nShe has had 80-plus exhibitions since 2006 across the US, Italy, Portugal, the UK, Singapore, Australia, and Hong Kong. She is represented by Signet Contemporary Art in London. She won a Georgia Visual Artists Grant (2020) and Best Book Cover of 2023 from Electric Literature. Her "Flourishing Florence" mural in Florence, Alabama spans 246 feet.\n\nFor SHINE 2018 she painted "Citrus Medley" — two seven-by-sixteen panels flanking a sliding metal gate at Green Bench, oversized cross-sections of blood oranges, grapefruits, lemons, limes, and navel oranges. It was her largest work at the time.',desc:'Two matching wall sections flanking a sliding metal gate at Green Bench Brewing Company, each approximately seven feet high by sixteen feet long. The mural features oversized cross-section slices of citrus fruits — blood oranges, grapefruits, lemons, limes, and navel oranges — painted in vivid reds, purples, magentas, yellows, greens, and oranges against a warm background. The fruit slices are rendered in Faustina\'s signature hyperrealistic style, showing the detailed membrane structure, seeds, and juice of each variety. The left wall leans toward warmer tones with more blood orange and grapefruit, while the right wall balances warm and cool with prominent lemon and lime slices. Both walls are signed "ANGELA FAUSTINA" at the bottom. She completed the piece in nine days using only a brush — it was her largest work at the time.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2018/angela-faustina.jpeg',from:'Atlanta, GA',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Georgia Visual Artists Grant (2020); Best Book Cover 2023 (Electric Literature); SHINE 2018',fw:[{name:'angelafaustina.com',url:'https://angelafaustina.com'}],sbio:'She lives with Lupus. She paints fruit at macro scale — beauty, fragility, strength, resilience, all in one orange.\n\nAngela Faustina was born in 1986 and grew up in South Florida. She has called the Atlanta, Georgia area home since 2015. She is an American contemporary realism painter whose body of work consists mainly of oil paintings and outdoor murals depicting close-ups of fruit — pomegranate, peaches, figs, kiwi, and citrus — capturing the macro details of fresh fruit cut open when ripe. Her work walks the line between realism and idealism, treating close-cropped fruit as a complex landscape that bridges the universal and the personal. Magnifying overlooked details, textures, and patterns beneath the peel parallels the heightened awareness her body demands due to her lupus, with the dichotomy between vibrant color and delicate intricacy echoing the strength and fragility inherent to chronic illness.\n\nIn 2009 she graduated summa cum laude from New College of Florida in Sarasota, Florida, with concentrations in Studio Art and History (described in the display bio as a BA in Liberal Arts covering fine art and history). She completed additional coursework at Ringling College of Art & Design in Sarasota and at Parsons: The New School for Design in New York, NY. She credits her schooling with grounding her in the essentials of form, value, and color theory, fundamentals she returns to whenever a painting stalls.\n\nHer artwork has been collected and exhibited internationally for 16 years, with shows across North America, Europe, Asia, and Australia — specifically the United States, Italy, Portugal, the United Kingdom, Singapore, Australia, and Hong Kong. The display bio notes 80-plus exhibitions since 2006. She is represented by Signet Contemporary Art in London, England, which has described her work as "intimate, surprising, enigmatic, and bold." She is also represented by The Loft in Marietta, Georgia. Educators around the world include her artwork in their still life, photorealism, and color curricula.\n\nHer murals are located throughout the Southeastern United States, in cities including Atlanta, Marietta, St. Petersburg, and Florence, Alabama. The display bio notes a Georgia Visual Artists Grant in 2020 and a Best Book Cover of 2023 award from Electric Literature, plus a 246-foot mural titled "Flourishing Florence" in Florence, Alabama. Her downtown Florence mural was covered by Bernie Delinski in TimesDaily in June 2021. Her Atlanta murals were profiled by Aileen Farshi for ARTS ATL in April 2020 in a piece titled "Today in street art: Angela Faustina\'s mouthwatering murals grew from still-lifes." Her Marietta work was covered by Kristal Dixon in The Atlanta Journal-Constitution in April 2019, and by Cobb Life Magazine via MDJOnline.com.\n\nShe competed in the 2018 SHINE St. Petersburg Mural Festival and the 2019 OuterSpace Project Mural Festival. For SHINE 2018 she painted "Citrus Medley" (also referenced as "Citrus Melody" by stpeteartsalliance.org) in collaboration with Green Bench Brewing — two seven-by-sixteen-foot panels, brush only, completed in nine days. The St. Petersburg muralist lineup was announced August 30, 2018, by Tampa Bay Reporter (tbreporter.com), and Maggie Duffy covered the Tampa Bay fall art scene including her work in the Tampa Bay Times in September 2018. Bill DeYoung wrote about her in St Pete Catalyst in April 2020 in connection with the Arts Relief Fund. Her SHINE mural is documented at visitstpeteclearwater.com and stpeteartsalliance.org. Creative Loafing has profiled her as well.\n\nShe describes her oil paintings as sticky-sweet, transforming glistening fruit into its own world, capturing perfectly ripe juicy flesh in each brushstroke. The paintings are bright, lively, intimate, and bold, close-cropped but not confined. She says the common subject matter of fruit represents a universally shared experience that spans borders, religion, and culture, and her stated mission is to teach people to slow down and explore the beauty and vibrancy of life in unexpected places. She learned her craft through trial and error, making many bad fruit paintings before the good ones came, and has spoken about how fear of judgment held her back early on before she pushed past it.\n\nOnline she can be found at angelafaustina.com, on Instagram as @angelafaustina (instagram.com/angelafaustina), on Facebook at facebook.com/AngelaFaustina, on YouTube at youtube.com/angelafaustina, and via linktr.ee/AngelaFaustina. Her studio shop offers original oil paintings, featured interiors, murals and public art, and commissions for paintings or murals. She was profiled by CanvasRebel Magazine in September 2024.',fn:['Lives with Lupus — paints through it','Macro-scale fruit paintings','Hyperrealistic botanical still life'],src:['https://angelafaustina.com/artist-info/about/','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angela_Faustina','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/angela-faustina-georgia/','https://canvasrebel.com/meet-angela-faustina/']},
  {id:140,a:'Cecilia Lueza',t:'Encounter',loc:'2612 22nd St S',bldg:'ArtsXchange',lat:27.765763,lng:-82.663528,y:2018,cat:'commercial',ig:'cecilialuezaap',bio:'She painted an entire intersection. Fifty volunteers helped. Then a storm washed it away, so they painted it again.\n\nCecilia Lueza was born in 1971 in Posadas, in Argentina\'s subtropical northeast — a landscape of dense flora and fauna that still runs through everything she makes. She earned her MA in Visual Arts from the National University of La Plata in Buenos Aires, held her first solo exhibition there in 1998, and moved to the United States shortly after. She settled in Pinellas County in 2007.\n\nHer work spans murals, monumental sculptures, and mixed-media installations, all built on bold geometric patterns, clean lines, and jewel-tone color. Birds, women, and children recur. She was originally a monochromatic painter in Argentina — Florida\'s light changed that.\n\nThe public art resume is staggering: nearly 60 installations since 2013. Starbucks, the North Carolina Museum of Art, the Atlanta BeltLine, the James Museum, a kinetic angel sculpture in Chattanooga acquired for permanent public display. Her work sits in the Bass Museum of Art, the Museum of Contemporary Art Miami, and the Florida Department of State collection.\n\nShe is a four-time SHINE artist — "Common Ground" in 2016 (the intersection), "Encounter" and "The Blue Hour" in 2018, "The Sky Is the Limit" in 2019, and "Radiance" at the Haddy Factory for SHINE 2025 Origins.',desc:'Two stylized birds face each other on a cinder-block wall at the entrance to the ArtsXchange campus. On the left, a red bird with a dark head faces right, its fanned tail and wing feathers radiating outward in warm geometric wedges of red, orange, pink, magenta, and yellow. On the right, a blue bird faces left, its feathers spreading in cool geometric wedges of deep navy, royal blue, teal, and green. The two birds are silhouetted against a pale sky-blue background, their mirrored poses creating a sense of dialogue or greeting. "LUEZA" is signed in blue at the lower left. The style is Lueza\'s signature geometric abstraction — each feather rendered as a clean, flat color wedge radiating from a central spiral.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/cecilia-lueza-encounter.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'Originally painted in 2018, the mural deteriorated over time from fading and nearby construction damage. In April 2023, Lueza restored the piece with funding from a National Endowment for the Arts grant administered through Creative Pinellas. She and ArtsXchange leadership chose restoration over a new design because they remained "very fond of this one."',maw:'',aaw:'Sculpture Key West People\'s Choice Award (2006); Karrvaz Artist-in-Residency, Spain (2006); Florida Artist Enhancement Grant (2008); Creative Pinellas Professional Development Grant (2008); Tourist Development Council Grant, Miami-Dade (2011); four-time SHINE festival artist',fw:[{name:'lueza.com',url:'https://www.lueza.com'}],sbio:'She painted an entire intersection. Fifty volunteers helped. Then a storm washed it away, so they painted it again.\n\nCecilia Lueza was born in 1971 in Posadas, Misiones Province, Argentina — subtropical northeast, dense flora and fauna, a landscape that still runs through everything she makes. She earned her MA in Visual Arts (Painting) from the National University of La Plata in Buenos Aires (1990-1994), held her first solo exhibition in Buenos Aires in 1998, and moved to the United States in 1999 after meeting Miami-based artists. She lived in Miami before settling in Pinellas County, Florida in 2007. She cites Georgia O\'Keeffe and Francis Bacon as influences.\n\nHer work spans murals, monumental sculptures, and mixed-media installations — bold geometric patterns, clean lines, jewel-tone color gradations. Birds, women, and children recur. She was originally a monochromatic painter in Argentina; Florida\'s light and vibrancy changed that. Nature is her primary subject. She calls her art an attempt to embody "universal meaning such as innocence, beauty, freedom."\n\nThe public art resume is staggering: nearly 60 installations since 2013, over 20 years as a working public artist. Major murals include "I Am My Own Muse" at the Marriott City Center in Durham NC (2019), "Exuberance" at HCC Dale Mabry Campus in Tampa (2020), "Summertime" in Clearwater (2021, selected from 190+ nationwide applicants), "Chromascape" in Jersey City (2021), "The Legend of Ondine" in Lakeland (2022), "The Starbucks Siren" at 3939 4th St N in St. Petersburg (2023), "Life Force" at Arya Residences in St. Petersburg (2024), and "Morning Ritual" at Joffrey\'s Coffee HQ in Tampa (2026).\n\nPublic sculptures include "Winds of Change" — a kinetic stainless steel angel acquired by the Chattanooga Public Art Commission for permanent display — "Luminarium" at the Arts and Innovation Center in Rockville MD (interactive wall sculptures with fiber optics and LEDs activated by sound), "Time Flies Sundial" at River Terrace Education Campus in Washington DC, multiple school commissions for the DC Department of General Services, "Delta" and "Nova" in Jacksonville, "Dual Nature" in Reno, "Journey of Hope" in Fort Myers, "Nexus" in Tampa, "Caleidoscopio" in Toledo, "Sea Orb" in Jacksonville, and "Golden Hour" at the Orange County Administration Center in Orlando (2026).\n\nClients and commissions include Starbucks, the North Carolina Museum of Art (mural for a Frida Kahlo/Diego Rivera exhibition), the Atlanta BeltLine, the City of Atlanta Mayor\'s Office of Cultural Affairs, the Chattanooga Public Art Commission, Hillsborough Community College, and municipalities in Indianapolis, Clearwater, Fort Myers, and Jacksonville. Her work is in the collections of the Bass Museum of Art Miami, the Museum of Contemporary Art Miami, the Florida Department of State, and 13+ institutional collections. Gallery representation includes Kavachnina Contemporary, Onessimo Fine Art (Palm Beach Gardens), Mary Martin Art Gallery (Naples), and Naomi Silva Gallery (Atlanta).\n\nAwards include a Tourist Development Council Grant (Miami-Dade, 2011), Professional Development Individual Artist Grant (Pinellas County, 2008), Florida Artist Enhancement Grant (2008), Puffin Foundation Grant (2006), Karrvaz Artist-in-Residency in Spain (2006), and Sculpture Key West People\'s Choice Award (2006).\n\nShe is a four-time SHINE artist: "Common Ground" (2016) — a street mural at Central Ave and 5th Street outside Florida CraftArt, painted with 50+ volunteers, washed away by a storm, painted again; "Encounter" (2018) at the ArtsXchange in the Warehouse Arts District; "The Blue Hour" (2018) at ONE St. Petersburg; "The Sky Is the Limit" (2019) — a 164 x 11 foot ground mural at North Shore Park pier; and "Radiance" (2025) at the Haddy Factory for SHINE Origins. Instagram: @cecilialuezaap. Website: lueza.com.',fn:['Painted an entire intersection with volunteers','Argentine, trilingual (Spanish, English, Portuguese)','Geometric public art at urban scale'],src:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cecilia_Lueza','https://www.lueza.com/bio/','https://www.lueza.com/mural-art/','https://www.lueza.com/public-sculpture/','https://canvasrebel.com/meet-cecilia-lueza/','https://www.letstalkdurham.com/lets-talk-magazine/cecilia-lueza-artist-at-work','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/cecilia-lueza-colors-our-world/','https://kavachnina.com/other-collected-artists/cecilia-lueza/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/cecilia-lueza-2','https://monochronicle.com/artist/cecilia-lueza-art-projects/']},
  {id:210,a:'Cecilia Lueza',t:'The Blue Hour',loc:'100 1st Ave N',bldg:'ONE St. Petersburg',lat:27.771342,lng:-82.634121,y:2018,cat:'shine',ig:'cecilialuezaap',bio:'She painted an entire intersection. Fifty volunteers helped. Then a storm washed it away, so they painted it again.\n\nCecilia Lueza was born in 1971 in Posadas, in Argentina\'s subtropical northeast — a landscape of dense flora and fauna that still runs through everything she makes. She earned her MA in Visual Arts from the National University of La Plata in Buenos Aires, held her first solo exhibition there in 1998, and moved to the United States shortly after. She settled in Pinellas County in 2007.\n\nHer work spans murals, monumental sculptures, and mixed-media installations, all built on bold geometric patterns, clean lines, and jewel-tone color. Birds, women, and children recur. She was originally a monochromatic painter in Argentina — Florida\'s light changed that.\n\nThe public art resume is staggering: nearly 60 installations since 2013. Starbucks, the North Carolina Museum of Art, the Atlanta BeltLine, the James Museum, a kinetic angel sculpture in Chattanooga acquired for permanent public display. Her work sits in the Bass Museum of Art, the Museum of Contemporary Art Miami, and the Florida Department of State collection.\n\nShe is a four-time SHINE artist — "Common Ground" in 2016 (the intersection), "Encounter" and "The Blue Hour" in 2018, "The Sky Is the Limit" in 2019, and "Radiance" at the Haddy Factory for SHINE 2025 Origins.',desc:'A large-scale mural wrapping the corner of the ONE St. Petersburg residential tower at street level. A woman\'s face in profile, rendered in deep cobalt and navy blue with sculpted highlights, faces right across the long wall. Long ribbons of color flow from the left side of the composition toward her — bands of red, orange, yellow, green, teal, blue, magenta, and pink rolling and curling in Lueza\'s signature geometric wave style. A smaller cluster of blue and rainbow swirls coils near the woman\'s neck. "LUEZA" is signed in black in the upper right above her forehead. The crisp white architecture of the ONE building and a strip of low landscaping frame the piece.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2018/cecilia-lueza-blue-hour.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Sculpture Key West People\'s Choice Award (2006); Karrvaz Artist-in-Residency, Spain (2006); Florida Artist Enhancement Grant (2008); Creative Pinellas Professional Development Grant (2008); Tourist Development Council Grant, Miami-Dade (2011); four-time SHINE festival artist',fw:[{name:'lueza.com',url:'https://www.lueza.com'}],sbio:'She painted an entire intersection. Fifty volunteers helped. Then a storm washed it away, so they painted it again.\n\nCecilia Lueza was born in 1971 in Posadas, Misiones Province, Argentina — subtropical northeast, dense flora and fauna, a landscape that still runs through everything she makes. She earned her MA in Visual Arts (Painting) from the National University of La Plata in Buenos Aires (1990-1994), held her first solo exhibition in Buenos Aires in 1998, and moved to the United States in 1999 after meeting Miami-based artists. She lived in Miami before settling in Pinellas County, Florida in 2007. She cites Georgia O\'Keeffe and Francis Bacon as influences.\n\nHer work spans murals, monumental sculptures, and mixed-media installations — bold geometric patterns, clean lines, jewel-tone color gradations. Birds, women, and children recur. She was originally a monochromatic painter in Argentina; Florida\'s light and vibrancy changed that. Nature is her primary subject. She calls her art an attempt to embody "universal meaning such as innocence, beauty, freedom."\n\nThe public art resume is staggering: nearly 60 installations since 2013, over 20 years as a working public artist. Major murals include "I Am My Own Muse" at the Marriott City Center in Durham NC (2019), "Exuberance" at HCC Dale Mabry Campus in Tampa (2020), "Summertime" in Clearwater (2021, selected from 190+ nationwide applicants), "Chromascape" in Jersey City (2021), "The Legend of Ondine" in Lakeland (2022), "The Starbucks Siren" at 3939 4th St N in St. Petersburg (2023), "Life Force" at Arya Residences in St. Petersburg (2024), and "Morning Ritual" at Joffrey\'s Coffee HQ in Tampa (2026).\n\nPublic sculptures include "Winds of Change" — a kinetic stainless steel angel acquired by the Chattanooga Public Art Commission for permanent display — "Luminarium" at the Arts and Innovation Center in Rockville MD (interactive wall sculptures with fiber optics and LEDs activated by sound), "Time Flies Sundial" at River Terrace Education Campus in Washington DC, multiple school commissions for the DC Department of General Services, "Delta" and "Nova" in Jacksonville, "Dual Nature" in Reno, "Journey of Hope" in Fort Myers, "Nexus" in Tampa, "Caleidoscopio" in Toledo, "Sea Orb" in Jacksonville, and "Golden Hour" at the Orange County Administration Center in Orlando (2026).\n\nClients and commissions include Starbucks, the North Carolina Museum of Art (mural for a Frida Kahlo/Diego Rivera exhibition), the Atlanta BeltLine, the City of Atlanta Mayor\'s Office of Cultural Affairs, the Chattanooga Public Art Commission, Hillsborough Community College, and municipalities in Indianapolis, Clearwater, Fort Myers, and Jacksonville. Her work is in the collections of the Bass Museum of Art Miami, the Museum of Contemporary Art Miami, the Florida Department of State, and 13+ institutional collections. Gallery representation includes Kavachnina Contemporary, Onessimo Fine Art (Palm Beach Gardens), Mary Martin Art Gallery (Naples), and Naomi Silva Gallery (Atlanta).\n\nAwards include a Tourist Development Council Grant (Miami-Dade, 2011), Professional Development Individual Artist Grant (Pinellas County, 2008), Florida Artist Enhancement Grant (2008), Puffin Foundation Grant (2006), Karrvaz Artist-in-Residency in Spain (2006), and Sculpture Key West People\'s Choice Award (2006).\n\nShe is a four-time SHINE artist: "Common Ground" (2016) — a street mural at Central Ave and 5th Street outside Florida CraftArt, painted with 50+ volunteers, washed away by a storm, painted again; "Encounter" (2018) at the ArtsXchange in the Warehouse Arts District; "The Blue Hour" (2018) at ONE St. Petersburg; "The Sky Is the Limit" (2019) — a 164 x 11 foot ground mural at North Shore Park pier; and "Radiance" (2025) at the Haddy Factory for SHINE Origins. Instagram: @cecilialuezaap. Website: lueza.com.',fn:['Painted an entire intersection with volunteers','Argentine, trilingual (Spanish, English, Portuguese)','Geometric public art at urban scale'],src:['https://www.lueza.com/mural-art/','https://www.lueza.com/bio/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/cecilia-lueza-2/','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cecilia_Lueza']},
  {id:123,a:'DAAS',t:'We Are Going to Be Friends',loc:'128 20th St S',bldg:'Tri-J Towing',lat:27.769537,lng:-82.660215,y:2018,cat:'shine',ig:'daas',bio:'He apprenticed six years under a muralist in Daytona Beach. Then he moved to Japan and worked for Universal Studios Osaka.\n\nDAAS — his legal name — grew up in Jacksonville and St. Petersburg. He earned an associate\'s in graphic design from Pinellas Technical College. He spent roughly a decade in Osaka, fell in love with the city, and developed a style based on Japanese origami crease patterns — bold, geometrically-inspired murals in vivid color, precision-based.\n\nHe has painted across Japan, China, Nepal, Portugal, Jordan, and Canada. Recent clients include Amazon, PayPal, and Kimpton Hotels. He worked on the Nippon Paint "Color Way of Love" school revitalization project in Shanghai. He is now based in Austin, Texas.\n\nFor SHINE 2018, he painted "We Are Going to Be Friends" — 25 feet by 103 feet, spray paint only, seven days.',desc:'A full building-side mural depicting a group of rabbits rendered in DAAS\'s signature geometric faceted style. The bunnies are built from interlocking angular planes of soft grays, whites, lavenders, and pale blues, giving them a fractured, cubist look while remaining clearly recognizable. They sit in green grass along the base of the wall. The background is a patchwork of large triangles in warm tones — golds, olive greens, corals, and tans — creating a quilt-like geometric pattern behind the animals. The DAAS tag appears in blocky letters in the upper right corner. The piece covers the entire side of the Tri-J Towing building on 20th St.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2018/daas.jpeg',from:'Austin, TX',aud:'',insp:'Fourth in an ongoing narrative series featuring a young girl interacting with nature and animals. The previous three murals in the series were painted in Italy and China. The title references the White Stripes song. Completed in 7 days using only spray paint.',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2018 selected artist',fw:[{name:'artist-daas.com',url:'https://www.artist-daas.com'}],sbio:'He apprenticed six years under a muralist in Daytona Beach. Then he moved to Japan and worked for Universal Studios Osaka.\n\nDAAS — his legal name — grew up in Jacksonville and St. Petersburg. He earned an A.A.S. in Graphic Design from Pinellas Technical College. After graphic design school he met a locally established muralist in Daytona Beach who hired him as an assistant. They worked together for nearly six years producing murals mainly for commercial clients. That apprenticeship taught him materials, styles, methods, and application, and pulled him toward large-scale work.\n\nHe flew around the world at the behest of Universal Studios Japan, working as an artist and performer. He moved to Japan in 2008, fell in love with Osaka, and decided to make it home. He stayed in Japan for over a decade — by his own count roughly 12 years — before relocating back to the United States. When he arrived in Osaka there was very little street art in the city, and he set out to change that. The more walls he painted and documented, the more recognized he became, and the easier it got to charge a fee. His focus shifted to public art projects funded by cities.\n\nHis style is based on the precise and tender folds of Japanese origami — bold, geometrically-inspired murals in vivid color, precision-based. Living in Japan and being exposed to origami was a major influence, and the precision-based mentality of Japanese craftsmen helped him refine his work and pay attention to small details. Over time the style evolved into something more relaxed and fluid, with a greater variety of dynamic colors and subject matter.\n\nHe has painted across Japan, China, Nepal, Portugal, Jordan, and Canada. Documented locations include Osaka, Tokyo, and Takatsuki in Japan; Chongqing and Shanghai and rural Chengdu and Anhui in China; and across the United States in South Salt Lake City, Utah; Salt Lake City International Airport; Dallas, Austin, San Antonio, Houston, Fort Worth, and Kerrville in Texas; Atlanta, Georgia; Loveland, Colorado; Marion, Illinois; Naples, Clearwater, and Coconut Creek in Florida; Stamford, Connecticut; San Diego, California; Johnson City, Tennessee; Winston-Salem, North Carolina; and water tank commissions in Florida and North Carolina.\n\nIn China he was commissioned by Nippon Paint Co. Ltd. — one of the largest manufacturers of paint in Asia — for the Color Way of Love project, a corporate responsibility program. The program has two parts: Color, Way of Love, which rejuvenates primary schools in rural China, and Color, Way of Love Art + City, which produces large-scale murals in Shanghai. He participated in 2017 and 2018, painting elementary schools in remote parts of Chengdu and Anhui. He calls the project one of the most meaningful of his career — it coincided with growing international recognition and his relocation back to the U.S. from Japan, and it gave him direct contact with rural students and locals.\n\nIn 2017 he collaborated with St. Petersburg artist Cecilia Lueza on "Efflugence," a piece for the Crescent Heights Neighborhood Association and the City of St. Petersburg, painted on the side of JWags Saloon at 2312 4th Street North.\n\nFor SHINE 2018 — his first year in the festival — he painted "We Are Going to Be Friends" on the west-facing wall of Tri-J Towing at 125 19th Street S (the wall actually faces 20th Street). 25 feet by 103 feet, spray paint only, seven days. SHINE 2018 ran October 6 through 14 with 37 artists — six international, five national, twenty-six local — across nine wall murals, six Coast Bike Wraps, and twelve Corner Canvas pieces.\n\nHe considers himself a public artist with a dominant focus on muralism. He emphasizes community engagement and how artwork affects the well-being of people who see it daily. Over twenty years as a professional artist he has worked through financial, mental, and spiritual challenges, and credits resilience, craftsmanship, professional relationships, and openness to other people\'s ideas with sustaining the career. He cites his decision to leave Japan after 12 years and return to the States as the most impactful pivot of his career — driven by a need to remove language and cultural barriers and pursue more public art opportunities.\n\nRecent clients include Amazon, PayPal, and Kimpton Hotels. Books he credits as influential: "The Artist\'s Way" by Julia Cameron, "The Artist\'s Guide to Public Art" by Lynn Basa, "ART/WORK" by Heather Darcy Bhandari and Jonathan Melber, and "Launching Your Art Career" by Alix Sloan.\n\nHe is now based in Austin, Texas. Website: artofdaas.com and artist-daas.com. Instagram: @daas. Twitter: @artist_daas.',fn:['Apprenticed six years under a muralist in Daytona','Self-taught turned master craftsman','Vivid tropical realism'],src:['https://www.artist-daas.com/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/shine-up-close-mural-artist-daas/','https://www.artist-daas.com/','https://canvasrebel.com/meet-daas/','https://cliburn.org/mural/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/daas']},
  {id:115,a:'Derek Donnelly',t:'365 Million Things To Do',loc:'54 7th St N',bldg:'Morean Arts Center (east wall)',lat:27.771661,lng:-82.644305,y:2018,cat:'shine',ig:'saintpaintarts',bio:'He painted a T-Rex in a Hawaiian shirt going fishing — for AARP. That tells you most of what you need to know about Derek Donnelly.\n\nA Pinellas County native, Donnelly grew up in Pinellas Park, graduated from the Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School in 2001, and studied graphic design and digital illustration at IADT Tampa. He went full-time on murals in 2010. A year later he opened Saint Paint Arts gallery on the 600 block of Central Avenue — not as a vanity project, but as a launchpad. Several artists got their first real exposure there before he closed it in 2014 to focus on walls.\n\nHis style sits at the intersection of illustration and realism — playful without being childish, bold without being loud. His son Syre shows up in murals across the city. So does Florida wildlife. He doesn\'t separate the personal from the public work. In 2012, when mentor Bill "Woo" Correira died, Donnelly organized the Woomorial — a 40-artist tribute mural on the 600 block that became a landmark. That instinct to build things for other people keeps showing up. He founded COVE gallery in Pinellas Park, helped establish the Pinellas Arts Village district, and started Public Art Project Inc., a nonprofit that pushes public art into places it wouldn\'t otherwise reach and raised pandemic relief funds for local artists.\n\nHis client list runs from Fender Guitars to the Tampa Bay Rays to Sirata Beach Resort — the first major St. Pete Beach resort to commission a local artist. Multiple Best of the Bay wins. Inaugural Artmaker 2026 at Ruth Eckerd Hall. For SHINE 2025, he wasn\'t just painting — he ran operations and served as artist liaison for the entire festival while producing two murals of his own.',desc:'A whimsical mural on the east wall of the Morean Arts Center depicting an anthropomorphic T-Rex dinosaur wearing a brown fedora, round glasses, and a blue-and-white Hawaiian shirt, holding a fishing rod over the waterfront. A pink-and-blue sunset sky frames a distant city skyline across the bay. The mural is part of Donnelly\'s AARP "Age Friendly St. Pete" series — the T-Rex going fishing represents "the older generation of St. Pete enjoying life to the fullest." Signed "Donnelly @saintpaintarts."',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2018/derek-donnelly-365-million.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'https://cdn.muralquest.app/audio/115.mp3',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Multiple Best of the Bay winner (Creative Loafing Tampa Bay); inaugural Artmaker 2026 at Ruth Eckerd Hall; SHINE 2025 Operations Lead; SHINE 2018 Open Call curator; co-founded downtown St. Pete mural movement',fw:[{name:'saintpaintarts.com',url:'https://www.saintpaintarts.com'}],sbio:'He painted a T-Rex in a Hawaiian shirt going fishing — for AARP. That tells you most of what you need to know about Derek Donnelly.\n\nA Pinellas County native who grew up in Pinellas Park, Donnelly graduated from the Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School in 2001 and studied graphic design and digital illustration at IADT Tampa. He has 20-plus years of painting experience and went full-time on murals in 2010. Within a year he opened Saint Paint Arts gallery on the 600 block of Central Avenue in downtown St. Petersburg — not as a vanity project, but as a launchpad for other artists. Several got their first real exposure there before he closed it in 2014 to focus entirely on walls.\n\nIn the early 2010s, Donnelly and collaborator Sebastian Coolidge co-founded the downtown St. Pete mural movement when the area was, by his own account, "drab and virtually art-free." Together they painted "A Moment to Reflect" on the Florida Craftsmen building — at the time the largest mural in St. Petersburg, four stories tall, completed in ten days. It depicts a businessman removing his shoes to embrace creativity, with a green tie nodding to sponsor Regions Bank.\n\nIn November 2012, mentor Bill "Woo" Correira died from complications of brain surgery. Correira was known for his fish paintings and his fierce support of the 600 block arts community. Donnelly organized the Woomorial, a tribute mural on the side of the Saint Paint gallery where 40-plus artists contributed — Donnelly painted Woo\'s portrait while others added marine life. It became a 600 block landmark.\n\nHis style blends illustration and realism — bold character work, signature gradients, hand-drawn detail, playful without being childish. His son Syre appears in murals across the city, including the Outshine project at Planet Retro Records on Dr. MLK Jr. Street N, where he collaborated with Hinson the Healer, R2 Romero, and Artist Slim. Florida wildlife is a constant. He has painted portraits of Prince, Kurt Cobain, and Tupac Shakur. His canvases featuring Syre were exhibited in the "Son-Shine" show at the Wayward Goose.\n\nDonnelly\'s client list includes AARP, Hilton Hotels, the Tampa Bay Rays, MLB, Fender Guitars, St. Petersburg College, Sirata Beach Resort (two murals as part of a $15 million renovation — the first major St. Pete Beach resort to commission a local artist), Rococo Steak (a ceiling mural), Station House, and Smith & Associates Real Estate. His work spans Clearwater Beach, Gulfport, Treasure Island, Madeira Beach, Largo, Dunedin, Tampa, Seminole, North Ft. Myers, and Anna Maria Island.\n\nThat instinct to build things for other people keeps showing up. He founded COVE gallery (Creative Opportunities & Visual Entertainment) in late 2016 in the Pinellas Arts Village — a city-funded project using rehabbed shipping containers at 5705 Park Blvd in Pinellas Park. He helped establish the Pinellas Arts Village district from the beginning and hosts ArtWalk events there every fourth Saturday. He started Public Art Project Inc., a nonprofit that creates large-scale beautification projects, educates the public on the social and economic impact of the arts, and raised pandemic relief funds for local artists. He is a member of the Bloom Collective and a participant in the Carmada art car movement alongside Sebastian Coolidge, James Oleson, Daniel Barojas, and Zulu Painter.\n\nHe helped originate the SHINE Mural Festival and in 2018 curated five "Open Call" walls for the festival. Multiple Best of the Bay wins from Creative Loafing Tampa Bay. Selected as the inaugural Artmaker 2026 at Ruth Eckerd Hall\'s Marcia P. Hoffman School of the Arts — a year-long exhibition in the hall\'s galleries with a featured artwork on the annual holiday ornament. He appeared on The Catalyst Sessions (May 2020) and RHStv\'s "Jabari\'s Beautiful People: Featuring St. Pete Murals."\n\nFor SHINE 2025, executive director Helen French brought him on as Operations Lead and artist liaison — the person with "the pulse" of the city. It was his first time as a featured SHINE artist after a decade of painting city walls. He produced two murals: a vintage postcard piece reading "Greetings from Hollander Hotel" at 421 4th Ave N, and a panoramic ocean scene with his signature pelican-riding boy at 1847 1st Ave N.\n\nThe dinosaur in the Hawaiian shirt fishing off the waterfront? That\'s his AARP "Age Friendly St. Pete" series on the east wall of the Morean Arts Center — the older generation, he says, enjoying life to the fullest.',fn:['Opened Saint Paint Arts on the 600 block','His son Syre appears in murals','Festival operations + two murals for SHINE 2025'],src:['https://www.visitstpeteclearwater.com/profile/365-million-things-to-do-derek-donnelly/140385','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/age-friendly-st-pete-with-aarp/121-shine-aarp/609-keep-on-cruising-aka-extreme-ethel-by-derek-donnelly','https://www.saintpaintarts.com/','https://www.rutheckerdhall.com/news/detail/artmaker-2026','https://stpetecatalyst.com/catalyst-sessions-recap-derek-donnelly/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/muralist-derek-donnelly-wants-art-scene-changes/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/for-year-11-shine-mural-festival-brings-it-back-home/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/w/art-inspired-by-hip-hop-legends-meet-derek-donnelly/','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/heres-how-derek-donnellys-jazzing-up-the-sirata-with-murals-on-st-pete-beach-12223445','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/derek-donnelly-will-talk-about-his-recently-uncovered-st-pete-mural-this-weekend-19808486/','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/derek-donnellys-cove-displays-a-broad-spectrum-of-visual-art-trendsetters/','https://www.localshops1.com/blog/business-profile-painting-the-town-171','https://www.tbnweekly.com/diversions/museums_theater/article_9b54abd0-3339-5496-88fd-bcf4034ffb04.html','https://www.paradisenewsfl.com/artists-spotlight-nov-2025/','https://www.pinellasartsvillage.com/donnelly-cove.html','https://stpetemuraltour.com/2014/08/12/derek-donnelly/','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/bill-correira-woo-remembered-by-the-600-block-arts-community-he-ardently-supported-12299303','https://patch.com/florida/stpete/bill-woo-correira-was-humble-challenged-people-to-be-great','https://www.smithandassociates.com/blog/smith--associates-real-estate-commissions-local-mural-titan-derek-donnelly-for-public-art-project.html','https://ilovetheburg.com/st-pete-shine-origins/','https://stpeteissupercool.com/blogs/latest-stories/developers-build-condos-shine-builds-culture-this-year-is-all-about-local-talent','https://83degreesmedia.com/tampa-bay-arts-roundup-shine-mural-festival-arts-annual-more/']},
  {id:125,a:'Illsol',t:'Notice Us',loc:'2010 1st Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.76994,lng:-82.660513,y:2018,cat:'shine',ig:'',bio:'The name means "illuminated soul" — sol is sun in Spanish, a nod to Michelle\'s half-Cuban heritage.\n\nMichelle Sawyer and Tony Krol are Illsol, a husband-and-wife team based in Tampa. Michelle completed a traditional tattoo apprenticeship and has a teaching background. Tony is a self-taught stencil artist. They have worked as a duo since 2014, with Michelle going solo since 2021. Their style blends clean-lined, often symmetrical imagery with distressed, layered textures — stencil work, graffiti, aerosol portraits, and solid color blocks.\n\nThey founded Mergeculture Gallery in Tampa Heights (2017) and co-founded the Heights Walls community mural program. Clients include T-Mobile, the City of Tampa, and the Tampa Convention Center. Their SHINE 2018 mural "Notice Us" depicted an endangered Florida panther.',desc:'A massive Florida panther portrait dominates the wall, its face rendered in photorealistic detail with piercing gold-green eyes, whiskers, and tawny fur blending into the surrounding abstract elements. The panther looms 22 feet high over the intersection. Behind and around the big cat, concentric rings in turquoise and coral create a haloed effect, while scattered teal dots, monstera leaf silhouettes, and fragmented geometric patterns fill the background. The palette balances the warm realism of the panther against cool abstract elements in teals, corals, and greens.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2018/notice-us.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL',aud:'',insp:'A plea on behalf of Florida\'s endangered wildlife. The Florida Panther was nearly declared extinct in the 1990s, with only 120-230 adults remaining. \'Notice Us\' asks humans to recognize the impact of development on Florida\'s fragile ecosystem.',maw:'',aaw:'Founded Mergeculture Gallery (2017); co-founded Heights Walls; SHINE 2018',fw:[{name:'illsol.com',url:''}],sbio:'The name means "illuminated soul" — sol is sun in Spanish, a nod to Michelle\'s half-Cuban heritage.\n\nMichelle Sawyer and Tony Krol are Illsol, an artist duo based in Tampa, Florida. The name is pronounced "ee-sol" and means Illuminated Soul. The project began as Illusionz in 1996 and went through several iterations before becoming Illsol. Sawyer and Krol married in 2013 and started painting together in 2014–2015, working as a duo through 2021. Since 2022, Sawyer has worked under the Illsol name as a solo artist; the duo\'s joint output spans 2014 through 2021.\n\nSawyer earned her degree in Fine Arts from the University of South Florida and brings a teaching background and a traditional tattoo apprenticeship to the work. Krol is a self-taught stencil artist with roots in graffiti. The Illsol style combines clean-lined, often symmetrical, bold imagery with distressed, layered, muted textures — moving between stencil work, graffiti foundations, rendered aerosol portraits, illustration, and solid blocks of color.\n\nIn 2017, the duo founded Mergeculture Gallery in Tampa Heights at 1544 N Franklin Street, a contemporary art space focused on the urban contemporary, graffiti, and new contemporary art movements. Mergeculture has shown work by internationally known contemporary artists alongside Tampa Bay area artists including Justin Wagher, Jujmo, Bekky Beukes, Palehorse (Chris Parks), Tes One, and the muralist Sentrock, among others. They also founded Heights Walls, a community mural initiative that grows neighborhood connections through public art installations in Tampa Heights.\n\nTheir large-scale mural work spans commercial, historical, and community-centered projects across the Tampa Bay area. Documented walls (most co-designed and co-painted as a duo through 2021) include the Tampa Heights mural at Cafe Hey raising awareness of interstate expansion in a historic neighborhood, the West Tampa Community Mural at Salcines Park, "For the Love of THIS City" at The Portico, "Riverwalk Memories" at Anchor Riverwalk, the Tampa Heights Mural at Shuffle, and the TPA Mural at Westshore City Center. Sawyer\'s solo work since 2022 includes the 1937 Antifascist Women\'s March mural in Ybor City and displays for the Skatepark of Tampa\'s 30th anniversary. Her most recent public piece was created as part of Valspar\'s 2025 "Be Bright" campaign and is located on the Feeding Tampa Bay building just outside Ybor City.\n\nBrand and institutional clients include T-Mobile, the City of Tampa, the Tampa Convention Center, and Valspar. For SHINE Mural Festival 2018, Illsol painted "Notice Us" — a mural depicting an endangered Florida panther — in St. Petersburg. They have credited Palehorse Design (Chris Parks), Chad Mize, and Tes One as influences and friends in the regional scene.\n\nBeyond the gallery and mural work, Sawyer\'s experience as a gallery co-founder has carried into commercial art consulting; most recently she served as Art Events Coordinator at The Bricks in Ybor City and organized the Ybor Arts Tour in spring 2024. She continues to assist local businesses in integrating art into their establishments and is recognized by Creative Pinellas as a multifaceted visual artist whose work blends community and history through public art.\n\nContact and links: Mergeculture Gallery at 1544 N Franklin Street, Tampa, FL 33602; phone (813) 586-4765. Website: illsol.com. Instagram: @_illsol_. Facebook: illsol. Twitter: illsol. Mergeculture is on Facebook, Instagram, and Twitter as @mergeculture.',fn:['\'Illuminated soul\' — sol is sun in Spanish','Bold graphic street art','Local Florida artist'],src:['https://voyagemia.com/interview/meet-illsol-michelle-sawyer-tony-krol-illsol-located-tampa/','https://illsol.com/us','https://creativepinellas.org/artist/michelle-sawyer/','https://stpetemuraltour.com/2018/10/10/illsol/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/illsol']},
  {id:114,a:'J&S Signs',t:'Point of Pines',loc:'553 9th St N',bldg:'',lat:27.778632,lng:-82.646407,y:2018,cat:'shine',ig:'jandssigns',bio:'The Spanish for "Pinellas" is "point of pines." They painted three vintage 1950s St. Pete storefronts on this wall and gave it the same name.\n\nJeffrey Sincich (born 1990, Belleair, FL) and Josh Stover met as ceramics majors at the University of Florida and graduated in 2012. They founded J&S Signs in St. Petersburg in 2013, started by painting positive-message murals around the city ("You Are My Sunshine" at the Morean Arts Center, "Love Your Neighborhood" on Central Avenue), then transitioned into professional sign painting in a style drawn from 1930s-1950s lettering — bold, simple, with a signature mint-and-red palette.\n\nThey relocated to Portland, Oregon around 2015 and rebranded as "Variety Shop" in 2021. Their client list runs through Nike, Adidas, Patagonia, Airbnb, and the Portland Trail Blazers. For SHINE 2018 they came back to St. Pete and painted "Point of Pines" — a 15-by-60 retro storefront scene of buildings that never quite existed.',desc:'A 15-by-60-foot mural in a retro, bold children\'s-book style using muted pink, mustard brown, sea green, and orange. The composition depicts three vintage St. Pete buildings: a green one-story motel at left with a 1950s neon "Motel" sign and an "All Are Welcome" placard; a two-story mustard post office at center with archways and a vintage "Oranges $1.99" sign; and a pink two-story apartment at right with orange and brown columns. The title "Point of Pines" references the Spanish meaning of "Pinellas" — the county\'s original name. Period details include a green bench, palm trees, and a layered skyline evoking mid-century Florida.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2018/js-signs-point-of-pines.jpeg',from:'Portland, OR (originally St. Petersburg, FL)',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2018 festival artist',fw:null,sbio:'They met as ceramics majors at the University of Florida. Their clients include Nike, Adidas, Patagonia, and the Portland Trail Blazers.\n\nJeffrey Sincich (born 1990, Belleair, FL) and Josh Stover — J&S Signs — met as ceramics majors at the University of Florida and graduated in 2012. They began painting positive-message murals in St. Petersburg and transitioned into professional sign painting, founding J&S Signs in 2013. Their style draws from 1930s-1950s hand lettering — bold, simple, with distinctive letter shapes and a signature mint-and-red palette. They relocated to Portland, Oregon around 2015 and rebranded as "Variety Shop" in 2021. Clients include Nike, Adidas, Patagonia, Airbnb, and the Portland Trail Blazers.\n\nSt. Pete murals include "You Are My Sunshine" at the Morean Arts Center and "Love Your Neighborhood" on Central Avenue. For SHINE 2018 they painted "Point of Pines" at 553 9th St N — a 15-by-60-foot retro mural in muted pink, mustard, sea green, and orange depicting three vintage St. Pete buildings. The title references the Spanish meaning of "Pinellas" — the county\'s original name. Period details include a green bench, palm trees, and a mid-century skyline.\n\nInstagram: @jandssigns.',fn:['Met as ceramics majors at University of Florida','Traditional sign painting craft','Hand-lettered typography'],src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/js-signs/','https://www.pdxmonthly.com/home-and-real-estate/2019/02/this-duo-makes-some-of-portland-s-most-eye-catching-signs','https://www.jeffreysincich.com/about']},
  {id:135,a:'LOOK the Weird',t:'Existence is Absurd',loc:'1114 Central Ave',bldg:'Central Arcade',lat:27.770858,lng:-82.650443,y:2018,cat:'shine',ig:'looktheweird',bio:'He co-founded The Weird — an international graffiti crew alongside Nychos, Low Bros, and DXTR. He has been painting walls since he was fifteen in East Germany.\n\nLars Wunderlich — LOOK the Weird — was born in 1981 in Neubrandenburg, East Germany. He studied art at Ernst Moritz Arndt University in Greifswald and earned a graphic design degree from HTW Berlin. His current work uses algorithm-generated portraits with distortions and glitches — post-graffiti visual collages exploring technology and art, parallel to the New Leipzig School but centered on AI aesthetics.\n\nHe co-founded PEACHBEACH design company (2007-2019) and KIDZONCOMPUTERS animation studio (2020-present). His "AM I JUST A CODE FOR YOU?" showed at Urban Nation Museum in Berlin (2022-2024). He has done residencies in Denver, Belgrade, and Chennai.\n\nFor SHINE 2018, he painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A wide mural on the west-facing wall of the Central Arcade building at 1114 Central Avenue, visible to eastbound traffic on 1st Avenue South. The piece is a surreal, dreamlike panorama painted in Wunderlich\'s signature distortion-collage style. Flowing organic shapes in pink, magenta, teal, mint green, and cream swirl across the wall, evoking melting landscapes and wavy glass. Among the abstract forms, recognizable elements emerge: a figure reading or holding books, rippling striped surfaces, plant-like tendrils, and architectural fragments. The composition references Florida\'s environment — the piece was inspired by hurricane damage, red tide, and the contradictions of contemporary life in the state. The overall effect is of something familiar viewed through a funhouse mirror: colorful and inviting at a distance, unsettling and thought-provoking up close.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2018/look-the-weird.jpeg',from:'Berlin, Germany',aud:'',insp:'"My piece in St. Pete is all about Florida and the environment. I love to provoke questions." — LOOK the Weird',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2018 selected artist; The Weird founding member',fw:null,sbio:'He co-founded The Weird — an international graffiti crew alongside Nychos, Low Bros, and DXTR. He has been painting walls since he was fifteen in East Germany. Lars Wunderlich, working as LOOK the Weird, was born in 1981 in Neubrandenburg in the GDR and has been making murals, paintings, and drawings since 1996. He studied art at Ernst Moritz Arndt University in Greifswald and earned a graphic design degree from the University of Applied Sciences Berlin (HTW Berlin). He is based in Berlin.\n\nHe is a founding member of The Weird, the nine-member international street art crew and artist collective established in 2011-2012, whose roster includes Nychos, Low Bros, DXTR, and Vidam. Before that, in 1999, he co-founded Gangsters of Doom, a Berlin-Hamburg graffiti crew started in the woods of Mecklenburg. He co-founded PEACHBEACH, the design company he ran with Vidam from 2007 to 2019. In 2020 he and Niklas Warnecke from Instant Waves founded the animation studio KIDZONCOMPUTERS as a joint venture for animated work. He also works as a senior designer specialized in illustration, motion, branding, concepts, games, and murals.\n\nHis current work consists of visual collages and post-graffiti pieces that replicate portraits generated by algorithms, juxtaposing different time elements to produce collisions, distortions, and glitches. The work explores the unreal nature of those representations and contrasts tranquil landscapes surrounded by heavy industry with elements from action movies, pop culture, and celebrities. His paintings bear similarities with the New Leipzig School, with technology at the core and aesthetics boosted by artificial intelligence and modern faux-semblants.\n\nHis piece "AM I JUST A CODE FOR YOU?" was included in the exhibition "Talking… & Other Banana Skins," curated by Michelle Houston at Urban Nation Museum for Urban Contemporary Art on Bülowstraße in Berlin, running June 2022 through August 2024. He was previously featured in "Urban Nation 01" at Urban Nation Museum for Urban Art in Berlin in 2017. He has also been working at the Urban Spree artist residency in Berlin preparing a solo show.\n\nFor SHINE 2018 he painted in St. Petersburg, Florida, and returned for the "shine on st pete alumni show" group exhibition and residency at the Morean Art Center in St. Petersburg, FL.\n\nHis exhibition and residency history includes "Walls - a Louvre Show" group exhibition at Urban Spree Gallery in Berlin in 2020; "Awaking" group show at 2KnewConcious Gallery with Ryan Joseph Gallery in Denver, USA in 2020; "quatre" group show at Loft du 32 Galery in Paris in 2019; his solo show "whatarewelookingfor?" at Urban Spree Gallery in Berlin in 2019; Urban Art Fair Paris with Urban Spree Gallery in 2018 and 2019; Urvanity Madrid with Urban Spree Gallery in 2019; "what the weekend is gallery" during Gallery Weekend Berlin at Art Union in 2017; "WE 48" curation at 48h Neukölln in Berlin in 2017; his solo show "Eastern German Realities" at Gallerie Altes Zollamt in Hamburg in 2017; "South x so weird" group show at Mindzai Art & Vinyl in Austin, Texas in 2017; "Knotenpunkt" group shows at Affenfaust Gallery in Hamburg in 2014 and 2016; "5 years the weird crew" group show and residency at Mural Harbour in Linz and REM Artspace in Vienna in 2016; the "WE FESTIVAL" curation at RIXBOX Neukölln, Berlin in 2016; the duo show "momentum" at PROOF Gallery in London in 2015; "the weird world nyc" group show and residency at Exit Room Gallery in New York in 2015; his solo show "the nightsky is full of rainbows" at REM Artspace in Vienna in 2015; "The Haul" at Affenfaust Gallery in Hamburg in 2015; the "Conquer the Concrete" residency at the Goethe Institute in Chennai, India in 2015; SCOPE at Art Basel Miami with ABV Gallery of Atlanta, Georgia in 2014; his solo show "Meanwhile in ULTRALAND" at Epicentro Art Gallery in Berlin in 2014; "the weird goes Belgrade" group show and residency at the Goethe Institut in Belgrade, Serbia in 2014; his solo show "it was all green instead" at Balsamo Gallery in Mexico DF in 2013; "The weird rehab 2" group show at Art is a 4 letter word in Basel in 2013; "The weird rehab" group show and residency at Clienia in Littenheid, Switzerland in 2013; "the weird in Detroit" group show and residency at Innerstate Gallery in Detroit in 2013; "ich bin ein Berliner" group show and residency at the Musée des Beaux-arts d\'Arras in France in 2013; and the "4560 Urban Art Show" group show and residency at Stadtgalerie Saarbrücken in 2013.\n\nHis residencies have taken him to Denver, Belgrade, Chennai, St. Petersburg (FL), Detroit, New York, Linz, Vienna, Littenheid, Arras, Saarbrücken, and Berlin\'s Urban Spree. Press coverage includes a Juxtapoz studio visit and artist feature, Widewalls interviews and a podcast appearance, the Widewalls Chennai Travel Diaries, a Vantage Point radio show interview, Spraydays artist talks, a VNA Magazine artist feature, a Graffiti Art exhibition feature, and a Down by Law Magazine interview. His portfolio is at larswunderlich.myportfolio.com and he can also be found on Artsy, Widewalls, MutualArt, Facebook, and Instagram.',fn:['Co-founded The Weird — international graffiti crew','Vibrant character-based murals','St. Pete local artist'],src:['https://urban-nation.com/artist/look-the-weird/','https://larswunderlich.myportfolio.com/about','https://gallery1to1.com/looktheweird']},
  {id:152,a:'Sebastian Coolidge & Rif Raf Giraffe',t:'',loc:'Central Ave',bldg:'',lat:27.771525,lng:-82.662163,y:2018,cat:'shine',ig:'sebastiancoolidge',bio:'Coolidge dropped out of St. Petersburg College his first semester. His first two years as an artist he dumpster-dived for paint and bartered murals for food.\n\nSebastian Coolidge moved from Independence, Missouri to St. Petersburg around 2008 and biked downtown nightly to paint, carrying supplies on his back. His 2012 mural at Freshly Squeezed on MLK led to steady commissions. His style blends classic animation and Lowbrow art — dark and surreal one wall, light and whimsical the next. He co-painted "A Moment to Reflect" with Derek Donnelly on the Florida CraftArt building — four stories tall, finished in ten days, the largest mural in St. Petersburg at the time. He also runs the clothing brand SC1912.\n\nJason Harrington — Rif Raf Giraffe — grew up in Kansas City, studied Painting and Film at the School of the Art Institute of Chicago, and painted graffiti in Chicago starting in 2002. He returned to KC in 2013 and co-founded the SpraySeeMO mural festival with his wife Ami in 2017 — the festival has produced roughly a hundred and fifty murals in Kansas City and expanded to Miami and Los Angeles. His work focuses on futurism and "future pop" — characters reflecting societal behaviors, ranging from comedic to political. Clients include Tiffany & Co. and Giphy Arts.\n\nFor SHINE 2018 they collaborated on this mural in St. Petersburg. The two share Kansas City roots and have worked together repeatedly — Wynwood in 2015 (with Oleson Art and Zulu Painter), SpraySeeMO 2018, and KC\'s Crossroads district in 2021.',desc:'Two surreal, stylized heads side by side against a dark gray background. On the left, a drooping flesh-toned face with melting features — heavy lidded eyes, elongated nose, sagging mouth — rendered in pale pinks and creams. On the right, a robot-like head with fiery orange hair, piano keys for teeth, three teal circular eyes, and a small mouse or creature piloting from inside the open skull. The robot wears a mint-green collared shirt. The style blends lowbrow illustration with surrealist character design.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2018/coolidge-rifraf.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL / Kansas City, MO',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2018 selected artists; SpraySeeMO co-founder (Rif Raf Giraffe)',fw:[{name:'rifrafgiraffe.com',url:'https://www.rifrafgiraffe.com'}],sbio:'Coolidge dropped out of St. Petersburg College his first semester. His first two years as an artist he dumpster-dived for paint and bartered murals for food.\n\nSebastian Coolidge (born approximately 1989) moved from Independence, Missouri to St. Petersburg, Florida around 2008 because his aunt lived there and he wanted a change of scenery. He enrolled at St. Petersburg College but dropped out first semester to pursue art full-time. His first two years were financially difficult — he and friend Elijah Barrett sourced discarded paint from dumpsters and bartered mural work for food, money, or clothing. He worked at Vans Store, Starbucks, and a smoke shop simultaneously while biking downtown nightly to create live paintings.\n\nHis 2012 mural at Freshly Squeezed clothing store (MLK & 1st Ave N) depicting "a kid in a newsboy cap with long outstretched arms squeezing an orange" led to steady commissions. He has painted approximately forty to fifty exterior murals. His style blends classic animation and Lowbrow art, ranging from dark and surreal to light and whimsical. He works in pencils, pens, spray paint, and oil paint with a spontaneous approach — sometimes no sketches. Notable murals include "A Moment to Reflect" on the Florida CraftArt building (co-painted with Derek Donnelly, four stories tall, completed in ten days, the largest mural in St. Petersburg at the time), "The Bends" (representing "the irrelevance of time"), and a giant face on an industrial silo near Woodlawn Park visible from I-275. He painted "Cherish Yourself" for SpraySeeMO 2024 in Kansas City. He runs the SC1912 clothing brand and participated in the Carmada art car movement. Featured in Hi-Fructose and Huffington Post.\n\nJason Harrington — Rif Raf Giraffe — is a Lebanese-Irish American artist born around 1980 in Kansas City, Missouri. He attended Johnson County Community College before transferring to the School of the Art Institute of Chicago (SAIC), where he studied Painting and Film. He lived in Chicago for a decade, started painting graffiti in 2002, and returned to Kansas City in 2013. He became a full-time artist in 2016. The name came from National Geographic footage of a giraffe being hunted, then finding a toy giraffe on his walk home — he added "Rif Raf" because he saw himself as "working-class trouble."\n\nHis work focuses on futurism and "future pop" — characters reflecting societal behaviors, messages ranging from comedic to political commentary. He works across mural painting, canvas, digital illustration, animated GIFs, and film. Clients include Tiffany & Co., Giphy Arts, Emporium at Area 15 (Las Vegas), and MoDOT. He co-founded SpraySeeMO mural festival with wife Ami Ayars in 2017 (originally called SolaNoir) — approximately 150 murals in Kansas City, expanded to Miami and Los Angeles. They co-run Upper Level Gallery in Kansas City. He has painted in London, Barcelona, Mexico, and Hawaii.\n\nFor SHINE 2018 they collaborated on a mural in St. Petersburg. The two share Kansas City roots and have worked together repeatedly: Wynwood, Miami (2015, with Oleson Art and Zulu Painter), SpraySeeMO 2018 (514 Southwest Blvd, KC), American Shaman CBD (2018, with Jake Merten), KC Crossroads (2021, with Ghost Beard), and SpraySeeMO 2020 at Royal Liquor.\n\nInstagram: @sebastiancoolidge, @rifrafgiraffe. Websites: sc1912.com, rifrafgiraffe.com.',src:['https://www.instagram.com/sebastiancoolidge/','https://www.instagram.com/rifrafgiraffe/','https://www.rifrafgiraffe.com/','https://hifructose.com/2019/03/28/the-cross-dimensional-murals-of-sebastian-coolidge/','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/ask-the-locals-sebastian-coolidge-mural-master-12301960','https://canvasrebel.com/meet-jason-rif-raf-giraffe-harrington/','https://www.startlandnews.com/2018/07/rif-raf-giraffe-crossroads/','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/bees-in-the-hive-secret-walls-with-sebastian-coolidge/']},
  {id:145,a:'Daniel Barojas',t:'Tlaloc',loc:'1211 1st Ave N',bldg:'',lat:27.772362,lng:-82.650755,y:2017,cat:'shine',ig:'r5imaging',bio:'He painted Tlaloc, the Aztec god of water and rain, as an offering — an ofrenda — to protect St. Petersburg from drought and storms.\n\nDaniel "R5" Barojas (also known as Rope5) is a Mexico-born muralist based in St. Petersburg. His work blends traditional cultures, ancient mythologies, and popular fables with urban influences — bold geometric patterns, vibrant color, sharp linework. The Tlaloc face on this wall — green-skinned with spiral eyes and a fanged mouth, framed by interlocking cube patterns and crashing ocean waves — is a representative example.\n\nHe painted this wall for SHINE 2017. He went on to win the 2018 St. Pete Arts Alliance Individual Artist Award and a Creative Pinellas Emerging Artist Award. Signed "@R5Imaging."',desc:'A wide mural on the upper half of a two-story commercial building depicting Tlaloc, the Aztec god of fertility and rain. The central figure is a massive green-faced deity with spiral eyes, fanged mouth, and a golden crown or headdress, rendered in sharp geometric patterns. The face is framed by outstretched wing-like forms filled with interlocking cube patterns in coral, teal, and brown. Crashing ocean waves in deep blues and teals flank both sides. A teal hexagonal cube pattern fills the background. Signed "@R3Imaging" in the lower right. The palette is dominated by teal, coral, green, gold, and deep blue.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/daniel-barojas-tlaloc.jpeg',from:'Mexico',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,src:['https://r5imaging.com/','https://www.instagram.com/r5imaging/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/daniel-r5-barojas','https://creativepinellas.org/artist/daniel-barojas/','https://monochronicle.com/artist/daniel-barojas/','https://stpetemuraltour.com/2017/11/15/daniel-r5-barojas/','https://www.artsy.net/artist/daniel-r5-barojas','https://www.visitstpeteclearwater.com/article/murals-edge-district']},
  {id:163,a:'Jujmo',t:'Aquatic Forest',loc:'1701 1st Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.770471,lng:-82.656416,y:2017,cat:'shine',ig:'jujmo',bio:'She grew up as a military kid moving between bases overseas with limited access to cartoons. When she finally got to watch anime in the U.S., she decided to become an artist.\n\nCheryl Weber — Jujmo — is a Filipina American artist based in Tampa. She earned her BFA from the University of South Florida and started freelancing immediately after graduation. Her style is colorful and playful — saturated colors, no straight lines, happy intertwined worlds of strange creatures influenced by anime, Japanese folklore, and Filipino folklore. She paints on walls, helmets, shoes, tote bags, and cars.\n\nShe has ten-plus years of mural experience and painted the "B" in the Black Lives Matter street mural in front of the Dr. Carter G. Woodson Museum for Juneteenth 2020. She gave a lecture at the Imagine Museum in 2024 and was featured on WEDU Arts Plus (PBS). Her work is her true love: "I want it to be your time to shine, just as much as mine."',desc:'A wide panoramic mural covering a commercial wall, filled edge to edge with colorful abstract blob creatures and organic shapes. The palette spans soft pinks, greens, teals, yellows, purples, and deep blues. Playful characters with simple dot eyes and small mouths peek out from amorphous cloud-like forms. Some shapes resemble mushrooms, coral, or amoebas. The composition flows like an underwater garden or alien landscape — dense, layered, and cheerful. Trees frame the mural on both sides.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2017/jujmo-space-rainbows.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2017 and 2021 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'She grew up as a military kid moving between bases overseas with limited access to cartoons. When she finally got to watch anime in the U.S., she decided to become an artist.\n\nCheryl Weber — Jujmo (pronounced "jooj-moe") — is a Filipina American artist based in Tampa, FL. She earned her BFA from the University of South Florida (approximately 2013) and began freelancing immediately via email, Instagram, and Facebook. Her style is colorful and playful with saturated colors and heavy influence from anime, Japanese folklore, and Filipino folklore. She is characterized by bright colors and the absence of straight lines — happy intertwined worlds of fanciful creatures. She paints on walls, helmets, shoes, tote bags, and cars.\n\nSHINE credits: 2017 ("Aquatic Forest," 1701 1st Ave S — 48 feet wide by 14 feet high) and 2021 (A-1 Recovery, 2221 5th Ave S, plus a week-long mural education course for the Shirley Proctor Puller Foundation). She painted the "B" in the Black Lives Matter street mural in front of the Dr. Carter G. Woodson African American Museum for Juneteenth 2020. Other murals include Black Radish Grocery and Love Food Central in St. Petersburg.\n\nShe gave a lecture at the Imagine Museum Artist Lecture Series (September 2024) and was featured on WEDU Arts Plus (PBS, Season 14 Episode 8). She launched a free Instagram livestream drawing show during COVID-19. She created a limited-edition design for WEDU PBS supporters.\n\nInstagram: @jujmo. Website: jujmo.com.',fn:['Grew up as a military kid between bases overseas','Anime and street art fusion','Vibrant character illustration'],src:['https://jujmo.com/about','https://creativepinellas.org/artist/cheryl-weber/','https://ilovetheburg.com/jujmo-murals-tampa-st-pete/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/jujmo','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/blm-mural','https://www.tampabay.com/life-culture/arts/visual-arts/2020/06/19/a-new-black-lives-matter-mural-has-been-unveiled-in-st-petersburg/','https://www.eventbrite.com/e/artist-lecture-series-featuring-jujmo-tickets-976117282067','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/tampa-artist-jujmo-to-discuss-public-art-and-her-origin-story-at-museum-lecture-series-18468229','https://www.pbs.org/video/1408-jujmo-oebmso/','https://www.wtsp.com/article/news/community/jujmo-tampa-bay-area-filipino-artist/67-00a27014-b3f2-417a-bc9d-fa96e85af452','https://voyagemia.com/interview/meet-cheryl-weber-aka-jujmo-miami-tampa-bay/','https://registrytampabay.com/shine-mural-festival-2021-its-a-world-tour/']},
  {id:146,a:'Mikael B',t:'Inner Visions',loc:'1123 1st Ave N',bldg:'',lat:27.772337,lng:-82.650497,y:2017,cat:'shine',ig:'mikaelb',bio:'He titled this wall "Inner Visions." Crystals erupt from a turbulent sea. "I want to keep dreams alive."\n\nMikael B was tagging in Copenhagen at fifteen under the name KETS. By 2014, his abstract world map "Vibrant World" had crashed his website with a million Facebook views. He paints for Nike and Bentley, shows alongside Banksy and Kaws at Taglialatella Galleries, and runs the Mikael B Foundation, which builds schools in Cambodia. He shifted toward pure abstraction in 2017, paints multiple canvases at once, and uses four-meter ladders to view them from above.\n\nThe graphic-design sensibility is underneath: sharp geometric crystal forms colliding with fluid organic energy, references from Baroque to Cubism, spray paint and acrylic. He has painted Wynwood Walls (2022), the ArtShare Building in Downtown LA (9,300 sq ft, Certificate of Recognition from the City of LA), and Google\'s Santa Monica HQ.\n\nFor SHINE 2017 he painted at 1123 1st Avenue North in the Edge District. The crystals erupting from the blue sea — that\'s him.',desc:'A long horizontal mural on a dark-toned wall in the Edge District. The composition features a dynamic collision of sharp, faceted geometric crystal forms — angular star shapes and prismatic shards in icy whites, pale blues, warm corals, and deep burgundy — interwoven with sweeping brushstroke-like curves in dark olive, black, and pink. Misty teal and aqua clouds billow along the bottom edge, and paint splatters dot the surface. The overall effect suggests crystals erupting from a turbulent atmospheric field. The palette moves between cool icy blues on the left and warm sunset pinks and corals on the right. "PRIVATE PROPERTY" signs are mounted on the wall surface.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2017/mikael-b.jpeg',from:'Copenhagen / Los Angeles',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Wynwood Walls 2022; Certificate of Recognition from City of Los Angeles (2018); Red Cross Denmark ambassador',fw:[{name:'mikael-b.com',url:'https://shop.mikael-b.com'}],sbio:'He started painting graffiti at fifteen in Copenhagen under the name KETS. He co-founded a design agency, moved to LA, and in 2014 his abstract world map "Vibrant World" crashed his website with a million Facebook views.\n\nMikael Brandrup — Mikael B — was born June 5, 1984 in Holbaek, Denmark, raised on Tuse Naes west of Copenhagen. Non-identical twin brother Morten Brandrup became his manager. He started graffiti at age 15 (2000) under the name KETS in Copenhagen\'s Sydhavnen scene, immersed in hip-hop culture. He attended Copenhagen Technical School for graphic and web design, left his internship early to pursue entrepreneurship. In 2008 he co-founded design agency Identity Provided with Morten and Mickie Storm (clients: Steen & Stroem, Chiquita). He moved to Los Angeles in 2013; the agency merged with MeyerBukdahl in 2014. His abstract world map "Vibrant World" (2014) went viral with approximately one million Facebook views.\n\nHis style fuses Wildstyle graffiti lettering with graphic design sensibility — references from Baroque to Cubism expressed through spray paint and acrylic. In 2017 he shifted toward pure abstraction and gestural painting. He paints multiple pieces simultaneously and uses four-meter ladders to view canvases from above. He describes himself as a painter, muralist, sculptor, print-master, and designer.\n\nNotable murals: "Vivid Rhythms" (2018, ArtShare Building, Downtown LA, 9,300 sq ft — Certificate of Recognition from City of LA), "Everything Starts With Nothing" (2022, Wynwood Walls), "Room for Reflection" (2019, Artscape Festival, Sweden), "Moment of Clarity" (2021, West Hollywood), and Google headquarters in Santa Monica (with Bumblebee). Solo exhibitions (most sold out): "Just Be" (Copenhagen, 2016), "Reality Shift" (Gregorio Escalante Gallery, LA, 2017), "Never Tomorrow" (Copenhagen, 2018), "Flow State" (Maddox Gallery, West Hollywood, 2021), "The Evolution of Everything" (Copenhagen, 2022). Represented by Taglialatella Galleries (New York, Paris, Toronto, Palm Beach) — work shown alongside Kaws, Banksy, Jeff Koons, and Damien Hirst.\n\nClients include Nike/Jordan Brand, American Express, Bentley, Google, LinkedIn, Heineken, Mercedes-Benz, Chase, The Black Eyed Peas, and Topps (Project 70 baseball cards). He founded the Mikael B Foundation (2017) to build schools in Cambodia and is an ambassador for Red Cross Denmark. Published monographs: "Infinite Universes" (2016) and "Never Tomorrow" (2018). Featured in LA Times, Hi-Fructose, and Cultured Magazine.\n\nFor SHINE 2017 he painted "Inner Visions" at 1123 1st Avenue North in St. Petersburg — spiky triangular bursts of purple, white, and orange with gold-tipped points emerging from a turbulent blue sea. Quote: "With Inner Visions I want to keep dreams alive."\n\nInstagram: @mikaelb. Website: mikael-b.com.',src:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mikael_Brandrup','https://shop.mikael-b.com/the-artist/','https://thewynwoodwalls.com/artists/mikael-b/','https://blog.molotow.com/introducing-mikael-brandrup-aka-kets-x-interview-photos/','https://www.culturedmag.com/article/2022/12/05/mikael-b-denmark-artist-graffiti/']},
  {id:195,a:'Sam Yong',t:'',loc:'',bldg:'',lat:27.77012,lng:-82.66147,y:2017,cat:'shine',ig:'samyongart',bio:'Two ospreys face each other across a building corner. The left bird descends toward a brass lantern wrapped in chain, a portrait glowing inside it. The right bird rises from breaking waves with the chain coiled around its body. Photorealistic feather work on dark navy, painted by an Australian who flew across an ocean to paint two birds that don\'t fly across oceans.\n\nSam Yong is a Melbourne-based painter and illustrator born in 1986. He is self-taught, with a Bachelor of Visual Communication in Graphic Design as his only formal training. His work explores "the connection between human narratives which are represented through nature," and is shaped by what his own bio calls a self-confessed introvert\'s love of solitude and solo adventure.\n\nHe is represented by BeinArt Gallery in Victoria and has shown solo work at Off the Kerb Gallery (Unnatural Selection, 2013), Outré Gallery (Keepsakes, 2016), and BeinArt Gallery (Ritual, 2017), all in Melbourne. Group shows have run through Paris, New York, Los Angeles, Oakland, Portland, Auckland, and Melbourne. His mural festival list is short and selective — Temple Children (Hilo, Hawaii, 2016), SHINE (St. Petersburg, 2017), and Pow! Wow! Hawaii (Honolulu, 2018). Press coverage in Juxtapoz, Booooooom, Invurt, and Pilerats.',desc:'A two-sided mural wrapping the corner of a building. On the left wall, a photorealistic osprey with white head and dark brown body swoops down from the upper right with wings spread; its talons grip an old brass lantern wrapped in chain, with what appears to be a portrait visible inside the lantern, and the bird descends into breaking white-capped waves below. The number "2060" is visible in white text in the lower-left corner. On the right wall, a second osprey rises from the waves with similar plumage, beak open, also wrapped in coiling chain. Both birds are rendered with detailed feather work in shades of brown, white, gray, and cream. The dark navy background lets the foam of the waves and the metallic sheen of the chains stand out. The corner column between the two walls is painted gray with dark brick pillars on either side.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2016/angela-delaplane-ospreys.jpeg',from:'Melbourne, Australia',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Represented by BeinArt Gallery (Victoria, AU); SHINE 2017 selected artist; Pow! Wow! Hawaii 2018 selected artist; Temple Children Hilo 2016',fw:[{name:'samyong.art',url:''}],sbio:'Two ospreys face each other across a building corner. The left bird descends toward a brass lantern wrapped in chain, a portrait glowing inside it. The right bird rises from breaking waves with the chain coiled around its body. Photorealistic feather work in browns, whites, grays, and cream against dark navy. The address "2060" is painted in white text on the left panel. Painted by Australian artist Sam Yong for the SHINE Mural Festival in 2017.\n\nSam Yong is a self-taught Melbourne, Australia-based painter and illustrator born in 1986. His only formal training is a Bachelor of Visual Communication in Graphic Design. He works in traditional painting and illustration across multiple mediums, exploring what he describes as "the connection between human narratives which are represented through nature." His own bio calls him "a self-confessed introvert" with "a love of nature and solo adventure," and that voice runs through the work — quiet, observational, tied to wilderness rather than urban scenes.\n\nHe is currently represented by BeinArt Gallery in Victoria, Australia. Solo exhibitions include Unnatural Selection at Off the Kerb Gallery in Melbourne (2013), Keepsakes at Outré Gallery in Melbourne (2016), and Ritual at BeinArt Gallery in Melbourne (2017). Group exhibitions have run through Paris, New York, Los Angeles, Oakland, Portland, Auckland, and Melbourne across 2013–2018.\n\nHis mural festival history is short and curated rather than prolific. Documented walls include Temple Children in Hilo, Hawaii (2016); SHINE on St. Petersburg, Florida (2017) — the two-osprey wall now catalogued as Mural Quest #195; Pow! Wow! Hawaii in Honolulu (2018); and the Collingwood Senior Citizens Centre in Melbourne, Victoria (2020). The two Hawaii commissions plus the SHINE St. Pete trip mark the period when he was traveling internationally for mural work; the Collingwood Senior Citizens Centre piece is closer to home.\n\nIn 2014 he gave a public talk titled Analogue / Digital in Melbourne. Press coverage spans Juxtapoz, Booooooom, Invurt Magazine, Pilerats, and additional publications between 2013 and 2017.\n\nInstagram: @samyongart. Facebook: samyongart. Website: samyong.art (with portfolio archives organized by year, 2013–2018).',src:['https://samyong.art','https://samyong.art/about','https://samyong.art/murals']},
  {id:111,a:'Sarah Sheppard',t:'Life Reimagined',loc:'719 Central Ave',bldg:'Morean Arts Center',lat:27.771673,lng:-82.644074,y:2017,cat:'shine',ig:'sarah.g.sheppard',bio:'She studied metalsmithing to continue work her late father began. Art runs in the family — both her parents were artists.\n\nSarah Sheppard grew up in Dallas, studied photography, drawing, painting, graphic design, ceramics, and fabric art at the University of North Texas, and has lived in St. Petersburg for over twelve years. She also trained in metalsmithing at the Creative Jewelry Academy.\n\nHer style is colorful, high-contrast figurative work — new romanticism, movement as metaphor for continuous loss and seeking. She started her mural career painting for a spa in St. Petersburg after earlier work in Venetian plaster and faux finishing for model homes. She has multiple SHINE murals across the city, including "Life Reimagined" on the back wall of the Morean Arts Center, commissioned by AARP.\n\nFor SHINE 2023, she returned to paint in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A mural on the back wall of the Morean Arts Center framed with a navy blue border and ornate gold trim, giving it the appearance of a steampunk painting. At center, a woman merges with a banyan tree — her body becoming the trunk, her roots spreading into the ground. She reaches out to a red phoenix bird at left, symbolizing rebirth. The right side features massive interlocking bronze and gold gears representing the passage of time and the mechanics of working life. Teal water fills the base. Commissioned by AARP as part of the "Age Friendly St. Pete" SHINE All Year program in 2017.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2017/sarah-sheppard-life-reimagined.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'https://cdn.muralquest.app/audio/111.mp3',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Multiple SHINE festival murals; AARP/Morean Arts Center commission',fw:[{name:'sarahsheppardsart.com',url:'http://www.sarahsheppardsart.com'}],sbio:'She studied metalsmithing to continue work her late father began. Art runs in the family — both her parents were artists.\n\nSarah Sheppard\'s father was her mother\'s art teacher in college, so as she has put it, her life as an artist started from conception. Art surrounded her growing up and dominated her attention in school and at home. She studied photography, drawing, painting, graphic design, ceramics, and fabric art at the University of North Texas — pretty much anything she could get her hands into. While in college she moved from Texas to Florida, where her parents were living, for a change of scenery during the housing market boom.\n\nHer parents helped her start her own business doing Venetian Plaster, Faux Finishing, and Murals in model homes, businesses, and residences. The work meant tight deadlines and 14-hour days, with the satisfaction of transforming sparse white rooms into spaces with character and ambiance. In 2008 she became pregnant with her son Sebastian, right as the economy started to slide. She did not want to be on scaffolding 20 feet in the air while pregnant, and the calls for work slowed anyway. She spent the next three years as a stay-at-home mom.\n\nWhen she returned to working life, she started part-time at Brew D\'Licious, her favorite coffee shop in St. Petersburg, Florida. While working there she painted her first mural — for the spa across from the coffee shop. The wall was 30 feet long and 10 feet high, the biggest canvas she had ever worked on. She has since painted approximately 15 exterior murals as of her March 2019 Voyage Houston interview.\n\nHer best-known St. Pete piece is "Life Reimagined" on the back wall of the Morean Arts Center at Central Avenue and Seventh Street. AARP and the Morean approached Sheppard in 2015 to create a mural responding to the themes of "Life Reimagined" and "Disrupt Aging." She built a narrative on the wall: gears representing the grind of daily life and the mechanics of the working world; the central figure rendered as a Banyan tree sinking and spreading roots into the community; and a Phoenix reaching outward to symbolize rebirth and the next stage of life. AARP framed its involvement as supporting the arts in Tampa Bay because a vibrant cultural life connects people to their community. Coverage by Julie Garisto in Creative Loafing Tampa Bay (September 26, 2015) documented the piece.\n\nSheppard moved back to Texas about two years before her March 2019 Voyage Houston interview but continued to paint murals in both states. Documented out-of-state work includes a large mural on Skull & Cakebones Vegan Craft Bakery in Dripping Springs, Texas. Around the same period she began working in metal — initially Jewelry 101 at the Creative Side Jewelry Academy to finish pieces her late father had given her, then continuing with further courses. Jewelry became, in her words, a new love. Both her painting and her jewelry tend toward large, statement-making pieces, with size and color doing the heavy lifting.\n\nHer style is colorful, high-contrast figurative work with elaborate narrative elements and rich color. She describes her aim as conveying a sense of beauty, romanticism, and mysticism. Personal artworks draw from her life events and the surrounding natural world, with motifs and symbolism pulled from various historical sources — sometimes mixed with modern touches in color or application. For client work, she focuses on what the business or home wants to communicate: commercial murals build around the brand message and visual attention to the building, while residential murals tend to be more personal.\n\nSheppard has lived in St. Petersburg for over twelve years across her two stints in the city and has multiple SHINE Mural Festival pieces across St. Pete. For SHINE 2023, she returned to paint in St. Petersburg.\n\nWebsite: sarahsheppardsart.com. Instagram: @thee.divine.sarah. Facebook: theedivinesarah. Contact: 727.417.2687.',fn:['Studied metalsmithing after her late father','Multiple SHINE murals','Colorful figurative new romanticism'],src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/age-friendly-st-pete-with-aarp/121-shine-aarp/611-life-reimagined-by-sarah-sheppard','https://www.visitstpeteclearwater.com/profile/life-reimagined-sarah-sheppard/140395','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/life-reimagined-sarah-sheppard-completes-new-mural-in-downtown-st-pete-12280945','http://www.sarahsheppardsart.com/','https://voyagehouston.com/interview/art-life-sarah-sheppard/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2023_SHINE_sarah_sheppard']},
  {id:108,a:'Stephen Palladino',t:'',loc:'276 Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd N',bldg:'Benson Worley Architecture',lat:27.774637,lng:-82.646987,y:2017,cat:'shine',ig:'stephenpalladino',bio:'He started painting large-scale murals across New York City as a teenager. Tiffany and Lady Gaga are clients.\n\nStephen Palladino was born in 1986 in the Bronx. He attended a magnet art program at age ten for classical technique, but his real education was on the streets. His style merges street culture, fine art, and raw emotional storytelling — cartoon and graffiti-rooted characters that are "slick and gritty, dark and lowbrow" with hidden layers of popular imagery.\n\nClients include Tiffany & Co., Playboy, Lady Gaga, Reebok, American Express, and Capital One. He created "The Late Night Drawing Show" on social media and is a regular at Art Basel. He is based between Los Angeles and New York.\n\nFor SHINE 2017, he painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A 17-by-95-foot mural on the south wall of Benson Worley Architecture featuring a dense composition of distorted human figures and faces in bright fluorescent colors — aqua, orange, lime green, purple, blue, and rosy red. From a distance the forms read as a cheery abstract puzzle, but up close bold black line drawings reveal Palladino\'s expressive, German Expressionism-influenced style. Characters include an orange woman, a blue-suited man, and a red woman in profile, interspersed with phrases like "LOVE ME," "AIN\'T," "NOBODY but YOU," "LOOKIN NEAT," "GAS," "YO!," and "NOT TRUE" scrawled in handwritten text across the wall.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2017/stephen-palladino.jpeg',from:'Los Angeles / New York',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2017 selected artist',fw:[{name:'palladinoart.com',url:''}],sbio:'He started painting large-scale murals across New York City as a teenager. Tiffany and Lady Gaga are clients. Stephen Palladino was born November 3, 1986 in the Bronx, New York. His father, a carpenter and pinstriper, introduced him to airbrushing. His mother brought a Sicilian heritage into the household. By age ten Palladino had earned a spot in a magnet art program where he began formal training in classical techniques. The other half of his education came on the streets — painting large-scale murals throughout New York City as a teenager to earn money, sharpen his craft, and find his voice.\n\nThe result is a signature style that merges street culture, fine art, and raw emotional storytelling. Cartoon and graffiti-rooted characters sit inside dense, highly detailed compositions, often blurring the line between chaos and control. The work carries hidden layers of popular imagery and themes of identity, culture, and the imperfect nature of being human.\n\nPalladino has grown from a local muralist to a commissioned creative force, with a portfolio of high-profile collaborations and brand work. His clients and collaborators include Tiffany & Co., Playboy, Lady Gaga, Reebok, American Express, and Capital One. His work lives in private collections across Europe, Asia, and the United States. Beyond walls, his practice extends to large-scale installations, intricate drawings, and immersive digital experiences. He lives and works between Los Angeles and New York.\n\nDocumented projects include STP x Thumpers at Journal Standard in Tokyo in 2019.\n\nFor SHINE 2017, Palladino painted in St. Petersburg, Florida as part of the festival\'s third edition. The 2017 SHINE artist roster, curated by Chris Parks, included Axel Void & L.E.O., Cryptic (also written Kryptk), Daniel "R5" Barojas, Herbert Scott Davis, Hueman, James Oleson, Joram Roukes, Jose Mertz, Jujmo, Lauren YS, Mikael B, Ricky Watts, Sentrock, Sam Young, Stephen Palladino, Suarezart, Thirst & Zulu Painter, Vitale Bros., and Yok & Sheryo. The 2017 edition was organized with involvement from Iryna Kanishcheva and ran alongside an "Outside In" group exhibition that featured large-scale installations by The Yok and Sheryo, James Oleson, The Artwork of Ricky Watts, Vitale Bros., Sentrock, and others. Coverage of the festival ran on Brooklyn Street Art with photos by Iryna Kanishcheva. SHINE follows a roughly 5:10 ratio of local to traveling artists.\n\nPalladino was profiled in Hypebeast\'s "Essentials" series in May 2016 under the heading "Good and Shiddy." His own description of his characters is "slick and gritty, dark and lowbrow." He created "The Late Night Drawing Show" on social media and is a regular at Art Basel.\n\nKeywords and proper nouns associated with Stephen Palladino: Bronx, New York City, Sicilian, magnet art program, classical technique, airbrush, pinstriping, street art, graffiti, cartoon characters, lowbrow, fine art, Tiffany & Co., Playboy, Lady Gaga, Reebok, American Express, Capital One, Journal Standard Tokyo, STP x Thumpers 2019, Art Basel, The Late Night Drawing Show, Hypebeast Essentials, Good and Shiddy, Los Angeles, New York, SHINE Mural Festival 2017, St. Petersburg Florida, Chris Parks, Iryna Kanishcheva, Brooklyn Street Art, Outside In group show, Axel Void, L.E.O., Cryptic, Kryptk, Daniel R5 Barojas, Herbert Scott Davis, Hueman, James Oleson, Joram Roukes, Jose Mertz, Jujmo, Lauren YS, Mikael B, Ricky Watts, Sentrock, Sam Young, Suarezart, Thirst, Zulu Painter, Vitale Bros., Yok & Sheryo, The Yok, Sheryo. His website is palladinoart.com.',fn:['Large-scale murals across New York City','Photorealistic portraiture','East Coast to St. Pete'],src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/stephen-palladino/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine-2017','https://www.palladinoart.com/about','https://hypebeast.com/2016/5/essentials-stephen-palladino-good-and-shiddy','https://www.brooklynstreetart.com/2017/10/28/selections-from-shine-mural-festival-in-st-petersburg-florida/']},
  {id:112,a:'Alex Pardee',t:'Brightmares',loc:'251 10th St N',bldg:'',lat:27.774628,lng:-82.647193,y:2016,cat:'shine',ig:'alexpardee',bio:'He designed creatures for Zack Snyder\'s Sucker Punch. He made the album art for The Used. He calls his monsters "Brightmares."\n\nAlex Pardee was born in 1976 in Antioch, California. He is self-taught — no formal art education. His style is dark, fantastical worlds filled with ghoulish creatures in unexpectedly vibrant, saturated colors, inspired by Garbage Pail Kids and Tales From the Crypt.\n\nHe was art director, executive producer, and creature designer for Digging Up The Marrow (2014, directed by Adam Green). He has designed album art for The Used, In Flames, Aesop Rock, and Cage. Clients include Hurley International, KidRobot, and Upper Playground. He shows at Spoke Art, Corey Helford Gallery, and Gallery 1988.\n\nFor SHINE 2016, he painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A 46-by-19-foot mural depicting an emaciated, skeletal gray figure hunched beneath an enormous dripping concentric-circle orb in pink, blue, green, yellow, and orange — like a deflating rainbow balloon where the figure\'s head should be. The creature has elongated bony limbs and stands on bulbous white and tan blobs that spurt upward from the ground. A swirling black vortex fills the background. Colors drip downward from the orb as if melting in the Florida heat. This was the largest mural Pardee had ever attempted at the time, completed with assistance from fellow artist Ricky Watts.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2016/alex-pardee.jpeg',from:'Los Angeles, CA',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2016 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'He designed creatures for Zack Snyder\'s Sucker Punch. He made the album art for The Used. He calls his monsters "Brightmares."\n\nAlex Pardee was born February 5, 1976 in Antioch, California. He is self-taught — no formal art education. His style is dark, fantastical worlds filled with ghoulish creatures in unexpectedly vibrant, saturated colors, inspired by Garbage Pail Kids and Tales From the Crypt. He works in pens, ink, watercolors, and dye, and has been moving toward more acrylics, oils, and latex. He has spoken about using his art to work through depression, anxiety disorders, and emotional struggles.\n\nPardee is a freelance artist, apparel designer, and comics creator. He is best known for illustrating The Used\'s album artwork, which includes In Love and Death ("All That I\'ve Got," "I Caught Fire"), Berth ("The Bird and the Worm"), Lies for the Liars ("Liar Liar (Burn in Hell)," "Pretty Handsome Awkward"), Shallow Believer, and the single "Take It Away." Beyond The Used, he has designed album and single art for In Flames (the cover of their ninth studio album A Sense of Purpose and its first single "The Mirror\'s Truth"), Aesop Rock (The Impossible Kid), Aiden (Conviction), Street Drum Corps (Street Drum Corps, We Are Machines), Bay Area rapper TopR (Cheap Laughs for Dead Comedians, Marathon of Shame), and Cage (Hell\'s Winter, Kennylz, I Never Knew You EP, Depart from Me). He directed and produced music videos including "All That I\'ve Got," LiveLavaLive (Mitchell Davis), and the WWE Theme Song, and produced Dredg\'s "The Thought of Losing You."\n\nIn film, Pardee designed the artwork for Zack Snyder\'s Sucker Punch (first revealed at Comic-Con in 2009). In 2014 he served as Art Director, Executive Producer, and Creature Designer on Adam Green\'s biographical creature film Digging Up The Marrow. In 2015 he collaborated with hip hop artist Justin Bua on a piece titled "I Got Your Back."\n\nHis commercial client list includes Hurley International, Twenty Twenty Skateboards, Upper Playground, Kid Robot, Aiden, and Street Drum Corps. He is a member of the artist groups Cardboard City and Zerofriends, and runs the website EyeSuck Ink, which links to his blog and store. He shows at Spoke Art, Corey Helford Gallery, and Gallery 1988 (1988 Gallery).\n\nPardee released his first official art book, Awful Homesick, in October 2008. His comics and published works include And So He Bathed in the Blood of the Lepers, Bunnywith, My Book of Poetry, My Book of Feelings, The Ugliest Fairy (2006), Good Night Lava (2007 Upper Playground limited edition), Into the Marrow (2009 1988 Gallery limited edition), and The Secrets of Hollywood (2004). He has also produced calendars: Astrology Is Dead (2002), The Beautiful People Calendar (2003), and Snoring Blood Calendar (2004). His web series Chadam premiered in 2010, and he worked on a motion picture for Chadam (not to be confused with Chadam Mihlberger). He appeared at the 2012 New York Comic Con.\n\nJustin Bua has described Pardee as "a prolific artist whose imagination is populated with wicked, warped, and wacky characters that walk a tightrope between playful and grotesque. His artistic world inspires all artists to not only strive for greatness, but more importantly, to have fun."\n\nFor SHINE 2016, he painted in St. Petersburg, Florida. His series of monster characters, which he has nicknamed Brightmares, brings the same dark-yet-saturated palette he uses in his album art and creature design into mural form.\n\nOnline: EyeSuck Ink (official website), Zerofriends (official webstore), and his work has been featured in WWA gallery shows including Color Ink Book\'s 3rd Anniversary Show "Shade\'s of Ray."',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/alex-pardee/','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alex_Pardee','http://shineonstpete.com/2016/09/06/alex-pardee-brightmares/','https://stpetemuraltour.com/2016/09/15/alex-pardee/','https://spoke-art.com/collections/alex-pardee']},
  {id:148,a:'Caratoes',t:'',loc:'2125 1st Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.77042,lng:-82.661691,y:2016,cat:'shine',ig:'caratoes',bio:'Two faces dissolve into waves and leaves on a grayscale wall. The only color is a single sweep of soft pink. That contradiction — black-and-white realism colliding with a slash of vibrant linework — is her signature.\n\nCara To — Caratoes — was born and raised in Zottegem, a small Belgian village near Ghent, to Hong Kong immigrant parents. She studied two years of biochemistry, switched to game design, then took a post-production job at Wieden+Kennedy in Amsterdam. She discovered street art there in 2011, quit the agency a few years later, and moved to Hong Kong to dig into where her parents came from. She has been nomadic since 2013, with Hong Kong as her base.\n\nHer practice spans murals, metal sculptures, installations, wearable performance suits, and immersive environments. The STRAAT Museum in Amsterdam holds her "Untitled" (2016, spray and latex on polyester); she has shown at the Urban Nation Museum Berlin (Wall Poetry, 2015), painted at HKwalls Sham Shui Po (2016), Mural Festival Montreal, and Wide Open Walls Sacramento, and been featured in Juxtapoz and Hi-Fructose. Clients include Nike, Facebook, Four Seasons, and Tiger Beer (the Air-Ink campaign — ink made from air-pollution particles, painted on Tai Ping Shan Street in Sheung Wan).\n\n"Making a wall pretty isn\'t my sole responsibility," she has said. "It\'s also my chance to put a message on a wall for everyone to see in their own time." She painted SHINE 2016 (this wall) and returned for SHINE 2022.',desc:'A wide monochromatic mural spanning a concrete block wall. Two surrealist female faces emerge from flowing organic forms — the larger face on the right gazes forward with one visible eye, features dissolving into waves, leaves, and curving lines. The smaller face on the left peers out from behind swirling abstract shapes. Soft pink circles and a pale pink swooping curve on the left provide the only color against the otherwise grayscale palette of grays, whites, and blacks. Geometric elements — bridge-like structures, dashed road lines — weave through the organic forms. Signed "CARATOES" in the lower left. The wall shows weathering and graffiti tags near the door.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2016/carla-sa-fernandes.jpeg',from:'Belgium / Hong Kong',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2016 and 2022 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'Two faces dissolve into waves and leaves on a grayscale wall. The only color is a single sweep of soft pink. That contradiction — black-and-white realism colliding with a slash of vibrant linework — is her signature.\n\nCara To — known professionally as Caratoes — is a Belgian-born street artist, illustrator, sculptor, costume designer, and immersive-art maker of Hong Kong descent. She was born and raised in Zottegem, a small Belgian village near Ghent, to Hong Kong immigrant parents. She studied two years of biochemistry before switching to game design at university, then took a post-production / 3D-artist role at the Amsterdam office of advertising agency Wieden+Kennedy. She discovered street art in Amsterdam in 2011, quit the agency several years later to dig into her parents\' homeland, and moved to Hong Kong as her base. She has been nomadic since 2013.\n\nHer practice spans large-scale murals, metal sculptures, installations, wearable performance suits, and immersive environments. The STRAAT Museum\'s curatorial note describes her work as a "fusion of fragmented realism and abstract shapes," with compositions balancing black-and-white realistic portraits against vibrant — often neon — juxtaposing linework. She self-describes as a "space designer who increases the beauty and serenity of a place with my artwork and a precise message." Her stated approach to commercial walls: "Making a wall pretty isn\'t my sole responsibility — it\'s also my chance to put a message on a wall for everyone to see in their own time." On gender bias: "I wanted to do things people say girls can\'t do."\n\nDocumented mural and festival credits include HKwalls 2016 in Sham Shui Po, Hong Kong; Mural Festival Montreal; Wide Open Walls Sacramento; the Urban Nation "Wall Poetry — We Paint the Music, You Love to Hear" project (Berlin, September 30, 2015), pairing ten street artists with ten audio artists; the inaugural Urban Nation Museum opening (Berlin, 2017); and "Ode to Mother" in Reykjavik, Iceland. Her self-described count is 30-plus murals across 24 locations including Bali and Iceland. The STRAAT Museum Amsterdam holds a 2016 "Untitled" piece in spray and latex paint on polyester.\n\nNamed commercial / brand projects include Nike, Facebook, Four Seasons, and Tiger Beer\'s Air-Ink campaign — a wall painted on Tai Ping Shan Street in Sheung Wan, Hong Kong, using ink manufactured from air-pollution particles. Press features include Juxtapoz, Hi-Fructose, the South China Morning Post (HK Magazine cover-style profile by Adrienne So, September 13, 2016, "Graffiti Artist Cara \'Caratoes\' To on Materialism, Gender Bias and Repping Hong Kong Art"), Sassy Hong Kong\'s "That Girl: Caratoes" feature, and a Frankie Beane WordPress profile (May 21, 2015) describing her then-base in Zottegem, Belgium / Hong Kong.\n\nFor SHINE Mural Festival 2016 in St. Petersburg, Florida, she painted at 2125 1st Avenue South — a wide monochromatic wall with two surrealist female faces emerging from flowing organic forms (waves, leaves, curving lines), pale pink accents the only color in an otherwise grayscale palette of grays, whites, and blacks, geometric bridge-like structures and dashed road lines weaving through the organics. Signed "CARATOES" in the lower left. She returned for SHINE 2022.\n\nInstagram: @caratoes. Website: caratoes.com.',fn:['Signs her walls \'Caratoes\'','Belgian-Hong Kong artist — Cara To','Surrealist portrait style'],src:['https://caratoes.com/','https://caratoes.com/about1','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine-2016','https://straatmuseum.com/en/collection-database/caratoes','https://urban-nation.com/artist/caratoes/','https://hkwalls.org/festivals/hkwalls-2016-sham-shui-po/caratoes-2016/','https://muralfestival.com/artist/caratoes/','https://www.scmp.com/magazines/hk-magazine/article/2038265/graffiti-artist-cara-caratoes-materialism-gender-bias-and','https://www.sassyhongkong.com/that-girl-cara-toes-style/','https://www.wideopenwalls.com/artists/caratoes/','https://artpublicmontreal.ca/en/artiste/caratoes/','https://www.thedustyrebel.com/post/701102616429625344/ode-to-mother-caratoes-reykjavik']},
  {id:141,a:'Daniel Mrgan',t:'A Picnic',loc:'1701 1st Ave S',bldg:'FRSTeam by Rogers',lat:27.770584,lng:-82.656358,y:2016,cat:'shine',ig:'danielmrgan',bio:'He immigrated to the US at twenty-one as a refugee. His family fled the Yugoslav conflict from a contested city near the Serbian border, first to Berlin, then to America.\n\nDaniel Mrgan is originally from Croatia, now based in St. Petersburg. He earned a graphic design degree from St. Petersburg College and also studied at Schiller International University. His fine art is whimsical and illustrative — introspective characters, wood burnings, immersive narrative works combining illustrations, sculptures, and texts. He treats fine art as personal passion alongside his graphic design career.\n\nHis solo show "Sir Dr. Admiral Rupert Xavier\'s Paper Plane Flights to Exotic Mind Destinations" at Bluelucy captured the tone. For SHINE 2016, he painted "A Picnic" at 1701 1st Avenue South.',desc:'A 25-by-12-foot mural on the south side of the FRSTeam by Rogers building at 1701 1st Ave S, facing the sidewalk. The piece depicts a whimsical picnic scene starring two cartoon characters named Leon and Olive, rendered in a flat, graphic illustration style using a limited palette of green, gold, red, dark navy, and white. Against a rich green grass background bordered by red trees, Leon reclines reading a book while Olive daydreams at a table with coffee and art supplies. A white text bubble in the upper left reads: "Meanwhile, in a city park, Leon was reading a library book of Latino poetry while Olive daydreamed about her next business venture. They both wondered whether to have another piece of cheese." Playful details fill the scene — a smiling log, a worm in a derby hat, bicycle wheels, a butterfly, and scattered food. The building number "1701" is visible in red at the lower right. The style recalls vintage children\'s book illustration with bold outlines, flat color fills, and a gentle sense of humor.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2016/daniel-mrgan.jpeg',from:'Missoula, MT',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2016 selected artist',fw:null,sbio:'He immigrated to the US at twenty-one as a refugee. His family fled the Yugoslav conflict from a hotly contested Croatian city near the Serbian border, where his parents ran a butcher shop. His mother and father went briefly to Berlin first, leaving Daniel and a younger brother behind to finish high school, before the family received refugee visas to the United States and resettled in Pinellas County. He was 36 and living in St. Petersburg when Creative Loafing profiled him in February 2014.\n\nDaniel Mrgan is originally from Croatia, now based in St. Petersburg. He earned a graphic design degree from St. Petersburg College and also studied at Schiller International University. While he was studying at Schiller and St. Petersburg College, he began making wood burnings of whimsical characters. The work caught the eye of Kaya Parwanicka, then a curator at the Dunedin Fine Art Center and a classmate at Schiller, who put him in his first exhibition.\n\nHe treats fine art as personal passion alongside his graphic design career. As he told writer Megan Voeller, "I like to make art to have fun, so if it\'s something my livelihood depends on, it\'s not fun anymore — it\'s a job." He creates what he feels like creating, which makes him not particularly prolific by choice. He has become something of a mainstay in St. Pete art shows, with work exhibited at Bluelucy, C. Emerson Fine Arts and the Dunedin Fine Art Center.\n\nHis fine art is whimsical and illustrative — introspective characters, wood burnings, and immersive narrative works combining illustrations, sculptures, and texts. "The inner world is to me the most interesting world to explore," he told Creative Loafing. "I\'m more interested in someone who is sitting quietly than somebody who is jumping around." He uses entry-level wood burning pens and planks from Michaels, embracing the medium\'s associations with home craft and folk art, the irregularity of wood surfaces, and a kitsch aesthetic of vignetted scenes. He picked up the technique after probably seeing it on television.\n\nHis solo show at Bluelucy Gallery, Sir Dr. Admiral Rupert Xavier\'s Paper Plane Flights to Exotic Mind Destinations, ran through February 8, 2014 at 653 Central Avenue in St. Petersburg. Megan Voeller reviewed it for Creative Loafing on February 6, 2014. The show was staged like an immersive book, with viewers walking left to right around the gallery reading framed texts and seeing illustrations of Sir Dr. Admiral Rupert Xavier, the fictional and acutely neurotic protagonist. Sir Rupert wears high-waisted pants, suspenders and a formidable mustache, and a ragtime-ish soundtrack played over the gallery speakers. A wall-mounted sculpture of the character\'s head let visitors duck inside his noggin.\n\nThe exhibition included more than 30 works — texts, drawings, wood burnings and sculptures — and unfolded as a narrative about the two psychic forces governing Sir Rupert\'s life: the anxieties that constrain him and the imagination that sets him free. Half of the drawings devoted themselves to Rupert\'s paranoid solitude, like his yellow orb of a head peeking out from behind a picket fence and blue house collapsed together in space, and his melancholy anxieties, like an actual blue monster wrapping itself around his head. The wood burnings gave form to his imagined adventures, transforming the character\'s cranium into a hot air balloon, the sail of a boat, a spaceship, and a puff of smoke. One running joke is that Rupert\'s day job consists of driving a Google car as it forges a virtual copy of the world.\n\nHis childhood in Croatia included a surreal decade when armed conflict co-existed with an otherwise civilized eastern European standard of living. He has stories about venturing outside at age 12 to salvage a ping pong table with his brother and being fired at by a cannon. He was already making zines with his brother in those days. That brother also became a graphic designer and lives in Portland, Oregon. A still younger brother is a concept artist and character designer in Los Angeles.\n\nMrgan shrugs at the idea that Rupert\'s quirkiness — the beard and suspenders — could look like a hipster affectation, pointing out that the same garb recalls socialist eastern Europe from a different perspective. "Hipsters are not the first people to invent the beard or suspenders," he says. He told Voeller, "At a very young age I realized that a lot of the stuff that you\'re told, or that we read, is basically just not true."\n\nFor SHINE 2016, he painted "A Picnic" at 1701 1st Avenue South in St. Petersburg.\n\nAfter his St. Petersburg years he relocated to Missoula, Montana, where he continued working as a graphic designer and illustrator. His Tumblr at danielmrgan.tumblr.com (active through October 2021) documents Missoula-era projects including a poster for the Moon Randolph Homestead annual event (February 2021), a label for Soil Cycle composting service (October 2020) and a recycled-plywood mural for the same service (April 2020), a woodburning piece for Longstreet Gallery\'s Labor Day group show (October 2020), a Blundstone boots art-contest submission (November 2020), and a bandana design for friend Jess\'s Kakigori Bar Japanese shaved-ice business in a restored Airstream trailer. He also kept a COVID-lockdown sketchbook series of apartment objects and cat illustrations (April 2020) and a comic on voting. The Tumblr\'s about page frames his influences as "silent cinema, Eastern-European stop-motion, folk art, and melancholy aesthetics," and lists drawing, cooking, reading, and cycling in wooded areas as personal interests.',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/daniel-mrgan/','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/the-exotic-mind-destinations-of-daniel-mrgan-12308389','https://danielmrgan.tumblr.com','https://danielmrgan.tumblr.com/about']},
  {id:132,a:'Dasic Fernández',t:'Multicolored Lady',loc:'1720 Central Ave',bldg:'FRSTeam by Rogers',lat:27.770821,lng:-82.656651,y:2016,cat:'shine',ig:'dasicfernandez',bio:'He painted a 27-story building for the Super Bowl. He was the only Latin American artist selected.\n\nDasic Fernandez was born in Rancagua, Chile. He studied architecture at the University of Chile for four years, leaving in his final year to pursue art full-time. His style is portraiture with an "inverse drip" — rivulets of color floating upward from subjects. Light impressionism with political undertones, influenced by graffiti, Van Gogh, Kandinsky, and Roberto Matta.\n\nHe co-filmed Pan-American Graffiti, a documentary tracing a 100-day journey from New York to Santiago with thirteen large-scale murals. He worked with the Rebel Diaz Arts Collective on a 70-foot rooftop protest mural reading "No human being is illegal." He has painted at Wynwood Walls and was recognized by The New York Times and Americas Quarterly as an Arts Innovator.\n\nFor SHINE 2016, he painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A 90-by-17-foot mural on the east side of the FRSTeam by Rogers building, facing 17th Street. On a black background, a woman tilts her face upward with her eyes closed and arms spread wide, receiving multicolored rain. Her skin and flowing hair are rendered in dripping layers of blue, purple, magenta, orange, green, and gold — Dasic\'s signature "inverse drip" style that gives the colors a sense of upward motion. She wears a pale translucent blue sleeveless top that contrasts with the rich colors streaming across her body. White clouds float in the dark sky and white puddles frame the base of the composition. Near each shoulder, a hummingbird hovers — the one on the left sipping a colored raindrop as it falls.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2016/dasic-fernandez.jpeg',from:'New York, NY',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Super Bowl LIV mural commission (NFL/Goldman Global Arts); Americas Quarterly Arts Innovator; New York Times feature (2016); SHINE 2016',fw:[{name:'dasicfernandez.com',url:'https://dasicfernandez.com'}],sbio:'He painted a 27-story building for the Super Bowl. He was the only Latin American artist selected.\n\nDasic Fernández was born in Rancagua, a small rural town in Chile, and grew up immersed in the country\'s burgeoning hip-hop culture of the 1990s. By age 14 he had found his way into graffiti, beginning by tagging buildings with the stylized letters of his name. He moved to Santiago to study architecture at the University of Chile, where he explored the impact of art on urban spaces while experimenting with content, themes and style for his own work. "I became more in love with painting on the street than with being in class," he recalls. In his fourth year he left the university to pursue art full-time, traveling through Rio de Janeiro, Buenos Aires and Montevideo before moving to New York in December 2009.\n\nHis style is portraiture with an "inverse drip" — rivulets of color floating upward from his subjects. He cites graffiti as the root of everything he paints, alongside Vincent Van Gogh, Wassily Kandinsky and Roberto Matta as core influences. He prefers street art for what he calls its "essential" quality: a space to connect with the public directly. "Painting on the street carries great responsibility — both artistic and social."\n\nFernández is a member of the New York-based Rebel Díaz Arts Collective, whom he calls his "family from the Bronx." With them he helped paint a 70-foot rooftop mural in 2010 protesting Arizona\'s anti-immigration law. Dominated by giant yellow block letters spelling "No human being is illegal," the mural is interspersed with silhouetted human figures of all shapes and sizes, including a pregnant woman, a dancing child and an old man walking with a cane. "Our intent with the design was to restore humanity and respect" to the individual immigrant, he explained. Earlier in Santiago, after a Chilean TV station distorted his remarks in an interview, he painted a graffito of a person with a lock on his mouth near his apartment in the heart of the city. The piece became a well-known protest of censorship, a consequence he never anticipated.\n\nHis documentary project Pan-American Graffiti, three years in the making, sent him on a 100-day journey from New York to Santiago, painting 13 large-scale murals along the way. The goal was to document street art across the continent and to build a pan-American art gallery and network. "It is a project for the long term," he said, "but I believe I am laying the foundations for a community as I journey through this marvelous continent [which is] full of talent yet short on resources."\n\nIn 2010, at age 24, he was named an Arts Innovator by Americas Quarterly in a profile by Nina Agrawal. He has been recognized by The New York Times. He was selected as a Wynwood Walls artist, with works at the Miami complex spanning 2016 to 2021.\n\nFor Super Bowl LIV in February 2020, Fernández was commissioned by the NFL and Goldman Global Arts to create a mural on a 27-story downtown Miami building, the only Latin American artist among the five chosen for the project. Because the building\'s facade is entirely windows, the painting was vectorized and printed as a large-scale wrap rather than painted directly. The work centers on the fans — the people around football — and aims to be universal rather than tied to any single community, age or culture. "My personal mission through art is to talk to the people, to remind people what we are, where we are from, where we come from, what we are made of and we are all the same," he said. After roughly a decade in New York, he calls Miami his second or third city in the States: "I always felt the love of the people for my work in general and for my person too."\n\nHis broader catalog of named projects spans murals, floor and wall installations, and canvas work, including Terrazas de Arroz, Aprobemos Dignidad, The Clock, Paseo Bandera, Super Bowl, Dolphins Stadium, New York, Ultra Music Festival, Plaza de Bolsillo, The Covenant, Miami Basel Blooming, Pow Wow Worcester, Pichilemu, Bob Marley\'s Crib, Newburgh, Estadio Monumental, San Miguel and Viva Las Vegas. His mural locations and clients include Miami, New York, Santiago, Worcester, Newburgh, Las Vegas, Pichilemu, San Miguel, the Dolphins Stadium, Ultra Music Festival, Art Basel Miami, the Pow Wow Worcester festival and Estadio Monumental.\n\nFor SHINE 2016, he painted in St. Petersburg.\n\nWebsite: dasicfernandez.com.\n\nAdditional projects documented on his archival blog (dasic.blogspot.com) include "Virgin and Child" in Brooklyn, NY (2014), A3C HipHop Music Festival in Atlanta, Georgia (2014), tagging work in 1st Ave Manhattan and Queens, NY (2014), an IPic Manhattan commission (2018), and a documented session being photographed by Martha Cooper in Miami (2018). The blog also chronicles 2018 activity in Santiago (Chile), Atlanta, Toronto, Brooklyn, Philadelphia ("PHI"), Miami including the Dolphins Stadium piece, Pow Wow Worcester, and a 2018 "Florida!" post that aligns with his SHINE St. Pete stop. Contact email listed on the blog: dasic7@gmail.com.',src:['https://dasicfernandez.com/','https://americasquarterly.org/fulltextarticle/arts-innovator-dasic-fernandez-chile/','https://remezcla.com/features/culture/meet-dasic-fernandez-a-chilean-artist-commissioned-to-create-this-stunning-mural-for-the-super-bowl/','https://thewynwoodwalls.com/artists/dasic-fernandez/','https://dasic.blogspot.com/']},
  {id:151,a:'Jade Rivera',t:'El Templo - Find the Light',loc:'2536 Central Ave',bldg:'',lat:27.770883,lng:-82.66805,y:2016,cat:'shine',ig:'jade_rivera_artist',bio:'He has his own museum, gallery, clothing line, and creative lab on one street in Lima\'s Barranco district.\n\nJonatan "Jade" Rivera is a self-taught Peruvian muralist born in 1983 in Junin, raised in Lima\'s Chorrillos district. He first encountered graffiti at fourteen and has been painting walls since 1998. A visit to the Louvre helped shape his ambitions, but he never finished formal art school — he defines himself as self-taught since 1997.\n\nHis signature is large-format surrealist photorealism: human figures in dreamlike stillness surrounded by disproportionately large birds, butterflies, and candle flames. Birds recur as "messengers between worlds." The work explores Latin identity, faith, and the importance of believing in one\'s own dreams. Beyond murals he works in oil, watercolor, and wood carving.\n\nHe has painted in seventeen-plus countries across South America, North America, Europe, and the Middle East. Festival credits include Forest for the Trees (Portland), Constructo (Mexico), and Mirafau (Lima). In 2024 he painted "La Florista" in Rahway, New Jersey — his daughter Emilia holding lilies.\n\nFor SHINE 2016 he painted "El Templo — Find the Light" at 2536 Central Avenue — a boy lying across the full width of the wall, a blue ibis standing on his belly, a monarch butterfly at his neck, and a single candle flame burning in the darkness.',desc:'A surrealist photorealistic mural on a deep blue-black background. A young man lies on his back with eyes closed, wearing a light-colored shirt. A monarch butterfly with orange and black wings rests near his neck, and a blue ibis stands on his torso, bending its long neck downward into a small pool of water. A single candle flame burns between the boy and the bird. The composition spans the full width of the wall — forty-five feet of dreamlike stillness, nature and the human figure merged in silence.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2016/jade-rivera.jpeg',from:'Lima, Peru',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2016 selected artist',fw:[{name:'jaderivera.pe',url:'https://www.jaderivera.pe'}],sbio:'He has his own museum, gallery, clothing line, and creative lab on one street in Lima\'s Barranco district.\n\nJonatan "Jade" Rivera is a self-taught Peruvian street artist and muralist born in 1983 in Junin (Huancayo), Peru. At seven months old he and his mother moved to Lima, where he grew up in the Chorrillos district. His interest in art began around age eleven; he first encountered graffiti at fourteen. At seventeen he enrolled at the National Pre-School of Fine Arts (Escuela Nacional de Bellas Artes) but gave up applying at the last moment. He defines himself as self-taught since 1997 and has been making murals since 1998. A visit to the Louvre Museum in Paris was a significant formative influence.\n\nHis signature is large-format surrealist photorealism: human figures in dreamlike states surrounded by disproportionately large birds, butterflies, insects, and candle flames. Birds recur as "messengers between worlds." His work explores Latin identity, environment, faith originating from oneself, and believing in one\'s dreams. His murals are "reflections on life and its constant eventfulness, portrayed with optimism and wonder." Beyond murals he works in oil painting, watercolor, and wood carving.\n\nHe has painted in seventeen-plus countries: Peru, Colombia, Mexico, Chile, Argentina, Ecuador, Paraguay, Dominican Republic, Costa Rica, the United States, Denmark, France, Sweden, Germany, Switzerland, Spain, Italy, Slovenia, Poland, and the United Arab Emirates. Festival credits include SHINE 2016 (St. Petersburg), Forest for the Trees (Portland, Oregon), Constructo (Mexico), Mirafau Festival (Lima), and Barrio Arte (Santiago, Chile).\n\nNotable murals include "La Florista" in Rahway, New Jersey (November 2024, a collaboration between Peruvian restaurant QUILLA and the city, depicting his daughter Emilia holding lilies), "Buscando la verdad" in Santiago de Chile (2018), and "El Abrazo De Un Deseo."\n\nIn Lima\'s Barranco district he operates Jade Rivera World on Bajada de Banos street, which includes: Museo Jade Rivera (a museum of his work), Jade Rivera Lab (workshops and creative encounters), Jade Rivera Gallery (original pieces, sculptures, prints), and Despierta (his clothing line). He is represented by Quai 36 (French street art production company) and listed with the South American Street Art Fund (SASA Fund). He has a daughter named Emilia.\n\nFor SHINE 2016 he painted "El Templo — Find the Light" at 2536 Central Avenue in St. Petersburg — forty-five feet wide by thirteen feet high. A boy lies on his back across the full length of the wall against a deep blue-black background, a blue ibis standing on his belly in a pool of water, a monarch butterfly at his neck, and a single candle flame burning.\n\nInstagram: @jade_rivera_artist. Website: jaderivera.pe.',fn:['Has his own museum, gallery, and clothing line','Puerto Rican heritage, based in Tampa','Bold Latin American iconography'],src:['https://www.jaderivera.pe/','https://www.museojaderivera.com/','https://www.instagram.com/jade_rivera_artist/','https://quai36.com/en/talent/jade-rivera/','https://sasafund.org/jade-rivera/1650/','https://www.booooooom.com/2019/07/18/artist-spotlight-jade-rivera/','https://www.wionews.com/entertainment/-peru-and-latin-america-are-two-constant-heartbeats-in-my-work-says-peruvian-artist-jade-rivera-interview-1766323549732']},
  {id:133,a:'Michael Reeder',t:'Beneath the Shell',loc:'1701 1st Ave S',bldg:'FRSTeam by Rogers',lat:27.77066,lng:-82.656498,y:2016,cat:'shine',ig:'reederone',bio:'He did a residency at Red Bull House of Art in Detroit. His work has been featured in Forbes and Juxtapoz.\n\nMichael Reeder was born in 1982 in Dallas and earned his BFA in Painting from the School of Visual Arts in New York in 2007. His figurative work mixes realism with flat graphic space — vibrant colors, geometric patterns, lifelike imagery exploring identity, ambiguity, ego, and mortality. Skate and street culture run through everything.\n\nHe is represented by Allouche Gallery (LA and NY), Hashimoto Contemporary (SF), and Thinkspace Gallery. He has shown at the Fort Wayne Museum of Art, Long Beach Museum of Art, Mesa Contemporary Arts Museum, and internationally in London, Basel, Melbourne, and Honolulu. For SHINE 2016, he painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A mural on the north side of the FRSTeam by Rogers building, facing the alley between 1st Avenue South and Central Avenue. The left half of the wall is covered in a repeating three-dimensional cube pattern in pink, teal, and gold on a salmon-pink background — an optical illusion that appears to project outward from the wall. The pattern extends over a roll-up garage door. On the right, a photorealistic greyscale human skull emerges from behind the cubes, its jaw and cranium rendered in precise detail. To the far right, a fragment of a living face — just the nose, eyes, and brow — peeks from behind the skull in warm flesh tones. The composition moves left to right from abstraction to death to life, illustrating the title\'s idea of what exists beneath outward appearances.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2016/michael-reeder.jpeg',from:'Los Angeles, CA',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Red Bull House of Art residency (Detroit, 2016); SHINE 2016',fw:[{name:'reederone.com',url:'https://reederone.com'}],sbio:'He did a residency at Red Bull House of Art in Detroit. His work has been featured in Forbes and Juxtapoz.\n\nMichael Reeder is an Urban Contemporary artist from Dallas, Texas, born in 1982. He graduated with a BFA in Painting from the School of Visual Arts in New York. Alongside his exhibiting practice he works as a freelance graphic artist and at Eyecon Studios, a custom mural and design company based in Dallas. His extensive studies in fine art, combined with his background in graphic design and early exposure to graffiti, define the style he is known for: a fusion of vibrant colors, geometric patterns, and lifelike figurative imagery exploring identity, ambiguity, ego, and mortality. The work frequently expands beyond gallery confines onto expansive exterior walls.\n\nReeder\'s working method is built around ambiguity. He repeatedly works from the limited reference of a single person whom he himself does not know, constructing a fresh identity with each painting. The protagonists live in a kind of limbo, their identities seemingly within reach — strengthened by clean, voidlike geometric backdrops — yet personalities remain blurry and distant, as if a key component is missing. Critic Matthew Eaton of Uncommon Editions wrote in 2017 that Reeder\'s figures look as if they "had their skin carefully removed, their wardrobe replaced by graphic designers and thrust into the living world of Monty Python," settling into a calm, slightly amusing harmony.\n\nHe is represented by Thinkspace Projects (4217 W. Jefferson Blvd, Los Angeles, California). Documented Thinkspace shows include "The Otherealm" (2019, Main Room) — works "The Wizard," "Shapeshifter," "Dead Again," "Doomsayer," "Mortal Man," "Blockhead," "Vigilante Manny," "The Prophet," "The Outsider," "Transcendental Stencil," the "Screen Glitch I–III" prints, and the tinted concrete series "Black Skull I–V" and "Spectrum I–VIII." Earlier "mOMENt" (2018, Project Room) — "False Guru," "Epiphany I," "Split II," "Space Bandit," "Flashback," "Don Juan Matus," "Reverie I," and "Sacred Journey." "15 Years of Thinkspace" featured "BFF" (2019); the inaugural group show at Thinkspace\'s new gallery space featured "Hype Sniper" (2020); the "SWANK" show featured "Gold Digger," "Gasp," "Supreme Being," "The Moment It All Made Sense," and "Gimme The Loot."\n\nOther documented exhibitions: "Crusader" (2016) at Nexus, Brand Library & Art Center; "The Upside Down" (2016) at LAX / ORD, a curated show at Vertical Gallery in Chicago; "Release The Introvert" (2016) at SCOPE Miami; "Gold Digger" also shown at "Flourish" at the Mesa Contemporary Arts Museum (Mesa, AZ). SCOPE Miami Beach 2017 included "Made In The Image Of God," "For You My Love," "Self," "Soul," "Spirit," "Elevated," "Split I," and "Ride or Die." SCOPE Basel (Booth B03) included "Lord of the Land In Between," "Pushing Up Daisies," and "Temporal Spaced" (2017). "The New Vanguard II" at the Lancaster MOAH featured "Gestalt Dreams" (2017). "CONVERGENCE" at the University of Texas featured "Lord Commander" and "Bodhi One" (2019). POW! WOW! Hawai\'i 2019 featured "Sweet Delight (Endless Night)." "UNITY" at Volery Gallery in Dubai, UAE featured "No Dice" (2022). SCOPE Miami Beach 2025 included "Levitation IV" and "Vigilante III"; "Vigilante III" also showed at the Stand Strong Urban Art Fair at the Brand Library and Art Center in Glendale, California.\n\nHis materials extend across acrylic, oil, spray paint, screen print, latex, tinted concrete, faux turf, laser-cut steel, hand-cut wood assemblage, and recycled studio wood, on multilayered panel and cradled wood. His work has been featured in printed publications including New American Paintings, Le Petit Voyeur, and HiFructose Magazine. Brand collaborations include Santa Cruz Skateboards, New Balance, and Specialized Bicycles.\n\nFor SHINE Mural Festival 2016 in St. Petersburg, Florida, he extended his exterior wall practice to the festival — a regular thread in his career, where the work frequently moves from gallery walls onto large outdoor surfaces.\n\nWebsite: reederone.com (also michael-reeder.com per the Thinkspace artist listing).',fn:['Residency at Red Bull House of Art, Detroit','Split-face portraits — dual identity','Los Angeles-based painter'],src:['https://reederone.com/bio','https://thinkspaceprojects.com/artists/michael-reeder/','https://www.artsy.net/artist/michael-reeder','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/michael-reeder']},
  {id:33,a:'Pantonio',t:'',loc:'1901 Central Ave',bldg:'Imagine Museum',lat:27.771478,lng:-82.65896,y:2016,cat:'shine',ig:'pantonio.o',bio:'His sixty-six-meter fish mural on a Paris housing tower was the tallest piece of street art in Europe when it went up in 2014.\n\nAntónio Correia — Pantonio — grew up on Terceira Island in the Azores, surrounded by the Atlantic. He studied sculpture, drawing, and photography in the Azores and graphic arts at the Polytechnic Institute of Tomar before moving to Lisbon in the 1990s. He worked in animation design, advertising, and children\'s illustration before dedicating himself to urban art full-time around 2012.\n\nHis signature is swarms of marine life — sardines, cod, manta rays, octopus — rendered in Atlantic blues, volcanic blacks, and seafoam whites with simple, fluid lines that make the walls feel like they are moving. The palette comes straight from the Azores. He has painted walls in over eighteen countries, from a Paris bridge (Pont des Arts, after the city removed the love locks) to grain silos on his home island.\n\nFor SHINE 2016 he wrapped two walls of the Imagine Museum on Central Avenue with a swirling school of dark fish and aquatic foliage in black, gray, and teal — one of the festival\'s largest pieces, spanning roughly a hundred and thirteen feet across the corner of the building.',desc:'A massive wraparound composition covering two walls of the Imagine Museum. Swirling schools of dark fish — rendered in black, charcoal gray, and deep teal — spiral and twist through pale aquatic foliage in mint green and sage tones against a light background. The fish move in dense, fluid formations that create a vortex-like sense of motion across the full width of the building. Broad leaves and plant forms weave between the fish, adding depth and organic rhythm. The piece wraps around the corner of the building, creating a continuous underwater scene visible from multiple angles.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2016/pantonio.jpeg',from:'Lisbon, Portugal',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Macaronesian Young Artists Show distinction (2005); represented Portugal at UNESCO\'s 50th anniversary (1993)',fw:[{name:'Galerie Itinerrance',url:'https://itinerrance.fr'}],sbio:'His sixty-six-meter fish mural on a Paris housing tower was the tallest piece of street art in Europe when it went up in 2014.\n\nAntónio Correia — Pantonio — was born in 1975 on Terceira Island in the Azores, Portugal. He studied sculpture, modeling, drawing, and painting at the Instituto da Cultura dos Acores, then drawing and photography at the Professional School of Artistic Crafts in Vila Nova de Cerveira starting in 1989, and graphic arts technologies at the Polytechnic Institute of Tomar. He moved to Lisbon in the 1990s and worked professionally in animation design, advertising, and children\'s illustration. In 1993 he represented Portugal at UNESCO\'s fiftieth anniversary celebration in Romania. In 2005 he received a distinction at the Macaronesian Young Artists Show for illustration. He dedicated himself exclusively to urban art and fine art around 2011-2012.\n\nHis signature subjects are swarms of marine life — sardines, cod, manta rays, octopus — along with birds, rabbits, ropes, boats, and fantastical creatures. His palette draws from the Azores: Atlantic blues, volcanic blacks, seafoam whites, with occasional greens and purples. The style blends realism with abstraction through simple, flexible, fluid lines and undulating compositions that create a sense of movement on static walls.\n\nMajor murals include the Tour Sienne in Paris\'s 13th arrondissement (2014, sixty-six meters high by fifteen meters wide — the largest street art mural in Europe at the time, taking one month to complete), the Pont des Arts bridge in Paris (2015, painted after the city removed the love-lock grilles), the Aquarium of the Pacific in Long Beach, California (2016, his first US mural, a manta ray on approximately 2,600 square feet), and grain silos on Terceira Island in the Azores. He has painted in over eighteen countries including Portugal, France, Italy, Spain, Belgium, Denmark, Poland, Sweden, Ukraine, Russia, China, Brazil, Canada, the United States, Tunisia, Morocco, Senegal, and Cape Verde.\n\nHe is represented by Galerie Itinerrance in Paris. Solo exhibitions include "Exodus" at Galerie Itinerrance (2014), "Atlantida" at Galerie Itinerrance (2017), and "Vento" at the Instituto Acoriano de Cultura in the Azores (2019).\n\nFor SHINE 2016 he wrapped the east and north walls of the Imagine Museum at 1901 Central Avenue in St. Petersburg with a swirling underwater scene of black fish and aquatic plant life, approximately 113 feet wide by 19 feet high. The piece is rendered in black, gray, and teal tones, full of fluid movement.\n\nFestival credits include SHINE 2016 (St. Petersburg), POW! WOW! Long Beach (2016), Artscape White Moose Project (Karlstad, Sweden, 2017), Big City Life (Rome, 2015), and Fresh Paint OLLN (Louvain-la-Neuve, Belgium, 2021).\n\nInstagram: @pantonio.o.',fn:['His 66-meter fish mural covers a Paris housing tower','Portuguese artist, trained in Lisbon','Kinetic black-and-white marine life'],src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/pantonio','https://www.instagram.com/pantonio.o/','https://www.cps.pt/en/artistas/pantonio','https://www.thisiscolossal.com/2016/02/pantonio-street-art/','https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pantonio','https://itinerrance.fr','https://www.widewalls.ch/artists/pantonio']},
  {id:192,a:'Ink Werkz Crew',t:'',loc:'1610 Central Ave',bldg:'',lat:27.770947,lng:-82.655105,y:2015,cat:'shine',ig:'',bio:'Three friends painted this at SHINE\'s first year in 2015. A decade later, a building covers most of it. The signatures are still there.\n\nThe Ink Werkz Crew was Reid Jenkins, Scott Hillis, and Sovoth Chan — three St. Petersburg-based artists who painted together at the inaugural SHINE Mural Festival in late 2015. Their collaborative style leans into bold geometric forms, lightning-bolt linework, and saturated color blocks — a vocabulary that reads as graffiti-influenced public art.\n\nThis wall at 1610 Central Avenue dates to the festival\'s first year. A new building was constructed directly in front of it, covering nearly all of the original artwork. What remains visible is the slice of blue, yellow, and white geometric work along the edge — and the crew\'s three Instagram tags (@reidjenkinsart, @hillis727, @duke_727) in the lower-left corner.',desc:'A geometric SHINE 2015 mural on the side of a Central Avenue commercial building, now mostly hidden behind a new building constructed directly in front of the wall. Only a slice of the original artwork is visible — layered blue tones (light blue base, navy mid-band, yellow zigzag at the top) overlaid with jagged white lightning-bolt linework — along with the crew\'s three Instagram handles (@duke_727, @hillis727, @reidjenkinsart) and a stylized signature in the lower-left corner. A SHINE Mural Festival sticker is affixed near the signage. The full composition was bold, graphic, graffiti-influenced public art.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2015/ink-werkz-2015.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2015 (inaugural festival) selected artists',fw:null,sbio:'The Ink Werkz Crew is a St. Petersburg-based collaborative collective made up of Reid Jenkins (@reidjenkinsart), Scott Hillis (@hillis727), and Sovoth Chan (@doke_727). The three painted together at the inaugural SHINE Mural Festival in late 2015 at 1610 Central Avenue.\n\nTheir collaborative work leans into geometric forms, lightning-bolt linework, layered blue and yellow color blocks, and graffiti-influenced composition — a vocabulary that reads as bold, playful public art.\n\nA new building was later constructed directly in front of this wall, covering nearly all of the original mural. What remains visible is a slice of geometric work and the three crew signatures in the lower-left corner.',fn:['Three-artist crew — Reid Jenkins, Scott Hillis, Sovoth Chan','Painted at the inaugural SHINE 2015','A building has since been built in front — only the crew signatures are visible now']},
  {id:178,a:'Matt Callahan & Angela Delaplane',t:'St. Tampasburg',loc:'661 Central Ave',bldg:'The Lure',lat:27.771662,lng:-82.642997,y:2015,cat:'shine',ig:'manmademurals',bio:'Matt Callahan was a Tampa-based muralist who could paint just about anything and make it look effortless. Working under the name Man Made Murals, he built a reputation across Florida for detailed, technically jaw-dropping work — restaurant interiors, brewery walls, hotel lobbies, even a project at Disneyland in California. Fellow artists consistently pointed to him as the most skilled painter in the Tampa Bay area, someone who could render photorealistic detail at any scale.\n\nA Tallahassee native, Callahan studied illustration at the Atlanta College of Art, graduating in 2001 before settling in Tampa and launching Man Made Murals. His client list ran deep — Cigar City Brewing, Rick\'s on the River, Bella\'s Italian Cafe, Beach Shack Harley-Davidson in Panama City Beach — but he was equally comfortable doing large-scale outdoor work and fine commercial illustration.\n\n"St. Tampasburg" came together during the first SHINE Mural Festival in 2015, a collaboration with fellow Tampa artist Angela Delaplane of Delaplane Studios. The two wanted to celebrate the connection between Tampa and St. Petersburg, bridging the gap between the cities\' art communities. Though Callahan participated in every SHINE-related event, he was never officially on the festival roster. Instead, he showed up to help other artists — bringing extra tools, offering advice, calming nerves.\n\nCallahan passed away in April 2018 at age 40. His organs were donated, and his heart saved the life of another person. A scholarship for young artists was established in his name at LeMoyne Art Gallery in Tallahassee. The murals he left behind across Tampa Bay remain some of the most technically accomplished work in the region.',desc:'Large-scale mural featuring oversized block letters spelling "ST PETE," each letter filled with a different illustrated scene capturing the spirit of St. Petersburg. The S holds a pelican in bold graphic style; other letters frame architectural landmarks, a striking face rendered in deep blue and black tones, a tropical flower, and marine life. The palette combines rich blues, greens, blacks, and warm accents in a punchy, illustrative style. A circular "MAN MADE" logo sits to the right of the lettering. The wall is co-signed @delaplanestudios and @manmademurals.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2015/matt-callahan-st-tampasburg.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL',aud:'',insp:'A celebration of the bond between Tampa and St. Pete\'s art communities, bridging the two cities through shared creative identity.',maw:'',aaw:'Scholarship in his name at LeMoyne Art Gallery, Tallahassee',fw:null,sbio:'Matt Callahan was a Tampa-based muralist and illustrator who operated under the name Man Made Murals. Born in Tallahassee, Florida in 1978, he studied illustration at the Atlanta College of Art, graduating in 2001. After college, he returned to the Tampa Bay area and founded Man Made Murals, building a career that spanned restaurants, breweries, hotels, retail spaces, and large-scale outdoor murals across Florida and beyond.\n\nCallahan\'s technical ability set him apart. Fellow muralists and SHINE Mural Festival organizers consistently described him as the most skilled painter in the Tampa Bay area. SHINE organizer Tes One said Callahan was at the top of any list of detail-oriented, photorealistic painters who could render anything at any scale, equally impressive from one inch or twenty feet away. His range was enormous — photorealistic portraits, abstract compositions, hand-lettered signage, and densely illustrated narrative scenes.\n\nHis commercial portfolio stretched across Florida. He painted murals for Cigar City Brewing in Tampa, Rick\'s on the River, Bella\'s Italian Cafe, the Red Star Rock Bar in Seminole Heights, Union 72 BBQ, The Avenue burger restaurant, Beach Shack Harley-Davidson in Panama City Beach, and numerous other restaurants, hotels, bakeries, and bowling alleys. His work even reached Disneyland in Southern California.\n\n"St. Tampasburg" was painted during the inaugural SHINE Mural Festival in 2015, a collaboration between Callahan and fellow Tampa artist Angela Delaplane of Delaplane Studios. Both were Tampa-based artists who had done significant work in St. Pete, and the mural reflected that cross-bay bond.\n\nAlthough Callahan was deeply embedded in the SHINE community and participated in every festival-related event, he was never officially on the artist roster. He showed up anyway — to help other painters, offer advice, lend tools, and provide moral support. Artist Bekky Beukes recalled how Callahan reached out before her first St. Pete mural, unprompted, to share tricks of the trade. Derek Donnelly described Callahan as the best artist he had ever known in terms of both technical skill and people skills.\n\nMatt Callahan died on April 13, 2018, at age 40, from a head injury sustained outside a bar on Gandy Boulevard in Tampa. His family chose organ donation, and his heart saved the life of another person. A scholarship fund for young artists was established in his name at LeMoyne Art Gallery in Tallahassee.\n\nInstagram: @manmademurals. Collaborator: Angela Delaplane (@delaplanestudios).',fn:['Callahan passed away in 2018 — his heart saved another person\'s life','Painted during the first SHINE festival in 2015','Collaboration with Angela Delaplane of Delaplane Studios'],src:['https://stpetecatalyst.com/remembering-matt-callahan-the-most-incredible-artist-we-all-knew/','https://tampabaydatenightguide.com/st-pete-murals/']},
  {id:18,a:'Morning Breath',t:'',loc:'2042 Central Ave',bldg:'',lat:27.770954,lng:-82.661095,y:2015,cat:'shine',ig:'morningbreathinc',bio:'They met at Think Skateboards in San Francisco in 1996. They have designed over a hundred album covers since.\n\nDoug Cunningham and Jason Noto are Morning Breath Inc., a Brooklyn-based design studio they formalized in 2002. Cunningham studied at the Academy of Art University in San Francisco. Their style pulls from 1950s-1980s Americana — comics, Mad magazine, punk rock, hip-hop, heavy metal, and skateboard graphics — rendered in limited color palettes with bold lines.\n\nThe client list is absurd: Foo Fighters, Kanye West, Jay-Z, Eminem, Slayer, Queens of the Stone Age, AFI. Brands: Vans, Adidas, Ray-Ban, Absolut Vodka, MTV, KFC (a full restaurant redesign). They won the Grammy for Best Recording Package in 2021 for Desert Sessions Vols. 11 and 12 — a Josh Homme project with a mix-and-match booklet and sticker set. They have published two monographs: The Early Bird (2007) and By the Skin of Our Teeth (HarperCollins, 2016).\n\nTheir SHINE 2015 mural on Central Avenue is mostly hidden behind new construction — only a narrow vertical slice survives. They returned for SHINE 2019 with a 101-foot-wide piece at 250 Dr. MLK Jr. Street North in their signature black-white-and-red style: open lips, aviator sunglasses, a smiling devil face.',desc:'Only a narrow vertical slice of Morning Breath\'s 2015 SHINE mural remains visible, sandwiched between a blue-painted building at 2044 and a newer white structure built in 2019 that obscured most of the original work. The surviving fragment shows their signature style on a golden-yellow background — bold black hand-lettering reading "WARNING," "UNSIGHTLY CROOKED," "A WICKED EYEFUL!," "PANIC," "Have a SECOND LOOK," and "SIGNS YOU NEED MONEY." A grinning skull, all-seeing eye, and the "Morning Breath" nameplate at top are still intact. A barber pole stands in front of the adjacent storefront. The address 2044 is visible on the blue building to the left, with a palm tree partially blocking the lower portion of the remaining mural.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2015/morning-breath.jpeg',from:'Brooklyn, NY',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Grammy Award for Best Recording Package (2021, Desert Sessions Vols. 11 and 12); SHINE 2015 and 2019 festival artist',fw:[{name:'morningbreathinc.com',url:'http://www.morningbreathinc.com'}],oimg:'images/murals/2015/morning-breath-original.jpeg',sbio:'They met at Think Skateboards in San Francisco in 1996. They have designed over a hundred album covers since.\n\nDoug Cunningham and Jason Noto started together as the in-house design department at Think, a small skateboard company in San Francisco. That is where they developed the shared visual language and collaborative approach that still defines their work. In 2002 they formalized the partnership as Morning Breath, Inc., a boutique design studio based in Brooklyn, New York. Cunningham studied at the Academy of Art University in San Francisco.\n\nThe studio is multidisciplinary. What started in skateboarding culture quickly expanded into music packaging, apparel, advertising, and poster design, plus an extensive body of personal and gallery work. The aesthetic pulls from 1950s through 1980s Americana — comics, Mad magazine, punk rock, hip-hop, heavy metal, and skateboard graphics — rendered in limited color palettes with bold lines. The blend of retro themes and fonts with a modern punk twist produces clean, bright, often funny work.\n\nThe client list runs across music, fashion, and global brands. Music: Foo Fighters, Kanye West, Jay-Z, Eminem, Slayer, Queens of the Stone Age, AFI. Brands: Vans, Adidas, Ray-Ban, Absolut Vodka, MTV, Pepsi, and KFC, where they handled a full restaurant redesign. The album-packaging output alone passes one hundred covers.\n\nIn 2021 they won the Grammy Award for Best Recording Package for Desert Sessions Vols. 11 and 12, the Josh Homme project credited to The Desert Sessions. The package is built around a mix-and-match booklet and sticker set. The award is presented by the National Academy of Recording Arts and Sciences to the art directors of the winning album — Cunningham and Noto in this case — and sits in a category whose recent winners include Jonathan Barnbrook for David Bowie\'s Blackstar (2017), Willo Perron for St. Vincent\'s Masseduction (2019), and Charli XCX, Brent David Freaney and Imogene Strauss for Brat (2025).\n\nThey have published two monographs. The Early Bird came out in 2007. By the Skin of Our Teeth: The Art and Design of Morning Breath was released by HarperCollins in 2017 (with a 2016 imprint date) as a comprehensive look at the studio\'s work and creative evolution. The Daily Beast covered the book in December 2017 in a piece by Sarah Rogers titled "Inside the Work of Morning Breath, A Pop-Art Design Duo."\n\nThe studio has been featured in numerous design publications and Cunningham and Noto have been invited to speak about and present their work internationally. They continue to divide their time between commercial assignments and personal and gallery projects.\n\nIn St. Petersburg they have painted twice for the SHINE Mural Festival. Their 2015 piece on Central Avenue is mostly hidden now behind new construction — only a narrow vertical slice survives. They returned for SHINE 2019 with a 101-foot-wide mural at 250 Dr. MLK Jr. Street North, executed in their signature black, white, and red palette: open lips, aviator sunglasses, and a smiling devil face.\n\nSearch terms and aliases: Morning Breath Inc, Morning Breath, Inc., Doug Cunningham, Jason Noto, Brooklyn design studio, Think Skateboards, Academy of Art University San Francisco, album cover designers, music packaging, Grammy Best Recording Package 2021, Desert Sessions Vols. 11 and 12, Josh Homme, Foo Fighters, Kanye West, Jay-Z, Eminem, Slayer, Queens of the Stone Age, AFI, Vans, Adidas, Ray-Ban, Absolut Vodka, MTV, Pepsi, KFC restaurant redesign, The Early Bird 2007, By the Skin of Our Teeth, HarperCollins 2016, 2017 monograph, Sarah Rogers Daily Beast, pop art design duo, retro punk graphics, 1950s 1960s 1970s 1980s Americana, Mad magazine, comics, hip-hop, heavy metal, skateboard graphics, limited color palette, bold lines, black white and red, SHINE 2015, SHINE 2019, Central Avenue mural St. Petersburg, 250 Dr. MLK Jr. Street North, 101-foot-wide mural, aviator sunglasses, smiling devil face, open lips. Website: morningbreathinc.com.',fn:['Met at Think Skateboards in San Francisco, 1996','Apple hired them for product campaigns','Signature skulls and hand-drawn typography'],src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/morning-breath-2015','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/morning-breath','http://www.morningbreathinc.com/about','https://www.thedailybeast.com/inside-the-work-of-morning-breath-a-pop-art-design-duo/','https://dumbo.is/blogging/q-a-doug-cunningham-and-jason-noto-morning-breath-inc','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grammy_Award_for_Best_Recording_Package']},
  {id:110,a:'Shark Toof',t:'',loc:'687 Central Ave',bldg:'State Theater',lat:27.771639,lng:-82.643215,y:2015,cat:'shine',ig:'shark__toof',bio:'Samsung put his shark in a commercial with LeBron James. Apple put it in an iPad ad.\n\nDavid Lew — Shark Toof — studied at the Art Center College of Design in Pasadena. His iconic image is a hand-drawn wheat-pasted great white shark that has appeared on walls from Wynwood to Cambodia. His other series include "Guilty Pleasures" (cigarette packs and slot machines) and "Mayday" (pulp-comic romance).\n\nClients include Apple, Samsung, Ritz Carlton, Heineken, Burton, Toyota, Adidas, and Axl Rose (a personal tour jacket). He has shown at Corey Helford Gallery, Thinkspace, Inner State Gallery in Detroit, and GO Gallery in Amsterdam. His work is in the permanent collection of the Chinese American Museum in Los Angeles. He painted the Heavy Metal Magazine cover (Issue 288).\n\nFor SHINE 2015, he painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A massive great white shark painted in dark gray and black across the east wall of the State Theater, a pink/coral building with arched windows on Central Avenue. The shark\'s head dives downward with its mouth wide open, baring rows of white teeth, positioned above a red metal-grate door. The piece was completed over four days using approximately 100 cans of aerosol paint on the 55-by-33-foot wall. The shark\'s menacing form contrasts with the ornate architectural details of the historic theater building.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/2015/shark-toof.jpeg',from:'Los Angeles, CA',aud:'https://cdn.muralquest.app/audio/110.mp3',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2015 selected artist; Chinese American Museum permanent collection',fw:[{name:'www.sharktoof.com',url:'www.sharktoof.com'}],sbio:'Samsung put his shark in a commercial with LeBron James. Apple put it in an iPad ad. David Lew, working under the name Shark Toof, is an American graffiti artist, stencillist, muralist, painter, and activist based in Los Angeles. He is a graduate of ArtCenter College of Design in Pasadena, California, and his work grew out of the California underground graffiti scene.\n\nHis signature image is a hand-drawn, gape-mouthed great white shark with the word "TOOF" lettered in its mouth, reproduced as a wheat-paste and pasted on walls in urban neighborhoods across multiple countries. Although the shark\'s reputation is often fearsome, Toof uses the image to convey strength, optimism, and possibility, treating the icon as a voice of rebellion, a conduit for the unheard, and an homage to graffiti art and its purpose. His broader practice creates gritty juxtapositions between animals — most often sharks — and anachronistic situations, layering comic and advertising iconography with street-art sensibilities.\n\nBeyond the shark wheat-paste, his ongoing series include "Guilty Pleasures," iconographic paintings of cigarette packs and slot machines, and "Mayday," a pulp-comic-inspired series of idealized romance imagery. He has also produced pop-culture caricature shark heads, which anchored his solo exhibition "I\'m Shark Toof, Who The Hell Are You?" at Inner State Gallery (also written as Innerstate Gallery) in Detroit. A more recent solo show, "Red Everything," was mounted at CASS Contemporary, with attention to juxtaposition of shapes, line quality, composition, varying materials, and tension of content — fine-art paintings framed as an intimate look at belief systems and boundaries.\n\nShark Toof\'s commercial client list spans global brands, including Apple, Samsung, Ritz Carlton, Heineken, Burton, Hurley, Toyota, Adidas, and Axl Rose, for whom he painted a personal tour jacket. His murals have appeared in commercials for Samsung and Apple Inc., the Samsung spot featuring LeBron James and an Apple iPad ad. He painted the cover of Heavy Metal Magazine, Issue 288.\n\nHis gallery and museum credits include Corey Helford Gallery, Thinkspace, Inner State Gallery in Detroit, GO Gallery in Amsterdam, and CASS Contemporary. He has had a second showing at the Chinese American Museum in Los Angeles, where his work is held in the permanent collection. He has also exhibited with The Everhart Museum in Pennsylvania and the Portsmouth Museum of Art. A monograph titled "Shark Toof" was published in 2012 by Zero+ Publishing in Claremont, California (ISBN 978-1937222109).\n\nIn 2020 he sued the Chinese American Museum in Los Angeles after it accidentally threw out his work; the case was dismissed by the United States District Court for the Central District of California in an order granting the defendants\' motion to dismiss dated August 31, 2021. The dispute was covered by the BBC under the headline "David Lew: Artist sues Los Angeles museum after work thrown out."\n\nHis hand-drawn great-white wheat-pastes have appeared on walls from Wynwood to Cambodia, and his work is collected globally — from young graffiti writers who peel the icon from the streets to contemporary collectors who pursue the full repertoire. Writers describe him as possessing the technical skills of an Old Master and the spirit of an art-minded vandal, moving between styles while keeping the shark icon as a through-line.\n\nFor SHINE 2015, the St. Petersburg Mural Festival in St. Petersburg, Florida, he painted on-site as one of the graffiti artists putting up murals around the city that week. He is also listed in the SHINE St. Pete artist portfolio for the festival.\n\nKeywords and proper nouns associated with Shark Toof include: David Lew, Shark Toof, ArtCenter College of Design, Pasadena, Los Angeles, California, great white shark, wheat paste, TOOF, Guilty Pleasures, Mayday, "I\'m Shark Toof Who The Hell Are You?", Red Everything, Inner State Gallery, Innerstate Gallery, Detroit, CASS Contemporary, Corey Helford Gallery, Thinkspace, GO Gallery, Amsterdam, Chinese American Museum, Everhart Museum, Pennsylvania, Portsmouth Museum of Art, Zero+ Publishing, Claremont, Heavy Metal Magazine Issue 288, Apple, Samsung, LeBron James, iPad, Ritz Carlton, Heineken, Burton, Hurley, Toyota, Adidas, Axl Rose, tour jacket, Wynwood, Cambodia, BBC, United States District Court for the Central District of California, SHINE 2015, SHINE Mural Festival, St. Petersburg, Florida, graffiti, stencillist, muralist, activist, pulp comic, cigarette packs, slot machines, pop culture caricature, sharktoof.com.',fn:['Samsung put his shark in a LeBron James commercial','Apple featured his work','Iconic shark character in every piece'],src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shark-toof/','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shark_Toof','http://shineonstpete.com/portfolio/shark-toof/','https://stpetemuraltour.com/2016/08/01/shark-toof/','http://www.sharktoof.com/about']},
  {id:134,a:'BASK',t:'',loc:'648 Central Ave',bldg:'Times Square Properties',lat:27.77102167,lng:-82.64247167,y:2014,cat:'shine-legacy',ig:'knownasbask',bio:'He fled communist Czechoslovakia as a child and grew up in Florida. His work explores the parallels between state propaganda and American consumerism.\n\nAles Bask Hostomsky — BASK (Because Art Should Kill) — was born in Czechoslovakia (birth year disputed: 1977 or 1984) and is self-taught. He works in mixed media on wood and canvas — rich textures and "anti-iconic" imagery deconstructing media. His art appeared in Iron Man 3.\n\nHis work is in the permanent collection of the Jacksonville Museum of Modern Art and the Florida International Museum. He shows at Parlor Gallery in Asbury Park and Signari Gallery. For SHINE, he painted in both 2014 and 2020.',desc:'A roughly 100-foot-long black-and-white collage mural on the east-facing wall of 648 Central Avenue. The piece is a dense retrospective of BASK\'s graphic vocabulary: overlapping panels of greyscale portraits, faces, figures, and icons rendered in his signature distressed, wheat-paste-influenced style. A salmon-pink geometric panel near the top right provides the only color. On the right side, "BASK" is repeated five times in stacked block letters — alternating black-on-white and white-on-black — with a single red "S" breaking the monochrome. Below the stacked name, "AMERICAN ANIMAL" appears in bold white type. The overall effect is a visual manifesto — layered, gritty, and unapologetically personal.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/pre-shine/bask-make-history.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Jacksonville Museum of Modern Art permanent collection; SHINE 2014 and 2020',fw:null,sbio:'He fled communist Czechoslovakia as a child and grew up in Florida. His work explores the parallels between state propaganda and American consumerism.\n\nAles Bask Hostomsky — BASK (Because Art Should Kill) — was born in Czechoslovakia (birth year disputed: 1977 or 1984) and is self-taught. He works in mixed media on wood and canvas — rich textures and "anti-iconic" imagery deconstructing media. His art appeared in Iron Man 3.\n\nHis work is in the permanent collection of the Jacksonville Museum of Modern Art and the Florida International Museum. He shows at Parlor Gallery in Asbury Park and Signari Gallery. For SHINE, he painted in both 2014 and 2020.',src:['https://www.artsy.net/artist/ales-bask-hostomsky','https://www.parlor-gallery.com/artists/bask/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/bask-2020']},
  {id:187,a:'Derek Donnelly',t:'',loc:'Central Ave',bldg:'ZaZoo\'d',lat:27.771642,lng:-82.642395,y:2014,cat:'commercial',ig:'saintpaintarts',bio:'He painted a T-Rex in a Hawaiian shirt going fishing — for AARP. That tells you most of what you need to know about Derek Donnelly.\n\nA Pinellas County native, Donnelly grew up in Pinellas Park, graduated from the Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School in 2001, and studied graphic design and digital illustration at IADT Tampa. He went full-time on murals in 2010. His style sits at the intersection of illustration and realism — playful without being childish, bold without being loud.\n\nHis client list runs from Fender Guitars to the Tampa Bay Rays to Sirata Beach Resort. Multiple Best of the Bay wins. Inaugural Artmaker 2026 at Ruth Eckerd Hall. For SHINE 2025, he ran operations and served as artist liaison for the entire festival while producing two murals of his own.',desc:'A large iguana head fills the wall of ZaZoo\'d, painted on cinder block in vibrant teal, yellow, orange, and red tones. The reptile faces right with a detailed eye, textured scales along its jaw and throat, and spiny crest along the top of its head. The background transitions from light on the left to dark navy-black on the right with scattered white dots suggesting a night sky or underwater scene. A smaller figure appears in the dark section at right. Signed "DONNELLY 2K14" at lower left in graffiti lettering.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/derek-donnelly-iguana-2014.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:[{name:'saintpaintarts.com',url:'https://www.saintpaintarts.com'}]},
  {id:126,a:'Sarah Sheppard',t:'The Siren',loc:'600 27th St S',bldg:'Zen Glass Studio',lat:27.764593,lng:-82.669381,y:2014,cat:'commercial',ig:'sarah.g.sheppard',bio:'The compass on this wall is a question. Will you follow the siren?\n\nJosh at Zen Glass Studio gave Sarah Sheppard free rein on this 2014 commercial commission. She built the design around his glassblowing subjects — an octopus and a mermaid in a golden porthole, the octopus tentacle and the mermaid forming a yin-yang inside the circle to fold in the "zen." The compass rose asks the viewer to choose.\n\nThe piece landed her in Creative Loafing Tampa Bay\'s December 2014 "8 murals that make us heart the Burg," alongside Sebastian Coolidge, Chad Mize, Derek Donnelly, J&S Signs, Bask, and Jennifer Kosharek. One of her first major exterior walls.\n\nSarah Sheppard grew up in Dallas in a family of artists (both parents — her father was her mother\'s college art teacher), studied at the University of North Texas, and trained in metalsmithing at Creative Side Jewelry Academy in Austin to continue work her late father began. She moved to Florida during the housing boom and ran her own faux-finishing and Venetian-plaster business before pivoting to murals. Her style is colorful, high-contrast figurative work — new romanticism, movement as metaphor for continuous loss and seeking.\n\nOther St. Pete walls include "Life Reimagined" on the back of the Morean Arts Center (commissioned by AARP, 2015) and multiple SHINE festival murals.',desc:'A nautical-themed piece centered on a golden porthole frame containing a mermaid/siren figure with flowing dark hair and pale skin, entwined with bright red octopus tentacles that curl through and around the circular frame. The porthole is set within an elaborate compass rose or ship\'s wheel pattern in gold, teal, and white, with geometric Art Deco-style rays radiating outward. The background alternates between teal triangles and white cross-hatched sections. The title "THE SIREN" appears in gold lettering on a dark plaque at the bottom center. The composition has a strong symmetrical, heraldic quality.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/the-siren.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'Commissioned by Josh of Zen Glass Studio, who gave Sheppard free reign. The octopus and mermaid reflect his glassblowing subjects. The tentacle inside the circle creates a yin-yang incorporating the \'zen\' aspect, while the compass poses a question about temptation and direction.',maw:'',aaw:'Multiple SHINE festival murals; AARP/Morean Arts Center commission',fw:[{name:'sarahsheppardsart.com',url:'http://www.sarahsheppardsart.com'}],sbio:'She studied metalsmithing to continue work her late father began. Art runs in the family — both her parents were artists, and her father was her mother\'s art teacher in college.\n\nSarah Sheppard grew up in Dallas and studied photography, drawing, painting, graphic design, ceramics, and fabric art at the University of North Texas. Art surrounded her from childhood and held her attention through school and at home. While still in college she moved from Texas to Florida, where her parents were living, for a change of scenery. The housing market was booming and construction was widespread, so her parents helped her start her own business doing Venetian plaster, faux finishing, and murals in model homes, businesses, and residences. The work involved tight deadlines and 14-hour days. She has lived in St. Petersburg for over twelve years.\n\nIn 2008 she became pregnant with her son, Sebastian, as the economy turned. She stepped away from scaffolding work and spent the next three years as a stay-at-home mom. When she returned to working, she took a part-time job at her favorite coffee shop, Brew D\' Licious, in St. Petersburg. While working there she painted her first mural, for the spa across the street. The wall was about 30 feet long and 10 feet high, the largest canvas she had worked on. She has since completed roughly 15 exterior murals.\n\nAfter moving back to Texas, she enrolled in a metalsmith class at Creative Side Jewelry Academy in Austin. She initially took Jewelry 101 to finish a few pieces her father had given her when he passed away, then continued taking courses. She makes large pieces in both jewelry and painting, working at scale through size, color, or both.\n\nHer style is colorful, high-contrast figurative work — new romanticism, movement as metaphor for continuous loss and seeking. Her personal work draws from life events and the natural world, and she sources from varied historical motifs and historical symbolism, sometimes mixing in modern touches through color or application. The goal is a sense of beauty, romanticism, and mysticism. When working for clients on businesses or homes, she takes in their focus and message — for businesses the design centers on brand and visual attention to the building, for homes the work runs more personal — but always carries her personal style.\n\nHer mural at Sixth Avenue South and 27th Street in the Warehouse Arts District of St. Petersburg was painted for Zen Glass Studio, on the property where the mural is located. Josh from Zen Glass Studio gave her free rein over the design. Because Zen Glass does glassblowing incorporating sea life and mermaids, Sheppard built the composition around an octopus and a mermaid. The octopus tentacle inside the circle that contains the mermaid forms a yin and yang to incorporate the "zen" aspect, and a compass asks what direction the viewer will take with the siren — whether to follow temptation or not. The piece was featured in Creative Loafing Tampa Bay\'s December 2014 roundup "8 murals that make us heart the Burg" by Julie Garisto, alongside work by Sebastian Coolidge, Chad Mize, Derek Donnelly, Jeffrey Sincich and Josh Stover of J&S Signs, Bask, and Jennifer Kosharek.\n\nIn September 2015 she completed "Life Reimagined" on the back wall of the Morean Arts Center at Central Avenue and Seventh Street in downtown St. Petersburg, commissioned by AARP and the Morean to engage the ideas of "Life Reimagined" and "Disrupt Aging." The narrative she built into the piece uses gears to represent the grind of daily life, the lapse of time, and the mechanics of the working world. The central figure is a Banyan tree sinking and spreading her roots into the community, reaching out to a Phoenix that represents her rebirth into the next stage of life. AARP\'s involvement, Sheppard noted, came from the organization\'s work in Tampa Bay supporting the arts as a way to connect older Americans to community life. Creative Loafing\'s Julie Garisto covered the unveiling.\n\nShe has multiple SHINE murals across St. Petersburg. For SHINE 2023, she returned to paint in the city. Her work also appears on the massive wall of Skull & Cakebones Vegan Craft Bakery in Dripping Springs, Texas.\n\nOn the challenges of mural work she points to weather (rain and wet paint), the unique demands of each wall, and painting in front of passersby where mistakes are visible. The upside is the people she meets on the street, the conversations with the community, and seeing children smile at the work or hearing from commuters that the mural is the best part of their drive. On the broader struggle artists face she names the difficulty of staying positive through the creative process and learning to be gentle with herself, trusting the process, taking breaks, and shaking up perspective with new experiences.\n\nHer work can be seen on her website at sarahsheppardsart.com and on Instagram at @thee.divine.sarah.',src:['https://www.cltampa.com/arts/8-murals-that-make-us-heart-the-burg-12287690','https://stpetemuraltour.com/2019/12/10/sarah-sheppard/','https://voyagehouston.com/interview/art-life-sarah-sheppard/','http://www.sarahsheppardsart.com/','https://www.cltampa.com/arts/life-reimagined-sarah-sheppard-completes-new-mural-in-downtown-st-pete-12280945','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2023_SHINE_sarah_sheppard']},
  {id:209,a:'BASK',t:'Because Art Should Kill',loc:'5013 20th Ave S, Unit F, Gulfport',bldg:'Overspray',lat:27.75087,lng:-82.70136,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'knownasbask',bio:'His name is the slogan. BASK stands for "Because Art Should Kill," and he painted the full phrase across this wall at Overspray in his signature worn-poster stencil — fluorescent red drip outline, weathered black-and- white figures squaring up to fight beneath two angry slit eyes. The "BASK" tag is tucked between the boxers\' feet. Painted at the Vitale Bros\' graffiti shop in Gulfport.\n\nAles Bask Hostomsky — BASK — fled communist Czechoslovakia as a child and grew up in Florida. His work explores the parallels between state propaganda and American consumerism. He is self-taught, works in mixed media on wood and canvas, and is known for rich textures and anti-iconic imagery deconstructing media.\n\nHis art appeared in Iron Man 3. His work is in the permanent collection of the Jacksonville Museum of Modern Art and the Florida International Museum. He shows at Parlor Gallery in Asbury Park and Signari Gallery. For SHINE, he painted in both 2014 and 2020.',desc:'A tall vertical mural on a white concrete-block exterior wall, framed in fluorescent red dripping paint. At the top, two angry slit eyes with heavy arched brows peer down at the viewer in distressed black silhouette against the white wall. Below them, a dark gray rectangular block bears the phrase "BECAUSE ART SHOULD KILL" in jagged fluorescent-red stencil lettering, paint visibly dripping. Two stenciled figures stand in front of the block: on the left, a figure in a long-sleeve striped tank top with a star pattern (vaguely American-flag), fists raised in a boxing stance; on the right, a figure in a long patterned skirt with a thick braid, also in a fighting stance. The whole image is rendered in distressed black-and- white aerosol-on-poster aesthetic with heavy paint speckle and weathering. The small "BASK" tag is hidden between the boxers\' feet at the bottom.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/bask-overspray-art-should-kill.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'BASK\'s name itself is an acronym: \'Because Art Should Kill.\' This wall paints out the full phrase he goes by.',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:[{name:'knownasbask.com',url:'https://www.knownasbask.com'}],sbio:'BASK — see full bio on his other entry (id 134). This is a 2026 wall at Overspray in Gulfport — the Vitale Bros\' street-art shop and graffiti school. The phrase "BECAUSE ART SHOULD KILL" (which is what the BASK name itself stands for) is painted in red drippy stencil text across a weathered black-and-white tableau of two figures squaring up to fight: one in a striped tank top with stars (vaguely American-flag), one in a patterned skirt with a long braid. Two angry slit eyes peer down from above. The whole composition is framed by a fluorescent red drip outline, in BASK\'s signature decayed-propaganda-poster style. Small "BASK" tag visible at the bottom between the figures\' feet.\n\nAles Bask Hostomsky was born in Czechoslovakia (birth year disputed: 1977 or 1984) and is self-taught. His work explores the parallels between state propaganda and American consumerism. He works in mixed media on wood and canvas with rich textures and anti-iconic imagery. His art appeared in Iron Man 3. Permanent collection: Jacksonville Museum of Modern Art and the Florida International Museum. He shows at Parlor Gallery (Asbury Park) and Signari Gallery. SHINE artist 2014 and 2020.\n\nInstagram: @knownasbask.',src:['https://www.knownasbask.com/','https://www.instagram.com/knownasbask/']},
  {id:202,a:'Blake Emory',t:'',loc:'2001 1st Ave S',bldg:'Cage Brewing',lat:27.770425,lng:-82.66057,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'emory_blake',bio:'He sculpted a bronze Babe Ruth pointing to center field. His house sits on the former golf course where Ruth used to play.\n\nBlake Emory is a Florida native, born 1983, now based in St. Petersburg. With his brother James — out of Santa Fe — he runs ESP Gallery and the Emory Arts collective. The day job is wide: Neo-Renaissance figure painting, Optic Modernism, sculpture, set design, experimental film. He doesn\'t pick a lane.\n\nHis murals are scattered across St. Pete in places people don\'t expect, including a few rooftops. The work doesn\'t always announce itself — you find it by looking up, or by walking past somewhere you\'ve passed a hundred times.\n\nIn 2025 the City of St. Petersburg unveiled "Babe Calls the Shot," his life-size bronze of Ruth at the called-shot moment, sited at the St. Petersburg Museum of History on the Pier.\n\nThis wall sits inside Cage Brewing, on the women\'s-room exterior in the patio. A goggled woman stares out from the right; betta and koi swim across the left half through a nautilus shell. Painted directly onto the cinder block — the door and the women\'s-room sign cut through the composition.',desc:'An interior mural at Cage Brewing\'s covered patio area, painted on the cinder-block exterior of the women\'s restroom. The right half of the wall shows a portrait of a woman in three-quarter view: deep red lips, large brown eyes set within a sculpted orange-and-yellow goggle/mask shape that glows like flame. Her face is framed in waves of orange, red, and teal that spread upward like a sunset corona. Her hair flows leftward across the wall into a dreamy underwater scene: a large iridescent nautilus shell sits in the center (around the metal door), its spiral painted in violet, teal, and yellow. Multiple ornamental koi and betta fish swim through the field — one pale gold koi in profile near the lower-left, an orange-and-white pair near the door, a teal betta with trailing fins above the shell. A green peacock feather and a fantastical fish silhouette stretch across the upper-left edge. A bright lotus flower with rainbow-mandala petals blooms at the woman\'s chin. The dominant background is deep blue-violet, scattered with tiny stars. The metal women\'s restroom door, with its accessibility-and-women signage, sits at the center of the composition.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/blake-emory-cage-interior-goddess.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:[{name:'emorybrothersart.com',url:'https://www.emorybrothersart.com'},{name:'espgallery.com',url:'https://www.espgallery.com'}],sbio:'He sculpted a bronze Babe Ruth pointing to center field. His house sits on the former golf course where Ruth used to play.\n\nBlake Emory was born in Florida in 1983 and is now based in St. Petersburg, with a studio at 1114 Farragut Dr N. With his brother James — based in Santa Fe — he runs ESP Gallery and the Emory Arts collective (EMORY ARTS LLC and the affiliated Emory Arts 501(c)(3)). His practice spans Neo-Renaissance figure painting, Optic Modernism, sculpture, set design, and experimental film. He is represented by Marcolina\'s Gallery.\n\nHis murals are scattered across St. Petersburg in places people don\'t expect, including a few rooftops. He has been painting in the Tampa Bay area since 2017, with most of his documented work falling in 2021–2023. Beyond Florida, he has painted along Route 66 in Grants, New Mexico.\n\nIn 2025 the City of St. Petersburg unveiled "Babe Calls the Shot," his life-size bronze of Babe Ruth at the called-shot moment. It was sited at the St. Petersburg Museum of History on the Pier.\n\nThis wall is inside Cage Brewing\'s covered patio, painted on the women\'s restroom exterior. A goggled woman occupies the right half, her face emerging from a glowing aura of orange, magenta, and teal. Her hair flows leftward across the wall, dissolving into water — koi and betta fish swim through it, and a large iridescent nautilus shell sits at the center, around the door. Painted on cinder block, with the door and women\'s-room sign integrated into the composition. Year not documented; attribution confirmed by Rob meeting Blake Emory in person at Cage Brewing on 2026-05-01.\n\nInstagram: @emory_blake. Galleries: ESP Gallery, Marcolina\'s. Website: emorybrothersart.com.',src:['https://www.emorybrothersart.com/biography','https://marcolinas.com/collections/blake-emory','https://www.instagram.com/emory_blake/']},
  {id:203,a:'Blake Emory',t:'',loc:'2001 1st Ave S',bldg:'Cage Brewing',lat:27.770581,lng:-82.660612,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'emory_blake',bio:'He sculpted a bronze Babe Ruth pointing to center field. His house sits on the former golf course where Ruth used to play.\n\nBlake Emory is a Florida native, born 1983, now based in St. Petersburg. With his brother James — out of Santa Fe — he runs ESP Gallery and the Emory Arts collective. The day job is wide: Neo-Renaissance figure painting, Optic Modernism, sculpture, set design, experimental film. He doesn\'t pick a lane.\n\nHis murals are scattered across St. Pete in places people don\'t expect, including a few rooftops. The work doesn\'t always announce itself — you find it by looking up, or by walking past somewhere you\'ve passed a hundred times.\n\nIn 2025 the City of St. Petersburg unveiled "Babe Calls the Shot," his life-size bronze of Ruth at the called-shot moment, sited at the St. Petersburg Museum of History on the Pier.\n\nThis wall is one of three Cage Brewing murals he has painted. A vintage copper-and-brass deep-sea diver kneels on the wall, helmet pulled off and cradled in his arms. Three jellyfish drift overhead, the largest one trailing tendrils that spill across the diver\'s shoulders.',desc:'An exterior wall mural at Cage Brewing\'s outdoor space. The lower half of the wall shows a kneeling deep-sea diver in a vintage copper-and-brass hard hat suit, painted in cool teal and silver-blue tones. He has removed his spherical brass-and-glass dive helmet, which sits in his lap, cradled by both gloved hands; a faint reflection or yellow porthole glows on the helmet\'s side. A small bunch of purple grapes leans against the diver\'s right shoulder, growing out of a slim turquoise vase or column. The upper two-thirds of the wall is open ocean rendered in deep teal and royal blue with greenish atmospheric haze. Three jellyfish drift overhead: a small pale-blue jellyfish with orange tendrils on the upper left, a larger teal-and-yellow jellyfish in the upper center with bright orange-pink tendrils streaming down toward the diver\'s helmet, and a smaller orange-pink jellyfish on the upper right. A small yellow glow on the right edge reads like a distant submarine porthole. Painted on a smooth concrete-block wall with a dark wood-paneled overhang above. A small section of a dark gray metal door frames the lower-left edge.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/blake-emory-cage-diver-jellyfish.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:[{name:'emorybrothersart.com',url:'https://www.emorybrothersart.com'},{name:'espgallery.com',url:'https://www.espgallery.com'}],sbio:'He sculpted a bronze Babe Ruth pointing to center field. His house sits on the former golf course where Ruth used to play.\n\nBlake Emory was born in Florida in 1983 and is now based in St. Petersburg, with a studio at 1114 Farragut Dr N. With his brother James — based in Santa Fe — he runs ESP Gallery and the Emory Arts collective (EMORY ARTS LLC and the affiliated Emory Arts 501(c)(3)). His practice spans Neo-Renaissance figure painting, Optic Modernism, sculpture, set design, and experimental film. He is represented by Marcolina\'s Gallery.\n\nHis murals are scattered across St. Petersburg in places people don\'t expect, including a few rooftops. He has been painting in the Tampa Bay area since 2017, with most of his documented work falling in 2021–2023. Beyond Florida, he has painted along Route 66 in Grants, New Mexico.\n\nIn 2025 the City of St. Petersburg unveiled "Babe Calls the Shot," his life-size bronze of Babe Ruth at the called-shot moment, sited at the St. Petersburg Museum of History on the Pier.\n\nThis wall is one of three documented Blake Emory murals at Cage Brewing. The exterior corner octopus (Mural Quest #194, painted 2023 with Guillo Perez III) is the most visible from the street. The covered-patio interior wall (#202) shows a goggled woman framed by koi and a nautilus shell. This diver-and-jellyfish wall is on a side elevation in the brewery\'s outdoor space. A copper-and-brass vintage hard-hat dive helmet sits in the diver\'s arms; three glowing jellyfish drift through the upper register. Year not documented; attribution confirmed by Rob meeting Blake Emory at Cage on 2026-05-01.\n\nInstagram: @emory_blake. Galleries: ESP Gallery, Marcolina\'s. Website: emorybrothersart.com.',src:['https://www.emorybrothersart.com/biography','https://marcolinas.com/collections/blake-emory','https://www.instagram.com/emory_blake/']},
  {id:206,a:'Blake Emory',t:'St Pete / The Burg',loc:'14th Ave S & 16th St S',bldg:'',lat:27.75665,lng:-82.65449,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'emory_blake',bio:'This one is a different gear for him. A pelican carries a sub sandwich across a Skyway-sunset, sea turtles cross the dunes, a pirate eats peanuts with a parrot, and a hand-painted "I ♥ 16TH ST" road sign anchors the corner. Cursive "St Pete," ornate "The Burg" lettering. Painted on the side of a corner shop at 14th Ave S and 16th St S.\n\nBlake Emory is a Florida native, born 1983, now based in St. Petersburg. With his brother James — out of Santa Fe — he runs ESP Gallery and the Emory Arts collective. The day job is wide: Neo-Renaissance figure painting, Optic Modernism, sculpture, set design, experimental film. He doesn\'t pick a lane.\n\nHis murals are scattered across St. Pete in places people don\'t expect, including a few rooftops. In 2025 the City of St. Petersburg unveiled "Babe Calls the Shot," his life-size bronze of Babe Ruth at the called-shot moment, sited at the St. Petersburg Museum of History on the Pier.',desc:'A long horizontal commercial mural on the side of a single-story corner shop building. Reading left to right: a Black man with a gray beard, blue cap and bandana sits leaning against the wall at the base. Above him, a large brown pelican with a yellow head soars to the right, gripping a sub sandwich in its bill, a small SHINE-style logo medallion at its feet. The background behind the pelican is a Sunshine Skyway bridge silhouette at golden-hour: pink/orange sky, blue water, the Skyway\'s twin sails of cable. "St Pete" in white cursive script floats in the upper left. A wooden road sign in the middle reads "14TH AVE / ♥ 16TH ST." Two large green sea turtles cross the sand below the sign. The upper right is dominated by "The Burg" in red-and-blue ornate filigreed lettering with curlicue flourishes. The right third is a beach scene: bright teal-and-blue ocean, pale sand, a sun-bleached wooden table where a pirate (red bandana, tricorn hat, brown coat) feeds a white parrot a peanut from a red can of "Hot Roasted Peanuts." A woman in a red-and-white-polka-dot bikini lounges in a wooden beach chair beside him, looking out at the painter.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/blake-emory-the-burg.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:[{name:'emorybrothersart.com',url:'https://www.emorybrothersart.com'},{name:'espgallery.com',url:'https://www.espgallery.com'}],sbio:'Blake Emory — see full bio on his other entries (ids 194, 202, 203). This is a commercial wall at 14th Ave S & 16th St S, a cartoon-boardwalk composition tying together the Sunshine Skyway, a pelican carrying a sub sandwich, two sea turtles, a beach scene with a pirate and a parrot, and a hand-painted "I ♥ 16TH ST / 14TH AVE" road sign. Cursive "St Pete" upper left and ornate "The Burg" upper right. A wider-appeal commercial commission, very different in tone from his Neo-Renaissance gallery and Cage Brewing work.\n\nInstagram: @emory_blake. Galleries: ESP Gallery, Marcolina\'s. Website: emorybrothersart.com.',src:['https://www.emorybrothersart.com/biography','https://www.instagram.com/emory_blake/']},
  {id:149,a:'Carrie Jadus',t:'Awakening Bodhisattva',loc:'23rd St S',bldg:'Dog Bar',lat:27.770808,lng:-82.664828,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'jadusfineart',bio:'She has a degree in electrical engineering. She worked as a radio-frequency design engineer. Then she quit to paint.\n\nCarrie Jadus was born in 1976 in Tampa and moved to St. Petersburg at five. She graduated from the Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School, traveled to Europe, fell for Impressionism, and then — detour — earned a BS in Electrical Engineering from USF and designed RF circuits for several years. She creates 200 to 300 pieces annually, working primarily in oils at every scale from notecards to fifty-foot walls.\n\nHer SHINE 2015 Nikola Tesla portrait is on 22nd Street South. She also painted "Little Miss Sisyphus" — a girl pushing an enormous reflective sphere up a mountain — around the corner from the Tesla. For Grand Central Brewhouse, she painted "Last Call" — six musical icons chosen because "they\'re all extremely talented and socially conscious." She finished the fifty-by-thirty-foot piece in three weeks.',desc:'A photorealistic memorial mural covering the side of Dog Bar on Central Avenue. A large dog — Bodhi Kaiser — lies sleeping against a deep blue starry sky, paws curled, eyes closed. The rendering is detailed and luminous, the fur and shadows painted with gallery-level realism. Text at the bottom reads "Awakening Bodhisattva" with "Bodhi Kaiser 2013-2023" and the artist\'s signature.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/carrie-jadus-dog-bar.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'Memorial to Bodhi Kaiser (2013-2023).',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'She has a degree in electrical engineering. She worked as a radio-frequency design engineer. Then she quit to paint.\n\nCarrie Jadus was born in 1976 in Tampa, FL. Her family moved to St. Petersburg when she was five. She comes from an artistic lineage — family populated with creatives. She attended the Pinellas County Center for the Arts magnet program at Gibbs High School, training across multiple mediums. A post-graduation trip to Europe sparked a fascination with Impressionism. She then earned a BS in Electrical Engineering from the University of South Florida and worked as a radio-frequency design engineer for several years before quitting to pursue art full-time.\n\nShe works primarily in oils, comfortable at every scale from notecards to fifty-foot murals. She occupies "the rich and fertile ground between realism and impressionism" with a "distinctive, soft and subtly subversive style." Key influences: Winslow Homer (storytelling) and Egon Schiele (the human figure). She creates 200-300 pieces annually. She has expanded into large-scale sculpture through collaboration with MGA Sculpture Studio. Licensed as a professional artist since 2016, she uses "the business plan of a Toymaker as a model" — multiple product lines to sustain income.\n\nHer SHINE 2015 murals include the Nikola Tesla portrait at 22nd St S and 5th Ave S, painted with assistant Tara Radosevich over two weeks, and "Little Miss Sisyphus," a girl pushing an enormous reflective sphere up a mountain. At Grand Central Brewhouse she painted "Last Call" (2020), a 50x30 ft mural of six musical icons: Bob Marley, Bono, Bruce Springsteen, Lady Gaga, Eddie Vedder, and John Legend — chosen because "they\'re all extremely talented and socially conscious." Heads roughly 8 ft tall, completed in three weeks, commissioned by Kevin Milkey. "Awakening Bodhisattva" at Dog Bar (2020) is a memorial to Bodhi, dog of artist Marianne Wysocki — a double entendre: the dog waking up, and the Buddhist concept of a being who delays nirvana out of compassion.\n\nShe maintains a studio at Soft Water Studios in the Warehouse Arts District. She illustrates for St. Pete Preservation and designed a Kahwa Coffee Art Canister (4th installment) benefiting the St. Petersburg Arts Endowment. Solo series include "Blue Nudes," "Imaginary Friends," and "Grand Portraits" (Einstein, Nina Simone). Instagram: @jadusfineart. Website: carriejadus.com.',src:['http://www.carriejadus.com/bio.html','https://theartisanmagazine.com/carrie-jadus/','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/carrie-jadus/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/art-business-business-of-art-carrie-jadus/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/hustle/hustle-carrie-jadus/','https://dogbarstpete.com/st-pete-is-going-to-the-dogs-2-2-2-2/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/carrie-jadus-tesla/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/carrie-jadus-little-miss-sisyphus/','https://kahwacoffee.com/blogs/news/kahwa-releases-fourth-installment-of-artist-canister','https://thegabber.com/local-artists-morning-routine-1200-collages/']},
  {id:150,a:'Carrie Jadus',t:'Last Call',loc:'2324 Central Ave',bldg:'Grand Central Brewhouse',lat:27.770771,lng:-82.665143,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'jadusfineart',bio:'Bob Marley, Bono, Bruce Springsteen, Lady Gaga, Eddie Vedder, John Legend — six musicians she calls "extremely talented and socially conscious." She put them at the bar with beers. Three weeks, fifty by thirty feet, heads eight feet tall.\n\nKevin Milkey commissioned "Last Call" for Grand Central Brewhouse in 2020. Carrie Jadus had been an electrical engineer for years before quitting to paint full-time, and her style sits in what she calls "the rich and fertile ground between realism and impressionism" — figures rendered with photographic care, edges softened. She picked the six musicians for who they are, not just because they\'re famous.\n\nBorn in Tampa in 1976, she grew up in St. Petersburg from age five, trained at the Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High, fell for Impressionism on a post-graduation Europe trip, then earned a BS in Electrical Engineering from USF before pivoting to art full-time. She works primarily in oils at every scale — from notecards to fifty-foot walls — and creates 200 to 300 pieces a year. Her studio is at Soft Water Studios in the Warehouse Arts District.\n\nOther St. Pete walls include her Nikola Tesla SHINE 2015 portrait at 22nd & 5th, "Little Miss Sisyphus" around the corner, and "Awakening Bodhisattva" — a memorial to Bodhi Kaiser — on the Dog Bar side of this same building.',desc:'A photorealistic mural on the side of Grand Central Brewhouse depicting six iconic musicians and performers gathered together as if at a bar, each holding a glass of beer or wine. From left to right: a figure resembling Bob Marley in a white jacket, Bono in sunglasses, Eddie Vedder in a headband leaning in conspiratorially, Lady Gaga in blonde hair holding a beer, Bruce Springsteen in a casual shirt, and John Legend in profile. The background is a deep blue with circular bokeh lights. "Grand Central" signage is visible at the bottom center.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/carrie-jadus-grand-central.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'She has a degree in electrical engineering. She worked as a radio-frequency design engineer. Then she quit to paint.\n\nCarrie Jadus was born in 1976 in Tampa, FL. Her family moved to St. Petersburg when she was five. She comes from an artistic lineage — family populated with creatives. She attended the Pinellas County Center for the Arts magnet program at Gibbs High School, training across multiple mediums. A post-graduation trip to Europe sparked a fascination with Impressionism. She then earned a BS in Electrical Engineering from the University of South Florida and worked as a radio-frequency design engineer for several years before quitting to pursue art full-time.\n\nShe works primarily in oils, comfortable at every scale from notecards to fifty-foot murals. She occupies "the rich and fertile ground between realism and impressionism" with a "distinctive, soft and subtly subversive style." Key influences: Winslow Homer (storytelling) and Egon Schiele (the human figure). She creates 200-300 pieces annually. She has expanded into large-scale sculpture through collaboration with MGA Sculpture Studio. Licensed as a professional artist since 2016, she uses "the business plan of a Toymaker as a model" — multiple product lines to sustain income.\n\nHer SHINE 2015 murals include the Nikola Tesla portrait at 22nd St S and 5th Ave S, painted with assistant Tara Radosevich over two weeks, and "Little Miss Sisyphus," a girl pushing an enormous reflective sphere up a mountain. At Grand Central Brewhouse she painted "Last Call" (2020), a 50x30 ft mural of six musical icons: Bob Marley, Bono, Bruce Springsteen, Lady Gaga, Eddie Vedder, and John Legend — chosen because "they\'re all extremely talented and socially conscious." Heads roughly 8 ft tall, completed in three weeks, commissioned by Kevin Milkey. "Awakening Bodhisattva" at Dog Bar (2020) is a memorial to Bodhi, dog of artist Marianne Wysocki — a double entendre: the dog waking up, and the Buddhist concept of a being who delays nirvana out of compassion.\n\nShe maintains a studio at Soft Water Studios in the Warehouse Arts District. She illustrates for St. Pete Preservation and designed a Kahwa Coffee Art Canister (4th installment) benefiting the St. Petersburg Arts Endowment. Solo series include "Blue Nudes," "Imaginary Friends," and "Grand Portraits" (Einstein, Nina Simone). Instagram: @jadusfineart. Website: carriejadus.com.',src:['https://stpetecatalyst.com/carrie-jadus-paint-grand-central-beer-garden-music/','http://www.carriejadus.com/bio.html','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/carrie-jadus/']},
  {id:168,a:'Carrie Jadus',t:'Are You My Mother?',loc:'515 22nd St S',bldg:'ArtsXchange',lat:27.766276,lng:-82.663354,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'jadusfineart',bio:'She has a degree in electrical engineering. She worked as a radio-frequency design engineer. Then she quit to paint.\n\nCarrie Jadus was born in 1976 in Tampa and moved to St. Petersburg at five. She graduated from the Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School, traveled to Europe, fell for Impressionism, and then — detour — earned a BS in Electrical Engineering from USF and designed RF circuits for several years. She creates 200 to 300 pieces annually, working primarily in oils at every scale from notecards to fifty-foot walls.\n\nHer SHINE 2015 Nikola Tesla portrait is on 22nd Street South. She also painted "Little Miss Sisyphus" — a girl pushing an enormous reflective sphere up a mountain — around the corner from the Tesla. For Grand Central Brewhouse, she painted "Last Call" — six musical icons chosen because "they\'re all extremely talented and socially conscious." She finished the fifty-by-thirty-foot piece in three weeks.',desc:'A monochromatic blue-green mural depicting an oversized hundred-dollar bill. The central oval portrait — where Benjamin Franklin would be — is replaced by a pair of hands gently cradling a curled-up newborn baby. The bill\'s design faithfully mimics real currency: "FEDERAL RESERVE NOTE" across the top, "THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA" at right, "100" in all four corners, and ornate scrollwork borders. The serial number reads "AXΔ515 22ND ST S.O." The signature line reads "Are You My Mother?" The entire piece is rendered in shades of teal, blue-green, and white, matching the color of real US bills.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/carrie-jadus-mother.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'Began as a 2015 study for The Motherhood Project; Jadus enlarged it to a mural in 2017 for the ArtsXchange grand opening. She leaves the fetus-and-currency pairing open to interpretation.',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'She has a degree in electrical engineering. She worked as a radio-frequency design engineer. Then she quit to paint.\n\nCarrie Jadus was born in 1976 in Tampa, FL. Her family moved to St. Petersburg when she was five. She comes from an artistic lineage — family populated with creatives. She attended the Pinellas County Center for the Arts magnet program at Gibbs High School, training across multiple mediums. A post-graduation trip to Europe sparked a fascination with Impressionism. She then earned a BS in Electrical Engineering from the University of South Florida and worked as a radio-frequency design engineer for several years before quitting to pursue art full-time.\n\nShe works primarily in oils, comfortable at every scale from notecards to fifty-foot murals. She occupies "the rich and fertile ground between realism and impressionism" with a "distinctive, soft and subtly subversive style." Key influences: Winslow Homer (storytelling) and Egon Schiele (the human figure). She creates 200-300 pieces annually. She has expanded into large-scale sculpture through collaboration with MGA Sculpture Studio. Licensed as a professional artist since 2016, she uses "the business plan of a Toymaker as a model" — multiple product lines to sustain income.\n\nHer SHINE 2015 murals include the Nikola Tesla portrait at 22nd St S and 5th Ave S, painted with assistant Tara Radosevich over two weeks, and "Little Miss Sisyphus," a girl pushing an enormous reflective sphere up a mountain. At Grand Central Brewhouse she painted "Last Call" (2020), a 50x30 ft mural of six musical icons: Bob Marley, Bono, Bruce Springsteen, Lady Gaga, Eddie Vedder, and John Legend — chosen because "they\'re all extremely talented and socially conscious." Heads roughly 8 ft tall, completed in three weeks, commissioned by Kevin Milkey. "Awakening Bodhisattva" at Dog Bar (2020) is a memorial to Bodhi, dog of artist Marianne Wysocki — a double entendre: the dog waking up, and the Buddhist concept of a being who delays nirvana out of compassion.\n\nShe maintains a studio at Soft Water Studios in the Warehouse Arts District. She illustrates for St. Pete Preservation and designed a Kahwa Coffee Art Canister (4th installment) benefiting the St. Petersburg Arts Endowment. Solo series include "Blue Nudes," "Imaginary Friends," and "Grand Portraits" (Einstein, Nina Simone). Instagram: @jadusfineart. Website: carriejadus.com.',src:['http://www.carriejadus.com/bio.html','http://www.carriejadus.com/murals.html','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/carrie-jadus-paints-on/','https://theartisanmagazine.com/carrie-jadus/','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/carrie-jadus/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/art-business-business-of-art-carrie-jadus/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/hustle/hustle-carrie-jadus/']},
  {id:109,a:'Chad Mize',t:'Twiggy',loc:'653 Central Ave (alley)',bldg:'Behind Bluelucy Gallery',lat:27.77164,lng:-82.642647,y:0,cat:'shine-legacy',ig:'',bio:'CHAD MIZE is a multimedia artist, designer and curator residing in St. Petersburg, Florida. He was born in New Orleans, Louisiana in 1974, and then moved to Tampa Bay Florida, where he spent most of his childhood. An early interest in art resulted in his earning a BFA in Visual Communications at the University of Mississippi.\n\nHis early career included drawing caricature portraits at Faneuil Hall in Boston and illustrating Flash animations for a digital textbook company in the late 1990s. In 2003, MIZE moved to St. Petersburg, FL and has been championing the Tampa Bay art scene ever since.\n\nMIZE created the "Paris/Tokyo/London/St. Pete" shirt in 2010. After 16 years, it is still selling in local shops, popular among both locals and tourists.\n\nMIZE co-founded BLUELUCY gallery on Central Avenue in 2010 (representing 450-plus artists before closing in 2015).\n\nIn 2018, Mize opened the gallery MIZE in the Historic Uptown neighborhood of St Petersburg. Following the closing of MIZE in 2022 he set his sights on a larger gallery project entitled SPACE. Currently he is curating exhibits throughout Tampa Bay at various venues.\n\nHe served on SHINE\'s organizing committee for a decade (2015-2025).\n\nWith a love for past and contemporary culture, he takes a humorous, tongue-in-cheek approach to his designs, which include playful doodles, colorful patterns, political art and graphics with text.\n\nMize\'s client list has included Aids Healthcare Foundation, The Dali Museum, Facebook, COACH, NUTRL, Tampa Bay Lightning, Tampa Bay Rays, Sprouts, Kahwa, Busch Gardens, The Vinoy Hotel, and JW Marriott.\n\nMize\'s work is held in numerous private and corporate collections nationally and internationally.',desc:'A stencilled black-and-white mural of 1960s fashion icon Twiggy\'s face in the alley behind the 600 block of Central Avenue. The image references the iconic 1966 photograph by Barry Lategan, showing Twiggy\'s signature doe eyes with stars, rendered with an iridescent sheen against a faded light blue wall. The deliberately muted, pastel-hued palette contrasts with the bolder murals in the surrounding area.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/pre-shine/chad-mize-twiggy.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'https://cdn.muralquest.app/audio/109.mp3',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE organizing committee (6+ years); James Museum featured artist (2025); Dali Museum \'Outside In\' (2025); founded BLUELUCY and MIZE Gallery',fw:[{name:'chadmize.com',url:'http://chadmize.com'}],sbio:'He created the "Paris/Tokyo/London/St. Pete" shirt in 2010. It is still everywhere. Chad Mize was born in 1974 in New Orleans, Louisiana, and spent most of his childhood in Tampa Bay — raised in Bradenton, south of St. Petersburg, where family trips to the Dali Museum and Jannus Live first hooked him on the area. He earned a BFA in Visual Communications from the University of Mississippi (Ole Miss), near Oxford, Mississippi, then moved to Boston in his twenties. There he drew sidewalk character portraits in front of Faneuil Hall, an unpolished gig that laid the groundwork for the improvisational style he still uses. In 1998 he took a job as an illustrator for a digital textbook company, building Flash animations for educational content. Laid off a week before 9/11, he pivoted, co-founding the creative studio Bluelucy with partner Phillip Clark and moving back to Florida. He considered Sarasota but chose St. Petersburg in 2003, reasoning that any small city supporting a world-class institution like the Dali Museum had room for more.\n\nIn the early 2000s St. Pete had few galleries or open downtown businesses, but the renaissance was beginning. Mize was part of the warehouse-party scene with the Vitale Brothers, alongside a regular cast of artists and dreamers. Galleries opened along Central Avenue — Craftsman House, Shapiro\'s, Davidson Fine Art — and in 2009 the 600 block became a hub for artist-run shops. Bluelucy, at 653 Central Avenue, thrived there. Some of the city\'s first murals were painted by Mize behind Bluelucy, including the iconic "Twiggy" and "Mr. Sun" images, both of which still remain (partially obscured by later upgrades). Bluelucy hosted monthly themed exhibitions featuring dozens, sometimes more than a hundred, local artists, representing 450-plus artists in total before closing in 2014/2015 due to rising rents.\n\nIn 2010 Mize met his now-husband Dan Schmidt, and on a trip spotted a "Paris/Tokyo/London/Torrance" shirt that inspired his "Paris/Tokyo/London/St. Pete" version — a design now seen on sidewalks and in shops across the city. In 2015 he was asked to help launch the brand-new SHINE Mural Festival, coordinating closely with director Jenee Priebe and serving on the organizing committee for six-plus years. He has personally painted over 120 murals, mostly in Tampa Bay but also in Miami and other cities. In 2018 he opened MIZE Gallery in the Historic Uptown neighborhood on MLK Jr. Street, running monthly exhibitions of Bay Area talent for five years and adapting with virtual shows and online sales during the pandemic. After MIZE closed in 2022, he opened SPACE Gallery in 2023 in a former PSTA bus depot on south 28th Street, hosting boisterous performance-and-art nights with Bay-area stalwarts including Bask, Zulu Painter, the Vitale Brothers, IBOMBS, Frank Strunk III, and Andrea Pawlisz, who collaborated closely with him at both SPACE and MIZE Gallery. The city objected to the large gatherings and SPACE shuttered after a few shows. He now curates art pop-ups at Green Bench Brewery and runs "Side Spot," a mini-gallery next to his husband Dan\'s restaurant Uptown Eats. He also helped start the St. Pete Shuffle in 2005.\n\nHis client list includes AIDS Healthcare Foundation, The Dali Museum, COACH, NUTRL, Tampa Bay Lightning, Tampa Bay Rays, Sprouts, Kahwa, Busch Gardens, The Vinoy Hotel, and JW Marriott. His unmistakable cartoon-like imagery — playful doodles, colorful patterns, political art, graphics with text, commentary on politics, religion and contemporary culture — has appeared on Coach bags, paddle-ball rackets, t-shirts and walls across the city. Recent and current mural work includes University Town Center in Sarasota (2026), AIDS Healthcare Foundation in St. Petersburg (2025), Fit 2 Run in St. Petersburg (2025), IMG Academy in Bradenton (2025), The Factory in St. Petersburg (2025), the St. Pete Pier (2025), the Troyer Residence (2025), the Dali Museum (2025), 821 16th Street N in St. Petersburg (2025), the Jackson Residence (2025), The Adderley in Ft. Lauderdale (2025), the Antoinette Residence (2025), the Gibson/DeSomery Residence (2025), Wyndham Grand Clearwater Beach (2024), the Wagner Residence (2024), Creative Contractors in Clearwater (2024), Element in Tampa (2024), and the NUTRL x Hollander Hotel collaboration in St. Petersburg (2024). His work is held in private and corporate collections nationally and internationally.\n\nIn 2025 he is a featured artist at the James Museum, displaying work in the stone entry canyon, and was included in "Outside In: New Murals Inspired by Dali" at the Dali Museum, opening May 24, 2025 — a show featuring twelve former SHINE mural artists painting inside the museum walls. Keith Haring is a stated influence; Mize visited Haring\'s Pop Shop in New York as a kid and embraces Haring\'s belief that art should be accessible. His mantra is "make time," and he credits a year of saying yes to everything with pushing his career forward. The Artisan Magazine, in an April 2025 cover story by Robin O\'Dell, called him "St. Pete\'s Godfather of Art." Contact: info@chadmize.com, 727-251-8529, chadmize.com.\n\nCHAD MIZE is a multimedia artist, designer and curator residing in St. Petersburg, Florida. He was born in New Orleans, Louisiana in 1974, and then moved to Tampa Bay Florida, where he spent most of his childhood. An early interest in art resulted in his earning a BFA in Visual Communications at the University of Mississippi.\n\nHis early career included drawing caricature portraits at Faneuil Hall in Boston and illustrating Flash animations for a digital textbook company in the late 1990s. In 2003, MIZE moved to St. Petersburg, FL and has been championing the Tampa Bay art scene ever since.\n\nMIZE created the "Paris/Tokyo/London/St. Pete" shirt in 2010. After 16 years, it is still selling in local shops, popular among both locals and tourists.\n\nMIZE co-founded BLUELUCY gallery on Central Avenue in 2010 (representing 450-plus artists before closing in 2015).\n\nIn 2018, Mize opened the gallery MIZE in the Historic Uptown neighborhood of St Petersburg. Following the closing of MIZE in 2022 he set his sights on a larger gallery project entitled SPACE. Currently he is curating exhibits throughout Tampa Bay at various venues.\n\nHe served on SHINE\'s organizing committee for a decade (2015-2025).\n\nWith a love for past and contemporary culture, he takes a humorous, tongue-in-cheek approach to his designs, which include playful doodles, colorful patterns, political art and graphics with text.\n\nMize\'s client list has included Aids Healthcare Foundation, The Dali Museum, Facebook, COACH, NUTRL, Tampa Bay Lightning, Tampa Bay Rays, Sprouts, Kahwa, Busch Gardens, The Vinoy Hotel, and JW Marriott.\n\nMize\'s work is held in numerous private and corporate collections nationally and internationally.',fn:['Created the Paris/Tokyo/London/St. Pete shirt','Godfather of St. Pete art','Over 120 murals across the city'],src:['https://www.visitstpeteclearwater.com/profile/twiggy-chad-mize/5944','https://stpetemuraltour.com/2014/08/12/chad-mize-blue-lucy/','https://theartisanmagazine.com/chad-size-stpete/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/influencers/chad-mize/','http://chadmize.com/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/chad-mize-2022']},
  {id:116,a:'Chad Mize',t:'',loc:'421 4th Ave N',bldg:'Hollander Hotel',lat:27.777079,lng:-82.63887,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'chadmize',bio:'CHAD MIZE is a multimedia artist, designer and curator residing in St. Petersburg, Florida. He was born in New Orleans, Louisiana in 1974, and then moved to Tampa Bay Florida, where he spent most of his childhood. An early interest in art resulted in his earning a BFA in Visual Communications at the University of Mississippi.\n\nHis early career included drawing caricature portraits at Faneuil Hall in Boston and illustrating Flash animations for a digital textbook company in the late 1990s. In 2003, MIZE moved to St. Petersburg, FL and has been championing the Tampa Bay art scene ever since.\n\nMIZE created the "Paris/Tokyo/London/St. Pete" shirt in 2010. After 16 years, it is still selling in local shops, popular among both locals and tourists.\n\nMIZE co-founded BLUELUCY gallery on Central Avenue in 2010 (representing 450-plus artists before closing in 2015).\n\nIn 2018, Mize opened the gallery MIZE in the Historic Uptown neighborhood of St Petersburg. Following the closing of MIZE in 2022 he set his sights on a larger gallery project entitled SPACE. Currently he is curating exhibits throughout Tampa Bay at various venues.\n\nHe served on SHINE\'s organizing committee for a decade (2015-2025).\n\nWith a love for past and contemporary culture, he takes a humorous, tongue-in-cheek approach to his designs, which include playful doodles, colorful patterns, political art and graphics with text.\n\nMize\'s client list has included Aids Healthcare Foundation, The Dali Museum, Facebook, COACH, NUTRL, Tampa Bay Lightning, Tampa Bay Rays, Sprouts, Kahwa, Busch Gardens, The Vinoy Hotel, and JW Marriott.\n\nMize\'s work is held in numerous private and corporate collections nationally and internationally.',desc:'A large three-story commercial mural on the side of the Hollander Hotel in Chad Mize\'s signature bold-line illustration style. On a white background, colorful vignettes in orange, teal, yellow, and pink depict Florida icons — flamingos, a pink dolphin, an orange slice, a mermaid, a seahorse, beach chairs, palm trees, and a retro couple in swimwear splashing in the surf. "St. Pete" appears in playful lettering at top, "HH Hollander Hotel" in gold at center, and "Florida" in a retro script below. NÜTRL Vodka Seltzer branding appears in the upper right. Signed "@CHADMIZE."',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/chad-mize-hollander.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'https://cdn.muralquest.app/audio/116.mp3',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE organizing committee member; founder of MIZE Gallery',fw:null,sbio:'CHAD MIZE is a multimedia artist, designer and curator residing in St. Petersburg, Florida. He was born in New Orleans, Louisiana in 1974, and then moved to Tampa Bay Florida, where he spent most of his childhood. An early interest in art resulted in his earning a BFA in Visual Communications at the University of Mississippi.\n\nHis early career included drawing caricature portraits at Faneuil Hall in Boston and illustrating Flash animations for a digital textbook company in the late 1990s. In 2003, MIZE moved to St. Petersburg, FL and has been championing the Tampa Bay art scene ever since.\n\nMIZE created the "Paris/Tokyo/London/St. Pete" shirt in 2010. After 16 years, it is still selling in local shops, popular among both locals and tourists.\n\nMIZE co-founded BLUELUCY gallery on Central Avenue in 2010 (representing 450-plus artists before closing in 2015).\n\nIn 2018, Mize opened the gallery MIZE in the Historic Uptown neighborhood of St Petersburg. Following the closing of MIZE in 2022 he set his sights on a larger gallery project entitled SPACE. Currently he is curating exhibits throughout Tampa Bay at various venues.\n\nHe served on SHINE\'s organizing committee for a decade (2015-2025).\n\nWith a love for past and contemporary culture, he takes a humorous, tongue-in-cheek approach to his designs, which include playful doodles, colorful patterns, political art and graphics with text.\n\nMize\'s client list has included Aids Healthcare Foundation, The Dali Museum, Facebook, COACH, NUTRL, Tampa Bay Lightning, Tampa Bay Rays, Sprouts, Kahwa, Busch Gardens, The Vinoy Hotel, and JW Marriott.\n\nMize\'s work is held in numerous private and corporate collections nationally and internationally.',fn:['Paris/Tokyo/London/St. Pete shirt creator','James Museum featured artist (2025)','Over 120 murals — Godfather of Art'],src:['http://chadmize.com/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/influencers/chad-mize/','https://theartisanmagazine.com/chad-size-stpete/','https://www.instagram.com/chadmize/']},
  {id:166,a:'Chad Mize',t:'',loc:'Pinellas Trail',bldg:'',lat:27.763205,lng:-82.669829,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'chadmize',bio:'CHAD MIZE is a multimedia artist, designer and curator residing in St. Petersburg, Florida. He was born in New Orleans, Louisiana in 1974, and then moved to Tampa Bay Florida, where he spent most of his childhood. An early interest in art resulted in his earning a BFA in Visual Communications at the University of Mississippi.\n\nHis early career included drawing caricature portraits at Faneuil Hall in Boston and illustrating Flash animations for a digital textbook company in the late 1990s. In 2003, MIZE moved to St. Petersburg, FL and has been championing the Tampa Bay art scene ever since.\n\nMIZE created the "Paris/Tokyo/London/St. Pete" shirt in 2010. After 16 years, it is still selling in local shops, popular among both locals and tourists.\n\nMIZE co-founded BLUELUCY gallery on Central Avenue in 2010 (representing 450-plus artists before closing in 2015).\n\nIn 2018, Mize opened the gallery MIZE in the Historic Uptown neighborhood of St Petersburg. Following the closing of MIZE in 2022 he set his sights on a larger gallery project entitled SPACE. Currently he is curating exhibits throughout Tampa Bay at various venues.\n\nHe served on SHINE\'s organizing committee for a decade (2015-2025).\n\nWith a love for past and contemporary culture, he takes a humorous, tongue-in-cheek approach to his designs, which include playful doodles, colorful patterns, political art and graphics with text.\n\nMize\'s client list has included Aids Healthcare Foundation, The Dali Museum, Facebook, COACH, NUTRL, Tampa Bay Lightning, Tampa Bay Rays, Sprouts, Kahwa, Busch Gardens, The Vinoy Hotel, and JW Marriott.\n\nMize\'s work is held in numerous private and corporate collections nationally and internationally.',desc:'A teal wall packed edge to edge with hundreds of hand-drawn doodle icons in pink, blue, white, orange, and navy. The composition is a maximalist collage of Florida and pop culture imagery: flamingos, octopus, dolphins, sharks, jellyfish, a UFO, smiley faces, mushrooms, a VW bus, lightning bolts, pizza slices, anchors, palm trees, suns, moons, an all-seeing eye, a hot air balloon, starfish, seahorses, and countless other playful figures. Two windows and a white door are incorporated into the design. Signed "Mize" in the upper right. Small trees flank the base.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/chad-mize-doodle-wall.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'Chad Mize — see full bio on his other entries (ids 58, 77, 109, 116). This is a commercial doodle wall in the Warehouse Arts District. Hundreds of hand-drawn icons packed edge to edge on a teal background in his signature style — flamingos, octopus, UFOs, smiley faces, sharks, dolphins, mushrooms, VW buses, lightning bolts, pizza slices, jellyfish, anchors, suns, moons, and countless other playful icons. Signed "Mize" in the upper right corner.\n\nCHAD MIZE is a multimedia artist, designer and curator residing in St. Petersburg, Florida. He was born in New Orleans, Louisiana in 1974, and then moved to Tampa Bay Florida, where he spent most of his childhood. An early interest in art resulted in his earning a BFA in Visual Communications at the University of Mississippi.\n\nHis early career included drawing caricature portraits at Faneuil Hall in Boston and illustrating Flash animations for a digital textbook company in the late 1990s. In 2003, MIZE moved to St. Petersburg, FL and has been championing the Tampa Bay art scene ever since.\n\nMIZE created the "Paris/Tokyo/London/St. Pete" shirt in 2010. After 16 years, it is still selling in local shops, popular among both locals and tourists.\n\nMIZE co-founded BLUELUCY gallery on Central Avenue in 2010 (representing 450-plus artists before closing in 2015).\n\nIn 2018, Mize opened the gallery MIZE in the Historic Uptown neighborhood of St Petersburg. Following the closing of MIZE in 2022 he set his sights on a larger gallery project entitled SPACE. Currently he is curating exhibits throughout Tampa Bay at various venues.\n\nHe served on SHINE\'s organizing committee for a decade (2015-2025).\n\nWith a love for past and contemporary culture, he takes a humorous, tongue-in-cheek approach to his designs, which include playful doodles, colorful patterns, political art and graphics with text.\n\nMize\'s client list has included Aids Healthcare Foundation, The Dali Museum, Facebook, COACH, NUTRL, Tampa Bay Lightning, Tampa Bay Rays, Sprouts, Kahwa, Busch Gardens, The Vinoy Hotel, and JW Marriott.\n\nMize\'s work is held in numerous private and corporate collections nationally and internationally.'},
  {id:196,a:'Chad Mize',t:'Eye Love St. Pete',loc:'',bldg:'',lat:27.770053,lng:-82.662247,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'chadmize',bio:'CHAD MIZE is a multimedia artist, designer and curator residing in St. Petersburg, Florida. He was born in New Orleans, Louisiana in 1974, and then moved to Tampa Bay Florida, where he spent most of his childhood. An early interest in art resulted in his earning a BFA in Visual Communications at the University of Mississippi.\n\nHis early career included drawing caricature portraits at Faneuil Hall in Boston and illustrating Flash animations for a digital textbook company in the late 1990s. In 2003, MIZE moved to St. Petersburg, FL and has been championing the Tampa Bay art scene ever since.\n\nMIZE created the "Paris/Tokyo/London/St. Pete" shirt in 2010. After 16 years, it is still selling in local shops, popular among both locals and tourists.\n\nMIZE co-founded BLUELUCY gallery on Central Avenue in 2010 (representing 450-plus artists before closing in 2015).\n\nIn 2018, Mize opened the gallery MIZE in the Historic Uptown neighborhood of St Petersburg. Following the closing of MIZE in 2022 he set his sights on a larger gallery project entitled SPACE. Currently he is curating exhibits throughout Tampa Bay at various venues.\n\nHe served on SHINE\'s organizing committee for a decade (2015-2025).\n\nWith a love for past and contemporary culture, he takes a humorous, tongue-in-cheek approach to his designs, which include playful doodles, colorful patterns, political art and graphics with text.\n\nMize\'s client list has included Aids Healthcare Foundation, The Dali Museum, Facebook, COACH, NUTRL, Tampa Bay Lightning, Tampa Bay Rays, Sprouts, Kahwa, Busch Gardens, The Vinoy Hotel, and JW Marriott.\n\nMize\'s work is held in numerous private and corporate collections nationally and internationally.',desc:'A wide horizontal mural on a white house wall with a steeply pitched shingled roof above. The composition is a dense black-and-white line drawing of St. Petersburg venues, signs, and landmarks — Ruby\'s Elixir, BABA Bakery & Cafe, Ringside Cafe, Coliseum, Rowdies (with player #15), Uptown Eats, Three Birds Tavern, Daddy Kool Records, Dairy Inn, Sunken Garden, Cage Brewing, Vinoy, Mazaro, Beer & Tacos, Bodega, Janus Live, and dozens more — drawn in Mize\'s signature playful doodle style. At the center, three colored elements pop against the otherwise monochrome composition: a blue eye with concentric circles, a red heart, and "ST. PETE" in chunky blue rounded lettering with bold black outline. A small white dog with a red tongue stands below the heart. Signed "MIZE" in plain black lettering in the upper right.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/chad-mize-eye-love-stpete.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE organizing committee (6+ years); James Museum featured artist (2025); Dali Museum \'Outside In\' (2025); founded BLUELUCY and MIZE Gallery; Artisan Magazine cover story April 2025 (\'St. Pete\'s Godfather of Art\')',fw:[{name:'chadmize.com',url:'http://chadmize.com'}],sbio:'An eye, a heart, and "ST. PETE" in fat blue letters at the center. Surrounding it: a black-and-white doodle map of every St. Pete venue worth knowing — Ruby\'s Elixir, BABA Bakery, Ringside Cafe, Coliseum, Rowdies, Uptown Eats, Three Birds Tavern, Daddy Kool Records, Dairy Inn, Sunken Garden, Cage Brewing, the Vinoy, Mazaro, Tampa Bay Lightning, and more. Signed "MIZE" in the upper right corner. The wall is essentially a hand-drawn city map, populated with the bars, restaurants, music venues, sports teams, museums, and landmarks that define the city\'s character.\n\nChad Mize was born in 1974 in New Orleans, grew up in Tampa Bay, and has been in St. Petersburg for over seventeen years. He earned a BFA in Visual Communications from the University of Mississippi. His early career included drawing sidewalk portraits at Faneuil Hall in Boston and illustrating Flash animations for a digital textbook company. His style is bold, cartoon-like imagery commenting on politics, religion, and contemporary culture — humorous, tongue-in-cheek, influenced by Keith Haring\'s belief that art should be accessible.\n\nHe has painted over 120 murals. He co-founded BLUELUCY gallery on Central Avenue in 2010 (representing 450-plus artists before closing in 2015), then opened MIZE Gallery on MLK Jr. Street (2018-2022), then SPACE Gallery in a former PSTA bus depot (2023), and now curates pop-ups at Green Bench Brewery and runs "Side Spot," a mini-gallery next to his husband Dan\'s restaurant Uptown Eats — which appears as one of the venues drawn into this wall.\n\nCommercial clients include the Tampa Bay Lightning, Tampa Bay Rays, Busch Gardens, JW Marriott, the Vinoy, NUTRL, COACH (he designed bag patterns), Sprouts, Kahwa Coffee, and AIDS Healthcare Foundation. Recent commercial walls include the Hollander Hotel (NÜTRL Vodka Seltzer sponsor), the Wyndham Grand in Clearwater Beach, Element Tampa, IMG Academy in Bradenton, the St. Pete Pier, the Dali Museum, the Adderley in Ft. Lauderdale, and University Town Center Sarasota.\n\nHe is a featured artist at the James Museum (2025) and was included in "Outside In: New Murals Inspired by Dali" at the Dali Museum (2025). The Artisan Magazine cover story by Robin O\'Dell (April 28, 2025) called him "St. Pete\'s Godfather of Art." Website: chadmize.com. Instagram: @chadmize.\n\nCHAD MIZE is a multimedia artist, designer and curator residing in St. Petersburg, Florida. He was born in New Orleans, Louisiana in 1974, and then moved to Tampa Bay Florida, where he spent most of his childhood. An early interest in art resulted in his earning a BFA in Visual Communications at the University of Mississippi.\n\nHis early career included drawing caricature portraits at Faneuil Hall in Boston and illustrating Flash animations for a digital textbook company in the late 1990s. In 2003, MIZE moved to St. Petersburg, FL and has been championing the Tampa Bay art scene ever since.\n\nMIZE created the "Paris/Tokyo/London/St. Pete" shirt in 2010. After 16 years, it is still selling in local shops, popular among both locals and tourists.\n\nMIZE co-founded BLUELUCY gallery on Central Avenue in 2010 (representing 450-plus artists before closing in 2015).\n\nIn 2018, Mize opened the gallery MIZE in the Historic Uptown neighborhood of St Petersburg. Following the closing of MIZE in 2022 he set his sights on a larger gallery project entitled SPACE. Currently he is curating exhibits throughout Tampa Bay at various venues.\n\nHe served on SHINE\'s organizing committee for a decade (2015-2025).\n\nWith a love for past and contemporary culture, he takes a humorous, tongue-in-cheek approach to his designs, which include playful doodles, colorful patterns, political art and graphics with text.\n\nMize\'s client list has included Aids Healthcare Foundation, The Dali Museum, Facebook, COACH, NUTRL, Tampa Bay Lightning, Tampa Bay Rays, Sprouts, Kahwa, Busch Gardens, The Vinoy Hotel, and JW Marriott.\n\nMize\'s work is held in numerous private and corporate collections nationally and internationally.',src:['http://chadmize.com/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/influencers/chad-mize/','https://theartisanmagazine.com/chad-size-stpete/','https://www.instagram.com/chadmize/']},
  {id:182,a:'Chris Dyer',t:'',loc:'Gulfport',bldg:'',lat:27.744337,lng:-82.699638,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'chris_dyer',bio:'He has painted in forty-five countries. His mural is in the Bob Marley Museum.\n\nChris Dyer was born in Ottawa in 1979, moved to Lima, Peru at four, and returned to Canada at seventeen. He studied illustration at Dawson College in Montreal and trained under visionary masters Alex Grey, Robert Venosa, and Amanda Sage. His style fuses psychedelic imagery, Incan and pre-Columbian influences, sacred geometry, and 1980s skate culture.\n\nHe founded the Positive Creations brand in 2003 — clothing, tapestries, books, skateboards, footwear. He has been art director at Creation Skateboards and designed over a hundred commercial skateboard graphics for Santa Cruz, Deathwish, and Think. He has done live painting for Diplo, Tipper, and Immortal Technique at festivals from Burning Man to Boom to Rainbow Serpent. He published two books and sold out 5,555 NFTs in 2021.\n\nHe now lives in a purple house in Gulfport with his partner Paulina Casati.',desc:'A large mandala fills the side of a residential house in Gulfport. The central figure is a symmetrical face composed of interlocking leaves, eyes, and organic patterns in Chris Dyer\'s signature psychedelic style — greens, blues, purples, pinks, and yellows densely layered with sacred geometry motifs. A lightning bolt runs down the center of the forehead. Multiple eyes peer out from within the design. The mandala radiates outward into petal-like forms edged in yellow, pink, and blue. Behind it, bold orange and red sunburst rays fill the entire wall. Signed "Chris Dyer" in purple graffiti lettering at lower left.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/chris-dyer-gulfport-mandala.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:[{name:'positivecreations.ca',url:''}],fn:['Painted in 45 countries','Lives in a purple house in Gulfport','Psychedelic spiritual art meets street culture']},
  {id:186,a:'Chris Dyer',t:'',loc:'Gulfport',bldg:'Smoke Shop',lat:27.748562,lng:-82.699713,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'chris_dyer',bio:'He has painted in forty-five countries. His mural is in the Bob Marley Museum.\n\nChris Dyer was born in Ottawa in 1979, moved to Lima, Peru at four, and returned to Canada at seventeen. He studied illustration at Dawson College in Montreal and trained under visionary masters Alex Grey, Robert Venosa, and Amanda Sage. His style fuses psychedelic imagery, Incan and pre-Columbian influences, sacred geometry, and 1980s skate culture.\n\nHe founded the Positive Creations brand in 2003 — clothing, tapestries, books, skateboards, footwear. He now lives in a purple house in Gulfport with his partner Paulina Casati.',desc:'A psychedelic mural covering the front facade of a purple smoke shop in Gulfport. The central figure is a symmetrical creature with multiple eyes and ornate geometric patterns in Chris Dyer\'s signature style — greens, golds, pinks, and purples interlocking in sacred geometry motifs. A lightning bolt runs through the design. The surrounding wall is painted deep purple with pink drip effects along the roofline. The shop sign reads "SMOKE SHOP — CIGARS & TOBACCO" in green lettering above.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/chris-dyer-gulfport-smokeshop.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:[{name:'positivecreations.ca',url:''}],fn:['Painted in 45 countries','Lives in a purple house in Gulfport','Psychedelic spiritual art meets street culture']},
  {id:207,a:'Chris Dyer',t:'',loc:'5013 20th Ave S, Unit F, Gulfport',bldg:'Overspray',lat:27.75088,lng:-82.70128,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'chris_dyer',bio:'Same vocabulary in compressed form: an all-seeing eye in a flame-tipped teardrop, geometric wings, ceremonial folds, a meditating figure at the base. Painted on a tall narrow side wall at Overspray, the Vitale Bros\' graffiti shop in Gulfport. Signed "CHRIS DYER \'25" across the top.\n\nChris Dyer was born in Ottawa in 1979, moved to Lima, Peru at four, and returned to Canada at seventeen. He studied illustration at Dawson College in Montreal and trained under visionary masters Alex Grey, Robert Venosa, and Amanda Sage. His style fuses psychedelic imagery, Incan and pre-Columbian influences, sacred geometry, and 1980s skate culture.\n\nHe founded the Positive Creations brand in 2003 — clothing, tapestries, books, skateboards, footwear. He has been art director at Creation Skateboards and designed over a hundred commercial skateboard graphics for Santa Cruz, Deathwish, and Think. He has painted in forty-five countries including a mural in the Bob Marley Museum. He lives in a purple house in Gulfport with his partner Paulina Casati.',desc:'A tall narrow vertical mural on the side of a single-story white cinder block building. The composition is symmetrical and totemic, stacked from top to bottom. At the top, a red-and-yellow flame-tipped teardrop shape cradles a wall-mounted utility box and security camera. Below it, a green teardrop with a central blue-and-orange single eye sits inside a pink lotus petal, flanked by yellow chevron wings. Below the eye, a vertical stack of ceremonial folds and totemic forms: gold-and-pink chevrons, magenta zigzag patterns, teal triangles, mandala-style flowers, and at the base a small blue figure seated cross-legged in meditation. Color palette is saturated visionary — magenta, teal, gold, green, blue, purple — outlined in heavy black graphic lines. Signed at the very top in purple wildstyle lettering: "CHRIS DYER \'25."',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/chris-dyer-overspray-totem.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:[{name:'positivecreations.ca',url:'https://positivecreations.ca'}],sbio:'Chris Dyer — see full bio on his other entries (ids 39, 182, 186). This is a 2025 wall on the side of Overspray, the Vitale Bros\' street-art shop and graffiti school in Gulfport. Vertical narrow composition: a flame-tipped teardrop with a single blue/orange eye at center, geometric chevron wings, cascading ceremonial folds in pink, magenta, teal, and gold, and a small seated meditating figure at the base. Signed "CHRIS DYER \'25" in purple graffiti tag across the top.\n\nBorn in Ottawa 1979, raised in Lima, Peru. Studied illustration at Dawson College, Montreal. Trained under Alex Grey, Robert Venosa, and Amanda Sage. Founded Positive Creations in 2003. Has painted in forty-five countries including the Bob Marley Museum. Designed over a hundred skateboard graphics for Santa Cruz, Deathwish, and Think. Sold out 5,555 NFTs in 2021. Lives in a purple house in Gulfport with Paulina Casati.',src:['https://chrisdyerart.com/','https://www.instagram.com/chris_dyer/']},
  {id:159,a:'Derek Donnelly',t:'Sonshine',loc:'226 Dr MLK Jr St N',bldg:'',lat:27.774037,lng:-82.647108,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'derekdonnelly',bio:'He painted a T-Rex in a Hawaiian shirt going fishing — for AARP. That tells you most of what you need to know about Derek Donnelly.\n\nA Pinellas County native, Donnelly grew up in Pinellas Park, graduated from the Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School in 2001, and studied graphic design and digital illustration at IADT Tampa. He went full-time on murals in 2010. A year later he opened Saint Paint Arts gallery on the 600 block of Central Avenue — not as a vanity project, but as a launchpad. Several artists got their first real exposure there before he closed it in 2014 to focus on walls.\n\nHis style sits at the intersection of illustration and realism — playful without being childish, bold without being loud. His son Syre shows up in murals across the city. "Sonshine" is likely one of those portraits — a monochrome boy in sunglasses reflecting a warm sunset, set against a deep starfield, near Planet Retro Records.',desc:'A monochrome portrait of a young boy fills the wall. He wears dark sunglasses whose lenses reflect a warm orange-gold sunset — the only color in the piece. His hair, skin, and clothing are rendered in shades of gray and silver-blue against a deep blue-black starfield background dotted with tiny white stars. The boy gazes slightly upward with a calm expression. "Donnelly" is signed in the upper right. Some paint has peeled from the cheek and forehead, revealing the wall beneath.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/derek-donnelly-sonshine.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'Derek Donnelly — see full bio on his SHINE 2025 entries (ids 5, 6) and SHINE 2018 entry (id 115). Pinellas County native, Gibbs High School Center for the Arts (2001), IADT Tampa (graphic design / digital illustration). Full-time muralist since 2010. Founded Saint Paint Arts gallery (2011-2014), COVE gallery (Pinellas Park), Pinellas Arts Village district, and Public Art Project Inc. nonprofit. Clients include Fender Guitars, Tampa Bay Rays, Sirata Beach Resort, and AARP. Multiple Best of the Bay wins. Inaugural Artmaker 2026 at Ruth Eckerd Hall. SHINE 2025 artist liaison and operations while producing two murals.\n\n"Sonshine" is a commercial mural near Planet Retro Records in St. Petersburg. A monochrome portrait of a young boy wearing sunglasses that reflect a warm orange-gold sunset, set against a deep blue-black starfield. Signed "Donnelly" in the upper right corner. Some paint peeling visible as of April 2026. The boy may be his son Syre, who appears in murals across the city.\n\nInstagram: @derekdonnelly.'},
  {id:177,a:'Derek Donnelly',t:'Cultasaurus',loc:'269 16th St N',bldg:'',lat:27.774503,lng:-82.654687,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'derekdonnelly',bio:'He painted a T-Rex in a Hawaiian shirt going fishing — for AARP. That tells you most of what you need to know about Derek Donnelly.\n\nA Pinellas County native, Donnelly grew up in Pinellas Park, graduated from the Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School in 2001, and studied graphic design and digital illustration at IADT Tampa. He went full-time on murals in 2010. A year later he opened Saint Paint Arts gallery on the 600 block of Central Avenue — not as a vanity project, but as a launchpad. Several artists got their first real exposure there before he closed it in 2014 to focus on walls.\n\nHis style sits at the intersection of illustration and realism — playful without being childish, bold without being loud. His son Syre shows up in murals across the city. So does Florida wildlife. He doesn\'t separate the personal from the public work. In 2012, when mentor Bill "Woo" Correira died, Donnelly organized the Woomorial — a 40-artist tribute mural on the 600 block that became a landmark. That instinct to build things for other people keeps showing up. He founded COVE gallery in Pinellas Park, helped establish the Pinellas Arts Village district, and started Public Art Project Inc., a nonprofit that pushes public art into places it wouldn\'t otherwise reach and raised pandemic relief funds for local artists.\n\nHis client list runs from Fender Guitars to the Tampa Bay Rays to Sirata Beach Resort — the first major St. Pete Beach resort to commission a local artist. Multiple Best of the Bay wins. Inaugural Artmaker 2026 at Ruth Eckerd Hall. For SHINE 2025, he wasn\'t just painting — he ran operations and served as artist liaison for the entire festival while producing two murals of his own.',desc:'A wild, multi-story mural wrapping the corner of a commercial building. A massive photorealistic T-Rex with jaws wide open dominates the upper half, rendered in detailed brown-orange tones with textured scales and green eyes. Below and around it: a chaotic pop-culture collage against a sky-blue background — green aliens in flying saucers, UFOs, a grinning Slimer-like ghost, a robot in overalls, an Alfred E. Neuman-style figure in a Hawaiian shirt, a gorilla in a sweatshirt, crystal skulls, purple flowers, palm trees, and tropical foliage. The overall effect is a fever dream mashup of B-movies, sci-fi, and Florida kitsch in Donnelly\'s signature playful illustrative style.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/derek-donnelly-cultasaurus.jpeg',from:'Pinellas County, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Multiple Best of the Bay wins; Inaugural Artmaker 2026 at Ruth Eckerd Hall; SHINE 2025 artist liaison',fw:[{name:'derekdonnelly.com',url:'https://derekdonnelly.com'}],sbio:'He painted a T-Rex in a Hawaiian shirt going fishing — for AARP. That tells you most of what you need to know about Derek Donnelly.\n\nA Pinellas County native from Pinellas Park. Graduated from the Center for the Arts at Gibbs High School in 2001, studied graphic design and digital illustration at IADT Tampa. Full-time muralist since 2010. Founded Saint Paint Arts gallery (2011-2014), COVE gallery in Pinellas Park, Pinellas Arts Village district, and Public Art Project Inc. Clients include Fender Guitars, Tampa Bay Rays, Sirata Beach Resort. Multiple Best of the Bay wins. SHINE 2025 artist liaison and operations. Instagram: @derekdonnelly.',src:['https://stpetecatalyst.com/muralist-derek-donnelly-wants-art-scene-changes/']},
  {id:169,a:'Dreamweaver',t:'',loc:'415 20th St S',bldg:'',lat:27.767103,lng:-82.660247,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'dream.weavin',bio:'She painted her first mural at seven, on her aunt\'s backyard shed in Waco, Texas. She has not stopped.\n\nTaylor Smith — Dreamweaver — grew up between Portland, Oregon and just south of Atlanta, born in Dallas. She studied communications and fine art at Georgia College and State University, with a minor in graphic design, graduating in 2017. She apprenticed at a tattoo shop at sixteen and spent time doing live painting at music festivals — Electric Daisy Carnival, Okeechobee, Electric Forest — before relocating to Florida around 2019.\n\nShe works almost entirely in spray paint, blending expressive abstraction with realism. Nature runs through everything: wildlife, water, birds, flora. She is left-handed. She has painted an estimated 120 to 150 murals, 15 to 20 in St. Pete alone. She is a resident artist at AER Tampa and a member of Few and Far, an international women\'s graffiti crew.\n\nThis piece at 415 20th St S wraps the full front of the building in swirling rainbow aerosol flames — flowing ribbons of orange, magenta, teal, green, and purple against a dark background.',desc:'Swirling ribbons of color wrap the entire front facade of a low commercial building. Flowing aerosol flames in orange, magenta, hot pink, teal, electric green, and deep purple twist and curl against a dark purple-black background. The effect is liquid and luminous — like molten rainbow glass in motion. The piece covers both sides of the building symmetrically, framing a central window and entrance. Plants sit at the base. The bold, fluid style is Dreamweaver\'s signature aerosol work.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/dreamweaver-20th-st.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'Taylor Smith — Dreamweaver / @dream.weavin — born in Dallas, grew up between Portland and south of Atlanta. BFA in Communications and Fine Art from Georgia College and State University (2017), minor in graphic design. Private painting lessons as a kid, apprenticed at a tattoo shop at 16. Live painting at Electric Daisy Carnival (Vegas), Okeechobee, Electric Forest. Relocated to Florida around 2019. Works almost entirely in spray paint — expressive abstraction blended with realism, nature themes. Left-handed. Estimated 120-150 murals total, 15-20 in St. Pete. Resident artist at AER Tampa. Member of Few and Far international women\'s graffiti crew. Also painted for SHINE 2022 (id 59) and SHINE 2025 (id 7). Other projects include Clearwater Municipal Parking Garage, sea turtle mural at Coachman Park for Art Oasis 2024, and "The Garden Within" in downtown Kissimmee for Osceola Arts.\n\nThis commercial mural at 415 20th St S wraps the full front of a building in swirling rainbow aerosol flames — flowing ribbons of orange, magenta, teal, electric green, and deep purple against a dark purple-black background.\n\nInstagram: @dream.weavin.'},
  {id:127,a:'Ernesto Maranje',t:'',loc:'2606 Fairfield Ave S',bldg:'Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation HQ',lat:27.763898,lng:-82.668085,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'ernestomaranje',bio:'He discovered painting at twenty-seven because a knee injury kept him off Coast Guard boats. His first attempt was a California flag on a mechanic\'s shop window. Four years later he was painting at Wynwood Walls.\n\nErnesto Maranje is a Cuban-American artist born in Chicago in 1983, raised in Miami\'s Hialeah neighborhood. He served seven years in the U.S. Coast Guard and is entirely self-taught. By 2015 he had painted his first large-scale mural in Little Havana and his career took off. His style builds detailed, nature-immersive compositions — animals emerging from lush organic backgrounds of flowers, foliage, and painterly brushstrokes.\n\nHe was selected for Wynwood Walls\' ten-year anniversary in 2019, where he painted "Sentries." Clients include Johnnie Walker (Blue Label Miami Edition bottle), Disney Springs, Starbucks, Chase, and Chewy. He has painted walls in Beirut, Sulaymaniyah (Iraq, for UNHCR/World Refugee Day), a refugee camp in Athens, and three walls in Kyiv, Ukraine.\n\nThis piece at the Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation headquarters shows a great blue heron, a Florida black bear, and a swallowtail kite — all moving in the same direction, symbolizing wildlife connectivity across Florida.',desc:'A richly detailed nature scene on the facade of the Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation headquarters at The Factory. A great blue heron dominates the center, wings spread mid-stride, rendered in naturalistic grays, blues, and whites with remarkable feather detail. A Florida black bear peers from the left side among lush green foliage. A swallowtail kite swoops through the upper portion while the lower background suggests dark water and vegetation. The palette is moody and atmospheric — deep blues, greens, and stormy grays — giving it a more serious, conservation-minded tone than typical mural work. All the animals face the same direction, symbolizing the corridor concept of wildlife movement and connectivity.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/ernesto-maranje-wildlife-corridor.jpeg',from:'Miami, FL',aud:'',insp:'Commissioned by the Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation for their headquarters. All animals are depicted moving in the same direction, symbolizing the need for wildlife movement and ecological connectivity across Florida.',maw:'',aaw:'Wynwood Walls 10-year anniversary (2019); Johnnie Walker Blue Label Miami Edition bottle design; Disney Springs Art Walk commission',fw:null,sbio:'He discovered painting at twenty-seven because a knee injury kept him off Coast Guard boats. His first attempt was a California flag on a mechanic\'s shop window. Four years later he was painting at Wynwood Walls.\n\nErnesto Maranje is a Cuban-American artist born in Chicago in 1983 and raised in Miami\'s Hialeah neighborhood. He served seven years in the U.S. Coast Guard and is entirely self-taught — he discovered painting during his service when a knee injury kept him off boats during his last year. By 2015 he had painted his first large-scale mural in Little Havana and his career took off. His style builds detailed, nature-immersive compositions where animals emerge from lush organic backgrounds of flowers, foliage, and painterly brushstrokes.\n\nHe was selected to paint at Wynwood Walls for its prestigious ten-year anniversary in 2019, where he painted "Sentries." Commercial clients include Johnnie Walker (the Blue Label Miami Edition bottle design), Disney Springs (Art Walk commission), Starbucks, Chase, and Chewy. International work includes murals in Beirut, Lebanon; Sulaymaniyah, Iraq (with aptART for UNHCR/World Refugee Day); a refugee camp in Athens; and three walls in Kyiv, Ukraine. He has also painted at Beyond Walls in Lynn, Massachusetts. Environmental work includes murals for PangeaSeed Sea Walls and Florida Atlantic University.\n\nThis mural at the Florida Wildlife Corridor Foundation headquarters (2606 Fairfield Ave S, at The Factory) depicts a great blue heron with wings spread, a Florida black bear, and a swallowtail kite — all animals moving in the same direction, symbolizing the corridor concept of wildlife movement and ecological connectivity across Florida. The palette is moody and atmospheric — deep blues, greens, and stormy grays.\n\nInstagram: @ernestomaranje. Website: maranje.com.',src:['https://www.maranje.com/projects/florida-wildlife-corridor','https://floridawildlifecorridor.org/corridor-foundation-hq-mural/','https://www.maranje.com/about','https://thewynwoodwalls.com/artists/ernesto-maranje/']},
  {id:144,a:'Gibbs High School Mural Club',t:'Raising Local Spirits',loc:'800 31st St S',bldg:'St. Petersburg Distillery',lat:27.7618,lng:-82.67518667,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'stpetedistillery',bio:'"It\'s not kids doodling on walls. This is significant, professional work."\n\nThe Gibbs High School Mural Club is run by Brian McAllister, Department Chair of Visual Arts at the Pinellas County Center for the Arts at Gibbs High — the same magnet program that produced Derek Donnelly and Reid Jenkins. McAllister earned his BFA in Illustration from Ringling, freelanced across the US, taught mural painting in Saipan, and came back to St. Pete to start the club. He donates his time. Donations and commissions cover paint, lifts, and materials.\n\nThirty-six students. Two to three murals a year. Thirteen years running. McAllister estimates comparable professional murals would cost $20,000 each. Notable projects include the Royal Theater (six feet by 133 feet, with Boys & Girls Club members), the Alden Suites in St. Pete Beach for the resort\'s 50th anniversary, and the "Fighting Chance" mural at the Morean Arts Center commemorating COVID — designed with Leadership St. Pete Class of 2020 to support the Fighting Chance Fund for affected businesses.\n\nTheir St. Petersburg Distillery mural at 800 31st Street South is "Raising Local Spirits" / "Voyage Under the Sea" — a sponge diver and a giant octopus retrieving rum barrels from a sunken ship. It references the site\'s rum-running history. Senior club president Anderson Bray, a four-year member, painted with the octopus team. His take: "It is really exciting to have it right across the street because I can tell my friends, \'Hey I did this.\'"\n\nMcAllister\'s principle: "Once you\'re in the mural club, you\'re always in the mural club."',desc:'A massive underwater scene spanning the side of the distillery building along 31st Street. A giant orange octopus wraps its tentacles around the left side, its suckers curling around rum barrels from a sunken ship. Sharks and a spotted eagle ray glide through turquoise water around ghostly gray shipwreck masts and rigging. On the right, a vintage deep-sea diver in a copper helmet explores the wreckage. The title "Voyage Under the Sea" and "Just Add Water!" appear in retro lettering. The concept references St. Pete\'s rum-running history — sponge divers recovering barrels from sunken sailboats.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/gibbs-mural-club-distillery.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Brian McAllister: BFA Illustration, Ringling College of Art and Design; Tampa Bay Rays / MLB official featured print series artist; SHINE 2019 (Royal Theater with Boys & Girls Club)',fw:[{name:'jbmstudios.com',url:'https://jbmstudios.com'}],sbio:'"It\'s not kids doodling on walls. This is significant, professional work."\n\nThe Gibbs High School Mural Club is run by Brian McAllister, Department Chair of Visual Arts at the Pinellas County Center for the Arts (PCCA) at Gibbs High School — the same magnet program that produced Derek Donnelly and Reid Jenkins. PCCA was founded in 1984 as Pinellas County\'s arts magnet high school. McAllister earned his BFA in Illustration from Ringling College of Art and Design in Sarasota, freelanced across the US, taught mural painting in Saipan in the Northern Mariana Islands, and came back to St. Petersburg to start the club. He donates his time entirely. Donations and commission revenue cover paint, lifts, and materials.\n\nHis own studio is JBM Studios. He works in oil, acrylic, mixed media, digital painting, and printmaking. He is the official artist for the Tampa Bay Rays / MLB featured print series. His personal mural projects include "The Spirit of St. Pete" at Ulele, work at The Schu, the Gulf Coast Alliance Skyway series, the Tichenor Orthopedic Clinic for Children in Long Beach CA, and the Fred Held South Pasadena Habitat.\n\nThe Mural Club has thirty-six students and produces two to three murals a year. It has been running for thirteen-plus years. McAllister: "Everything we do, everything we receive, goes back into the club." And: "Not only are they learning things they can use in the classroom and in their own artwork — they are learning about community, learning how to work together as a team, building relationships."\n\nNotable club projects: - "Raising Local Spirits" / "Voyage Under the Sea" at the St. Petersburg Distillery (800 31st St S) — a 10-day project depicting a sponge diver and a giant octopus retrieving rum barrels from a sunken ship, referencing the site\'s rum-running history. Partnership with Matt Risley, Director of Brands and Marketing at SPD. - "Great Futures Start Here" — SHINE 2019, collaboration with Boys & Girls Club members. - Royal Theater mural — historic Royal Theater, six feet tall by 133 feet long, with Boys & Girls Club participants. - Alden Suites, St. Pete Beach — commemorating the resort\'s 50th anniversary (2019). - "Fighting Chance" mural at the Morean Arts Center, 719 Central Ave — designed with Leadership St. Pete Class of 2020 to commemorate COVID-19. Features native Florida passion vine (community diversity), masked children — one reading with doves emerging from book pages, another with a camera documenting history — a pelican against the St. Pete skyline, a "+" symbol for healthcare workers, and the MLK quote "What are you doing for others?" Proceeds supported the Fighting Chance Fund for pandemic-affected businesses. - Morean Arts Center mural. - Gulf Coast Arts Alliance mural. - Northeast Little League.\n\nSenior club president Anderson "Andy" Bray, a four-year member, worked on the Distillery octopus team. He said: "Usually when we paint, we paint on a smaller canvas, but this is a huge wall, so we have to use our technique and skills in different ways." McAllister\'s recurring line: "Once you\'re in the mural club, you\'re always in the mural club."\n\nInstagram: @gibbs_mural_club. McAllister\'s site: jbmstudios.com.',src:['https://thegabber.com/gibbs-mural-club-students-tackle-st-pete-walls/','https://stpetersburgdistillery.com/gibbs-high-school-mural-club-unveil-artistic-collaboration-in-st-pete/','https://www.fox13news.com/news/teens-learn-life-lessons-by-painting-larger-than-life-murals-on-downtown-st-pete-buildings','https://jbmstudios.com/','https://jbmstudios.com/public-art/boys-and-girls-club/','https://pccavisualarts.com/faculty','https://ilovetheburg.com/new-fighting-chance-mural-designed-by-gibbs-high-students-arriving-in-st-pete/']},
  {id:184,a:'IBOMS',t:'',loc:'St Pete Youth Farm',bldg:'St Pete Youth Farm',lat:27.753037,lng:-82.650763,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'i.b.o.m.s',bio:'He partnered with the St. Pete Youth Farm in 2021 on a custom PSTA bus wrap. This time they handed him the wall.\n\nAnthropomorphic tomatoes, bananas, broccoli, eggplants, pumpkins, grapes, and sunflowers work the garden beds. A Pac-Man-faced sun beams down from the top. A starfruit character lounges by the pond. The arch reads "STPETE YOUTH FARM Est. 2019."\n\nJabari Jahi Babatu Reed-Diop — IBOMS — is a St. Petersburg native. He attended Douglas Anderson School of the Arts in Jacksonville and the Gibbs High PCCA Magnet Program. The acronym stands for "illegal. Beauty. On. My. Streets." His signature character "Ashi" (a young Black male drawn from his childhood in south St. Pete) anchors most of his other walls — but this one shifted into community-mural mode for the kids.\n\nHe works in 2D illustration, 3D sculpture, and digital design. His solo show "Ego Death" at Mize Gallery sold out in 48 hours. He designed a Tampa Bay Rays Artist Print Series in 2019. His SHINE 2020 wall "Where the Bloom Begins" sits at Grand Central Brewhouse on Central Ave, and he returned to SHINE in 2021 as a Bright Spots artist.',desc:'A vibrant, cartoon-style mural on a cinder block wall at the St Pete Youth Farm. The scene depicts a community garden bustling with anthropomorphic fruit and vegetable characters — tomatoes, bananas, broccoli, eggplants, pumpkins, grapes, sunflowers, and more — all rendered as cartoon people with expressive faces, working in raised garden beds. A smiling sun with a Pac-Man-style face beams down from the top center. An archway reads "STPETE YOUTH FARM Est. 2019." The background features green hills, trees laden with fruit, and a city skyline. A pond with a starfruit character sits at the right. Signed @i.B.O.M.S at upper left.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/iboms-youth-farm.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'He partnered with the St. Pete Youth Farm in 2021 on a custom PSTA bus wrap titled "Together We Grow," designed by IBOMS together with the students of the St. Pete Youth Farm and revealed on October 23, 2021. This commercial commission at the Youth Farm itself extends that ongoing collaboration onto a wall on the property.\n\nJabari Jahi Babatu Reed-Diop — IBOMS — is a St. Petersburg native based in St. Petersburg, Florida. The acronym stands for "illegal. Beauty. On. My. Streets." His artistic acumen comes from personal experience and formal education at Douglas Anderson School of the Arts in Jacksonville and the Gibbs High PCCA Magnet Program in St. Petersburg.\n\nHis signature character "Ashi" — a young Black male — was drawn from his childhood in south St. Pete. The name "ASHI" appears in capital letters below the character\'s left eye on his SHINE 2020 mural at Grand Central Brewhouse. IBOMS uses the "iceberg theory" in his practice: a celebratory surface with deeper narratives running underneath, like a dark river beneath a blissful exterior. His characters are complex and often represent aspects of his psyche as a young Black male, transmuting emotional and physical states of being to mirror the relationships between spirit and god. His work covers themes of self-love, destruction, discovery, omens, and love, and aims to give his community a lens into the experience of an African American male in the Sunshine City.\n\nHe works across multiple mediums: 2D illustration, 3D sculpture, and digital design. At first glance his work may read as simple illustrations or graffiti, but each piece carries a deeper message. Recent work features mushroom-headed characters exploring "Ego Death." He has shown at d-Gallerie and Mize Gallery (also styled MIZE Gallery) in St. Petersburg. His solo show "Ego Death" with Chad Mize at Mize Gallery sold out within 48 hours of its debut, with select pieces remaining for sale at d-Gallerie afterward.\n\nHe designed a Tampa Bay Rays Artist Print Series in 2019. A portrait of IBOMS by muralist and curator Derek Donnelly is painted in the alley behind the 600 Block of Central Avenue in St. Petersburg.\n\nFor SHINE 2020, he painted "Where the Bloom Begins" on the side of the then-new Grand Central Brewhouse — 30 feet high and 36 feet wide on a brilliant yellow background, his signature character Ashi towering with his back to the viewer, looking over his left shoulder. He returned to SHINE in 2021 as a Bright Spots artist, partnering with the Pinellas Suncoast Transit Authority (PSTA) and the St. Pete Youth Farm on the "Together We Grow" PSTA bus wrap.\n\nThis Youth Farm wall is a vibrant cartoon-style scene on a cinder block wall depicting a community garden bustling with anthropomorphic fruit and vegetable characters — tomatoes, bananas, broccoli, eggplants, pumpkins, grapes, sunflowers, and more — all rendered as cartoon people with expressive faces, working in raised garden beds. A smiling sun with a Pac-Man-style face beams from the top center. The archway reads "STPETE YOUTH FARM Est. 2019." Background features green hills, fruit trees, and a city skyline. A pond with a starfruit character sits at the right. Signed @i.B.O.M.S at upper left.\n\nIBOMS was profiled in the Tampa Bay Born Murals video series from I Love the Burg and That\'s So Tampa (October 11, 2021 feature by Andrew Harlan), and is listed as a Creative Pinellas artist. Instagram: @i.b.o.m.s.',fn:['Cartoon fruit and veggie characters working the farm','St Pete Youth Farm established 2019','IBOMS is a St. Pete native — SHINE 2020 artist'],src:['https://creativepinellas.org/directory/jabari-reed-diop/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2020_SHINE_Mural_IBOMS','https://creativepinellas.org/artist/jabari-reed-diop-i-b-o-m-s/','https://ilovetheburg.com/tampa-bay-murals-iboms/','https://www.instagram.com/i.b.o.m.s/']},
  {id:198,a:'James Oleson',t:'',loc:'2993 Tyrone Blvd N',bldg:'Stonehenge Park (Tyrone Blvd overpass on Pinellas Trail)',lat:27.79915,lng:-82.73745,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'olesonart',bio:'James Oleson describes himself as "Sculptor Painter Rebarbarian." This wall is the rebarbarian one — a face the size of the underpass, one teal eye, one purple, crooked teeth across a navy mouth.\n\nOleson Jr. is a sculptor, painter, and installation artist based in St. Petersburg. He works with welded steel and reclaimed industrial materials, "transforms mundane cast-off materials into art, drawing characters from his subconscious." He is self-taught.\n\nHe co-founded the Bloom Art Center at 910 5th Ave N — a downtown St. Pete property where he curates exhibits to complement existing murals on the building. His permanent collections sit at the Crealde School of Art, the Industrial Arts Center, the Lake Wales Art Center Sculpture Garden, and corporate placements at Brooksville City Hall, Ocala City Hall, and Hernando County School System. He took Best in Show at ArtLoud Tampa (2010) and Best of Show at the Kissimmee Sculpture Experience.\n\nOther St. Pete walls of his include a "Goblin" piece at Planet Retro Records and the murals around the Bloom Art Center building.',desc:'A wide concrete wall under the Tyrone Blvd overpass at Stonehenge Park. The full wall is taken up by a single giant face rendered in neo-surrealist style. The skin is a deep maroon-burgundy with highlights in lighter pink and cream. Two heavy-lidded eyes look down at the viewer — the left eye has a bright teal iris ringed with light blue, the right eye has a deep purple iris. Between them, a small bulbous nose with a darker tip and a tiny gold-ring detail. Below, a wide closed-lipped grin curves across nearly the full width of the wall. Big square teeth sit in two rows — upper and lower — some white, some pale green-yellow, some pink-tinted, spaced unevenly across a dark navy interior of the mouth. Two stylized curved red shapes (reading as horns or tusks or curling ribbons) sweep inward from the far left and far right edges, framing the face. The composition is signed "OL3SON" in cyan block-letter graffiti style in the lower-left corner. A small cursive signature appears in the lower-right corner; the lettering cannot be reliably identified from photo alone.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/unknown/james-oleson-stonehenge-park.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'Best in Show, ArtLoud Tampa (2010); Best of Show, Kissimmee Sculpture Experience',fw:[{name:'artandsculptures.com',url:'https://artandsculptures.com'},{name:'Instagram @olesonart',url:'https://www.instagram.com/olesonart/'}],sbio:'A giant neo-surrealist face fills a low concrete wall under an overpass: heavy-lidded eyes — one with a teal iris, one with a purple iris — a bulbous nose, and a wide grin of crooked square teeth across a dark navy mouth interior. Two curved red horn/tusk shapes sweep inward from either edge. Signed "OL3SON" in cyan in the lower-left. There is a smaller cursive signature in the lower-right that has not been verified as a co-artist or as Oleson\'s own secondary mark. Year not documented in public sources. Painted at Stonehenge Park (under the Tyrone Blvd overpass on the Pinellas Trail, north of Tyrone Square Mall) at 2993 Tyrone Blvd N, St. Petersburg, Florida.\n\nJames Oleson Jr. is a multidisciplinary artist based in St. Petersburg, Florida, working in sculpture, painting, and installation. He is self-taught. His practice is described in his St. Pete Arts Alliance directory listing as work that "transforms mundane cast-off materials into art, drawing characters from his subconscious," and he works extensively with welded steel and reclaimed/industrial materials. His own Instagram bio reads "SCULPTOR PAINTER REBARBARIAN." His Morean Arts Center profile lists a Brooksville mailing P.O. box (P.O. Box 1293, Brooksville 34605) for sales, but his studio operation is in St. Petersburg.\n\nHe is the co-founder of the Bloom Art Center, located at 910 5th Avenue North in downtown St. Petersburg. He is described in St. Pete press coverage as a "renowned sculptor and muralist" who curates exhibits at the Bloom property to complement existing murals around the building.\n\nHis awards include Best in Show at ArtLoud Tampa (2010) and Best of Show at the Kissimmee Sculpture Experience. His sculpture work sits in permanent collections at the Crealde School of Art, the Industrial Arts Center, the Lake Wales Art Center Sculpture Garden, and corporate / civic placements at Brooksville City Hall, Ocala City Hall, and the Hernando County School System.\n\nOther documented St. Petersburg walls include a piece titled "Goblin" at Planet Retro Records, an untitled "blue building / green eyes" wall (a Pinterest pin dates that piece 9/15 — September 2015 — but it is not the Stonehenge Park wall), and the murals on the Bloom Art Center building at 910 5th Ave N.\n\nInstagram: @olesonart. Email: olesonart@gmail.com (per Instagram bio). Website: artandsculptures.com (per Morean Arts Center).',src:['https://stpeteartsalliance.org/arts-culture-directory/details?itemid=85','https://moreanartscenter.org/james-oleson/','https://www.instagram.com/olesonart/','http://crowsneststpete.com/2018/04/08/perennial-bloom-captures-essence-of-st-petersburgs-art-scene/','https://www.wescover.com/p/street-murals-by-james-oleson-at-planet-retro-records--Pr1lwjl5Xm','https://www.linkedin.com/in/james-oleson-76bb166a/','https://www.yelp.com/biz/stonehenge-park-st-petersburg']},
  {id:154,a:'Justin Bass',t:'',loc:'1650 Central Ave',bldg:'Tru',lat:27.770672,lng:-82.655992,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'dropthelove',bio:'On January 1, 1914, a Benoist flying boat lifted off the St. Pete waterfront, climbed to 50 feet, and crossed Tampa Bay in 23 minutes. First scheduled airline flight in history. Tony Jannus at the controls. The mayor — Abram Pheil — had paid $400 at auction for the seat next to him.\n\nJustin Bass painted the sequel. A flamingo glides across the sky carrying St. Pete itself, a figure perched on its back, a boombox along for the ride. A flamingo flies escort. Same route the Benoist took, retold in pastel.\n\nBass works under the name DropTheLove — artist, designer, restoration specialist out of South Florida. He\'s collaborated with Miami scenic painter Ryan Zook on projects around the region. Beyond that the trail goes quiet: an Instagram, a LinkedIn, no interviews, no press packet. The wall does the talking.\n\nIt wraps the Tru by Hilton parking garage on Central Avenue, blocks from the bay the Benoist crossed.',desc:'A wide mural along a parking garage wall on a teal-green background. A surreal aviation scene features a pink flamingo in flight on the left, a figure riding atop a giant flamingo carrying a boombox through the sky at center, and a winged flamingo diving from the upper right. Puffy blue-gray clouds and small triangular shapes fill the sky. The style is playful and illustrative with a pastel palette of teal, pink, lavender, and gray.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/justin-bass-tru.jpeg',from:'',aud:'',insp:'The world\'s first scheduled commercial airline flight took off from the St. Petersburg waterfront on January 1, 1914 — a Benoist XIV flying boat piloted by Tony Jannus, carrying former mayor Abram C. Pheil to Tampa in 23 minutes. Pheil paid $400 at auction for the seat. Bass\'s mural retells that flight in surreal pastel: the flamingo stands in for the Benoist, the rider for Mayor Pheil, and St. Pete itself is along for the ride. The boombox is the 112-year update.',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'On January 1, 1914, the world\'s first scheduled commercial airline flight took off from the St. Petersburg Municipal Pier. The aircraft was a Benoist XIV flying boat — a pontoon-equipped biplane carrying one pilot and one passenger. The pilot was Tony Jannus. The passenger was former St. Petersburg mayor Abram C. Pheil, who had won the seat at auction for $400. They climbed to 50 feet, crossed Tampa Bay in 23 minutes, and landed near the mouth of the Hillsborough River. A crowd of over 3,000 lined the St. Pete waterfront to watch them go. The St. Petersburg–Tampa Airboat Line, conceived by Florida entrepreneur Percival Fansler in partnership with aircraft manufacturer Thomas Benoist, ran for nearly four months and carried 1,205 passengers before shutting down. It is the origin point of commercial aviation.\n\nJustin Bass turned that flight into a mural on Central Avenue 112 years later. In his version a flamingo stands in for the Benoist, a rider for Mayor Pheil, and the city of St. Petersburg itself is the cargo. A pink flamingo flies escort. A second winged flamingo dives in from the upper right. Puffy blue-gray clouds and small triangular shapes fill the teal-green sky. The flamingo is the 112-year update — same route, different vibe.\n\nBass works as an artist, designer, and restoration specialist under the brand DropTheLove. His LinkedIn title is "Artist / Designer / Restorations at DropTheLove." He has collaborated with Miami-based scenic artist and designer Ryan Zook (@zookart, ryanzookart.com) — the pair were described as "the dynamic duo" by Project In The Paint. He also goes by "Jb Jb" on Instagram and "jmovement" on Facebook and Flickr.\n\nThe mural wraps the parking garage wall at the Tru by Hilton on Central Avenue in St. Petersburg. It is an exterior piece, distinct from the hotel\'s interior lobby art by Mike Traina. Two blocks from the waterfront where the Benoist took off in 1914.\n\nInstagram: @dropthelove. TikTok: @justinbass511.',src:['https://www.instagram.com/dropthelove/','https://www.linkedin.com/in/justin-bass-a8814410a','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tony_Jannus','https://foawa.org/the-first-commercial-flight/','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benoist_XIV']},
  {id:119,a:'Matt Kress',t:'',loc:'1st St N & Dr. MLK Jr St N',bldg:'Animal Hospital',lat:27.772306,lng:-82.646803,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'mattkressart',bio:'Air traffic controllers call his airplane "Skittles." He spray-painted a 1967 Mooney in forty-five colors — it headlined Sun \'n Fun and Oshkosh and won Best Unique Paint Scheme.\n\nMatt Kress grew up in Minerva, Ohio, studied painting and graphic design at Kent State, then spent nearly sixteen years in a desk job because he was afraid art wasn\'t a real career. In 2014 he visited St. Petersburg on a business trip, found a town alive with murals, and moved. His friend Christian McAlhaney of the band Anberlin told him: "I love the art you\'re doing, but don\'t forget you\'re nothing right now. Get your work out there and do some murals."\n\nHe applied to SHINE 2018 and got his first mural — a pirate ship sponsored by the Tampa Bay Buccaneers. It paid a thousand dollars for two weeks of work. After that the phone started ringing. He has since painted over thirty murals including the seventy-foot sea turtle at Channel Club in Tampa — the tallest mural in the city at the time.',desc:'A long wall covered with oversized portraits of dogs and cats in a colorful geometric pop-art style. A Boston terrier, a large dark dog, and a tabby cat with prominent whiskers are painted in bold, faceted color blocks — blues, oranges, greens, reds — set against a kaleidoscopic background of intersecting diamond patterns. The style is vibrant and eye-catching from the parking lot.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/unknown-1503.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2018 festival artist; Best Unique Paint Scheme at Sun \'n Fun (Mooney Anomaly); tallest mural in Tampa (Channel Club, 2022)',fw:[{name:'mattkress.art',url:'https://mattkress.art'}],sbio:'Air traffic controllers call his airplane "Skittles." He spray-painted a 1967 Mooney in forty-five colors — it headlined Sun \'n Fun and Oshkosh and won Best Unique Paint Scheme.\n\nMatt Kress was born and raised in Minerva, Ohio (1994 Minerva High School graduate). He studied art and graphic design at Kent State University, originally enrolling as an architecture student before switching to painting. Inspired by Klimt, Chuck Close, Dali, Picasso, Warhol, and Mondrian. He spent nearly sixteen years in business (ten years at Fastenal in sales, plus process engineering) before moving to St. Petersburg in 2014 and going full-time as an artist at age 43 in 2018.\n\nHis friend Christian McAlhaney (lead guitarist of Anberlin) encouraged him to pursue murals and referenced the SHINE festival. His first mural was "The Voyage" for SHINE 2018 — a 25-foot pirate ship sponsored by the Tampa Bay Buccaneers at 128 20th St S. It paid $1,000 for two weeks. He has since painted 30-plus murals nationwide including the 70-foot sea turtle "Gaspar\'s Direction" at Channel Club Apartments in Tampa (tallest mural in Tampa at the time, 2022), a Roman goddess Minerva mural in his hometown, and "PiCOWso" — a Picasso-inspired 14-foot fiberglass cow for Minerva Dairy (possibly the world\'s largest painted art cow).\n\n"The Mooney Anomaly" is a 1967 Mooney M20F airplane (N3205F) owned by pilot Liam Hawkins, based at Albert Whitted Airport in downtown St. Pete. Kress spray-painted it in 40-45 Montana Colors graffiti cans with an asymmetrical design — no two parts alike. It won Best Unique Paint Scheme at Sun \'n Fun Aerospace Expo in Lakeland and appeared at EAA AirVenture Oshkosh (world\'s largest airshow, 677,000 attendees). Air traffic controllers nicknamed it "Skittles." Featured in AOPA, Flying Magazine, General Aviation News, and Axios Tampa Bay.\n\nHe exhibits at Red Dot Miami (Art Basel) and Art Santa Fe. Original paintings range $5,000-$20,000. Revenue is approximately 75% murals, 25% originals. He cycles 100 miles weekly on the Pinellas Trail and mentors young artists in Tampa Bay. His motto: "The eye is the lamp of the body."\n\nInstagram: @mattkressart. Website: mattkress.art.',fn:['Air traffic controllers call his airplane \'Skittles\'','Spray-paints a Beechcraft Bonanza in geometric color','Aviation + art — Mooney Anomaly project'],src:['https://mattkress.art/','https://eyesonthestreets.blog/2022/04/23/at-1st-street-north-mlk/','https://www.aopa.org/news-and-media/all-news/2023/february/22/the-mooney-anomaly','https://artbusinessnews.com/2024/07/meet-the-artist-matt-kress-the-kress-collection-gallery/','https://blog.artstorefronts.com/artist-matt-kress/','https://www.axios.com/local/tampa-bay/2024/06/25/mooney-anomaly-plane-mural-st-petersburg']},
  {id:107,a:'Melanie Posner',t:'',loc:'421 4th Ave N',bldg:'Hollander Hotel',lat:27.776736,lng:-82.638808,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'therealmelpoz',bio:'She painted the woman in the red headscarf on the back of the Hollander Hotel in under five days, then went straight to Walt Disney World to paint scenic art for Star Wars: Galaxy\'s Edge.\n\nMelanie Posner was born around 1993 in a small town outside Philadelphia. She moved to Tampa in 2013 and earned her BFA in Painting from the University of Tampa in 2016. Her work is photorealistic portraits of women — oversized, expressive eyes set against rainbow palettes, psychedelic paisley backgrounds, and florals. "Painting females and exploring how complex they are kind of helped me figure out myself, and who I was."\n\nShe was a cast member on Amazon Prime\'s docuseries Tampa Baes (2021), a show about a Tampa Bay lesbian friend group. Amazon commissioned her to paint a mural on a bus that toured the city promoting the show. Her painting "Passion" appears on screen.\n\nIn 2024 she painted a forty-foot mural on the LGBT Center of Los Angeles for TikTok\'s "Create the Future" campaign. She is now based in Los Angeles.\n\nHer SHINE 2018 mural "New Beginnings" is a twenty-three by sixty-five foot photorealistic woman lying on her side, one eye peeking through her fingers, on 7th Terrace South.',desc:'An 18-foot-tall commercial mural on the back entrance of the Hollander Hotel near the swimming pool and tiki bar. A confident woman wearing a red head scarf and dark sunglasses holds a tall glass of beer, rendered in photorealistic style. The background features a bold psychedelic paisley pattern in mint green, yellow, and pink with black and white teardrop motifs. Signed "Melanie Posner" in the upper right, with "@muralcerveza" and "@therealmelpoz" at the bottom. Commissioned by New Belgium Brewing to promote their Mural Agua Fresca Cerveza summer beer.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/melanie-posner-hollander.jpeg',from:'Los Angeles, CA',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE 2018 Open Call artist; Walt Disney World Star Wars: Galaxy\'s Edge scenic artist; Tampa Baes (Amazon Prime, 2021) cast member; TikTok LA Pride campaign (2024)',fw:[{name:'melanieposner.com',url:'https://www.melanieposner.com'}],sbio:'She painted the woman in the red headscarf on the back of the Hollander Hotel in under five days, then went straight to Walt Disney World to paint scenic art for Star Wars: Galaxy\'s Edge.\n\nMelanie "Mel" Posner was born around 1993 in a small town just outside Philadelphia. She moved to Tampa in 2013 and earned her BFA in Painting from the University of Tampa in 2016, where she also studied art therapy. She was previously based in Gulfport, Florida, and is now in Los Angeles.\n\nHer style is photorealistic portraits of women — oversized, expressive eyes, vibrant rainbow color palettes, psychedelic paisley backgrounds, floral elements. She blends lifelike detail with touches of dreamy surrealism. She works in oil on canvas, collage, prints, and large-scale murals. She sources her subjects from Pinterest, Google, and social media, looking for eyes, story, and confidence. "Working large is just really exciting," she says. "I feel like I\'m a little bit more free doing murals." Her stated philosophy: paint strong, confident, independent women to be inspired by them.\n\nFor SHINE 2018, she was one of five local "Open Call" artists. Her mural "New Beginnings" at 4139 7th Terrace South is twenty-three feet high by sixty-five feet long — a photorealistic woman lying horizontally on her side, her hand cupping the right side of her face, one eye peeking through her fingers. She completed it in October 2018.\n\nHer commercial clients include Walt Disney World (scenic artwork for Star Wars: Galaxy\'s Edge at Hollywood Studios, Orlando), New Belgium Brewing (the eighteen-foot Hollander Hotel mural of a woman in a red headscarf with a glass of Mural Agua Fresca Cerveza, completed in under five days), Amazon Prime Video (a mural on a promotional bus for Tampa Baes), and TikTok. For TikTok\'s 2024 "Create the Future" campaign, she painted a forty-foot mural on the LGBT Center of Los Angeles at 8745 Santa Monica Blvd in West Hollywood.\n\nShe was a cast member on all eight episodes of Tampa Baes (Amazon Prime Video, 2021), a docuseries about a Tampa Bay lesbian friend group produced by 3 Ball Productions and filmed over roughly ten weeks starting April 2021. She described herself as "neutral" and avoiding drama on the show. "My anxiety during filming was up to the roof, my mental health totally depleted. It was very stressful and I will say, I love how the show came out." Her oil painting "Passion" (also called "Summer and Marissa") appears on screen. She worked for years as a bartender at The Honey Pot, a landmark LGBTQ+ club in Ybor City, Tampa.\n\nShe has exhibited at Enigma gallery in downtown St. Petersburg. Instagram: @therealmelpoz. Website: melanieposner.com.',src:['https://stpetecatalyst.com/melanie-posner/','https://www.melanieposner.com/','https://www.melanieposner.com/murals-portfolio/hollander-hotel','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2018_SHINE_Mural_Melanie_Posner','https://outcoast.com/get-to-know-mel-posner-from-tampa-baes/','http://watermarkoutnews.com/2021/12/02/tampa-baes-star-melanie-posner-talks-filming-season-one/','https://stpetecatalyst.com/amazon-picks-up-docuseries-on-it-lesbians-in-tampa-bay/','https://findmasa.com/artist/melanie-posner','https://graffiti-artist.net/business-murals/lgbtq_artist/','https://www.imdb.com/name/nm12771071/','https://www.artsy.net/artist/melanie-posner','https://crowsneststpete.com/2018/10/15/mural-festival-shines-down-on-st-petersburg/','https://streetartunitedstates.com/highlights-from-the-2018-shine-mural-festival-in-st-petersburg-florida/']},
  {id:185,a:'Melanie Posner',t:'',loc:'St. Petersburg',bldg:'',lat:27.757783,lng:-82.638612,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'therealmelpoz',bio:'Two hands — one lighter, one darker — form a heart on this wall. Concentric rainbow hearts radiate inward toward a small awareness ribbon. Colorful silhouettes of diverse people flank both sides.\n\nThis is a community/awareness piece — graphic silhouettes and flat color rather than Posner\'s usual photorealism — celebrating connection, diversity, and care.\n\nMelanie Posner was born around 1993 outside Philadelphia, moved to Tampa in 2013, and earned her BFA in Painting from the University of Tampa in 2016. She is now based in Los Angeles. Her usual signature is photorealistic portraits of women — oversized expressive eyes, vibrant rainbow palettes, psychedelic paisley backgrounds, florals.\n\nHer commercial commissions include scenic art for Walt Disney World\'s Star Wars: Galaxy\'s Edge, the eighteen-foot Hollander Hotel mural for New Belgium Brewing (a woman in a red headscarf, completed in under five days), and a forty-foot wall on the LGBT Center of Los Angeles for TikTok\'s 2024 "Create the Future" campaign. She was a cast member on Amazon Prime\'s docuseries Tampa Baes (2021).\n\nOther St. Pete walls: the Mint House on 11th St N (a woman in giant reflective sunglasses with the waterfront skyline in the lenses), the Iryna Zarutska memorial on Central Ave (gold-blonde hair, pink hibiscus, mint-to-gold), and her SHINE 2018 wall "New Beginnings" — a 23-by-65-foot photorealistic woman lying on her side — at 4139 7th Terrace South.',desc:'Two hands — one lighter-skinned, one darker-skinned — form a heart shape at the center of a white cinder block wall. Inside the heart, concentric rainbow-colored hearts radiate inward toward a small awareness ribbon at the center. Flanking the hands on both sides are overlapping silhouettes of people in profile — men and women with different hairstyles and builds — rendered in bold, flat colors: yellow, green, pink, magenta, blue, teal, orange, and red. The silhouettes layer and overlap transparently, creating rich color intersections. Signed @therealmelipoz at lower right.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/melanie-posner-heart-hands.jpeg',from:'Los Angeles, CA',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'Two hands — one lighter, one darker — form a heart on this wall. Concentric rainbow hearts radiate inward toward a small awareness ribbon. Colorful silhouettes of diverse people flank both sides. This is a community/awareness piece — graphic silhouettes and flat color rather than Posner\'s usual photorealism — celebrating connection, diversity, and care.\n\nMelanie "Mel" Posner was born around 1993 in a small town just outside Philadelphia. She moved to Tampa in 2013 and earned her BFA in Painting from the University of Tampa in 2016, where she also studied art therapy. She was previously based in Gulfport, Florida, and is now in Los Angeles. Her usual style is photorealistic portraits of women — oversized expressive eyes, vibrant rainbow color palettes, psychedelic paisley backgrounds, floral elements. She blends lifelike detail with touches of dreamy surrealism. She works in oil on canvas, collage, prints, and large-scale murals. She sources her subjects from Pinterest, Google, and social media, looking for eyes, story, and confidence. "Working large is just really exciting," she says. "I feel like I\'m a little bit more free doing murals." Her stated philosophy: paint strong, confident, independent women to be inspired by them.\n\nCommercial clients include Walt Disney World (scenic artwork for Star Wars: Galaxy\'s Edge at Hollywood Studios, Orlando), New Belgium Brewing (the eighteen-foot Hollander Hotel mural of a woman in a red headscarf with a glass of Mural Agua Fresca Cerveza, completed in under five days), Amazon Prime Video (a mural on a promotional bus for Tampa Baes), and TikTok. For TikTok\'s 2024 "Create the Future" campaign, she painted a forty-foot mural on the LGBT Center of Los Angeles at 8745 Santa Monica Blvd in West Hollywood.\n\nShe was a cast member on all eight episodes of Tampa Baes (Amazon Prime Video, 2021), a docuseries about a Tampa Bay lesbian friend group produced by 3 Ball Productions and filmed over roughly ten weeks starting April 2021. Her oil painting "Passion" (also called "Summer and Marissa") appears on screen. She worked for years as a bartender at The Honey Pot, a landmark LGBTQ+ club in Ybor City, Tampa.\n\nOther St. Petersburg walls include: the Mint House at 77 11th St N (a 2023 commercial commission — a woman in giant reflective sunglasses with the waterfront skyline reflected in the lenses, polka dots, palm trees), the Iryna Zarutska memorial mural on the 2200 block of Central Ave (a 2026 portrait of the 23-year-old Ukrainian refugee killed on the Charlotte light rail in August 2025; mint-to-gold background, pink hibiscus), and her SHINE 2018 Open Call mural "New Beginnings" at 4139 7th Terrace South — twenty-three feet high by sixty-five feet long, a photorealistic woman lying horizontally on her side, her hand cupping the right side of her face, one eye peeking through her fingers. She has exhibited at Enigma gallery in downtown St. Petersburg.\n\nInstagram: @therealmelpoz. Website: melanieposner.com. Signed @therealmelipoz at lower right of this wall.',fn:['Two hands forming a heart with rainbow concentric hearts inside','Colorful silhouettes of diverse people flanking the center','Awareness ribbon at the center of the heart'],src:['https://stpetecatalyst.com/melanie-posner/','https://www.melanieposner.com/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/shine?alias=/2018_SHINE_Mural_Melanie_Posner','https://outcoast.com/get-to-know-mel-posner-from-tampa-baes/','https://www.instagram.com/therealmelpoz/']},
  {id:164,a:'Morgan Harper Nichols',t:'',loc:'1901 Central Ave',bldg:'Art Jewelry Boutique',lat:27.770912,lng:-82.659295,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'morganharpernichols',bio:'She started a project to write a million poems to strangers in her lifetime. She has two million followers on Instagram.\n\nMorgan Harper Nichols is a poet, author, and interdisciplinary media artist born in Los Angeles in 1990, raised in Georgia, now based in Athens where she is pursuing a PhD in Communication Studies at the University of Georgia. She earned a BFA in English from Point University and an MFA in Interdisciplinary Media Arts from Lindenwood University. In 2017 she began writing personalized poems for strangers who submit their stories through her website — brief letters of encouragement paired with visual art, sent free and shared anonymously.\n\nShe has published four books with Zondervan/HarperCollins, including the Wall Street Journal bestseller "All Along You Were Blooming." Clients include Google, Apple, Adobe, Starbucks, COACH, and Hallmark. She was diagnosed with autism at thirty-one.',desc:'An octopus mural wrapping around the corner of a small commercial building (Art Jewelry Boutique). The octopus tentacles are rendered in warm earth tones — browns, rusts, and tans — studded with colorful concentric circles in teal, pink, purple, yellow, and brown, like suction cups reimagined as abstract eyeballs. A parchment scroll at center contains a handwritten poem about inner beauty and strength, signed "Morgan Harper Nichols." The circles continue onto the sidewalk in front of the building. "Affluent Creatives" is painted at the base. The background is a muted sage green.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/morgan-harper-nichols.jpeg',from:'',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'She started a project to write a million poems to strangers in her lifetime. She has two million followers on Instagram.\n\nMorgan Harper Nichols (born Morgan Novelate Harper, February 4, 1990, Los Angeles) is a poet, author, and interdisciplinary media artist. Her family moved to Georgia in 1993 where her parents started Kingdom City Church in Stone Mountain. Her younger sister is Christian music artist Jamie Grace. She was homeschooled growing up. She began attending Point University at sixteen and earned a BA in English. She earned an MFA in Interdisciplinary Media Arts from Lindenwood University (2024) and is pursuing a PhD in Communication Studies at the University of Georgia (began August 2024).\n\nIn 2017 she began writing personalized poems for strangers — people submit stories via her website and she writes brief letters of encouragement paired with visual art, sent free and shared anonymously on social media. She creates all words and art on an iPad Pro. Her approach is collage-based, drawing from worldbuilding, assemblage, and remixing.\n\nBooks include "Storyteller: 100 Poem Letters" (2017, self-published), "All Along You Were Blooming" (2020, Zondervan — Wall Street Journal bestseller), "How Far You Have Come" (2021, Zondervan), "Peace Is a Practice" (2021, Zondervan), and "You Are Only Just Beginning" (2023, Zondervan). She signed a two-book contract with Zondervan in June 2020.\n\nClients include Google, Meta, Apple, Adobe, Starbucks, KIND Snacks, COACH, Paul Mitchell, Skillshare, Hallmark, Instagram, Aerie, and Vogue Singapore. Products found at Target, Barnes & Noble, and Anthropologie. She is Vice President of the Board of Directors for TWLOHA (To Write Love on Her Arms). She founded Garden24, an e-commerce platform and physical shop in Phoenix. She launched the "Storyteller by MHN" app (2019) and "The Morgan Harper Nichols Show" podcast (2019).\n\nShe was diagnosed with autism at thirty-one along with ADHD and sensory processing disorder. She married Patrick Kekoa Nichols on October 17, 2010. They have a son, Jacob.\n\nInstagram: @morganharpernichols (approximately 2 million followers). Website: morganharpernichols.com. Shop: garden24.co.',src:['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morgan_Harper_Nichols','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jamie_Grace','https://twloha.com/board/','https://morganharpernichols.com/bio','https://comm.uga.edu/directory/people/morgan-harper-nichols','https://www.lindenwood.edu/arts-and-humanities/art-history-ai-and-visual-culture/interdisciplinary-media-arts-mfa/','https://parade.com/1235103/leighweingus/morgan-harper-nichols-autism/','https://www.frontgatemedia.com/artist-and-poet-morgan-harper-nichols-signs-two-book-contract-with-zondervan/','https://www.publishersweekly.com/pw/by-topic/industry-news/religion/article/83552-religion-book-deals-june-10-2020.html','https://garden24.co/']},
  {id:162,a:'R2romero',t:'City of STPETERSRUN',loc:'933 Burlington Ave N',bldg:'',lat:27.773947,lng:-82.647051,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'r2romero',bio:'He paints portraits on actual cotton-paper banknotes using acrylic paint. He has been doing it since 2015.\n\nArturo Romero — R2ROMERO — is a Mexican American artist originally from Santa Ana, California, now based in St. Petersburg. He works across murals, canvases, metal signs, and comic books, mixing fine art, cartoons, and nature. His signature project "$1000 DOLLAR2" turns real banknotes into miniature paintings — subjects range from the Sumerians to Sid Vicious and Fear and Loathing in Las Vegas.\n\nHe has painted in England, Spain, France, the Netherlands, Mexico, and across the US. In St. Petersburg he participated in the 2026 Paint It Pink project, transforming the former Ventnor Apartments with twenty-plus local artists.',desc:'A surreal composition on a light blue wall. A headless figure stands at center, its body covered in hand-drawn symbols — an all-seeing eye, a heart, palm trees, "LORD ENKI" across the torso, and the number "11 33." The figure wears red shorts and sneakers and holds a globe with a heart over its chest. Scattered around the scene: a gramophone playing a record labeled "RETRO," a green crocodile, a life buoy reading "City of STPETERSRUN," a pelican, a gloved hand with eye symbols, a "Private Property" sign, and a boat. Signed "@romero" in the upper left. The style is folk-art surrealist with a hand-drawn, tattoo-flash quality.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/romero-stpetersrun.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'He paints portraits on actual cotton-paper banknotes using acrylic paint. He has been doing it since 2015.\n\nArturo Romero — R2ROMERO — is a Mexican American artist born and raised in Santa Ana, California, now based in Saint Petersburg, Florida. He works in murals, canvases, metal signs, comic books, and his signature banknote art project "$1000 DOLLAR2" — painting portraits and scenes on actual cotton-paper banknotes using acrylic paint since 2015. His style blends fine art, cartoons, and nature with mystical and pop culture themes. Known banknote subjects include The Sumerians (2023), Chalino Sanchez (2022), Sid Vicious (2022), Fear and Loathing in Las Vegas (2021), and Hunter S. Thompson & Oscar Zeta Acosta (2021).\n\nHe has worked in England, Spain, France, the Netherlands, Mexico, and the United States. Known murals include "Miss Lure" at Osage Alley & 24th St in San Francisco (with Goop Massta, 2016). In St. Petersburg he participated in the 2026 Paint It Pink project on the former Ventnor Apartments. He is listed in the Creative Pinellas artist directory.\n\n"City of STPETERSRUN" is a surreal folk-art mural near Planet Retro Records featuring a headless figure covered in symbols including "LORD ENKI" and "11 33," a gramophone, a crocodile, a life buoy, and a pelican — themes consistent with his Sumerian and mystical body of work.\n\nInstagram: @r2romero. Website: r2romero.com.',src:['https://creativepinellas.org/artist/arturo-romero-r2romero/','https://banknoteartconcept.com/gallery/r2romero/','https://www.streetartsf.com/tag/r2romero/','https://www.fox13news.com/news/st-petersburg-showcases-murals-paint-pink-project-transforming-aging-buildings-artwork','https://stpeterising.com/home/downtown-st-pete-apartments-painted-pink-before-demolition-makes-way-for-29-story-condo-tower','https://hoodline.com/2026/03/downtown-st-pete-s-pink-block-party-sends-doomed-ventnor-packing','https://r2romero.com','https://www.instagram.com/r2romero/']},
  {id:190,a:'Up & Over',t:'',loc:'2214 5th Ave S',bldg:'',lat:27.7723,lng:-82.6634,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'upandovermurals',bio:'Up & Over is a St. Petersburg mural collective that includes the Vitale Brothers. Their work ranges from photorealistic portraits to bold graphic compositions mixing classical imagery with contemporary street art techniques.',desc:'A striking two-story mural on a commercial building. A massive classical sculpture face — reminiscent of Michelangelo\'s Moses or a Greek god — rendered in photorealistic grayscale dominates the upper portion, looking downward with an intense expression and flowing beard. One enormous hand reaches forward gripping a small branch with a figure. The composition is split between the photorealistic figure and bold pop-art graphic elements — halftone dot patterns in black, white, and blue, geometric orange and gold shapes, swirling wave patterns, and tiger-stripe accents. Signed "Vitale Broz" at lower right. Address 2214 visible on the building.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/vitale-bros-classical-face.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null},
  {id:201,a:'Various / Unknown',t:'Stonehenge Park Pillars',loc:'2993 Tyrone Blvd N',bldg:'Stonehenge Park (Tyrone Blvd overpass on Pinellas Trail)',lat:27.79911,lng:-82.7374,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'',bio:'Stonehenge Park is not on any St. Pete park map. It\'s an underpass at Tyrone Blvd that 22 artists turned into a kaleidoscope — 21 painted pillars, 10 walls, and a name that took.\n\nThe site sits at 2993 Tyrone Blvd N, directly on the Pinellas Trail just north of Tyrone Square Mall. Concrete bridge columns rise out of grass and dirt mounds; the crossbeams above the road deck are striped in safety-orange. Trail riders coast through the shade.\n\nFrogs, elephants, mermaids, photoreal portraits, neo-surrealist faces, jungle scenes, palm silhouettes. Different styles, different years, different hands. It reads less like a curated gallery and more like a community paint day that kept going.\n\nNo city, county, or Creative Pinellas record documents who organized it, what year it started, or who paid for the paint. Two artists are publicly attributed: James Oleson (the maroon grinning face — Mural Quest #198) and Zulu Painter (three palm silhouettes — #199). Everyone else is unidentified.\n\nIf you painted one of these walls or know who did, email support@muralquest.app — we\'ll credit you and write a real bio.',desc:'A wide view from the south side of Stonehenge Park looking north through the Tyrone Blvd underpass on the Pinellas Trail. The composition is dominated by tall concrete bridge pillars supporting the road deck above. Six painted pillars are visible in this frame. The leftmost pillar shows a stylized blue frog or fish-like creature with big black eyes against a teal-and-green background; a small pink figure with raised arms is painted on the lower band of the same pillar. The next pillar over shows a gray elephant in a crown rendered in a comic-book style, painted on a magenta-purple background. Behind it, the road deck\'s crossbeams are painted in thick bands of safety-orange alternating with bare gray concrete. In the middle distance, a pillar is painted with a green grimacing face that has a single large open eye and a wide closed mouth. To its right, another pillar is painted bright blue and shows a long-stemmed orange-and-magenta rose with a small ladybug on the stem and water-bubble shapes underneath. Further back, a tall narrow column is painted red-orange and dark blue with a vertical zigzag pattern. Foreground grass and dirt mounds inside the underpass are visible. On the far right, a wider concrete pillar shows a red-haired mermaid with a green tail sitting on a small rock against a bright bubbly blue ocean background. Two large palm trees and pale-green palmettos frame the right side of the frame. Bare grass, dirt, and a paved path run across the foreground.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/unknown/stonehenge-park-pillars.jpeg',from:'',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:null,sbio:'Stonehenge Park is not on any St. Pete park map. It\'s an underpass at Tyrone Blvd that 22 artists turned into a kaleidoscope — 21 painted pillars, 10 walls, and a name that took.\n\nThe site sits at 2993 Tyrone Blvd N in St. Petersburg, Florida, directly on the Pinellas Trail just north of Tyrone Square Mall. Concrete bridge columns rise out of grass and dirt mounds. The crossbeams above the road deck are striped in safety-orange. Benches, lighting, and a sidewalk connect the trail through the underpass. Trail riders coast through the shade.\n\nThe painted walls and pillars run the gamut: frogs, elephants, mermaids, photoreal portraits, neo-surrealist faces, jungle scenes, palm silhouettes. Different styles, different years, different hands. It reads less like a curated gallery and more like a community paint day that kept going.\n\nNo city, county, or Creative Pinellas record documents who organized the site, what year it started, or who funded the paint. Stonehenge Park does not appear on the City of St. Petersburg\'s parks list and is not part of the Creative Pinellas "Pinellas Trail ArtWorks" program (those four commissions are in Palm Harbor and Tarpon Springs). It appears to be an informally named site rather than a chartered city park. The 22-artists / 21-pillars / 10-walls count comes from a Yelp reviewer summary and is treated as approximate.\n\nTwo artists are publicly attributed for specific walls: James Oleson — the maroon neo-surrealist grinning face wall, signed "OL3SON" (Mural Quest #198) — and Zulu Painter — the three palm silhouettes with the green Old-English Stonehenge Park sign, signed "zulupainter" (#199). The jungle-themed entry-sign wall (#200) and the painted pillar cluster (#201) are catalogued with artists unknown.\n\nIf you painted any of these walls or know who did, email support@muralquest.app and we will credit you and write a proper bio.',src:['https://www.yelp.com/biz/stonehenge-park-st-petersburg','https://foursquare.com/v/stonehenge-park/51bc88dd498e8d6f75faf8df','https://creativepinellas.org/opportunity/pinellas-trail-artworks/','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_parks_in_St._Petersburg,_Florida']},
  {id:156,a:'Vitale Bros',t:'Ride \'em Cowboy',loc:'2451 Central Ave',bldg:'Ride \'em Cowboy bar',lat:27.771234,lng:-82.666803,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'vitalebros',bio:'Their first mural came from a sketch on a paper napkin, handed to a restaurant owner. That was 1992.\n\nJohnny Vitale and his brother Paul started Vitale Bros in Tampa Bay hand-lettering signs and painting storefront windows. Their parents are artists. So were their grandparents. No formal training — just family tradition and thirty-plus years of practice. Their late aunt Nancy, who had Down syndrome and attended Parc Center for Disabilities, turned out to be an artist too, discovered in her final decade.\n\nIn the late 1990s they opened a warehouse studio that hosted group art, music, and fashion shows that helped birth St. Pete\'s emerging art scene. They collaborated on set design for Harmony Korine\'s Spring Breakers. They have painted for the Tampa Bay Rays, the Buccaneers, L\'Oreal, and dozens of local businesses. They call themselves the "mural police" — restoring damaged walls around the city for free.\n\nIn 2023, Johnny launched Overspray, a street art shop and graffiti school now based in Gulfport, teaching tagging, block letters, Wildstyle, and advanced mural technique to classes of up to twenty students. After Hurricane Milton, they painted free signs for businesses that lost theirs.\n\nThey have painted SHINE every year since its inception — "The Cool Kids" in 2015, "Bait and Switch" in 2019, and multiple walls since. For SHINE Origins 2025, Johnny painted two solo murals: one on the 600 block where it all began, and another at The Factory on Fairfield Ave South with his daughter Sionna.',desc:'A vibrant retro-style mural framed by a painted trompe-l\'oeil wooden frame with visible grain and nail heads. On the left, a pin-up cowgirl with brown curls, a red cowboy hat, and a studded red bustier smiles confidently. On the right, a cowboy in blue jeans and hat swings a lasso while riding a green alligator through a tropical Florida landscape — palm trees, hibiscus flowers, a sandy beach, and a warm sunset sky. "Ride \'em Cowboy" is lettered in bold cursive across the top center. The Vitale Bros signature is visible at the bottom left. The whole piece has a classic Americana postcard feel with a distinctly Florida twist.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/vitale-bros-ridem-cowboy.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE festival artist every year since inception; set design for Spring Breakers (Harmony Korine)',fw:[{name:'vitalebrothers.com',url:'https://vitalebrothers.com'}],sbio:'See Vitale Bros entries (ids 11, 12, 38, 73, 142) for full search bio.',fn:['Vitale Bros — family tradition since 1992','Set design for Spring Breakers','Local St. Pete legends']},
  {id:174,a:'Vitale Bros',t:'',loc:'Central Ave',bldg:'',lat:27.771298,lng:-82.669796,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'vitalebros',bio:'Their first mural came from a sketch on a paper napkin, handed to a restaurant owner. That was 1992.\n\nJohnny Vitale and his brother Paul started Vitale Bros in Tampa Bay hand-lettering signs and painting storefront windows. Their parents are artists. So were their grandparents. No formal training — just family tradition and thirty-plus years of practice. Their late aunt Nancy, who had Down syndrome and attended Parc Center for Disabilities, turned out to be an artist too, discovered in her final decade.\n\nIn the late 1990s they opened a warehouse studio that hosted group art, music, and fashion shows that helped birth St. Pete\'s emerging art scene. They collaborated on set design for Harmony Korine\'s Spring Breakers. They have painted for the Tampa Bay Rays, the Buccaneers, L\'Oreal, and dozens of local businesses. They call themselves the "mural police" — restoring damaged walls around the city for free.\n\nIn 2023, Johnny launched Overspray, a street art shop and graffiti school now based in Gulfport, teaching tagging, block letters, Wildstyle, and advanced mural technique to classes of up to twenty students. After Hurricane Milton, they painted free signs for businesses that lost theirs.\n\nThey have painted SHINE every year since its inception — "The Cool Kids" in 2015, "Bait and Switch" in 2019, and multiple walls since. For SHINE Origins 2025, Johnny painted two solo murals: one on the 600 block where it all began, and another at The Factory on Fairfield Ave South with his daughter Sionna.',desc:'A photorealistic great horned owl rendered in detailed gray, brown, and cream tones dominates the wall. The owl faces forward with piercing orange-amber eyes. Behind it, a military-style star emblem in dark gray and radiating sunburst stripes in teal, coral, and orange fan outward against a warm orange background. Signed "Vitale Broz" in a small blue tag at lower left.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/vitale-bros-owl.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'SHINE festival every year since inception; Spring Breakers set design; Tampa Bay Rays City Connect campaign',fw:[{name:'vitalebrothers.com',url:'https://vitalebrothers.com'}],sbio:'Their first mural came from a sketch on a paper napkin, handed to a restaurant owner. That was 1992.\n\nJohnny Vitale and his brother Paul founded Vitale Bros in 1992 in Tampa Bay, starting with hand-lettered signs and painted storefront windows. Youngest brother Joey later joined. Their parents are artists — the family artistic tradition goes back to their grandparents. No formal art education; the craft is entirely self-taught and family-transmitted. They have painted for the Tampa Bay Rays, the Buccaneers, L\'Oreal, and dozens of local businesses. They collaborated on set design for Harmony Korine\'s Spring Breakers.\n\nIn 2023, Johnny launched Overspray, a street art shop and graffiti school in Gulfport. After Hurricane Milton, they painted free signs for businesses. They have painted SHINE every year since its inception.',fn:['Vitale Bros — painting since 1992','Photorealistic wildlife murals','Local Pinellas County family'],src:['https://www.fox13news.com/news/vitale-brothers-paint-the-town-with-murals','https://vitalebrothers.com/']},
  {id:208,a:'Vitale Bros',t:'',loc:'5013 20th Ave S, Unit F, Gulfport',bldg:'Overspray',lat:27.75087,lng:-82.70133,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'vitalebros',bio:'This one is on their own wall. Johnny Vitale launched Overspray in 2023 — a street-art shop and graffiti school in Gulfport — and this green-skinned cartoon monster with a spray-can-cap head guards the side of the building, red can in one claw, "O.S." (Overspray) branded on its belly. Signed "VITALE BROS" lower right.\n\nJohnny Vitale and his brother Paul started Vitale Bros in Tampa Bay in 1992 hand-lettering signs and painting storefront windows. Their parents are artists. So were their grandparents. No formal training — just family tradition and thirty-plus years of practice.\n\nIn the late 1990s they opened a warehouse studio that hosted group art, music, and fashion shows that helped birth St. Pete\'s emerging art scene. They collaborated on set design for Harmony Korine\'s Spring Breakers. They have painted for the Tampa Bay Rays, the Buccaneers, L\'Oreal, and dozens of local businesses. They call themselves the "mural police" — restoring damaged walls around the city for free.\n\nThey have painted SHINE every year since its inception.',desc:'A large vertical mural on a white horizontal-clapboard exterior wall. The subject is a chunky cartoon monster, full-figure, facing the viewer. Its head is a stylized spray-paint can cap — silver dome with a black spray nozzle. The face is bright green with bulging bloodshot eyes (one rolled up, one rolled side), a wide fanged grin showing pointed teeth, and a long red tongue lolling out. In its left claw it holds a red spray can with a green cap, pointed away from the body, drips of paint dribbling off. Its green body is hairy/scruffy around the edges, with the letters "O.S." branded in red on its belly inside a black outline. The legs are stubby with blue cuffs and bare green clawed feet planted on the ground. A small navy-blue squiggle floats in the upper left, possibly representing motion or stink. Signed in dark-blue wildstyle lettering at the lower right: "VITALE BROS."',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/vitale-bros-overspray-monster.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'Painted on the side wall of Overspray, the Vitale Bros\' own street-art shop and graffiti school in Gulfport.',maw:'',aaw:'',fw:[{name:'vitalebros.com',url:'https://www.vitalebros.com'}],sbio:'Vitale Bros — see full bio on their other entries (ids 27, 73, 142, 156, 174). This is the side wall of Overspray, the Vitale Bros\' own street-art shop and graffiti school in Gulfport (launched by Johnny Vitale in 2023). Cartoon graffiti subject: a fanged green monster with a spray-can-cap for a head, holding a red spray can in its left claw, branded "O.S." (Overspray) across its belly, drool dripping from its long red tongue. Heavy black-outline cartoon style, signed "VITALE BROS" in wildstyle lettering lower right.\n\nJohnny and Paul Vitale grew up in a family of artists across three generations. No formal training. They have painted for the Tampa Bay Rays, the Buccaneers, L\'Oreal, and dozens of local businesses. They have painted every SHINE since 2015 — including "The Cool Kids" (2015) and "Bait and Switch" (2019). After Hurricane Milton, they painted free signs for businesses that lost theirs. Johnny launched Overspray in Gulfport in 2023 and teaches tagging, block letters, Wildstyle, and advanced mural technique to classes of up to twenty students.\n\nInstagram: @vitalebros.',src:['https://www.vitalebros.com/','https://www.instagram.com/vitalebros/']},
  {id:172,a:'Ya La\'Ford',t:'Golden Wave',loc:'719 Central Ave',bldg:'Morean Arts Center',lat:27.771625,lng:-82.644242,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'yalaford',bio:'She has an MFA and a law degree. She represented the United States at the Dakar Biennale in Senegal.\n\nYa La\'Ford is a first-generation American of Jamaican heritage, from the Bronx. She earned her MFA from the Art Institute of Boston (now Lesley University) and her Juris Doctor from the University of Florida. She maintains studios in St. Petersburg, the Bronx, Jamaica, and Maine.\n\nHer work spans painting, sculpture, sound, print, video, and immersive installations using light, water, sound, and motion. Sharp geometric lines, bold contrasting colors, vibrant circles and triangles. She sits on the Board of Trustees of the Museum of Fine Arts in St. Petersburg. She designed an Arrow McLaren SP IndyCar and was a featured artist for Super Bowl 2021.\n\nClients include the Tampa Bay Rays, Toronto Blue Jays, and Nike. Her work is in the collections of Tropicana Field, Al Lang Stadium, the Morean Arts Center, and the Boston Public Library. She has shown at The James Museum, The Ringling Museum of Art, and Florida CraftArt.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, she painted in St. Petersburg.',desc:'A striking gold-on-black mural on the exterior of the Morean Arts Center. Ya La\'Ford\'s signature dense geometric pattern — interlocking angular shapes rendered in gold leaf or metallic gold paint — forms a large arch or wave sweeping across a dark charcoal wall. The pattern flows over and around a pair of black double doors, with a matching geometric heart motif painted on the doors themselves. The composition creates an almost architectural portal effect. A small gold plaque on the lower right reads "Ya La\'Ford / Golden Wave / Morean Arts Center / muralofia.com." Sunlight catches the metallic lines, giving the piece a luminous, shifting quality.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/commercial/ya-la-ford-golden-wave.jpeg',from:'Tampa, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'U.S. representative, Dakar Biennale Senegal (2024); Super Bowl 2021 featured artist; Arrow McLaren IndyCar designer; Board of Trustees, Museum of Fine Arts St. Petersburg; SHINE 2021',fw:[{name:'yalaford.com',url:'https://www.yalaford.com'}],sbio:'She has an MFA and a law degree. She represented the United States at the Dakar Biennale in Senegal.\n\nYa La\'Ford is a first-generation American of Jamaican heritage, from the Bronx. She earned her MFA from the Art Institute of Boston (now Lesley University) and her Juris Doctor from the University of Florida. She maintains studios in St. Petersburg, the Bronx, Jamaica, and Maine.\n\nHer work spans painting, sculpture, sound, print, video, and immersive installations using light, water, sound, and motion. Sharp geometric lines, bold contrasting colors, vibrant circles and triangles. She sits on the Board of Trustees of the Museum of Fine Arts in St. Petersburg. She designed an Arrow McLaren SP IndyCar and was a featured artist for Super Bowl 2021.\n\nClients include the Tampa Bay Rays, Toronto Blue Jays, and Nike. Her work is in the collections of Tropicana Field, Al Lang Stadium, the Morean Arts Center, and the Boston Public Library. She has shown at The James Museum, The Ringling Museum of Art, and Florida CraftArt.\n\nFor SHINE 2021, she painted in St. Petersburg.'},
  {id:199,a:'Zulu Painter',t:'Stonehenge Park',loc:'',bldg:'Stonehenge Park (Tyrone Blvd overpass on Pinellas Trail)',lat:27.79917,lng:-82.73745,y:0,cat:'commercial',ig:'zulupainter1',bio:'Zulu Painter usually crowds the wall — gators, portraits, geometric patterns stacked on top of each other. At Stonehenge Park he painted three palm silhouettes and stopped.\n\nCarlos Culbertson — Zulu Painter — is a native West Virginian who started pursuing art at age eight. He holds a degree in fine arts painting and works out of a studio at the ArtsXchange on 22nd Street South. He paints in acrylic and spray paint, favors reclaimed materials, and has worked as a scenic artist for Disney on Ice, the Science Channel, HSN, and SeaWorld Parks and Entertainment.\n\nHis mural projects span the country: Denver, Chattanooga, Atlanta, Austin, Miami, Las Vegas, Los Angeles, Kansas City, Flint, and Binghamton, New York — where he painted "Cultivate Kindness," a 40-foot mural with West Side Middle School students. He is a four-time SHINE artist (2016, 2017, 2018, 2024) and returned for SHINE Origins 2025. He received a St. Pete Arts Alliance Muse Award in 2022.',desc:'A wide concrete wall under the Tyrone Blvd overpass at the entrance to Stonehenge Park on the Pinellas Trail. Three deep-teal coconut palm trees stand in silhouette across the wall, rooted in pale sand. Behind them, a soft turquoise-to-aqua sky holds soft white cumulus clouds. The horizon line is a band of cream-colored beach. A green Old-English-lettered "Stonehenge Park" sign with white border is mounted on the upper-center pillar above the middle palm. Signed "zulupainter" in cyan in the lower-right corner. The composition reads as a stripped-down beachscape — silhouettes only, no detail on the trees themselves, letting the negative space carry it.',imp:[],img:'images/murals/unknown/zulu-painter-stonehenge-palms.jpeg',from:'St. Petersburg, FL',aud:'',insp:'',maw:'',aaw:'St. Pete Arts Alliance Muse Award (2022); SHINE 2016, 2017, 2018, 2024 selected artist',fw:[{name:'zulupainter.com',url:'https://www.zulupainter.com'}],sbio:'Three palm trees in silhouette against a soft turquoise sky, with sand and clouds beneath. The Stonehenge Park sign in green Old-English lettering hangs on the upper-center pillar. Signed "zulupainter" in cyan in the lower-right corner. Painted on a low concrete wall under the Tyrone Blvd overpass on the Pinellas Trail in St. Petersburg, Florida.\n\nCarlos Culbertson — Zulu Painter — is a native West Virginian who started pursuing art in elementary school and never looked back. He holds a degree in fine arts painting and works out of his studio at the ArtsXchange in St. Petersburg, located at 515 22nd Street South, Studio 111. He works in acrylic and spray paint, favors reclaimed materials, and produces work across traditional mediums, body paint, special effects, and scenic art. He calls his approach "Mindful Murals."\n\nHis scenic art client list includes The Science Channel, Disney on Ice, Home Shopping Network (HSN), SeaWorld Parks and Entertainment, and a series of national ad campaigns. He has also served as a Representative Artist for Visit Florida.\n\nHis mural work spans the country: Denver, Chattanooga, Atlanta, Austin, Miami, Las Vegas, Los Angeles, Kansas City, Flint, Sanford (FL), Jacksonville, and Binghamton, New York. In Binghamton he painted "Spread Love" — an approximately 40-foot mural on the M&T Bank building at 68 Exchange Street. He has painted at Paint Memphis 2019, the 2018 Colorado Black Arts Festival, the 48 Hour Mural Competition in Jacksonville (Sept 29, 2023), HULAWEEN at Spirit of Suwannee, and the Art With Me Festival in Miami.\n\nIn St. Petersburg, he painted CASA Family Justice Center (Arlington Ave, domestic violence survivor themes) and a portrait of Betty Reid Soskin at the Florida Botanical Gardens in Largo for the 2024 Pinellas African American Heritage Celebration. He is a four-time SHINE Mural Festival artist: "Moon Beam Dream 2" (2016), "Legacy" with Thirst (2017), "Wisdom, Growth, Progress" (2018), and the AMIKids wall (2024). He returned for SHINE Origins 2025 at 31st St & 7th Ave South. He collaborated with Reid Jenkins and Iboms on "Imagine Gas Plant and Laurel Park" at Campbell Park Recreational Center.\n\nIn 2022 he received a St. Petersburg Arts Alliance Muse Award. He has also been recognized with a 2021 Business Arts Award, was a winner of the 2021 UF Big Idea Competition, and was featured in the Innovate Gainesville Book.\n\nStudio open Mon–Fri, 9am–6pm. Contact: info@zulupainter.com, 727-768-4737. Website: zulupainter.com. Instagram: @zulupainter1. Facebook: facebook.com/ZULUPAINTER1.',src:['https://www.zulupainter.com/about.html','https://creativepinellas.org/magazine/the-mindful-murals-of-zulu-painter/','https://stpeteartsalliance.org/zulupainter-2']}
];

